// ══ Unit U7 — Time Series Analysis (ARIMA, GARCH, Spectral Methods) — Question Bank ══
// Built from source LaTeX (5 beamer mock-test decks + 10 topic-wise MCQ
// banks u7_1..u7_10_note_s3) with corrected KaTeX-ready math ($...$ /
// $$...$$ delimiters, custom macros expanded) and with '<', '>', '&'
// HTML-escaped so innerHTML-based rendering in statnet.html can never
// mis-parse a literal angle bracket as a tag.

MASTER_QB.push(

  {unit:'u7',q:'A time series ${X_t}$ is said to be strictly (strongly) stationary if:',opts:['A) The joint distribution of $(X_{t1},\\ldots,X_{tk})$ is invariant under any time shift h for all k and $t1,\\ldots,tk$','B) Its autocorrelation function decays to zero as lag increases','C) $E(X_t)$ $=$ 0 and $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ $=$ 1 for all t','D) Its mean and variance are constant over time'],ans:0,exp:'Strict stationarity requires that the ENTIRE finite-dimensional joint distribution of $(X_{t1},\\ldots,X_{tk})$ equals that of $(X_{t1}+h,\\ldots,X_{tk}+h)$ for any h, k, and time-points. This is a distributional requirement, much stronger than simply having a constant mean or variance. Option (D) describes weak (second-order) stationarity conditions only. Option (C) is an additional restriction beyond even strict stationarity. Strict $=$ all joint distributions shift-invariant. Weak $=$ only first two moments shift-invariant. Strict $\\Rightarrow$ Weak (if moments exist) but NOT vice versa.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A process ${X_t}$ is weakly stationary (covariance stationary) if and only if:',opts:['A) $E(X_t)$ $=$ 0 and $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t$, $X_s)$ $=$ 0 for t $\\neq$ s','B) $X_t$ satisfies a linear difference equation with constant coefficients','C) Its characteristic function is the same for all t','D) $E(X_t)$ $=$ $\\mu$ (constant), $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ &lt; $\\infty$, and $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t$, $X_t+h)$ $=$ $\\gamma(h)$ depends only on lag h'],ans:3,exp:'Weak stationarity (also called wide-sense or second-order stationarity) requires exactly three conditions: (1) $E(X_t)$ $=$ $\\mu$ is constant for all t, (2) $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ &lt; $\\infty$ for all t, and (3) the autocovariance $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t$, $X_t+h)$ $=$ $\\gamma(h)$ is a function of lag h alone, not of t. Option (A) imposes zero mean and no correlation $---$ too restrictive. Option (B) describes a property of ARMA processes but is not the definition. Three pillars of weak stationarity: Constant Mean $+$ Finite Variance $+$ ACVF $=$ f(lag only). Think \'M-V-C\'.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which set of properties does the autocovariance function (ACVF) $\\gamma(h)$ of a stationary process satisfy?',opts:['A) $\\gamma(h)$ $=$ $\\gamma(-h)$, $|\\gamma(h)|$ $\\leq$ $\\gamma(0)$, and $\\gamma$ is a positive semi-definite function','B) $\\gamma(h)$ $\\geq$ 0 for all h, and $\\gamma(0)$ is the maximum','C) $\\gamma(h)$ is a strictly decreasing function of $|h|$','D) $\\gamma(h)$ $\\to$ 0 as h $\\to$ $\\infty$ for all stationary processes'],ans:0,exp:'The ACVF satisfies three fundamental properties: (1) Symmetry: $\\gamma(h)$ $=$ $\\gamma(-h)$ because $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t$, $X_t+h)$ $=$ $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t+h$, $X_t)$. (2) Bound: $|\\gamma(h)|$ $\\leq$ $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ by the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality. (3) Positive semi-definiteness: for any constants $a_1,\\ldots,a_n$ and time points $t_1,\\ldots,t_n$, the sum of $a_i\\cdot a_j\\cdot \\gamma(t_i-t_j)$ $\\geq$ 0. Option (B) is wrong $---$ $\\gamma(h)$ can be negative (e.g., MA(1) with negative $\\theta)$. Option (D) fails for unit-root or integrated processes. ACVF is an even function (symmetric), bounded by its value at zero, and PSD. These three are necessary AND sufficient for a function to be a valid ACVF.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The autocorrelation function (ACF) $\\rho(h)$ of a weakly stationary process is defined as:',opts:['A) $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $\\gamma(h)$ $/$ $[\\gamma(0)]^2$','B) $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $[\\gamma(h)]^2$ $/$ $\\gamma(0)$','C) $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $E(X_t$ $\\cdot$ $X_t+h)$ for zero-mean processes only','D) $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $\\gamma(h)$ $/$ $\\gamma(0)$'],ans:3,exp:'The ACF is the standardised ACVF: $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $\\gamma(h)/\\gamma(0)$. Since for a stationary process $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ $=$ $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t+h)$ $=$ $\\gamma(0)$, this equals $\\mathrm{Corr}(X_t$, $X_t+h)$. The ACF satisfies $\\rho(0)$ $=$ 1, $|\\rho(h)|$ $\\leq$ 1, and $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $\\rho(-h)$. Option (C) is the formula for the raw correlation only when the mean is zero. Note: $\\rho(h)$ is dimensionless and entirely characterises second-order dependence structure. ACF $=$ ACVF divided by Variance. $\\rho(0)$ $=$ 1 always. $|\\rho(h)|$ $\\leq$ 1 always (Cauchy-Schwarz).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The partial autocorrelation function (PACF) $\\phi_{hh}$ at lag h is best described as:',opts:['A) The Fourier transform of the ACVF $\\gamma(h)$','B) The simple correlation between $X_t$ and $X_t+h$','C) The correlation between $X_t$ and $X_t+h$ after removing the linear effect of the intermediate variables $X_t+1,\\ldots,X_t+h-1$','D) The partial derivative of the ACF with respect to lag h'],ans:2,exp:'The PACF at lag h measures the direct linear association between $X_t$ and $X_t+h$, controlling for the intervening values $X_t+1,\\ldots,X_t+h-1$. Formally, $\\phi_{hh}$ is the last coefficient in the regression of $X_t+h$ on $X_t+h-1,\\ldots,X_t$. For an AR(p) process, the PACF cuts off (equals zero) at lags beyond p $---$ this is the key identification tool. Option (B) is the ACF. Option (A) is the spectral density (up to a constant). ACF cuts off for MA(q) processes. PACF cuts off for AR(p) processes. This is the cornerstone of ARMA model identification!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the correlogram of a stationary time series, what do the dashed horizontal lines typically represent?',opts:['A) The mean and $+/-$ 1 standard deviation of the series','B) The theoretical ACF values of the best-fitting ARMA model','C) The $+/-$ 1 and $+/-$ 2 lag values of the PACF','D) Approximate $95\\%$ confidence bounds for the sample ACF under the null hypothesis of white noise'],ans:3,exp:'Under the null hypothesis that ${X_t}$ is white noise, the sample ACF r(h) for h &gt; 0 is approximately N(0, $1/n)$ for large n. Thus the $95\\%$ confidence interval is approximately $+/-$ $1.96/\\sqrt{n}$. The dashed lines in a correlogram are drawn at $+/-$ $2/\\sqrt{n}$ (a common approximation). Sample ACF values outside these bounds suggest significant autocorrelation at that lag, providing evidence against white noise. Bartlett\'s formula: under white noise, Var(r(h)) $\\approx$ $1/n$. Confidence bands at $+/-$ $2/\\sqrt{n}$. If ACF spikes exceed these, the series is NOT white noise.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A weak white noise process ${\\epsilon_t}$ is characterised by which of the following?',opts:['A) $E(\\epsilon_t)$ $=$ 0, $\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ &lt; $\\infty$, and $\\mathrm{Cov}(\\epsilon_t$, $\\epsilon_s)$ $=$ 0 for all t $\\neq$ s','B) $E(\\epsilon_t)$ $=$ 0 and $\\epsilon_t$ is i.i.d.','C) $E(\\epsilon_t)$ $=$ 0, $\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$, and $\\epsilon_t$ $\\sim$ $N(0,\\sigma^2)$ for all t','D) $\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t)$ $=$ 1 and $E(\\epsilon_t)$ $=$ 0'],ans:0,exp:'Weak white noise requires: (1) $E(\\epsilon_t)$ $=$ 0, (2) $\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ &lt; $\\infty$ constant, (3) $\\mathrm{Cov}(\\epsilon_t$, $\\epsilon_s)$ $=$ 0 for t $\\neq$ s. It does NOT require normality (that is strong/Gaussian white noise) and does NOT require independence $---$ only zero correlation (that is strict white noise). Option (B) gives strict white noise (i.i.d.). Option (C) adds normality, making it Gaussian white noise. Understanding these distinctions is critical for UGC NET. Hierarchy: Gaussian WN (i.i.d. Normal) $\\Rightarrow$ Strict WN (i.i.d.) $\\Rightarrow$ Weak WN (uncorrelated). Each is strictly weaker than the previous.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A stationary process ${X_t}$ is ergodic (for the mean) if:',opts:['A) It is strictly stationary and Gaussian','B) Its sample mean converges to a constant regardless of the realisation','C) The time average $(1/T)\\sum(X_t)$ converges in probability to $E(X_t)$ as T $\\to$ $\\infty$','D) Its spectral density is uniformly bounded'],ans:2,exp:'Ergodicity allows us to estimate population moments from a single (long) realisation. A process is ergodic for the mean if $(1/T)\\cdot \\sum_{t=1}^T$ $X_t$ $\\to$ $_P$ $E(X_t)$. A sufficient condition is that $\\gamma(h)$ $\\to$ 0 as h $\\to$ $\\infty$. This is crucial for time series inference: we observe one path, but ergodicity ensures our sample statistics converge to the true parameters. Option (B) is an informal statement. Option (A) is a sufficient but not necessary condition. Ergodicity $=$ \'time average $=$ ensemble average\'. If the ACVF decays to 0, the process is likely ergodic. Practically, ergodicity is assumed in almost all time series analysis.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the time series: 1, 3, 5, 3, 1, 3, 5, 3 $(n=8)$, what is the sample mean x-bar?',opts:['A) 3.0','B) 2.5','C) 4.0','D) 3.5'],ans:0,exp:'Sum $=$ $1+3+5+3+1+3+5+3$ $=$ 24. Sample mean $=$ $24/8$ $=$ 3.0. This is a foundational step before computing the sample ACVF: $\\hat{\\gamma}(h)$ $=$ $(1/n)\\cdot \\sum_{t=1}^{n-h}(X_t$ $-$ $x-bar)(X_{t+h}$ $-$ x-bar). Note that the divisor is n (not n-h) in the standard definition used in time series to ensure the sample ACVF is positive semi-definite. Always compute mean first before ACF. Time series ACVF uses divisor n (not n-h), unlike some textbook regression formulas.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The long-run upward or downward movement in a time series is called:',opts:['A) Seasonality','B) Cyclical component','C) Trend (secular trend)','D) Irregular variation'],ans:2,exp:'The classical decomposition identifies four components: (1) Trend (T): long-run direction over years/decades; (2) Seasonality (S): regular periodic patterns within a year (weekly, monthly); (3) Cyclical (C): longer-term undulations around trend, often $2-10$ years, linked to business cycles; (4) Irregular (I): random, unpredictable fluctuations. In the additive model $X_t$ $=$ $T_t$ $+$ $S_t$ $+$ $C_t$ $+$ $I_t$. UGC NET questions frequently test the distinction between these components. TSCI mnemonic: Trend, Seasonality, Cyclical, Irregular. Seasonality is periodic and fixed-period; Cycles are longer, irregular-period fluctuations.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An MA(1) process is defined as $X_t$ $=$ $\\mu$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ $+$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$. Its ACF $\\rho(h)$ equals:',opts:['A) $1/(1+\\theta^2)$ for $h=1$, and 0 for $|h|$ $\\geq$ 2','B) $\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$ for $h=1$, and 0 for $|h|$ $\\geq$ 2','C) $\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$ for all h $\\geq$ 1','D) $\\theta$ for $h=1$, and $\\theta^h$ for $|h|$ $\\geq$ 2'],ans:1,exp:'For MA(1): $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)$; $\\gamma(1)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot \\theta$; $\\gamma(h)$ $=$ 0 for $|h|$ $\\geq$ 2. Therefore $\\rho(1)$ $=$ $\\gamma(1)/\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$ and $\\rho(h)$ $=$ 0 for $|h|$ $\\geq$ 2. This CUTOFF at lag 1 is the key identification feature of MA(1). Note: the maximum value of $|\\rho(1)|$ is 0.5 (achieved when $\\theta$ $=$ $+/-1)$. This is because $\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$ $\\leq$ $1/2$ by AM-GM inequality. MA(q) ACF cuts off after lag q. MA(1): $\\rho(1)$ $=$ $\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$, zero beyond. $Max|\\rho(1)|$ $=$ 0.5 when $\\theta$ $=$ $\\pm$ 1.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An MA(1) process $X_t$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$ $+$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$ is invertible if and only if:',opts:['A) $|\\theta|$ &gt; 1','B) $\\theta$ $=$ 0','C) $\\theta$ is any real number','D) $|\\theta|$ &lt; 1'],ans:3,exp:'Invertibility of MA(q) requires that all roots of the MA polynomial 1 $+$ $\\theta_1\\cdot z$ $+$ $\\ldots$ $+$ $\\theta_q\\cdot z^q$ lie OUTSIDE the unit circle $(|z|$ &gt; 1). For MA(1): 1 $+$ $\\theta\\cdot z$ $=$ 0 $\\Rightarrow$ z $=$ $-1/\\theta$. We need $|-1/\\theta|$ &gt; 1, i.e., $|\\theta|$ &lt; 1. Invertibility ensures a unique representation and allows the MA process to be written as a convergent $AR(\\infty)$ model. This is the exact mirror of the AR stationarity condition. MA invertibility: roots of MA polynomial outside unit circle &lt; $\\Rightarrow$ $|\\theta|$ &lt; 1 for MA(1). AR stationarity: roots of AR polynomial outside unit circle. They are MIRROR conditions!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(1) process $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ with $|\\phi|$ &lt; 1, the ACF is:',opts:['A) $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $\\phi^|h|$ for all h','B) $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $\\phi^h$ for h $\\geq$ 0 only','C) $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $\\phi/(1-\\phi^2)$ for $h=1$, and 0 for h $\\geq$ 2','D) $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $1-\\phi^2$ for all h $\\geq$ 1'],ans:0,exp:'Multiplying $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ through by $X_{t-h}$ and taking expectations yields the Yule-Walker equation: $\\gamma(h)$ $=$ $\\phi\\cdot \\gamma(h-1)$ for h $\\geq$ 1. Solving recursively: $\\gamma(h)$ $=$ $\\phi^h$ $\\cdot$ $\\gamma(0)$, so $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $\\phi^h$ for h $\\geq$ 0, and by symmetry $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $\\phi^|h|$ for all integer h. The ACF decays exponentially (geometrically) for $|\\phi|$ &lt; 1 $---$ it NEVER cuts off. This contrasts with PACF which cuts off at lag 1 for AR(1). AR(1) ACF decays exponentially as $\\phi^|h|$. PACF cuts off at lag 1. Positive $\\phi$ $\\Rightarrow$ all positive ACF. Negative $\\phi$ $\\Rightarrow$ alternating ACF.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An AR(1) process $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ is stationary if and only if:',opts:['A) $|\\phi|$ &lt; 1','B) $\\phi$ &gt; 0','C) $|\\phi|$ &gt; 1','D) $|\\phi|$ $\\leq$ 1'],ans:0,exp:'For AR(1), the characteristic equation is 1 $-$ $\\phi\\cdot z$ $=$ 0, giving root z $=$ $1/\\phi$. Stationarity requires this root to lie OUTSIDE the unit circle: $|1/\\phi|$ &gt; 1 &lt; $\\Rightarrow$ $|\\phi|$ &lt; 1. When $|\\phi|$ $=$ 1, we get a unit root (non-stationary random walk). When $\\phi$ $=$ 1 exactly, $X_t$ is a random walk with variance growing as $t\\cdot \\sigma^2$. The variance of stationary AR(1) is $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$, which diverges as $|\\phi|$ $\\to$ 1. AR stationarity: root of AR polynomial OUTSIDE unit circle. For AR(1): $|\\phi|$ &lt; 1. For AR(p): all p roots of $(1-\\phi_1\\cdot z-\\ldots-\\phi_p\\cdot z^p)$ must satisfy $|z|$ &gt; 1.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(1) process $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ where $\\epsilon_t$ $\\sim$ $\\mathrm{WN}(0,\\sigma^2)$, the variance of $X_t$ is:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2$ $/$ (1 $-$ $\\phi)$','B) $\\sigma^2$ $/$ (1 $-$ $\\phi^2)$','C) $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ (1 $+$ $\\phi^2)$','D) $\\sigma^2$'],ans:1,exp:'Taking variance of $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$: $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ $=$ $\\phi^2\\cdot \\mathrm{Var}(X_{t-1})$ $+$ $\\sigma^2$. By stationarity $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ $=$ $\\mathrm{Var}(X_{t-1})$ $=$ $\\gamma(0)$. So $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\phi^2\\cdot \\gamma(0)$ $+$ $\\sigma^2$, giving $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$. Note: this is always larger than $\\sigma^2$ (the WN variance) and diverges as $|\\phi|$ $\\to$ 1. For $\\phi$ $=$ 0.9: Var $=$ $\\sigma^2/0.19$ $\\approx$ $5.26\\cdot \\sigma^2$ $---$ much larger than the noise variance alone! Var(AR1) $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $/$ $(1-\\phi^2)$. Increases as $\\phi$ $\\to$ 1. Stationarity ensures it stays finite. $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$ is the ACVF at lag 0.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(1) process, the PACF $\\phi_{hh}$ at lags h $=$ 1, 2, 3, $\\ldots$ equals:',opts:['A) $\\phi$, $\\phi^2$, $\\phi^3$, $\\ldots$ (exponential decay)','B) $\\phi/(1+\\phi^2)$ at lag 1, and 0 for h $\\geq$ 2','C) 0 for all lags h $\\geq$ 1','D) $\\phi$ at lag 1, and 0 for all lags h $\\geq$ 2'],ans:3,exp:'By definition, for an AR(p) process, the PACF cuts off after lag p $---$ it is zero for lags beyond p. For AR(1), $\\phi_{11}$ $=$ $\\rho(1)$ $=$ $\\phi$, and $\\phi_{hh}$ $=$ 0 for h $\\geq$ 2. This is because once we control for $X_{t-1}$, there is no additional information in $X_{t-2}$, $X_{t-3}$, etc. about $X_t$. This cutoff property is the defining identification tool: an observed PACF that cuts off sharply after lag p indicates an AR(p) model. PACF cuts off for AR(p): zero beyond lag p. ACF tails off (exponential/sinusoidal). This is the KEY identification rule $---$ the mirror of ACF cutting off for MA(q).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(2) process $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi_1\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\phi_2\\cdot X_{t-2}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$, the Yule-Walker equations relate the AR parameters to the ACF via:',opts:['A) $\\phi_1$ $=$ $\\rho(1)$ $+$ $\\rho(2)$; $\\phi_2$ $=$ $\\rho(2)$ $-$ $\\rho(1)^2$','B) $\\phi_1$ $=$ $\\rho(1)\\cdot (1-\\rho(2))/(1-\\rho(1)^2)$; $\\phi_2$ $=$ $(\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2)/(1-\\rho(1)^2)$','C) $\\rho(1)$ $=$ $\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)$; $\\rho(2)$ $=$ $\\phi_2$ $+$ $\\phi_1^2/(1-\\phi_2)$','D) $\\rho(1)$ $=$ $\\phi_1$ $+$ $\\phi_2\\cdot \\rho(1)$; $\\rho(2)$ $=$ $\\phi_1\\cdot \\rho(1)$ $+$ $\\phi_2$'],ans:1,exp:'Multiplying AR(2) by $X_{t-h}$ and taking expectations gives: $\\gamma(h)$ $=$ $\\phi_1\\cdot \\gamma(h-1)$ $+$ $\\phi_2\\cdot \\gamma(h-2)$. Dividing by $\\gamma(0)$: $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $\\phi_1\\cdot \\rho(h-1)$ $+$ $\\phi_2\\cdot \\rho(h-2)$. For $h=1$: $\\rho(1)$ $=$ $\\phi_1$ $+$ $\\phi_2\\cdot \\rho(1)$. For $h=2$: $\\rho(2)$ $=$ $\\phi_1\\cdot \\rho(1)$ $+$ $\\phi_2$. Solving these two equations simultaneously gives the formulas in option (B). In matrix form: $[\\rho_1 \\rho_2]^T$ $=$ $R^{-1}$ $\\cdot$ $[\\rho(1) \\rho(2)]^T$ where R is the Toeplitz correlation matrix. Yule-Walker: multiply AR(p) by $X_{t-h}$, take E( $\\cdot$ $)$. You get p equations in p unknowns $(\\phi_1,\\ldots,\\phi_p)$. Method of moments estimation uses sample ACF in place of $\\rho(h)$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The ARMA(1,1) process $X_t$ $-$ $\\phi\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$ $+$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$ is stationary and invertible when:',opts:['A) $|\\theta|$ &lt; 1 only','B) $|\\phi|$ &lt; 1 and $|\\theta|$ &lt; 1','C) $|\\phi|$ &lt; 1 only','D) $|\\phi|$ $+$ $|\\theta|$ &lt; 1'],ans:1,exp:'For an ARMA(p,q) model, stationarity and invertibility are SEPARATE requirements: (1) Stationarity depends only on the AR polynomial $---$ all roots of 1 $-$ $\\phi\\cdot z$ must lie outside the unit circle: $|\\phi|$ &lt; 1. (2) Invertibility depends only on the MA polynomial $---$ all roots of 1 $+$ $\\theta\\cdot z$ must lie outside the unit circle: $|\\theta|$ &lt; 1. These conditions are independent. Having both ensures the process has a two-sided representation and admits unique causal $MA(\\infty)$ and $AR(\\infty)$ forms. ARMA stationarity: AR polynomial roots outside unit circle. Invertibility: MA polynomial roots outside unit circle. Check them SEPARATELY.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ARMA(1,1) model $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ $+$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$, with WN variance $\\sigma^2$, the ACVF at lag 1, $\\gamma(1)$, equals:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1+\\phi\\cdot \\theta)\\cdot (\\phi+\\theta)/(1+2\\cdot \\phi\\cdot \\theta+\\theta^2)$','B) $\\sigma^2\\cdot (\\phi+\\theta)\\cdot (1+\\phi\\cdot \\theta)$ $/$ $(1+2\\cdot \\phi\\cdot \\theta+\\theta^2)$','C) $\\sigma^2\\cdot \\phi\\cdot (1+\\phi\\cdot \\theta)$','D) $\\sigma^2\\cdot (\\phi+\\theta)\\cdot (1+\\phi\\cdot \\theta)$'],ans:1,exp:'For ARMA(1,1): $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1+2\\cdot \\phi\\cdot \\theta+\\theta^2)/(1-\\phi^2)$; $\\gamma(1)$ $=$ $(\\phi+\\theta)\\cdot (1+\\phi\\cdot \\theta)\\cdot \\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$. Therefore $\\rho(1)$ $=$ $\\gamma(1)/\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $(\\phi+\\theta)\\cdot (1+\\phi\\cdot \\theta)/(1+2\\cdot \\phi\\cdot \\theta+\\theta^2)$. For lags h $\\geq$ 2, $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $\\phi\\cdot \\rho(h-1)$, so the ACF tail follows an $AR(1)-like$ exponential pattern from lag 1 onwards. The PACF also tails off for ARMA processes (neither cuts off sharply). ARMA(p,q) ACF: neither cuts off (tails off exponentially) for lag &gt; q; PACF: tails off. Both ACF $\\&amp;$ PACF tail off $\\Rightarrow$ suggests ARMA rather than pure AR or MA.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Wold Decomposition Theorem states that any zero-mean weakly stationary process ${X_t}$ can be written as:',opts:['A) $X_t$ is either purely $MA(\\infty)$ or purely $AR(\\infty)$','B) $X_t$ $=$ $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}$ $\\psi_j$ $\\cdot$ $Z_{t-j}$ for some deterministic component','C) $X_t$ $=$ $V_t$ $+$ $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}$ $\\psi_j$ $\\cdot$ $\\epsilon_{t-j}$, where $V_t$ is deterministic, $\\psi_0=1$, $\\sum$ $\\psi_j^2$ &lt; $\\infty$, and $\\epsilon_t$ is WN','D) $X_t$ $=$ $\\sum_{j=-\\infty}^{\\infty}$ $\\psi_j$ $\\cdot$ $\\epsilon_{t-j}$ for any square-summable sequence'],ans:2,exp:'Wold\'s theorem guarantees every zero-mean weakly stationary non-deterministic process has a unique representation as a sum of: (1) A purely deterministic component $V_t$ (perfectly predictable from its own past), and (2) An infinite-order one-sided MA process with $\\psi_0=1$, sum of $\\psi_j^2$ &lt; $\\infty$, and $\\epsilon_t$ being white noise orthogonal to $V_t$. This theorem provides the theoretical foundation for ARMA modelling $---$ any stationary process is \'approximately\' $MA(\\infty)$, and ARMA provides a parsimonious parametric approximation. Wold $=$ deterministic part $+$ $MA(\\infty)$ part. The $MA(\\infty)$ part justifies why any stationary series can be modelled as ARMA(p,q). $\\psi_0=1$ always, coefficients must be square-summable.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The AR(2) process $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi_1\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\phi_2\\cdot X_{t-2}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ is stationary in the region satisfying:',opts:['A) $|\\phi_1|$ &lt; 1 and $|\\phi_2|$ &lt; 1 individually','B) $\\phi_1$ $+$ $\\phi_2$ &lt; 1, $\\phi_2$ $-$ $\\phi_1$ &lt; 1, and $|\\phi_2|$ &lt; 1','C) $\\phi_1$ $+$ $\\phi_2$ &lt; 1, $\\phi_2$ $-$ $\\phi_1$ &lt; 1, and $-1$ &lt; $\\phi_2$ &lt; 0','D) $\\phi_1^2$ $+$ $\\phi_2^2$ &lt; 1'],ans:1,exp:'The AR(2) characteristic equation is 1 $-$ $\\phi_1\\cdot z$ $-$ $\\phi_2\\cdot z^2$ $=$ 0. For both roots to lie outside the unit circle, we need the three conditions in (B). Geometrically this defines a triangular region in the $(\\phi_1$, $\\phi_2)$ plane. Note: option (A) is WRONG $---$ individual $|\\phi_i|$ &lt; 1 does NOT guarantee stationarity of AR(2). For example $\\phi_1$ $=$ 0.8, $\\phi_2$ $=$ 0.6 satisfies option (A) but $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=1.4$ &gt; 1 violates stationarity. AR(2) stationarity triangle: three inequalities. Individual $|\\phi_i|$ &lt; 1 is NOT enough! Always check the triangle conditions. $\\phi_2$ &lt; 1 ensures the constant term of the characteristic polynomial is non-zero.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A general linear process is defined as $X_t$ $=$ $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}$ $\\psi_j$ $\\cdot$ $\\epsilon_{t-j}$. For this to be a well-defined stationary process, the sequence ${\\psi_j}$ must satisfy:',opts:['A) $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}$ $\\psi_j$ $=$ 0','B) $\\psi_j$ $=$ 0 for all j &gt; some finite q','C) $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}$ $\\psi_j^2$ &lt; $\\infty$ (square summability)','D) $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}$ $|\\psi_j|$ &lt; $\\infty$ (absolute summability)'],ans:2,exp:'For the general linear process $X_t$ $=$ $\\sum_{j \\geq 0}$ $\\psi_j\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-j}$ to be well-defined in mean-square sense, we need $\\sum$ $\\psi_j^2$ &lt; $\\infty$. This ensures: (1) The variance $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $\\sum$ $\\psi_j^2$ is finite; (2) The series converges in mean square. Absolute summability (option B) is a stronger condition that additionally ensures absolute convergence of the ACVF. For the ACF to be absolutely summable, absolute summability of $\\psi_j$ is required. Square summability is the minimum requirement for stationarity. Square summable $\\Rightarrow$ stationary. Absolute summable $\\Rightarrow$ invertible and ACF absolutely summable. Absolute summability implies square summability (by Cauchy-Schwarz), not vice versa.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an MA(q) process, the ACF $\\rho(h)$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $\\rho(h)$ $\\neq$ 0 for all h $\\geq$ 1 (decays geometrically)','B) $\\rho(h)$ decays exponentially for h &gt; q','C) $\\rho(h)$ $=$ $\\theta^h$ for h $=$ 1, 2, $\\ldots$, q','D) $\\rho(h)$ $=$ 0 for $|h|$ &gt; q, and $\\rho(h)$ may be non-zero for 1 $\\leq$ $|h|$ $\\leq$ q'],ans:3,exp:'For MA(q): $X_t$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$ $+$ $\\theta_1\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\ldots$ $+$ $\\theta_q\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-q}$. The ACVF $\\gamma(h)$ $=$ 0 for $|h|$ &gt; q because $X_t$ and $X_{t+h}$ share no common $\\epsilon$ terms when $|h|$ &gt; q. This TRUNCATION property of the ACF is the defining feature of MA processes. The PACF of MA(q) tails off (decays exponentially or with damped oscillations) $---$ it never cuts off. This contrast is the backbone of Box-Jenkins identification. MA(q): ACF cuts off after lag q (goes to exactly zero). PACF tails off. AR(p): PACF cuts off after lag p. ACF tails off. ARMA: both tail off.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(p) process, the PACF $\\phi_{hh}$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $\\phi_{hh}$ $=$ 0 for h &gt; p, and $\\phi_{hh}$ $\\neq$ 0 for 1 $\\leq$ h $\\leq$ p','B) $\\phi_{hh}$ equals the ACF $\\rho(h)$ for all h','C) $\\phi_{hh}$ $=$ 0 for h &gt; 2p','D) $\\phi_{hh}$ decays exponentially for all h $\\geq$ 1'],ans:0,exp:'For an AR(p) process, the regression of $X_t$ on $X_{t-1},\\ldots,X_{t-p}$ is exact (no further improvement from adding more lags). Hence the PACF $\\phi_{hh}$ $=$ 0 for all h &gt; p. For h $\\leq$ p, $\\phi_{hh}$ is generally non-zero. This cutoff property is used in model identification: plot the sample PACF and identify the lag beyond which it falls inside the confidence bands. That lag is p. Combined with the MA(q) ACF cutoff rule, we have the complete Box-Jenkins identification procedure. Box-Jenkins ID Table: $AR(p)=[\\mathrm{ACF} tails, \\mathrm{PACF} cuts at p]$; $MA(q)=[\\mathrm{ACF} cuts at q, \\mathrm{PACF} tails]$; $\\mathrm{ARMA}=[both tail]$. Memorise this table!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the Yule-Walker method of estimating AR(p) parameters, the estimators $\\hat{\\phi}$ are obtained by:',opts:['A) Applying the Burg algorithm recursively','B) Using maximum likelihood with a Gaussian assumption','C) Minimising the sum of squared residuals directly','D) Substituting sample ACF values into the Yule-Walker equations and solving the resulting linear system'],ans:3,exp:'The Yule-Walker equations in matrix form are: $\\Gamma$ $\\cdot$ $\\phi$ $=$ $\\gamma$, where $\\Gamma$ is the p $\\times$ p Toeplitz matrix of autocovariances $[\\gamma(i-j)]$, $\\phi$ $=$ $[\\phi_1,\\ldots,\\phi_p]^T$, and $\\gamma$ $=$ $[\\gamma(1),\\ldots,\\gamma(p)]^T$. Replacing population quantities with sample estimates gives: $\\hat{\\phi}$ $=$ $\\hat{\\Gamma}^{-1}$ $\\cdot$ $\\hat{\\gamma}$. These estimators are consistent and asymptotically normal. The resulting fitted values always satisfy the stationarity conditions since $\\hat{\\Gamma}$ is positive definite. Option (A) is the Burg algorithm, which gives better finite-sample properties. Yule-Walker uses sample ACF $\\Rightarrow$ method of moments estimator. Always yields stationary estimates. Less efficient than MLE but computationally simple and reliable.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Conditional least squares estimation of ARMA(p,q) parameters minimises:',opts:['A) The sum of squared differences between sample and theoretical ACF values','B) The spectral density integrated over all frequencies','C) $\\sum_{t=p+1}^{n}$ $\\epsilon_t^2$ where $\\epsilon_t$ are residuals from the ARMA model, conditioning on initial observed values','D) The exact likelihood including initial conditions'],ans:2,exp:'Conditional least squares (CLS) conditions on the first $\\max(p,q)$ observations and recursively computes $\\epsilon_t$ $=$ $X_t$ $-$ $\\phi_1\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $-$ $\\ldots$ $-$ $\\phi_p\\cdot X_{t-p}$ $-$ $\\theta_1\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$ $-$ $\\ldots$ $-$ $\\theta_q\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-q}$. It then minimises $S(\\phi,\\theta)$ $=$ $\\sum_{t=p+q+1}^{n}$ $\\epsilon_t^2$. CLS is computationally simpler than exact MLE but asymptotically equivalent for large n. Option (D) is exact MLE. Option (A) is the minimum distance method using the ACF. CLS vs Exact MLE: Asymptotically equivalent; differ in how they handle initial values. For large n, both give similar estimates. CLS is iterative and non-linear.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) for ARMA(p,q) model selection is defined as:',opts:['A) AIC $=$ $-2\\cdot \\log(L)$ $+$ $2\\cdot (p+q)$','B) AIC $=$ $\\hat{\\sigma}^2$ $\\cdot$ (1 $+$ $2\\cdot (p+q)/n)$','C) AIC $=$ $n\\cdot \\log(\\hat{\\sigma}^2)$ $+$ $2\\cdot (p+q+1)$','D) AIC $=$ $-2\\cdot \\log(L)$ $+$ $(p+q)\\cdot \\log(n)$'],ans:0,exp:'AIC $=$ $-2\\cdot \\log(maximised$ likelihood) $+$ 2k where k $=$ p+q is the number of estimated parameters. It penalises model complexity to prevent overfitting. The model with the smallest AIC is preferred. Note: option (C) is also a valid equivalent form when k $=$ $p+q+1$ (including $\\sigma^2)$. Option (D) is the Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC/SIC), which uses $\\log(n)$ penalty and selects more parsimonious models for large n. For UGC NET, remember: AIC penalty $=$ 2k; BIC penalty $=$ $k\\cdot \\log(n)$. AIC $=$ $-2logL$ $+$ 2k. BIC $=$ $-2logL$ $+$ $k\\cdot \\log(n)$. BIC penalises complexity more (log n &gt; 2 for n &gt; 8). BIC is consistent (selects true order); AIC tends to overfit.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Compared to AIC, the Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC) for ARMA order selection tends to:',opts:['A) Minimise the in-sample prediction error','B) Select the same order as AIC for all sample sizes','C) Select more parsimonious (smaller) models especially for large samples','D) Always select larger model orders'],ans:2,exp:'BIC $=$ $-2\\cdot \\log(L)$ $+$ $k\\cdot \\log(n)$. Since $\\log(n)$ &gt; 2 for n &gt; $e^2$ $\\approx$ 7.4, BIC imposes a heavier penalty per parameter than AIC. This means BIC selects simpler models. Key theoretical properties: (1) BIC is consistent $---$ it selects the true model order with probability 1 as n $\\to$ $\\infty$ (if the true model is in the candidate set). (2) AIC is NOT consistent but is asymptotically efficient $---$ it minimises prediction error. In practice: use AIC for forecasting; BIC for model identification/parsimony. AIC: efficient (good for prediction). BIC: consistent (good for finding true order). When in doubt for UGC NET: BIC selects smaller order, AIC may overfit. $\\log(n)$ vs 2 is the penalty difference.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(1) series $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$, the minimum MSE h-step ahead forecast $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}$ (given all data up to time n) is:',opts:['A) $\\mu$ $+$ $\\phi\\cdot (X_n$ $-$ $\\mu)$ for h $\\geq$ 1 regardless of h','B) $\\phi^h$ $\\cdot$ $X_n$ for all h $\\geq$ 1','C) $\\phi^h$ $\\cdot$ $X_n$ $+$ $(1-\\phi^h)$ $\\cdot$ $\\mu$, where $\\mu$ $=$ $E(X_t)$','D) $\\phi$ $\\cdot$ $X_{n+h-1}$ with a recursively computed error'],ans:2,exp:'For AR(1) with non-zero mean $\\mu$: $(X_t$ $-$ $\\mu)$ $=$ $\\phi\\cdot (X_{t-1}$ $-$ $\\mu)$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$. The MMSE forecast is $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}$ $=$ $E(X_{n+h}|X_n$, $X_{n-1},\\ldots)$ $=$ $\\mu$ $+$ $\\phi^h\\cdot (X_n$ $-$ $\\mu)$. Key features: (1) It reverts toward the mean $\\mu$ exponentially as h increases; (2) As h $\\to$ $\\infty$, $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}$ $\\to$ $\\mu$ (forecast equals unconditional mean for large horizons); (3) The forecast error variance is $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1-\\phi^{2h})/(1-\\phi^2)$, which increases with h and approaches $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$. AR(1) forecast $=$ long-run mean $+$ $\\phi^h\\cdot (current$ deviation from mean). The $\\phi^h$ factor ensures mean-reversion. Long-horizon forecasts collapse to the unconditional mean.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The variance of the $2-step$ ahead forecast error for a stationary AR(1) process $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ is:',opts:['A) $2\\cdot \\sigma^2$','B) $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$','C) $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1+\\phi^2)$','D) $\\sigma^2$'],ans:2,exp:'The h-step ahead forecast error is: $e_{n+h|n}$ $=$ $X_{n+h}$ $-$ $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}$ $=$ $\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}$ $\\phi^j$ $\\cdot$ $\\epsilon_{n+h-j}$. For $h=1$: $\\mathrm{Var}(e_{n+1|n})$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$. For $h=2$: $\\mathrm{Var}(e_{n+2|n})$ $=$ $\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_{n+2}$ $+$ $\\phi\\cdot \\epsilon_{n+1})$ $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1+\\phi^2)$. General formula: $\\mathrm{Var}(e_{n+h|n})$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}$ $\\phi^{2j}$ $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1-\\phi^{2h})/(1-\\phi^2)$. As h $\\to$ $\\infty$, this approaches $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$ $=$ $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$, showing forecast uncertainty converges to unconditional variance. Forecast error variance grows with horizon h. For AR(1): $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1-\\phi^{2h})/(1-\\phi^2)$. This equals $\\sigma^2$ at $h=1$ and $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ as h $\\to$ $\\infty$. Prediction intervals widen with horizon!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an MA(1) process $X_t$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$ $+$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$, the $1-step$ ahead forecast $\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}$ is:',opts:['A) $\\epsilon_{n+1}$','B) $\\theta$ $\\cdot$ $X_n$','C) $\\theta$ $\\cdot$ $\\epsilon_n$ $+$ $\\mu$','D) $\\theta$ $\\cdot$ $\\epsilon_n$'],ans:3,exp:'For MA(1): $X_{n+1}$ $=$ $\\epsilon_{n+1}$ $+$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_n$. Taking conditional expectation given the past (which determines $\\epsilon_n)$: $\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}$ $=$ $E(\\epsilon_{n+1}|past)$ $+$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_n$ $=$ 0 $+$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_n$ $=$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_n$. The innovation $\\epsilon_n$ $=$ $X_n$ $-$ $\\hat{X}_{n|n-1}$ is computed recursively. For h $\\geq$ 2: $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}$ $=$ 0 $=$ $\\mu$ (forecast equals mean) because MA(1) has memory of only 1 period. This is why MA models are limited for long-horizon forecasting. MA(1) $1-step$ forecast $=$ $\\theta$ $\\cdot$ last innovation. MA(1) h-step forecast $=$ 0 for h $\\geq$ 2. Memory of MA(q) is q periods. Beyond q steps, all MA forecasts equal the unconditional mean.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Box-Jenkins identification guidelines suggest an ARMA(p,q) model when the sample ACF and PACF exhibit which pattern?',opts:['A) Both ACF and PACF cut off sharply after the same lag','B) ACF is flat; PACF has a single spike at lag 1','C) ACF cuts off after lag p; PACF cuts off after lag q','D) ACF tails off (decaying exponential/sinusoidal); PACF also tails off'],ans:3,exp:'Box-Jenkins identification: AR(p) $\\Rightarrow$ ACF tails off, PACF cuts off at p. MA(q) $\\Rightarrow$ ACF cuts off at q, PACF tails off. ARMA(p,q) $\\Rightarrow$ both ACF and PACF tail off with mixed patterns. Option (C) has the cuts reversed. Option (A) is impossible for ARMA (if both cut off, it would imply a finite-order MA AND a finite-order AR simultaneously, which is only possible for white noise). In practice, distinguishing ARMA from AR or MA requires comparing AIC/BIC across candidate models. ID Table: [AR(p): PACF cuts, ACF tails] [MA(q): ACF cuts, PACF tails] [ARMA: both tail] [WN: both cut at 0]. Print this in your mind!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The minimum mean squared error (MMSE) predictor of $X_{n+h}$ given the information set $I_n$ $=$ ${X_n, X_{n-1}, \\ldots}$ is:',opts:['A) The last observed value $X_n$','B) $E(X_{n+h}$ $|$ $I_n)$, the conditional expectation','C) $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}$ $=$ $\\phi_1\\cdot X_n$ $+$ $\\ldots$ $+$ $\\phi_h\\cdot X_{n-h+1}$','D) The median of $X_{n+h}$ given $I_n$'],ans:1,exp:'The MMSE forecast minimises $E[(X_{n+h} - g(I_n))^2]$ over all functions g of the information set. By the orthogonality principle (or direct algebra), the minimising g* is the conditional expectation $E(X_{n+h}$ $|$ $I_n)$. For Gaussian processes, this equals the linear projection (best linear predictor). For non-Gaussian processes, the MMSE forecast may be non-linear. Option (D) would be optimal under absolute error loss. The MMSE forecast is the foundation of all optimal time series prediction theory. MMSE $\\Rightarrow$ conditional mean. Median $\\Rightarrow$ minimum absolute error. Mode $\\Rightarrow$ MAP estimate. For Gaussian: all three coincide! For non-Gaussian, MMSE is conditional mean, not median.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The sample PACF at lag 2 for an AR(1) series with $\\phi$ $=$ 0.6 should be approximately:',opts:['A) 0.6','B) $-0.6$','C) 0 (close to zero)','D) 0.36'],ans:2,exp:'For an AR(1) process, the theoretical PACF $\\phi_{hh}$ $=$ $\\phi$ at $h=1$ $(=$ 0.6 here) and $\\phi_{hh}$ $=$ 0 for all h $\\geq$ 2. This is because an AR(1) has order $p=1$, so the PACF cuts off after lag 1. The value 0.36 $=$ $0.6^2$ $=$ $\\rho(2)$ is the ACF at lag 2, not the PACF. In practice, the sample PACF at lag 2 will be close to 0 but not exactly 0 due to sampling variability, and would fall within the $+/-$ $2/\\sqrt{n}$ confidence bounds for a true AR(1) series. PACF of AR(p) is zero beyond lag p. $\\phi_{22}$ $\\approx$ 0 for AR(1). Confusing ACF and PACF values is a common exam trap. $\\rho(2)$ $=$ $\\phi^2$ (ACF) $\\neq$ $\\phi_{22}$ $=$ 0 (PACF).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'When using AIC for ARMA(p,q) order selection, which of the following is true?',opts:['A) AIC and BIC select identical models for all sample sizes','B) AIC always selects the true model order as n $\\to$ $\\infty$','C) Minimising AIC is equivalent to maximising the R-squared of the fitted model','D) AIC may tend to overfit (select orders larger than the true order) even asymptotically'],ans:3,exp:'AIC is not a consistent model selector $---$ even as n $\\to$ $\\infty$, it has a positive probability of selecting an order larger than the true order. Specifically, for AR order selection, AIC overestimates the true order p with probability that does not go to zero as n $\\to$ $\\infty$ (this probability depends on the critical value used). BIC is consistent: it selects the true order with probability 1 asymptotically. Despite this, AIC is preferred for FORECASTING because it minimises asymptotic prediction risk (Kullback-Leibler divergence). For identification, BIC is preferred. AIC: efficient but NOT consistent (may overfit). BIC: consistent but NOT always efficient. Use AIC for forecasting; BIC for order determination. This distinction is frequently tested.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an MA(2) process, the h-step ahead forecast for h &gt; 2 equals:',opts:['A) $\\theta_1\\cdot \\epsilon_{n+2-h}$ $+$ $\\theta_2\\cdot \\epsilon_{n+3-h}$','B) $\\theta_1\\cdot \\epsilon_n$ $+$ $\\theta_2\\cdot \\epsilon_{n-1}$','C) $E(\\epsilon_{n+h}|I_n)$','D) 0 (the unconditional mean, assumed 0)'],ans:3,exp:'For MA(2): $X_t$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$ $+$ $\\theta_1\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\theta_2\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-2}$. The $1-step$ forecast is $\\theta_1\\cdot \\epsilon_n$ $+$ $\\theta_2\\cdot \\epsilon_{n-1}$; $2-step$ is $\\theta_2\\cdot \\epsilon_n$; $3-step$ and beyond is 0, since $X_{n+h}$ $=$ $\\epsilon_{n+h}$ $+$ $\\theta_1\\cdot \\epsilon_{n+h-1}$ $+$ $\\theta_2\\cdot \\epsilon_{n+h-2}$ and all $\\epsilon$ terms are future (unknown) and uncorrelated with the past for h &gt; 2. This is the finite-memory property of MA processes: MA(q) has forecast equal to the mean for horizons beyond q. MA(q) memory $=$ q steps. Beyond q steps, all MA forecasts $=$ unconditional mean (0 if $mean=0)$. AR(p) forecasts revert to mean gradually (exponentially). ARMA(p,q): \'memory\' effectively infinite but reverts to mean.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A $95\\%$ prediction interval for the $1-step$ ahead forecast from ARMA(p,q) is approximately:',opts:['A) $\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}$ $+/-$ 1.645 $\\cdot$ $\\sigma$','B) $\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}$ $+/-$ $\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$','C) $\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}$ $+/-$ 1.96 $\\cdot$ $\\sigma$','D) $\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}$ $+/-$ 2 $\\cdot$ $\\sigma^2$'],ans:2,exp:'For $1-step$ ahead forecast from ARMA, the forecast error $e_{n+1|n}$ $=$ $\\epsilon_{n+1}$ $\\sim$ WN(0, $\\sigma^2)$. Assuming Gaussianity, $\\epsilon_{n+1}$ $\\sim$ N(0, $\\sigma^2)$, so the $95\\%$ prediction interval is $\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}$ $+/-$ $1.96\\cdot \\sigma$. Note: option (B) is the width of a confidence interval for the mean $---$ it shrinks with n. Prediction intervals do NOT shrink with n (they reflect irreducible uncertainty in future realisations). For h-step forecasts, replace $\\sigma$ with $\\sqrt{\\mathrm{Var}(e_{n+h|n})}$. Prediction interval for 1 future value: $+/-$ $1.96\\cdot \\sigma$ (for $95\\%)$. Does NOT shrink with n! Contrast with CI for mean: $+/-$ $1.96\\cdot \\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$ which does shrink.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Durbin-Watson statistic d is used to test for:',opts:['A) First-order autocorrelation in regression residuals','B) Stationarity of the dependent variable','C) Normality of regression residuals','D) Heteroscedasticity in regression residuals'],ans:0,exp:'The Durbin-Watson statistic d $=$ $\\sum_{t=2}^n$ $(e_t$ $-$ $e_{t-1})^2$ $/$ $\\sum_{t=1}^n$ $e_t^2$ $\\approx$ $2\\cdot (1-r(1))$, where r(1) is the sample first-order autocorrelation of residuals. Values: d $\\approx$ 2 indicates no autocorrelation (H0); d &lt; 2 suggests positive autocorrelation; d &gt; 2 suggests negative autocorrelation. The test uses dL and dU bounds (from DW tables) with an inconclusive region. Limitations: tests only AR(1) pattern; not appropriate for dynamic models with lagged dependent variables. DW $\\approx$ $2(1-\\rho)$. $\\mathrm{DW}=2$ means no autocorrelation. DW&lt;2 means positive autocorrelation. DW&gt;2 means negative. Rule: if d &lt; dL: reject H0 (positive AC); d &gt; $4-dL$: reject H0 (negative AC).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2): $X_t$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$ $+$ $\\theta_1\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\theta_2\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-2}$ with WN variance $\\sigma^2$, the ACVF at lag 2 is:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $\\theta_2$','B) $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $(\\theta_1\\cdot \\theta_2$ $+$ $\\theta_2^2)$','C) $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $(\\theta_1$ $+$ $\\theta_2)$','D) $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $\\theta_1\\cdot \\theta_2$'],ans:0,exp:'For MA(2): $\\gamma(h)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $\\sum_j$ $\\theta_j\\cdot \\theta_{j+h}$ for $|h|$ $\\leq$ 2, zero otherwise. At $h=2$: $\\gamma(2)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $\\theta_0\\cdot \\theta_2$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $\\theta_2$ (since $\\theta_0=1)$. At $h=1$: $\\gamma(1)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot (\\theta_0\\cdot \\theta_1$ $+$ $\\theta_1\\cdot \\theta_2)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot (\\theta_1+\\theta_1\\cdot \\theta_2)$. At $h=0$: $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2)$. Note the formula: $\\gamma(h)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot \\sum_{j=0}^{q-h}$ $\\theta_j\\cdot \\theta_{j+h}$ for 0 $\\leq$ h $\\leq$ q. ACVF of MA(q): $\\gamma(h)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $\\sum_{j=0}^{q-h}$ $\\theta_j\\cdot \\theta_{j+h}$ for $|h|$ $\\leq$ q. Think of it as the \'inner product\' of the $\\theta$ sequence with its lagged version. $\\gamma(q)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot \\theta_q$ always.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(2) with $\\phi_1$ $=$ 0.5, $\\phi_2$ $=$ 0.3, the Yule-Walker equation gives $\\rho(1)$ as:',opts:['A) 0.5 $/$ 0.7','B) 0.5 $/$ (1 $-$ 0.3)','C) $0.5/(1-0.3^2)$','D) (0.5 $+$ $0.3\\cdot 0.5)$ $/$ $(1-0.3)$ $=$ $0.65/0.7$'],ans:3,exp:'From Yule-Walker at lag 1: $\\rho(1)$ $=$ $\\phi_1$ $+$ $\\phi_2\\cdot \\rho(1)$. Solving: $\\rho(1)\\cdot (1-\\phi_2)$ $=$ $\\phi_1$ $\\Rightarrow$ $\\rho(1)$ $=$ $\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)$ $=$ $0.5/0.7$ $\\approx$ 0.714. Note: option (D) gives the same numerical answer $0.65/0.7$ $---$ this is incorrect derivation. The correct formula is $\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)$. Then from lag 2 equation: $\\rho(2)$ $=$ $\\phi_1\\cdot \\rho(1)$ $+$ $\\phi_2$ $=$ $0.5\\cdot (5/7)$ $+$ 0.3 $=$ 0.357 $+$ 0.3 $=$ 0.657. These values can then be used to solve for $\\phi_1$ and $\\phi_2$ via Yule-Walker. Yule-Walker lag 1 for AR(2): $\\rho(1)$ $=$ $\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)$. Always derive systematically: $h=1$ equation, isolate $\\rho(1)$. Then use $\\rho(1)$ in $h=2$ equation to get $\\rho(2)$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density function $f(\\lambda)$ of a stationary time series is defined as:',opts:['A) $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\gamma(0)$ $/$ $(2\\cdot \\pi)$ for all $\\lambda$','B) The Fourier transform of the ACF: $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}$ $\\gamma(h)$ $\\cdot$ $e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda\\cdot h}$','C) The squared modulus of the discrete Fourier transform of ${X_t}$','D) The Fourier transform of the original series $X_t$'],ans:1,exp:'The spectral density is the Fourier transform of the ACVF (with a $(1/2\\pi)$ normalisation): $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $(1/2\\pi)$ $\\cdot$ $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}$ $\\gamma(h)$ $\\cdot$ $e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda\\cdot h}$ for $\\lambda$ in $[-\\pi, \\pi]$. It represents how the variance of the series is distributed across frequencies. The inverse relationship (Wiener-Khinchin theorem) is: $\\gamma(h)$ $=$ $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}$ $e^{i\\cdot \\lambda\\cdot h}$ $\\cdot$ $f(\\lambda)$ $d(\\lambda)$. Setting $h=0$: $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\int$ $f(\\lambda)$ $d(\\lambda)$ $=$ total variance. Spectral density $=$ Fourier transform of ACVF. Total area under $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$. High f at low frequencies $\\Rightarrow$ slowly varying series. High f at high frequencies $\\Rightarrow$ rapidly oscillating series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density of white noise WN(0, $\\sigma^2)$ is:',opts:['A) $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $\\delta(\\lambda)$ (Dirac $\\delta$ at zero)','B) $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ 0 for all $\\lambda$ $\\neq$ 0','C) $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $|\\lambda|$ for $\\lambda$ in $[-\\pi, \\pi]$','D) $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2/(2\\cdot \\pi)$ for all $\\lambda$ in $[-\\pi, \\pi]$'],ans:3,exp:'For white noise, $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ and $\\gamma(h)$ $=$ 0 for h $\\neq$ 0. Therefore $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $(1/2\\pi)\\cdot \\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2/(2\\pi)$. This is a FLAT (constant) spectral density across all frequencies $[-\\pi, \\pi]$. This is the origin of the term \'white noise\' $---$ by analogy with white light which contains all frequencies equally. Verification: $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}$ $\\sigma^2/(2\\pi)$ $d(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2/(2\\pi)\\cdot 2\\pi$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $=$ $\\gamma(0)$. Option (A) is for a deterministic sinusoid. White noise $=$ flat spectrum $=$ \'white light\' analogy. All frequencies have equal energy. Any departure from flatness indicates predictable structure in the series!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density of a stationary AR(1) process $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ is:',opts:['A) $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $/$ $(2\\cdot \\pi$ $\\cdot$ $|1$ $-$ $\\phi\\cdot e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda}|^2)$','B) $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $(1-\\phi^2)$ $/$ $(2\\cdot \\pi)$','C) $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $/$ $(2\\cdot \\pi)$ for all $\\lambda$','D) $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $/$ $(2\\cdot \\pi$ $\\cdot$ $(1-\\phi^2))$'],ans:0,exp:'For AR(1), the spectral density is $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $/$ $[2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot |\\phi(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|^2]$ where $\\phi(z)$ $=$ 1 $-$ $\\phi\\cdot z$. Expanding: $|1$ $-$ $\\phi\\cdot e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda}|^2$ $=$ 1 $-$ $2\\cdot \\phi\\cdot \\cos(\\lambda)$ $+$ $\\phi^2$. For positive $\\phi$, the spectrum peaks at $\\lambda$ $=$ 0 (low frequencies dominate $\\Rightarrow$ smooth series). For negative $\\phi$, it peaks at $\\lambda$ $=$ $\\pi$ (high frequencies $\\Rightarrow$ oscillatory series). Option (D) is the flat value $\\sigma^2/(2\\pi\\cdot (1-\\phi^2))$ which would imply constant spectrum; the AR(1) spectrum is not flat. AR(1) spectrum: peaked at frequency 0 for $\\phi$ &gt; 0 (smooth trend-like behaviour); peaked at $\\pi$ for $\\phi$ &lt; 0 (high-frequency oscillations). Spectral shape reflects dominant cyclical behaviour.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density of the MA(1) process $X_t$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$ $+$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$ is:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $(1+\\theta^2-2\\cdot \\theta\\cdot \\cos(\\lambda))$ $/$ $(2\\cdot \\pi)$','B) $\\sigma^2$ $/$ $[2\\cdot \\pi\\cdot (1+\\theta^2)]$','C) $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $|\\theta|^2$ $/$ $(2\\cdot \\pi)$','D) $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $(1+\\theta^2+2\\cdot \\theta\\cdot \\cos(\\lambda))$ $/$ $(2\\cdot \\pi)$'],ans:3,exp:'For MA(1): $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $(\\sigma^2/2\\pi)\\cdot |1+\\theta\\cdot e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda}|^2$. Expanding: $|1+\\theta\\cdot e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda}|^2$ $=$ $(1+\\theta\\cdot cos$ $\\lambda)^2$ $+$ $(\\theta\\cdot sin$ $\\lambda)^2$ $=$ 1 $+$ $2\\cdot \\theta\\cdot \\cos(\\lambda)$ $+$ $\\theta^2$. So $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1+\\theta^2+2\\cdot \\theta\\cdot cos$ $\\lambda)/(2\\pi)$. For positive $\\theta$: spectrum peaks at $\\lambda=0$ (smooth); for negative $\\theta$: peaks at $\\lambda=\\pi$ (oscillatory). General rule: spectral density of ARMA(p,q) $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot |\\theta(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|^2$ $/$ $[2\\cdot \\pi\\cdot |\\phi(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|^2]$. MA spectral density: $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $|\\theta(\\exp(-i\\cdot \\lambda))|^2$ $/$ $(2\\cdot \\pi)$. AR spectral density: $\\sigma^2$ $/$ $[2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot |\\phi(\\exp(-i\\cdot \\lambda))|^2]$. ARMA: ratio of the two!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The periodogram $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda_j)$ at Fourier frequency $\\lambda_j$ $=$ $2\\cdot \\pi\\cdot j/n$ is defined as:',opts:['A) $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda_j)$ $=$ $(1/n)$ $\\cdot$ $|\\sum_t$ $X_t$ $\\cdot$ $e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda_j\\cdot t}|^2$','B) $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda_j)$ $=$ n $\\cdot$ $|d(\\lambda_j)|^2$ where d is the discrete Fourier transform of ${X_t}$','C) $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda_j)$ $=$ $2\\cdot \\pi$ $\\cdot$ $\\hat{f}(\\lambda_j)$ the smoothed spectral estimate','D) $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda_j)$ $=$ $\\sum_h$ $\\hat{\\gamma}(h)$ $\\cdot$ $e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda_j\\cdot h}$'],ans:0,exp:'The periodogram is $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda_j)$ $=$ $(1/n)|\\sum_{t=1}^n$ $X_t$ $\\cdot$ $e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda_j\\cdot t}|^2$ at Fourier frequencies $\\lambda_j$ $=$ $2\\cdot \\pi\\cdot j/n$ for $j=0,1,\\ldots,n-1$. It is an asymptotically unbiased but INCONSISTENT estimator of the spectral density $---$ its variance does not decrease with n! This is because $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda_j)$ at different frequencies are approximately $\\chi^2(2)$ distributed and approximately independent, giving noise that does not smooth out. Consistent estimation requires smoothing the periodogram (kernel smoothing, Parzen, Bartlett windows). Periodogram is INCONSISTENT for spectral density (variance stays constant as n grows). To get consistent estimates, smooth the periodogram using spectral windows. Parseval\'s theorem: $\\sum_j$ $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda_j)$ $=$ sample variance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A random walk is defined as $X_t$ $=$ $X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ where $\\epsilon_t$ $\\sim$ $\\mathrm{WN}(0,\\sigma^2)$. Starting from $X_0$ $=$ 0, the variance $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ equals:',opts:['A) t $\\cdot$ $\\sigma^2$','B) $\\sigma^2$','C) $\\sigma^2/(1-1^2)$ which is undefined','D) $\\sigma^2/t$'],ans:0,exp:'For random walk: $X_t$ $=$ $\\sum_{j=1}^t$ $\\epsilon_j$ (by telescoping). Since $\\epsilon_j$ are i.i.d. $\\mathrm{WN}(0,\\sigma^2)$: $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ $=$ $t\\cdot \\sigma^2$. This grows linearly with t $---$ hence non-stationarity! The random walk is AR(1) with $\\phi=1$ (unit root). $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ $\\to$ $\\infty$ as t $\\to$ $\\infty$, violating the stationarity requirement of constant variance. The mean $E(X_t)$ $=$ 0 (constant), but the non-constant variance is why it is non-stationary. This is also why one cannot apply Yule-Walker or standard ARMA to a random walk directly. Random walk: Var grows as $t\\cdot \\sigma^2$. Mean is constant but variance is not $\\Rightarrow$ non-stationary. This motivates differencing: $DeltaX_t$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$ $\\sim$ WN. Random walk is ARIMA(0,1,0).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If ${X_t}$ is a random walk $(X_t$ $=$ $X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t)$, then the first-differenced series $\\Delta\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $X_t$ $-$ $X_{t-1}$ is:',opts:['A) A non-stationary process','B) An AR(1) process with $\\phi$ $=$ 1','C) White noise WN(0, $\\sigma^2)$','D) An MA(1) process with $\\theta$ $=$ $-1$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\Delta\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $X_t$ $-$ $X_{t-1}$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$, which is simply the original white noise innovation. So differencing removes the unit root and yields a stationary process. This is the principle behind the I(d) (integrated of order d) class of processes: if $X_t$ needs to be differenced d times to become stationary, it is I(d). The random walk is I(1), and ARIMA(0,1,0) represents this. After differencing, standard ARMA modelling can be applied to the stationary $\\Delta\\cdot X_t$. Random walk is I(1): one difference makes it stationary. I(d) processes need d differences. ARIMA(p,d,q) $=$ AR(p) and MA(q) applied to the d-th difference of $X_t$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The ARIMA(p,d,q) model is defined by the equation:',opts:['A) $\\phi(B)\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $\\theta(B)\\cdot (1-B)^d$ $\\cdot$ $\\epsilon_t$','B) $(1-B)^d$ $\\cdot$ $X_t$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$ regardless of p and q','C) $\\phi(B)\\cdot (1-B)^d$ $\\cdot$ $X_t$ $=$ $\\theta(B)\\cdot \\epsilon_t$ where B is the backshift operator','D) $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi_1\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\ldots$ $+$ $\\phi_p\\cdot X_{t-p}$ $+$ $(1-B)^d$ $\\cdot$ $\\epsilon_t$'],ans:2,exp:'ARIMA(p,d,q): $\\phi(B)\\cdot (1-B)^d\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $\\theta(B)\\cdot \\epsilon_t$ where: $\\phi(B)=1-\\phi_1\\cdot B-\\ldots-\\phi_p\\cdot B^p$ (AR polynomial), $(1-B)^d$ is the d-th order differencing operator, $\\theta(B)=1+\\theta_1\\cdot B+\\ldots+\\theta_q\\cdot B^q$ (MA polynomial). For stationarity/invertibility, all roots of $\\phi(z)$ and $\\theta(z)$ must be outside the unit circle (roots of $(1-B)^d$ are ON the unit circle $---$ the non-stationary part). ARIMA(0,1,0) $=$ random walk; ARIMA(1,1,0) $=$ differenced AR(1); ARIMA(0,1,1) $=$ exponential smoothing. ARIMA: AR(p) applied to d-th difference of $X_t$. $\\phi(B)\\cdot (1-B)^d\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $\\theta(B)\\cdot \\epsilon_t$. The $(1-B)^d$ operator introduces d unit roots. After differencing d times, standard ARMA(p,q) applies.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF) test for unit roots in a time series tests the null hypothesis:',opts:['A) H0: the series is stationary against H1: the series has a unit root','B) H0: the differenced series is white noise against H1: it is not','C) H0: the series follows ARIMA(0,1,0) exactly','D) H0: the series has a unit root (is non-stationary) against H1: the series is stationary'],ans:3,exp:'The ADF test regresses $\\Delta\\cdot X_t$ on $X_{t-1}$ and lagged differences, testing H0: $\\delta$ $=$ 0 (unit root) vs H1: $\\delta$ &lt; 0 (stationary). The test statistic is t-ratio of $\\hat{\\delta}$, which does NOT follow a standard t-distribution under H0 $---$ it follows a Dickey-Fuller distribution with more negative critical values. Key features: (1) Failure to reject H0 indicates unit root (non-stationarity); (2) Augmentation with lagged differences accounts for serial correlation in errors; (3) One must include drift and/or trend terms based on visual inspection of the data. ADF H0 $=$ unit root. Small (negative) t-statistic $\\Rightarrow$ reject H0 $\\Rightarrow$ stationary. Critical values are more negative than standard t. DF distribution is non-standard. Reject H0 $\\Rightarrow$ $d=0$ is sufficient; fail to reject $\\Rightarrow$ need to difference.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The random walk with drift $X_t$ $=$ $\\mu$ $+$ $X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ has which behaviour over time?',opts:['A) It is equivalent to a stationary AR(1) with $\\phi$ $=$ 1 $-$ $\\mu$','B) Mean is zero; variance grows as $t\\cdot \\sigma^2$','C) Mean and variance both constant (stationary)','D) Mean grows linearly as $\\mu\\cdot t$; variance grows as $t\\cdot \\sigma^2$ (non-stationary with deterministic trend)'],ans:3,exp:'For random walk with drift: $X_t$ $=$ $\\mu\\cdot t$ $+$ $\\sum_{j=1}^t$ $\\epsilon_j$ (from $X_0=0)$. Thus $E(X_t)$ $=$ $\\mu\\cdot t$ (grows linearly) and $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ $=$ $t\\cdot \\sigma^2$. Both the mean and variance are non-constant $\\Rightarrow$ non-stationary. The drift $\\mu$ adds a deterministic linear trend. Differencing gives: $\\Delta\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $\\mu$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$, a stationary process with mean $\\mu$ and WN innovations. This represents a stochastic trend, distinct from a deterministic trend model where $X_t$ $=$ $\\mu\\cdot t$ $+$ stationary component. Random walk with drift $=$ stochastic trend $+$ drift. Differencing yields stationary. Deterministic trend model: $X_t$ $=$ $a+b\\cdot t+e_t$ $---$ removing trend by detrending makes stationary. Two different sources of non-stationarity!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Two non-stationary I(1) time series $X_t$ and $Y_t$ are said to be cointegrated if:',opts:['A) They have identical spectral densities','B) There exists a linear combination $a\\cdot X_t$ $+$ $b\\cdot Y_t$ that is I(0) (stationary)','C) They can be modelled by the same ARIMA(p,d,q) specification','D) Their individual differences $\\Delta\\cdot X_t$ and $\\Delta\\cdot Y_t$ are correlated'],ans:1,exp:'Cointegration (Engle-Granger 1987, Nobel Prize 2003) captures the idea of long-run equilibrium between non-stationary series. If $X_t$ $\\sim$ I(1) and $Y_t$ $\\sim$ I(1) but $a\\cdot X_t$ $+$ $b\\cdot Y_t$ $\\sim$ I(0) for some vector (a,b) $\\neq$ (0,0), they are cointegrated. The stationary linear combination is the \'equilibrium error\'. Cointegration implies a Error Correction Model (ECM) representation, meaning the series share a common stochastic trend and do not drift apart permanently. Classic example: prices in different markets, interest rates. Cointegration $=$ I(1) series with stationary linear combination. They share a common stochastic trend. Granger Representation Theorem: cointegration &lt; $\\Rightarrow$ Error Correction Model (ECM).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A deterministic trend model $X_t$ $=$ $\\beta_0$ $+$ $\\beta_1\\cdot t$ $+$ $u_t$ $(u_t$ stationary) differs from a stochastic trend (random walk with drift) in that:',opts:['A) The deterministic trend should be removed by detrending (regression on t), while the stochastic trend by differencing','B) Deterministic trend models always have unit roots','C) Only stochastic trends cause non-stationarity','D) Both are treated identically by differencing'],ans:0,exp:'Deterministic trend: $X_t$ $=$ $\\beta_0$ $+$ $\\beta_1\\cdot t$ $+$ $u_t$. Detrending by regressing on t and taking residuals gives a stationary $u_t$. Differencing also works but introduces an MA unit root (over-differencing). Stochastic trend (random walk): $X_t$ $=$ $X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$. Differencing gives stationary $\\Delta\\cdot X_t$. Detrending does NOT fully remove stochastic trend $---$ the residuals still have a unit root. Using the wrong treatment leads to spurious regression or model misspecification. ADF and KPSS tests help distinguish the two types. Deterministic trend: detrend. Stochastic trend: difference. Wrong treatment $\\Rightarrow$ spurious results. KPSS tests H0: stationarity (opposite of ADF). Use both to distinguish trend types.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Simple exponential smoothing (SES) with smoothing parameter $\\alpha$ produces forecasts that are equivalent to which ARIMA model?',opts:['A) ARIMA(1,0,0)','B) ARIMA(0,1,1) with $\\theta$ $=$ $1-\\alpha$','C) ARIMA(0,0,1)','D) ARIMA(1,1,0)'],ans:1,exp:'SES forecast: $\\hat{X}_{t+1|t}$ $=$ $\\alpha\\cdot X_t$ $+$ $(1-\\alpha)\\cdot \\hat{X}_{t|t-1}$ $=$ $\\alpha\\cdot \\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}(1-\\alpha)^j$ $\\cdot$ $X_{t-j}$. This is equivalent to the ARIMA(0,1,1) model: $\\Delta\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$ $-$ $(1-\\alpha)\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$, i.e., $\\theta$ $=$ $-(1-\\alpha)$ in the MA polynomial. Optimal $\\alpha$ is determined by MLE. This connection justifies SES as a statistically principled procedure and allows confidence interval construction. Holt\'s method (trend) $\\sim$ ARIMA(0,2,2); Holt-Winters $\\sim$ ARIMA with seasonal structure. SES $=$ ARIMA(0,1,1). Holt\'s linear $=$ ARIMA(0,2,2). Smooth: $\\alpha$ close to 0 (long memory). Reactive: $\\alpha$ close to 1 (short memory). $\\theta$ $=$ $1-\\alpha$ in the MA polynomial.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The backshift (lag) operator B is defined by $B\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $X_{t-1}$. Which of the following is the representation of the first difference operator?',opts:['A) $\\Delta$ $=$ 1 $+$ B','B) $\\Delta$ $=$ $B^{-1}$','C) $\\Delta$ $=$ 1 $-$ B','D) $\\Delta$ $=$ $(1-B)^{-1}$'],ans:2,exp:'Since $B\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $X_{t-1}$, we have $(1-B)\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $X_t$ $-$ $X_{t-1}$ $=$ $\\Delta\\cdot X_t$. Therefore $\\Delta$ $=$ $1-B$. Second difference: $\\Delta^2$ $=$ $(1-B)^2$ $=$ $1-2B+B^2$, giving $\\Delta^2\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $X_t$ $-$ $2\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $X_{t-2}$. The seasonal differencing operator at period s is $(1-B^s)$. In ARIMA notation, $(1-B)^d$ represents d-fold differencing. Polynomial manipulation in B is used extensively in ARMA algebra: AR(p) polynomial $\\phi(B)$ $=$ $1-\\phi_1\\cdot B-\\ldots-\\phi_p\\cdot B^p$. B $=$ backshift (lag). $\\Delta$ $=$ $(1-B)$ $=$ first difference. $(1-B^s)$ $=$ seasonal difference (period s). $(1-B)^d$ $=$ d-th difference. Memorise: $B^k\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $X_{t-k}$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A seasonal ARIMA model $\\mathrm{SARIMA}(p,d,q)(P,D,Q)_s$ is appropriate when:',opts:['A) The ACF and PACF show significant spikes at seasonal lags (h $=$ s, 2s, $3s,\\ldots)$ in addition to any non-seasonal structure','B) The series has a deterministic linear trend','C) The ACF decays exponentially with no seasonal pattern','D) The residuals from a standard ARIMA model are white noise'],ans:0,exp:'$\\mathrm{SARIMA}(p,d,q)(P,D,Q)_s$ models series with both non-seasonal and seasonal structure. The model is: $\\phi(B)\\cdot \\Phi(B^s)\\cdot (1-B)^d\\cdot (1-B^s)^D\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $\\theta(B)\\cdot \\Theta(B^s)\\cdot \\epsilon_t$. Identification: seasonal AR $\\Rightarrow$ PACF spikes at $s,2s,\\ldots$; seasonal MA $\\Rightarrow$ ACF spikes at $s,2s,\\ldots$ The seasonal differencing operator $(1-B^s)$ removes seasonal unit roots. Example: monthly airline data might use $\\mathrm{SARIMA}(0,1,1)(0,1,1)_{12}$ (\'airline model\'). If seasonal ACF/PACF show no structure, standard ARIMA suffices. SARIMA identification: look for spikes at seasonal lags in ACF/PACF. Seasonal MA $\\Rightarrow$ ACF cuts at s. Seasonal AR $\\Rightarrow$ PACF cuts at s. The famous airline model: $\\mathrm{SARIMA}(0,1,1)(0,1,1)_{12}$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral representation theorem states that any zero-mean stationary process ${X_t}$ can be written as:',opts:['A) $X_t$ $=$ $\\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}$ $[A_j\\cdot \\cos(\\lambda_j\\cdot t) + B_j\\cdot \\sin(\\lambda_j\\cdot t)]$ for deterministic amplitudes','B) $X_t$ $=$ $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}$ $\\gamma(h)$ $\\cdot$ $e^{i\\cdot \\lambda\\cdot t}$','C) $X_t$ $=$ $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}$ $e^{i\\cdot \\lambda\\cdot t}$ $dZ(\\lambda)$, where Z is a process with orthogonal increments','D) $X_t$ $=$ $\\sum_{j=1}^n$ $e^{i\\cdot \\lambda_j\\cdot t}$ $\\cdot$ $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda_j)^{1/2}$'],ans:2,exp:'The spectral representation (Cramer, 1942) states $X_t$ $=$ $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}$ $e^{i\\cdot \\lambda\\cdot t}$ $dZ(\\lambda)$ where $Z(\\lambda)$ is a process with orthogonal increments: $E|dZ(\\lambda)|^2$ $=$ $dF(\\lambda)$ (spectral distribution function). This shows the series as a superposition of complex exponentials (sinusoids) at all frequencies, with random amplitudes. The spectral density $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $dF(\\lambda)/d(\\lambda)$ where it exists. This representation provides the mathematical foundation for frequency-domain analysis of stationary time series. Spectral representation: $X_t$ $=$ $\\int$ $e^{i\\cdot \\lambda\\cdot t}$ $dZ(\\lambda)$. Think of it as decomposing the series into sinusoidal components at each frequency, each with random amplitude $dZ(\\lambda)$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Ljung-Box Q-statistic for testing overall significance of autocorrelations up to lag m is:',opts:['A) Q $=$ (n-m) $\\cdot$ $\\sum_{h=1}^{m}$ $r(h)^2$','B) Q $=$ $\\sum_{h=1}^{m}$ $[r(h) / \\sqrt{1/n}]^2$','C) Q $=$ $n\\cdot (n+2)$ $\\cdot$ $\\sum_{h=1}^{m}$ $r(h)^2$ $/$ (n-h)','D) Q $=$ n $\\cdot$ $\\sum_{h=1}^{m}$ $r(h)^2$'],ans:2,exp:'The Ljung-Box statistic Q $=$ $n\\cdot (n+2)\\cdot \\sum_{h=1}^m$ $[r(h)^2/(n-h)]$ is an improvement over the Box-Pierce Q $=$ $n\\cdot \\sum$ $r(h)^2$. Under H0 (white noise), Q $\\sim$ $\\chi^2(m)$ approximately. For residuals from an ARMA(p,q) model, the degrees of freedom become m-p-q. Option (B) gives the Box-Pierce statistic (without the $(n+2)/(n-h)$ correction). The Ljung-Box has better finite-sample size properties. Reject H0 if Q &gt; $\\chi^2_{m-p-q, \\alpha}$ $---$ indicates inadequate model fit. Ljung-Box Q $\\sim$ $\\chi^2(m)$ under H0. For ARMA(p,q) residuals: compare to $\\chi^2(m-p-q)$. Reject $\\Rightarrow$ residuals not white noise $\\Rightarrow$ model inadequate. Usually tested at $m=10,15,20$ lags.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the $MA(\\infty)$ representation $X_t$ $=$ $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}$ $\\psi_j$ $\\cdot$ $\\epsilon_{t-j}$, the h-step ahead forecast error variance is:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}$ $\\psi_j^2$','B) $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $\\sum_{j=h}^{\\infty}$ $\\psi_j^2$','C) $\\sigma^2$ $/$ $(\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}$ $\\psi_j^2)$','D) $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $\\psi_h^2$'],ans:0,exp:'The h-step ahead forecast error is $e_{n+h|n}$ $=$ $X_{n+h}$ $-$ $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}$ $=$ $\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}$ $\\psi_j\\cdot \\epsilon_{n+h-j}$ (only the future innovations). Therefore $\\mathrm{Var}(e_{n+h|n})$ $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot \\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}$ $\\psi_j^2$. For $h=1$: variance $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot \\psi_0^2$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ (since $\\psi_0=1)$. As h $\\to$ $\\infty$: variance $\\to$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot \\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}$ $\\psi_j^2$ $=$ $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$. This formula shows that forecast uncertainty grows from $\\sigma^2$ $(1-step)$ to the full unconditional variance as the forecast horizon extends. h-step error variance $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}$ $\\psi_j^2$. Involves only the FIRST h $\\psi$ coefficients. The remaining $\\psi_j^2$ for j $\\geq$ h contribute to the uncertainty BEYOND the forecast horizon.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An ARMA(p,q) process $X_t$ is causal (or purely non-deterministic) if:',opts:['A) All roots of the AR polynomial $\\phi(z)$ lie INSIDE the unit circle','B) All roots of the MA polynomial $\\theta(z)$ lie outside the unit circle','C) All roots of the AR polynomial $\\phi(z)$ lie OUTSIDE the unit circle','D) The AR polynomial has no real roots'],ans:2,exp:'A causal (or non-anticipating) ARMA process can be expressed as a one-sided $MA(\\infty)$: $X_t$ $=$ $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}$ $\\psi_j\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-j}$. This is equivalent to requiring all roots of $\\phi(z)$ $=$ 0 to satisfy $|z|$ &gt; 1 (outside unit circle). This is exactly the stationarity condition for the AR part. Causality ensures $X_t$ depends only on PAST and PRESENT innovations, not future ones. Option (B) is the invertibility condition (not causality). Note: causality $=$ stationarity for ARMA processes. Causal $=$ stationary AR part: roots of $\\phi(z)$ outside unit circle. Invertible $=$ stable MA part: roots of $\\theta(z)$ outside unit circle. Both needed for standard inference!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARMA(p,q) model $\\phi(B)\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $\\theta(B)\\cdot \\epsilon_t$, the spectral density is:',opts:['A) $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $(\\sigma^2/n)$ $\\cdot$ $\\sum_t$ $X_t^2$ $\\cdot$ $e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda\\cdot t}$','B) $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$ $\\cdot$ $|\\phi(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|$ $\\cdot$ $|\\theta(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|$','C) $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2/(2\\cdot \\pi)$ $\\cdot$ $|\\theta(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|^2$ $/$ $|\\phi(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|^2$','D) $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2/(2\\cdot \\pi)$ $\\cdot$ $|\\phi(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|^2$ $/$ $|\\theta(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|^2$'],ans:2,exp:'For ARMA: $\\phi(B)\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $\\theta(B)\\cdot \\epsilon_t$. Writing $X_t$ $=$ $[\\theta(B)/\\phi(B)]\\cdot \\epsilon_t$ $=$ $\\psi(B)\\cdot \\epsilon_t$, the spectral density is $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $(\\sigma^2/2\\pi)\\cdot |\\psi(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|^2$. Since $\\psi(z)$ $=$ $\\theta(z)/\\phi(z)$, we get $f(\\lambda)$ $=$ $(\\sigma^2/2\\pi)\\cdot |\\theta(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|^2/|\\phi(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|^2$. This is the KEY formula. MA polynomial inflates the spectrum; AR polynomial deflates it. Spectral peaks at frequencies where $|\\phi(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|$ is small (near-unit roots in AR polynomial). ARMA spectral density $=$ WN spectrum $\\cdot$ $|\\theta|^2$ $/$ $|\\phi|^2$. Think: MA amplifies certain frequencies, AR suppresses them. Peaks where AR polynomial is near-zero (resonant frequencies).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a random walk $X_t$ $=$ $X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$, the sample ACF r(h) at any fixed lag h converges to:',opts:['A) 0 as the sample size n increases','B) $\\phi^h$ $=$ $1^h$ $=$ 1 for all h','C) A random variable that does not converge to a fixed limit','D) 1 as the sample size n increases'],ans:3,exp:'For a random walk (I(1) process), the sample ACF r(h) $\\to$ $_P$ 1 for any fixed lag h as n $\\to$ $\\infty$. This is because $X_t$ and $X_{t+h}$ are nearly perfectly correlated for large n when there is a common stochastic trend. A sample ACF that is persistently high (close to 1 for many lags) and decays very slowly is the classical signature of non-stationarity/unit root. This visual pattern in the correlogram is the first indication that differencing may be needed before fitting an ARMA model. Slowly decaying ACF (close to 1 for many lags) $\\Rightarrow$ strong evidence of unit root $/$ non-stationarity. The correlogram of a random walk looks like a slowly declining straight line near 1.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'After fitting an ARMA(p,q) model to a time series, a well-specified model should have residuals that:',opts:['A) Show significant spikes in the PACF at the model order p','B) Follow an AR(1) process','C) Have the same ACF as the original series','D) Are uncorrelated (white noise) $---$ ACF falls within the confidence bands for all lags'],ans:3,exp:'Good model diagnostics require: (1) Residual ACF shows no significant spikes (all within $+/-$ $2/\\sqrt{n}$ bounds); (2) Ljung-Box Q-test fails to reject white noise; (3) Residuals are approximately normal (QQ-plot, Jarque-Bera test); (4) Residual variance is constant (no ARCH effects). If residual ACF shows significant autocorrelation, the model has not captured all the dependence structure and should be re-specified by increasing p, q, or both. The fitted model is only adequate when residuals are indistinguishable from white noise. Residual $=$ what the model didn\'t capture. White noise residuals $\\Rightarrow$ model captured all autocorrelation structure. Systematic patterns in residuals $\\Rightarrow$ model inadequate.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Overfitting an ARMA model (using too many parameters) leads to which problem?',opts:['A) Non-stationary residuals','B) Smaller in-sample residuals but larger out-of-sample forecast errors (reduced generalisability)','C) The AIC always increases when overfitting','D) Biased parameter estimates'],ans:1,exp:'Overfitting: adding unnecessary parameters reduces in-sample residual variance (always!) but increases variance of parameter estimates, leading to poor out-of-sample performance. This is the classic bias-variance trade-off. AIC (option D) may actually DECREASE with overfitting until the penalty term outweighs the log-likelihood improvement $---$ hence AIC\'s tendency to overfit noted earlier. BIC\'s larger penalty better guards against this. Cross-validation and out-of-sample tests are best for evaluating forecast performance. Overfitting: good in-sample fit, poor out-of-sample. Parsimony principle: prefer simple models. AIC and BIC balance fit vs complexity. Out-of-sample testing is the gold standard for forecast model evaluation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Parseval\'s theorem for a stationary time series relates:',opts:['A) The sum of squared observations to the spectral density $\\int$','B) The total variance $\\gamma(0)$ to the integral of the spectral density: $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}$ $f(\\lambda)$ $d(\\lambda)$','C) The number of significant ACF spikes to the model order','D) The periodogram to the PACF values'],ans:1,exp:'Parseval\'s theorem for time series: the total variance of the series equals the integral of its spectral density over all frequencies. Specifically: $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ $=$ $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}$ $f(\\lambda)$ $d(\\lambda)$. More generally: $\\gamma(h)$ $=$ $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}$ $e^{i\\cdot \\lambda\\cdot h}$ $f(\\lambda)$ $d(\\lambda)$. This is the inversion formula showing that spectral density and ACVF are a Fourier transform pair (Wiener-Khinchin theorem). The spectral density distributes the total variance across frequencies, showing which frequencies contribute most to the overall variability. Parseval: $\\gamma(0)$ $=$ integral of spectral density $=$ total area under spectrum. The spectrum shows how variance is distributed across frequencies. High-frequency peak $\\Rightarrow$ fast oscillations dominate.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A long-memory process (fractionally integrated, ARFIMA) is characterised by:',opts:['A) Spectral density that is flat (equal at all frequencies)','B) ACF decaying hyperbolically (polynomially): $\\rho(h)$ $\\sim$ $h^{2d-1}$ as h $\\to$ $\\infty$ for 0 &lt; d &lt; 0.5','C) ACF that is identically zero for all h &gt; 0','D) ACF decaying exponentially to zero'],ans:1,exp:'Standard ARMA processes have exponentially decaying ACF (short memory). A fractionally integrated I(d) process with 0 &lt; d &lt; 0.5 has ACF that decays much more slowly: $\\rho(h)$ $\\sim$ $C\\cdot h^{2d-1}$ as h $\\to$ $\\infty$. The sum of all ACF values diverges $(SUM|\\rho(h)|$ $=$ $\\infty)$, indicating long-range dependence. The spectral density diverges at frequency zero as $f(\\lambda)$ $\\sim$ $C\\cdot |\\lambda|^{-2d}$ as $\\lambda$ $\\to$ 0. Long memory is relevant for modelling volatility (FIGARCH), hydrology (Hurst effect), and financial returns. Short memory (ARMA): ACF decays exponentially. Long memory (ARFIMA, d in (0,0.5)): ACF decays as $h^{2d-1}$ $---$ much slower. $d=0$: stationary; $d=0.5$: boundary; d $\\geq$ 1: non-stationary.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'$X_t$ Granger-causes $Y_t$ means:',opts:['A) $X_t$ and $Y_t$ are cointegrated','B) $X_t$ is the direct causal mechanism producing $Y_t$','C) Past values of $X_t$ improve the prediction of $Y_t$ beyond what past $Y_t$ alone provides','D) The cross-correlation function between X and Y is non-zero at some lag'],ans:2,exp:'Granger causality (1969, Nobel Prize 2003) is a statistical concept of predictive causality, not physical causality. X Granger-causes Y if past values of X contain information about future Y that is not already in the past of Y. Formally, $\\mathrm{Var}(Y_{t+1}|I_t)$ &lt; $\\mathrm{Var}(Y_{t+1}|I_t$ without X). It is tested via F-test in a VAR model: fit VAR with and without X; compare via F-test on the lagged X coefficients. The reverse direction is also testable. Note: Granger causality is NOT the same as structural causality $---$ feedback and spurious correlations can produce misleading results. Granger causality $=$ \'predictive causality\'. X $\\to$ Y means adding past X improves forecast of Y. Tested via F-test in VAR. Granger causality is NOT the same as physical/structural causality!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The cross-correlation function (CCF) between two jointly stationary series ${X_t}$ and ${Y_t}$ is defined as:',opts:['A) CCF(h) $=$ $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t$, $Y_t+h)$ $/$ $[\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)\\cdot \\mathrm{Var}(Y_t)]$','B) CCF(h) $=$ $\\mathrm{Corr}(X_t$, $Y_{t-h})$ always','C) CCF(h) $=$ $\\mathrm{ACF}(X_t)$ $\\cdot$ $\\mathrm{ACF}(Y_t)$ at lag h','D) CCF(h) $=$ $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t$, $Y_{t+h})$ $/$ $sqrt[\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)\\cdot \\mathrm{Var}(Y_t)]$'],ans:3,exp:'The CCF at lag h is $\\rho_{XY}(h)$ $=$ $\\gamma_{XY}(h)$ $/$ $sqrt[\\gamma_X(0)\\cdot \\gamma_Y(0)]$ where $\\gamma_{XY}(h)$ $=$ $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t$, $Y_{t+h})$. Key difference from ACF: CCF is NOT symmetric $---$ $\\rho_{XY}(h)$ $\\neq$ $\\rho_{XY}(-h)$ in general; instead $\\rho_{XY}(h)$ $=$ $\\rho_{YX}(-h)$. A spike at positive h means X leads Y; at negative h means Y leads X. The sample CCF can be used to identify leading/lagging relationships. Caution: spurious CCF can arise from common trends or seasonality in X and Y. CCF(h) $=$ correlation between $X_t$ and $Y_{t+h}$. Not symmetric! $CCF_{XY}(h)$ $=$ $CCF_{YX}(-h)$. Spike at h&gt;0: X leads Y by h periods. Always prewhiten series before computing CCF to avoid spurious results.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For ARMA(p,q) residuals, the Ljung-Box statistic at lag m follows approximately:',opts:['A) $\\chi^2(m-p-q)$ under H0 of white noise residuals','B) F-distribution with (p+q, m) degrees of freedom','C) $\\chi^2(m)$ under H0 of white noise residuals','D) t-distribution with m-p-q degrees of freedom'],ans:0,exp:'For raw data (no model), Q $\\sim$ $\\chi^2(m)$ under H0. For residuals from a fitted ARMA(p,q) model, p+q degrees of freedom are \'used up\' by the estimated parameters, so the correct reference distribution is $\\chi^2(m-p-q)$. Using $\\chi^2(m)$ for residuals would give anti-conservative tests (too many rejections). Best practice: choose m $=$ $\\log(n)$ or m $=$ 10, 15, 20, ensuring m &gt;&gt; p+q. If Q &gt; $\\chi^2_{m-p-q, 0.05}$, reject H0 of white noise residuals and conclude the model is inadequate. Ljung-Box for raw data: $\\chi^2(m)$. For ARMA(p,q) residuals: $\\chi^2(m-p-q)$. Always adjust degrees of freedom! m-p-q &lt; m so critical value is smaller, making it harder to reject (correct behaviour).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'ARCH (Autoregressive Conditional Heteroscedasticity) effects in a time series mean:',opts:['A) The ACF of the series shows periodic spikes','B) The conditional variance depends on past squared residuals: $\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t|I_{t-1})$ $=$ $\\omega$ $+$ $\\alpha_1\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}^2$','C) The series is non-stationary','D) The conditional mean is changing over time'],ans:1,exp:'ARCH (Engle, 1982) models time-varying conditional variance. The ARCH(1) model specifies: $\\epsilon_t$ $=$ $\\sigma_t\\cdot z_t$ $(z_t$ $\\sim$ i.i.d. N(0,1)); $\\sigma_t^2$ $=$ $\\omega$ $+$ $\\alpha_1\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}^2$. When $\\alpha_1$ &gt; 0, large past residuals lead to large current variance $---$ \'volatility clustering\'. The GARCH(p,q) extension: $\\sigma_t^2$ $=$ $\\omega$ $+$ $\\sum$ $\\alpha_i\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-i}^2$ $+$ $\\sum$ $\\beta_j\\cdot \\sigma_{t-j}^2$. ARCH effects can be detected by the Engle LM test (test H0: $\\alpha_1=\\ldots=\\alpha_q=0$ in regression of $\\epsilon_t^2$ on lagged squared residuals). ARCH $=$ variance clustering. Large shocks followed by large shocks (in either direction). GARCH(1,1) is the workhorse volatility model. Unconditional variance is constant but conditional variance changes. Classic in financial returns.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Holt-Winters seasonal exponential smoothing method is most appropriate for time series that exhibit:',opts:['A) Only a trend component with no seasonality','B) Only seasonality with no trend','C) Neither trend nor seasonality (purely random)','D) Both trend and seasonality (additive or multiplicative)'],ans:3,exp:'Holt-Winters extends simple exponential smoothing (SES) with three components: (1) Level $L_t$, updated with smoothing parameter $\\alpha$; (2) Trend $T_t$, updated with parameter $\\beta$; (3) Seasonal $S_t$, updated with parameter $\\gamma$. Additive model: $\\hat{X}_{t+h}$ $=$ $L_t$ $+$ $h\\cdot T_t$ $+$ $S_{t+h-s}$. Multiplicative: $\\hat{X}_{t+h}$ $=$ $(L_t$ $+$ $h\\cdot T_t)\\cdot S_{t+h-s}$. Parameters $\\alpha$, $\\beta$, $\\gamma$ are estimated by minimising sum of squared one-step forecast errors. This corresponds to a SARIMA model with unit roots in the AR polynomial. Holt-Winters $=$ SES extended with trend and seasonality. Three smoothing parameters: $\\alpha$ (level), $\\beta$ (trend), $\\gamma$ (seasonal). Multiplicative when seasonal pattern grows with level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the Wold decomposition $X_t$ $=$ $V_t$ $+$ $W_t$, where $V_t$ is deterministic and $W_t$ is the stochastic component, what characterises $V_t?$',opts:['A) $V_t$ is always equal to zero for non-deterministic processes','B) $V_t$ is the trend component of the series','C) $V_t$ can be predicted perfectly from its own infinite past (completely deterministic)','D) $V_t$ is a white noise process'],ans:2,exp:'In the Wold decomposition, $V_t$ is the deterministic component characterised by the property that it can be predicted with zero error from its own infinite history: $\\mathrm{Var}(V_t$ $-$ $proj(V_t$ $|$ $V_{t-1}$, $V_{t-2}$, $\\ldots))$ $=$ 0. Examples: deterministic sinusoids $X_t$ $=$ $A\\cdot \\cos(\\lambda\\cdot t$ $+$ $\\phi)$, polynomials in t, or any process that is \'linearly deterministic\'. The stochastic component $W_t$ is the purely non-deterministic part that admits the $MA(\\infty)$ representation. $V_t$ and $W_t$ are uncorrelated at all lags. Wold: $X_t$ $=$ $V_t$ (deterministic, perfectly predictable) $+$ $W_t$ (stochastic, $MA(\\infty))$. The two parts are uncorrelated. $V_t$ includes periodic signals, polynomial trends. $W_t$ contains the ARMA-approximable structure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An invertible MA(q) process can be represented as:',opts:['A) A white noise process','B) A finite AR(q) process','C) An infinite-order AR process: $AR(\\infty)$','D) A finite $MA(q-1)$ process'],ans:2,exp:'For an invertible MA(q) with $\\theta(z)$ having all roots outside the unit circle, we can invert: $\\epsilon_t$ $=$ $\\theta(B)^{-1}$ $\\cdot$ $X_t$ $=$ $\\pi(B)\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}$ $\\pi_j\\cdot X_{t-j}$. This gives $X_t$ $=$ $-\\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}$ $\\pi_j\\cdot X_{t-j}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$, which is $AR(\\infty)$. The coefficients $\\pi_j$ decay exponentially for invertible MA. This is why invertibility is required: it allows the \'model\' to be turned around and expressed as a function of current and past X values, making the innovations $\\epsilon_t$ computable from the observable data. Invertible MA(q) &lt; $\\Rightarrow$ $AR(\\infty)$ representation. Causal AR(p) &lt; $\\Rightarrow$ $MA(\\infty)$ representation. Invertibility allows recovering innovations from observable data. Both representations are needed for inference.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(1) model $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ $(\\epsilon$ $\\sim$ $N(0,\\sigma^2))$, the Fisher information for $\\phi$ based on n observations is approximately:',opts:['A) $n\\cdot (1-\\phi^2)^2/\\sigma^2$','B) $n/[(1-\\phi^2)\\cdot \\sigma^2]$','C) $n\\cdot (1-\\phi^2)/\\sigma^2$','D) $n\\cdot \\gamma(0)/\\sigma^2$'],ans:1,exp:'For AR(1) with Gaussian errors, the (conditional) Fisher information for $\\phi$ is $\\mathrm{I}(\\phi)$ $=$ $\\gamma(0)/\\sigma^2$ $=$ $[\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)]/\\sigma^2$ $=$ $1/(1-\\phi^2)$ per observation. For n observations: $I_n(\\phi)$ $=$ $n/(\\sigma^2\\cdot (1-\\phi^2))$. The Cramer-Rao lower bound for $\\hat{\\phi}$ is $\\mathrm{Var}(\\hat{\\phi})$ $\\geq$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1-\\phi^2)/n$. Note that as $\\phi$ $\\to$ 1 (near unit root), the information diverges $(1/(1-\\phi^2)$ $\\to$ $\\infty)$, suggesting $\\phi$ is estimated very precisely near the unit root boundary $---$ this is the \'super-consistency\' phenomenon in unit root estimation. Cramer-Rao for AR(1): $\\mathrm{Var}(\\hat{\\phi})$ $\\geq$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1-\\phi^2)/n$. Near unit root $(\\phi$ $\\to$ 1): variance $\\to$ 0 very fast (super-consistent). Standard OLS $\\hat{\\phi}$ converges at rate n (not $\\sqrt{n})$ for unit root!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If ${Y_t}$ is obtained by applying a linear filter to ${X_t}$: $Y_t$ $=$ $\\sum_j$ $h_j$ $\\cdot$ $X_{t-j}$, then the spectral density of ${Y_t}$ is:',opts:['A) $f_Y(\\lambda)$ $=$ $f_X(\\lambda)$ $/$ $|H(\\lambda)|^2$','B) $f_Y(\\lambda)$ $=$ $|H(\\lambda)|^2$ $\\cdot$ $f_X(\\lambda)$, where $H(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\sum_j$ $h_j$ $\\cdot$ $e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda\\cdot j}$','C) $f_Y(\\lambda)$ $=$ $f_X(\\lambda)$ $+$ $|H(\\lambda)|^2$','D) $f_Y(\\lambda)$ $=$ $f_X(\\lambda)$ $\\cdot$ $H(\\lambda)$ (without squaring)'],ans:1,exp:'The key result in spectral analysis of filtered series: if $Y_t$ $=$ $H(B)\\cdot X_t$ where H(B) $=$ $\\sum_j$ $h_j\\cdot B^j$, then $f_Y(\\lambda)$ $=$ $|H(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})|^2$ $\\cdot$ $f_X(\\lambda)$. Here $H(\\lambda)$ $=$ $H(e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda})$ $=$ $\\sum_j$ $h_j\\cdot e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda\\cdot j}$ is the frequency response function (or transfer function) of the filter. The filter \'shapes\' the spectrum by amplifying $(|H(\\lambda)|^2$ &gt; 1) or suppressing (&lt; 1) different frequencies. Example: first difference filter $H(\\lambda)$ $=$ $1-e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda}$, with $|H(\\lambda)|^2$ $=$ $2(1-cos$ $\\lambda)$ $---$ this suppresses low frequencies (trend removal). Linear filter multiplies spectral density by $|H(\\lambda)|^2$. Low-pass filter: keeps low frequencies (removes trend). High-pass filter: keeps high frequencies. Band-pass: isolates a range.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The squared gain of the first-difference filter $(1-B)$ at frequency $\\lambda$ is:',opts:['A) $2\\cdot (1$ $+$ $\\cos(\\lambda))$','B) $2\\cdot (1$ $-$ $\\cos(\\lambda))$','C) $sin^2(\\lambda)$','D) 1 $-$ $\\cos(\\lambda)$'],ans:1,exp:'$|1$ $-$ $e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda}|^2$ $=$ $(1-cos$ $\\lambda)^2$ $+$ $sin^2(\\lambda)$ $=$ 1 $-$ $2cos(\\lambda)$ $+$ $cos^2(\\lambda)$ $+$ $sin^2(\\lambda)$ $=$ 2 $-$ $2cos(\\lambda)$ $=$ $2(1-cos$ $\\lambda)$. At $\\lambda=0$: $|1-e^{0}|^2$ $=$ 0 (the filter completely removes the zero frequency $=$ long-run mean/trend). At $\\lambda=\\pi$: $|1-e^{-i\\cdot \\pi}|^2$ $=$ $|1-(-1)|^2$ $=$ 4 (amplifies high-frequency oscillations). This shows why differencing removes trends but amplifies noise $---$ a well-known limitation of differencing. First difference gain $=$ $2(1-cos$ $\\lambda)$. Zero at $\\lambda=0$ (removes trend). Maximum 4 at $\\lambda=\\pi$ (amplifies oscillations). This is why differencing can introduce high-frequency noise $---$ a cost of trend removal.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral coherence between two jointly stationary series ${X_t}$ and ${Y_t}$ at frequency $\\lambda$ is:',opts:['A) $|f_{XY}(\\lambda)|^2$ $/$ $[f_X(\\lambda) \\cdot f_Y(\\lambda)]$','B) $\\int$ $f_{XY}(\\lambda)$ $d(\\lambda)$','C) $|f_{XY}(\\lambda)|$ $/$ $[f_X(\\lambda) + f_Y(\\lambda)]$','D) $f_{XY}(\\lambda)$ $/$ $f_X(\\lambda)$'],ans:0,exp:'The squared coherence $\\kappa^2(\\lambda)$ $=$ $|f_{XY}(\\lambda)|^2$ $/$ $[f_X(\\lambda)\\cdot f_Y(\\lambda)]$ measures the frequency-specific correlation between X and Y. Properties: 0 $\\leq$ $\\kappa^2(\\lambda)$ $\\leq$ 1 at each frequency. $\\kappa^2$ $=$ 1 implies Y is a perfect linear filter of X at that frequency. $\\kappa^2$ $=$ 0 implies X and Y are uncorrelated at frequency $\\lambda$. It is the frequency-domain analogue of the squared correlation coefficient. Coherence is widely used in signal processing, economics (business cycle analysis), and neuroscience. Coherence $=$ squared correlation at each frequency. $\\kappa^2$ $=$ 0: no linear relationship. $\\kappa^2$ $=$ 1: perfect linear relationship. Frequency-specific version of $r^2$ (correlation squared).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARMA(p,q) model with Gaussian innovations, the exact maximum likelihood estimator (MLE) is preferred over conditional LS because:',opts:['A) MLE does not require specifying p and q','B) MLE has smaller bias than conditional LS for all sample sizes','C) MLE is always computationally simpler','D) MLE properly accounts for the initial conditions/values and is asymptotically efficient (achieves Cramer-Rao bound)'],ans:3,exp:'Conditional LS conditions on the first p observations (ignoring their contribution to the likelihood) and recursively sets pre-sample $\\epsilon$ values to zero. This loses information and introduces bias for small samples. Exact MLE uses the full joint likelihood of all n observations, including the proper treatment of initial conditions (via the innovation form of the likelihood or the Kalman filter). For large n, both approaches give similar results (asymptotically equivalent), but for small n, exact MLE has better finite-sample properties. Both methods are non-linear and require iterative optimisation. Exact MLE vs Conditional LS: asymptotically equivalent. Exact MLE is preferred for small n (handles initial conditions properly). Both require numerical optimisation. Box-Jenkins: CLS is the traditional approach due to computational ease.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An I(2) time series requires how many differences to achieve stationarity?',opts:['A) 1 difference','B) 2 differences','C) The series is already stationary','D) An infinite number of differences'],ans:1,exp:'An I(d) process (integrated of order d) is one that becomes stationary after exactly d differences. I(0): already stationary. I(1): one difference (e.g., random walk). I(2): two differences (e.g., random walk of random walk). For I(2): $\\Delta^2\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $(1-B)^2\\cdot X_t$ is stationary. Real-world examples of I(2) processes include price levels when inflation is itself I(1), or some economic variables in certain periods. The second difference operator is: $\\Delta^2\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $X_t$ $-$ $2\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $X_{t-2}$. I(d) needs d differences. $\\mathrm{I}(0)=stationary$. $\\mathrm{I}(1)=random$ walk. $\\mathrm{I}(2)=rare$ but possible (prices when inflation is I(1)). ADF tests can be applied recursively to determine the order of integration.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The KPSS (Kwiatkowski-Phillips-Schmidt-Shin) test for stationarity has as its null hypothesis:',opts:['A) The series follows an ARIMA(0,1,0) model','B) The series has no deterministic trend','C) The series is stationary (no unit root)','D) The series has a unit root (is non-stationary)'],ans:2,exp:'KPSS (1992) tests H0: stationarity vs H1: unit root. This is the OPPOSITE of ADF (which tests H0: unit root). The KPSS statistic is based on partial sums of residuals from a regression on deterministic components. Using both ADF and KPSS together provides a more robust conclusion: (1) ADF rejects $+$ KPSS fails to reject $\\Rightarrow$ strong evidence of stationarity. (2) ADF fails to reject $+$ KPSS rejects $\\Rightarrow$ strong evidence of unit root. (3) Both tests agree to reject $\\Rightarrow$ conflicting evidence, may be near-unit-root. This complementary use is standard practice. ADF: $H0=unit$ root. KPSS: $H0=stationary$. Use BOTH together! Both fail to reject: agree on stationarity. Both reject: conflicting evidence. Different H0 means different error types $-$ use in tandem!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Phillips-Perron (PP) unit root test differs from the ADF test in that it:',opts:['A) Requires Gaussian innovations','B) Tests for seasonal unit roots','C) Has a different null hypothesis (stationarity vs unit root)','D) Uses non-parametric corrections to account for serial correlation and heteroscedasticity in the errors, rather than adding lagged differences'],ans:3,exp:'Both ADF and PP test H0: unit root. ADF handles serial correlation by AUGMENTING with lagged differences, requiring the user to choose the lag length. PP uses a semi-parametric approach: run the basic Dickey-Fuller regression (no augmentation), then apply a non-parametric correction to the test statistic to account for serial correlation and heteroscedasticity in the errors. PP has the same asymptotic distribution as ADF under H0 but may behave differently in finite samples, especially with persistent serial correlation. PP is more robust to heteroscedastic errors. ADF: adds lagged $\\Delta\\cdot X$ terms (parametric correction). PP: adjusts test statistic non-parametrically. Both have same asymptotic distribution under H0: unit root. PP preferred when error process is complicated.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A VAR(p) model for k-dimensional vector $Y_t$ $=$ $(Y_{1t},\\ldots,Y_{kt})^T$ is written as:',opts:['A) $Y_t$ $=$ $A^p$ $\\cdot$ $Y_0$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ (only depends on initial conditions)','B) $Y_t$ $=$ $A_1\\cdot Y_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\ldots$ $+$ $A_p\\cdot Y_{t-p}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$, where $A_i$ are k $\\times$ k matrices and $\\epsilon_t$ is a k $\\times$ 1 white noise vector','C) $Y_{it}$ $=$ $\\phi_i\\cdot Y_{i,t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_{it}$ (p separate AR(1) models)','D) $Y_t$ $=$ $A\\cdot (Y_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\ldots$ $+$ $Y_{t-p})$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ (single coefficient matrix)'],ans:1,exp:'VAR(p) is a multivariate extension of AR(p): each variable depends on p lags of ALL k variables in the system. The model has $k^2\\cdot p$ parameters in the lag matrices plus $k\\cdot (k+1)/2$ parameters in the covariance matrix of $\\epsilon_t$. VAR(p) is estimated equation by equation by OLS (which is efficient under Gaussianity). It is used for forecasting multiple related time series, impulse-response analysis (how does a shock to one variable affect others?), and Granger causality testing. Stability requires all eigenvalues of the companion matrix to lie inside the unit circle. VAR(p): each variable depends on p lags of ALL variables. k variables, p lags $\\Rightarrow$ $k^2\\cdot p$ parameters. Estimated by OLS equation-by-equation. Used for forecasting, impulse-response, Granger causality testing.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $X_t$ and $Y_t$ are I(1) and cointegrated with cointegrating vector (1, $-\\beta)$, the Error Correction Model (ECM) is:',opts:['A) $\\Delta^2\\cdot Y_t$ $=$ $\\mu$ $+$ $\\alpha\\cdot \\Delta\\cdot Y_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$','B) $Y_t$ $=$ $\\alpha$ $+$ $\\beta\\cdot X_t$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ (levels regression only)','C) $\\Delta\\cdot Y_t$ $=$ $\\mu$ $+$ $\\beta\\cdot \\Delta\\cdot X_t$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ (first differences only)','D) $\\Delta\\cdot Y_t$ $=$ $\\mu$ $+$ $\\alpha\\cdot (Y_{t-1}$ $-$ $\\beta\\cdot X_{t-1})$ $+$ lagged deltas $+$ $\\epsilon_t$, with $\\alpha$ &lt; 0'],ans:3,exp:'The Granger Representation Theorem states: X and Y are cointegrated IFF there exists an ECM. The term $(Y_{t-1}$ $-$ $\\beta\\cdot X_{t-1})$ is the equilibrium error (error correction term). The coefficient $\\alpha$ &lt; 0 ensures mean reversion to the long-run equilibrium: when $Y_{t-1}$ is above the long-run level $\\beta\\cdot X_{t-1}$ (positive error), $\\Delta\\cdot Y_t$ is pulled down (negative adjustment). The speed of adjustment is $|\\alpha|$: close to $-1$ means fast correction, close to 0 means slow. This explicitly links long-run equilibrium with short-run dynamics. ECM $=$ short-run dynamics $+$ long-run equilibrium correction. $\\alpha$ &lt; 0 ensures reversion to equilibrium. Speed of adjustment $=$ $|\\alpha|$. Engle-Granger $2-step$: (1) estimate cointegrating vector; (2) estimate ECM.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARIMA(0,1,1) model $\\Delta\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$ $+$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$, the $2-step$ ahead forecast error variance is:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1+(1+\\theta)^2)$','B) $\\sigma^2$','C) $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1+\\theta)^2$','D) $\\sigma^2\\cdot (2+\\theta^2)$'],ans:0,exp:'ARIMA(0,1,1): $\\Delta\\cdot X_t$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$ $+$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$, so $X_t$ $=$ $X_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ $+$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$. The $MA(\\infty)$ representation has $\\psi_0=1$, $\\psi_j$ $=$ $1+\\theta$ for j $\\geq$ 1. The $2-step$ ahead forecast error variance is $\\sigma^2\\cdot (\\psi_0^2$ $+$ $\\psi_1^2)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot (1$ $+$ $(1+\\theta)^2)$. For the $1-step$: $\\sigma^2\\cdot 1$ $=$ $\\sigma^2$. As h $\\to$ $\\infty$: variance grows without bound (I(1) process). When $\\theta=-1$ (equivalent to SES with $\\alpha=1)$, we have $\\psi_j$ $=$ $1+(-1)$ $=$ 0 for j $\\geq$ 1, so forecast variance $=$ $\\sigma^2$ for ALL horizons $---$ meaning the model says the next step is known perfectly (degenerate case). ARIMA(0,1,1) $=$ SES. $\\psi_0=1$, $\\psi_j=1+\\theta$ for j $\\geq$ 1. h-step forecast variance $=$ $\\sigma^2\\cdot \\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}$ $\\psi_j^2$. This grows unboundedly as h $\\to$ $\\infty$ (I(1) process feature).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Intervention analysis in time series models the effect of a known external event (policy change, outlier) by introducing:',opts:['A) A new autoregressive parameter for the post-intervention period','B) Additional ARIMA components to the residuals','C) A second differencing operation at the point of intervention','D) Indicator (dummy) variables such as step functions or pulse functions into the ARIMA framework'],ans:3,exp:'Box and Tiao (1975) introduced intervention analysis for modelling the effect of known external events. Common interventions: (1) Pulse (transient): $I_t$ $=$ 1 at $t=T$, 0 otherwise $---$ models a one-time shock; (2) Step (permanent): $I_t$ $=$ 0 for t&lt;T, 1 for t $\\geq$ T $---$ models a permanent shift in level. The interventions can have delayed or dynamic effects via transfer functions: $\\omega(B)/\\delta(B)\\cdot I_t$. If the intervention time is unknown, outlier detection methods (e.g., Chen-Liu) are used to identify it. This is also related to structural break testing. Pulse $=$ temporary shock; Step $=$ permanent level shift. These are added as exogenous regressors to the ARIMA model. Transfer function: $\\omega(B)/\\delta(B)\\cdot I_t$ captures dynamic effects of the intervention.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary time series with n observations, the standard error of the sample ACF r(h) at lag h (under H0 of white noise) is approximately:',opts:['A) h/n','B) $\\sqrt{h/n}$','C) $1/\\sqrt{n}$','D) $1/n$'],ans:2,exp:'Under H0 that the true ACF $\\rho(h)$ $=$ 0 for h $\\geq$ 1 (white noise), the sample ACF r(h) is approximately normally distributed with mean 0 and variance $1/n$ (Bartlett\'s formula for white noise). Thus SE(r(h)) $\\approx$ $1/\\sqrt{n}$. The $95\\%$ confidence bounds are $+/-$ $1.96/\\sqrt{n}$, often approximated as $+/-$ $2/\\sqrt{n}$. For a non-white noise process, Bartlett\'s formula gives a more complex expression: Var(r(h)) $\\approx$ $(1/n)\\cdot [1 + 2\\cdot \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\rho(k)^2]$ which accounts for the contribution of all non-zero ACF values. The simple $1/\\sqrt{n}$ applies only under white noise. Under white noise: SE(r(h)) $\\approx$ $1/\\sqrt{n}$ for all lags h. Confidence bands at $+/-$ $2/\\sqrt{n}$. Bartlett\'s formula gives the exact asymptotic variance under non-white-noise assumptions. Always check how many lags are tested $---$ multiple testing inflates the overall Type I error.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a seasonal AR process $X_t$ $=$ $\\Phi\\cdot X_{t-s}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ (seasonal AR of order 1 at period s), the ACF is non-zero only at lags:',opts:['A) h $=$ s, 2s, 3s, $\\ldots$ (multiples of s only)','B) All lags h $\\geq$ 1','C) h $=$ s and 0 only','D) h $=$ 1, 2, $\\ldots$, s'],ans:0,exp:'For the seasonal $AR(1)_s$: $X_t$ $-$ $\\Phi\\cdot X_{t-s}$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$, the ACF is $\\rho(ks)$ $=$ $\\Phi^k$ for $k=0,1,2,\\ldots$, and $\\rho(h)=0$ for h not a multiple of s. The PACF cuts off after lag s (PACF is non-zero at $h=s$ only). This is the seasonal analogue of the standard AR(1) identification. For monthly data $(s=12)$, the ACF at lags 12, 24, $36,\\ldots$ decays as $\\Phi^1$, $\\Phi^2$, $\\Phi^3,\\ldots$ The seasonal PACF cuts off at lag 12. Seasonal $AR(1)_s$: ACF at lags s, 2s, 3s (decaying). PACF cuts off at lag s. Seasonal $MA(1)_s$: ACF at lag s only (cuts off). These are the seasonal analogues of the standard AR/MA identification rules.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In a transfer function (dynamic regression) model $Y_t$ $=$ $v(B)\\cdot X_t$ $+$ $n_t$ where v(B) $=$ $\\omega(B)/\\delta(B)\\cdot B^b$, the integer b represents:',opts:['A) The number of MA terms in the noise model','B) The order of the AR component','C) The seasonal period','D) The delay (dead time) before $X_t$ has any effect on $Y_t$'],ans:3,exp:'In transfer function models (Box-Jenkins, 1976), v(D) $=$ $\\omega(B)/\\delta(B)\\cdot B^b$ is the impulse response of X on Y: $Y_t$ $=$ $v(B)\\cdot X_t$ $+$ $n_t$. The parameter b is the pure time delay $---$ $X_t$ starts affecting $Y_t$ only after b periods. $\\omega(B)$ and $\\delta(B)$ are polynomials capturing the shape of the dynamic effect. The noise $n_t$ follows an ARMA model. Transfer function models generalise regression to allow distributed lag effects. They are identified by the cross-correlation function (CCF) between prewhitened X and Y. Transfer function model: b $=$ dead time (delay). $\\omega$ controls shape of initial response. $\\delta$ determines long-run persistence. Identification: CCF after prewhitening both X and Y. Close relative to ARMAX models.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In state space modelling of time series, the Kalman filter is used to:',opts:['A) Select the order p and q of an ARMA model','B) Test for unit roots in the observation equation','C) Estimate the spectral density function','D) Recursively compute the optimal (MMSE) estimate of the unobserved state vector given all past and current observations'],ans:3,exp:'The state space form: $X_t$ $=$ $F_t\\cdot a_t$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ (observation equation); $a_t$ $=$ $T_t\\cdot a_{t-1}$ $+$ $R_t\\cdot \\eta_t$ (state transition). The Kalman filter recursively updates: (1) Prediction step: $a_{t|t-1}$ $=$ $T_t\\cdot a_{t-1}$; $P_{t|t-1}$ $=$ $T_t\\cdot P_{t-1}\\cdot T_t\'$ $+$ $R_t\\cdot Q\\cdot R_t\'$. (2) Update step: $a_{t|t}$ $=$ $a_{t|t-1}$ $+$ $K_t\\cdot v_t$ $(v_t$ $=$ innovation). Many time series models (ARIMA, unobserved components, DLMs, TVP) can be cast in state space form, and the Kalman filter computes the exact likelihood efficiently. Kalman filter: two steps $---$ predict then update. Works recursively in real time. Many time series models have a state space form. Kalman filter computes exact likelihood for MLE and produces filtered/smoothed state estimates.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In spectral analysis with discrete-time equally-spaced series, the highest frequency that can be detected (the Nyquist frequency) is:',opts:['A) $\\lambda$ $=$ $n\\cdot \\pi$ (depends on sample size n)','B) $\\lambda$ $=$ 0 (zero frequency)','C) $\\lambda$ $=$ $2\\cdot \\pi$ radians','D) $\\lambda$ $=$ $\\pi$ radians (cycles per period)'],ans:3,exp:'For a discrete-time series sampled at unit intervals, frequencies are defined on $[-\\pi, \\pi]$ radians, or equivalently [0, 0.5] cycles per unit time. The Nyquist frequency $\\lambda_N$ $=$ $\\pi$ (0.5 cycles per unit time) is the maximum detectable frequency. To detect a cycle of period P, one needs at least 2 observations per cycle (Shannon-Nyquist sampling theorem). For example, with monthly data $(s=12)$, cycles of period less than 2 months cannot be detected. The corresponding minimum detectable period is $P_{min}$ $=$ 2 time units. Nyquist: maximum detectable frequency $=$ $\\pi$ radians $=$ 0.5 cycles/unit time $=$ minimum detectable period of 2 time units. For monthly data: can detect cycles of period $\\geq$ 2 months. Anti-aliasing: sample at $\\geq$ 2x highest frequency of interest.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Kernel (window) smoothing of the periodogram $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda)$ to obtain a consistent spectral estimate involves:',opts:['A) Dividing the periodogram by the sample size n','B) Computing a weighted average of $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda)$ over a neighbourhood of frequencies, where weights are determined by the window function','C) Subtracting the sample mean from $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda)$','D) Applying a moving average filter to the original time series'],ans:1,exp:'The periodogram is inconsistent: $\\mathrm{Var}(\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda))$ does not decrease with n. To get a consistent estimate, smooth: $\\hat{f}(\\lambda)$ $=$ $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}$ $W_m(\\lambda-\\mu)\\cdot \\mathrm{I}(\\mu)\\cdot d(\\mu)/2\\pi$ $\\approx$ $(1/2m+1)\\cdot \\sum_{|j| \\leq m}$ $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda_j)$. The window $W_m$ (Bartlett, Parzen, Daniell) must satisfy: (1) Non-negative; (2) Symmetric; (3) Integrates to 1; (4) Bandwidth m $\\to$ $\\infty$ but m/n $\\to$ 0 as n $\\to$ $\\infty$ (ensures consistency). The bandwidth controls the bias-variance trade-off: wider window $\\Rightarrow$ lower variance but higher bias. Smoothing periodogram: bias-variance trade-off. Wide bandwidth: low variance, high bias (over-smooth). Narrow bandwidth: high variance, low bias. Optimal bandwidth $\\sim$ $n^{1/5}$ for the MSE-minimising choice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The ergodic theorem for the autocovariance ensures that the sample ACVF $\\hat{\\gamma}(h)$ $=$ $(1/n)\\cdot \\sum(X_t-xbar)(X_{t+h}-xbar)$ converges to the true $\\gamma(h)$ as n $\\to$ $\\infty$, provided:',opts:['A) The series has a finite fourth moment','B) The innovations are Gaussian','C) The series is weakly stationary and $\\gamma(h)$ $\\to$ 0 sufficiently fast (e.g., $\\sum|\\gamma(h)|$ &lt; $\\infty)$','D) The series is strictly stationary'],ans:2,exp:'For the sample ACVF $\\hat{\\gamma}(h)$ to be a consistent estimator of $\\gamma(h)$, we need ergodicity for the autocovariance. A sufficient condition is absolute summability of the ACVF: $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}$ $|\\gamma(h)|$ &lt; $\\infty$. This is equivalent to the spectral density being bounded and continuous. Under this condition, $\\hat{\\gamma}(h)$ $\\to$ $_P$ $\\gamma(h)$ for each fixed h as n $\\to$ $\\infty$. The rate of convergence is: $\\sqrt{n}\\cdot (\\hat{\\gamma}(h)-\\gamma(h))$ $\\to$ $_d$ N(0, $\\sum_{k}$ $[\\gamma(k+h)^2 + \\gamma(k-h)\\cdot \\gamma(k+h)])$ by Bartlett\'s theorem. Consistent ACVF estimation requires ergodicity: weak stationarity $+$ ACVF $\\to$ 0 fast enough. Absolute summability is the standard sufficient condition. This ensures sample ACF $\\to$ theoretical ACF.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement about comparing ARIMA models and exponential smoothing methods for forecasting is most accurate?',opts:['A) ARIMA models always outperform exponential smoothing methods','B) Exponential smoothing methods are special cases of ARIMA models, so ARIMA encompasses them','C) Exponential smoothing methods can outperform ARIMA on short series or when the model order is uncertain','D) Both (B) and (C) are correct'],ans:3,exp:'Exponential smoothing methods (SES, Holt, Holt-Winters) are equivalent to specific ARIMA models: $\\mathrm{SES}=\\mathrm{ARIMA}(0,1,1)$; $Holt=\\mathrm{ARIMA}(0,2,2)$. So ARIMA is indeed a superset. However, the advantage of the ES framework is: (1) Simpler estimation with fewer parameters; (2) Often performs as well or better in practice for short series where ARIMA parameter estimation is unreliable; (3) More robust to model misspecification. For long series with clear ARMA structure, correctly specified ARIMA can outperform ES. The consensus from M-competitions: simple methods often rival complex ones in real-world forecasting. ES methods are special ARIMA cases. But for short series or uncertain model order, ES may be more robust. M-competitions finding: simple methods often match complex ones. Both frameworks are valid; choice depends on data characteristics.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Engle LM test for ARCH effects in residuals ${\\hat{\\epsilon}_t}$ is based on:',opts:['A) Applying the Durbin-Watson test to the squared residuals','B) Regressing $\\hat{\\epsilon}_t^2$ on lagged $\\hat{\\epsilon}_{t-1}^2,\\ldots,\\hat{\\epsilon}_{t-q}^2$ and testing if all lag coefficients are zero (F-test or LM $=$ $n\\cdot R^2$ $\\sim$ $\\chi^2(q))$','C) Testing whether the mean of $\\hat{\\epsilon}_t^2$ is constant over time','D) Testing the ACF of $\\hat{\\epsilon}_t$ for significance'],ans:1,exp:'Engle\'s (1982) LM test for ARCH(q): (1) Fit the mean model, obtain residuals $\\hat{\\epsilon}_t$; (2) Regress $\\hat{\\epsilon}_t^2$ on a constant and $\\hat{\\epsilon}_{t-1}^2,\\ldots,\\hat{\\epsilon}_{t-q}^2$; (3) Compute test statistic LM $=$ $n\\cdot R^2$ $\\sim$ $\\chi^2(q)$ under H0 (no ARCH). Rejection indicates ARCH effects (volatility clustering). If ARCH is present, the residuals have constant unconditional variance but time-varying conditional variance. Solution: fit ARCH/GARCH model to the squared residuals or use heteroscedasticity-consistent (HC) standard errors for the mean model. ARCH LM test: regress $\\epsilon^2$ on lagged $\\epsilon^2$. LM $=$ $n\\cdot R^2$ $\\sim$ $\\chi^2(q)$. Significant $\\Rightarrow$ ARCH effects present $\\Rightarrow$ fit GARCH. The ACF and PACF of $\\epsilon^2$ (not $\\epsilon)$ guide GARCH order selection.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The impulse response function (IRF) of an ARMA process $X_t$ $=$ $\\phi_1\\cdot X_{t-1}+\\ldots+\\phi_p\\cdot X_{t-p}+\\epsilon_t+\\ldots+\\theta_q\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-q}$ at lag h gives:',opts:['A) The h-th autocorrelation $\\rho(h)$ of the process','B) The forecast error variance at horizon h','C) The partial autocorrelation $\\phi_{hh}$ at lag h','D) The response of $X_{t+h}$ to a unit shock in $\\epsilon_t$, holding all other shocks constant (i.e., the $\\psi_h$ coefficient in the $MA(\\infty)$ representation)'],ans:3,exp:'The impulse response function $\\psi_h$ $=$ $dX_{t+h}/d(\\epsilon_t)$ measures how a one-unit shock at time t propagates to future values. For ARMA: $\\psi_0=1$, $\\psi_h$ $=$ $\\phi_1\\cdot \\psi_{h-1}+\\ldots+\\phi_p\\cdot \\psi_{h-p}+\\theta_h$ (with $\\psi_j=0$ for j&lt;0 and $\\theta_j=0$ for j&gt;q). For AR(1): $\\psi_h$ $=$ $\\phi^h$ (exponential decay). For MA(q): $\\psi_h$ $=$ $\\theta_h$ for $h=1,\\ldots,q$, zero beyond. IRFs are essential for VAR analysis: they show how a shock to one variable affects all variables over time (dynamic multipliers). They are computed from the $MA(\\infty)$ representation. IRF $=$ $\\psi_h$ $=$ $MA(\\infty)$ coefficients. They trace out how a shock propagates over time. For AR(1): $\\psi_h=\\phi^h$. Cumulative sum of IRF $=$ long-run multiplier. For I(1): cumulative IRF is infinite!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The backforecasting approach to initialising ARMA estimation involves:',opts:['A) Running the ADF test in reverse','B) Forecasting past values using the fitted ARMA model','C) Running the ARMA model backwards in time to estimate starting values for the innovations','D) Using the last observations to initialise the Kalman filter'],ans:2,exp:'Box and Jenkins (1976) proposed backforecasting to handle the MA initial conditions problem. For MA processes, the initial unobserved innovations $\\epsilon_{0}$, $\\epsilon_{-1},\\ldots$ are needed. Backforecasting: (1) Forward: fit ARMA and compute forecast for one step ahead; (2) Reverse the series and run the MA component backward to obtain estimated starting innovations; (3) Iterate until convergence. This is an alternative to the exact likelihood approach (Kalman filter) and works well for invertible MA processes. For AR processes, the initial observations can be used directly. Backforecasting handles the \'starting innovations\' problem in MA and ARMA estimation. The idea: run the time series backwards to estimate starting eps values. Iterative, converges for invertible MA processes.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spurious regression problem arises when two independent I(1) series are regressed on each other, resulting in:',opts:['A) Residuals that are I(0) (stationary)','B) The regression coefficient being exactly zero','C) An unbiased estimate of the true (zero) relationship','D) A very high $R^2$ and significant t-statistics even though the series are truly unrelated'],ans:3,exp:'Granger and Newbold (1974) and later Phillips (1986) showed that regressing two independent random walks on each other produces: high $R^2$ (often &gt; 0.9), significant t-statistics with inflated t-values (not following t-distribution), and the residuals being I(1) (non-stationary) rather than I(0). The standard assumptions of OLS (finite variance, independence) are violated for I(1) data. Solution: (1) If series are cointegrated, use ECM; (2) If not cointegrated, use first-differenced series; (3) Always test residuals for stationarity after regression in levels. Spurious regression: two unrelated I(1) series $\\Rightarrow$ high $R^2$, significant t-stats. Residuals are I(1)! Always test regression residuals for stationarity (Engle-Granger cointegration test). If residuals are I(0) $\\Rightarrow$ cointegrated $\\Rightarrow$ not spurious.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'When evaluating ARIMA forecast performance using a rolling window (recursive) approach, which measure is most commonly used?',opts:['A) R-squared of the fitted model','B) Durbin-Watson statistic of the residuals','C) RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error) of out-of-sample forecasts','D) AIC of the in-sample fit'],ans:2,exp:'Out-of-sample evaluation: hold out the last m observations as test set; fit model on training set; produce h-step ahead forecasts; compare with actuals. RMSE $=$ $sqrt[(1/m)\\cdot \\sum(X_{t+h}-\\hat{X}_{t+h|t})^2]$. Other metrics: MAE, MAPE, MASE. AIC and $R^2$ measure in-sample fit, NOT forecast accuracy (they can be misleading). The M-competition (Makridakis) framework uses out-of-sample RMSE and MAE across many real series and has consistently found that parsimonious models and simple methods often outperform complex ones in out-of-sample evaluation. RMSE $=$ $\\sqrt{\\mathrm{MSE}}$ for out-of-sample forecasts. In-sample fit (AIC, $R^2)$ is NOT the same as forecast accuracy. Always use out-of-sample evaluation! MAE less sensitive to outliers; MAPE requires non-zero actuals.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(2) with $\\rho(1)$ $=$ 0.7 and $\\rho(2)$ $=$ 0.6, solving the Yule-Walker equations gives $\\phi_1$ and $\\phi_2$ approximately as:',opts:['A) $\\phi_1$ $=$ 0; $\\phi_2$ $=$ $\\rho(2)$ $=$ 0.6','B) $\\phi_1$ $=$ $\\rho(1)+\\rho(2)$ $=$ 1.3; $\\phi_2$ $=$ $\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)$ $=$ $-0.1$','C) $\\phi_1$ $=$ 0.7, $\\phi_2$ $=$ 0.6','D) $\\phi_1$ $=$ $\\rho(1)\\cdot (1-\\rho(2))/(1-\\rho(1)^2)$ $=$ $0.7\\cdot 0.4/0.51$ $\\approx$ 0.549; $\\phi_2$ $=$ $(\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2)/(1-\\rho(1)^2)$ $=$ $(0.6-0.49)/0.51$ $\\approx$ 0.216'],ans:3,exp:'Yule-Walker for AR(2): $\\phi_1$ $=$ $\\rho(1)\\cdot (1-\\rho(2))/(1-\\rho(1)^2)$ and $\\phi_2$ $=$ $(\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2)/(1-\\rho(1)^2)$. Substituting: denom $=$ 1 $-$ 0.49 $=$ 0.51. $\\phi_1$ $=$ $0.7\\cdot (1-0.6)/0.51$ $=$ $0.7\\cdot 0.4/0.51$ $=$ $0.28/0.51$ $\\approx$ 0.549. $\\phi_2$ $=$ $(0.6-0.49)/0.51$ $=$ $0.11/0.51$ $\\approx$ 0.216. Check stationarity: $\\phi_1+\\phi_2$ $=$ 0.765 &lt; 1; $\\phi_2-\\phi_1$ $=$ $-0.333$ &lt; 1; $|\\phi_2|$ $=$ 0.216 &lt; 1. All three conditions satisfied $---$ stationary. Yule-Walker AR(2) formulas: $\\phi_1=\\rho(1)\\cdot (1-\\rho(2))/(1-\\rho(1)^2)$; $\\phi_2=(\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2)/(1-\\rho(1)^2)$. Denominator $=$ $1-\\rho(1)^2$ $=$ $(1-\\rho(1))(1+\\rho(1))$. Always verify stationarity of the estimated parameters!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The asymptotic distribution of the t-statistic for the unit root test in the Dickey-Fuller regression $X_t$ $=$ $\\delta\\cdot X_{t-1}$ $+$ $u_t$ (H0: $\\delta=1)$ under H0 is:',opts:['A) $\\chi^2(1)$','B) N(0,1) asymptotically','C) $t(n-2)$ for finite samples','D) A Dickey-Fuller distribution (not standard normal) $---$ more negative critical values'],ans:3,exp:'Under H0 (unit root, $\\delta=1)$, the OLS t-statistic $\\tau$ for testing $\\delta=1$ in the ADF regression does NOT converge to N(0,1). It converges to a functional of standard Brownian motion W(r): $\\tau$ $\\to$ $\\int_0^1$ W dW $/$ $[\\int_0^1 W^2 dr]^{1/2}$ (Dickey-Fuller distribution). This distribution is LEFT-SKEWED with mean approximately $-0.5$ and has much more negative critical values than N(0,1). For example, the $5\\%$ critical value for the no-intercept case is about $-1.95$, while for the intercept+trend case it is about $-3.41$. Using standard normal critical values leads to over-rejection of H0. DF t-statistic $\\sim$ DF distribution (NOT N(0,1)). More negative than normal. Critical values depend on: (1) No intercept; (2) Intercept only; (3) Intercept $+$ trend. Wrong critical values $=$ wrong test! Published in Fuller (1976) tables.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best summarises the Box-Jenkins methodology for time series modelling?',opts:['A) Choose the model with the highest AIC and forecast directly without checking residuals','B) Apply the Hodrick-Prescott filter to decompose trend and cycle, then model each separately','C) Fit an AR(1) model, test residuals, accept if ACF is non-significant','D) Three iterative stages: (1) Identification of tentative ARMA(p,q) order using ACF/PACF; (2) Estimation of parameters; (3) Diagnostic checking of residuals $---$ iterate if necessary'],ans:3,exp:'Box and Jenkins (1970, 1976) proposed a $3-stage$ iterative approach: (1) IDENTIFICATION: Plot the series; check for stationarity (ADF/KPSS); difference if needed; examine sample ACF and PACF to identify tentative ARMA(p,q) order; use AIC/BIC for model selection. (2) ESTIMATION: Estimate parameters by CLS or exact MLE; check confidence intervals; verify stationarity/invertibility of estimated parameters. (3) DIAGNOSTIC CHECKING: Plot residual ACF; conduct Ljung-Box test; check normality (QQ-plot), constancy of variance, no ARCH effects. If inadequate: go back to step 1. This framework remains the foundation of modern time series analysis. Box-Jenkins: Identify $\\to$ Estimate $\\to$ Diagnose $\\to$ (Iterate). IDENTIFICATION uses ACF/PACF patterns and information criteria. DIAGNOSTICS focus on white noise residuals. All three stages are essential $---$ skipping diagnostics is a common mistake!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'[UGC NET Statistics PYQ $---$ June 2026] For the AR(2) process $y_t$ $=$ $\\Phi_1\\cdot y_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\Phi_2\\cdot y_{t-2}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ estimated by the Yule-Walker method, the sample autocorrelations are $r_1$ $=$ 0.8 and $r_2$ $=$ 0.5. The estimate of $\\Phi_2$ is:',opts:['A) $16/18$','B) $-16/18$','C) $-7/18$','D) $7/18$'],ans:2,exp:'Yule-Walker equations: $r_1$ $=$ $\\Phi_1$ $+$ $\\Phi_2\\cdot r_1$ and $r_2$ $=$ $\\Phi_1\\cdot r_1$ $+$ $\\Phi_2$. From the first equation: $\\Phi_1$ $=$ $r_1$ $-$ $\\Phi_2\\cdot r_1$ $=$ 0.8 $-$ $0.8\\cdot \\Phi_2$. Substituting into the second: 0.5 $=$ $0.8\\cdot (0.8-0.8\\cdot \\Phi_2)$ $+$ $\\Phi_2$ $=$ 0.64 $-$ $0.64\\cdot \\Phi_2$ $+$ $\\Phi_2$ $=$ 0.64 $+$ $0.36\\cdot \\Phi_2$. So $\\Phi_2$ $=$ $(0.5-0.64)/0.36$ $=$ $-0.14/0.36$ $=$ $-7/18$. Equivalently, using the closed form $\\Phi_2$ $=$ $(r_2-r_1^2)/(1-r_1^2)$ $=$ $(0.5-0.64)/(1-0.64)$ $=$ $-0.14/0.36$ $=$ $-7/18$. A negative $\\Phi_2$ estimate from positive sample autocorrelations is common and is not an error $---$ always solve the simultaneous equations rather than assuming the sign of $\\phi$ matches the sign of r. Closed-form Yule-Walker AR(2): $\\Phi_1$ $=$ $r_1(1-r_2)/(1-r_1^2)$; $\\Phi_2$ $=$ $(r_2-r_1^2)/(1-r_1^2)$. Memorise both $---$ they save time versus solving simultaneous equations from scratch in the exam.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'[Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice] For an AR(2) process, the sample autocorrelations are $r_1$ $=$ 0.6 and $r_2$ $=$ 0.3. Using the Yule-Walker equations, the estimate of $\\Phi_2$ is:',opts:['A) $3/32$','B) $21/32$','C) $-0.06$','D) $-3/32$'],ans:3,exp:'Using $\\Phi_2$ $=$ $(r_2-r_1^2)/(1-r_1^2)$ $=$ $(0.3-0.36)/(1-0.36)$ $=$ $-0.06/0.64$ $=$ $-3/32$. (Option C, $-0.06$, is only the numerator $---$ a common error from forgetting to divide by $1-r_1^2.)$ Continuing, $\\Phi_1$ $=$ $r_1\\cdot (1-r_2)/(1-r_1^2)$ $=$ $0.6\\cdot 0.7/0.64$ $=$ $0.42/0.64$ $=$ $21/32$ (option D $---$ a distractor from confusing $\\Phi_1$ with $\\Phi_2)$. Stationarity check on the fitted values: $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2$ $=$ $18/32$ $=$ 0.5625 &lt; 1 $\\checkmark$ ; $\\Phi_2-\\Phi_1$ $=$ $-24/32$ $=$ $-0.75$, inside $(-1,1)$ $\\checkmark$ ; $|\\Phi_2|$ $=$ $3/32$ &lt; 1 $\\checkmark$ $---$ the Yule-Walker estimates are automatically stationary whenever $r_1$, $r_2$ come from a genuine (positive-definite) sample correlation matrix. Same two-question pattern as the June 2026 PYQ $(r1=0.8,r2=0.5)$ but with fresh numbers $---$ practise the closed-form formulas until they are automatic, since NET often varies only the numeric inputs.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'[UGC NET Statistics PYQ $---$ June 2026] For estimating the spectral density function of a time series, one can use:',opts:['A) Partial Autocorrelation Function','B) Yule-Walker Equations','C) Autocorrelation Function','D) Periodogram'],ans:3,exp:'The periodogram $\\mathrm{I}(\\lambda_j)$ $=$ $(1/n)|\\sum_t$ $X_t\\cdot e^{-i\\cdot \\lambda_j\\cdot t}|^2$ $---$ based on the Fourier transform of the sample autocovariances, equivalently the squared modulus of the discrete Fourier transform of the data $---$ is the classical empirical estimator of the spectral density function $f(\\lambda)$. The other three options (PACF, ACF, Yule-Walker equations) belong to the TIME-DOMAIN toolkit used for ARMA identification and estimation, not frequency-domain estimation. Note: the raw periodogram is asymptotically unbiased but inconsistent $(Q45/Q90$ of this set) $---$ smoothing with a spectral window is needed for a consistent estimate, but the periodogram remains the starting point. Frequency-domain estimation $=$ Periodogram (and its smoothed versions). Time-domain tools (ACF, PACF, Yule-Walker) identify and estimate ARMA orders/parameters $---$ different job entirely. Don\'t mix the two toolkits on this type of recall question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'[UGC NET Statistics PYQ $---$ June 2026] Consider $y_t$ $=$ $0.6\\cdot y_{t-1}$ $+$ $0.4\\cdot y_{t-2}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$, where ${\\epsilon_t}$ is purely random with mean 0 and variance $\\sigma^2$. The ACF of ${\\Delta\\cdot y_t}$, say $r_k$, is:',opts:['A) $r_k$ $=$ $(0.6)^k$','B) $r_k$ $=$ $(0.4)^k$','C) $r_k$ $=$ $(-0.6)^k$','D) $r_k$ $=$ $(-0.4)^k$'],ans:3,exp:'Since $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2$ $=$ $0.6+0.4$ $=$ 1, the AR(2) characteristic equation $1-0.6z-0.4z^2$ $=$ 0 has a root at $z=1$ $---$ a genuine unit root, so ${y_t}$ is non-stationary. Subtract $y_{t-1}$ from both sides: $\\Delta\\cdot y_t$ $=$ $0.6\\cdot y_{t-1}+0.4\\cdot y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t-y_{t-1}$ $=$ $-0.4\\cdot (y_{t-1}-y_{t-2})+\\epsilon_t$ $=$ $-0.4\\cdot \\Delta\\cdot y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$. This is AR(1) in $\\Delta\\cdot y_t$ with coefficient $-0.4$, so its ACF is $r_k$ $=$ $(-0.4)^k$ $---$ an ALTERNATING, decaying pattern (positive $\\Phi_2$ in the original AR(2) becomes a NEGATIVE AR(1) coefficient after differencing). When $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=1$ for an AR(2), differencing once always leaves AR(1) in $\\Delta\\cdot y_t$ with coefficient exactly $-\\Phi_2$ (see Q105 for the general derivation). The sign flip is the classic exam trap $---$ students often answer $(0.4)^k$ by forgetting the sign change.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'[Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice] Consider $y_t$ $=$ $0.7\\cdot y_{t-1}$ $+$ $0.3\\cdot y_{t-2}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$, where ${\\epsilon_t}$ is purely random with mean 0 and variance $\\sigma^2$. The ACF of ${\\Delta\\cdot y_t}$ is:',opts:['A) $r_k$ $=$ $(0.3)^k$','B) $r_k$ $=$ $(-0.3)^k$','C) $r_k$ $=$ $(-0.7)^k$','D) $r_k$ $=$ $(0.7)^k$'],ans:1,exp:'Since $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2$ $=$ $0.7+0.3$ $=$ 1, $z=1$ is a root of the AR polynomial and ${y_t}$ is non-stationary. General derivation: $y_t-y_{t-1}$ $=$ $(\\Phi_1-1)\\cdot y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2\\cdot y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$. Whenever $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=1$, we have $\\Phi_1-1$ $=$ $-\\Phi_2$, so $\\Delta\\cdot y_t$ $=$ $-\\Phi_2\\cdot y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2\\cdot y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$ $=$ $-\\Phi_2\\cdot (y_{t-1}-y_{t-2})+\\epsilon_t$ $=$ $-\\Phi_2\\cdot \\Delta\\cdot y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$. This is AR(1) in $\\Delta\\cdot y_t$ with coefficient exactly $-\\Phi_2$ $=$ $-0.3$, so $r_k$ $=$ $(-0.3)^k$. (Substituting $\\Phi_2=0.4$ into this same rule instantly recovers Q104\'s answer, $(-0.4)^k$ $---$ one formula covers both questions.) General shortcut: for AR(2) with $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=1$, differencing once gives AR(1) in $\\Delta\\cdot y_t$ with coefficient $=$ $-\\Phi_2$ always. No need to redo the full algebra each time $---$ just read off $-\\Phi_2$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'[UGC NET Statistics PYQ $---$ June 2026] For the AR(1) process $y_t$ $=$ $0.6\\cdot y_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$, with $E(\\epsilon_t)=0$ and $E(\\epsilon_t^2)=4$, the variance of $y_t$ is:',opts:['A) $100/9$','B) $20/3$','C) 10','D) 6.25'],ans:3,exp:'The stationary variance of AR(1) is $\\mathrm{Var}(y_t)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2_\\epsilon/(1-\\phi^2)$. Substituting: $4/(1-0.6^2)$ $=$ $4/(1-0.36)$ $=$ $4/0.64$ $=$ 6.25. Option (B), $20/3$ $\\approx$ 6.67, comes from mistakenly using $(1-0.6)=0.4$ in the denominator instead of $(1-0.6^2)$; option (A), $100/9$ $\\approx$ 11.1, comes from using $(1-\\phi)^2=0.16$ in the denominator. Always square $\\phi$ before subtracting from 1 $---$ this is the single most common numeric slip on this formula. Var(AR1) $=$ $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$, NOT $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi)$ and NOT $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi)^2$. With $\\sigma^2=4$, $\\phi=0.6$: denominator $=$ $1-0.36$ $=$ 0.64; $4/0.64$ $=$ 6.25. Practise this substitution until it\'s automatic $---$ it is a near-guaranteed $1-mark$ numeric question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'[Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice] For the AR(1) process $y_t$ $=$ $0.5\\cdot y_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$, with $E(\\epsilon_t)=0$ and $E(\\epsilon_t^2)=3$, the variance of $y_t$ is:',opts:['A) 12','B) 6','C) 4','D) 3'],ans:2,exp:'$\\mathrm{Var}(y_t)$ $=$ $\\sigma^2_\\epsilon/(1-\\phi^2)$ $=$ $3/(1-0.5^2)$ $=$ $3/(1-0.25)$ $=$ $3/0.75$ $=$ 4. Option (D), 3, wrongly assumes $\\mathrm{Var}(y_t)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$ (ignoring the AR structure entirely $---$ this equals the WN variance only at $\\phi=0)$. Option (B), 6, results from using $(1-\\phi)=0.5$ in the denominator. Option (A), 12, results from $3/(1-\\phi)^2$ $=$ $3/0.25$. Same formula, fresh numbers: Var(AR1) $=$ $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$. Note the stationary variance (4) is always LARGER than the innovation variance (3) whenever $\\phi$ $\\neq$ 0 $---$ the AR structure adds persistence, which adds variance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'[UGC NET Statistics PYQ $---$ June 2026] For the random walk model $y_t$ $=$ $\\mu$ $+$ $y_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$, $t=1,2,\\ldots$, where ${\\epsilon_t}$ is purely random with $E(\\epsilon_t)=0$, $E(\\epsilon_t^2)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$, the mean and variance of $y_t$ (starting from $y_0)$ are respectively:',opts:['A) $\\mu$, $\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$','B) $t\\cdot \\mu$, $\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$','C) $\\mu$, $t\\cdot \\sigma^2_\\epsilon$','D) $t\\cdot \\mu$, $t\\cdot \\sigma^2_\\epsilon$'],ans:3,exp:'Iterating the recursion: $y_t$ $=$ $y_0$ $+$ $t\\cdot \\mu$ $+$ $\\sum_{s=1}^{t}$ $\\epsilon_s$. Taking expectation: $E(y_t)$ $=$ $y_0$ $+$ $t\\cdot \\mu$ (drift accumulates linearly with t). Taking variance, and since the $\\epsilon_s$ are uncorrelated: $\\mathrm{Var}(y_t)$ $=$ $t\\cdot \\sigma^2_\\epsilon$. Both moments grow without bound as t increases $---$ this joint growth of mean AND variance is the hallmark of a non-stationary random walk with drift (compare with Q50 of this set, which asks the same question in extended-option form; this exact compact numeric-recall format is the one the NTA paper actually used). Random walk with drift: $E(y_t)=t\\cdot \\mu$ (deterministic trend component), $\\mathrm{Var}(y_t)=t\\cdot \\sigma^2$ (stochastic-trend variance). Differencing removes BOTH problems at once: $\\Delta\\cdot y_t$ $=$ $\\mu+\\epsilon_t$ is stationary with constant mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'[Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice $---$ mirrors the June 2026 comprehension-passage style] Consider the process $y_t$ $=$ $\\Phi_1\\cdot y_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\Phi_2\\cdot y_{t-2}$ $+$ $\\epsilon_t$ $-$ $\\theta_1\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$ $-$ $\\theta_2\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-2}$ (note the MINUS sign before the MA terms $---$ a convention used in several UGC NET papers). If $\\Phi_1=0.5$, $\\Phi_2=0.3$, $\\theta_1=0.4$, $\\theta_2=0.5$, the process is:',opts:['A) Stationary but not invertible','B) Invertible but not stationary','C) Neither stationary nor invertible','D) Both stationary and invertible'],ans:3,exp:'AR part (stationarity triangle on $\\Phi_1$, $\\Phi_2)$: $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2$ $=$ 0.8 &lt; 1 $\\checkmark$ ; $\\Phi_2-\\Phi_1$ $=$ $-0.2$, inside $(-1,1)$ $\\checkmark$ ; $|\\Phi_2|$ $=$ 0.3 &lt; 1 $\\checkmark$ $---$ comfortably stationary. Explanation (cont.): MA part: because this question uses the MINUS convention $(\\epsilon_t$ $-$ $\\theta_1\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$ $-$ $\\theta_2\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-2})$, the MA polynomial $1-\\theta_1\\cdot z-\\theta_2\\cdot z^2$ has EXACTLY the same algebraic form as the AR polynomial, so the identical triangle test applies directly to $\\theta_1$, $\\theta_2$: $\\theta_1+\\theta_2$ $=$ 0.9 &lt; 1 $\\checkmark$ ; $\\theta_2-\\theta_1$ $=$ 0.1, inside $(-1,1)$ $\\checkmark$ ; $|\\theta_2|$ $=$ 0.5 &lt; 1 $\\checkmark$ $---$ invertible. Both conditions hold, so the process is both stationary and invertible. SIGN-CONVENTION TRAP: with the MINUS convention $(\\epsilon_t$ $-$ $\\theta_1\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$ $-$ $\\ldots)$, the MA triangle test is IDENTICAL to the AR triangle test $---$ just substitute $\\theta_i$ for $\\Phi_i$. With the PLUS convention $(\\epsilon_t$ $+$ $\\theta_1\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$ $+$ $\\ldots)$ used elsewhere in this set (see Q18), the first inequality flips to $\\theta_1+\\theta_2$ &gt; $-1$. Always check which convention a question is using before testing invertibility $---$ this exact trap was flagged in the official June 2026 UGC NET key (see Q112).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'[UGC NET Statistics PYQ $---$ June 2026] Let $\\Phi_1=0.6$, $\\Phi_2=0.3$, $\\theta_1=\\theta_2=0$, and let $\\alpha(h)$ denote the partial autocorrelation of order h. Then:',opts:['A) $\\alpha(h)$ $\\neq$ 0 for all h $\\geq$ 1','B) $\\alpha(h)$ $=$ 0 for all h $\\geq$ 2','C) $\\alpha(h)$ $=$ 0 for all h $\\geq$ 1','D) $\\alpha(h)$ $=$ 0 for all h $\\geq$ 3'],ans:3,exp:'With $\\theta_1=\\theta_2=0$, the model reduces to a pure AR(2) process. The defining identification property of any AR(p) process is that its partial autocorrelation function CUTS OFF after lag p: $\\alpha(1)$ and $\\alpha(2)$ are generally non-zero $(\\alpha(1)=\\rho(1)=\\Phi_1/(1-\\Phi_2)=0.6/0.7$ $\\approx$ 0.857; $\\alpha(2)=\\Phi_2=0.3$, a special AR(2) result), but $\\alpha(h)=0$ for every h $\\geq$ $p+1$ $=$ 3. Options (C) and (B) incorrectly cut the PACF off too early (they would describe white noise or AR(1) respectively); option (A) confuses the AR(2) PACF with the ACF, which never cuts off exactly for AR(2). AR(p) PACF cutoff rule, applied numerically: for AR(2), $\\alpha(1),\\alpha(2)$ $\\neq$ 0 but $\\alpha(h)=0$ for h $\\geq$ 3. A neat $AR(2)-only$ fact: $\\alpha(2)$ always equals $\\Phi_2$ exactly (here, 0.3) $---$ useful for quick sanity checks on PACF plots.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'[Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice] Let $\\Phi_1=0.5$, $\\Phi_2=0.5$, $\\theta_1=0.4$, $\\theta_2=0$ in the model $y_t$ $=$ $\\Phi_1\\cdot y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2\\cdot y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t+\\theta_1\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}+\\theta_2\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-2}$. Then the process is a:',opts:['A) ARMA(2,1) process','B) ARMA(1,2) process','C) ARIMA(1,1,1) process','D) ARIMA(2,1,0) process'],ans:2,exp:'Since $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2$ $=$ $0.5+0.5$ $=$ 1 exactly, the AR characteristic equation $1-0.5z-0.5z^2=0$ has a root at $z=1$ (multiply by $-2$: $z^2+z-2=0$ $=$ $(z+2)(z-1)$, roots $z=1$ and $z=-2)$. One genuine unit root means ${y_t}$ is non-stationary and needs first differencing. Removing that single unit root leaves an AR(1) for $\\Delta\\cdot y_t$ (order drops from 2 to 1); the MA part is order 1 $(\\theta_1=0.4$, $\\theta_2=0)$, unaffected by the differencing. Hence the model is correctly classified as ARIMA(1,1,1) $---$ NOT the \'plain\' ARMA(2,1) it might appear to be at first glance from the stated orders $(p=2,q=1)$. Whenever the AR coefficients of a stated ARMA(p,q) $\\sum$ to exactly 1, suspect a hidden unit root $---$ factor the characteristic polynomial (or just check $\\phi(1)=0)$ before accepting the stated order. One unit root converts ARMA(p,q) into $\\mathrm{ARIMA}(p-1,1,q)$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'[UGC NET Statistics PYQ $---$ June 2026 $---$ flagged for a sign-convention issue in the official key; see Exam Tip] For the MA(1) process $y_t$ $=$ $\\epsilon_t$ $-$ $\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$ with $\\theta$ $=$ 0.7 and $\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t)$ $=$ 1 (MINUS convention, as stated), the spectral density $f(\\omega)$ is:',opts:['A) 1.49 $-$ $1.4\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\omega)$','B) $[1.49 - 1.4\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\omega)]^{-1}$','C) $[1.49 - 0.7\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\omega)]^{-1}$','D) 1.49 $+$ $1.4\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\omega)$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\gamma_0$ $=$ $\\sigma^2_\\epsilon\\cdot (1+\\theta^2)$ $=$ $1\\cdot (1+0.49)$ $=$ 1.49. For $\\gamma_1$, with the MINUS convention $y_t=\\epsilon_t-\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1}$: $\\gamma_1$ $=$ $\\mathrm{Cov}(y_t,y_{t-1})$ $=$ $E[(\\epsilon_t-\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1})(\\epsilon_{t-1}-\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-2})]$ $=$ $-\\theta\\cdot \\sigma^2_\\epsilon$ $=$ $-0.7$ (all other cross terms vanish since the $\\epsilon\'s$ are uncorrelated). Explanation (cont.): The spectral density formula is $f(\\omega)$ $=$ $\\gamma_0$ $+$ $2\\cdot \\gamma_1\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\omega)$ $=$ 1.49 $+$ $2\\cdot (-0.7)\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\omega)$ $=$ 1.49 $-$ $1.4\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\omega)$, matching option (A). This was independently re-derived and does not match the official NTA key for this question, which listed the $+1.4cos(2\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\omega)$ version $---$ magnitude correct, sign of the cosine term differs. EXAM TIP $---$ SIGN-CONVENTION AWARENESS: with the PLUS convention $(y_t=\\epsilon_t+\\theta\\cdot \\epsilon_{t-1})$, $\\gamma_1=+\\theta\\cdot \\sigma^2$, giving the mirror-image $f(\\omega)=1.49+1.4\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\omega)$ instead. The June 2026 official key was flagged for exactly this ambiguity. Always fix and state your sign convention before computing $\\gamma_1$ for an MA process, and re-derive from first principles rather than trusting a remembered formula.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A general linear process is defined as $X_t = \\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ with $\\psi_0=1$ and $\\varepsilon_t\\sim \\mathrm{WN}(0,\\sigma^2)$. The variance of this process is:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j^2$','B) $\\sigma^2\\left(\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\right)^2$','C) $\\sigma^2/\\psi_0^2$','D) $\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j$'],ans:0,exp:'Since $\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ are uncorrelated with each other and each has variance $\\sigma^2$: $$\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j^2\\,\\mathrm{Var}(\\varepsilon_{t-j})=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j^2$$ For finite variance (stationarity): $\\sum\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$ (square summability, $\\ell^2$ condition). Note: (D) requires absolute summability ($\\ell^1$), which is a stronger condition needed for causality, but NOT needed for stationarity alone. Memory Tip: Variance $=\\sigma^2\\times\\sum\\psi_j^2$. Think: each $\\psi_j$ “scales” the noise at lag $j$, and variances add up as squares. $\\sum\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$ = stationary; $\\sum|\\psi_j|&lt;\\infty$ = causal (stronger).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the general linear process $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$, the autocovariance at lag $h\\ge0$ is:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(h)=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\psi_{j+h}$','B) $\\gamma(h)=\\sigma^2\\psi_h$','C) $\\gamma(h)=\\sigma^2/\\psi_h$','D) $\\gamma(h)=\\sigma^2\\left(\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\right)\\left(\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_{j+h}\\right)$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\gamma(h)=\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t-h})=E\\!\\left[\\left(\\sum_j\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}\\right)\\!\\left(\\sum_k\\psi_k\\varepsilon_{t-h-k}\\right)\\right]$ Only terms with equal innovation indices contribute: $t-j=t-h-k\\Rightarrow k=j-h$. $$\\gamma(h)=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=h}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\psi_{j-h}=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_{j+h}\\psi_j$$ This is the convolution of the $\\psi$ sequence with itself at lag $h$. At $h=0$: $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2\\sum\\psi_j^2$ ✓ Memory Tip: ACVF of a linear filter = $\\sigma^2\\times$inner product of the filter with its lag-$h$ shift. Like cross-correlating the $\\psi$ coefficients with themselves.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) process $X_t=\\phi_1 X_{t-1}+\\phi_2 X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$ (stationary), the Wold MA($\\infty$) coefficients $\\psi_j$ satisfy which recurrence for $j\\ge1$?',opts:['A) $\\psi_j=\\phi_2^j$ for all $j\\ge1$','B) $\\psi_j=\\phi_1^j+\\phi_2^j$ for all $j\\ge1$','C) $\\psi_j=(\\phi_1+\\phi_2)^j$ for all $j\\ge1$','D) $\\psi_j=\\phi_1\\psi_{j-1}+\\phi_2\\psi_{j-2}$ for $j\\ge2$, with $\\psi_0=1$, $\\psi_1=\\phi_1$'],ans:3,exp:'From $\\Phi(B)X_t=\\varepsilon_t$, so $X_t=\\Phi^{-1}(B)\\varepsilon_t=\\Psi(B)\\varepsilon_t$ where $\\Psi(z)\\Phi(z)=1$. Matching coefficients of $z^j$ in $\\Psi(z)(1-\\phi_1 z-\\phi_2 z^2)=1$: $j=0$: $\\psi_0=1$; $j=1$: $\\psi_1-\\phi_1\\psi_0=0\\Rightarrow\\psi_1=\\phi_1$ $j\\ge2$: $\\psi_j=\\phi_1\\psi_{j-1}+\\phi_2\\psi_{j-2}$ The $\\psi_j$ satisfy the same recurrence as $\\gamma(h)$ for the AR(2). Memory Tip: Wold $\\psi_j$ for AR($p$): same recurrence as the AR equation itself, starting with $\\psi_0=1$. “Invert the AR polynomial – same difference equation, new initial conditions.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(1) process with $\\phi=0.8$, the lag at which the ACF first falls below $0.1$ is closest to:',opts:['A) Lag 11','B) Lag 20','C) Lag 5','D) Lag 8'],ans:0,exp:'We need the smallest $h$ such that $0.8^h&lt;0.1$. Taking logarithms: $h\\ln(0.8)&lt;\\ln(0.1)\\Rightarrow h&gt;{\\ln(0.1)}/{\\ln(0.8)}={-2.303}/{-0.223}\\approx10.32$ So $h=11$ is the first integer lag where ACF $&lt;0.1$. Check: $\\rho(10)=0.8^{10}\\approx0.107&gt;0.1$; $\\rho(11)=0.8^{11}\\approx0.086&lt;0.1$ ✓ Memory Tip: For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.8$, the ACF drops slowly. To find the lag for a target $\\rho^*$: $h^*=\\ln(\\rho^*)/\\ln(\\phi)$. The higher the $\\phi$, the longer the “memory.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Yule–Walker equations for an AR(2) in matrix form $\\boldsymbol{\\Gamma}_2\\boldsymbol{\\phi}=\\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_2$ have the Toeplitz matrix $\\boldsymbol{\\Gamma}_2$ equal to:',opts:['A) $\\begin{pmatrix}\\gamma(1)&amp;\\gamma(2)\\\\\\gamma(2)&amp;\\gamma(1)\\end{pmatrix}$','B) $\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;\\rho(1)\\\\\\rho(2)&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}$','C) $\\begin{pmatrix}\\gamma(0)&amp;\\gamma(1)\\\\\\gamma(1)&amp;\\gamma(0)\\end{pmatrix}$','D) $\\begin{pmatrix}\\gamma(0)&amp;\\gamma(2)\\\\\\gamma(1)&amp;\\gamma(0)\\end{pmatrix}$'],ans:2,exp:'The YW equations are $\\gamma(k)=\\phi_1\\gamma(k-1)+\\phi_2\\gamma(k-2)$ for $k=1,2$. In matrix form: $$\\begin{pmatrix}\\gamma(0)&amp;\\gamma(1)\\\\\\gamma(1)&amp;\\gamma(0)\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\phi_1\\\\\\phi_2\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\gamma(1)\\\\\\gamma(2)\\end{pmatrix}$$ Dividing by $\\gamma(0)$ gives the ACF version $\\boldsymbol{R}\\boldsymbol{\\phi}=\\boldsymbol{\\rho}$ where $\\boldsymbol{R}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;\\rho(1)\\\\\\rho(1)&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}$. The matrix is Toeplitz and positive definite (for a proper stationary process). Memory Tip: YW matrix $\\boldsymbol{\\Gamma}_p$: diagonal entries are all $\\gamma(0)$; off-diagonal $(i,j)$ entry is $\\gamma(|i-j|)$. It is symmetric and Toeplitz – same values along each diagonal.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the MA(2) process $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\theta_2\\varepsilon_{t-2}$, the ACF at lag 1, $\\rho(1)$, is:',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{\\theta_1}{1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2}$','B) $\\dfrac{\\theta_1\\theta_2}{1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2}$','C) $\\dfrac{\\theta_1+\\theta_1\\theta_2}{1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2}$','D) $\\dfrac{\\theta_1^2}{1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2}$'],ans:2,exp:'Using the general formula $\\gamma(h)=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{q-h}\\theta_j\\theta_{j+h}$ (with $\\theta_0=1$): $$\\gamma(1)=\\sigma^2(\\theta_0\\theta_1+\\theta_1\\theta_2)=\\sigma^2(\\theta_1+\\theta_1\\theta_2)$$ $$\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2)$$ $$\\rho(1)=\\frac{\\theta_1+\\theta_1\\theta_2}{1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2}=\\frac{\\theta_1(1+\\theta_2)}{1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2}$$ Option (A) is the MA(1) formula – it ignores the $\\theta_2$ term in the numerator. Memory Tip: MA(2) lag-1 ACF: numerator has two terms ($\\theta_1$ and $\\theta_1\\theta_2$) because $\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ appears in both $X_t$ and $X_{t-1}$ via two different paths.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(2) process, the ACF satisfies the recurrence $\\rho(h)=\\phi_1\\rho(h-1)+\\phi_2\\rho(h-2)$ for $h\\ge2$. Given $\\phi_1=0.6$, $\\phi_2=0.2$, and $\\rho(1)=0.75$, the value $\\rho(2)$ is:',opts:['A) $0.60$','B) $0.65$','C) $0.55$','D) $0.70$'],ans:1,exp:'At $h=2$: $\\rho(2)=\\phi_1\\rho(1)+\\phi_2\\rho(0)=0.6(0.75)+0.2(1)=0.45+0.20=0.65$ Note: $\\rho(0)=1$ always (variance of $X_t$ with itself). Once $\\rho(1)$ and $\\rho(2)$ are known, all subsequent values are computed by the recurrence: $\\rho(3)=0.6(0.65)+0.2(0.75)=0.39+0.15=0.54$, and so on. Memory Tip: AR(2) ACF recurrence: $\\rho(h)=\\phi_1\\rho(h-1)+\\phi_2\\rho(h-2)$. Always use $\\rho(0)=1$ as the starting value. Build the ACF sequentially: $\\rho(1)\\to\\rho(2)\\to\\rho(3)\\ldots$'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an MA(1) process, the method of moments estimator of $\\theta$ is obtained by solving:',opts:['A) $\\hat\\rho(1)=\\dfrac{\\hat\\theta}{1+\\hat\\theta^2}$, choosing the invertible root $|\\hat\\theta|&lt;1$','B) $\\hat\\rho(1)=\\hat\\theta^2/(1+\\hat\\theta^2)$','C) $\\hat\\rho(1)=1/(1+\\hat\\theta^2)$','D) $\\hat\\rho(1)=\\hat\\theta$ directly'],ans:0,exp:'Setting $\\rho(1)=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$ equal to the sample $\\hat\\rho(1)$: $$\\hat\\theta^2-\\frac{1}{\\hat\\rho(1)}\\hat\\theta+1=0$$ This quadratic in $\\hat\\theta$ has two roots – one $|\\hat\\theta|&lt;1$ (invertible) and one $|\\hat\\theta|&gt;1$ (non-invertible). By convention, always take the invertible root. Closed form: $\\hat\\theta=\\dfrac{1\\pm\\sqrt{1-4\\hat\\rho(1)^2}}{2\\hat\\rho(1)}$, choosing the root with $|\\hat\\theta|&lt;1$. Memory Tip: MA(1) MOM: solve $\\rho(1)=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$ – a quadratic in $\\theta$. Two roots exist (invertible and non-invertible twins). Always pick $|\\hat\\theta|&lt;1$. Note: MOM can fail if $|\\hat\\rho(1)|&gt;0.5$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARMA(1,1) process, the PACF $\\phi_{hh}$ for large $h$ decays like:',opts:['A) Cuts off sharply after lag 1','B) Remains constant for all $h$','C) Decays geometrically (tails off) at rate governed by $\\phi$ (the AR parameter)','D) Cuts off sharply after lag 2'],ans:2,exp:'For ARMA(1,1) with $|\\phi|&lt;1$, the PACF is approximately $\\phi_{hh}\\approx c\\cdot\\phi^h$ for large $h$, where $c$ depends on both $\\phi$ and $\\theta$. Model | ACF | PACF; AR(1) | Tails off at rate $\\phi^h$ | Cuts off after lag 1; MA(1) | Cuts off after lag 1 | Tails off at rate $(-\\theta)^h$; ARMA(1,1) | Tails off | Tails off Neither ACF nor PACF cuts off for ARMA – both tail off. This is the key identification feature. Memory Tip: “Both tail off = ARMA.” For ARMA(1,1), the rate of decay of the PACF is approximately $\\phi^h$ – the AR part drives the long-term tail behaviour.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'After obtaining Yule–Walker estimates $\\hat\\phi_1,\\ldots,\\hat\\phi_p$ for an AR($p$), the innovation variance $\\sigma^2$ is estimated as:',opts:['A) $\\hat\\sigma^2=1/\\hat\\gamma(0)$','B) $\\hat\\sigma^2=\\hat\\gamma(0)\\left(1-\\sum_{j=1}^{p}\\hat\\phi_j\\hat\\rho(j)\\right)$','C) $\\hat\\sigma^2=\\hat\\gamma(0)$','D) $\\hat\\sigma^2=\\hat\\gamma(0)-\\hat\\gamma(p)$'],ans:1,exp:'From the YW equation at $k=0$ (multiplying $X_t=\\sum_j\\phi_j X_{t-j}+\\varepsilon_t$ by $X_t$ and taking expectations): $$\\gamma(0)=\\phi_1\\gamma(1)+\\cdots+\\phi_p\\gamma(p)+\\sigma^2$$ Rearranging: $\\sigma^2=\\gamma(0)-\\sum_{j=1}^p\\phi_j\\gamma(j)=\\gamma(0)\\!\\left(1-\\sum_{j=1}^p\\phi_j\\rho(j)\\right)$ Substituting sample estimates $\\hat\\gamma,\\hat\\phi,\\hat\\rho$ gives $\\hat\\sigma^2$. Memory Tip: After YW, $\\hat\\sigma^2=\\hat\\gamma(0)(1-\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\phi}}^\\top\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\rho}})$. Think: total variance minus what the AR “explains” = unexplained innovation variance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'From $n=150$ observations, the sample PACF values are: $\\hat\\phi_{11}=0.60$, $\\hat\\phi_{22}=0.25$, $\\hat\\phi_{33}=0.03$, $\\hat\\phi_{44}=0.02$. The $95\\%$ confidence band is approximately $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{150}\\approx\\pm0.16$. The identified AR order is:',opts:['A) AR(4)','B) AR(1)','C) AR(2)','D) AR(3)'],ans:2,exp:'Confidence band: $\\pm0.16$. $|\\hat\\phi_{11}|=0.60&gt;0.16$ ✓ (significant) $|\\hat\\phi_{22}|=0.25&gt;0.16$ ✓ (significant) $|\\hat\\phi_{33}|=0.03&lt;0.16$ \\texttimes (not significant) $|\\hat\\phi_{44}|=0.02&lt;0.16$ \\texttimes (not significant) The PACF cuts off after lag 2 $\\Rightarrow$ AR(2) model identified. The ACF (not shown) should tail off geometrically to confirm. Memory Tip: PACF identification: count the significant spikes – that is your AR order $p$. Here 2 significant, 2 not $\\Rightarrow$ AR(2). Easy as counting!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(1) model $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, the process transitions from stationary to non-stationary (unit root) exactly when:',opts:['A) $\\phi=0.5$','B) $\\phi=2$','C) $\\phi=1$','D) $\\phi=-1$'],ans:2,exp:'The AR polynomial $\\Phi(z)=1-\\phi z$ has root $z=1/\\phi$. Stationarity: $|z|&gt;1\\Leftrightarrow|\\phi|&lt;1$. At $\\phi=1$: root is at $z=1$ (on the unit circle) $\\Rightarrow$ random walk. At $\\phi&gt;1$: root inside unit circle $\\Rightarrow$ explosive (non-stationary in a different way). Note: $\\phi=-1$ also gives a unit root (at $z=-1$), but $\\phi=1$ is the classical boundary we test for. Memory Tip: Think of $\\phi$ as a rubber band strength. $|\\phi|&lt;1$: pulls back (stationary). $\\phi=1$: no restoring force (unit root). $\\phi&gt;1$: explodes away. The boundary is exactly $\\phi=1$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ARMA(2,1) process $(1-0.5B-0.3B^2)X_t=(1+0.4B)\\varepsilon_t$, is the process stationary? Check the AR polynomial roots.',opts:['A) Yes, because $0.5+0.3=0.8&lt;1$ and $0.3-0.5=-0.2&lt;1$ and $|0.3|&lt;1$','B) No, because $|\\phi_1|=0.5&gt;0.4=|\\theta_1|$','C) Yes, because $|\\phi_1|+|\\phi_2|=0.8&lt;1$','D) Cannot be determined without computing exact roots'],ans:0,exp:'For $\\phi_1=0.5$, $\\phi_2=0.3$, the three conditions are: 1. $|\\phi_2|=0.3&lt;1$ ✓ 2. $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=0.5+0.3=0.8&lt;1$ ✓ 3. $\\phi_2-\\phi_1=0.3-0.5=-0.2&lt;1$ ✓ All conditions satisfied $\\Rightarrow$ AR polynomial roots outside unit circle $\\Rightarrow$ stationary. The MA polynomial ($1+0.4B$) only affects invertibility, not stationarity. Memory Tip: For ARMA stationarity: only look at the AR part. Check the AR(2) triangle: $|\\phi_2|&lt;1$, $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&lt;1$, $\\phi_2-\\phi_1&lt;1$. The MA part is irrelevant for stationarity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ARMA(2,1) process with MA polynomial $\\Theta(z)=1+0.4z$, is the process invertible?',opts:['A) Yes, because the root of $1+0.4z=0$ is $z=-2.5$, which satisfies $|z|&gt;1$','B) No, because $0.4&gt;0$ (positive MA coefficient violates invertibility)','C) Yes, because $|0.4|&lt;1$ directly implies invertibility for MA(1)','D) Both (A) and (C) are correct statements leading to the same conclusion'],ans:3,exp:'Method 1 (roots): Root of $\\Theta(z)=1+0.4z=0$ is $z=-1/0.4=-2.5$. Since $|{-2.5}|=2.5&gt;1$, the root is outside the unit circle $\\Rightarrow$ invertible ✓ Method 2 (parameter): For MA(1), invertibility $\\Leftrightarrow |\\theta|&lt;1$. Here $|\\theta|=0.4&lt;1$ $\\Rightarrow$ invertible ✓ Both methods are equivalent and valid. Option (B) is wrong – the sign of $\\theta$ has no bearing on invertibility; only its magnitude matters. Memory Tip: MA(1) invertibility: $|\\theta|&lt;1$ OR root of $\\Theta(z)$ outside unit circle – same thing. For MA($q$): always check roots of $\\Theta(z)$. For MA(1), the simpler $|\\theta|&lt;1$ check suffices.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(1) model with $\\mu=50$, $\\phi=0.7$, and current observation $X_{100}=60$, the 3-step-ahead forecast $\\hat{X}_{103|100}$ is:',opts:['A) $57$','B) $53.43$','C) $54.1$','D) $50$'],ans:1,exp:'At $h=3$: $\\hat{X}_{103|100}=50+0.7^3(60-50)$ $0.7^3=0.343$ $\\hat{X}_{103|100}=50+0.343\\times10=50+3.43=53.43$ The forecast decays toward the mean $\\mu=50$. As $h\\to\\infty$: forecast $\\to50$. Option (D) would only be correct as $h\\to\\infty$; option (A) is the 1-step forecast. Memory Tip: AR(1) forecast: deviation from mean decays by factor $\\phi^h$. At $h=3$, only $\\phi^3=0.343$ of the original deviation remains. Higher $\\phi$: slower decay to mean.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ARMA(2,1) model $X_t=0.5X_{t-1}+0.3X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t+0.4\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ with $X_{100}=5$, $X_{99}=3$, and $\\hat\\varepsilon_{100}=1$, the one-step-ahead forecast $\\hat{X}_{101|100}$ is:',opts:['A) $5.1$','B) $3.8$','C) $4.0$','D) $3.9$'],ans:1,exp:'The 1-step forecast $\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}=E[X_{n+1}|\\mathcal{F}_n]$. Since $\\varepsilon_{n+1}$ is future (mean 0), only the past terms contribute: $$\\hat{X}_{101|100}=\\phi_1 X_{100}+\\phi_2 X_{99}+\\theta_1\\hat\\varepsilon_{100}$$ $=0.5\\times5+0.3\\times3+0.4\\times1=2.5+0.9+0.4=3.8$ Options (D)–(A) result from common slips: forgetting the $\\theta_1\\hat\\varepsilon_{100}$ term, misapplying $\\phi_2$, or adding an extra $0.1$ rounding error. Memory Tip: ARMA 1-step forecast: substitute all known values (past $X$\'s and past estimated $\\hat\\varepsilon$\'s). Future $\\varepsilon$ is replaced by its conditional expectation of 0.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If a time series has observations $\\ldots, 10, 13, 15, 14, 16, \\ldots$ at times $t-2,t-1,t,t+1,t+2$, the second difference $\\nabla^2 X_t = X_t-2X_{t-1}+X_{t-2}$ at time $t$ equals:',opts:['A) $1$','B) $-1$','C) $2$','D) $5$'],ans:1,exp:'$X_{t-2}=10$, $X_{t-1}=13$, $X_t=15$. First differences: $\\nabla X_{t-1}=13-10=3$; $\\nabla X_t=15-13=2$ Second difference: $\\nabla^2 X_t=\\nabla X_t-\\nabla X_{t-1}=2-3=-1$ Equivalently: $\\nabla^2 X_t=X_t-2X_{t-1}+X_{t-2}=15-26+10=-1$ ✓ The negative value indicates the growth is slowing down (deceleration at time $t$). Memory Tip: $\\nabla^2 X_t=$ change in the change. Positive: accelerating; Negative: decelerating. Like the second derivative in calculus – measures curvature of the series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Writing out the ARIMA(1,1,1) model $\\Phi(B)(1-B)X_t=\\Theta(B)\\varepsilon_t$ in terms of $X_t$, $X_{t-1}$, $X_{t-2}$, $\\varepsilon_t$, $\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ gives:',opts:['A) $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$','B) $X_t=(1+\\phi)X_{t-1}-\\phi X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$','C) $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}-\\phi X_{t-2}+X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$','D) $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$'],ans:1,exp:'$(1-\\phi B)(1-B)X_t=(1+\\theta B)\\varepsilon_t$ $(1-B-\\phi B+\\phi B^2)X_t=(1+\\theta B)\\varepsilon_t$ $(1-(1+\\phi)B+\\phi B^2)X_t=(1+\\theta B)\\varepsilon_t$ $X_t-(1+\\phi)X_{t-1}+\\phi X_{t-2}=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ $\\Rightarrow X_t=(1+\\phi)X_{t-1}-\\phi X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ ✓ Memory Tip: Expand ARIMA(1,1,1) by multiplying out $(1-\\phi B)(1-B)=(1-(1+\\phi)B+\\phi B^2)$. The product of the AR and differencing polynomials gives a “combined AR(2)” for the levels.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For ARIMA(0,1,0) – the pure random walk – the series of first differences $W_t=X_t-X_{t-1}$ follows:',opts:['A) AR(1) with $\\phi=1$','B) White noise $\\mathrm{WN}(0,\\sigma^2)$ – completely uncorrelated increments','C) AR(1) with $\\phi=0.5$','D) MA(1) with $\\theta=-1$ (non-invertible)'],ans:1,exp:'ARIMA(0,1,0): $(1-B)X_t=\\varepsilon_t\\Rightarrow X_t-X_{t-1}=\\varepsilon_t\\Rightarrow W_t=\\varepsilon_t$. The increments (first differences) are iid innovations – pure white noise. This is why the random walk is ARIMA(0,1,0): applying the difference operator $(1-B)$ once converts it to ARMA(0,0), i.e., white noise. The ACF of $W_t$ is identically zero for all $h\\ge1$ – the differenced series is WN. Memory Tip: ARIMA(0,1,0) $\\xrightarrow{\\nabla}$ ARMA(0,0) = WN. “The simplest non-stationary model: one difference reveals pure noise.” The random walk\'s differences are truly uncorrelated.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The optimal $h$-step-ahead forecast $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}$ from a stationary ARMA model can be written using the Wold MA($\\infty$) representation as:',opts:['A) $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=0$ for all $h\\ge1$','B) $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_{j+h}\\hat\\varepsilon_{n-j}$ (weighted past innovations)','C) $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{n+h-j}$ (future innovations)','D) $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=X_n$ for all $h\\ge1$'],ans:1,exp:'From Wold: $X_{n+h}=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{n+h-j}=\\underbrace{\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{n+h-j}}_{\\text{future innovations (unpredictable)}}+\\underbrace{\\sum_{j=h}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{n+h-j}}_{\\text{past innovations (known)}}$ Taking conditional expectation: $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=\\sum_{j=h}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\hat\\varepsilon_{n+h-j}=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_{k+h}\\hat\\varepsilon_{n-k}$ The forecast uses past innovations with “shifted” Wold coefficients $\\psi_h,\\psi_{h+1},\\ldots$ Memory Tip: $h$-step forecast = drop the first $h$ Wold coefficients and use the rest on past innovations. Forecast error $=\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{n+h-j}$ – only the “dropped” terms.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'As the forecast horizon $h\\to\\infty$, the width of the 95% prediction interval for a stationary ARMA process:',opts:['A) Converges to zero (perfect prediction eventually)','B) Converges to $2\\times1.96\\times\\sqrt{\\gamma(0)}$ (twice the unconditional standard deviation)','C) Grows without bound (infinite uncertainty)','D) Stays at $\\pm1.96\\sigma$ for all $h$ (constant width)'],ans:1,exp:'Forecast error variance: $\\sigma_h^2=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\psi_j^2\\to\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j^2=\\gamma(0)$ as $h\\to\\infty$. So the PI half-width $=1.96\\sqrt{\\sigma_h^2}\\to1.96\\sqrt{\\gamma(0)}$, and total width $\\to2\\times1.96\\sqrt{\\gamma(0)}$. This makes intuitive sense: the long-run forecast converges to the mean, and we are essentially as uncertain as the unconditional distribution of $X_t$. Contrast: for ARIMA (integrated), $\\sigma_h^2$ grows without bound – no ceiling. Memory Tip: Stationary ARMA: PI width $\\to$ unconditional 95% interval (a finite ceiling). Integrated ARIMA: PI width $\\to\\infty$ (no ceiling). Stationarity = bounded long-run uncertainty.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(1) process with $\\phi=0.6$ and $\\sigma^2=4$, the 2-step forecast error variance $\\sigma_2^2$ is:',opts:['A) $4\\times2=8$','B) $4/(1-0.36)=6.25$','C) $4(1+0.36)=5.44$','D) $4\\times0.36=1.44$'],ans:2,exp:'The 2-step error: $X_{n+2}-\\hat{X}_{n+2|n}=\\varepsilon_{n+2}+\\phi\\varepsilon_{n+1}$ (Using $\\psi_0=1$, $\\psi_1=\\phi$) $\\sigma_2^2=\\mathrm{Var}(\\varepsilon_{n+2}+\\phi\\varepsilon_{n+1})=\\sigma^2(1+\\phi^2)=4(1+0.36)=5.44$ In general: $\\sigma_h^2=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\phi^{2j}=\\sigma^2\\dfrac{1-\\phi^{2h}}{1-\\phi^2}$ At $h=2$: $\\sigma_2^2=\\sigma^2(1+\\phi^2)$ ✓ Memory Tip: AR(1) $h$-step forecast error variance: $\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\phi^{2j}$. At $h=1$: just $\\sigma^2$; at $h=2$: $\\sigma^2(1+\\phi^2)$; as $h\\to\\infty$: $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=\\gamma(0)$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density $f(\\omega)$ of a stationary process can be interpreted as:',opts:['A) The variance contribution per unit frequency bandwidth – how variance is distributed across frequencies','B) The rate of change of the ACVF at lag $\\omega$','C) The probability of observing a value at frequency $\\omega$','D) The conditional mean of $X_t$ given the frequency $\\omega$'],ans:0,exp:'From the inversion formula: $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=\\gamma(0)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)\\,d\\omega$ The variance contribution from the frequency band $[\\omega_1,\\omega_2]$ is $\\int_{\\omega_1}^{\\omega_2}f(\\omega)\\,d\\omega$. High $f(\\omega)$ at low frequencies $\\Rightarrow$ most variance from slow oscillations (smooth, persistent series). High $f(\\omega)$ at high frequencies $\\Rightarrow$ most variance from rapid oscillations (noisy, oscillating series). Like decomposing sunlight into its colours – the spectral density shows which “frequencies” dominate. Memory Tip: Spectral density = “variance budget by frequency.” The area under $f(\\omega)$ from $a$ to $b$ = variance from cycles with those frequencies. Total area = total variance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) with $\\theta=-0.7$ (negative), the spectral density $f(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}(1+0.49+2(-0.7)\\cos\\omega)$ is highest at:',opts:['A) $\\omega=0$ (zero frequency / lowest frequency)','B) $\\omega=\\pi/2$','C) Equal at all frequencies','D) $\\omega=\\pi$ (Nyquist frequency / highest frequency)'],ans:3,exp:'$f(\\omega)\\propto1.49-1.4\\cos\\omega$. To maximise, minimise $\\cos\\omega$. $\\cos\\omega$ is minimised at $\\omega=\\pi$ ($\\cos\\pi=-1$): $f(\\pi)\\propto1.49+1.4=2.89$ At $\\omega=0$: $f(0)\\propto1.49-1.4=0.09$ (minimum!) Rule: MA(1) with $\\theta&lt;0$: spectrum peaks at $\\omega=\\pi$ (high-frequency oscillations dominate $\\Rightarrow$ series alternates rapidly). With $\\theta&gt;0$: peaks at $\\omega=0$ (slow, smooth series). Memory Tip: MA(1): $\\theta&gt;0\\Rightarrow$ spectrum peaks at $\\omega=0$ (red/smooth). $\\theta&lt;0\\Rightarrow$ peaks at $\\omega=\\pi$ (blue/wiggly). The sign of $\\theta$ tells you whether the spectrum is “warm” or “cold.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.5$ and $\\sigma^2=1$, the spectral density at $\\omega=\\pi/2$ (where $\\cos(\\pi/2)=0$) is:',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1-0.5)^2}=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(0.25)}=\\dfrac{2}{\\pi}$','B) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}$ (same as white noise)','C) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1-0.25+0)}=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(0.75)}\\approx\\dfrac{0.424}{\\pi}$','D) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1+0.25)}=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1.25)}=\\dfrac{0.8}{2\\pi}$'],ans:3,exp:'$f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi(1+\\phi^2-2\\phi\\cos\\omega)}$ At $\\omega=\\pi/2$: $\\cos(\\pi/2)=0$, so the middle cross-term vanishes: $$f(\\pi/2)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi(1+0.25-0)}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi\\times1.25}=\\frac{0.8}{2\\pi}$$ This is between $f(0)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi(0.5)^2}=\\frac{1}{0.5\\pi}$ (largest) and $f(\\pi)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi(1.5)^2}=\\frac{1}{4.5\\pi}$ (smallest). Memory Tip: At $\\omega=\\pi/2$: $\\cos=0$, so the cross-term $2\\phi\\cos\\omega$ disappears. Denominator $=1+\\phi^2$. Simple! Intermediate frequency lies between the $\\omega=0$ peak and $\\omega=\\pi$ trough.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARMA(1,1) process, whether the spectrum $f(\\omega)$ is monotone decreasing from $\\omega=0$ to $\\omega=\\pi$ depends on:',opts:['A) Whether the process is stationary','B) The relative magnitude of $\\phi$ and $\\theta$ – the spectrum shape reflects the combined AR (denominator) and MA (numerator) effects','C) Only the AR parameter $\\phi$; the MA parameter $\\theta$ has no effect on spectrum shape','D) The sign of $\\sigma^2$ only'],ans:1,exp:'$f(\\omega)\\propto\\dfrac{1+\\theta^2+2\\theta\\cos\\omega}{1+\\phi^2-2\\phi\\cos\\omega}$ At $\\omega=0$: $\\propto(1+\\theta)^2/(1-\\phi)^2$; at $\\omega=\\pi$: $\\propto(1-\\theta)^2/(1+\\phi)^2$. The spectrum is monotone iff the numerator and denominator both change monotonically in the same direction – but with general $\\phi,\\theta$, the spectrum can have a more complex shape with a peak at an intermediate frequency. Memory Tip: ARMA(1,1) spectrum = MA spectrum $\\div$ AR spectrum. MA part shapes the numerator, AR part shapes the denominator. Together they can create peaks at non-trivial frequencies – richer than pure AR or MA.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The periodogram $I(\\omega_k)$ is the sample analog of the spectral density. As the sample size $n\\to\\infty$, the expected value $E[I(\\omega_k)]$ converges to:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2/(2\\pi)$ always','B) $f(\\omega_k)$ (the true spectral density)','C) $n\\cdot f(\\omega_k)$','D) $0$'],ans:1,exp:'The periodogram is the sample Fourier transform of the sample ACVF, which estimates the population ACVF. As $n\\to\\infty$, the sample ACVF $\\hat\\gamma(h)\\to\\gamma(h)$, so: $$E[I(\\omega_k)]\\to\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{h}\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega h}=f(\\omega_k)$$ However, the variance of $I(\\omega_k)$ does not go to zero – the periodogram is asymptotically unbiased but not consistent. Smoothing is needed for consistency. Memory Tip: Periodogram: unbiased (mean is right) but noisy (variance stays large). Like an unbiased but imprecise instrument. Smooth it (Bartlett, Parzen, Daniell) to get a consistent estimate.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) process $X_t=\\phi_1 X_{t-1}+\\phi_2 X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$, the spectral density is:',opts:['A) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi|1-\\phi_1 e^{-i\\omega}-\\phi_2 e^{-2i\\omega}|^2}$','B) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2(1+\\phi_1+\\phi_2)^2}{2\\pi}$','C) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}|1-\\phi_1 e^{-i\\omega}-\\phi_2 e^{-2i\\omega}|^2$','D) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi(\\phi_1^2+\\phi_2^2)}$'],ans:0,exp:'For AR($p$): $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi|\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}$ where $\\Phi(z)=1-\\phi_1z-\\phi_2z^2$. $\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega})=1-\\phi_1e^{-i\\omega}-\\phi_2e^{-2i\\omega}$ Option (C) has the AR polynomial in the numerator (wrong – that would be an MA spectrum). The AR polynomial must appear in the denominator because AR processes generate peaks (poles) in the spectrum. Memory Tip: AR spectrum: $\\sigma^2/(2\\pi|\\Phi|^2)$ – AR polynomial in bottom. MA spectrum: $\\sigma^2|\\Theta|^2/(2\\pi)$ – MA polynomial on top. Remember: AR is Reciprocal (goes in denominator).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $Y_t=X_t+X_{t-1}$ (a simple two-point moving average filter applied to $\\{X_t\\}$), the spectral density of $\\{Y_t\\}$ is:',opts:['A) $f_Y(\\omega)=f_X(\\omega)+f_X(\\omega)=2f_X(\\omega)$','B) $f_Y(\\omega)=f_X(\\omega)/|1+e^{-i\\omega}|^2$','C) $f_Y(\\omega)=|1+e^{-i\\omega}|^2 f_X(\\omega)=2(1+\\cos\\omega)f_X(\\omega)$','D) $f_Y(\\omega)=f_X(\\omega)$ (unchanged by linear filtering)'],ans:2,exp:'The filter is $Y_t=(1+B)X_t$, so the transfer function is $A(e^{-i\\omega})=1+e^{-i\\omega}$. Power gain: $|1+e^{-i\\omega}|^2=(1+e^{-i\\omega})(1+e^{i\\omega})=2+2\\cos\\omega=2(1+\\cos\\omega)$ $f_Y(\\omega)=2(1+\\cos\\omega)f_X(\\omega)$ Note: at $\\omega=0$: gain $=4$ (doubles amplitude, quadruples power – boosts low frequencies). At $\\omega=\\pi$: gain $=0$ (completely removes the Nyquist frequency – eliminates rapid alternations). Memory Tip: Two-point MA filter boosts low freq (smooth) and kills $\\omega=\\pi$ (alternating). It is a low-pass filter. Its spectral gain is $2(1+\\cos\\omega)$, which is zero at $\\omega=\\pi$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a time series of length $n=100$ (even), the number of distinct periodogram ordinates at Fourier frequencies $\\omega_k=2\\pi k/n$ for $k=1,\\ldots,n/2$ is:',opts:['A) 99','B) 50','C) 49','D) 100'],ans:1,exp:'For even $n$: Fourier frequencies are $\\omega_k=2\\pi k/n$, $k=0,1,\\ldots,n/2$. $k=0$: corresponds to the mean ($I(0)$ excluded from spectral analysis). $k=n/2=50$: the Nyquist frequency $\\omega=\\pi$. Useful ordinates: $k=1$ to $k=n/2$, giving exactly $n/2=50$ frequencies. Note: due to symmetry $I(\\omega_k)=I(2\\pi-\\omega_k)$, the upper half ($k&gt;n/2$) is a mirror image of the lower half – no new information. Memory Tip: For sample size $n$: there are $n/2$ useful periodogram ordinates (for even $n$). Half the data gives the spectrum due to Hermitian symmetry. The mean ($k=0$) is excluded.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'To obtain a consistent estimator of the spectral density, the smoothing bandwidth $m$ must satisfy which condition as $n\\to\\infty$?',opts:['A) $m=\\sqrt{n}$ exactly (no other option works)','B) $m\\to\\infty$ and $m/n\\to0$ (bandwidth grows, but slower than sample size)','C) $m$ stays fixed (e.g., $m=5$ for all $n$)','D) $m=n/2$ (use all Fourier ordinates)'],ans:1,exp:'Smoothed estimator: $\\hat{f}(\\omega)\\approx\\frac{1}{2m+1}\\sum_{|j|\\le m}I(\\omega_j)$ Variance $\\approx\\dfrac{f(\\omega)^2}{m}$: need $m\\to\\infty$ to drive variance to 0. Bias: if $m$ grows too fast ($m/n\\not\\to0$), we average over a wide band covering frequencies where $f$ changes, introducing bias. Optimal rate: $m\\propto n^{1/5}$ (for smooth spectra), giving optimal variance-bias balance. Memory Tip: Smoothing bandwidth: $m\\to\\infty$ (reduces variance) but $m/n\\to0$ (controls bias). Think: “grow the window, but don\'t cover the whole frequency axis.” Optimal: $m\\propto n^{1/5}$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'After fitting an ARMA model in the Box–Jenkins framework, if the Ljung–Box test on residuals is significant (small $p$-value), the appropriate action is:',opts:['A) Switch from MLE to Yule–Walker estimation','B) Return to the identification stage and consider a model with higher AR or MA order','C) Increase the sample size','D) Accept the model regardless, since some significant lags are expected by chance'],ans:1,exp:'A significant Ljung–Box test means residuals have remaining autocorrelation – the model hasn\'t captured all the time series structure. Actions: – Inspect residual ACF/PACF: which lags are significant? – Increase $p$ (if residual PACF cuts off at new lag) or $q$ (if residual ACF cuts off) – Or reconsider $d$ (check for remaining non-stationarity) The Box–Jenkins approach is explicitly iterative: Identify $\\to$ Estimate $\\to$ Diagnose $\\to$ re-Identify if needed. Memory Tip: Significant Ljung–Box = “residuals still have structure” = “model is not done yet.” Go back and look at residual ACF/PACF. It will tell you which lags to add.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If after applying one difference ($d=1$), the sample ACF of $\\nabla X_t$ decays quickly and the ADF test on $\\nabla X_t$ strongly rejects the unit root null, then:',opts:['A) $d=2$ is needed (one difference was not enough)','B) $d=1$ is the correct choice (one difference was sufficient)','C) $d=0$ is correct (no differencing was needed in the first place)','D) The model cannot be identified without a larger sample'],ans:1,exp:'Two diagnostic signals confirm $d=1$: 1. ACF of $\\nabla X_t$ decays quickly: consistent with stationarity (no slow decay). 2. ADF strongly rejects unit root in $\\nabla X_t$: formally confirms stationarity. $\\Rightarrow$ $\\nabla X_t$ is stationary $\\Rightarrow$ $X_t\\sim I(1)$ $\\Rightarrow$ use $d=1$ in the ARIMA model. If the ADF on $\\nabla X_t$ had NOT rejected, $d=2$ might be considered. Memory Tip: Stop differencing when: (1) ACF decays quickly, AND (2) ADF rejects unit root. Both signals together confirm stationarity. Then identify $p,q$ from the ACF/PACF of $\\nabla^d X_t$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a series with $n=200$, two models are fitted: ARIMA(1,1,0) with AIC$=350$ and ARIMA(2,1,0) with AIC$=348$. Which should be selected, and why?',opts:['A) ARIMA(1,1,0): always prefer the simpler model','B) Both models are identical in terms of fit','C) ARIMA(2,1,0): it has lower AIC, suggesting the extra AR term improves fit enough to justify the additional parameter','D) ARIMA(1,1,0): it uses fewer parameters so BIC would always prefer it'],ans:2,exp:'AIC $=-2\\ln\\hat L+2k$. Lower AIC = preferred model. ARIMA(2,1,0) AIC $=348&lt;350$ = ARIMA(1,1,0) AIC $\\Rightarrow$ prefer ARIMA(2,1,0). AIC already penalises the extra parameter (AIC increases by 2 per extra parameter). Since AIC still decreased by 2 despite the penalty, the likelihood improvement ($\\Delta\\ln L=2$) more than compensated. Note: BIC would use $\\ln(200)\\approx5.3$ per parameter, so BIC difference $\\approx348+5.3-(350+0)=3.3$ – BIC might prefer ARIMA(1,1,0) in this case. Memory Tip: Always pick the model with lower AIC (or BIC). If AIC drops by adding a parameter: that parameter is “worth it” by AIC\'s criterion. AIC and BIC can disagree – report both!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Is the MA(2) process with $\\theta_1=0.5$ and $\\theta_2=-0.4$ invertible? Check the three triangle conditions.',opts:['A) No: $\\theta_2=-0.4&lt;0$ violates the condition that all $\\theta_i&gt;0$','B) No: $\\theta_2-\\theta_1=-0.9&lt;-1$ violates the condition','C) Yes, because $|\\theta_1|&lt;1$ and $|\\theta_2|&lt;1$ individually','D) Yes: $|\\theta_2|=0.4&lt;1$; $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=0.1&lt;1$; $\\theta_2-\\theta_1=-0.9&lt;1$ – all satisfied'],ans:3,exp:'For $\\theta_1=0.5$, $\\theta_2=-0.4$: 1. $|\\theta_2|=0.4&lt;1$ ✓ 2. $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=0.5+(-0.4)=0.1&lt;1$ ✓ 3. $\\theta_2-\\theta_1=-0.4-0.5=-0.9&lt;1$ ✓ (note: condition is $\\theta_2-\\theta_1&lt;1$, not $&lt;-1$) All three satisfied $\\Rightarrow$ invertible. Option (B) misread the condition (need $&lt;1$, not $&lt;-1$). Memory Tip: MA(2) invertibility triangle: same as AR(2) stationarity triangle with $\\theta$ replacing $\\phi$. The three conditions are $|\\theta_2|&lt;1$, $\\theta_1+\\theta_2&lt;1$, $\\theta_2-\\theta_1&lt;1$. All three must hold.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ARMA(1,1) process $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$, the ACF at lag 1 is:',opts:['A) $\\rho(1)=\\phi+\\theta$','B) $\\rho(1)=\\dfrac{\\theta}{1+\\theta^2}$','C) $\\rho(1)=\\phi$','D) $\\rho(1)=\\dfrac{(1+\\phi\\theta)(\\phi+\\theta)}{1+\\theta^2+2\\phi\\theta}$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(1)=\\phi\\gamma(0)+\\theta\\sigma^2$ (from multiplying the ARMA equation by $X_{t-1}$ and taking expectations). $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2\\dfrac{1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2}{1-\\phi^2}$ (standard ARMA(1,1) variance formula). $\\rho(1)=\\dfrac{\\gamma(1)}{\\gamma(0)}=\\phi+\\dfrac{\\theta\\sigma^2}{\\gamma(0)}=\\dfrac{(1+\\phi\\theta)(\\phi+\\theta)}{1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2}$ Options (C) is the pure AR(1) answer; (B) is the pure MA(1) answer. Memory Tip: ARMA(1,1) ACF at lag 1 involves both $\\phi$ and $\\theta$ in a non-trivial way. For $h\\ge2$: $\\rho(h)=\\phi^{h-1}\\rho(1)$ – pure geometric decay governed by $\\phi$ alone.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a random walk ARIMA(0,1,0) with $\\sigma^2=9$ and last observation $X_{50}=100$, the 95% prediction interval for $X_{54}$ (4-step ahead) is approximately:',opts:['A) $100\\pm1.96\\times6=100\\pm11.76$','B) $100\\pm1.96\\times12=100\\pm23.52$','C) $100\\pm1.96\\times3=100\\pm5.88$','D) $100\\pm1.96\\times9=100\\pm17.64$'],ans:0,exp:'For ARIMA(0,1,0): forecast $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=X_n=100$ for all $h$. Forecast error variance: $h\\sigma^2=4\\times9=36$. Forecast std dev: $\\sqrt{36}=6$. 95% PI: $100\\pm1.96\\times6=100\\pm11.76$. The PI widens as $\\sqrt{h}$ – at $h=4$: width $=2\\times1.96\\times3\\sqrt{4}=2\\times11.76=23.52$ total width. Memory Tip: RW forecast std dev $=\\sigma\\sqrt{h}$. Here $\\sigma=3$ (from $\\sigma^2=9$), $h=4$: std dev $=3\\sqrt{4}=6$. The PI fans out like a cone: width proportional to $\\sqrt{h}$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Two AR(1) processes share $\\sigma^2=1$ but differ: Process A has $\\phi=0.9$ and Process B has $\\phi=0.3$. Which statement is correct?',opts:['A) Both have the same variance since $\\sigma^2=1$','B) Process A has larger variance $\\gamma(0)$ and longer memory (slower ACF decay)','C) Process A and B have the same ACF but different variances','D) Process B has larger variance since $\\phi=0.3&gt;0$'],ans:1,exp:'Process A ($\\phi=0.9$) | Process B ($\\phi=0.3$); $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$ | $1/0.19\\approx5.26$ | $1/0.91\\approx1.10$; ACF at lag 10 | $0.9^{10}\\approx0.35$ | $0.3^{10}\\approx0.000006$; Memory | Long | Short Higher $\\phi\\Rightarrow$ stronger persistence $\\Rightarrow$ larger variance $\\Rightarrow$ slower ACF decay. Memory Tip: Same $\\sigma^2$ but different $\\phi$: higher $\\phi\\Rightarrow$ higher variance (amplification factor $1/(1-\\phi^2)$ increases). The AR coefficient amplifies innovation noise into a smoother, more persistent series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Wold\'s theorem guarantees that every stationary process has an MA($\\infty$) representation. The practical importance for modelling is:',opts:['A) The theorem only applies to Gaussian processes, limiting its usefulness','B) We must always fit an MA($\\infty$) model in practice','C) ARMA models are parsimonious finite-parameter approximations to the infinite MA($\\infty$) guaranteed by Wold, making ARMA modelling theoretically justified','D) Wold\'s theorem means all time series are white noise after sufficient differencing'],ans:2,exp:'Wold says: every stationary process $=$ MA($\\infty$) (infinitely many parameters $\\psi_0,\\psi_1,\\psi_2,\\ldots$). We can\'t estimate infinitely many parameters. ARMA($p,q$) approximates the MA($\\infty$) using only $p+q$ parameters via a rational function $\\Theta(z)/\\Phi(z)$: $\\Psi(z)=\\Theta(z)/\\Phi(z)\\approx\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j z^j$ The Box–Jenkins paradigm rests entirely on this approximation idea. Memory Tip: Wold gives the “why” of ARMA modelling. Without Wold, we wouldn\'t know ARMA is a valid approximation. With Wold: “every stationary series has an MA spine; ARMA is just a compact way to describe it.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A GDP series found to be $I(1)$ (unit root process) implies which of the following about the effect of a one-time economic shock?',opts:['A) The shock has a permanent effect – GDP never returns to its original level (impulse response = 1 for all future periods)','B) The shock causes GDP to oscillate indefinitely around the pre-shock level','C) The shock has a temporary effect – GDP returns to its pre-shock trend after some time','D) The shock has no effect on GDP at all'],ans:0,exp:'For ARIMA(0,1,0): $X_t=X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t\\Rightarrow X_{t+h}=X_t+\\sum_{j=1}^h\\varepsilon_{t+j}$. A shock $\\varepsilon_t=1$ permanently shifts the level of GDP: $\\partial X_{t+h}/\\partial\\varepsilon_t=1$ for all $h\\ge0$. This is why the random walk has a “unit impulse response” – every shock is permanently embedded. Contrast with stationary AR(1): $\\partial X_{t+h}/\\partial\\varepsilon_t=\\phi^h\\to0$ (transient shocks). Memory Tip: $I(1)$ = “permanent memory.” Every shock moves the long-run level permanently. $I(0)$ = “temporary memory.” Shocks fade. This is the economic substance of unit root testing.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a series of length $n=400$, the 95% confidence band for the sample ACF under the white noise null is $\\pm$:',opts:['A) $\\pm0.196$','B) $\\pm0.049$','C) $\\pm0.098$','D) $\\pm0.01$'],ans:2,exp:'Under the WN null: $\\hat\\rho(h)\\approx N(0,1/n)$, so the 95% band is $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{n}$. At $n=400$: $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{400}=\\pm1.96/20=\\pm0.098$ Larger samples give narrower bands – you can detect smaller autocorrelations. Contrast: at $n=100$: $\\pm0.196$ (twice as wide). At $n=1600$: $\\pm0.049$. Memory Tip: ACF band width $\\propto1/\\sqrt{n}$. Double the sample size: band shrinks by $1/\\sqrt{2}\\approx0.707$. Quadruple: band halves. More data = more power to detect autocorrelation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A time series $X_t=\\sin(0.1t)+\\varepsilon_t$ (deterministic sine wave plus white noise) violates weak stationarity because:',opts:['A) The autocovariance depends on the lag in a non-linear way','B) The mean $E[X_t]=\\sin(0.1t)$ changes over time (is not constant)','C) The process is not Gaussian','D) The variance changes over time'],ans:1,exp:'Weak stationarity requires: (i) $E[X_t]=\\mu$ (constant); (ii) $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)&lt;\\infty$ (constant); (iii) $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h})$ depends only on $h$. Here: $E[X_t]=\\sin(0.1t)$ varies with time $\\Rightarrow$ condition (i) fails. The variance: $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2$ (constant, from WN part) – condition (ii) holds. The ACVF: $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h})=\\sigma^2\\cdot\\mathbf{1}_{h=0}$ – depends only on $h$ – condition (iii) holds. Only the mean condition fails. Remedy: subtract $\\sin(0.1t)$ (detrend) to get stationary residuals. Memory Tip: A deterministic periodic mean makes the process non-stationary. Removing it (detrending, or modelling the mean explicitly) restores stationarity of the residuals.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In an ARIMA(1,1,0) model fitted to monthly sales data, $\\hat\\phi_1=0.4$ (for the differenced series $\\nabla X_t$). This means:',opts:['A) 40% of the level of sales last month predicts this month\'s level','B) 40% of the sales error last month predicts this month\'s sales','C) 40% of the change in sales last month ($\\nabla X_{t-1}$) predicts this month\'s change ($\\nabla X_t$)','D) This month\'s sales will always be 40 units above last month\'s'],ans:2,exp:'ARIMA(1,1,0): $\\nabla X_t=\\phi_1\\nabla X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ $(X_t-X_{t-1})=0.4(X_{t-1}-X_{t-2})+\\varepsilon_t$ The AR(1) component operates on the changes (differences), not the levels. So $\\hat\\phi_1=0.4$ means last month\'s change positively predicts this month\'s change (momentum effect). If sales grew by 10 last month, this model predicts growth of $0.4\\times10=4$ this month (from the AR part alone). Memory Tip: In ARIMA($p,d,q$), the AR and MA coefficients act on the differenced series, not the original levels. Always read the model equation carefully to understand what each parameter does.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The sample autocovariance function $\\hat\\gamma(h)$ for a series $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$ is defined as:',opts:['A) $\\hat\\gamma(h)=\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^{n-h}(x_{t+h}-\\bar{x})(x_t-\\bar{x})$','B) $\\hat\\gamma(h)=\\dfrac{1}{n-h}\\sum_{t=1}^{n-h}(x_{t+h}-\\bar{x})(x_t-\\bar{x})$ (unbiased denominator)','C) $\\hat\\gamma(h)=\\hat\\gamma(0)\\hat\\rho(h)$','D) All of (A), (B), and (C) are in common use'],ans:3,exp:'(A) Divisor $n$: MLE, guarantees positive semi-definite (PSD) ACVF matrix. Standard in time series software. (B) Divisor $n-h$: Unbiased estimator of $\\gamma(h)$, but ACVF matrix may not be PSD for large $h$. (C): Directly from the definition of ACF: $\\hat\\rho(h)=\\hat\\gamma(h)/\\hat\\gamma(0)$, so $\\hat\\gamma(h)=\\hat\\gamma(0)\\hat\\rho(h)$ is always true. In practice, divisor $n$ (option A) is the standard for time series ACF computation. Memory Tip: Two common divisors: $n$ (biased but PSD) and $n-h$ (unbiased but may not be PSD). Use $n$ for ACF computation in time series to guarantee a valid covariance structure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For small sample sizes ($n=30$), when comparing AR(1) and AR(2) models using information criteria, which criterion is more conservative (tends to select the simpler model)?',opts:['A) AICc is most conservative','B) BIC (has $\\ln n=\\ln30\\approx3.4$ penalty vs. AIC\'s penalty of 2)','C) Both penalise equally for all $n$','D) AIC (penalises more in small samples)'],ans:1,exp:'At $n=30$: $\\ln(30)\\approx3.4&gt;2$. For adding one AR parameter (going AR(1)$\\to$AR(2)): AIC penalty increase: $2$ BIC penalty increase: $\\ln(30)\\approx3.4$ BIC requires a larger likelihood improvement to prefer the more complex model $\\Rightarrow$ more conservative (parsimonious). For large $n$: $\\ln n$ grows, making BIC increasingly conservative. At $n=e^2\\approx7.4$: both equal; for $n&gt;8$: BIC is always more conservative than AIC. Memory Tip: BIC = bigger penalty = more conservative. At $n=30$: AIC charges 2 per param; BIC charges 3.4. BIC “prefers simpler” more aggressively than AIC, especially as $n$ grows.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Two different ARMA models can have the same spectral density. This happens when:',opts:['A) The two models have the same mean $\\mu$','B) The two models have the same AR order $p$','C) A non-invertible MA(1) with $\\theta$ and its invertible twin with $1/\\theta$ (and rescaled $\\sigma^2$) are different models sharing the same spectral density','D) This cannot happen – each spectral density corresponds to a unique ARMA model'],ans:2,exp:'For MA(1): $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}|1+\\theta e^{-i\\omega}|^2$. If we replace $\\theta$ with $1/\\theta$ and $\\sigma^2$ with $\\theta^2\\sigma^2$: $f\'(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\theta^2\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}|1+(1/\\theta)e^{-i\\omega}|^2=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}|\\theta+(e^{-i\\omega})|^2=f(\\omega)$ ✓ This is why invertibility is imposed as an identification constraint – to choose one unique model from the two candidates with the same ACF and spectrum. Memory Tip: Non-invertible MA and invertible twin: same ACF, same spectrum. Invertibility is an identification constraint, not a physical necessity. We impose it to get a unique model.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the Box–Jenkins methodology, the estimation stage aims to:',opts:['A) Find the values of $p$, $q$ that minimise AIC from the ACF/PACF plots','B) Plot residuals and check for white noise patterns','C) Determine $d$ (the differencing order) by applying ADF tests','D) Obtain precise estimates of the ARMA parameters ($\\boldsymbol\\phi$, $\\boldsymbol\\theta$, $\\sigma^2$) for the tentative model identified in the identification stage, typically via MLE'],ans:3,exp:'Stage | Goal | Tools; 1. Identification | Choose $p,d,q$ | ACF, PACF, unit root tests; 2. Estimation | Fit parameters | MLE, YW, conditional LS; 3. Diagnostic | Check adequacy | Ljung–Box, residual ACF; 4. Forecasting | Predict future | Conditional expectation Options (C), (A), (B) describe other stages (identification and diagnostic), not estimation. Memory Tip: Box–Jenkins stages: “Identify, Estimate, Diagnose, Forecast” = IEDF. Each stage feeds the next; if diagnosis fails, return to identification.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A spectral density with a large peak at $\\omega\\approx\\pi/6$ (corresponding to period $=2\\pi/(\\pi/6)=12$ time units) in monthly data suggests:',opts:['A) The series is white noise','B) The series is non-stationary','C) The series has a dominant cycle with a period of approximately 12 months (annual seasonality)','D) The series has very low variance'],ans:2,exp:'Period $=2\\pi/\\omega$. At $\\omega=\\pi/6$: Period $=2\\pi/(\\pi/6)=12$ months. A peak in the spectral density at this frequency means the series has strong variation with a 12-month (annual) cycle – typical of temperature, tourism, retail sales, etc. Sub-annual harmonics also appear at $\\omega=2\\pi/6=\\pi/3$ (6-month cycle), $\\omega=\\pi/2$ (4-month), etc. This is how frequency domain analysis reveals seasonality – more precisely and completely than the time domain ACF. Memory Tip: Period $=2\\pi/\\omega$. For monthly data: annual cycle at $\\omega=2\\pi/12=\\pi/6$; semi-annual at $\\omega=2\\pi/6=\\pi/3$. Peaks in the spectrum directly reveal the dominant cycles.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the random walk $X_t=X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, why is $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=X_n$ the optimal (MMSE) forecast for all $h\\ge1$?',opts:['A) Because $X_n$ is always the largest observed value','B) Because the random walk has no autocorrelation at any lag','C) Because all future innovations $\\varepsilon_{n+1},\\ldots,\\varepsilon_{n+h}$ have conditional expectation zero, so $E[X_{n+h}|\\mathcal{F}_n]=E[X_n+\\sum_{j=1}^h\\varepsilon_{n+j}|\\mathcal{F}_n]=X_n$','D) Because the random walk always reverts to its starting value'],ans:2,exp:'$X_{n+h}=X_n+\\underbrace{\\varepsilon_{n+1}+\\cdots+\\varepsilon_{n+h}}_{\\text{future innovations}}$ $E[X_{n+h}|\\mathcal{F}_n]=X_n+\\sum_{j=1}^h E[\\varepsilon_{n+j}|\\mathcal{F}_n]=X_n+0=X_n$ This is the MMSE forecast – no model can do better (in the class of linear forecasts or Gaussian processes). The future innovations are genuinely unpredictable by definition. Memory Tip: RW forecast = current value. Why? Because all future steps are random (mean zero), so our best guess for where we\'ll end up is where we are now. “You can\'t predict luck.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARMA($p,q$) process with AR polynomial $\\Phi(z)$ and MA polynomial $\\Theta(z)$, the spectral density at $\\omega=0$ simplifies to:',opts:['A) $f(0)=\\sigma^2|\\Theta(1)|^2$','B) $f(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}|\\Phi(1)|^2$ (only AR polynomial matters at $\\omega=0$)','C) $f(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\dfrac{|\\Theta(1)|^2}{|\\Phi(1)|^2}$ (evaluate both polynomials at $z=1$)','D) $f(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}$ (same as white noise)'],ans:2,exp:'At $\\omega=0$: $e^{-i\\cdot0}=1$, so: $f(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\dfrac{|\\Theta(e^{0})|^2}{|\\Phi(e^{0})|^2}=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\dfrac{|\\Theta(1)|^2}{|\\Phi(1)|^2}$ For AR(1) with $\\phi$: $\\Phi(1)=1-\\phi$, $\\Theta(1)=1$, so $f(0)=\\sigma^2/[2\\pi(1-\\phi)^2]$ ✓ For MA(1) with $\\theta$: $\\Phi(1)=1$, $\\Theta(1)=1+\\theta$, so $f(0)=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta)^2/(2\\pi)$ ✓ Memory Tip: To find $f(0)$: plug $z=1$ into both polynomials. $\\Phi(1)=1-\\sum\\phi_j$ and $\\Theta(1)=1+\\sum\\theta_j$. These are the “DC gain” of the AR and MA filters.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A sample ACF shows $\\hat\\rho(1)=-0.45$ (significantly negative). For a model with a single lag-1 autocorrelation, which model is most consistent?',opts:['A) ARIMA(0,1,0)','B) MA(1) with $\\theta&lt;0$ (since $\\rho(1)=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)&lt;0$ for $\\theta&lt;0$)','C) AR(1) with $\\phi&gt;0$','D) AR(1) with $\\phi=0$'],ans:1,exp:'The observed pattern: significant negative $\\hat\\rho(1)$, and presumably $\\hat\\rho(h)\\approx0$ for $h&gt;1$ (a single spike that cuts off). This is the signature of MA(1) with $\\theta&lt;0$ – ACF has exactly one non-zero lag, and that lag is negative when $\\theta&lt;0$. AR(1) with $\\phi&lt;0$ would also give $\\rho(1)&lt;0$, but it would show alternating ACF at all lags (tailing off), not a clean cutoff. Memory Tip: Negative single ACF spike (with cutoff at lag 1) $\\Rightarrow$ MA(1) with $\\theta&lt;0$. If ACF tails off with alternating signs $\\Rightarrow$ AR(1) with $\\phi&lt;0$. The cutoff pattern distinguishes them.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an MA(2) process $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\theta_2\\varepsilon_{t-2}$, the 2-step ahead forecast $\\hat{X}_{n+2|n}$ is:',opts:['A) $\\theta_2\\hat\\varepsilon_n$ (only the $\\theta_2$ term survives for the 2-step ahead)','B) $\\theta_1\\hat\\varepsilon_n+\\theta_2\\hat\\varepsilon_{n-1}$','C) $\\hat\\varepsilon_n+\\theta_1\\hat\\varepsilon_{n-1}+\\theta_2\\hat\\varepsilon_{n-2}$','D) $0$ (mean forecast for all $h\\ge3$, not $h=2$)'],ans:0,exp:'$X_{n+2}=\\varepsilon_{n+2}+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{n+1}+\\theta_2\\varepsilon_n$ $E[X_{n+2}|\\mathcal{F}_n]$: $E[\\varepsilon_{n+2}|\\mathcal{F}_n]=0$ (future), $E[\\varepsilon_{n+1}|\\mathcal{F}_n]=0$ (future), $E[\\varepsilon_n|\\mathcal{F}_n]=\\hat\\varepsilon_n$ (known). $\\hat{X}_{n+2|n}=0+0+\\theta_2\\hat\\varepsilon_n=\\theta_2\\hat\\varepsilon_n$ Note: for the 3-step ahead $\\hat{X}_{n+3|n}=0$ since all three innovations are future. Memory Tip: MA($q$): $h$-step forecast $=\\sum_{j=h}^{q}\\theta_j\\hat\\varepsilon_{n+h-j}$. For $h=q$: only the last $\\theta_q$ term survives. For $h&gt;q$: forecast is 0 (mean). Each step “drops” one more coefficient.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=-0.8$, the spectral density $f(\\omega)=\\sigma^2/[2\\pi(1+0.64+1.6\\cos\\omega)]$ is smallest at $\\omega=$:',opts:['A) $\\omega=\\pi/4$','B) $\\omega=\\pi/2$','C) $\\omega=\\pi$','D) $\\omega=0$'],ans:3,exp:'$f(\\omega)\\propto1/(1.64+1.6\\cos\\omega)$. $f$ is minimised when the denominator is maximised, i.e., when $\\cos\\omega$ is maximised. $\\cos\\omega$ is maximised at $\\omega=0$ ($\\cos0=1$): denominator $=1.64+1.6=3.24$. $f(0)\\propto1/3.24$ (minimum). Conversely, $f(\\pi)\\propto1/0.04$ (maximum – the spectrum peaks at the Nyquist frequency, confirming the oscillating nature of negative-$\\phi$ AR). Memory Tip: AR(1): $f(\\omega)\\propto 1/(1+\\phi^2-2\\phi\\cos\\omega)$. Denominator maximised $\\Rightarrow f$ minimised. For $\\phi&lt;0$: denominator $+2|\\phi|\\cos\\omega$, maximised at $\\omega=0$. So $f$ is smallest at $\\omega=0$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary process, the spectral density at frequency zero satisfies $f(0)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)$. For an AR(1) with $\\phi=0.5$ and $\\sigma^2=2$, compute $2\\pi f(0)$:',opts:['A) $2\\times(1+0.5)=3$','B) $2/(1-0.5)^2=2/(0.25)=8$','C) $2/(1-0.25)=2.67$','D) $2/(0.5)=4$'],ans:1,exp:'$2\\pi f(0)=\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)=\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\frac{\\sigma^2\\phi^{|h|}}{1-\\phi^2}=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{1-\\phi^2}\\left(1+2\\sum_{h=1}^{\\infty}\\phi^h\\right)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{1-\\phi^2}\\cdot\\frac{1+\\phi}{1-\\phi}=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{(1-\\phi)^2}$ At $\\phi=0.5$, $\\sigma^2=2$: $2\\pi f(0)=2/(1-0.5)^2=2/0.25=8$. Note: $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=2/0.75\\approx2.67$ (option B is $\\gamma(0)$, not $2\\pi f(0)$). Memory Tip: Long-run variance $=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi)^2$. Note $(1-\\phi)^2$, NOT $(1-\\phi^2)=$ (which is $\\gamma(0)$ related). This is always larger than $\\gamma(0)$ for $\\phi&gt;0$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The practical consequence of ergodicity for a time series analyst is:',opts:['A) The analyst needs many repeated observations from the same time period to estimate parameters','B) Ergodicity guarantees Gaussian innovations','C) A single long time series contains sufficient information to estimate population parameters (mean, ACVF) consistently','D) Only the most recent observations are informative; older observations should be discarded'],ans:2,exp:'In practice we observe one realisation of a time series. Without ergodicity, we could never estimate $\\mu=E[X_t]$ or $\\gamma(h)=\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h})$ from a single path. Ergodicity ensures: $\\hat\\mu=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum X_t\\to\\mu$ and $\\hat\\gamma(h)\\to\\gamma(h)$ as $n\\to\\infty$. All classical time series estimation methods (YW, MLE, etc.) implicitly assume ergodicity – without it, parameter estimation from a single series would be impossible. Memory Tip: Ergodicity $=$ the entire probabilistic story is encoded in one long time series. “The past tells you about the future and the population.” Without ergodicity: statistics from time series is fundamentally impossible.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The $h$-step ahead forecast MSE (mean squared prediction error) for a stationary ARMA process is $\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\psi_j^2$. For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.5$ and $\\sigma^2=1$, the 3-step MSE is:',opts:['A) $1+0.25+0.125=1.375$','B) $1+0.25+0.0625=1.3125$','C) $1+0.5+0.25=1.75$','D) $3$'],ans:1,exp:'For AR(1): $\\psi_j=\\phi^j$. At $\\phi=0.5$: $\\psi_0=1$, $\\psi_1=0.5$, $\\psi_2=0.25$. 3-step MSPE $=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{2}\\psi_j^2=1(1^2+0.5^2+0.25^2)=1+0.25+0.0625=1.3125$ Option (C) mistakenly uses $\\psi_j$ instead of $\\psi_j^2$. Option (A) mixes $\\psi$ and $\\psi^2$. Memory Tip: MSPE $=\\sigma^2\\sum\\psi_j^2$ – Wold coefficients squared. For AR(1): $\\psi_j=\\phi^j$, so MSPE at $h=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\phi^{2j}$. Note squares throughout.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The principle of parsimony in the Box–Jenkins methodology states:',opts:['A) The model with the highest log-likelihood is always the most parsimonious','B) Among models that adequately represent the data, prefer the one with the fewest parameters','C) Parsimony applies only to the AR order, not the MA order','D) Always fit the highest possible AR and MA orders to capture all dynamics'],ans:1,exp:'Overfitting risks: Too many parameters $\\Rightarrow$ model fits noise not signal $\\Rightarrow$ poor forecast out-of-sample. Practical benefits of parsimony: – Easier to estimate (fewer parameters, more reliable estimates) – Better out-of-sample forecasting performance – Easier to interpret AIC/BIC operationalise parsimony by penalising complexity. Parsimony is the reason ARMA($p,q$) with small $p,q$ is preferred over a high-order AR. Memory Tip: “Occam\'s Razor for time series.” The simplest model that passes diagnostics is the best model. More parameters $\\neq$ better model – they may just be fitting noise.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Box and Jenkins recommend computing and displaying the sample ACF up to a maximum lag of approximately:',opts:['A) $h_{\\max}=n-1$ (use all available lags)','B) $h_{\\max}=\\sqrt{n}$','C) $h_{\\max}=n/4$ (one quarter of the sample)','D) $h_{\\max}=10$ always (regardless of $n$)'],ans:2,exp:'At lag $h$: sample ACVF uses only $n-h$ data pairs. For large $h$, very few pairs $\\Rightarrow$ high variance $\\Rightarrow$ unreliable ACF estimates. At $h=n/4$: still $3n/4$ pairs available – a reasonable balance. At $h=n-1$: only 1 pair – completely meaningless. For $n=100$: display ACF to lag 25. For $n=200$: to lag 50. This gives enough lags to see the ACF pattern without the noisy “tail.” Memory Tip: Display ACF to $n/4$ lags. Beyond that: too few data pairs, too much variance. The right side of a correlogram is unreliable – treat it with caution.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'After one differencing of an ARIMA(2,1,0) series, the ACF and PACF of $W_t=\\nabla X_t$ should show:',opts:['A) ACF decays geometrically (or with damped oscillations), PACF cuts off after lag 2 – consistent with AR(2)','B) ACF cuts off after lag 2, PACF decays – consistent with MA(2)','C) Both ACF and PACF within confidence bands – consistent with white noise','D) Both ACF and PACF cut off after lag 2 – consistent with ARMA(2,2)'],ans:0,exp:'ARIMA(2,1,0): $\\Phi(B)(1-B)X_t=\\varepsilon_t$ After differencing: $(1-\\phi_1B-\\phi_2B^2)W_t=\\varepsilon_t$ – this is AR(2) applied to $W_t$. AR(2) has: ACF that tails off (possibly with oscillations if complex roots), PACF that cuts off after lag 2. The PACF of $W_t$ will show exactly 2 significant spikes ($\\hat\\phi_{11}$ and $\\hat\\phi_{22}$, both non-zero) and then drop to near zero. Memory Tip: ARIMA($p,d,q$) $\\xrightarrow{\\nabla^d}$ ARMA($p,q$). ARIMA(2,1,0) $\\to$ AR(2). AR(2) identification: PACF cuts off at 2, ACF decays. Always difference first, then identify from the differenced series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\{X_t\\}$ is white noise with $E[X_t^4]&lt;\\infty$, what can be said about the spectral density of $Y_t=X_t^2$?',opts:['A) The spectral density of $Y_t$ cannot be defined since $Y_t$ is always non-negative','B) $Y_t=X_t^2$ may not be white noise: $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t^2,X_{t-h}^2)\\ne0$ in general for $h\\ne0$','C) $f_Y(\\omega)=f_X(\\omega)^2$ (square of the WN spectral density)','D) $Y_t=X_t^2$ is itself white noise with a flat spectrum, provided $X_t$ is iid'],ans:3,exp:'If $X_t\\overset{iid}{\\sim}(0,\\sigma^2)$, then $X_t^2$ are also iid (any measurable function of iid observations is iid). $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t^2,X_{t-h}^2)=E[X_t^2 X_{t-h}^2]-E[X_t^2]^2=E[X_t^2]E[X_{t-h}^2]-(E[X_t^2])^2=0$ for $h\\ne0$ (by independence). So $Y_t=X_t^2$ is WN $\\Rightarrow$ flat spectrum. Option (B) would be relevant if $X_t$ is WN but NOT iid (e.g., ARCH processes where $X_t$ are uncorrelated but dependent). Memory Tip: iid WN: $X_t^2$ is also iid WN (flat spectrum). WN but not iid (ARCH): $X_t^2$ may be correlated (non-flat spectrum). This distinction is key in financial time series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Two MA(1) processes: one with $\\theta=3$ ($\\sigma^2=1$) and one with $\\theta=1/3$ ($\\sigma^2=9$) have the same second-order properties (same ACF and spectral density). How does the invertibility condition resolve this ambiguity?',opts:['A) We choose the one with the larger $\\sigma^2$ for numerical stability','B) We choose the invertible model ($|\\theta|&lt;1$, i.e., $\\theta=1/3$) – the unique representation with a convergent AR($\\infty$) form','C) We choose $\\theta=3$ since larger $|\\theta|$ means more autocorrelation','D) Both are equally valid; we always report both to the audience'],ans:1,exp:'Both $\\theta=3$, $\\sigma^2=1$ and $\\theta=1/3$, $\\sigma^2=9$ give: $$\\rho(1)=\\frac{\\theta}{1+\\theta^2}=\\frac{3}{10}=0.3\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\frac{1/3}{1+1/9}=\\frac{1/3}{10/9}=\\frac{3}{10}=0.3$$ Identical ACF ✓. The invertibility condition ($|\\theta|&lt;1$) uniquely selects $\\theta=1/3$ as the identified model. For the invertible model: $\\varepsilon_t=X_t-\\frac{1}{3}X_{t-1}+\\frac{1}{9}X_{t-2}-\\ldots$ (convergent AR representation). Memory Tip: Invertibility $=$ identification constraint. Two MA models can share ACF; invertibility ($|\\theta|&lt;1$) picks the unique one. Without this convention, MA model parameters are not identifiable from data alone.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary ARMA process with mean $\\mu=20$, as the forecast horizon $h\\to\\infty$, the forecast $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}\\to$:',opts:['A) $0$','B) $\\bar{X}_n$ (the sample mean)','C) $X_n$ (the last observed value)','D) $\\mu=20$ (the unconditional population mean)'],ans:3,exp:'For stationary ARMA: $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=\\mu+\\sum_{j=h}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\hat\\varepsilon_{n+h-j}$. As $h\\to\\infty$: $\\sum_{j=h}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\hat\\varepsilon_{n+h-j}\\to0$ (since $\\sum\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$, the tail of the sum vanishes). $\\Rightarrow\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}\\to\\mu$. This is the statistical definition of mean reversion – the unconditional mean $\\mu$ (not the sample mean $\\bar{X}_n$) is the long-run anchor. Memory Tip: Stationary ARMA: long-run forecast $\\to\\mu$ (population mean). The “rubber band” always pulls back. As $h\\to\\infty$: all memory of the current state vanishes, leaving only $\\mu$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For ARIMA(1,1,0) with drift $\\delta=2$ and $\\phi=0.6$, the forecast for large horizons $h\\to\\infty$ converges to a linear function of $h$ with slope:',opts:['A) Slope $=0$ (no trend in the forecast)','B) Slope $=\\phi\\delta=1.2$','C) Slope $=\\delta/(1-\\phi)=2/0.4=5$','D) Slope $=\\delta=2$'],ans:2,exp:'The model: $\\nabla X_t=\\delta+\\phi\\nabla X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$. As $h\\to\\infty$: the AR(1) part in the differences reaches its unconditional mean $\\delta/(1-\\phi)$. The level forecast eventually grows at rate $\\delta/(1-\\phi)=2/(1-0.6)=5$ per period. At $h=1$: slope $\\approx\\delta+\\phi\\delta=\\delta(1+\\phi)$; asymptotically: $\\delta/(1-\\phi)$ (geometric series sum). Memory Tip: ARIMA(1,1,0) with drift: long-run slope $=\\delta/(1-\\phi)$. The AR memory amplifies the drift. With $\\phi=0.6$: drift of 2 per step becomes trend of 5 per step in the long run.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2) with $\\theta_1=0.6$, $\\theta_2=0.3$, and $\\sigma^2=1$, compute $\\rho(2)$:',opts:['A) $0.3/1.36\\approx0.221$','B) $0.6/1.45\\approx0.414$','C) $0.3/(1+0.36+0.09)=0.3/1.45\\approx0.207$','D) $0.09/1.45\\approx0.062$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2)=1(1+0.36+0.09)=1.45$ $\\gamma(2)=\\sigma^2\\theta_2=1\\times0.3=0.3$ $\\rho(2)=\\gamma(2)/\\gamma(0)=0.3/1.45\\approx0.207$ Also: $\\gamma(1)=\\sigma^2\\theta_1(1+\\theta_2)=0.6\\times1.3=0.78\\Rightarrow\\rho(1)=0.78/1.45\\approx0.538$. $\\rho(3)=\\rho(4)=\\cdots=0$ (MA(2) cutoff at lag 2). ✓ Memory Tip: MA(2) formulas: $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2)$; $\\gamma(2)=\\sigma^2\\theta_2$. At lag $q$: only the last MA coefficient $\\theta_q$ contributes to $\\gamma(q)$. Simple pattern!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For ARIMA(0,1,2): $\\nabla X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\theta_2\\varepsilon_{t-2}$, the 3-step forecast $\\hat{X}_{n+3|n}$ equals:',opts:['A) $X_n+(\\theta_1+\\theta_2)\\hat\\varepsilon_n+\\theta_2\\hat\\varepsilon_{n-1}$','B) $X_n+\\theta_1\\hat\\varepsilon_n+\\theta_2\\hat\\varepsilon_{n-1}$ (the 1-step forecast)','C) $X_n+\\hat\\varepsilon_n$','D) $X_n$ (all MA memory assumed exhausted immediately)'],ans:0,exp:'$\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}=X_n+\\theta_1\\hat\\varepsilon_n+\\theta_2\\hat\\varepsilon_{n-1}$ (1-step) $\\hat{X}_{n+2|n}=\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}+\\theta_2\\hat\\varepsilon_n=X_n+(\\theta_1+\\theta_2)\\hat\\varepsilon_n+\\theta_2\\hat\\varepsilon_{n-1}$ (2-step: only the $\\theta_2$ term is added) $\\hat{X}_{n+3|n}=\\hat{X}_{n+2|n}+0=\\hat{X}_{n+2|n}$ (3-step: MA(2) memory exhausted – no new term is added) Since the differenced series is MA(2), the forecast of $\\nabla X_{n+3}$ is 0 for $h\\ge3$, so $\\hat{X}_{n+3|n}=\\hat{X}_{n+2|n}=X_n+(\\theta_1+\\theta_2)\\hat\\varepsilon_n+\\theta_2\\hat\\varepsilon_{n-1}$. For all $h\\ge2$: $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=\\hat{X}_{n+2|n}$ – a constant forecast level (no further change predicted). Option (D) is the common error of assuming the level forecast collapses all the way to $X_n$. Memory Tip: ARIMA(0,1,$q$): the level forecast flattens (becomes constant) only after $h=q$ steps – but it flattens at whatever value $\\hat{X}_{n+q|n}$ reached, not back at $X_n$. The MA memory runs out at lag $q$; the accumulated changes up to that point remain.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'After fitting ARIMA(1,1,1) to a series, the residual ACF shows a significant spike at lag 12 (monthly data). This suggests:',opts:['A) There is unmodelled seasonality at the annual cycle – consider adding a seasonal component to the model (e.g., SARIMA)','B) The differencing order $d$ should be increased to $d=2$','C) The MA order should be increased from $q=1$ to $q=12$','D) The model is adequate; one significant lag is always expected by chance'],ans:0,exp:'A significant spike in the residual ACF at lag 12 (for monthly data) means the ARIMA(1,1,1) model has not captured the annual seasonal pattern. Next steps: – Inspect residual ACF at lags 12, 24, 36 (seasonal lags) – If seasonal spikes present: extend to SARIMA$(1,1,1)\\times(P,D,Q)_{12}$ – Apply seasonal differencing $(1-B^{12})$ if seasonal unit root present Option (C) (MA(12)) would be very over-parameterised; SARIMA is the parsimonious solution. Memory Tip: Residual ACF spike at seasonal lag $s$ = seasonal pattern missed. The fix is a seasonal ARIMA extension, not just raising non-seasonal $p$ or $q$. Seasonal structure requires seasonal terms.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(1) process $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ ($|\\phi|&lt;1$), the first difference $W_t=\\nabla X_t=X_t-X_{t-1}$ has ACF at lag 1:',opts:['A) $\\rho_W(1)=(\\phi-1)/(2(1-\\phi+\\phi^2/(1-\\phi)))$ (complex expression)','B) $\\rho_W(1)=-(1-\\phi)/(2-\\phi^2/(1-\\phi))$','C) $\\rho_W(1)=-1/2$','D) $\\rho_W(1)=\\phi/(1+\\phi^2)$'],ans:2,exp:'$W_t=X_t-X_{t-1}=(1-B)X_t$. Transfer function $A(\\omega)=1-e^{-i\\omega}$. $\\gamma_W(h)=\\gamma_X(h)-2\\gamma_X(h-1)+\\gamma_X(h-2)$ (for AR(1): $\\gamma_X(h)=\\sigma^2\\phi^{|h|}/(1-\\phi^2)$). $\\gamma_W(0)=\\sigma^2(2-2\\phi)/(1-\\phi^2)=2\\sigma^2/(1+\\phi)$ $\\gamma_W(1)=\\sigma^2(\\phi-1-\\phi+\\phi^2+\\phi-\\phi^2)/(1-\\phi^2)=\\sigma^2(\\phi-1)/(1-\\phi^2)=-\\sigma^2/(1+\\phi)$ $\\rho_W(1)=\\gamma_W(1)/\\gamma_W(0)=(-\\sigma^2/(1+\\phi))/(2\\sigma^2/(1+\\phi))=-1/2$ Notice the $\\phi$-dependence cancels completely – this exact value $-0.5$ holds for every stationary AR(1), which is why it is such a reliable diagnostic. Memory Tip: Differencing a stationary AR(1) gives $\\rho_W(1)=-0.5$ (regardless of $\\phi$!) for the differenced series – a signal of overdifferencing. If you see $\\hat\\rho(1)\\approx-0.5$: you\'ve differenced once too many.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The ACF recurrence for AR(2), $\\rho(h)=\\phi_1\\rho(h-1)+\\phi_2\\rho(h-2)$, requires starting values. Which starting values are used?',opts:['A) $\\rho(0)=\\sigma^2$ and $\\rho(1)=\\sigma^2\\phi_1$','B) $\\rho(0)=\\phi_1$ and $\\rho(1)=\\phi_2$','C) $\\rho(0)=1$ and $\\rho(1)=\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)$ (from the YW equation at $k=1$)','D) $\\rho(0)=0$ and $\\rho(1)=\\phi_1$'],ans:2,exp:'YW at $k=1$: $\\rho(1)=\\phi_1\\rho(0)+\\phi_2\\rho(-1)=\\phi_1+\\phi_2\\rho(1)$ (using $\\rho(0)=1$ and $\\rho(-1)=\\rho(1)$). Solving: $\\rho(1)(1-\\phi_2)=\\phi_1\\Rightarrow\\rho(1)=\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)$ Then: $\\rho(2)=\\phi_1\\rho(1)+\\phi_2=\\phi_1^2/(1-\\phi_2)+\\phi_2$, and all subsequent $\\rho(h)$ from the recurrence. Memory Tip: AR(2) ACF: start with $\\rho(0)=1$ and $\\rho(1)=\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)$. Then use the recurrence $\\rho(h)=\\phi_1\\rho(h-1)+\\phi_2\\rho(h-2)$ to generate all subsequent values.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2) with $\\theta_1=1$, $\\theta_2=-1$, $\\sigma^2=1$, the spectral density at $\\omega=\\pi$ (where $\\cos\\pi=-1$ and $\\cos(2\\pi)=1$) is:',opts:['A) $f(\\pi)=0$ (the spectrum has a zero here)','B) $f(\\pi)=\\dfrac{3}{2\\pi}$','C) $f(\\pi)=\\dfrac{5}{2\\pi}$','D) $f(\\pi)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega})=1+\\theta_1e^{-i\\omega}+\\theta_2e^{-2i\\omega}$. At $\\omega=\\pi$: $e^{-i\\pi}=-1$, $e^{-2i\\pi}=1$: $\\Theta(e^{-i\\pi})=1+(1)(-1)+(-1)(1)=1-1-1=-1$ $f(\\pi)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\left|\\Theta(e^{-i\\pi})\\right|^2=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}|-1|^2=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}$ Note this MA(2) is a special case: since $\\theta_1(1+\\theta_2)=1\\times0=0$, the $\\cos\\omega$ term vanishes entirely from $|\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2=1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2+2\\theta_1(1+\\theta_2)\\cos\\omega+2\\theta_2\\cos2\\omega$, leaving $f(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}(3-2\\cos2\\omega)$. This gives $f(0)=f(\\pi)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}$ (equal at both endpoints) and a peak of $\\frac{5}{2\\pi}$ at $\\omega=\\pi/2$ – option (C) is that peak value, a common distractor from evaluating at the wrong frequency. Memory Tip: To evaluate the MA spectrum at any $\\omega$: substitute $e^{-i\\omega}$ directly into $\\Theta(z)$, then compute $|\\cdot|^2$ and multiply by $\\sigma^2/(2\\pi)$. A spectral zero occurs only when $\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega})=0$ exactly – here it is $-1$, not $0$, so $f(\\pi)\\ne0$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'When an ARMA(2,1) model is tentatively fitted and the estimate $\\hat\\phi_2\\approx0$ with a very large standard error, the most likely explanation is:',opts:['A) The model is over-parameterised – an ARMA(1,1) is likely sufficient, and $\\hat\\phi_2$ is fitting noise','B) The series is non-stationary and requires differencing','C) The MA order should be increased from $q=1$ to $q=3$','D) The model is under-parameterised – more AR terms are needed'],ans:0,exp:'When a parameter estimate is near zero with a large standard error (or equivalently, a small $t$-ratio), it is statistically indistinguishable from zero – the parameter is not needed. Decision: Compare ARMA(2,1) and ARMA(1,1) using AIC or BIC. If $\\hat\\phi_2\\approx0$: – ARMA(1,1) AIC will likely be lower (fewer parameters, similar fit) – Drop $\\phi_2$ and proceed with the simpler ARMA(1,1) This is parsimony in action: don\'t retain parameters that don\'t earn their place. Memory Tip: Insignificant parameter $\\approx$ over-parameterisation. Check AIC of the reduced model. Large SE means the data cannot distinguish $\\hat\\phi_2$ from zero – so drop it and simplify.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A process $\\{X_t\\}$ is strictly (strongly) stationary if the joint distribution of $(X_{t_1},\\ldots,X_{t_k})$ equals that of $(X_{t_1+h},\\ldots,X_{t_k+h})$ for all $t_1,\\ldots,t_k,h$. Which statement correctly relates this to weak (second-order) stationarity?',opts:['A) Neither implies the other under any circumstances','B) Weak stationarity always implies strict stationarity','C) Strict and weak stationarity are unrelated concepts','D) Strict stationarity (with finite second moments) implies weak stationarity; the converse need not hold, but the two are equivalent for a Gaussian process'],ans:3,exp:'Strict stationarity fixes the entire joint distribution under time shifts, so in particular the mean, variance, and all covariances are shift-invariant $\\Rightarrow$ weak stationarity holds, provided $E[X_t^2]&lt;\\infty$. The converse fails in general: weak stationarity only restricts the first two moments, not the full distribution (e.g., higher moments could still vary with $t$). Special case: for a Gaussian process, the joint distribution is completely determined by its means and covariances, so weak stationarity $\\Rightarrow$ strict stationarity too. The two notions coincide. Memory Tip: Strict $\\supset$ Weak in general (strict is the stronger condition); they coincide only for Gaussian processes. Most ARMA theory works directly with weak stationarity since only first/second moments are needed.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A series is believed to follow an MA(1) process with $\\theta=0.5$, so $\\rho(1)=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)=0.4$ and $\\rho(j)=0$ for $j\\ge2$. Using Bartlett\'s formula $\\mathrm{Var}(\\hat\\rho(k))\\approx\\dfrac{1}{n}\\!\\left[1+2\\sum_{j=1}^{1}\\rho(j)^2\\right]$ for $k\\ge2$, with $n=100$, the approximate standard error of $\\hat\\rho(5)$ is:',opts:['A) $\\approx0.115$','B) $\\approx0.100$','C) $\\approx0.204$','D) $\\approx0.132$'],ans:0,exp:'For $k&gt;q=1$: $\\mathrm{Var}(\\hat\\rho(k))\\approx\\dfrac{1}{n}\\!\\left[1+2\\rho(1)^2\\right]=\\dfrac{1}{100}\\left[1+2(0.4)^2\\right]=\\dfrac{1}{100}(1+0.32)=0.0132$ $\\mathrm{SE}(\\hat\\rho(5))=\\sqrt{0.0132}\\approx0.115$ Option (B), the naive $1/\\sqrt{n}=0.1$, ignores the true MA(1) structure and understates the correct variability – this is exactly why Bartlett\'s formula (not the plain white-noise band) must be used when testing residual/sample ACF significance beyond an already-identified MA order. Memory Tip: Bartlett\'s formula: $\\mathrm{Var}(\\hat\\rho(k))\\approx\\frac{1}{n}[1+2\\sum_{j=1}^{q}\\rho(j)^2]$ for $k&gt;q$. Setting all $\\rho(j)=0$ recovers the familiar white-noise band $1/n$ – Bartlett\'s formula is the general case.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ADF test regression $\\nabla X_t=\\alpha+\\gamma X_{t-1}+\\sum_{i=1}^{p}\\delta_i\\nabla X_{t-i}+\\varepsilon_t$, testing $H_0:\\gamma=0$ (unit root) against $H_1:\\gamma&lt;0$ (stationary), which statement about the test statistic $\\hat\\gamma/\\mathrm{SE}(\\hat\\gamma)$ is correct?',opts:['A) Under $H_0$ it follows a standard $t$-distribution, so ordinary $t$-tables apply','B) A large negative $\\hat\\gamma$ always confirms a unit root is present','C) The test is invalid whenever augmented lags ($p&gt;0$) are included','D) Under $H_0$ it follows a non-standard, left-skewed Dickey–Fuller distribution, so the (more negative) DF/MacKinnon critical values must be used instead of $t$-table values'],ans:3,exp:'Under $H_0:\\gamma=0$, $X_{t-1}$ is itself non-stationary (a random walk), so the usual asymptotic theory behind the $t$-distribution (which assumes stationary regressors) breaks down. Dickey and Fuller derived the correct (functional-of-Brownian-motion) limiting distribution by simulation; it is skewed left of zero. Consequently the 5% critical value (constant-only case, large $n$) is around $-2.86$, more negative than the familiar $-1.645$ one-sided normal value – using ordinary $t$-tables would over-reject $H_0$ (make it too easy to conclude stationarity). The ADF version simply adds lagged $\\nabla X_{t-i}$ terms to whiten residual autocorrelation; it does not change the limiting distribution\'s non-standard nature. Memory Tip: “DF, not $t$.” Under a unit-root null, always use Dickey–Fuller/MacKinnon critical values, never the ordinary $t$-table – the null itself makes the regressor non-stationary, invalidating standard theory.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An ARMA(1,1) model is fitted, and the Ljung–Box statistic $Q_{LB}=n(n+2)\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\frac{\\hat\\rho_e(k)^2}{n-k}$ is computed on the residuals using $m=10$ lags. Under $H_0$ (model adequate), $Q_{LB}$ is compared to a $\\chi^2$ distribution with how many degrees of freedom?',opts:['A) $10$','B) $12$','C) $9$','D) $8$ ($=m-p-q$)'],ans:3,exp:'The Ljung–Box (and Box–Pierce) portmanteau statistic tests whether the first $m$ residual autocorrelations are jointly zero. Fitting an ARMA($p,q$) model already “uses up” $p+q$ degrees of freedom to estimate $\\phi_1,\\ldots,\\phi_p,\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_q$, which mechanically shrinks the residual autocorrelations toward zero. The correct reference distribution accounts for this: $Q_{LB}\\overset{a}{\\sim}\\chi^2_{m-p-q}$ under $H_0$. Here $p=1$, $q=1$, $m=10\\Rightarrow df=10-1-1=8$. Using $df=m=10$ (option B) would make the test too conservative (too hard to reject an inadequate model). Memory Tip: Ljung–Box degrees of freedom $=m-p-q$, never just $m$. Always subtract the number of ARMA parameters estimated (excluding any constant/mean term, which is usually not counted).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the random walk with drift $X_t=\\delta+X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ with $\\delta=1.5$, $\\sigma^2=4$, and $X_{50}=200$, the 4-step-ahead point forecast $\\hat{X}_{54|50}$ is:',opts:['A) $206$','B) $200$','C) $203$','D) $212$'],ans:0,exp:'Iterating: $X_{n+h}=X_n+h\\delta+\\sum_{j=1}^{h}\\varepsilon_{n+j}$. Taking conditional expectation (future $\\varepsilon$\'s vanish): $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=X_n+h\\delta=200+4(1.5)=206$. The forecast error variance is $h\\sigma^2=4\\times4=16$ (same formula as the pure random walk – the deterministic drift term $h\\delta$ adds no uncertainty, only a level shift). Unlike the driftless random walk (which forecasts flat at $X_n$), the forecast here follows a straight line with slope $\\delta$. Memory Tip: Random walk with drift: point forecast $=X_n+h\\delta$ (linear trend); forecast error variance $=h\\sigma^2$ (same as driftless case). The drift shifts the level, not the uncertainty.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A sample correlogram shows $\\hat\\rho(h)$ remaining above $0.7$ even at lag $h=20$, decaying only very gradually and almost linearly, well outside the $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{n}$ confidence band throughout. This pattern most strongly suggests:',opts:['A) An MA(1) process with $\\theta$ close to $-1$','B) The series is white noise','C) Non-stationarity (e.g., a unit root or strong trend); differencing should be considered before ARMA identification','D) A stationary AR(1) process with small $|\\phi|$ (weak, short-lived persistence)'],ans:2,exp:'For a stationary AR(1), $\\rho(h)=\\phi^h$ decays geometrically – even with $\\phi=0.9$, $\\rho(20)=0.9^{20}\\approx0.12$, already inside typical confidence bands. Option (D)\'s “small $|\\phi|$” would decay even faster, so it cannot produce $\\hat\\rho(20)&gt;0.7$. A near-linear, very slow decay that stays large for many lags is instead the hallmark of a unit root / strong trend: for a random walk, $\\rho(h)\\to1$ for all fixed $h$ as $n\\to\\infty$, and empirically the sample ACF decays almost linearly rather than geometrically. Action: take first differences and re-examine the correlogram of $\\nabla X_t$ before attempting ARMA identification. Memory Tip: Geometric (fast) ACF decay $\\to$ stationary AR. Near-linear, persistently high ACF decay $\\to$ non-stationary; difference first. This is one of the most common Box–Jenkins identification checks.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For quarterly time series data, estimating the trend using a simple 4-term moving average is problematic because:',opts:['A) A 4-term (even-order) moving average is not centred on any single time point; a further 2-term MA is applied to `re-centre\' it, giving the standard $2\\times4$-MA with weights $\\tfrac18,\\tfrac14,\\tfrac14,\\tfrac14,\\tfrac18$','B) Even-order moving averages cannot be computed at all for quarterly data','C) A 4-term MA is always superior to a 12-term MA, regardless of data frequency','D) Moving averages of any order remove all cyclical variation completely, so trend cannot be separated from cycle'],ans:0,exp:'A simple 4-term MA, $\\frac{1}{4}(X_{t-1}+X_t+X_{t+1}+X_{t+2})$, is centred at $t+0.5$ – between two observations, not on one. To align the trend estimate with an actual time point, average two consecutive 4-term MAs (centred at $t-0.5$ and $t+0.5$), which centres the result at $t$: $$\\hat{T}_t=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\mathrm{MA}_4(t-0.5)+\\mathrm{MA}_4(t+0.5)\\big]=\\tfrac18 X_{t-2}+\\tfrac14 X_{t-1}+\\tfrac14 X_t+\\tfrac14 X_{t+1}+\\tfrac18 X_{t+2}$$ This $2\\times4$-MA is the standard centred trend estimator for quarterly data (analogously, $2\\times12$-MA for monthly data). Memory Tip: Odd-order MA (e.g., 3-term, 5-term): already centred, no adjustment needed. Even-order MA (e.g., 4-term for quarterly, 12-term for monthly): always apply a second 2-term MA to re-centre – the “$2\\times$” trick.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given sample autocorrelations $\\hat\\rho(1)=0.6$ and $\\hat\\rho(2)=0.4$, the Durbin–Levinson recursion gives the lag-2 partial autocorrelation $\\hat\\phi_{22}=\\dfrac{\\hat\\rho(2)-\\hat\\rho(1)^2}{1-\\hat\\rho(1)^2}$ as:',opts:['A) $0.4$','B) $0.0625$','C) $0.6$','D) $0.25$'],ans:1,exp:'Substituting directly: $\\hat\\phi_{22}=\\dfrac{0.4-(0.6)^2}{1-(0.6)^2}=\\dfrac{0.4-0.36}{1-0.36}=\\dfrac{0.04}{0.64}=0.0625$ This recursive formula is exactly how the PACF is computed in practice (rather than solving the full Yule–Walker system at every lag from scratch); it generalises to $\\hat\\phi_{hh}$ via the full Durbin–Levinson recursion for $h\\ge3$. A small $\\hat\\phi_{22}\\approx0.0625$ (likely within the confidence band for moderate $n$) would suggest the PACF has already cut off after lag 1 – consistent with an AR(1), not AR(2), model. Memory Tip: $\\phi_{22}=\\dfrac{\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2}{1-\\rho(1)^2}$ – memorise this exact closed form; it is the fastest way to compute or verify a lag-2 PACF value by hand in an exam.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A key practical advantage of the Parzen spectral window over a simple rectangular (truncated) lag window is that:',opts:['A) The Parzen window can only be used for AR processes, not MA processes','B) The Parzen window guarantees a non-negative spectral density estimate everywhere, since its Fourier transform is non-negative, whereas the rectangular window\'s transform (a Dirichlet-type kernel) can go negative','C) The Parzen window always gives a narrower confidence interval, regardless of the chosen bandwidth','D) The Parzen window requires no choice of bandwidth $m$ at all'],ans:1,exp:'A smoothed spectral estimate is $\\hat{f}(\\omega)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{|h|&lt;m}w(h)\\hat\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega h}$, a weighted (windowed) Fourier transform of the sample ACVF. Simply truncating the lag window (rectangular weights $w(h)=1$ for $|h|&lt;m$) corresponds in the frequency domain to convolving with the Dirichlet kernel, which oscillates and takes negative values – so $\\hat{f}(\\omega)$ can come out negative, which is nonsensical for a variance density. The Parzen window tapers smoothly to zero and is constructed so its Fourier transform (the Fejér-type kernel) is everywhere $\\ge0$, guaranteeing $\\hat f(\\omega)\\ge0$ for any data. Memory Tip: Rectangular truncation $\\to$ can give a negative “variance” (a red flag). Parzen (and other non-negative-definite windows like Bartlett-Priestley) $\\to$ always non-negative. Prefer tapered windows in practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The complete statement of Wold\'s decomposition theorem for a stationary process is $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}+V_t$ (with $\\psi_0=1$, $\\sum\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$). The additional term $V_t$ represents:',opts:['A) A term that is mathematically forced to equal zero for any stationary process','B) The seasonal component of the series, always present in real data','C) An extra random noise term, uncorrelated with $\\varepsilon_t$ but sharing the same variance $\\sigma^2$','D) A linearly deterministic component – perfectly predictable from its own infinite past with zero prediction error – that is uncorrelated with the innovations $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$ driving the MA($\\infty$) part'],ans:3,exp:'Wold\'s theorem states that any stationary process splits uniquely into two mutually uncorrelated parts: – A purely non-deterministic part $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ (the MA($\\infty$) representation, driven by genuinely unpredictable white-noise innovations). – A linearly deterministic part $V_t$: one that can be forecast exactly (zero mean-square error) from its own infinite past – e.g., $A\\cos(\\lambda t+\\phi)$ with random but time-invariant $A,\\phi$. Most introductory ARMA theory implicitly assumes the purely non-deterministic case ($V_t=0$), which is why textbooks often present Wold\'s theorem without the $V_t$ term – but the complete statement always includes it. Memory Tip: Wold = “predictable part” ($V_t$) + “unpredictable part” (MA($\\infty)$), always uncorrelated with each other. ARMA modelling targets only the unpredictable part; $V_t$ is usually assumed absent in practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is NOT a necessary property of a valid autocovariance function $\\gamma(h)$ of a weakly stationary process?',opts:['A) $\\gamma(h)\\geq 0$ for all $h\\geq0$ (non-negativity)','B) $\\gamma(h)=\\gamma(-h)$ (symmetry)','C) $|\\gamma(h)|\\leq\\gamma(0)$ for all $h$ (dominated by the variance)','D) $\\gamma(0)\\geq0$ (non-negative variance)'],ans:0,exp:'A valid ACVF must be: (B) symmetric $\\gamma(h)=\\gamma(-h)$; (C) $|\\gamma(h)|\\leq\\gamma(0)$ (from Cauchy–Schwarz); (D) $\\gamma(0)\\geq0$ (variance is non-negative). However, $\\gamma(h)$ can be negative for $h\\geq1$. Example: MA(1) with $\\theta&lt;0$ has $\\gamma(1)=\\sigma^2\\theta&lt;0$. Or AR(1) with $\\phi&lt;0$: $\\gamma(h)=\\phi^h\\gamma(0)$ alternates in sign. The correct characterisation is that the Toeplitz matrix $[\\gamma(i-j)]$ is positive semidefinite – not that individual values are non-negative. Non-negativity at lag $h\\geq1$ is a very restrictive condition satisfied only by special processes. Memory Tip: ACVF can be negative! Only $\\gamma(0)\\geq0$ is mandatory. The PSD Toeplitz condition (Bochner\'s theorem) is what ensures validity – not pointwise non-negativity. Never confuse ACVF non-negativity with variance non-negativity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a weakly stationary process, the sample ACVF $\\hat\\gamma(h)=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^{n-h}(X_t-\\bar X)(X_{t+h}-\\bar X)$ is a consistent estimator of $\\gamma(h)$ provided:',opts:['A) The process is Gaussian','B) $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$ (absolute summability of the ACVF)','C) The process has a finite fourth moment and $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)^2&lt;\\infty$','D) $\\gamma(h)=0$ for all $h\\geq1$'],ans:2,exp:'The general consistency result for $\\hat\\gamma(h)$: we need $\\mathrm{Var}(\\hat\\gamma(h))\\to0$. A sufficient condition (Hannan 1970): finite fourth moment $E[X_t^4]&lt;\\infty$ and $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)^2&lt;\\infty$ (which holds whenever $\\sum|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$). Option (B): absolute summability is stronger than square summability and is sufficient but not necessary. Option (C) is the minimal sufficient condition in general. Under absolute summability (B): $\\hat\\gamma(h)\\xrightarrow{m.s.}\\gamma(h)$ and also the Bartlett formula for the asymptotic distribution of $\\hat\\rho(h)$ applies. Option (A): Gaussianity is not required; option (D) is unnecessarily restrictive (WN only). Memory Tip: Sample ACVF consistent: needs finite 4th moment $+$ square-summable ACVF. Absolute summability (stronger) also works and additionally gives the Bartlett asymptotic distribution. Both conditions hold for all stationary ARMA processes.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The PACF $\\phi_{hh}$ at lag $h$ can be interpreted as the correlation between $X_t$ and $X_{t-h}$ after:',opts:['A) Removing the linear effects of all intermediate observations $X_{t-1},X_{t-2},\\ldots,X_{t-h+1}$ from both $X_t$ and $X_{t-h}$','B) Standardising both variables by their standard deviations','C) Differencing both $X_t$ and $X_{t-h}$ once','D) Applying a band-pass filter to remove seasonal components'],ans:0,exp:'Formally: let $\\hat X_t^{(h)}$ = projection of $X_t$ onto $\\{X_{t-1},\\ldots,X_{t-h+1}\\}$ and $\\hat X_{t-h}^{(h)}$ = projection of $X_{t-h}$ onto the same set. Then: $$\\phi_{hh}=\\mathrm{Corr}(X_t-\\hat X_t^{(h)},\\; X_{t-h}-\\hat X_{t-h}^{(h)}).$$ This is the partial correlation between $X_t$ and $X_{t-h}$ given $\\{X_{t-1},\\ldots,X_{t-h+1}\\}$. Why it identifies AR order: For AR($p$): once you condition on $p$ intermediate observations, $X_t$ and $X_{t-h}$ are uncorrelated for $h&gt;p$. So $\\phi_{hh}=0$ for $h&gt;p$ – the PACF cuts off at lag $p$. Memory Tip: PACF $=$ partial correlation controlling for intermediate lags. Like a partial regression coefficient. For AR($p$): past $p$ lags “explain” all the structure $\\Rightarrow$ PACF $=0$ beyond lag $p$. This is the direct link between PACF and AR order identification.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the MA(1) process $X_t=\\epsilon_t+\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$, the ACF satisfies $|\\rho(1)|\\leq\\frac{1}{2}$ always. The maximum value $|\\rho(1)|=\\frac{1}{2}$ is achieved when:',opts:['A) $\\theta=1$ or $\\theta=-1$ (unit MA root)','B) $\\theta\\to\\pm\\infty$','C) $\\theta=0$ (white noise)','D) $\\theta=1$ or $\\theta=-1$, giving $\\rho(1)=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)=\\pm\\tfrac{1}{2}$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\rho(1)=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$. To find maximum: $\\frac{d}{d\\theta}\\frac{\\theta}{1+\\theta^2}=\\frac{1-\\theta^2}{(1+\\theta^2)^2}=0\\Rightarrow\\theta=\\pm1$. At $\\theta=1$: $\\rho(1)=1/2$. At $\\theta=-1$: $\\rho(1)=-1/2$. Note: $\\theta=\\pm1$ are exactly the boundary of invertibility ($|\\theta|=1$). For strictly invertible ($|\\theta|&lt;1$): $|\\rho(1)|&lt;1/2$. Practical implication: If estimated $|\\hat\\rho(1)|&gt;0.5$ from data supposedly from an MA(1), the model is misspecified – no MA(1) can produce $|\\rho(1)|&gt;0.5$. This is a useful diagnostic bound. Memory Tip: MA(1) ACF: $|\\rho(1)|\\leq\\frac{1}{2}$ always! Maximum at $\\theta=\\pm1$ (non-invertible boundary). If sample $|\\hat\\rho(1)|&gt;0.5$: MA(1) is inadequate – need MA(2) or AR structure. This bound is a quick sanity check in practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(1) process $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$, $|\\phi|&lt;1$, the ACF at lag $h\\geq1$ is:',opts:['A) $\\rho(h)=\\phi^h(1-\\phi^2)$ (scaled by the stationarity factor)','B) $\\rho(h)=1-\\phi^h$','C) $\\rho(h)=\\phi^h$ (exact, for all $h\\geq1$)','D) $\\rho(h)=|\\phi|^h$ (always positive)'],ans:2,exp:'Multiply $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$ by $X_{t-h}$ and take expectations: $\\gamma(h)=\\phi\\gamma(h-1)$ for $h\\geq1$. Since $\\rho(h)=\\gamma(h)/\\gamma(0)$: $\\rho(h)=\\phi\\rho(h-1)$ with $\\rho(0)=1$. Solution: $\\rho(h)=\\phi^h$. For $\\phi&gt;0$: $\\rho(h)&gt;0$ for all $h$ (positive, decaying). For $\\phi&lt;0$: $\\rho(h)=\\phi^h$ alternates in sign ($+,-,+,-,\\ldots$). Option (A) would give $\\rho(0)=1-\\phi^2\\neq1$ – wrong. Option (D): $|\\phi|^h$ loses sign information (incorrect for $\\phi&lt;0$). Option (B): $1-\\phi^h$ doesn\'t decay to 0. Memory Tip: AR(1) ACF: $\\rho(h)=\\phi^h$. Simplest and most important formula in time series. Geometric decay. Positive $\\phi$: all positive ACF. Negative $\\phi$: alternating ACF. Rate of decay: $|\\phi|$ (closer to 1 = slower decay = more persistence).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1), the Yule–Walker estimator $\\hat\\phi=\\hat\\rho(1)$. If $n=200$ and $\\hat\\rho(1)=0.72$, the estimated AR coefficient and its approximate 95% confidence interval are:',opts:['A) $\\hat\\phi=0.72$; CI: $0.72\\pm1.96\\sqrt{(1-0.72^2)/200}\\approx0.72\\pm0.097$','B) $\\hat\\phi=0.72$; CI: $0.72\\pm1.96/200$','C) $\\hat\\phi=0.72$; CI: $0.72\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{200}\\approx0.72\\pm0.139$','D) $\\hat\\phi=0.72$; CI: $0.72\\pm1.96\\times0.72/\\sqrt{200}$'],ans:0,exp:'From the asymptotic distribution $\\sqrt{n}(\\hat\\phi-\\phi)\\xrightarrow{d}N(0,1-\\phi^2)$: SE$(\\hat\\phi)\\approx\\sqrt{(1-\\hat\\phi^2)/n}=\\sqrt{(1-0.5184)/200}=\\sqrt{0.4816/200}=\\sqrt{0.002408}\\approx0.0491$. 95% CI: $0.72\\pm1.96\\times0.0491\\approx0.72\\pm0.096$. Option (C): uses $1/\\sqrt{n}$ (standard SE for the ACF under WN, not for $\\hat\\phi$ under AR(1)). The key formula: $\\mathrm{SE}(\\hat\\phi)=\\sqrt{(1-\\phi^2)/n}$ – decreases as $|\\phi|\\to1$ (easier estimation near unit root). Memory Tip: AR(1) $\\hat\\phi$ asymptotic SE: $\\sqrt{(1-\\phi^2)/n}$. For $\\phi=0.72$: $1-0.72^2=0.4816$, SE$=\\sqrt{0.4816/n}$. For $\\phi=0$ (WN): SE$=1/\\sqrt{n}$ (reduces to standard). The SE shrinks as $|\\phi|\\to1$ – a key feature of near-unit-root processes.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the two-sided linear process $X_t=\\sum_{j=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\epsilon_{t-j}$ with $\\sum\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$, the spectral density is:',opts:['A) $f(\\omega)=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\cos(\\omega j)$','B) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\left|\\sum_{j=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\psi_j e^{-i\\omega j}\\right|^2=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}|\\Psi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2$','C) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\sum_{j=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\psi_j^2$','D) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\Psi(e^{-i\\omega})$ (complex-valued)'],ans:1,exp:'$X_t=\\Psi(B)\\epsilon_t$ where $\\Psi(z)=\\sum_j\\psi_jz^j$. Since $\\epsilon_t\\sim\\mathrm{WN}(0,\\sigma^2)$ with spectral density $\\sigma^2/(2\\pi)$: $$f_X(\\omega)=|\\Psi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2\\cdot\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}.$$ The modulus squared $|\\Psi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2=\\Psi(e^{-i\\omega})\\overline{\\Psi(e^{-i\\omega})}$ is real and non-negative. Option (C): $\\sum\\psi_j^2=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int|\\Psi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2d\\omega$ (Parseval) – a scalar, not a function of $\\omega$. Option (A): misses the imaginary parts (the cross-terms $\\psi_j\\psi_k\\sin(\\omega(j-k))$ cancel when symmetrised but option (A) is not correctly derived). Option (D): spectral density must be real and non-negative; the complex $\\Psi(e^{-i\\omega})$ alone is not a spectral density. Memory Tip: Spectral density of linear process: $\\sigma^2/(2\\pi)\\times|\\Psi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2$. The modulus squared ensures the result is real and non-negative. This unifies ARMA ($\\Psi=\\Theta/\\Phi$), MA ($\\Psi=\\Theta$), and AR ($\\Psi=1/\\Phi$) spectral density formulas.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ARMA(2,1) process $X_t=\\phi_1X_{t-1}+\\phi_2X_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t+\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$, the Yule–Walker equations for lags $h\\geq2$ give the recursion:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(h)=0$ for $h\\geq3$','B) $\\gamma(h)=\\phi_1\\gamma(h-1)+\\phi_2\\gamma(h-2)$ for all $h\\geq2$ (homogeneous for $h\\geq q+1$)','C) $\\gamma(h)=\\phi_1\\gamma(h-1)+\\phi_2\\gamma(h-2)+\\theta\\gamma(h-1)$ for all $h$','D) $\\gamma(h)=\\phi_1\\gamma(h-1)+\\phi_2\\gamma(h-2)+\\theta\\sigma^2$ for all $h\\geq1$'],ans:1,exp:'Multiply $X_t=\\phi_1X_{t-1}+\\phi_2X_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t+\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$ by $X_{t-h}$ ($h\\geq1$) and take expectations: $$\\gamma(h)=\\phi_1\\gamma(h-1)+\\phi_2\\gamma(h-2)+\\theta\\sigma^2E[\\epsilon_{t-1}X_{t-h}/\\sigma^2].$$ The MA term $\\theta\\sigma^2E[\\epsilon_{t-1}X_{t-h}]$ is non-zero only when $t-1\\geq t-h$, i.e. $h\\leq1=q$. For $h\\geq2$ (i.e. $h&gt;q$): the MA contribution vanishes and the recursion becomes homogeneous. General rule: ARMA($p,q$) ACVF satisfies the homogeneous AR($p$) difference equation $\\gamma(h)=\\sum_{k=1}^p\\phi_k\\gamma(h-k)$ for all $h&gt;q$. Memory Tip: ARMA($p,q$) ACVF: for $h&gt;q$, satisfies the same recursion as AR($p$): $\\gamma(h)=\\sum_{k=1}^p\\phi_k\\gamma(h-k)$. The MA part only affects initial conditions $\\gamma(0),\\ldots,\\gamma(q)$. Beyond lag $q$: pure AR behaviour!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2) $X_t=\\epsilon_t+\\theta_1\\epsilon_{t-1}+\\theta_2\\epsilon_{t-2}$, consider the parameter point $(\\theta_1,\\theta_2)=(1.5,\\,-0.5)$. Is this process invertible?',opts:['A) Yes, because $|\\theta_2|=0.5&lt;1$','B) No: check the invertibility triangle: $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=1$ violates $\\theta_1+\\theta_2&lt;1$','C) Yes, because $|\\theta_1|&gt;1$ implies the MA polynomial has a root outside the unit circle','D) Cannot be determined without computing the roots numerically'],ans:1,exp:'Invertibility triangle conditions: $\\theta_1+\\theta_2&lt;1$;\\; $\\theta_2-\\theta_1&lt;1$;\\; $|\\theta_2|&lt;1$. Check: $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=1.5+(-0.5)=1$. This violates $&lt;1$ (equality means a root exactly on the unit circle). Verify algebraically: $\\Theta(z)=1+1.5z-0.5z^2$. Roots: $z=\\frac{-1.5\\pm\\sqrt{2.25+2}}{-1}=\\frac{-1.5\\pm\\sqrt{4.25}}{-1}$. One root: $z=(1.5-2.06)/(-1)=0.56/(-1)=-0.56$... let us use the triangle instead – it immediately flags the boundary. The triangle is the fastest check. One condition fails $\\Rightarrow$ not invertible. Memory Tip: MA(2) invertibility: check ALL THREE triangle conditions. If any one fails: not invertible. $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=1$ is the boundary (one root at $z=1$). Faster than computing roots! Memorise: $\\theta_1+\\theta_2&lt;1$; $\\theta_2-\\theta_1&lt;1$; $|\\theta_2|&lt;1$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a GARCH(1,1) process $\\epsilon_t=\\sigma_t z_t$, $z_t\\sim\\mathrm{iid}\\,N(0,1)$, the squared process $\\epsilon_t^2$ follows an ARMA-type representation. Specifically, $\\epsilon_t^2$:',opts:['A) Follows an ARMA(1,1) representation with AR root $\\alpha+\\beta$ and MA root $\\beta$','B) Has PACF that cuts off at lag 1 (like AR(1))','C) Has ACF $\\rho(h)=(\\alpha+\\beta)^h$ for $h\\geq1$ – geometrically decaying like ARMA(1,1)','D) Is white noise (uncorrelated squared residuals)'],ans:0,exp:'Define $v_t=\\epsilon_t^2-\\sigma_t^2$ (zero-mean noise). Then: $$\\epsilon_t^2=\\omega+(\\alpha+\\beta)\\epsilon_{t-1}^2-\\beta v_{t-1}+v_t,$$ which is an ARMA(1,1) for $\\epsilon_t^2$ with AR coefficient $\\alpha+\\beta$ and MA coefficient $-\\beta$. ACF of $\\epsilon_t^2$: $\\rho(h)=(\\alpha+\\beta)^{h-1}\\rho(1)$ for $h\\geq1$ where $\\rho(1)=\\alpha(1-\\beta^2-\\alpha\\beta)/(1-\\beta^2-2\\alpha\\beta)$. The ACF decays geometrically at rate $(\\alpha+\\beta)$ after the first lag – justifying option (C) as an approximation, but option (A) is the exact representation. This is why the McLeod–Li test (LB on $\\epsilon_t^2$) can detect ARCH/GARCH effects: it detects the ARMA(1,1) structure in the squared series. Memory Tip: GARCH(1,1): squared residuals $\\epsilon_t^2\\sim$ARMA(1,1) with AR$=\\alpha+\\beta$, MA$=-\\beta$. ACF of $\\epsilon_t^2$ decays at rate $\\alpha+\\beta$. This is why GARCH persistence ($\\alpha+\\beta$ near 1) shows as slow ACF decay in $\\epsilon_t^2$ – detectable by the McLeod–Li test.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A researcher applies the ADF test (rejects $H_0$: unit root) and the KPSS test (does not reject $H_0$: stationary) to the same series. The joint conclusion is:',opts:['A) The series may have a structural break, requiring further investigation','B) Strong evidence that the series is stationary I(0): both tests point to stationarity','C) The series is I(1) (ADF rejection is the primary result)','D) The tests are contradictory; no conclusion is possible'],ans:1,exp:'ADF | KPSS | Conclusion; Reject $H_0$ | Not reject $H_0$ | Stationary I(0) – both agree; Not reject $H_0$ | Reject $H_0$ | Unit root I(1) – both agree; Reject $H_0$ | Reject $H_0$ | Contradictory – structural break?; Not reject $H_0$ | Not reject $H_0$ | Inconclusive – low power In this question: ADF rejects (no unit root) AND KPSS does not reject (stationary) $\\Rightarrow$ both agree: stationary. This is the strongest possible evidence for I(0). Memory Tip: Memorise the $2\\times2$ ADF–KPSS table. Key: when both agree $\\Rightarrow$ strong conclusion. When both reject (contradictory): suspect structural break or nonlinearity. When neither rejects (inconclusive): try more data or a more powerful test (DF-GLS, ERS).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A difference stationary (DS) process and a trend stationary (TS) process both exhibit upward trends. They differ in that:',opts:['A) DS: shocks have permanent effects (random walk component). TS: shocks are transitory (process reverts to the deterministic trend)','B) DS: trend is deterministic. TS: trend is stochastic','C) DS and TS processes are observationally equivalent (cannot be distinguished)','D) DS processes have higher variance than TS processes always'],ans:0,exp:'Trend stationary (TS): $X_t=\\mu+\\beta t+u_t$ where $u_t\\sim$ I(0). Shocks affect $u_t$ but the process reverts to $\\mu+\\beta t$. Detrend by OLS $\\Rightarrow$ stationary residuals. Appropriate transformation: subtract trend. Difference stationary (DS): $X_t=\\mu+X_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$ (random walk with drift). Shocks to $\\epsilon_t$ accumulate permanently. Variance grows without bound. Appropriate transformation: first difference. Key distinction: DS has stochastic trend (random walk component); TS has deterministic trend (fixed function of $t$). Mistaking DS for TS (or vice versa) leads to spurious results. The ADF/PP tests help distinguish the two. Memory Tip: DS: difference to stationarise (shocks permanent, variance$\\to\\infty$). TS: detrend to stationarise (shocks temporary, variance bounded). Wrong transformation $\\Rightarrow$ spurious results. ADF tests DS null; adding a trend to ADF tests TS alternative.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $W_t=(1-B^{12})X_t$ (seasonal difference at $s=12$), the spectral density of $W_t$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $f_W(\\omega)=12\\,f_X(\\omega)$ (scaled by the seasonal period)','B) $f_W(\\omega)=|1-e^{-12i\\omega}|^2 f_X(\\omega)=2(1-\\cos12\\omega)\\,f_X(\\omega)$','C) $f_W(\\omega)=f_X(\\omega)/|1-e^{-12i\\omega}|^2$ (inverse of the filter gain)','D) $f_W(\\omega)=f_X(\\omega+2\\pi/12)$ (frequency-shifted spectrum)'],ans:1,exp:'The filter $A(B)=1-B^{12}$ has frequency response $A(e^{-i\\omega})=1-e^{-12i\\omega}$. $|A(e^{-i\\omega})|^2=|1-e^{-12i\\omega}|^2=(1-\\cos12\\omega)^2+\\sin^2(12\\omega)=2-2\\cos12\\omega=2(1-\\cos12\\omega)$. Therefore $f_W(\\omega)=2(1-\\cos12\\omega)f_X(\\omega)$. Key values: At seasonal frequencies $\\omega_k=2\\pi k/12$ ($k=0,1,\\ldots,6$): $\\cos(12\\omega_k)=\\cos(2\\pi k)=1$, so $f_W(\\omega_k)=0$ – the filter kills power at ALL seasonal frequencies (including $\\omega=0$). This is why $(1-B^{12})$ removes both the annual trend AND all seasonal harmonics. Memory Tip: Seasonal difference $(1-B^s)$ gain: $2(1-\\cos(s\\omega))$. Zeros at $\\omega=2\\pi k/s$ (all $s$ seasonal frequencies including zero). Kills trend AND seasonality simultaneously. Compare to regular difference $(1-B)$: zero only at $\\omega=0$. Seasonal differencing is a stronger operation!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'At a fixed non-Fourier frequency $\\omega_0\\neq2\\pi j/n$, the periodogram $I(\\omega_0)$ computed from $n$ observations of a stationary process:',opts:['A) Has variance that decreases as $1/n^2$ (super-consistent)','B) Converges to $f(\\omega_0)$ in probability (consistent at fixed frequencies)','C) Equals exactly $\\hat\\gamma(0)/(2\\pi)$ for all $\\omega_0$ not on the Fourier grid','D) Is still approximately $\\mathrm{Exp}(f(\\omega_0))$ distributed and does not converge to $f(\\omega_0)$'],ans:3,exp:'The periodogram $I(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi n}\\left|\\sum_{t=1}^n X_te^{-i\\omega t}\\right|^2$ is inconsistent regardless of whether $\\omega$ is a Fourier frequency or not. For any fixed $\\omega$ (Fourier or not): $I(\\omega)\\xrightarrow{d}f(\\omega)\\chi^2_2/2\\sim\\mathrm{Exp}(f(\\omega))$ as $n\\to\\infty$. The distribution does not degenerate to a point mass at $f(\\omega)$. Key: The variance of $I(\\omega)$ is $\\approx f(\\omega)^2$ for all $\\omega$ – constant, does not $\\to0$. This is the definition of inconsistency. At a Fourier frequency: the formula for $I(\\omega_j)$ is exact (the DFT is a unitary transform). At non-Fourier frequencies: the DFT interpolates, but the distributional result is the same. Memory Tip: Periodogram inconsistency holds at ALL frequencies – Fourier and non-Fourier alike. Variance $\\approx f(\\omega)^2$ never shrinks. To get consistency: smooth (Daniell, Bartlett, Parzen). The smoothing step is what converts the inconsistent periodogram into a consistent spectral estimator.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a real-valued weakly stationary process, which symmetry property does the spectral density $f(\\omega)$ satisfy?',opts:['A) $f(\\omega)=f(\\pi-\\omega)$ (symmetric about $\\pi/2$)','B) $f(\\omega)=f(\\omega+2\\pi)$ (periodic with period $2\\pi$) and $f(\\omega)=f(-\\omega)$ (symmetric about 0)','C) $f(\\omega)&gt;0$ for all $\\omega$ (strictly positive everywhere)','D) $f(\\omega)=f(\\omega+\\pi)$ (periodic with period $\\pi$)'],ans:1,exp:'From Wiener–Khinchin: $f(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega h}$. For real-valued process: $\\gamma(h)=\\gamma(-h)$ (real and symmetric). Therefore: $$f(-\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_h\\gamma(h)e^{i\\omega h}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_h\\gamma(-h)e^{i\\omega h}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_h\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega(-h)}=f(\\omega).$$ So $f(\\omega)=f(-\\omega)$ (even function). Also $f$ is $2\\pi$-periodic by definition of the Fourier series. Option (C): $f(\\omega)&gt;0$ is not required; $f(\\omega)\\geq0$ always, but it can equal zero at isolated frequencies. Option (D): $\\pi$-periodic would apply to symmetric processes with special structure, not in general. Memory Tip: Real process $\\Rightarrow$ $f(\\omega)=f(-\\omega)$ (symmetric about 0) AND $f$ is $2\\pi$-periodic. So we only need $f$ on $[0,\\pi]$ (or $[-\\pi,0]$ by symmetry). This is why the periodogram is plotted on $[0,\\pi]$ in practice – no information lost due to symmetry.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Box–Pierce statistic $Q_{BP}=n\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\hat\\rho(k)^2$ and the Ljung–Box statistic $Q_{LB}=n(n+2)\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\hat\\rho(k)^2/(n-k)$ are both portmanteau tests. In finite samples:',opts:['A) $Q_{BP}$ and $Q_{LB}$ are always identical','B) $Q_{BP}&gt;Q_{LB}$ always','C) $Q_{LB}&gt;Q_{BP}$ always, since each term is weighted by $(n+2)/(n-k)&gt;1$; LB has better finite-sample size','D) They differ only at lag 1'],ans:2,exp:'Each term in $Q_{LB}$: $\\frac{n(n+2)}{n-k}\\hat\\rho(k)^2$. Each term in $Q_{BP}$: $n\\hat\\rho(k)^2$. Ratio: $\\frac{n+2}{n-k}&gt;1$ for all $k\\geq1$ and $n&gt;k+2$ (always in practice). So $Q_{LB}&gt;Q_{BP}$ always. Why prefer LB? The Box–Pierce test has poor size in finite samples (rejects too rarely – under-sized). The LB correction $(n+2)/(n-k)$ compensates for the downward bias of $\\hat\\rho(k)^2$ in finite samples, giving better size control. Asymptotically: $Q_{LB}$ and $Q_{BP}$ are equivalent (both $\\to\\chi^2_{m-p-q}$ under $H_0$). Memory Tip: LB $&gt;$ BP always (LB upweights each lag by $(n+2)/(n-k)&gt;1$). LB has better finite-sample size. Both $\\sim\\chi^2_{m-p-q}$ asymptotically. Always use Ljung–Box in practice – Box–Pierce is obsolete for most applications. LB = the modern standard.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density of a random walk $X_t=X_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$ (ARIMA(0,1,0)):',opts:['A) Equals the spectral density of the first difference $\\nabla X_t$','B) Is flat: $f(\\omega)=\\sigma^2/(2\\pi)$ for all $\\omega$','C) Is proportional to $1/\\omega^2$ near $\\omega=0$ (diverges at zero frequency)','D) Does not exist as a proper spectral density because the process is non-stationary'],ans:3,exp:'The Wiener–Khinchin theorem requires weak stationarity. The random walk has $\\gamma_t(h)=\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t-h})=\\min(t,t-h)\\sigma^2$ (depends on $t$) – not shift-invariant. No spectral density exists. However, the first difference $\\nabla X_t=\\epsilon_t$ is WN with spectral density $\\sigma^2/(2\\pi)$ (flat). Option (C): heuristically, “integrating” the WN spectrum by $(1-e^{-i\\omega})^{-2}$ (the inverse differencing filter) gives a pseudo-spectrum $\\propto\\sigma^2/(2\\pi|1-e^{-i\\omega}|^2)\\approx\\sigma^2/(2\\pi\\omega^2)$ near $\\omega=0$. This is the “pseudo-spectral density” sometimes used in a generalised sense, but it is not a proper spectral density. Technically correct answer is (D): the strict spectral density does not exist for non-stationary processes. Memory Tip: Non-stationary process (random walk, I(1)): no proper spectral density. However, the pseudo-spectrum $\\propto1/\\omega^2$ near zero is used informally. First difference $\\nabla X_t$: WN, flat spectrum. Spectral analysis strictly applies only to stationary processes.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The BIC is strongly consistent for ARMA order selection, meaning: if the true model is ARMA($p_0,q_0$) with $p_0\\leq P$ and $q_0\\leq Q$, then:',opts:['A) $P(\\hat p=p_0,\\hat q=q_0)\\to0$ as $n\\to\\infty$ (BIC always overfits)','B) $E[\\hat p+\\hat q]\\to p_0+q_0$ (only the sum is consistently estimated)','C) BIC is consistent only if the innovations are Gaussian','D) $P(\\hat p=p_0,\\hat q=q_0)\\to1$ as $n\\to\\infty$ (BIC selects the true order with probability approaching 1)'],ans:3,exp:'Schwarz (1978) showed that BIC is strongly consistent for order selection: the probability of selecting the true order $\\to1$ as $n\\to\\infty$, provided the true model is in the search space. The key property: the BIC penalty $\\ln n$ grows faster than the log-likelihood improvement from overfitting ($O(1)$ per extra parameter), so overfit models are eventually penalised out. AIC is NOT consistent: AIC penalty (2 per parameter) is constant, and the log-likelihood improvement from overfitting has a non-negligible probability of exceeding 2, so AIC overselects with a fixed positive probability even as $n\\to\\infty$. Option (C): BIC consistency holds under non-Gaussian innovations too (under regularity conditions for quasi-MLE). Memory Tip: BIC: consistent ($P(\\text{correct order})\\to1$). AIC: inconsistent (overfits with fixed probability). BIC wins for model identification. AIC wins for prediction MSE. Both are valid; purpose determines choice. Non-Gaussian: BIC consistency still holds under mild conditions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The SARIMA$(1,0,0)(1,0,0)_{12}$ multiplicative model can be written as:',opts:['A) $(1-\\phi B-\\Phi B^{12})X_t=\\epsilon_t$ (same as additive)','B) $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\Phi X_{t-12}+\\epsilon_t$ (additive AR terms only)','C) $X_t=(\\phi+\\Phi)X_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$','D) $(1-\\phi B)(1-\\Phi B^{12})X_t=\\epsilon_t$, giving $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\Phi X_{t-12}-\\phi\\Phi X_{t-13}+\\epsilon_t$'],ans:3,exp:'Expanding $(1-\\phi B)(1-\\Phi B^{12})=1-\\phi B-\\Phi B^{12}+\\phi\\Phi B^{13}$. Applying to $X_t$: $X_t-\\phi X_{t-1}-\\Phi X_{t-12}+\\phi\\Phi X_{t-13}=\\epsilon_t$, i.e. $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\Phi X_{t-12}-\\phi\\Phi X_{t-13}+\\epsilon_t$. The interaction term $-\\phi\\Phi X_{t-13}$ is the key difference between the multiplicative and additive (option A/D) specifications. It captures the interaction between regular and seasonal AR behaviour. Option (B): additive specification – misses the interaction. Option (A): same as (B) in polynomial form. Multiplicative models are more parsimonious and better capture seasonal-non-seasonal interactions. Memory Tip: Multiplicative SARIMA: expand the product of polynomial operators – always generates “cross-terms” at combined lags. SARIMA$(1,0,0)(1,0,0)_{12}$: lags 1, 12, 13 (not just 1 and 12). The interaction lag $=p\\times s=1\\times12+1=13$. This cross-term is why multiplicative is more efficient than additive.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\hat X_{T+1}^*$ is any predictor of $X_{T+1}$ based on $\\mathcal{F}_T$ and $\\hat X_{T+1}$ is the minimum MSE predictor (conditional mean), then:',opts:['A) $\\mathrm{MSE}(\\hat X_{T+1}^*)=\\mathrm{MSE}(\\hat X_{T+1})$ for all predictors','B) $\\mathrm{MSE}(\\hat X_{T+1}^*)&lt;\\mathrm{MSE}(\\hat X_{T+1})$ is possible for Gaussian processes','C) $\\mathrm{MSE}(\\hat X_{T+1}^*)&lt;\\mathrm{MSE}(\\hat X_{T+1})$ is possible if $\\hat X_{T+1}^*$ is nonlinear','D) $\\mathrm{MSE}(\\hat X_{T+1}^*)\\geq\\mathrm{MSE}(\\hat X_{T+1})$ always'],ans:3,exp:'By the law of iterated expectations and the properties of conditional expectation: for ANY predictor $g(\\mathcal{F}_T)$, $$E[(X_{T+1}-g(\\mathcal{F}_T))^2]\\geq E[(X_{T+1}-E[X_{T+1}|\\mathcal{F}_T])^2].$$ The conditional expectation is the globally optimal predictor – among all functions of $\\mathcal{F}_T$ (linear or nonlinear). No other predictor can achieve a lower MSE. Options (C) and (B) are therefore impossible: no nonlinear or Gaussian-specific predictor can beat the conditional mean. The best linear predictor (BLP) achieves the conditional mean only when the process is Gaussian (since linear conditional expectation is a special property of Gaussians). Memory Tip: Conditional mean $=$ globally optimal MSE predictor. No predictor – linear or nonlinear – can beat it. BLP $=$ conditional mean only for Gaussian. For non-Gaussian: BLP is suboptimal (conditional mean may be nonlinear). The gap between BLP and conditional mean measures “nonlinear predictability.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A weakly stationary process is purely deterministic (linearly deterministic) if and only if:',opts:['A) Its spectral density is constant (flat spectrum)','B) Its mean is zero','C) The innovation variance $\\sigma^2=0$, equivalently $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\\ln f(\\omega)\\,d\\omega=-\\infty$','D) Its ACVF $\\gamma(h)=0$ for all $h\\geq1$'],ans:2,exp:'From the Kolmogorov formula: $\\sigma^2=\\exp\\!\\bigl[\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\\ln(2\\pi f(\\omega))d\\omega\\bigr]$. $\\sigma^2=0$ iff $\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int\\ln(2\\pi f(\\omega))d\\omega=-\\infty$ iff $\\int\\ln f(\\omega)d\\omega=-\\infty$. This occurs when $f(\\omega)=0$ on a set of positive measure (the spectrum has “gaps”). Example: A deterministic sinusoid $X_t=A\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi)$ has a spectral measure with point masses (Dirac deltas) at $\\pm\\omega_0$ – no absolutely continuous component, so $\\ln f(\\omega)$ is $-\\infty$ everywhere. This process is perfectly predictable. Option (D): $\\gamma(h)=0$ for $h\\geq1$ means WN – the opposite (purely non-deterministic, maximum unpredictability). Memory Tip: Purely deterministic: $\\sigma^2=0$ (perfect prediction). Szegő condition: $\\int\\ln f\\,d\\omega=-\\infty$ (spectrum has zeros). Purely non-deterministic: $\\sigma^2&gt;0$ (imperfect prediction). Wold decomposition separates these: $X_t=V_t$(det.) + $Z_t$(non-det.).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(2) process, the ACF exhibits oscillatory (damped sinusoidal) behaviour rather than monotone geometric decay if and only if:',opts:['A) $\\phi_2&lt;0$ always','B) $|\\phi_2|&gt;|\\phi_1|$','C) $\\phi_1&gt;0$ and $\\phi_2&gt;0$','D) The characteristic polynomial $\\Phi(z)=1-\\phi_1z-\\phi_2z^2$ has complex conjugate roots (discriminant $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2&lt;0$)'],ans:3,exp:'The roots of $\\Phi(z)=1-\\phi_1z-\\phi_2z^2=0$: $z=\\frac{\\phi_1\\pm\\sqrt{\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2}}{-2\\phi_2}$. Discriminant $\\Delta=\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2$: $\\bullet$ $\\Delta&gt;0$: two real roots $\\Rightarrow$ ACF is a sum of two geometric sequences (monotone or of mixed sign, but not oscillatory in the sinusoidal sense). $\\bullet$ $\\Delta&lt;0$: complex conjugate roots $re^{\\pm i\\lambda_0}$ $\\Rightarrow$ ACF $\\propto r^{-h}\\cos(\\lambda_0 h+\\psi)$ (damped sinusoid with period $2\\pi/\\lambda_0$). This is the oscillatory case. $\\Delta=0$: repeated real root (borderline). Option (A): $\\phi_2&lt;0$ is necessary but not sufficient for complex roots. Memory Tip: AR(2) oscillatory ACF: complex roots $\\Leftrightarrow$ $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2&lt;0$. Period of oscillation $=2\\pi/\\lambda_0$ where $\\lambda_0=\\arg(z_{\\text{root}})$. Real roots: monotone (or alternating if negative). Complex roots: sinusoidal decay. This determines whether the AR(2) models business cycles (complex) or trends (real).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a correctly specified ARMA model with non-Gaussian i.i.d. innovations, the quasi-MLE (QMLE) based on Gaussian likelihood is:',opts:['A) Consistent and asymptotically normal, but less efficient than the true MLE based on the correct innovation distribution','B) Consistent only when the innovations have zero skewness','C) Identical to OLS in all cases','D) Inconsistent, because the likelihood is misspecified'],ans:0,exp:'The QMLE (or pseudo-MLE) maximises the Gaussian log-likelihood even when innovations are non-Gaussian. Under regularity conditions (stationarity, invertibility, correct ARMA specification): $$\\sqrt{n}(\\hat\\theta_{QMLE}-\\theta_0)\\xrightarrow{d}N(\\mathbf{0},\\mathbf{I}^{-1}\\mathbf{J}\\mathbf{I}^{-1})$$ where $\\mathbf{I}$ is the Gaussian Fisher information and $\\mathbf{J}=E[s_ts_t^\\top]$ is the true score outer product. If Gaussian: $\\mathbf{J}=\\mathbf{I}$ $\\Rightarrow$ sandwich reduces to $\\mathbf{I}^{-1}$ (CRLB – efficient). If non-Gaussian: $\\mathbf{J}\\neq\\mathbf{I}$ $\\Rightarrow$ “sandwich” variance $\\mathbf{I}^{-1}\\mathbf{J}\\mathbf{I}^{-1}&gt;\\mathbf{I}^{-1}$ (consistent but inefficient). The QMLE is consistent because the ARMA score has zero expectation at the true parameter regardless of the innovation distribution. Memory Tip: QMLE = Gaussian likelihood on non-Gaussian data: consistent (score zero at true $\\theta$) but inefficient (“sandwich” variance). Use robust SE (sandwich) for valid inference. True MLE with correct distribution: efficient but requires knowing the distribution. QMLE is the practical compromise.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an absolutely summable ACVF ($\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$), the spectral density can be written as the Fourier cosine series:',opts:['A) $f(\\omega)=\\gamma(0)+\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\sum_{h=1}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)\\cos(\\omega h)$','B) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\left[\\gamma(0)+2\\sum_{h=1}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)\\cos(\\omega h)\\right]$','C) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{h=1}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)\\cos(\\omega h)$','D) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\left[\\gamma(0)+\\sum_{h=1}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega h}\\right]$'],ans:1,exp:'From Wiener–Khinchin: $f(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega h}$. For real $\\gamma(h)=\\gamma(-h)$: separate $h=0$, positive, and negative terms: $$f(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\left[\\gamma(0)+\\sum_{h=1}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)(e^{-i\\omega h}+e^{i\\omega h})\\right]=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\left[\\gamma(0)+2\\sum_{h=1}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)\\cos\\omega h\\right].$$ This is a real-valued Fourier cosine series. The factor of 2 comes from combining positive and negative lags. Option (C): misses $\\gamma(0)$ and the factor of 2. Option (A): wrong normalisation ($1/\\pi$ instead of $1/\\pi$ through the $2\\times1/(2\\pi)=1/\\pi$... actually option (A) is also valid if the outer factor is $1/(2\\pi)$ for $\\gamma(0)$). Option (B) is the standard form. Memory Tip: Spectral density as Fourier cosine: $f(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}[\\gamma(0)+2\\sum_{h\\geq1}\\gamma(h)\\cos\\omega h]$. The factor 2 doubles the positive-lag terms (combining $h$ and $-h$). This real-valued formula is used directly in estimating $f$ from the sample ACVF (the lag window estimator).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If a stationary process $X_t\\sim I(0)$ is unnecessarily differenced once to give $W_t=\\nabla X_t=(1-B)X_t$, the ACF of $W_t$ at lag 1 satisfies:',opts:['A) $\\rho_W(1)=\\rho_X(1)$ (unchanged by differencing)','B) $\\rho_W(1)=(\\rho_X(1)-1)/(2-2\\rho_X(1))=-\\frac{1-\\rho_X(1)}{2(1-\\rho_X(1))}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ only when $X_t$ is WN','C) $\\rho_W(1)=-0.5$ exactly (over-differencing always produces $\\rho=-0.5$ at lag 1)','D) $\\rho_W(1)=\\dfrac{\\gamma_X(1)-\\gamma_X(0)}{\\gamma_X(0)-\\gamma_X(1)}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{2}$, which is always $\\leq-0.5$ when $\\rho_X(1)\\geq0$'],ans:3,exp:'For $W_t=X_t-X_{t-1}$: $\\gamma_W(0)=2\\gamma_X(0)-2\\gamma_X(1)$; $\\gamma_W(1)=\\gamma_X(1)-\\gamma_X(0)-\\gamma_X(0)+\\gamma_X(1)=2\\gamma_X(1)-2\\gamma_X(0)$. $\\rho_W(1)=\\gamma_W(1)/\\gamma_W(0)=(2\\gamma_X(1)-2\\gamma_X(0))/(2\\gamma_X(0)-2\\gamma_X(1))=-1$. Wait – the exact formula: $\\rho_W(1)=(\\rho_X(1)-1)/(2(1-\\rho_X(1)))\\cdot(-1)$... Simplified: $\\rho_W(1)=\\frac{\\rho_X(1)-1}{2-2\\rho_X(1)}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for any $\\rho_X(1)$. Key result: For WN ($\\rho_X=0$): $\\rho_W(1)=-0.5$ exactly. For positively correlated I(0): $\\rho_W(1)$ is between $-0.5$ and $-1$. Over-differencing always creates strongly negative lag-1 autocorrelation – a diagnostic sign. Memory Tip: Over-differencing signature: $\\rho_W(1)\\leq-0.5$ (strongly negative). If you see $\\hat\\rho(1)&lt;-0.4$ after differencing, you may have over-differenced! The sample ACF of the differenced series tipping below $-0.5$ is the classic over-differencing diagnostic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Applying a split cosine bell data taper $h_t=\\frac{1}{2}[1-\\cos(\\pi t/m)]$ for $t=1,\\ldots,m$ (and $h_t=1$ for $m&lt;t\\leq n-m$) before computing the periodogram primarily:',opts:['A) Makes the periodogram consistent (variance $\\to0$)','B) Increases the spectral resolution (ability to separate nearby peaks)','C) Removes the linear trend from the data before spectral estimation','D) Reduces spectral leakage by smoothly tapering the series to zero at both ends, reducing sidelobes of the spectral window'],ans:3,exp:'The rectangular window (no taper) abruptly cuts off the series at both ends, creating large Dirichlet kernel sidelobes $\\Rightarrow$ high leakage. The split cosine bell taper gradually reduces the data to zero at both ends over the first and last $m$ observations. Effect on spectral window: Convolution of the rectangular window with the cosine bell reduces the sidelobes dramatically (typically 40+ dB reduction) at the cost of slightly widening the main lobe. Typical taper fraction: $p=2m/n=0.1$ (10% at each end). Higher $p$: less leakage but more main lobe widening (lower resolution). Option (B): tapering REDUCES resolution (wider main lobe). Option (A): tapering does not affect consistency (which requires smoothing). Option (C): tapering does not detrend. Memory Tip: Data taper: leakage$\\downarrow$, resolution$\\downarrow$ (trade-off). Always taper before computing periodogram of a series with strong low-frequency power (trend, seasonality) to prevent leakage contaminating other frequencies. 10% cosine bell taper is a common standard choice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Ljung–Box portmanteau test is preferred over testing each sample autocorrelation $\\hat\\rho(k)$ individually (using $|z_k|=\\sqrt{n}|\\hat\\rho(k)|&gt;1.96$) because:',opts:['A) The portmanteau test uses the PACF, not the ACF','B) The portmanteau test is more powerful at lag 1 specifically','C) Individual tests at $m$ lags simultaneously give an inflated Type I error (multiple testing problem); the portmanteau test controls the joint size at a single $\\alpha$ level','D) Individual ACF tests require Gaussianity; the portmanteau test does not'],ans:2,exp:'If each of $m$ individual ACF tests has size $\\alpha=0.05$: the probability of at least one false rejection $\\approx1-(1-\\alpha)^m\\approx m\\alpha$ (Bonferroni bound). For $m=20$ lags: $\\approx1-0.95^{20}\\approx64\\%$ chance of at least one spurious rejection even when all $\\rho(k)=0$! The Ljung–Box statistic $Q_{LB}=n(n+2)\\sum_{k=1}^m\\hat\\rho^2(k)/(n-k)$ provides a single joint test with controlled size $\\alpha$ across all $m$ lags simultaneously. Limitation of LB: Equal weighting of all $m$ lags – misses alternatives concentrated at a single lag (lower power there). Individual tests are more powerful when only one lag has significant autocorrelation. Memory Tip: Individual tests on $m$ lags: Type I error $\\approx m\\times0.05$ (inflated). LB: single test, controlled size. But: LB has lower power for alternatives concentrated at one lag. In practice: use LB as the primary diagnostic; inspect individual ACF plot to find where autocorrelation lies.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A spectral density with sharp peaks corresponds to which type of model, and what creates the peaks?',opts:['A) AR model with roots close to the unit circle (near-unit-root AR), which makes the denominator $|\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2$ near zero at the corresponding frequency','B) MA model with roots close to the unit circle (near-non-invertible MA)','C) MA model, because the numerator $|\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2$ diverges near unit circle roots','D) WN process, which has the flattest (no-peak) spectrum'],ans:0,exp:'$f(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\cdot\\frac{|\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}{|\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}$. Sharp peaks (large $f(\\omega_0)$): denominator $|\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega_0})|^2\\approx0$, i.e. AR root near $e^{i\\omega_0}$ (near unit circle at angle $\\omega_0$). Spectral troughs (small $f(\\omega_0)$): numerator $|\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega_0})|^2\\approx0$, i.e. MA root near $e^{i\\omega_0}$. Physical interpretation: A near-unit-circle AR root at frequency $\\omega_0$ means the process has a near-periodic component at period $2\\pi/\\omega_0$ – the spectrum concentrates energy there. The closer to the unit circle, the sharper and taller the peak. Memory Tip: AR roots near unit circle $\\Rightarrow$ spectral PEAKS (denominator near zero). MA roots near unit circle $\\Rightarrow$ spectral TROUGHS (numerator near zero). “AR makes peaks, MA makes troughs.” Use AR models to capture prominent cycles; MA terms to suppress specific frequencies.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the Box–Jenkins approach, the number of differences $d$ in ARIMA($p,d,q$) is determined by:',opts:['A) Setting $d=p+q$ (total model order determines differencing)','B) Fitting a polynomial trend and setting $d$ equal to the degree of the polynomial','C) Applying unit root tests (ADF/PP) or visual inspection of the sample ACF, differencing until the ACF of the differenced series decays rapidly to zero','D) Minimising AIC over all $(p,d,q)$ simultaneously'],ans:2,exp:'Visual approach: If the sample ACF decays very slowly (still large at lag 10+), $d=0$ is insufficient. First-difference: recheck ACF. Repeat until ACF decays rapidly. Formal approach: Apply ADF test to $X_t$. If not rejected (unit root): difference and test $\\nabla X_t$. Continue until the ADF rejects. The number of differences applied is $d$. Caution – over-differencing: If after one difference the ACF at lag 1 is $&lt;-0.4$, $d=1$ may already be one too many. The over-differencing diagnostic helps. Option (D): searching over $d$ in AIC is possible but risks over-differencing or treating a continuous parameter as discrete. Memory Tip: Box–Jenkins Step 1: determine $d$ via ADF test (formal) or ACF pattern (informal). Slowly decaying ACF $\\Rightarrow$ difference. Rapidly decaying ACF $\\Rightarrow$ stop. Over-differencing warning: $\\hat\\rho(1)&lt;-0.5$ after differencing. The goal is a stationary differenced series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The forecast encompassing test of Harvey, Leybourne and Newbold tests $H_0$: forecast $\\hat f_1$ encompasses $\\hat f_2$ (i.e. combining $\\hat f_2$ with $\\hat f_1$ does not reduce MSE). This is equivalent to testing:',opts:['A) $H_0$: $E[e_{1t}^2]=E[e_{2t}^2]$ (equal MSE, same as DM test)','B) $H_0$: $E[e_{1t}(e_{1t}-e_{2t})]=0$, i.e. the forecast error of $\\hat f_1$ is uncorrelated with the forecast error difference','C) $H_0$: The optimal combination weight on $\\hat f_2$ equals zero (adding $\\hat f_2$ to $\\hat f_1$ provides no benefit)','D) Both (B) and (C) are equivalent formulations of the encompassing null'],ans:3,exp:'$\\hat f_1$ encompasses $\\hat f_2$ means: knowing $\\hat f_2$ provides no additional forecasting power beyond $\\hat f_1$. Formally: Consider the combination $\\hat f_c=\\lambda\\hat f_1+(1-\\lambda)\\hat f_2$. The optimal $\\lambda$ minimises MSE. $\\hat f_1$ encompasses $\\hat f_2$ iff $\\lambda^*=1$ (no weight on $\\hat f_2$). Testing $\\lambda^*=1$ is equivalent to: $E[e_{1t}(e_{1t}-e_{2t})]=0$ (option B). Distinction from DM test: DM tests equal MSE ($E[e_1^2]=E[e_2^2]$); encompassing tests whether combining helps. A model with lower MSE may still not encompass the other – the other model may contain complementary information. The Harvey–Leybourne–Newbold (1998) test provides finite-sample corrections similar to the LB correction. Memory Tip: Encompassing $\\hat f_1\\supseteq\\hat f_2$: $\\hat f_2$ adds nothing to $\\hat f_1$. Test: $\\lambda^*=1$ in combination $\\Leftrightarrow$ $E[e_1(e_1-e_2)]=0$. Different from DM (equal MSE). Even if MSE($\\hat f_1$)$&lt;$MSE($\\hat f_2$): $\\hat f_1$ may not encompass $\\hat f_2$ if $\\hat f_2$ captures orthogonal information.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In forecast evaluation, the recursive (expanding window) approach estimates the model at each origin $T$ using all data $\\{X_1,\\ldots,X_T\\}$, while the rolling window approach uses a fixed window of the most recent $w$ observations. The rolling window is preferred when:',opts:['A) The forecast horizon $h$ is longer than the window $w$','B) The process is stationary with time-invariant parameters (rolling is always more efficient)','C) The sample size is very large (maximises data usage)','D) The model parameters are suspected to change over time (parameter instability / structural breaks)'],ans:3,exp:'Recursive | Rolling; Data usage | All available | Fixed window $w$; Best for | Stable parameters | Changing parameters; Variance | Lower (more data) | Higher (less data); Bias | Low if stable | Low if recent $\\approx$ future Rolling window “forgets” old data deliberately. If parameters change: old data is misleading, so discarding it reduces bias at the cost of higher variance. Optimal window: Bias–variance trade-off. Too small $w$: high variance. Too large $w$: structural break contaminates the window. Memory Tip: Rolling window $=$ “forget old data.” Use when: structural breaks likely, regime changes, macro policy shifts. Recursive $=$ “use all data.” Use when: parameters stable, want minimum variance. In practice: try both and compare MSPE – the better one reveals whether there is instability.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) model with $\\phi_1=0.5$, $\\phi_2=0.2$, $\\sigma^2=1$, the spectral density at $\\omega=0$ is:',opts:['A) $f(0)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)^2\\approx0.013$','B) $f(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\approx0.159$','C) $f(0)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1-\\phi_1^2-\\phi_2^2)}\\approx0.249$','D) $f(0)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)^2}=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\times0.09}\\approx1.768$'],ans:3,exp:'$f(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi|1-\\phi_1e^{-i\\omega}-\\phi_2e^{-2i\\omega}|^2}$. At $\\omega=0$: $e^{-i\\cdot0}=1$, so $|\\Phi(1)|^2=(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)^2$. $f(0)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi(1-0.5-0.2)^2}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi\\times0.3^2}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi\\times0.09}\\approx\\frac{1}{0.565}\\approx1.768$. This equals $\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\Psi(1)^2=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\left(\\frac{1}{1-0.5-0.2}\\right)^2=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)^2\\approx1.768$. ✓ Option (C): uses $1-\\phi_1^2-\\phi_2^2$ (incorrect – this appears in variance formula but not spectral density at 0). Option (B): flat WN spectrum – ignores the AR structure entirely. Memory Tip: AR($p$) spectral density at $\\omega=0$: $f(0)=\\sigma^2/[2\\pi(1-\\sum\\phi_k)^2]=\\sigma^2\\Psi(1)^2/(2\\pi)$. At $\\omega=0$: $e^{-i\\omega}=1$, so plug $z=1$ into $\\Phi(z)$. The long-run variance $2\\pi f(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\sum\\phi_k)^2$ – a key quantity in HAC inference.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Durbin–Watson (DW) statistic $d=\\sum_{t=2}^n(\\hat e_t-\\hat e_{t-1})^2/\\sum_{t=1}^n\\hat e_t^2$ approximately equals:',opts:['A) $d\\approx2\\hat\\rho(1)$','B) $d\\approx1-\\hat\\rho(1)$','C) $d\\approx\\hat\\rho(1)^2$','D) $d\\approx2(1-\\hat\\rho(1))$ where $\\hat\\rho(1)$ is the lag-1 residual autocorrelation'],ans:3,exp:'Expanding: $d=\\sum(\\hat e_t^2-2\\hat e_t\\hat e_{t-1}+\\hat e_{t-1}^2)/\\sum\\hat e_t^2\\approx(2\\sum\\hat e_t^2-2\\sum\\hat e_t\\hat e_{t-1})/\\sum\\hat e_t^2=2-2\\hat\\rho(1)\\approx2(1-\\hat\\rho(1))$. Key values: $\\hat\\rho(1)=0$ (WN): $d\\approx2$ (no autocorrelation). $\\hat\\rho(1)=1$ (perfect positive): $d\\approx0$ (strong positive autocorrelation). $\\hat\\rho(1)=-1$ (perfect negative): $d\\approx4$ (strong negative autocorrelation). Decision rule: $d&lt;d_L$: reject $H_0$ (positive autocorrelation); $d&gt;d_U$: accept $H_0$; $d_L\\leq d\\leq d_U$: inconclusive. Tabled critical values depend on $n$ and $k$ (number of regressors). Memory Tip: DW $\\approx2(1-\\hat\\rho(1))$. Range: $[0,4]$. DW$\\approx2$: WN (good). DW$&lt;2$: positive autocorrelation. DW$&gt;2$: negative autocorrelation. The DW only tests lag-1 autocorrelation; Ljung–Box tests all lags jointly – use LB for comprehensive diagnostics.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARMA($p,q$) model, both stationarity (AR roots outside unit circle) and invertibility (MA roots outside unit circle) are required. What does each condition guarantee?',opts:['A) Stationarity: MA($\\infty$) in terms of past innovations; Invertibility: AR($\\infty$) in terms of past observations','B) Both conditions together: the process has a unique spectral density','C) Stationarity: ACF decays to zero; Invertibility: PACF decays to zero','D) Stationarity: finite variance; Invertibility: finite mean'],ans:0,exp:'Stationarity (AR roots outside unit circle): $\\Phi(z)^{-1}$ converges for $|z|\\leq1$ $\\Rightarrow$ causal (one-sided, past-dependent) MA($\\infty$) representation: $X_t=\\Psi(B)\\epsilon_t=\\sum_{j\\geq0}\\psi_j\\epsilon_{t-j}$. Invertibility (MA roots outside unit circle): $\\Theta(z)^{-1}$ converges for $|z|\\leq1$ $\\Rightarrow$ convergent AR($\\infty$) representation in terms of past observations: $\\Pi(B)X_t=\\epsilon_t$, $\\Pi(z)=\\Phi(z)/\\Theta(z)=\\sum_{j\\geq0}\\pi_jz^j$. Practical importance of invertibility: The AR($\\infty$) representation enables construction of the innovations (residuals) from past data – essential for estimation (CLS, MLE) and forecasting. Memory Tip: Stationarity $\\Rightarrow$ MA($\\infty$) from past errors (causal). Invertibility $\\Rightarrow$ AR($\\infty$) from past observations (invertible). Both together: the process has a unique, tractable representation for both simulation (MA side) and estimation (AR side).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The ARIMA($p,d,q$) model is defined by the equation:',opts:['A) $\\Phi(B)X_t=\\mu+\\Theta(B)(1-B)^d\\epsilon_t$ (differencing applied to the noise)','B) $\\Phi(B)(1-B)^dX_t=\\mu+\\Theta(B)\\epsilon_t$ where $\\Phi(B)$ is degree $p$, $\\Theta(B)$ is degree $q$, and all roots of $\\Phi$ and $\\Theta$ are outside the unit circle','C) $(1-B)^d[\\Phi(B)X_t-\\Theta(B)\\epsilon_t]=\\mu$ (differencing the whole equation)','D) $\\Phi(B)X_t=\\Theta(B)\\epsilon_t$ for $X_t$ that has already been differenced $d$ times'],ans:1,exp:'The ARIMA($p,d,q$) model applies the AR polynomial to the $d$-times differenced series: $$\\underbrace{\\Phi(B)}_{\\text{AR, deg }p}\\underbrace{(1-B)^d}_{\\text{differencing}}X_t=\\mu+\\underbrace{\\Theta(B)}_{\\text{MA, deg }q}\\epsilon_t.$$ Key requirements: all roots of $\\Phi(B)$ and $\\Theta(B)$ lie outside the unit circle (so the ARMA part $\\Phi(B)W_t=\\mu+\\Theta(B)\\epsilon_t$ is stationary and invertible, where $W_t=(1-B)^dX_t$). The $(1-B)^d$ factor is what makes the full AR polynomial $\\Phi(B)(1-B)^d$ have $d$ roots on the unit circle (the unit roots). Option (D) is another valid description but less explicit about the structure. Memory Tip: ARIMA($p,d,q$): $\\Phi(B)(1-B)^dX_t=\\mu+\\Theta(B)\\epsilon_t$. The $(1-B)^d$ is between $\\Phi$ and $X$. $\\Phi$ and $\\Theta$ are both stationary/invertible polynomials. The $d$ unit roots are EXPLICITLY in $(1-B)^d$, NOT in $\\Phi$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The PACF $\\phi_{hh}$ at lag $h$ equals the OLS coefficient on $X_{t-h}$ in which regression?',opts:['A) Regressing $X_t$ on $X_{t-h}$ only, ignoring intermediate lags','B) Regressing $X_t$ on $X_{t-1},X_{t-2},\\ldots,X_{t-h}$ (multiple regression on $h$ lags)','C) Regressing $X_t$ on $X_{t-1}$ only (simple regression)','D) Regressing $X_{t-h}$ on $X_t$ (reverse regression)'],ans:1,exp:'Fit $X_t=\\phi_{h1}X_{t-1}+\\phi_{h2}X_{t-2}+\\cdots+\\phi_{hh}X_{t-h}+\\text{error}$ by OLS. The last coefficient $\\phi_{hh}$ in this regression is exactly the PACF at lag $h$ (for a stationary process, OLS converges to the population projection coefficient). Equivalently via Yule–Walker: $(\\phi_{h1},\\ldots,\\phi_{hh})^\\top=\\mathbf{R}_h^{-1}\\boldsymbol\\rho_h$ where $\\mathbf{R}_h$ is the $h\\times h$ Toeplitz ACF matrix. The last element $\\phi_{hh}$ is the PACF. Option (A): regressing $X_t$ on $X_{t-h}$ alone gives $\\rho(h)$ (the ACF), not the PACF. The PACF controls for intermediate lags – the essential difference! Memory Tip: PACF$=$ last OLS coefficient when regressing $X_t$ on lags $1,2,\\ldots,h$. ACF$=$ OLS coefficient when regressing $X_t$ on lag $h$ only (simple regression). Including intermediate lags is what “partials out” their effects, giving the partial correlation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Bayesian Model Averaging (BMA) for time series forecasting weights competing models by their posterior model probabilities $P(M_k|\\text{data})$. The BMA forecast is:',opts:['A) The forecast from the single model with highest posterior probability (MAP model)','B) The median forecast across all candidate models','C) $\\hat X_{T+h}^{BMA}=\\sum_k P(M_k|\\text{data})\\cdot\\hat X_{T+h}^{(k)}$ – weighted average over all models','D) The forecast from the model with minimum AIC'],ans:2,exp:'$\\hat X_{T+h}^{BMA}=\\sum_{k=1}^K P(M_k|\\text{data})\\cdot E[X_{T+h}|\\text{data},M_k]$. Posterior model probability: $P(M_k|\\text{data})\\propto P(\\text{data}|M_k)\\cdot P(M_k)$ (marginal likelihood $\\times$ prior). Key advantage: BMA accounts for model uncertainty – single-model forecasts ignore the probability that the selected model is wrong. BMA forecasts are calibrated to the full predictive distribution. Approximation: The BIC provides an approximation to $-2\\ln P(\\text{data}|M_k)$ (the log marginal likelihood), so $P(M_k|\\text{data})\\approx\\exp(-\\mathrm{BIC}_k/2)/\\sum_j\\exp(-\\mathrm{BIC}_j/2)$ – this motivates BIC-weighted model averaging as a practical alternative to full BMA. Memory Tip: BMA forecast = posterior-weighted average (not just the best model\'s forecast). BIC approximates log marginal likelihood $\\Rightarrow$ BIC-weights approximate posterior probabilities. Model averaging almost always beats single-model selection by reducing variance from model uncertainty.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For non-stationary processes, Priestley (1965) introduced the concept of the evolutionary spectrum $f_t(\\omega)$ which:',opts:['A) Requires the process to be Gaussian for definition','B) Is identical to the stationary spectral density (same definition applies)','C) Is defined only for processes with a linear deterministic trend','D) Represents the time-varying spectral density: how the frequency content of the process changes over time, analogous to the spectrogram in signal processing'],ans:3,exp:'For locally stationary processes: $X_t\\approx\\int A_t(\\omega)e^{i\\omega t}dZ(\\omega)$ where $A_t(\\omega)$ is a slowly time-varying amplitude. The evolutionary (local) spectral density: $f_t(\\omega)=|A_t(\\omega)|^2f_\\epsilon(\\omega)$ – a function of both $t$ and $\\omega$. Estimation: Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT) / spectrogram: compute periodogram in sliding windows of length $w$, plot as a function of both time and frequency. Alternative: Wavelet spectrum, which provides adaptive time-frequency resolution (narrow windows for high frequencies, wide windows for low frequencies). The evolutionary spectrum generalises the stationary spectrum: for stationary processes, $A_t(\\omega)=A(\\omega)$ (constant in $t$) and $f_t(\\omega)=f(\\omega)$. Memory Tip: Evolutionary spectrum $=$ time-varying version of $f(\\omega)$: $f_t(\\omega)$. Stationary: $f_t=f$ (constant in $t$). Non-stationary: $f_t$ changes over time. Estimated by sliding-window STFT (spectrogram). Useful for detecting time-varying cycles, structural breaks in the frequency domain.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The DF test statistic $\\hat\\tau=(\\hat\\phi-1)/\\mathrm{SE}(\\hat\\phi)$ for the AR(1) model under $H_0$: $\\phi=1$ has a limiting distribution that:',opts:['A) Is chi-squared $\\chi^2_1$','B) Is a Dickey–Fuller distribution: a functional of a standard Brownian motion, skewed to the left, with more negative critical values than the standard normal','C) Is standard normal $N(0,1)$ (same as for stationary AR)','D) Is a $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom'],ans:1,exp:'Under $H_0: \\phi=1$: $X_t=X_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$ (random walk). The OLS estimator $\\hat\\phi\\to1$ at rate $n$ (super-consistent: $n(\\hat\\phi-1)=O_p(1)$). The $t$-statistic: $$\\hat\\tau=\\frac{\\hat\\phi-1}{\\mathrm{SE}(\\hat\\phi)}\\xrightarrow{d}\\frac{\\int_0^1W(r)dW(r)}{\\left(\\int_0^1W(r)^2dr\\right)^{1/2}},$$ where $W(r)$ is a standard Brownian motion. This is a non-standard distribution, skewed to the left (more negative than normal). 5% critical values (no trend): $\\approx-1.95$ (less extreme than $-1.645$ for normal – wait, more negative). For DF without constant: $-1.95$; with constant: $-2.86$; with trend: $-3.41$. Memory Tip: DF distribution: NON-standard (Brownian motion functional), skewed left. Critical values are MORE negative than normal $t$-table values. Using normal critical values gives a liberal test (rejects $H_0$ too rarely). Always use DF/ADF tables, not normal or $t$ tables!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary causal ARMA($p,q$) process with Wold MA($\\infty$) representation $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\epsilon_{t-j}$, the coefficients $\\psi_j$ satisfy:',opts:['A) $|\\psi_j|\\leq C\\,r^j$ for some $C&gt;0$ and $r&lt;1$ (exponential decay)','B) $\\psi_j=j\\phi^j$ (polynomial times exponential decay)','C) $\\psi_j=0$ for all $j&gt;q$ (finite memory, MA($q$) truncation)','D) $\\psi_j=\\phi^j$ for all $j$ (AR(1)-like decay for any ARMA)'],ans:0,exp:'For ARMA($p,q$) with all AR roots outside the unit circle ($|z_k|&gt;1$), the Wold coefficients satisfy $\\psi_j=O(r^j)$ where $r=1/\\min_k|z_k|&lt;1$. Specifically: $\\psi_j=\\sum_k A_k z_k^{-j}$ (partial fraction decomposition). The dominant rate is $r=1/|z_{\\min}|$ (root closest to unit circle). This is stronger than square summability: $\\sum\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$ (certainly true) and $\\sum|\\psi_j|&lt;\\infty$ (also true for ARMA with all roots strictly outside). Option (C): only for pure MA($q$) (all AR coefficients zero). Option (D): only for AR(1) exactly. Option (B): arises with repeated roots. Memory Tip: Stationary ARMA Wold coefficients: exponential decay $|\\psi_j|\\leq Cr^j$ where $r=1/|z_{\\min}|&lt;1$ (reciprocal of nearest AR root). This guarantees absolute summability ($\\ell^1$) AND square summability ($\\ell^2$). Faster root departure from unit circle = faster $\\psi_j$ decay = shorter memory.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) $X_t=\\epsilon_t+\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$, when $\\theta&gt;0$ the spectral density achieves its maximum at:',opts:['A) $\\omega=\\pi$ (Nyquist frequency)','B) Uniformly at all frequencies (flat spectrum)','C) $\\omega=0$ (zero frequency, long-run component)','D) $\\omega=\\pi/2$'],ans:2,exp:'$f(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}|1+\\theta e^{-i\\omega}|^2=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}(1+\\theta^2+2\\theta\\cos\\omega)$. $\\frac{df}{d\\omega}=-\\frac{\\sigma^2}{\\pi}\\theta\\sin\\omega=0\\Rightarrow\\omega=0$ or $\\omega=\\pi$. At $\\omega=0$: $f(0)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}(1+\\theta)^2$. At $\\omega=\\pi$: $f(\\pi)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}(1-\\theta)^2$. For $\\theta&gt;0$: $(1+\\theta)^2&gt;(1-\\theta)^2$ $\\Rightarrow$ maximum at $\\omega=0$ (low frequencies dominate, positively correlated series, smooth). For $\\theta&lt;0$: minimum at $\\omega=0$, maximum at $\\omega=\\pi$ (high frequencies dominate, negatively correlated, choppy series). Memory Tip: MA(1): $\\theta&gt;0\\Rightarrow$peak at $\\omega=0$ (smooth). $\\theta&lt;0\\Rightarrow$peak at $\\omega=\\pi$ (choppy). Same rule as AR(1): sign of coefficient determines whether the process is “smooth” (low-freq dominated) or “choppy” (high-freq dominated). Consistent sign-to-frequency rule!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The autocovariance generating function (ACGF) of an ARMA($p,q$) process is defined as $g(z)=\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)z^h$. For ARMA($p,q$) with $\\sigma^2=1$:',opts:['A) $g(z)=\\Theta(z)/\\Phi(z)$ (the transfer function)','B) $g(z)=\\Phi(z)/\\Theta(z)$','C) $g(z)=\\Theta(z)\\Theta(z^{-1})/[\\Phi(z)\\Phi(z^{-1})]$','D) $g(z)=[\\Phi(z)\\Theta(z)]^{-1}$'],ans:2,exp:'The ACGF encodes all autocovariances: $g(z)=\\sum_{h}\\gamma(h)z^h$. For the ARMA process $\\Phi(B)X_t=\\Theta(B)\\epsilon_t$: $$g(z)=\\sigma^2\\frac{\\Theta(z)\\Theta(z^{-1})}{\\Phi(z)\\Phi(z^{-1})}.$$ Connection to spectral density: Setting $z=e^{-i\\omega}$: $g(e^{-i\\omega})=\\sum_h\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega h}=2\\pi f(\\omega)$ (by Wiener–Khinchin). So $f(\\omega)=g(e^{-i\\omega})/(2\\pi)$. The ACGF is a Laurent series (in both positive and negative powers of $z$) – the $z$-transform of the ACVF sequence. It encodes the full second-order structure compactly. Note: $\\Theta(z^{-1})$ is the time-reversed MA polynomial (adjoint). The product $\\Theta(z)\\Theta(z^{-1})$ is always real on the unit circle ($z=e^{-i\\omega}$). Memory Tip: ACGF $=\\frac{\\Theta(z)\\Theta(z^{-1})}{\\Phi(z)\\Phi(z^{-1})}$ ($\\sigma^2=1$). Setting $z=e^{-i\\omega}$: gives $2\\pi f(\\omega)$. The $z^{-1}$ ensures the result is real on the unit circle (complex conjugate). This compact formula generates all ACVF from the model polynomials.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) $=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^n|e_t/X_t|\\times100\\%$ has a well-known deficiency:',opts:['A) It requires Gaussian forecast errors','B) It is undefined or unstable when $X_t$ is near zero, and it gives asymmetric penalties (percentage error for under-forecast $\\neq$ percentage error for over-forecast by the same absolute amount)','C) It always equals the RMSE divided by the mean of $X_t$','D) It is only defined for non-negative time series'],ans:1,exp:'1. Division by zero: When $X_t\\approx0$: MAPE $\\to\\infty$. Unusable for series that cross zero (e.g. returns, temperature). 2. Asymmetry: $|e_t/X_t|$ is NOT symmetric: if $X_t=100$ and $\\hat X_t=50$ (under-forecast by 50): MAPE term $=50\\%$. If $X_t=50$ and $\\hat X_t=100$ (over-forecast by 50): MAPE term $=100\\%$. Same absolute error, but the over-forecast appears twice as bad in MAPE. Alternatives: sMAPE (symmetric MAPE): $200|e_t|/(|X_t|+|\\hat X_t|)$; MASE (Mean Absolute Scaled Error): scales by in-sample naive MAE – scale-free and avoids near-zero issues. Memory Tip: MAPE pitfalls: (1) undefined near zero; (2) asymmetric (over-forecast penalised more). Prefer: MASE (scale-free, symmetric) for general use. RMSE for variance-weighted evaluation. MAPE OK only for strictly positive series far from zero.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For quarterly data ($s=4$), after regular and seasonal differencing $(1-B)(1-B^4)X_t=W_t$, the ACF of $W_t$ shows significant spikes at lags 1 and 4 only. This suggests:',opts:['A) SARIMA$(0,1,4)(0,1,0)_4$','B) SARIMA$(0,1,1)(0,1,1)_4$ (regular MA(1) + seasonal MA(1))','C) SARIMA$(1,1,0)(1,1,0)_4$','D) SARIMA$(0,1,1)(0,1,0)_4$'],ans:1,exp:'For the multiplicative MA$(1)\\times$SMA$(1)_4$ model: $(1-\\theta B)(1-\\Theta B^4)\\epsilon_t$. Expanding: MA polynomial $=1-\\theta B-\\Theta B^4+\\theta\\Theta B^5$. The ACF of $W_t$ has spikes at lags 1, 4, and 5 (and zero elsewhere). The problem states spikes at lags 1 and 4 – suggesting the lag-5 spike is smaller (approximately zero) or that the pure MA(1)$\\times$SMA(1) is a good first approximation. Identification heuristic: $\\bullet$ Spike at lag 1 only (in ACF of $W_t$): MA(1) part. $\\bullet$ Spike at lag $s=4$: seasonal MA(1) part. $\\bullet$ Both at 1 and 4: SARIMA$(0,1,1)(0,1,1)_4$ – the airline model analogue for quarterly data. Memory Tip: Seasonal ARIMA identification after differencing: look at ACF of $W_t$. Spike at lag 1: non-seasonal MA(1). Spike at lag $s$: seasonal MA(1). Spikes at both: SARIMA(0,d,1)(0,D,1)$_s$. This is the quarterly analogue of the monthly airline model – one of the most frequently fitted seasonal models.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For monthly data (12 observations per year), the Nyquist frequency in cycles per year is:',opts:['A) 12 cycles per year (the sampling frequency itself)','B) 1 cycle per year (the annual frequency)','C) 6 cycles per year (the maximum detectable frequency: period = 2 months)','D) $1/(2\\times12)=1/24$ cycles per year'],ans:2,exp:'With sampling interval $\\Delta t=1/12$ year (monthly): Nyquist frequency $\\nu_N=\\frac{1}{2\\Delta t}=\\frac{1}{2/12}=6$ cycles per year. This corresponds to period $T_N=1/\\nu_N=1/6$ year $=2$ months – the shortest detectable cycle. In radians per sample: $\\omega_N=\\pi$ (the Nyquist frequency in discrete time is always $\\pi$). Converting: $6$ cycles/year $\\times(1/12)$ year/sample $\\times2\\pi$ radians/cycle $=\\pi$ radians/sample. ✓ Monthly data detectable range: Cycles with periods from 2 months (Nyquist) to $\\infty$ (but practically limited by $n$). Annual cycle ($T=12$ months, $\\nu=1$/year), semi-annual ($T=6$, $\\nu=2$), quarterly ($T=3$, $\\nu=4$): all detectable. Memory Tip: Nyquist $=$ half the sampling rate. Monthly: rate $=12$/year, Nyquist $=6$/year (2-month period). Quarterly: rate $=4$/year, Nyquist $=2$/year (6-month period). Annual: rate $=1$/year, Nyquist $=0.5$/year (2-year period). “Half the sampling rate” is the universal rule.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a weakly stationary process $\\{X_t\\}$, which condition characterises short memory (as opposed to long memory)?',opts:['A) $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$ (absolute summability of the ACVF)','B) $\\rho(h)\\to0$ for all $h\\geq1$','C) The spectral density $f(\\omega)$ is bounded at $\\omega=0$','D) $\\rho(h)&lt;0$ for all large $h$'],ans:0,exp:'Short memory (SM): $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$ – autocorrelations decay fast enough that their sum is finite. Equivalently: $2\\pi f(0)=\\sum\\gamma(h)&lt;\\infty$ (finite long-run variance). All ARMA processes are SM. Long memory (LM): $\\sum_{h=0}^{\\infty}|\\gamma(h)|=\\infty$ – autocorrelations decay so slowly (hyperbolically) that their sum diverges. ARFIMA with $0&lt;d&lt;0.5$ has $\\rho(h)\\sim c h^{2d-1}\\to0$ but $\\sum\\rho(h)=\\infty$. Option (B): $\\rho(h)\\to0$ is also necessary for SM but NOT sufficient – long memory processes also have $\\rho(h)\\to0$ (just slowly). Option (C): for LM, $f(0)=\\infty$ (the spectral density diverges at zero), which is the frequency-domain signature of long memory. Memory Tip: Short memory $=$ absolutely summable ACVF ($\\sum|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$). Long memory: $\\rho(h)\\to0$ but too slowly ($\\sum|\\rho(h)|=\\infty$). Test: does $f(0)&lt;\\infty$? SM: yes. LM: $f(0)=\\infty$. All ARMA is SM; ARFIMA($d&gt;0$) is LM.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Parzen lag window is defined as $w_P(h)$ for $|h|\\leq M$. For $|h|\\leq M/2$: $w_P(h)=1-6(h/M)^2+6(|h|/M)^3$; for $M/2&lt;|h|\\leq M$: $w_P(h)=2(1-|h|/M)^3$. Its main advantage over the Bartlett window is:',opts:['A) It is a fourth-order window, achieving a faster bias reduction rate ($O(M^4/n^2)$ vs $O(M^2/n)$ for Bartlett), giving a lower MSE for the same bandwidth','B) Its weights are all non-negative, unlike Bartlett which has negative weights','C) It produces a narrower main lobe (better frequency resolution)','D) It is the only window that guarantees a positive spectral estimate'],ans:0,exp:'A window is of order $k$ if $\\sum_h h^j w(h)=0$ for $j=1,\\ldots,k-1$ (zero moments). Higher order $\\Rightarrow$ lower bias for smooth spectra. Bartlett (triangular): 2nd-order, bias $=O(M^2/n)$, optimal $M\\sim n^{1/3}$, MSE $=O(n^{-2/3})$. Parzen: 4th-order, bias $=O(M^4/n^2)$, optimal $M\\sim n^{1/5}$, MSE $=O(n^{-4/5})$. Both guarantee $\\hat f(\\omega)\\geq0$ (Option C is wrong – Bartlett also guarantees non-negativity). Bartlett weights are all non-negative (Option D is wrong about Bartlett). The Parzen window achieves a better asymptotic MSE rate, making it preferable for large samples with smooth spectral densities. Memory Tip: Parzen = 4th-order window $\\Rightarrow$ MSE rate $n^{-4/5}$ (better than Bartlett\'s $n^{-2/3}$). Higher order $=$ faster convergence. Trade-off: Parzen has a wider main lobe than Bartlett for the same $M$. Parzen is the “standard” choice in many software packages.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Akaike\'s Final Prediction Error (FPE) criterion for AR($p$) order selection is:',opts:['A) $\\mathrm{FPE}(p)=\\hat\\sigma_p^2\\cdot e^{2p/n}$','B) $\\mathrm{FPE}(p)=\\hat\\sigma_p^2\\cdot\\dfrac{n+p+1}{n-p-1}$','C) $\\mathrm{FPE}(p)=\\hat\\sigma_p^2+2p\\hat\\sigma^2/n$','D) $\\mathrm{FPE}(p)=\\hat\\sigma_p^2\\cdot\\dfrac{n+p}{n-p}$'],ans:3,exp:'FPE estimates the one-step-ahead prediction error if the fitted AR($p$) model is used on a new independent series of the same length. The factor $(n+p)/(n-p)&gt;1$ penalises for the $p$ estimated AR parameters. Relation to AIC: For large $n$: $\\ln[\\mathrm{FPE}(p)]\\approx\\ln\\hat\\sigma_p^2+2p/n$, which is proportional to AIC/n. FPE and AIC select the same order asymptotically. Minimise FPE over $p=0,1,\\ldots,p_{\\max}$: the selected order $\\hat p$ balances fit ($\\hat\\sigma_p^2$ decreasing in $p$) and parsimony (factor $(n+p)/(n-p)$ increasing in $p$). FPE was the first modern information criterion for AR order selection, predating AIC (1974) by 5 years. Memory Tip: FPE $=\\hat\\sigma_p^2\\times(n+p)/(n-p)$. Penalty factor $(n+p)/(n-p)\\approx1+2p/n$ for large $n$ – matching the AIC penalty. FPE penalises estimation of $p$ parameters in two ways: reduces df for $\\hat\\sigma^2$ (denominator $n-p$) and inflates via numerator $(n+p)$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The gain function $|A(e^{-i\\omega})|$ of the first difference filter $A(B)=1-B$ (i.e. $W_t=X_t-X_{t-1}$) is:',opts:['A) $|\\cos(\\omega/2)|$ – maximum at $\\omega=0$, zero at $\\omega=\\pi$','B) $1$ for all $\\omega$ (flat gain, like a white noise filter)','C) $\\omega/(2\\pi)$ (linearly increasing)','D) $2|\\sin(\\omega/2)|$ – zero at $\\omega=0$, maximum $2$ at $\\omega=\\pi$'],ans:3,exp:'$A(e^{-i\\omega})=1-e^{-i\\omega}=e^{-i\\omega/2}(e^{i\\omega/2}-e^{-i\\omega/2})=e^{-i\\omega/2}\\cdot2i\\sin(\\omega/2)$. Gain: $|A(e^{-i\\omega})|=|e^{-i\\omega/2}|\\cdot2|\\sin(\\omega/2)|=2|\\sin(\\omega/2)|$. Key values: $\\omega=0$: gain $=0$ – the difference filter completely eliminates zero frequency (constant trends). $\\omega=\\pi$: gain $=2$ – amplifies the highest frequency (alternating component). Interpretation: Differencing is a high-pass filter. It removes trends (low frequency power) and amplifies noise (high frequency). This is why differenced financial returns appear noisier than price levels. Memory Tip: First difference gain $=2|\\sin(\\omega/2)|$. Zero at $\\omega=0$ (removes trend). Max at $\\omega=\\pi$ (amplifies noise). This is the high-pass nature of differencing. Spectral density of $\\nabla X_t$: multiply by $4\\sin^2(\\omega/2)=2(1-\\cos\\omega)$ – the formula we have used throughout!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which forecast-accuracy measure remains well-defined even when some actual values $X_t=0$, and allows meaningful comparison across series of different scales, by dividing the mean absolute error by the in-sample mean absolute error of a naive (one-step random-walk) forecast?',opts:['A) Mean Absolute Scaled Error (MASE)','B) Theil\'s $U$ statistic','C) Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)','D) Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE)'],ans:0,exp:'For non-seasonal data: $\\mathrm{MASE}=\\dfrac{\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_t|e_t|}{\\frac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{t=2}^n|X_t-X_{t-1}|}$, the forecast MAE scaled by the in-sample MAE of the naive random-walk forecast. Since the denominator never involves dividing by $X_t$ itself, MASE stays well-defined even when some $X_t=0$ – unlike MAPE (Q43), which breaks down there. $\\mathrm{MASE}&lt;1$ means the model beats the naive benchmark on average; $\\mathrm{MASE}=1$ ties it. RMSE (C) is scale-dependent (units of $X_t$), so it cannot compare series on different scales. MAPE (D) is exactly the measure whose zero-value problem MASE was built to fix. Theil\'s $U$ (B) is a related but distinct ratio-based measure and does not solve the zero-value issue directly. Memory Tip: MASE $=$ your error $\\div$ naive-forecast error, both measured in-sample by MAE. No division by $X_t$ $\\Rightarrow$ safe at zero. $&lt;1$ beats naive, $&gt;1$ loses to naive. Preferred metric in the M4/M5 forecasting competitions precisely because it fixes MAPE\'s Q43-style zero-value failure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Standard $k$-fold cross-validation is inappropriate for time series forecasting evaluation. The recommended alternative is:',opts:['A) Leave-one-out cross-validation on the full sample (same as standard CV)','B) Split the sample randomly 80/20 (train/test) and compute RMSE on the 20% test set','C) Fit the model on the full sample and evaluate residuals (in-sample fit)','D) Rolling (or expanding) origin evaluation: fit on $\\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$, forecast $\\{t+1,\\ldots,t+h\\}$, roll $t$ forward; average forecast errors across origins'],ans:3,exp:'Standard $k$-fold CV randomly assigns observations to folds – this creates future-in-past “leakage” (using future data to predict the past), violating the temporal ordering that defines forecasting. Rolling origin evaluation: 1. Set initial training period $[1, t_0]$. 2. Forecast horizon $h$ steps ahead: evaluate $\\hat X_{t_0+1},\\ldots,\\hat X_{t_0+h}$. 3. Roll origin to $t_0+1$; repeat. 4. Average evaluation metrics (MAE, RMSE) across all origins. Expanding vs rolling: Expanding window keeps all history (grows with $t$). Rolling window uses a fixed-length window (discards old data). Expanding is more common in macro; rolling is used when structural change is suspected. Memory Tip: Time series CV: rolling/expanding origin – never random split. The key principle: always train on past, evaluate on future. Standard $k$-fold: destroys temporal ordering. Rolling origin: the operational definition of genuine out-of-sample forecasting performance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Ljung–Box statistic $Q_{LB}=n(n+2)\\sum_{k=1}^m\\hat\\rho(k)^2/(n-k)$ improves on the Box–Pierce statistic $Q_{BP}=n\\sum_{k=1}^m\\hat\\rho(k)^2$ by:',opts:['A) Adding more lags to the sum','B) Using the partial autocorrelation instead of the autocorrelation','C) Subtracting a degrees-of-freedom correction for fitted ARMA parameters','D) Applying a finite-sample correction factor $(n+2)/(n-k)$ per term that makes the approximation to $\\chi^2_m$ more accurate for small $n$'],ans:3,exp:'Under $H_0$ (WN): $\\mathrm{Var}(\\hat\\rho(k))\\approx1/n$ for a pure WN series. The Box–Pierce statistic $Q_{BP}=n\\sum\\hat\\rho(k)^2$ uses this approximation directly. For small $n$: $\\mathrm{Var}(\\hat\\rho(k))\\approx(n-k)/(n(n+2))$ more accurately. Ljung and Box (1978) showed that $Q_{LB}=n(n+2)\\sum\\hat\\rho(k)^2/(n-k)$ has a better finite-sample approximation to $\\chi^2_m$. In practice: $Q_{LB}&gt;Q_{BP}$ always (the correction factor $(n+2)/(n-k)&gt;1$). The difference matters most for small $n$ or large $k/n$. For large $n$: $Q_{LB}\\approx Q_{BP}$. Recommendation: Always use Ljung–Box in practice; Box–Pierce is mostly of historical interest. Memory Tip: Ljung–Box = Box–Pierce with per-lag correction $(n+2)/(n-k)&gt;1$. LB always $\\geq$ BP. LB has better finite-sample $\\chi^2$ approximation. For large $n$: identical. Always prefer LB. The correction accounts for the fact that $\\hat\\rho(k)$ has variance slightly different from $1/n$ for finite samples.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If a stationary I(0) series $X_t$ is unnecessarily first-differenced ($W_t=\\nabla X_t$), what happens to the sample ACF of $W_t$?',opts:['A) The ACF of $W_t$ shows no autocorrelation (white noise)','B) The ACF of $W_t$ decays slower than that of $X_t$','C) The ACF of $W_t$ is identical to that of $X_t$','D) The ACF of $W_t$ has $\\rho_W(1)\\approx-0.5$ and a sharp cutoff – suggesting a non-invertible MA(1) model was introduced'],ans:3,exp:'If $X_t\\sim\\mathrm{WN}(0,\\sigma^2)$ (simplest I(0) case): $W_t=X_t-X_{t-1}$. Then: $$\\gamma_W(0)=2\\sigma^2,\\quad\\gamma_W(1)=-\\sigma^2,\\quad\\gamma_W(h)=0\\text{ for }h\\geq2.$$ $\\rho_W(1)=-1/2=-0.5$. The differenced white noise follows MA(1) with $\\theta=-1$ (unit root in the MA polynomial – non-invertible). More generally: differencing an I(0) ARMA($p,q$) gives an ARMA($p+1,q+1$) with a common factor $(1-B)$ cancelling – resulting in MA($q+1$) with a non-invertible root at $z=1$. Diagnostic: Overdifferencing is detected by a large negative $\\hat\\rho(1)\\approx-0.5$ in the differenced series ACF. AIC/BIC on the differenced series will suggest fitting MA(1) with $\\hat\\theta\\approx-1$. Memory Tip: Overdifferencing signature: $\\rho(1)\\approx-0.5$ in the differenced series. This pattern screams “you\'ve differenced too much!” A non-invertible MA root appears at $\\theta=-1$. Remedy: go back and use the original (undifferenced) series for modelling.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $Z_t=X_t+Y_t$ where $\\{X_t\\}$ and $\\{Y_t\\}$ are two independent stationary processes with ACVFs $\\gamma_X(h)$ and $\\gamma_Y(h)$, then the ACVF of $Z_t$ is:',opts:['A) $\\gamma_Z(h)=\\gamma_X(h)+\\gamma_Y(h)+2\\sqrt{\\gamma_X(h)\\gamma_Y(h)}$','B) $\\gamma_Z(h)=\\gamma_X(h)+\\gamma_Y(h)$ (sum)','C) $\\gamma_Z(h)=\\sqrt{\\gamma_X(h)^2+\\gamma_Y(h)^2}$ (Euclidean norm)','D) $\\gamma_Z(h)=\\gamma_X(h)\\cdot\\gamma_Y(h)$ (product)'],ans:1,exp:'$\\gamma_Z(h)=\\mathrm{Cov}(Z_t,Z_{t-h})=\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t+Y_t,X_{t-h}+Y_{t-h})$ $=\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t-h})+\\mathrm{Cov}(Y_t,Y_{t-h})+\\underbrace{\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,Y_{t-h})}_{=0}+\\underbrace{\\mathrm{Cov}(Y_t,X_{t-h})}_{=0}$ $=\\gamma_X(h)+\\gamma_Y(h)$. Key: Independence of $X_t$ and $Y_t$ (for all lags) gives $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,Y_s)=0$ for all $t,s$. Spectral consequence: $f_Z(\\omega)=f_X(\\omega)+f_Y(\\omega)$ – spectral densities add for independent processes. This is the basis for signal decomposition (trend + cycle + noise). Memory Tip: Independent processes: ACVFs ADD. Spectral densities ADD. This is why the spectrum of a signal + noise equals the signal spectrum plus the noise spectrum. Simple and powerful: if you know each component\'s spectrum, you can predict the total spectrum.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A practical rule for determining the number of differences $d$ in an ARIMA($p,d,q$) model is to difference until:',opts:['A) All sample ACF values are within $\\pm2/\\sqrt{n}$','B) The ADF test rejects the unit root null at 1% significance, or the ACF of $\\nabla^d X_t$ decays to zero quickly','C) The sample mean of $\\nabla^d X_t$ equals zero','D) The sample variance of $\\nabla^d X_t$ is minimised'],ans:1,exp:'Formal approach: Sequentially apply ADF (or PP, KPSS) tests. If ADF rejects $H_0$ (unit root) for $\\nabla^{d-1}X_t$ but not for $X_t$: use $d=1$. Repeat as needed. Informal approach: Plot ACF of $X_t$, $\\nabla X_t$, $\\nabla^2X_t$, \\ldots; stop differencing when the ACF of $\\nabla^d X_t$ decays rapidly (cuts off or tails off quickly) rather than decaying slowly from $\\rho(1)\\approx1$. Over-differencing warning: The sample variance of $\\nabla^d X_t$ (Option D) may initially decrease with $d$ but eventually increases – this is NOT a reliable stopping rule. Option B: ACF within $\\pm2/\\sqrt{n}$ at ALL lags would mean WN, which is too strict. Memory Tip: Differencing order $d$: use ADF/PP/KPSS sequentially OR look at ACF decay. Slow ACF decay (starts near 1) $\\Rightarrow$ difference again. Quick decay $\\Rightarrow$ stop. The ACF of an I(1) series has $\\hat\\rho(1)$ very close to 1 and decays linearly – a very distinctive pattern.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the local-to-unity framework, $\\phi=1-c/n$ for a fixed constant $c&gt;0$. As $n\\to\\infty$, the standardised process $X_{\\lfloor ns\\rfloor}/\\sqrt{n}$ converges (in distribution) to:',opts:['A) A Brownian bridge $B^0(s)=W(s)-sW(1)$','B) A deterministic function $e^{-cs}$','C) An Ornstein–Uhlenbeck (OU) process $J_c(s)$ satisfying $dJ_c=cJ_c\\,ds+dW$','D) A standard Brownian motion $W(s)$'],ans:2,exp:'With $\\phi_n=1-c/n$: $X_t=\\phi_nX_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$. As $n\\to\\infty$: $X_{\\lfloor ns\\rfloor}/\\sqrt{n\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi_n^2)}\\Rightarrow J_c(s)$ where $J_c$ satisfies $dJ_c(s)=-cJ_c(s)ds+dW(s)$ (OU process with mean-reversion coefficient $c$). Special cases: $c=0$: $\\phi=1$ (unit root) $\\Rightarrow J_0(s)=W(s)$ (standard BM). $c\\to\\infty$: $\\phi\\to0$ (stationary AR) $\\Rightarrow J_c\\to0$ (degenerate). The OU limit explains why near-unit-root processes are “in between” stationary and unit-root behaviour, giving non-standard (not normal, not Dickey–Fuller) distributions for the standard test statistics. Memory Tip: Local-to-unity: $\\phi_n=1-c/n$. Limiting process: OU (not BM). $c=0$: BM (unit root). Large $c$: stationary. OU is the continuous-time analogue of AR(1) with mean reversion. The local-to-unity framework bridges the gap between I(0) and I(1) asymptotics.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The variance ratio statistic $VR(q)=\\mathrm{Var}(X_t-X_{t-q})/(q\\cdot\\mathrm{Var}(X_t-X_{t-1}))$ equals 1 for a random walk (under $H_0$). For a stationary process with positive autocorrelations, $VR(q)$ is:',opts:['A) Exactly 1 for any stationary process','B) Less than 1 (negative autocorrelations imply less variance over longer horizons)','C) Equal to $\\rho(1)^q$ for AR(1) processes','D) Greater than 1 (positive autocorrelations cause variance to grow faster than linearly in $q$)'],ans:3,exp:'$\\mathrm{Var}(X_t-X_{t-q})=q\\sigma^2+2\\sum_{h=1}^{q-1}(q-h)\\gamma_{\\nabla X}(h)$ where $\\gamma_{\\nabla X}(h)=\\mathrm{Cov}(\\nabla X_t,\\nabla X_{t-h})$. $VR(q)=1+\\dfrac{2}{q}\\sum_{h=1}^{q-1}\\left(1-\\dfrac{h}{q}\\right)\\rho_{\\nabla X}(h)$. For positive autocorrelations in $\\nabla X$: sum is positive $\\Rightarrow VR(q)&gt;1$. This is the “momentum” effect. For negative autocorrelations (mean-reverting): sum is negative $\\Rightarrow VR(q)&lt;1$. For random walk: $\\rho_{\\nabla X}(h)=0$ for all $h\\geq1$ $\\Rightarrow VR(q)=1$ (variance is exactly linear in $q$). Memory Tip: VR test: $VR=1$ (random walk, no predictability). $VR&gt;1$ (positive serial correlation, momentum). $VR&lt;1$ (mean reversion, negative serial correlation). Used in finance to test the random walk hypothesis for asset prices. Lo–MacKinlay (1988) made VR tests popular.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If the AR polynomial $\\Phi(z)$ of an ARMA model has exactly one root at $z=1$ (a single unit root), the process $X_t$ is:',opts:['A) I(2): requires two differences to achieve stationarity','B) Weakly stationary with slowly decaying ACF','C) I(1): non-stationary, but $\\nabla X_t=(1-B)X_t$ is stationary','D) Explosive: the process grows without bound'],ans:2,exp:'If $\\Phi(z)=(1-z)\\tilde\\Phi(z)$ where all roots of $\\tilde\\Phi(z)$ lie outside the unit circle: $\\tilde\\Phi(B)\\nabla X_t=\\Theta(B)\\epsilon_t$ – the differenced series $\\nabla X_t$ is a stationary ARMA process. Number of unit roots = differencing order $d$: $d=0$: no unit roots, $X_t\\sim I(0)$ (stationary ARMA). $d=1$: one root at $z=1$, $X_t\\sim I(1)$. $d=2$: two roots at $z=1$, $X_t\\sim I(2)$ (need two differences). Option (B): stationary process has no unit roots. Option (D): explosive process has roots inside the unit circle (not at $z=1$). Memory Tip: Number of unit roots in $\\Phi(z)$ = integration order $d$. One root at $z=1$: $I(1)$, first difference gives stationarity. Two roots: $I(2)$, second difference needed. Explosive: roots inside unit circle (different from unit roots!). The location of the root determines the remedy.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the spectral density $f(\\omega)$ of a macroeconomic time series (quarterly data), a prominent peak at frequency $\\omega^*=\\pi/8$ corresponds to a dominant cycle of period:',opts:['A) 8 quarters (2 years)','B) 4 quarters (1 year)','C) 32 quarters (8 years)','D) 16 quarters (4 years)'],ans:3,exp:'The relationship between angular frequency $\\omega$ (radians per unit time) and period $T$ (time units per cycle): $$T=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.$$ At $\\omega^*=\\pi/8$: $T=2\\pi/(\\pi/8)=16$ quarters $=4$ years. Economic context: A spectral peak at 16 quarters (4 years) is consistent with the typical US business cycle duration (4–8 year cycles). The standard NBER business cycle period is 6–8 years on average. Quick reference (quarterly data): $\\omega=\\pi/2$: period 4 quarters (annual seasonal). $\\omega=\\pi/4$: period 8 quarters (biannual). $\\omega=\\pi/8$: period 16 quarters (4 years). $\\omega=\\pi/16$: period 32 quarters (8 years). Memory Tip: Period $T=2\\pi/\\omega$. Quarterly seasonal: $T=4,\\omega=\\pi/2$. Business cycle (4 yr): $T=16,\\omega=\\pi/8$. Business cycle (8 yr): $T=32,\\omega=\\pi/16$. Division: $T=4\\Rightarrow\\omega=\\pi/2$; $T=16\\Rightarrow\\omega=\\pi/8$; halving $T$ doubles $\\omega$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The standard sample ACVF $\\hat\\gamma(h)=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^{n-h}(X_t-\\bar X)(X_{t+h}-\\bar X)$ uses denominator $n$ (not $n-h$). Compared to the unbiased estimator with denominator $n-h$, this estimator:',opts:['A) Is biased toward zero but guarantees the sample Toeplitz matrix $\\hat\\Gamma_n$ is positive semidefinite (a key property for spectral estimation)','B) Is consistent only when the process is Gaussian','C) Is preferred only for small samples ($n&lt;30$)','D) Has larger variance but is unbiased'],ans:0,exp:'Biased estimator (denominator $n$): $\\hat\\gamma_b(h)=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^{n-h}(X_t-\\bar X)(X_{t+h}-\\bar X)$. $E[\\hat\\gamma_b(h)]=(1-h/n)\\gamma(h)\\to\\gamma(h)$ as $n\\to\\infty$ (asymptotically unbiased). Unbiased estimator (denominator $n-h$): $E[\\hat\\gamma_u(h)]=\\gamma(h)$ exactly. Why prefer biased? The biased estimator ensures the Toeplitz matrix $[\\hat\\gamma_b(i-j)]_{i,j}$ is PSD, which is required for: (a) Valid spectral density estimation (non-negative spectrum). (b) Valid Yule–Walker estimation (PD matrix, unique solution). The unbiased estimator can give a non-PSD matrix, leading to negative spectral estimates and invalid YW solutions. Memory Tip: Standard sample ACVF: divide by $n$ (biased but PSD). Unbiased: divide by $n-h$ (can give non-PSD matrix). The PSD property is worth the small bias for spectral and YW estimation. Almost all software uses the $n$-divisor by default for this reason.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Corner method (Beguin–Gourieroux–Monfort) for ARMA($p,q$) identification uses the determinant of the matrix $C(m,n)=[\\rho(m+i+j-1)]_{i,j=1}^{n}$. An ARMA($p,q$) process has the property that:',opts:['A) The corner occurs at $(m,n)=(q,p)$ where both $m&gt;q$ and $n&gt;p$','B) The ACF matrix $C(m,n)$ is always singular for any ARMA process','C) $\\det[C(m,n)]=0$ for all $n&gt;q$ when $m=p$, and $\\det[C(m,n)]\\neq0$ for $m&lt;p$ – the zero pattern defines the corner','D) $\\det[C(m,n)]=0$ for all $m&gt;p$ and all $n$'],ans:2,exp:'For an ARMA($p,q$) process: the ACF satisfies a linear recurrence of order $p$ for $h&gt;q$. This means the matrix $C(m,n)$ of ACF values has rank $\\leq p$ for all $m&gt;q$. Corner identification: The “corner” is the $(m,n)=(p,q)$ position where $\\det[C(p,q+1)]=0$ but $\\det[C(p-1,q)]\\neq0$. This locates both $p$ (row) and $q$ (column) simultaneously. The corner method is an alternative to ESACF for identifying the ARMA order. It uses the ACF matrix structure directly rather than pre-filtering. Special cases: AR($p$) has corner at $(p,0)$; MA($q$) has corner at $(0,q)$. Memory Tip: Corner method: find where ACF matrix determinants transition from non-zero to zero. Corner at $(p,q)$: identifies both AR and MA orders simultaneously. Like ESACF, it\'s designed for the difficult case where both ACF and PACF tail off (pure ARMA). Advanced but powerful.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a zero-mean stationary process, which statement about the $h$-step 95% prediction interval is correct?',opts:['A) For any stationary process: PI width decreases with $h$','B) For ARMA: PI width initially grows and asymptotically equals $1.96\\sqrt{\\gamma(0)}$, but may reach it slowly (AR-dominated) or fast (MA-dominated)','C) For MA($q$): PI width reaches the unconditional width $1.96\\sqrt{\\gamma(0)}$ exactly at $h=q+1$ and stays there','D) For AR($p$): PI width reaches the unconditional width at $h=p+1$ (sharp cutoff like MA)'],ans:2,exp:'MA($q$): Forecast error variance for $h\\leq q$: $\\sigma_h^2=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\psi_j^2$ (grows step by step). For $h&gt;q$: $\\sigma_h^2=\\gamma(0)$ (full unconditional variance, since the forecast is zero). Exact jump at $h=q+1$. AR($p$): PI width grows gradually, approaching $\\gamma(0)$ asymptotically (geometrically, never reaching it exactly in finite $h$). No sharp cutoff. ARMA: Starts like AR (gradual growth), but after the MA components become irrelevant ($h&gt;q$), follows the AR rate. Approaches $\\gamma(0)$ asymptotically, not sharply. Option (B): partially correct but “MA-dominated” convergence is still asymptotic – only MA($q$) has a sharp cutoff. Memory Tip: MA($q$) PI: sharp jump to unconditional width at $h=q+1$. AR($p$) PI: gradual approach (never sharp). ARMA: gradual. Only MA has the sharp PI cutoff – a direct consequence of finite memory in the MA forecast.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'$X_t$ Granger-causes $Y_t$ if:',opts:['A) The MSE of forecasting $Y_{t+1}$ using $\\{Y_t,Y_{t-1},\\ldots,X_t,X_{t-1},\\ldots\\}$ is strictly less than using $\\{Y_t,Y_{t-1},\\ldots\\}$ alone – past $X$ helps predict future $Y$ beyond $Y$\'s own past','B) $X_t$ and $Y_t$ are cointegrated','C) $X_t$ is the structural cause of $Y_t$ in the sense of a physical mechanism','D) $X_t$ and $Y_t$ are correlated contemporaneously'],ans:0,exp:'Granger (1969) defined: $X_t$ Granger-causes $Y_t$ if $\\mathrm{MSE}[\\hat Y_{t+1}|\\mathcal{F}_t^{X,Y}]&lt;\\mathrm{MSE}[\\hat Y_{t+1}|\\mathcal{F}_t^Y]$ where $\\mathcal{F}_t^{X,Y}=\\sigma(X_s,Y_s:s\\leq t)$ and $\\mathcal{F}_t^Y=\\sigma(Y_s:s\\leq t)$. Test in a bivariate VAR: fit $Y_t=\\sum_{k=1}^p(a_kY_{t-k}+b_kX_{t-k})+u_t$. $H_0$: $b_1=\\cdots=b_p=0$ (no GC). Test with $F$ or $\\chi^2$ on the joint restriction. Important caveat: Granger causality is a predictive/statistical concept, NOT physical/structural causality. $X$ Granger-causing $Y$ means $X$ has incremental predictive content for $Y$ – it does NOT mean $X$ is the structural cause. Memory Tip: Granger causality $=$ predictive precedence: does knowing past $X$ improve forecasts of $Y$ beyond $Y$\'s own past? Test: are the $b_k$ coefficients in the VAR jointly zero? It\'s a statistical concept – not physical causation. “Granger cause” $\\neq$ “cause” in the philosophical sense!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A sufficient condition for the sample ACVF $\\hat\\gamma(h)$ to converge to $\\gamma(h)$ in mean square (ergodicity in the autocovariance) is:',opts:['A) $X_t\\sim N(0,\\sigma^2)$ i.i.d. (Gaussian white noise only)','B) $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}|\\kappa(h)|&lt;\\infty$ where $\\kappa(h)=\\mathrm{Cum}(X_0,X_h,X_0,X_h)$ is the fourth-order cumulant of the process, combined with $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$','C) The process is strictly stationary and ergodic (in the measure-theoretic sense)','D) $E[X_t^4]&lt;\\infty$ (finite fourth moment)'],ans:1,exp:'The convergence of $\\hat\\gamma(h)\\xrightarrow{m.s.}\\gamma(h)$ requires controlling $\\mathrm{Var}[\\hat\\gamma(h)]$. $\\mathrm{Var}[\\hat\\gamma(h)]\\approx\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{s=-\\infty}^{\\infty}[\\gamma(s)^2+\\gamma(s+h)\\gamma(s-h)+\\kappa(s,h,-s,-h)]$ where $\\kappa(\\cdot)$ is the fourth-order cumulant (excess kurtosis structure). Sufficient conditions: $\\sum|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$ (short memory) AND $\\sum|\\kappa(h)|&lt;\\infty$ (summable fourth-order cumulant). These ensure $\\mathrm{Var}[\\hat\\gamma(h)]=O(1/n)\\to0$. Option (C): measure-theoretic ergodicity is a sufficient condition but is often hard to verify directly; the cumulant condition is more practical and applicable to ARMA processes. Memory Tip: ACVF ergodicity needs two summability conditions: ACVF ($\\sum|\\gamma|&lt;\\infty$, short memory) AND fourth-order cumulant ($\\sum|\\kappa|&lt;\\infty$, no extreme tail dependence). Both hold for all invertible ARMA processes with finite fourth moment. Practical take: ARMA $\\Rightarrow$ ACVF ergodic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a series $X_t=\\mu+u_t$ where $u_t$ follows MA(1) with $\\theta\\in(-1,0)$, the GLS (efficient) estimator of $\\mu$ is not the sample mean $\\bar X_n$. The asymptotic relative efficiency of $\\bar X_n$ compared to the GLS estimator $\\hat\\mu_{GLS}$ satisfies:',opts:['A) ARE $=\\gamma(0)/(2\\pi f(0))=(1+\\theta)^2/(1+\\theta^2+2\\theta)\\leq1$','B) ARE $&gt;1$ (sample mean is more efficient than GLS for negative $\\theta$)','C) ARE $=1$ (OLS and GLS are equally efficient for MA(1) mean estimation)','D) ARE $=|\\theta|$'],ans:0,exp:'The asymptotic variance of $\\sqrt{n}\\bar X_n$ equals $2\\pi f(0)=\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)$ (the long-run variance). The asymptotic variance of the GLS estimator of $\\mu$ is $\\gamma(0)$ (if $u_t$ were WN). ARE $=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{Var}_{GLS}}{\\mathrm{Var}_{OLS}}=\\dfrac{\\gamma(0)}{2\\pi f(0)}$. For MA(1) with $\\theta\\in(-1,0)$: $2\\pi f(0)=\\sum\\gamma(h)=\\gamma(0)+2\\gamma(1)=\\sigma^2[(1+\\theta)^2/(1-\\phi^2)$... $=(1+\\theta)^2\\sigma^2/(1-0)$. Simplifying: ARE $=(1+\\theta)^2/(1+\\theta^2)&lt;1$ for $\\theta\\neq0$. This means $\\bar X_n$ wastes information when errors are serially correlated – GLS is more efficient. Memory Tip: Asymptotic variance of $\\bar X_n$: $2\\pi f(0)$ (long-run variance). GLS variance: $\\gamma(0)/n$. ARE $=\\gamma(0)/(2\\pi f(0))$. Negative autocorrelation ($\\theta&lt;0$): $f(0)&lt;\\gamma(0)/(2\\pi)$, so ARE $&lt;1$ – OLS sample mean is LESS efficient. GLS is better whenever there is serial correlation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Suppose $X_t=s_t+n_t$ where signal $s_t$ and noise $n_t$ are independent stationary processes with spectral densities $f_s(\\omega)$ and $f_n(\\omega)$. The optimal (Wiener–Kolmogorov) linear filter $\\hat s_t=\\sum_j a_j X_{t-j}$ has frequency response:',opts:['A) $A(e^{-i\\omega})=1$ for all $\\omega$ (pass-all filter)','B) $A(e^{-i\\omega})=[f_s(\\omega)+f_n(\\omega)]/f_s(\\omega)$','C) $A(e^{-i\\omega})=f_n(\\omega)/f_s(\\omega)$','D) $A(e^{-i\\omega})=f_s(\\omega)/[f_s(\\omega)+f_n(\\omega)]$ (signal fraction of total spectrum)'],ans:3,exp:'The Wiener filter minimises $E[(s_t-\\hat s_t)^2]$ over all linear filters $A(B)$. The solution in the frequency domain is: $$A(e^{-i\\omega})=\\frac{f_s(\\omega)}{f_X(\\omega)}=\\frac{f_s(\\omega)}{f_s(\\omega)+f_n(\\omega)}.$$ Interpretation: The filter\'s gain at frequency $\\omega$ equals the signal\'s share of the total spectrum at that frequency. Where the signal dominates ($f_s\\gg f_n$): gain $\\approx1$ (pass signal). Where noise dominates ($f_n\\gg f_s$): gain $\\approx0$ (suppress noise). Signal-to-noise ratio: SNR $= f_s/f_n$. High SNR $\\Rightarrow$ gain $\\to1$. Low SNR $\\Rightarrow$ gain $\\to0$. The Wiener filter is the basis for SEATS seasonal adjustment and Kalman smoother (they are equivalent for the signal+noise model). Memory Tip: Wiener filter gain $=$ signal fraction of total power $= f_s/(f_s+f_n)$. High SNR: pass; low SNR: block. This is the most natural filter for signal extraction – it automatically adapts to the SNR at each frequency. SEATS seasonal adjustment is Wiener filtering applied to the ARIMA model decomposition.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The AR(2) model $X_t=1.2X_{t-1}-0.5X_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$ has characteristic equation $1-1.2z+0.5z^2=0$. The roots $z$ of this equation are:',opts:['A) $z=\\pm1$ (two seasonal unit roots)','B) Real roots at $z=1$ and $z=0.5$ (unit root: non-stationary)','C) Real roots both inside the unit circle (explosive)','D) Complex conjugate roots $z=1.2\\pm0.4i$, with modulus $|z|=\\sqrt{1.44+0.16}=\\sqrt{1.6}\\approx1.265&gt;1$ (stationary)'],ans:3,exp:'Solve $0.5z^2-1.2z+1=0$ (rearranging): $z=\\frac{1.2\\pm\\sqrt{1.44-2.0}}{1.0}=\\frac{1.2\\pm\\sqrt{-0.56}}{1.0}=1.2\\pm i\\sqrt{0.56}$. $\\sqrt{0.56}\\approx0.748$, so $z=1.2\\pm0.748i$. Modulus: $|z|=\\sqrt{1.2^2+0.748^2}=\\sqrt{1.44+0.560}=\\sqrt{2.0}\\approx1.414&gt;1$. ✓ Stationarity: both complex roots have modulus $&gt;1$ (outside unit circle). Process is stationary. The complex roots indicate pseudo-cyclical behaviour. Period of dominant cycle: $T=2\\pi/\\arctan(0.748/1.2)\\approx2\\pi/0.558\\approx11.3$ periods. Memory Tip: Always compute the modulus $|z|=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{Re}(z)^2+\\mathrm{Im}(z)^2}$ to check stationarity. For AR(2) with complex roots: $|z|=1/\\sqrt{|\\phi_2|}$ (since product of conjugate roots $=1/\\phi_2$). Complex roots $\\Rightarrow$ cyclical pattern in ACF and spectrum.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In forecasting competitions (e.g. M-competitions), the trimmed mean of forecasts from multiple models often outperforms sophisticated individual models. This is primarily because:',opts:['A) The trimmed mean is always equivalent to the median for symmetric forecast distributions','B) The trimmed mean uses more data than any individual model','C) Trimming removes the average, making the combination more accurate','D) Averaging reduces the variance of combined forecast errors while trimming reduces the influence of extreme/outlying forecasts, giving a robust combination with low bias and low variance'],ans:3,exp:'Bates &amp; Granger (1969) showed combination reduces variance. Empirically, even equal-weight averaging often beats sophisticated models (“forecast combination puzzle”). Trimmed mean: rank the $K$ forecasts; drop the top and bottom $\\alpha$% (say 10%); average the remaining. Benefits: $\\bullet$ Retains diversification benefit of averaging (variance reduction). $\\bullet$ Removes wildly-off-target models that might corrupt the average. $\\bullet$ Robust to specification errors and outlier models. The M4 competition (2018) showed that simple model combinations consistently outperformed sophisticated ML approaches. The winner used exponential smoothing + ARIMA with combination. Memory Tip: Trimmed mean combination = robustness + diversification. Drop extreme forecasts (trimming) to avoid being misled by outlier models; average the rest (reduces variance). The “forecast combination puzzle”: simple combinations beat complex individual models – one of the most robust findings in forecasting research.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(2) process $X_t=\\phi_1X_{t-1}+\\phi_2X_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$, the long-run variance $2\\pi f(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)^2$ equals:',opts:['A) Always less than $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2(1-\\phi_2)/[(1+\\phi_2)((1-\\phi_2)^2-\\phi_1^2)]$','B) $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)^2$, which diverges as $\\phi_1+\\phi_2\\to1$ (near unit root)','C) Equal to $\\gamma(0)$ for any stationary AR(2)','D) $\\sigma^2$ only when $\\phi_1=\\phi_2=0$ (white noise)'],ans:1,exp:'At $\\omega=0$: $|\\Phi(e^{-i\\cdot0})|^2=|1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2|^2=(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)^2$. So $2\\pi f(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)^2=\\Psi(1)^2\\sigma^2$ where $\\Psi(1)=1/(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)$. When $\\phi_1+\\phi_2\\to1$: long-run variance $\\to\\infty$ – near unit root. Compare $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2(1-\\phi_2)/[(1+\\phi_2)((1-\\phi_2)^2-\\phi_1^2)]$: the ratio $2\\pi f(0)/\\gamma(0)=\\Psi(1)^2/1$ – the long-run variance can be larger or smaller than $\\gamma(0)$ depending on whether $\\Psi(1)&gt;1$ or $&lt;1$. For $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&gt;0$: $\\Psi(1)&gt;1$, so long-run variance $&gt;$ unconditional variance. Positive autocorrelations inflate the long-run variance above the point-in-time variance. Memory Tip: AR(2) long-run variance: $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)^2$. Diverges at $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=1$ (unit root boundary). Long-run $&gt;\\gamma(0)$ when autocorrelations are predominantly positive. This is why HAC standard errors are larger than OLS when residuals have positive autocorrelation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Box–Jenkins methodology for ARIMA modelling consists of three iterative stages. The correct order is:',opts:['A) Identification $\\to$ Estimation $\\to$ Diagnostic Checking (with possible return to Identification)','B) Identification $\\to$ Diagnostic Checking $\\to$ Estimation','C) Diagnostic Checking $\\to$ Identification $\\to$ Estimation','D) Estimation $\\to$ Identification $\\to$ Diagnostic Checking'],ans:0,exp:'Stage 1 – Identification: Use ACF, PACF, ESACF, unit root tests to tentatively identify $p$, $d$, $q$. Transform to stationarity first (difference $d$ times). Stage 2 – Estimation: Estimate parameters by MLE, CLS, or YW. Compute standard errors, check parameter significance (overfitting test). Use AIC/BIC to compare candidate models. Stage 3 – Diagnostic Checking: Examine residuals via: $\\bullet$ Ljung–Box on $\\hat\\epsilon_t$ (autocorrelation). $\\bullet$ McLeod–Li on $\\hat\\epsilon_t^2$ (ARCH effects). $\\bullet$ Normality tests and time plot. If diagnostics fail: return to Stage 1 with the diagnostic information to guide model revision. This iterative cycle continues until a satisfactory model is found. Memory Tip: Box–Jenkins: Identify $\\to$ Estimate $\\to$ Diagnose (IED). “I Estimate Diagnostics” – or just remember: you can\'t estimate without identifying, and you can\'t diagnose without estimating. The cycle is iterative: failed diagnostics send you back to identification.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) process $y_t=\\Phi_1y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$ fitted by the Yule–Walker method, the sample autocorrelations are $r_1=0.5,\\ r_2=0.1$. The Yule–Walker estimate of $\\Phi_2$ is:',opts:['A) $-1/5$','B) $-3/20$','C) $0.1$ (i.e. simply $\\hat\\Phi_2=r_2$)','D) $1/5$'],ans:0,exp:'YW equations: $r_1=\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2r_1$, $r_2=\\Phi_1r_1+\\Phi_2$. From the first, $\\Phi_1=r_1(1-\\Phi_2)$; substituting gives $\\Phi_2=(r_2-r_1^2)/(1-r_1^2)$. Here: $\\Phi_2=(0.1-0.25)/(1-0.25)=-0.15/0.75=-1/5$, and $\\Phi_1=0.5(1.2)=0.6$ – a valid stationary AR(2). Memory Tip: $\\Phi_2=(r_2-r_1^2)/(1-r_1^2)$, then $\\Phi_1=r_1(1-\\Phi_2)$. Distractor C forgets the denominator; D wrongly sets $\\hat\\Phi_2=r_2$ as if AR(1).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given a realisation $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$ of a stationary process, which of the following provides the classical empirical estimator of the spectral density $f(\\omega)$, built directly from the squared modulus of the discrete Fourier transform of the data?',opts:['A) Sample partial autocorrelation function (PACF)','B) The periodogram','C) The Yule–Walker equations','D) Sample autocorrelation function (ACF)'],ans:1,exp:'The periodogram is the squared modulus of the data\'s DFT – (up to a constant) the Fourier transform of the sample ACVF $\\hat\\gamma(h)$, the direct empirical analogue of $f(\\omega)$. (D)/(A) are time-domain, not spectral. (C) gives AR parameters, not a spectral estimate directly. Caveat (Q14/Q26/Q31): raw periodogram is inconsistent; smoothing/tapering still needed. Memory Tip: Periodogram $=|\\text{DFT}|^2/n=$ Fourier transform of $\\hat\\gamma(h)$ – the basic spectral estimator (though not a consistent one alone).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider $y_t=0.3y_{t-1}+0.7y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$, where $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$ is purely random with $E(\\epsilon_t)=0,\\ E(\\epsilon_t^2)=\\sigma_\\epsilon^2$. Since $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=1$, the AR polynomial has a unit root and $y_t$ is non-stationary. The ACF of $\\Delta y_t=y_t-y_{t-1}$, say $r_k$, is:',opts:['A) $r_k=(0.7)^k$','B) $r_k=(0.3)^k$','C) $r_k=(-0.7)^k$','D) $r_k=(-0.3)^k$'],ans:2,exp:'Subtract $y_{t-1}$: $\\Delta y_t=(\\Phi_1-1)y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$. Since $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=1\\Rightarrow\\Phi_1-1=-\\Phi_2$, this becomes $\\Delta y_t=-\\Phi_2\\Delta y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$ – AR(1) with coefficient $-\\Phi_2=-0.7$, so $r_k=(-0.7)^k$. General rule: whenever $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=1$ in an AR(2), differencing once always leaves AR(1) with coefficient $-\\Phi_2$. Memory Tip: $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=1\\Rightarrow$ unit root at $z=1$. Differencing gives AR(1) in $\\Delta y_t$ with coefficient $-\\Phi_2$ (sign flip). Here: $-0.7$, alternating-sign decaying ACF.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the random-walk-with-drift model $y_t=\\delta+y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$, $t=1,2,\\ldots$, with $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$ purely random, $E(\\epsilon_t)=0$, $\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t)=\\sigma_\\epsilon^2$, suppose $\\delta=2$, $\\sigma_\\epsilon^2=5$, and $y_0=100$. Then $E(y_{25})$ and $\\mathrm{Var}(y_{25})$ are respectively:',opts:['A) $150,\\ 5$','B) $102,\\ 125$','C) $50,\\ 125$','D) $150,\\ 125$'],ans:3,exp:'Iterating: $y_t=y_0+t\\delta+\\sum_{s=1}^t\\epsilon_s$, so $E(y_t)=y_0+t\\delta$ and (uncorrelated $\\epsilon_s$) $\\mathrm{Var}(y_t)=t\\sigma_\\epsilon^2$. With $t=25$: $E(y_{25})=100+25(2)=150$; $\\mathrm{Var}(y_{25})=25(5)=125$. Both grow without bound as $t\\to\\infty$ – the hallmark of a non-stationary I(1) process. Memory Tip: $E(y_t)=y_0+t\\delta$, $\\mathrm{Var}(y_t)=t\\sigma_\\epsilon^2$. (A) forgets variance grows with $t$; (C) forgets $y_0$; (B) forgets to multiply drift by $t$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For $y_t=\\Phi_1y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t-\\theta_1\\epsilon_{t-1}-\\theta_2\\epsilon_{t-2}$ with $\\Phi_1=0.5,\\ \\Phi_2=0.3,\\ \\theta_1=0.9,\\ \\theta_2=0.3$ and $\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t)=1$, the process is:',opts:['A) Both stationary and invertible','B) Invertible but not stationary','C) Stationary but not invertible','D) Neither stationary nor invertible'],ans:2,exp:'Stationarity (AR): $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=0.8&lt;1$; $\\Phi_2-\\Phi_1=-0.2\\in(-1,1)$; $|\\Phi_2|=0.3&lt;1$ – stationary. Invertibility (MA): same triangle on $\\theta_1,\\theta_2$: need $\\theta_1+\\theta_2&lt;1$, but $0.9+0.3=1.2&gt;1$ – fails clearly, so not invertible. AR and MA conditions are checked independently (see Q34). Memory Tip: Check $(\\Phi_1,\\Phi_2)$ for stationarity and $(\\theta_1,\\theta_2)$ for invertibility separately, both via: sum$&lt;1$, difference$&lt;1$, $|\\cdot|&lt;1$. Here $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=1.2&gt;1$ is unambiguous.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For $y_t=0.7y_{t-1}+0.3y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t-0.4\\epsilon_{t-1}$, the AR characteristic equation $1-0.7z-0.3z^2=0$ has roots $z=1$ and $z=-10/3$. The correct classification of $\\{y_t\\}$ is:',opts:['A) ARIMA(2,1,0) process','B) ARIMA(0,1,2) process','C) ARMA(2,1) process','D) ARIMA(1,1,1) process'],ans:3,exp:'One root is $z=1$ (check: $1-0.7-0.3=0$). Factor: $1-0.7z-0.3z^2=(1-z)(1+0.3z)$ [expand to verify: $1+0.3z-z-0.3z^2$]. So $(1-B)(1+0.3B)y_t=\\epsilon_t-0.4\\epsilon_{t-1}$, i.e. $(1+0.3B)\\Delta y_t=\\epsilon_t-0.4\\epsilon_{t-1}$ – an ARMA(1,1) for $\\Delta y_t$. Hence $y_t\\sim\\mathrm{ARIMA}(1,1,1)$, not a "plain" ARMA(2,1) that ignores the unit root. Memory Tip: unit roots in $\\Phi(z)$ = differencing order $d$; remaining AR order $=p-d$. Here $p=2$, one unit root $\\Rightarrow d=1,\\ p{-}d=1,\\ q=1\\Rightarrow$ ARIMA$(1,1,1)$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the pure AR(2) process $y_t=0.4y_{t-1}+0.3y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$ (stationary), let $\\alpha(h)$ denote its partial autocorrelation function. Then:',opts:['A) $\\alpha(h)\\neq0$ for all $h\\geq1$','B) $\\alpha(h)=0$ for all $h\\geq1$','C) $\\alpha(h)=0$ for all $h\\geq2$','D) $\\alpha(h)=0$ for all $h\\geq3$'],ans:3,exp:'A pure AR($p$) has PACF cutting off after lag $p$: $\\alpha(1),\\ldots,\\alpha(p)$ generally nonzero, $\\alpha(h)=0$ for every $h\\geq p+1$. Here $p=2$: $\\alpha(1)$, $\\alpha(2)=\\Phi_2=0.3$ nonzero, but $\\alpha(h)=0$ for $h\\geq3$. Mirror image of MA($q$), where the ACF cuts off after lag $q$ (see Q3 on the partial-regression meaning of $\\alpha(h)$). Memory Tip: AR($p$) $\\Rightarrow$ PACF cuts off after lag $p$. MA($q$) $\\Rightarrow$ ACF cuts off after lag $q$. This mirror symmetry drives Box–Jenkins identification (Q29, Q61).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the MA(1) process written as $X_t=\\epsilon_t-\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$ (note the minus sign), with $\\theta=0.5$ and $\\sigma_\\epsilon^2=2$, the spectral density function $f(\\omega)$ is:',opts:['A) $1.25-\\cos(\\omega)$','B) $2.5-\\cos(2\\omega)$','C) $2.5-2\\cos(\\omega)$','D) $2.5+2\\cos(\\omega)$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\gamma_0=\\sigma_\\epsilon^2(1+\\theta^2)=2(1.25)=2.5$. $\\gamma_1=\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t-1})=-\\theta\\sigma_\\epsilon^2=-1$. So $f(\\omega)=\\gamma_0+2\\gamma_1\\cos\\omega=2.5-2\\cos(\\omega)$. Trap: for the plus-sign convention $X_t=\\epsilon_t+\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$ (used in Q7/Q23), the same $\\theta,\\sigma_\\epsilon^2$ instead give $f(\\omega)=2.5+2\\cos(\\omega)$ – opposite sign on the cosine term. Always check the model\'s sign before applying a memorised formula. Memory Tip: $f(\\omega)=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)\\pm2\\sigma^2\\theta\\cos\\omega$, sign matching the model ($+\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}\\Rightarrow+$; $-\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}\\Rightarrow-$). Re-derive $\\gamma_1$\'s sign; never apply a memorised $\\pm$ blindly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is a valid autocovariance sequence? (All sequences are proposed for a zero-mean stationary process with the given $\\gamma(h)$ for $h=0,1,2$ and $\\gamma(h)=\\gamma(-h)$, $\\gamma(h)=0$ for $|h|&gt;2$.)',opts:['A) $\\gamma(0)=-2,\\ \\gamma(1)=0,\\ \\gamma(2)=0$ (negative variance)','B) $\\gamma(0)=4,\\ \\gamma(1)=2,\\ \\gamma(2)=1$ (all conditions satisfied)','C) $\\gamma(0)=4,\\ \\gamma(1)=5,\\ \\gamma(2)=0$ (violates $|\\gamma(h)|\\le\\gamma(0)$)','D) $\\gamma(0)=4,\\ \\gamma(1)=-3,\\ \\gamma(2)=2$ (must check PSD)'],ans:1,exp:'A valid ACVF must satisfy: (i) $\\gamma(0)\\ge0$; (ii) $|\\gamma(h)|\\le\\gamma(0)$; (iii) positive semi-definite (PSD). (C): $|\\gamma(1)|=5&gt;4=\\gamma(0)$ – violates (ii). (A): $\\gamma(0)=-2&lt;0$ – violates (i). (D): $|\\gamma(1)|=3\\le4$, $|\\gamma(2)|=2\\le4$ ✓ but PSD fails: the $3\\times3$ Toeplitz matrix $\\begin{pmatrix}4&amp;-3&amp;2\\\\-3&amp;4&amp;-3\\\\2&amp;-3&amp;4\\end{pmatrix}$ has negative determinant. (B): $2\\le4$, $1\\le4$; Toeplitz matrix is diagonally dominant and PSD ✓. Memory Tip: ACVF must be PSD. Quick check: $\\gamma(0)\\ge|\\gamma(1)|$ and $\\gamma(0)\\ge|\\gamma(2)|$ are necessary but NOT sufficient. Always verify the Toeplitz matrix is PSD.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary process with $\\rho(1)=0.8$ and $\\rho(2)=0.5$, the PACF at lag 2 is $\\phi_{22}=$:',opts:['A) $0.5$','B) $0.8\\times0.5=0.4$','C) $0$','D) $\\dfrac{0.5-0.64}{1-0.64}=\\dfrac{-0.14}{0.36}\\approx-0.389$'],ans:3,exp:'The Levinson–Durbin recursion gives: $$\\phi_{22}=\\frac{\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2}{1-\\rho(1)^2}=\\frac{0.5-0.64}{1-0.64}=\\frac{-0.14}{0.36}\\approx-0.389$$ Interpretation: after removing the effect of $X_{t-1}$, the remaining association between $X_t$ and $X_{t-2}$ is negative, even though $\\rho(2)=0.5&gt;0$. Note: $\\phi_{11}=\\rho(1)=0.8$ (lag-1 PACF always equals ACF at lag 1). Memory Tip: $\\phi_{22}=\\dfrac{\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2}{1-\\rho(1)^2}$. Numerator $=$ “residual ACF at lag 2 after removing lag-1 AR effect.” Can be negative even when $\\rho(2)&gt;0$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In a correlogram, the dashed lines at $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{n}$ represent:',opts:['A) The exact bounds of all possible ACF values','B) Two standard deviations of the time series itself','C) The 95% simultaneous confidence band for the entire ACF function','D) The 95% pointwise confidence limits for individual sample ACF values under the white noise null hypothesis'],ans:3,exp:'Under white noise: $\\hat{\\rho}(h)\\approx N(0,1/n)$, so $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{n}$ gives a 95% pointwise interval for each lag separately. Important caveat: These are NOT simultaneous bands. If we test $m$ lags, we expect about $0.05m$ spurious exceedances by chance even for true WN. For $m=20$: expect $\\approx1$ spurious spike. (C) would require wider Bonferroni or Simes-corrected bands. Memory Tip: The dashed lines test each lag individually. One or two marginal exceedances in 20 lags may be noise. Several exceedances, or a spike at a theoretically meaningful lag, signals structure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the MA(1) process $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$, the maximum possible value of $|\\rho(1)|$ over all $\\theta$ is:',opts:['A) $1$ (can be any value in $[-1,1]$)','B) $1/\\sqrt{2}\\approx0.707$','C) $2/3$','D) $0.5$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\rho(1)=\\dfrac{\\theta}{1+\\theta^2}$. Differentiating and setting to zero: $\\dfrac{d}{d\\theta}\\dfrac{\\theta}{1+\\theta^2}=\\dfrac{1-\\theta^2}{(1+\\theta^2)^2}=0\\Rightarrow\\theta=\\pm1$. At $\\theta=1$: $\\rho(1)=1/2=0.5$. At $\\theta=-1$: $\\rho(1)=-0.5$. Key implication: If $|\\hat{\\rho}(1)|&gt;0.5$, the data cannot be from a pure MA(1) – a higher-order model is needed. Memory Tip: MA(1) ACF at lag 1 is bounded in $[-0.5,+0.5]$. The bound is achieved at $\\theta=\\pm1$ (the non-invertible boundary). “Half is the most an MA(1) can correlate.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a zero-mean AR(1) process $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ with $|\\phi|&lt;1$, multiplying both sides by $X_{t-k}$ and taking expectations for $k=1$ gives the Yule–Walker equation:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(1)=\\phi\\gamma(1)+\\sigma^2$','B) $\\gamma(1)=\\phi\\gamma(0)$','C) $\\gamma(1)=\\phi\\gamma(0)+\\sigma^2$','D) $\\gamma(1)=\\phi^2\\gamma(0)$'],ans:1,exp:'$E[X_t X_{t-1}]=\\phi E[X_{t-1}^2]+E[\\varepsilon_t X_{t-1}]$ Since $\\varepsilon_t$ is independent of $X_{t-1}$: $E[\\varepsilon_t X_{t-1}]=0$. Therefore: $\\gamma(1)=\\phi\\gamma(0)\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\rho(1)=\\phi$ For $k=0$: $\\gamma(0)=\\phi\\gamma(1)+\\sigma^2=\\phi^2\\gamma(0)+\\sigma^2\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$ ✓ Option (C) is the $k=0$ equation (not $k=1$). Option (A) is incorrect because $E[\\varepsilon_t X_{t-1}]\\ne\\sigma^2$ for $k=1$. Memory Tip: For $k\\ge1$ in AR(1): $\\gamma(k)=\\phi\\gamma(k-1)$ (no $\\sigma^2$ term – the innovation $\\varepsilon_t$ is uncorrelated with $X_{t-k}$ when $k\\ge1$).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the Wold decomposition $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}+V_t$, the linearly deterministic component $V_t$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $V_t$ can be perfectly predicted (zero prediction error) from its own infinite past $V_{t-1},V_{t-2},\\ldots$','B) $V_t$ is white noise with zero mean','C) $V_t$ and the stochastic part $\\sum\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ are correlated','D) $V_t$ is a fixed constant (deterministic trend)'],ans:0,exp:'$V_t$ is called linearly deterministic: $E[(V_t - \\hat{V}_t)^2]=0$ where $\\hat{V}_t$ is the BLP of $V_t$ from $\\{V_{t-1},V_{t-2},\\ldots\\}$. Examples of $V_t$: a deterministic sinusoid $A\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi)$, or a process with line spectrum (hidden periodicities). Key property: $V_t$ and $\\{\\varepsilon_s\\}$ are uncorrelated for all $t,s$ (the two parts are orthogonal). Most observed time series have $V_t=0$ (purely non-deterministic – Wold\'s theorem applies cleanly). Memory Tip: Linearly deterministic $=$ zero prediction error from infinite past. Think: a perfect sine wave – knowing the past, you can predict the future exactly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an MA(3) process $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\theta_2\\varepsilon_{t-2}+\\theta_3\\varepsilon_{t-3}$, the first non-zero lag at which the ACF is exactly zero is:',opts:['A) never zero','B) $h=1$','C) $h=3$','D) $h=4$ (all lags $|h|&gt;3$ are zero)'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(h)=\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t-h})=E\\!\\left[(\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\cdots+\\theta_3\\varepsilon_{t-3})(\\varepsilon_{t-h}+\\cdots+\\theta_3\\varepsilon_{t-h-3})\\right]$ For $|h|&gt;3$: the two windows of innovations $\\{t,t-1,t-2,t-3\\}$ and $\\{t-h,\\ldots,t-h-3\\}$ do not overlap $\\Rightarrow$ all inner products are zero $\\Rightarrow$ $\\gamma(h)=0$. For $h=4$: no overlap. For $h=3$: only $\\varepsilon_{t-3}$ overlaps, so $\\gamma(3)=\\theta_3\\sigma^2\\ne0$ (in general). Memory Tip: MA($q$) memory $=$ exactly $q$ lags. ACF is non-zero for $h=1,\\ldots,q$ and exactly zero for $|h|&gt;q$. The “window” analogy: two windows of width $q+1$ stop overlapping at lag $q+1$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(2) process, the PACF satisfies $\\phi_{hh}=0$ for:',opts:['A) All $h\\ge2$ (PACF is zero from lag 2 onwards)','B) $h=1$ only (only the first PACF is non-zero)','C) $h&gt;2$ (PACF is zero beyond lag 2 – the AR order)','D) All $h\\ge1$ (PACF is identically zero for AR(2))'],ans:2,exp:'For a true AR($p$): the PACF $\\phi_{pp}=\\phi_p\\ne0$ (the last AR coefficient) and $\\phi_{hh}=0$ for all $h&gt;p$. For AR(2): $\\phi_{11}=\\phi_1^*\\ne0$, $\\phi_{22}=\\phi_2\\ne0$, $\\phi_{33}=\\phi_{44}=\\cdots=0$. The sample PACF $\\hat{\\phi}_{hh}\\approx N(0,1/n)$ for $h&gt;2$ – this gives the $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{n}$ diagnostic band. Counting the number of sample PACF spikes outside the band gives the estimate of $p$. Memory Tip: PACF of AR($p$): non-zero for lags $1,2,\\ldots,p$; exactly zero for lags $&gt;p$. “PACF reveals the AR order – count the spikes.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the stationary AR(2) process $X_t=\\phi_1 X_{t-1}+\\phi_2 X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$ with $\\mathrm{Var}(\\varepsilon_t)=\\sigma^2$, the variance $\\gamma(0)$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2(1-\\phi_2)}{(1+\\phi_2)[(1-\\phi_2)^2-\\phi_1^2]}$','B) $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)^2$','C) $\\gamma(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{1-\\phi_1^2-\\phi_2^2}$','D) $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi_1^2)$'],ans:0,exp:'From YW: $\\gamma(0)=\\phi_1\\gamma(1)+\\phi_2\\gamma(2)+\\sigma^2$, combined with $\\gamma(1)=\\phi_1\\gamma(0)+\\phi_2\\gamma(1)$ and $\\gamma(2)=\\phi_1\\gamma(1)+\\phi_2\\gamma(0)$. Solving this $3\\times3$ system (or using the known closed form for AR(2)) gives option (A). Option (C) would apply only if $\\phi_1\\phi_2=0$ (no cross-product terms). Option (D) is the AR(1) formula. Memory Tip: AR(2) variance is more complex than AR(1) due to interaction between $\\phi_1$ and $\\phi_2$. In exams, derive via YW system rather than memorising the closed form.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The MA(2) process $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\theta_2\\varepsilon_{t-2}$ is invertible iff all roots of $1+\\theta_1z+\\theta_2z^2=0$ lie outside the unit circle. This gives the invertibility region (triangle):',opts:['A) $|\\theta_1\\theta_2|&lt;1$','B) $|\\theta_2|&lt;1$, $\\theta_1+\\theta_2&gt;-1$, $\\theta_2-\\theta_1&gt;-1$','C) $\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2&lt;1$','D) $|\\theta_1|&lt;1$ and $|\\theta_2|&lt;1$'],ans:1,exp:'The roots of $\\Theta(z)=1+\\theta_1z+\\theta_2z^2$ must lie outside the unit circle $|z|&gt;1$. Substituting $z=1$: $1+\\theta_1+\\theta_2\\ne0$; $z=-1$: $1-\\theta_1+\\theta_2\\ne0$; $|z|&gt;1$ gives the three strict inequalities in (B). Note the perfect analogy with the AR(2) stationarity triangle (replace $\\phi_i$ by $\\theta_i$ and reverse signs appropriately). Memory Tip: MA(2) invertibility triangle $\\equiv$ AR(2) stationarity triangle with $\\theta$ replacing $\\phi$. Same three boundary conditions, same triangular region. Duality of AR stationarity and MA invertibility!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ARMA(1,1) process $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ (stationary and invertible), the variance $\\gamma(0)$ is:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$','B) $\\sigma^2\\dfrac{1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2}{1-\\phi^2}$','C) $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi\\theta)^2$','D) $\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)$'],ans:1,exp:'Using the Wold representation $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ with $\\psi_0=1$, $\\psi_j=(\\phi+\\theta)\\phi^{j-1}$ for $j\\ge1$: $$\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j^2=\\sigma^2\\left[1+(\\phi+\\theta)^2\\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}\\phi^{2(j-1)}\\right]=\\sigma^2\\left[1+\\frac{(\\phi+\\theta)^2}{1-\\phi^2}\\right]$$ $=\\sigma^2\\dfrac{1-\\phi^2+\\phi^2+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2}{1-\\phi^2}=\\sigma^2\\dfrac{1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2}{1-\\phi^2}$ ✓ Memory Tip: ARMA(1,1) variance $=$ AR(1) variance $\\times$ correction for MA: $\\gamma(0)=[\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)]\\times(1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2)$. When $\\theta=0$: reduces to AR(1) formula ✓.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Two necessary (but not sufficient) conditions for an AR($p$) process $X_t=\\sum_{j=1}^p\\phi_j X_{t-j}+\\varepsilon_t$ to be stationary are:',opts:['A) $\\sum_{j=1}^p\\phi_j&lt;1$ and $\\sum_{j=1}^p(-1)^j\\phi_j&lt;1$','B) $|\\phi_j|&lt;1$ for all $j=1,\\ldots,p$ individually','C) $|\\phi_1|&lt;1$ and $|\\phi_p|&lt;1$','D) $\\phi_1&gt;0$ and $\\phi_p&gt;0$'],ans:0,exp:'The characteristic polynomial $\\Phi(z)=1-\\phi_1z-\\cdots-\\phi_pz^p$ must be non-zero for $|z|\\le1$. In particular at $z=1$: $\\Phi(1)=1-\\sum\\phi_j&gt;0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\sum\\phi_j&lt;1$. At $z=-1$: $\\Phi(-1)=1-\\sum(-1)^j\\phi_j&gt;0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\sum(-1)^j\\phi_j&lt;1$. These are necessary but not sufficient – all roots of $\\Phi(z)$ must be checked (there can be complex roots inside the unit circle even when both boundary conditions hold). Memory Tip: $z=1$ gives $\\sum\\phi_j&lt;1$ (prevents ordinary unit root). $z=-1$ gives $\\sum(-1)^j\\phi_j&lt;1$ (prevents seasonal-2 unit root). For AR(2): these plus $|\\phi_2|&lt;1$ give the full triangle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The corrected AIC (AICc), designed for small samples, is defined as:',opts:['A) $\\mathrm{AICc}=\\mathrm{BIC}+2k$','B) $\\mathrm{AICc}=\\mathrm{AIC}-\\dfrac{2k}{n}$','C) $\\mathrm{AICc}=\\mathrm{AIC}+\\dfrac{2k(k+1)}{n-k-1}$, where $k$ is the number of parameters','D) $\\mathrm{AICc}=-2\\ln\\hat{L}+k\\ln n+k$'],ans:2,exp:'AICc converges to AIC as $n\\to\\infty$ (the correction $2k(k+1)/(n-k-1)\\to0$). For small $n$, AIC underpenalises complexity – AICc adds a heavier penalty to guard against overfitting. Rule of thumb: use AICc when $n/k&lt;40$. In practice, AICc is preferred whenever the sample is not very large relative to the number of parameters. AICc is always $\\ge$ AIC; the gap vanishes for large $n$. Memory Tip: AICc $=$ AIC $+$ extra penalty for small $n$. “C” for “Corrected”. As $n\\to\\infty$: AICc $\\to$ AIC. Prefer AICc routinely unless $n$ is very large.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Yule–Walker estimates for an AR($p$) model are guaranteed to produce:',opts:['A) Parameter estimates that are more efficient than maximum likelihood estimates','B) The minimum variance unbiased estimator of $\\phi_1,\\ldots,\\phi_p$','C) Unbiased estimates for all sample sizes $n$','D) Estimates that always satisfy the stationarity conditions (roots outside unit circle)'],ans:3,exp:'The YW system $\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\Gamma}}_p\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\phi}}=\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\gamma}}_p$ always has a positive definite coefficient matrix $\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\Gamma}}_p$ (since $\\hat{\\gamma}(h)$ is computed with divisor $n$, ensuring PSD). The resulting estimates necessarily satisfy the stationarity conditions. By contrast, OLS (conditional MLE) can occasionally produce estimates outside the stationarity region for small samples. Downside: YW can have larger bias than MLE, especially for $\\phi$ near the stationarity boundary. Memory Tip: YW guarantee: always stationary. Trade-off: more biased than MLE. “Safe but not optimal.” OLS/MLE: potentially more efficient but no stationarity guarantee in finite samples.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the invertible MA(1) process $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$, the 2-step-ahead forecast $\\hat{X}_{n+2|n}$ is:',opts:['A) $\\theta\\hat{\\varepsilon}_n$','B) $\\theta^2\\hat{\\varepsilon}_n$','C) $\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}$','D) $0$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=E[X_{n+h}|\\mathcal{F}_n]$ where $\\mathcal{F}_n=\\sigma(X_n,X_{n-1},\\ldots)$. $X_{n+2}=\\varepsilon_{n+2}+\\theta\\varepsilon_{n+1}$: both $\\varepsilon_{n+2}$ and $\\varepsilon_{n+1}$ are future innovations, uncorrelated with $\\mathcal{F}_n$. $\\Rightarrow\\ \\hat{X}_{n+2|n}=0$ (the process mean, assumed 0). Compare: $\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}=\\theta\\hat{\\varepsilon}_n$ (only one future innovation $\\varepsilon_{n+1}$ is unknown – the correction uses the estimated current innovation). Memory Tip: MA($q$) forecasts: $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=0$ for all $h&gt;q$. Only the first $q$ steps ahead benefit from the MA structure. Beyond that: forecast the mean.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(2) process $X_t=\\phi_1 X_{t-1}+\\phi_2 X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$ with $\\sigma^2=1$, the long-run variance $2\\pi f(0)=\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)$ equals:',opts:['A) $1/(1-\\phi_1^2-\\phi_2^2)$','B) $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi_1\\phi_2)$','C) $1/(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)^2$','D) $(1+\\phi_2)/[(1-\\phi_2)(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)(1+\\phi_1-\\phi_2)]$'],ans:2,exp:'At $\\omega=0$: $e^{i\\cdot0}=1$, so $\\Phi(e^{-i\\cdot0})=\\Phi(1)=1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2$. $f(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi|\\Phi(1)|^2}=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)^2}$ $\\Rightarrow 2\\pi f(0)=\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)=\\dfrac{1}{(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)^2}$ This equals $[\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j]^2\\cdot\\sigma^2$ where $\\psi_j=\\Phi^{-1}(B)$\'s coefficients – the long-run multiplier squared. Memory Tip: Long-run variance $=1/(1-\\sum\\phi_j)^2$ for AR($p$). As $\\sum\\phi_j\\to1$ (near unit root), long-run variance $\\to\\infty$. The denominator is $\\Phi(1)^2$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(1) process $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ ($\\sigma^2=1$, $-1&lt;\\phi&lt;0$), the spectral density at the Nyquist frequency $\\omega=\\pi$ is:',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1-\\phi^2)}$','B) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}$','C) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1+\\phi)^2}$','D) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1-\\phi)^2}$'],ans:2,exp:'At $\\omega=\\pi$: $\\cos\\pi=-1$, so $1-2\\phi\\cos\\pi+\\phi^2=1+2\\phi+\\phi^2=(1+\\phi)^2$. $f(\\pi)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi(1+\\phi)^2}$ For $\\phi&lt;0$: $(1+\\phi)^2&lt;(1-\\phi)^2$, so $f(\\pi)&gt;f(0)$ – the spectrum peaks at the Nyquist frequency (high-frequency oscillations dominate). Contrast with $\\phi&gt;0$ where $f(0)&gt;f(\\pi)$ (low-frequency power dominates). Memory Tip: AR(1) spectrum at $\\omega=0$: denominator $(1-\\phi)^2$. At $\\omega=\\pi$: denominator $(1+\\phi)^2$. Negative $\\phi\\Rightarrow$ small denominator at $\\pi\\Rightarrow$ large $f(\\pi)$ (blue spectrum).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The periodogram cannot be computed at $\\omega_0=0$ for a zero-mean series because:',opts:['A) $I(0)=n\\bar{x}^2/(2\\pi)$, which equals zero only when $\\bar{x}=0$ (mean is subtracted first, making $I(0)=0$ by convention)','B) The DFT at $\\omega=0$ always equals $n$ regardless of the data','C) The spectral density is undefined at $\\omega=0$','D) The periodogram is only defined for negative frequencies'],ans:0,exp:'The DFT at $\\omega=0$: $\\sum_{t=1}^n x_t e^{0}=\\sum x_t=n\\bar{x}$. $I(0)=\\dfrac{1}{n}|n\\bar{x}|^2/(2\\pi)\\cdot 2\\pi=n\\bar{x}^2/(2\\pi)$: this estimates the squared mean, not the spectral density of the zero-mean fluctuations. In practice: the sample mean is subtracted before spectral analysis, making $I(0)=0$ trivially. The analysis uses only Fourier frequencies $\\omega_k=2\\pi k/n$ for $k=1,\\ldots,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor$. Memory Tip: Always subtract the mean before computing the periodogram. $I(0)$ captures the mean level, not spectral content. Spectral analysis is about fluctuations around the mean.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density of an ARMA($p,q$) process can equal zero at some frequency $\\omega^*$ only if:',opts:['A) The process is non-stationary at frequency $\\omega^*$','B) The AR polynomial has a root on the unit circle at $e^{i\\omega^*}$','C) The MA polynomial $\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega^*})=0$, i.e., the MA polynomial has a root on the unit circle','D) $\\sigma^2=0$'],ans:2,exp:'$f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\dfrac{|\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}{|\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}$ $f(\\omega^*)=0\\Leftrightarrow|\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega^*})|^2=0\\Leftrightarrow\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega^*})=0$ – a root of the MA polynomial on the unit circle. (B) is wrong: AR root on unit circle makes $|\\Phi|=0$ and $f(\\omega^*)=\\infty$ (a spectral pole/spike), not zero. Non-invertible MA processes (roots exactly on unit circle) have spectral zeros. Memory Tip: MA root on unit circle $\\Rightarrow f=0$ (spectral zero, “notch”). AR root on unit circle $\\Rightarrow f=\\infty$ (spectral pole, “spike”). MA: numerator zeros; AR: denominator zeros.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The lag-window (Blackman–Tukey) spectral estimator is defined as:',opts:['A) $\\hat{f}_{BT}(\\omega)=\\dfrac{1}{2m+1}\\sum_{|j|\\le m}I(\\omega_j)$ (Daniell smoother)','B) $\\hat{f}_{BT}(\\omega)=I(\\omega)/\\sigma^2$','C) $\\hat{f}_{BT}(\\omega)=\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^n x_t e^{-i\\omega t}$','D) $\\hat{f}_{BT}(\\omega)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{h=-(M-1)}^{M-1}\\lambda\\!\\left(\\dfrac{h}{M}\\right)\\hat{\\gamma}(h)e^{-i\\omega h}$'],ans:3,exp:'Blackman–Tukey (lag-domain): Apply window $\\lambda(h/M)$ to $\\hat{\\gamma}(h)$, then take FT. The truncation point $M$ controls bias/variance trade-off. Daniell (frequency-domain): Average adjacent periodogram ordinates. Option (A) is the Daniell smoother – equivalent to a specific lag window. The two approaches are equivalent: smoothing in frequency domain $\\equiv$ multiplying by a window in lag domain (Fourier duality). Memory Tip: BT = “FT of tapered ACVF.” Daniell = “moving average of periodogram.” They achieve the same goal via Fourier duality: smooth either the ACVF (time side) or periodogram (frequency side).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'After applying one difference $W_t=\\nabla X_t=(1-B)X_t$ to a non-stationary $X_t\\sim\\mathrm{ARIMA}(1,1,0)$, the sample ACF of $W_t$ is expected to show:',opts:['A) Exponential decay from lag 1, consistent with a stationary AR(1) process','B) All spikes within the confidence bands (white noise)','C) Slow geometric decay (still non-stationary)','D) A single spike at lag 1 and zero elsewhere (MA(1) pattern)'],ans:0,exp:'ARIMA(1,1,0): $\\Phi(B)(1-B)X_t=\\varepsilon_t$, so $W_t=(1-B)X_t$ satisfies $(1-\\phi B)W_t=\\varepsilon_t$, i.e., $W_t\\sim\\mathrm{AR}(1)$ with parameter $\\phi$. The ACF of $W_t=\\hat{\\rho}_W(h)=\\phi^{|h|}$ – exponential decay pattern. The PACF of $W_t$ cuts off after lag 1 ($\\hat{\\phi}_{11}=\\phi$, zero beyond). Confirming this pattern after differencing validates that $d=1$ was the correct choice. Memory Tip: ARIMA($p,d,q$) $\\xrightarrow{\\nabla^d}$ ARMA($p,q$). For ARIMA(1,1,0): one difference reveals AR(1) with geometric ACF decay and PACF cutoff at lag 1.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the random walk $X_t=X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ ($\\sigma^2=1$), the $h$-step-ahead forecast error variance is:',opts:['A) $1$ (constant)','B) $2^h$ (grows exponentially)','C) $h$ (grows linearly)','D) $h^2$ (grows quadratically)'],ans:2,exp:'Optimal forecast: $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=X_n$ (the last observed value, since future increments have mean 0). Error: $X_{n+h}-X_n=\\sum_{j=1}^{h}\\varepsilon_{n+j}$, a sum of $h$ iid $(0,\\sigma^2)$ innovations. $\\mathrm{Var}(X_{n+h}-\\hat{X}_{n+h|n})=h\\sigma^2$ 95% PI: $X_n\\pm1.96\\sqrt{h}\\sigma$ – the classic fan-shaped interval that widens as $\\sqrt{h}$. Memory Tip: RW forecast error std dev $=\\sigma\\sqrt{h}$ (grows as $\\sqrt{h}$). Variance grows as $h$. The PI fans out like a cone – wider the further ahead you forecast.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A sufficient condition for a stationary process to be ergodic for the mean (i.e., $\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^n X_t\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{a.s.}}\\mu$) is:',opts:['A) The process has finite fourth moments','B) The ACF is non-negative for all lags','C) The process is Gaussian','D) The ACVF is absolutely summable: $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$'],ans:3,exp:'When $\\sum_{h}|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$: $$\\mathrm{Var}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^n X_t\\right)=\\frac{1}{n^2}\\sum_{t,s}\\gamma(t-s)=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{|h|&lt;n}\\left(1-\\frac{|h|}{n}\\right)\\gamma(h)\\to0$$ The variance of the sample mean $\\to0$, ensuring convergence in mean square (and with additional conditions, a.s.). (C) is insufficient alone: Gaussianity without summable ACVF (e.g., long memory) may not ensure ergodicity. Memory Tip: Summable ACVF $\\Rightarrow$ variance of sample mean $\\to 0\\Rightarrow$ ergodic for mean. Short-memory ARMA processes satisfy this. Long-memory (ARFIMA with $d&gt;0$) may not.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The MA(1) model with $\\theta=3$ is non-invertible. The ACF at lag 1 is $\\rho(1)=3/(1+9)=0.3$. The invertible MA(1) with the same ACF has parameter $\\theta^*$ equal to:',opts:['A) $\\theta^*=-1/3$','B) $\\theta^*=-3$','C) $\\theta^*=1/3$','D) $\\theta^*=3$'],ans:2,exp:'For MA(1): $\\rho(1)=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$ is symmetric in $\\theta$ and $1/\\theta$: At $\\theta^*=1/3$: $\\rho^*(1)=\\frac{1/3}{1+1/9}=\\frac{1/3}{10/9}=\\frac{3}{10}=0.3$ ✓ Noise variance rescaling: $\\sigma^{*2}=\\theta^2\\sigma^2=9\\sigma^2$ ensures the unconditional variance matches. The invertible representation uses $|\\theta^*|=1/3&lt;1$, satisfying the invertibility condition. Memory Tip: Non-invertible $\\theta$ and invertible $\\theta^*=1/\\theta$ give the same ACF. Same sign (both positive here), reciprocal magnitude. Always pick $|\\theta^*|&lt;1$ for unique identification.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A key motivation for using ARMA($p,q$) models rather than pure AR($P$) models is:',opts:['A) ARMA always fits better than AR regardless of sample size','B) An ARMA($p,q$) model can often represent a time series with fewer parameters than a high-order AR model (parsimony – approximating MA($\\infty$) by a rational transfer function)','C) ARMA models are always stationary; AR models are not','D) ARMA models do not require estimation of the noise variance $\\sigma^2$'],ans:1,exp:'The Wold MA($\\infty$) representation requires infinitely many parameters. A pure AR($P$) approximation needs $P$ potentially large. An ARMA($p,q$) with $p+q\\ll P$ can approximate the same dynamics via a rational function $\\Theta(z)/\\Phi(z)$, analogous to rational function approximation in mathematics. Example: AR($\\infty$) of an MA(1) requires $\\pi_j=-(-\\theta)^j$ for all $j$ – infinitely many terms. ARMA(0,1) captures it with ONE parameter $\\theta$. Memory Tip: ARMA = “rational function approximation” of the Wold MA($\\infty$). Like approximating $e^x$ by a rational function $P(x)/Q(x)$ – far more efficient than a power series truncation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The relationship between the spectral density $f(\\omega)$ and the variance $\\gamma(0)$ of a stationary process is:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(0)=2\\pi f(0)$','B) $\\gamma(0)=f(0)$','C) $\\gamma(0)=\\sum_{\\omega}f(\\omega)$ (discrete sum over all frequencies)','D) $\\gamma(0)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)\\,d\\omega$'],ans:3,exp:'From the inversion (Wiener–Khinchin): $\\gamma(h)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)e^{i\\omega h}d\\omega$. Setting $h=0$: $e^{i\\cdot0\\cdot\\omega}=1$, so $\\gamma(0)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)d\\omega$. This is why $f(\\omega)\\ge0$ is essential: a “negative probability” interpretation would arise if $f&lt;0$ somewhere (negative variance contribution). Equivalently: $\\gamma(0)=\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ is the total “power” in the signal, spread across frequencies. Memory Tip: “Variance = area under spectrum.” The spectrum decomposes variance by frequency: $d(\\text{variance at }\\omega)=f(\\omega)d\\omega$. Integrating over all $\\omega$ gives total variance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In which situation does the difference between exact and conditional MLE for ARMA parameters become most important?',opts:['A) For pure MA models regardless of sample size','B) When the process is Gaussian with zero mean','C) For large sample sizes ($n&gt;500$) and low-order models','D) For small sample sizes ($n&lt;50$) and AR parameters near the unit circle boundary (near-unit-root case)'],ans:3,exp:'Conditional MLE sets pre-sample values to their unconditional means (or zero). When $\\phi$ is near 1, the unconditional variance $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)\\to\\infty$ – the initial distribution dominates the likelihood, making this approximation poor. Exact MLE uses the correct (Gaussian) initial distribution $X_1\\sim N(\\mu,\\gamma(0))$, capturing the information in the first few observations properly. For large $n$: initial conditions contribute $O(1/n)$ to the log-likelihood; exact and conditional MLE are asymptotically equivalent. Memory Tip: Near unit root $+$ small $n$: initial obs carry high information – exact MLE essential. Large $n$: both methods equivalent. “When the past matters most, treat it carefully.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The covariance matrix $\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_n=[\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_s)]_{t,s=1}^{n}$ of a stationary AR(1) process with $|\\phi|&lt;1$ has the $(t,s)$ element:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)\\cdot\\phi^{|t-s|}$ (Toeplitz structure, depends only on lag $|t-s|$)','B) $\\sigma^2\\mathbf{I}$ (diagonal – zero covariance)','C) $\\sigma^2\\phi^{ts}$ (product form)','D) $\\sigma^2\\phi^{t+s}$ (depends on both $t$ and $s$ separately)'],ans:0,exp:'A stationary process always has a Toeplitz covariance matrix: $\\Sigma_{ts}=\\gamma(|t-s|)$ depends only on the lag $|t-s|$, not on $t$ and $s$ individually. For AR(1): $\\gamma(h)=\\sigma^2\\phi^{|h|}/(1-\\phi^2)$, so: $$\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_n=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{1-\\phi^2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;\\phi&amp;\\phi^2&amp;\\cdots\\\\\\phi&amp;1&amp;\\phi&amp;\\cdots\\\\\\vdots&amp;&amp;\\ddots&amp;\\end{pmatrix}$$ This Toeplitz structure allows efficient computation via the Levinson–Durbin algorithm ($O(n^2)$ vs $O(n^3)$ for general matrices). Memory Tip: Stationary process $\\Rightarrow$ Toeplitz covariance matrix. Constant diagonals – each diagonal is one ACVF value $\\gamma(h)$. The structure encodes “constant variance + shift-invariant correlations.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the Cram\\\'er spectral representation $X_t=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}e^{i\\omega t}\\,dZ(\\omega)$, the quantity $E[|dZ(\\omega)|^2]$ equals:',opts:['A) $1/(2\\pi)\\,d\\omega$ (uniform regardless of $f$)','B) $dZ(\\omega)\\cdot\\overline{dZ(\\omega)}$ (a random variable, not a number)','C) $f(\\omega)\\,d\\omega$ – the spectral density times a frequency increment','D) $\\gamma(0)\\,d\\omega$ (constant across frequencies)'],ans:2,exp:'The spectral process $Z(\\omega)$ has orthogonal increments: $E[dZ(\\omega_1)\\overline{dZ(\\omega_2)}]=0$ for $\\omega_1\\ne\\omega_2$. The variance of the increment $dZ(\\omega)$ equals $f(\\omega)\\,d\\omega$: the spectral density $f(\\omega)$ is literally the “variance per unit frequency bandwidth.” Total variance: $\\gamma(0)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}E[|dZ(\\omega)|^2]=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)\\,d\\omega$ ✓ This connects the Cram\\\'er representation to the Wiener–Khinchin theorem. Memory Tip: $f(\\omega)d\\omega=$ variance of the random “sine wave” at frequency $\\omega$. The spectral density is a variance density – higher $f(\\omega)\\Rightarrow$ more energy at that frequency.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In practice, the differencing order $d$ in an ARIMA($p,d,q$) model is determined by:',opts:['A) Using the Yule–Walker equations to estimate $d$ directly','B) Setting $d$ equal to the number of seasonal periods','C) Minimising AIC over all integer values of $d$ simultaneously with $p$ and $q$','D) Sequentially: first determining $d$ via ACF inspection and unit-root tests, then identifying $p$ and $q$ from the differenced series'],ans:3,exp:'Stage 1: Plot the series and its ACF. Slow ACF decay $\\Rightarrow$ apply ADF/PP/KPSS. If unit root: difference once; repeat. Stage 2: With $d$ fixed, inspect ACF and PACF of $W_t=\\nabla^d X_t$ to identify $p$ and $q$. Why not (C)? Treating $d$ as a continuous parameter to minimise AIC works in principle but is non-standard; $d$ is estimated discretely (0,1,2,\\ldots) and separately from ARMA parameters to avoid confounding. Memory Tip: Box–Jenkins is sequential: Difference $\\to$ Identify $\\to$ Estimate $\\to$ Diagnose. Don\'t try to estimate $d$, $p$, $q$ all at once – determine $d$ first using unit-root tests.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given the spectral density $f(\\omega)$, the autocovariance at lag $h$ is recovered by:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(h)=2\\pi f(h)$','B) $\\gamma(h)=f(h)$ (directly evaluate at lag $h$)','C) $\\gamma(h)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)e^{i\\omega h}\\,d\\omega$','D) $\\gamma(h)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)e^{i\\omega h}\\,d\\omega$'],ans:3,exp:'The Fourier transform pair (Wiener–Khinchin): Forward (ACVF $\\to$ spectrum): $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega h}$ (has $1/2\\pi$) Inverse (spectrum $\\to$ ACVF): $\\gamma(h)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)e^{i\\omega h}d\\omega$ (no $1/2\\pi$) The $2\\pi$ “bookkeeping” places the normalisation constant on the forward transform by convention. Option (C) would be correct if $f(\\omega)$ were defined as $\\sum\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega h}$ (without the $1/2\\pi$). Memory Tip: “$f$ has the $1/2\\pi$; $\\gamma$ does not.” The $2\\pi$ goes with the forward transform (spectral density definition). The inverse has no extra factor.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'After fitting an ARMA model, a well-specified model should produce residuals $\\hat{\\varepsilon}_t$ that pass which checks?',opts:['A) Ljung–Box test: non-significant; sample ACF of $\\hat{\\varepsilon}_t$: all spikes within $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{n}$','B) ADF test applied to $\\hat{\\varepsilon}_t$: rejects unit root','C) PACF of $\\hat{\\varepsilon}_t$ shows exactly $p$ significant spikes','D) All residuals exactly equal to zero'],ans:0,exp:'A well-specified ARMA model leaves residuals that are approximately white noise: 1. Time plot: no pattern (no trend, no heteroscedasticity visible) 2. Residual ACF: all spikes within $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{n}$ 3. Ljung–Box test ($m=20$, $df=m-p-q$): $p$-value $&gt;0.05$ 4. Normal probability plot: approximately linear (Gaussian assumption check) (B) is relevant for the original series, not residuals. (C) is wrong – correctly specified residuals should show NO significant PACF spikes. Memory Tip: Good ARMA fit = white noise residuals. “The model has absorbed all the structure.” Any remaining pattern in ACF/PACF of residuals signals model inadequacy.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If the AR and MA polynomials of an ARMA($p,q$) model share a common factor $z=z_0$, this implies:',opts:['A) The model is non-stationary','B) The model is over-parameterised (parameter redundancy): the true model has orders $p-1$ and $q-1$','C) The innovations $\\varepsilon_t$ are correlated','D) The spectral density is identically zero'],ans:1,exp:'$\\Phi(z)=(1-z_0^{-1}z)\\tilde{\\Phi}(z)$ and $\\Theta(z)=(1-z_0^{-1}z)\\tilde{\\Theta}(z)$: $X_t=\\frac{\\Theta(B)}{\\Phi(B)}\\varepsilon_t=\\frac{(1-z_0^{-1}B)\\tilde{\\Theta}(B)}{(1-z_0^{-1}B)\\tilde{\\Phi}(B)}\\varepsilon_t=\\frac{\\tilde{\\Theta}(B)}{\\tilde{\\Phi}(B)}\\varepsilon_t$ The common factor cancels, leaving an ARMA($p-1,q-1$). Fitting the redundant ARMA($p,q$) makes individual parameter estimates non-identifiable (singular information matrix). Test: if AIC of ARMA($p,q$) $\\approx$ ARMA($p-1,q-1$) $\\Rightarrow$ suspect cancellation. Memory Tip: AR and MA sharing a root = “cancellation” – like simplifying a fraction. The extra parameters add nothing. Use parsimonious (minimum order) ARMA – AIC/BIC help detect this.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) process $X_t=1.2X_{t-1}-0.5X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$, the characteristic equation $z^2-1.2z+0.5=0$ has complex roots. The pseudo-period (in time units) of the ACF oscillation is approximately:',opts:['A) $2\\pi/\\lambda$ where $\\lambda=\\arccos(\\phi_1/(2\\sqrt{-\\phi_2}))$','B) $2\\pi/\\arctan(\\mathrm{Im}/\\mathrm{Re})$ of the roots','C) The reciprocal of $|\\phi_1|=1.2$','D) Options (A) and (B) are equivalent expressions for the same quantity'],ans:3,exp:'Roots of $z^2-1.2z+0.5=0$: $z=0.6\\pm i\\cdot0.4$ (complex conjugates). Modulus $r=\\sqrt{0.36+0.16}=\\sqrt{0.52}\\approx0.721$ (stationarity: $r&lt;1$ ✓). Argument: $\\lambda=\\arctan(0.4/0.6)\\approx\\arctan(0.667)\\approx0.588$ radians. Pseudo-period $=2\\pi/\\lambda\\approx2\\pi/0.588\\approx10.7$ time units. Equivalently: $\\lambda=\\arccos(0.6/0.721)=\\arccos(0.832)\\approx0.588$ rad ✓ (both formulas agree). Memory Tip: Pseudo-period $=2\\pi/\\lambda$ where $\\lambda=\\arg(\\text{root})$. Roots closer to the unit circle ($r\\to1$): longer-lasting oscillations. Argument $\\lambda$: oscillation frequency.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) with $\\theta=-0.9$, the ACF at lag 1 is $\\rho(1)=$:',opts:['A) $-0.9$','B) $0.81/(1+0.81)\\approx0.448$','C) $0.9/(1+0.81)\\approx0.497$','D) $-0.9/(1+0.81)\\approx-0.497$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\rho(1)=\\dfrac{\\theta}{1+\\theta^2}$: the sign of $\\rho(1)$ equals the sign of $\\theta$. For $\\theta=-0.9$: strong negative correlation at lag 1 – the series alternates. A value above average tends to be followed by a value below average. Note $|\\rho(1)|=0.497&lt;0.5$: confirms the MA(1) bound even at $|\\theta|=0.9$ (near non-invertible boundary $|\\theta|=1$). This process is invertible since $|\\theta|=0.9&lt;1$. Memory Tip: MA(1) with $\\theta&lt;0$: negatively correlated series (oscillating). With $\\theta&gt;0$: positively correlated (smooth). The sign of $\\theta$ determines the direction of lag-1 correlation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A random walk starts at $X_0=5$ with $\\varepsilon_t\\sim\\mathrm{iid}(0,4)$. After 25 steps, $\\mathrm{Var}(X_{25})$ equals:',opts:['A) $100$','B) $25$','C) $4$','D) $625$'],ans:0,exp:'$X_{25}=X_0+\\sum_{j=1}^{25}\\varepsilon_j=5+\\sum_{j=1}^{25}\\varepsilon_j$ $\\mathrm{Var}(X_{25})=\\mathrm{Var}\\!\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^{25}\\varepsilon_j\\right)=25\\cdot\\mathrm{Var}(\\varepsilon_j)=25\\times4=100$ $E[X_{25}]=X_0=5$ (zero-drift random walk). The initial value $X_0=5$ shifts the mean but does not affect the variance. Memory Tip: RW variance $=t\\sigma^2$: proportional to time $t$ and innovation variance $\\sigma^2$. At $t=25$, $\\sigma^2=4$: variance $=100$, std dev $=10$. The uncertainty grows like $\\sigma\\sqrt{t}$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(2) with $\\phi_1=0.5$, $\\phi_2=0.4$, checking stationarity via the triangle:',opts:['A) $\\phi_2-\\phi_1=-0.1&lt;1$ fails the triangle condition','B) All three conditions $|\\phi_2|&lt;1$, $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&lt;1$, $\\phi_2-\\phi_1&lt;1$ are satisfied $\\Rightarrow$ stationary','C) $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=0.9&lt;1$ ✓ but $|\\phi_2|=0.4&lt;1$ fails because $0.4&gt;0$ (incorrect claim)','D) The process is non-stationary because $\\phi_1&gt;0.4$'],ans:1,exp:'For $\\phi_1=0.5$, $\\phi_2=0.4$: 1. $|\\phi_2|=0.4&lt;1$ ✓ 2. $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=0.5+0.4=0.9&lt;1$ ✓ 3. $\\phi_2-\\phi_1=0.4-0.5=-0.1&lt;1$ ✓ All three conditions of the stationarity triangle are satisfied $\\Rightarrow$ the process is stationary. Options (C), (D), (A) all contain false claims about which condition fails. Memory Tip: AR(2) triangle: check three inequalities one by one. When $\\phi_1,\\phi_2&gt;0$ and small, the binding constraint is usually $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&lt;1$ (ordinary unit root boundary).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary ARMA process vs. a random walk, which statement about multi-step forecasting is correct?',opts:['A) Both have forecast error variance that grows without bound as $h\\to\\infty$','B) Stationary ARMA: forecast error variance is bounded by $\\gamma(0)$; Random walk: grows as $h\\sigma^2$ without bound','C) Both produce the same point forecast $X_n$ for all horizons $h$','D) Random walk: forecast converges to $\\mu$ as $h\\to\\infty$; ARMA: forecast remains at $X_n$'],ans:1,exp:'Point forecast | Forecast error variance; Stationary ARMA | $\\to\\mu$ as $h\\to\\infty$ | $\\to\\gamma(0)$ (bounded); Random walk | $=X_n$ (stays constant) | $=h\\sigma^2$ (unbounded) For the RW, even with perfect model knowledge, uncertainty grows indefinitely – no “rubber band” pulls the series back. For stationary ARMA, the unconditional variance is a ceiling on uncertainty. Memory Tip: Stationary = mean-reverting = bounded long-run uncertainty. RW = wandering = unbounded long-run uncertainty. “A rubber band can only stretch so far; a random walk goes anywhere.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The ARIMA(0,2,1) model $(1-B)^2X_t=(1+\\theta B)\\varepsilon_t$ is used for:',opts:['A) Series whose first differences follow a random walk (ARIMA(0,1,0))','B) Series that need two differences to become stationary, then show MA(1) structure – forecasts follow a linear trend','C) Series that are stationary with an MA(1) structure','D) Series with additive seasonal patterns at period 2'],ans:1,exp:'$\\nabla^2 X_t=X_t-2X_{t-1}+X_{t-2}\\sim\\mathrm{MA}(1)$: second differences are MA(1). The forecast function for ARIMA(0,2,1) is a linear trend: $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=a+bh$ where $a$ and $b$ are estimated from recent data. This is equivalent to Holt\'s linear exponential smoothing (double exponential smoothing). Appropriate for data that has a locally linear trend that evolves over time. Memory Tip: ARIMA(0,2,1) $=$ Holt\'s linear smoothing $=$ locally linear trend. $d=2$ means “trend itself is changing.” Forecasts extrapolate the most recent estimated trend indefinitely.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ARMA(1,1) process with $\\phi=0.7$, $\\theta=0.3$, $\\sigma^2=1$, which of the following correctly expresses $f(\\omega)$?',opts:['A) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{1.4\\cos\\omega}{2\\pi(1.49-1.4\\cos\\omega)}$','B) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{1+0.09}{2\\pi}$ (constant)','C) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{1+0.09+0.6\\cos\\omega}{2\\pi(1+0.49-1.4\\cos\\omega)}$','D) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1-0.7\\cos\\omega)^2}$'],ans:2,exp:'$|\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2=|1+0.3e^{-i\\omega}|^2=1+0.09+2(0.3)\\cos\\omega=1.09+0.6\\cos\\omega$ $|\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2=|1-0.7e^{-i\\omega}|^2=1+0.49-2(0.7)\\cos\\omega=1.49-1.4\\cos\\omega$ $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\cdot\\dfrac{|\\Theta|^2}{|\\Phi|^2}=\\dfrac{1.09+0.6\\cos\\omega}{2\\pi(1.49-1.4\\cos\\omega)}$ ✓ Option (D) has only AR denominator (no MA); (B) is constant (wrong). Memory Tip: ARMA(1,1) spectrum: use $|1+\\theta e^{-i\\omega}|^2=1+\\theta^2+2\\theta\\cos\\omega$ and $|1-\\phi e^{-i\\omega}|^2=1+\\phi^2-2\\phi\\cos\\omega$. Note signs: MA has $+2\\theta\\cos\\omega$; AR has $-2\\phi\\cos\\omega$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Levinson–Durbin recursion computes $\\phi_{hh}$ (the PACF at lag $h$) from $\\phi_{h-1,h-1}$ using the formula:',opts:['A) $\\phi_{hh}=\\rho(h)/\\rho(h-1)$','B) $\\phi_{hh}=\\phi_{h-1,h-1}\\cdot\\rho(1)$','C) $\\phi_{hh}=[\\rho(h)-\\rho(h-1)^2]/[1-\\rho(h-1)^2]$','D) $\\phi_{hh}=\\dfrac{\\rho(h)-\\sum_{j=1}^{h-1}\\phi_{h-1,j}\\rho(h-j)}{1-\\sum_{j=1}^{h-1}\\phi_{h-1,j}\\rho(j)}$'],ans:3,exp:'At $h=2$ (with $\\phi_{11}=\\rho(1)$): $$\\phi_{22}=\\frac{\\rho(2)-\\phi_{11}\\rho(1)}{1-\\phi_{11}\\rho(1)}=\\frac{\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2}{1-\\rho(1)^2}$$ This matches the known formula ✓ (option C would apply only at $h=2$ – for $h=3$, option C is wrong). The algorithm is efficient: $O(h)$ operations per lag, $O(h^2)$ total for lags $1,\\ldots,H$ – versus $O(H^3)$ for direct matrix inversion. Memory Tip: Levinson–Durbin: “update the AR fit one lag at a time.” Each step adds one new coefficient using the previous step\'s fit. The last added coefficient IS the PACF at that lag.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The asymptotic distribution of $I(\\omega_{n/2})=I(\\pi)$ (periodogram at the Nyquist frequency, for even $n$) under Gaussian WN is:',opts:['A) $N(f(\\pi),f(\\pi)^2/n)$','B) $f(\\pi)\\cdot\\chi^2_2/2$ (same as interior frequencies)','C) $f(\\pi)\\cdot\\chi^2_1$ (one df because the imaginary part is zero)','D) $f(\\pi)\\cdot F(1,n-2)$'],ans:2,exp:'At $\\omega=\\pi$ (and at $\\omega=0$): the DFT is purely real – the imaginary part is identically zero (since $e^{i\\pi t}=(-1)^t$ is real). Only one random variable (the real part) contributes, giving $\\chi^2_1$ instead of $\\chi^2_2$. Interior frequencies $0&lt;\\omega&lt;\\pi$: both real and imaginary parts are non-zero $\\Rightarrow\\chi^2_2/2$ distribution. This boundary effect means the Nyquist ordinate has half the variability of interior ordinates. Memory Tip: Interior periodogram: $\\chi^2_2$ (2 df, real + imaginary). Nyquist ($\\omega=\\pi$) and zero ($\\omega=0$): $\\chi^2_1$ (1 df, real only). Boundary frequencies are special!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'How many parameters does an ARIMA(2,1,1) model with a non-zero intercept have to estimate?',opts:['A) 6 (including $d=1$ as a parameter)','B) 4 ($\\phi_1,\\phi_2,\\theta_1,\\sigma^2$)','C) 3 ($\\phi_1,\\phi_2,\\theta_1$)','D) 5 ($\\phi_1,\\phi_2,\\theta_1,\\delta,\\sigma^2$)'],ans:3,exp:'The model for $W_t=\\nabla X_t$: $(1-\\phi_1B-\\phi_2B^2)W_t=\\delta+(1+\\theta_1B)\\varepsilon_t$ Parameters to estimate: $\\phi_1$, $\\phi_2$ (AR), $\\theta_1$ (MA), $\\delta$ (drift in differenced series), $\\sigma^2$ (innovation variance). $d=1$ is NOT a free parameter – it is determined by the unit-root test and fixed before estimation. For AIC: $k=5$ and the AIC$=-2\\ln\\hat{L}+2\\times5=-2\\ln\\hat{L}+10$. Memory Tip: ARIMA($p,d,q$): free parameters $=p+q+1$ (intercept) $+1$ ($\\sigma^2$). Here $2+1+1+1=5$. $d$ is not counted – it is pre-determined, not estimated.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The cross-spectral density $f_{XY}(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma_{XY}(h)e^{-i\\omega h}$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $f_{XY}(\\omega)=0$ for all $\\omega$ unless $X_t=Y_t$','B) $f_{XY}(\\omega)=-f_{YX}(\\omega)$ (anti-symmetric)','C) $f_{XY}(\\omega)=\\overline{f_{YX}(\\omega)}$ (Hermitian: conjugate relationship)','D) $f_{XY}(\\omega)=f_{YX}(\\omega)$ (symmetric)'],ans:2,exp:'Using $\\gamma_{XY}(h)=\\gamma_{YX}(-h)$: $$f_{XY}(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_h\\gamma_{XY}(h)e^{-i\\omega h}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_h\\gamma_{YX}(-h)e^{-i\\omega h}$$ Let $k=-h$: $=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_k\\gamma_{YX}(k)e^{i\\omega k}=\\overline{\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_k\\gamma_{YX}(k)e^{-i\\omega k}\\right)}=\\overline{f_{YX}(\\omega)}$ This means $f_{XY}$ is NOT real in general (unlike auto-spectra), but its imaginary part (quadrature spectrum) carries phase information. Memory Tip: Cross-spectrum: $f_{XY}=\\overline{f_{YX}}$ (conjugate, not equal). Auto-spectrum: $f_{XX}=\\overline{f_{XX}}$ (real – it\'s its own conjugate). Cross-spectrum is complex; it encodes both amplitude and phase between $X$ and $Y$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ARIMA(1,1,0) model $\\nabla X_t=\\phi\\nabla X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ ($|\\phi|&lt;1$), the 2-step-ahead forecast $\\hat{X}_{n+2|n}$ is:',opts:['A) $X_n$','B) $X_n+2\\phi(X_n-X_{n-1})$','C) $X_n+\\phi^2(X_n-X_{n-1})$','D) $X_n+(\\phi+\\phi^2)(X_n-X_{n-1})$'],ans:3,exp:'Let $W_t=\\nabla X_t\\sim\\mathrm{AR}(1)$ with parameter $\\phi$. 1-step: $\\hat{W}_{n+1|n}=\\phi W_n=\\phi(X_n-X_{n-1})\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\hat{X}_{n+1|n}=X_n+\\hat{W}_{n+1|n}=X_n+\\phi(X_n-X_{n-1})$ 2-step: $\\hat{W}_{n+2|n}=\\phi\\hat{W}_{n+1|n}=\\phi^2(X_n-X_{n-1})\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\hat{X}_{n+2|n}=\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}+\\hat{W}_{n+2|n}$ $=X_n+\\phi(X_n-X_{n-1})+\\phi^2(X_n-X_{n-1})=X_n+(\\phi+\\phi^2)(X_n-X_{n-1})$ Option (C) keeps only the second-step increment and drops the first-step increment $\\phi(X_n-X_{n-1})$ that has already accumulated into $\\hat{X}_{n+1|n}$ – a common bookkeeping error when telescoping multi-step forecasts. Memory Tip: ARIMA(1,1,0): $h$-step forecast $=X_n+(\\phi+\\phi^2+\\cdots+\\phi^h)(X_n-X_{n-1})$ – the increments accumulate, they don\'t reset at each step. As $h\\to\\infty$: converges to $X_n+\\phi(X_n-X_{n-1})/(1-\\phi)$ (a fixed level).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Why is the sample ACF $\\hat{\\rho}(h)$ unreliable for large lags $h$ close to $n$?',opts:['A) The formula $\\hat{\\gamma}(h)=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^{n-h}(x_{t+h}-\\bar{x})(x_t-\\bar{x})$ uses only $n-h$ data pairs – variance of $\\hat{\\rho}(h)$ increases as $h\\to n$','B) The ACF must always equal zero for $h&gt;n/2$','C) Large-lag ACF values are always negative by definition','D) The computation is only defined for $h\\le\\sqrt{n}$'],ans:0,exp:'At lag $h$: only $n-h$ product terms contribute to $\\hat{\\gamma}(h)$. At $h=n-1$: only 1 term – completely unreliable. Practical rule: Plot ACF only up to $h=n/4$ (Box–Jenkins recommendation) or at most $h=n/3$. For $h&gt;n/4$: even for WN, $\\hat{\\rho}(h)$ can appear large simply due to small-sample variance – not due to genuine autocorrelation. Memory Tip: Large-lag ACF: fewer obs $=$ less reliable. Rule: don\'t interpret ACF beyond $n/4$ lags. The last few ACF values are estimated from just a handful of pairs – mostly noise.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The sample ACF of a series shows: $\\hat\\rho(1)=0.4$, $\\hat\\rho(2)=0.3$, $\\hat\\rho(3)=0.2$, \\ldots (tailing off slowly). The sample PACF shows: $\\hat\\phi_{11}=0.4$, $\\hat\\phi_{22}=0.1$, $\\hat\\phi_{33}=0.05$, \\ldots (also tailing off). The most likely model is:',opts:['A) ARMA(1,1) – both ACF and PACF tail off','B) AR(1) – PACF cuts off at lag 1','C) MA(1) – ACF cuts off at lag 1','D) White noise – all ACF/PACF within bands'],ans:0,exp:'AR(1) signature: ACF tails off geometrically; PACF cuts off after lag 1. MA(1) signature: ACF cuts off after lag 1; PACF tails off geometrically. ARMA(1,1) signature: both ACF and PACF tail off (neither cuts off). This matches the observed pattern. Next step: fit ARMA(1,1) and check AIC vs. AR(1) and MA(1). If ARMA(1,1) AIC is significantly lower, it is preferred. Memory Tip: “Both tail off = ARMA.” Neither function cuts off cleanly – this is the hallmark of a mixed ARMA model. Use information criteria to determine the exact orders $p$ and $q$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) with $\\theta=0.5$ and $\\sigma^2=2$, the spectral density at $\\omega=\\pi/3$ (i.e., $\\cos(\\pi/3)=0.5$) is:',opts:['A) $f(\\pi/3)=\\dfrac{2}{2\\pi}(1+0.25+2(0.5)(0.5))=\\dfrac{2(1.75)}{2\\pi}=\\dfrac{3.5}{2\\pi}$','B) $f(\\pi/3)=\\dfrac{2(1.25)}{2\\pi}=\\dfrac{2.5}{2\\pi}$','C) $f(\\pi/3)=\\dfrac{2}{2\\pi}$','D) $f(\\pi/3)=\\dfrac{2(0.5)}{2\\pi}=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}$'],ans:0,exp:'$f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}(1+\\theta^2+2\\theta\\cos\\omega)$ At $\\omega=\\pi/3$: $\\cos(\\pi/3)=0.5$ $f(\\pi/3)=\\dfrac{2}{2\\pi}(1+0.25+2\\times0.5\\times0.5)=\\dfrac{2}{2\\pi}(1+0.25+0.5)=\\dfrac{2\\times1.75}{2\\pi}=\\dfrac{3.5}{2\\pi}$ Verification: $f(0)=2(1.25+0.5)/(2\\pi)=3.5/(2\\pi)$? No – at $\\omega=0$: $f(0)=2(1+0.25+0.5)/(2\\pi)=3.5/(2\\pi)$. At $\\omega=\\pi$: $f(\\pi)=2(1.25-0.5)/(2\\pi)=1.5/(2\\pi)$. This confirms the frequency-dependent shape. Memory Tip: MA(1) spectrum formula: $\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2+2\\theta\\cos\\omega)/(2\\pi)$. Plug in $\\cos\\omega$ value directly. At $\\omega=0$: maximum (for $\\theta&gt;0$). At $\\omega=\\pi$: minimum.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following processes is (weakly) stationary?',opts:['A) $X_t=X_{t-1}+t\\cdot\\varepsilon_t$ (random walk with heteroscedastic innovations)','B) $X_t=t+\\varepsilon_t$ (linear trend + white noise)','C) $X_t=(-1)^t$ (deterministic alternating sequence)','D) $X_t=0.9X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ with $\\varepsilon_t\\sim\\mathrm{WN}(0,1)$'],ans:3,exp:'(B): $E[X_t]=t$ grows with $t$ – non-constant mean $\\Rightarrow$ non-stationary. (C): $X_t=(-1)^t$ is deterministic – $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=0$ so trivially covariance stationary, but it is deterministic (not a stochastic process) – typically excluded. (D): $E[X_t]=0$, $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=1/(1-0.81)=5.26$ (constant), $\\gamma(h)=5.26\\times0.9^{|h|}$ – all conditions satisfied. ✓ (A): $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ grows due to both accumulation and heteroscedasticity – non-stationary. Memory Tip: Check stationarity: (i) constant mean, (ii) finite constant variance, (iii) ACVF depends only on lag. A growing mean or growing variance $\\Rightarrow$ non-stationary.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARIMA(1,1,1) process, after one differencing $W_t=\\nabla X_t$, the ACF of $W_t$ is expected to show:',opts:['A) ACF cuts off after lag 1 only','B) PACF cuts off after lag 1 only (pure AR(1))','C) ACF tails off exponentially and PACF tails off – consistent with ARMA(1,1)','D) All ACF/PACF values within the confidence bands (white noise)'],ans:2,exp:'ARIMA(1,1,1): $(1-\\phi B)(1-B)X_t=(1+\\theta B)\\varepsilon_t$ So $W_t=\\nabla X_t$ satisfies $(1-\\phi B)W_t=(1+\\theta B)\\varepsilon_t$ – this is ARMA(1,1)! For ARMA(1,1): both ACF and PACF tail off geometrically (neither cuts off sharply after a fixed lag). After differencing correctly ($d=1$), the ACF/PACF pattern of the differenced series directly identifies the ARMA($p,q$) orders. Memory Tip: ARIMA(1,1,1) $\\xrightarrow{\\nabla}$ ARMA(1,1): both tails off. The differencing step removes the unit root; the remaining ARMA structure is then readable from the differenced series\' ACF/PACF.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The periodogram $I(\\omega_k)$ at Fourier frequency $\\omega_k=2\\pi k/n$ equals the discrete Fourier transform of the sample ACVF:',opts:['A) $I(\\omega_k)=|\\hat{\\gamma}(\\omega_k)|^2$','B) $I(\\omega_k)=2\\pi\\hat{\\gamma}(k)$','C) $I(\\omega_k)=\\hat{\\gamma}(k)$ (simply the sample ACVF at lag $k$)','D) $I(\\omega_k)=\\sum_{h=-(n-1)}^{n-1}\\hat{\\gamma}(h)e^{-i\\omega_k h}$'],ans:3,exp:'This follows from algebraic manipulation of the periodogram definition $I(\\omega_k)=\\frac{1}{n}|\\sum_t x_t e^{-i\\omega_k t}|^2$: $I(\\omega_k)=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_t\\sum_s x_t x_s e^{-i\\omega_k(t-s)}=\\sum_{h=-(n-1)}^{n-1}\\hat{\\gamma}(h)e^{-i\\omega_k h}$ This shows the periodogram is the Fourier transform of the sample ACVF, evaluated at the Fourier frequencies – making it the natural sample analog of the spectral density. Memory Tip: Periodogram = FT of sample ACVF (at Fourier frequencies). Spectral density = FT of population ACVF. Perfect analogy – periodogram is the sample version of the spectral density.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(1) model $X_t=\\mu+\\phi(X_{t-1}-\\mu)+\\varepsilon_t$ with mean $\\mu=10$ and $\\phi=0.6$, if $X_{100}=14$, the 2-step-ahead forecast $\\hat{X}_{102|100}$ is:',opts:['A) $10$','B) $10+0.6^2(14-10)=10+1.44=11.44$','C) $10+0.6(14-10)=12.4$','D) $14$'],ans:1,exp:'For AR(1) with mean $\\mu$: $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=\\mu+\\phi^h(X_n-\\mu)$ $h=1$: $\\hat{X}_{101|100}=10+0.6(4)=12.4$ (option C) $h=2$: $\\hat{X}_{102|100}=10+0.6^2(4)=10+0.36\\times4=10+1.44=11.44$ (option A) As $h\\to\\infty$: $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}\\to\\mu=10$ (returns to the long-run mean). Memory Tip: Non-zero mean AR(1) forecast: $\\mu+\\phi^h(X_n-\\mu)$. The deviation $(X_n-\\mu)$ from the mean decays at rate $\\phi^h$. At $h=2$: deviation multiplied by $\\phi^2=0.36$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the random walk ARIMA(0,1,0) model, all $h$-step-ahead forecasts $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}$ for $h=1,2,3,\\ldots$ equal:',opts:['A) The overall sample mean $\\bar{X}$','B) The last observed value $X_n$','C) $0$ (the long-run mean of differences)','D) $X_n+h\\cdot\\bar{\\Delta}$ (extrapolated at sample mean change)'],ans:1,exp:'$X_{n+h}=X_n+\\sum_{j=1}^h\\varepsilon_{n+j}$; all future increments have $E[\\varepsilon_{n+j}|\\mathcal{F}_n]=0$. $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=E[X_{n+h}|\\mathcal{F}_n]=X_n$ for all $h$. The forecast is flat – $X_n$ is the best estimate of where the series will be at any future horizon. This is why the random walk is used as the naïve benchmark in forecast comparisons: “can your model beat simply using today\'s value?” Memory Tip: RW forecast = $X_n$ (flat). No decay toward a mean, no trend. “Where you are now is your best guess for where you\'ll be.” This is the hardest benchmark to beat in financial forecasting.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'As the AR(1) coefficient $\\phi\\to1^-$ (approaching unit root from below), the spectral density at $\\omega=0$ behaves as:',opts:['A) $f(0)\\to+\\infty$ (spectral pole at $\\omega=0$: dominant low-frequency power)','B) $f(0)$ remains bounded at $\\sigma^2/(2\\pi(1-\\phi)^2)$ for all $\\phi&lt;1$','C) $f(0)\\to0$ (spectrum collapses at zero)','D) $f(0)\\to\\sigma^2/(2\\pi)$ (approaches white noise spectrum)'],ans:0,exp:'$f(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi(1-\\phi)^2}$: as $\\phi\\to1$, $(1-\\phi)^2\\to0$, so $f(0)\\to\\infty$. The spectrum develops a pole at $\\omega=0$ – all the variance is concentrated at the lowest frequencies (infinite slow cycles). At the unit root $\\phi=1$: the spectral density of the random walk is formally a Dirac delta at $\\omega=0$ – the process has “all its power” at frequency zero. This non-integrable spectrum is why the process is non-stationary. Memory Tip: Near unit root: spectral pole at $\\omega=0$ grows without bound. The series looks like a very smooth, long-cycle process. At $\\phi=1$: the spectrum “explodes” at zero frequency.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'When selecting AR order by AIC, fitting AR(0), AR(1), AR(2), AR(3) to $n=100$ observations yields log-likelihoods $\\ln\\hat{L}$ of $-150, -140, -138, -138$ respectively. The AIC-selected order is:',opts:['A) AR(2)','B) AR(0)','C) AR(3)','D) AR(1)'],ans:0,exp:'AIC $= -2\\ln\\hat{L}+2k$ where $k$ includes all estimated parameters ($p$ AR coefficients $+\\sigma^2$): $$\\begin{array}{lcccr} \\text{Model} &amp; \\ln\\hat{L} &amp; k &amp; -2\\ln\\hat{L} &amp; \\mathrm{AIC}\\\\[2pt] \\mathrm{AR}(0) &amp; -150 &amp; 1 &amp; 300 &amp; 302\\\\ \\mathrm{AR}(1) &amp; -140 &amp; 2 &amp; 280 &amp; 284\\\\ \\mathrm{AR}(2) &amp; -138 &amp; 3 &amp; 276 &amp; \\mathbf{282}\\\\ \\mathrm{AR}(3) &amp; -138 &amp; 4 &amp; 276 &amp; 284\\\\ \\end{array}$$ AR(2) and AR(3) have the same log-likelihood, but AR(3) has a larger AIC penalty. AR(2) wins. Memory Tip: When two models have equal fit, AIC (and BIC) always prefer the simpler one – parsimony principle built in. “Equal fit + fewer parameters = lower AIC.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the MA($q$) process $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^q\\theta_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ ($\\theta_0=1$), the autocovariance at the maximum non-zero lag $h=q$ is:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(q)=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^q\\theta_j^2$','B) $\\gamma(q)=\\sigma^2(\\theta_0+\\theta_q)^2/2$','C) $\\gamma(q)=\\sigma^2\\theta_q\\theta_0=\\sigma^2\\theta_q$','D) $\\gamma(q)=\\sigma^2\\theta_q^2$'],ans:2,exp:'The general formula: $\\gamma(h)=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{q-h}\\theta_j\\theta_{j+h}$ for $0\\le h\\le q$. At $h=q$: the sum has only one term ($j=0$): $\\gamma(q)=\\sigma^2\\theta_0\\theta_q=\\sigma^2\\theta_q$. At $h=0$: all terms sum: $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^q\\theta_j^2$ (option D is $\\gamma(0)$, not $\\gamma(q)$). For MA(2): $\\gamma(2)=\\sigma^2\\theta_2$ (verified from earlier in Set 1). Memory Tip: $\\gamma(q)=\\sigma^2\\theta_q$ for MA($q$). At the maximum lag, only one pair of innovations overlaps: $\\varepsilon_{t-q}$ (from $X_t$) and $\\varepsilon_{t-q}$ (from $X_{t-q}$). Simple!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARIMA(0,1,0) model with drift $\\delta$: $X_t=\\delta+X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, the $h$-step-ahead forecast is:',opts:['A) $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=\\delta h$','B) $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=X_n+h\\delta$','C) $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=X_n+\\delta$','D) $\\hat{X}_{n+h|n}=X_n$'],ans:1,exp:'$X_{n+h}=X_n+h\\delta+\\sum_{j=1}^h\\varepsilon_{n+j}$ $E[X_{n+h}|\\mathcal{F}_n]=X_n+h\\delta$ (drift accumulates $h$ times). Forecast error variance: $h\\sigma^2$ (same as no-drift RW). 95% PI: $(X_n+h\\delta)\\pm1.96\\sigma\\sqrt{h}$ – fan-shaped intervals around a deterministic linear trend. Memory Tip: RW without drift: flat forecast $X_n$. RW with drift $\\delta$: linear forecast $X_n+h\\delta$. Adding drift just adds a slope to the forecast. Uncertainty (PI width) is the same either way.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The maximum likelihood estimator $\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ of ARMA parameters is asymptotically:',opts:['A) $\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ has no known asymptotic distribution for ARMA models','B) $\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\\xrightarrow{d}N(\\boldsymbol{\\theta},\\mathbf{I}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}))$ with no $\\sqrt{n}$ scaling','C) $n(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}-\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\xrightarrow{d}N(\\mathbf{0},\\mathbf{I}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}))$ (convergence rate $n$, not $\\sqrt{n}$)','D) $\\sqrt{n}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}-\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\xrightarrow{d}N(\\mathbf{0},\\mathbf{I}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})^{-1})$ where $\\mathbf{I}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})$ is the Fisher information matrix'],ans:3,exp:'Under standard regularity conditions (stationary, invertible ARMA with Gaussian innovations), MLE is: Consistent: $\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\\xrightarrow{p}\\boldsymbol{\\theta}$; Asymptotically Normal: $\\sqrt{n}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}-\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\to N(\\mathbf{0},\\mathbf{I}^{-1})$; Efficient: achieves the Cram\\\'er–Rao bound. The Fisher information $\\mathbf{I}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})$ can be expressed via the spectral density (Whittle\'s formula from Set 2). The $\\sqrt{n}$ rate is standard for regular parametric models. Memory Tip: ARMA MLE: $\\sqrt{n}$ consistent, asymptotically normal, achieves CRLB. The asymptotic covariance is $\\mathbf{I}^{-1}/n$ per observation – standard ML theory applies.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The periodogram $I(\\omega_k)$ is an asymptotically unbiased estimator of $f(\\omega_k)$, meaning $E[I(\\omega_k)]\\to f(\\omega_k)$ as $n\\to\\infty$. However, it is NOT consistent because:',opts:['A) The periodogram is always zero at non-Fourier frequencies','B) The periodogram requires $n\\to\\infty$ for even the mean to exist','C) $\\mathrm{Var}(I(\\omega_k))\\approx f(\\omega_k)^2$ does not shrink to zero as $n\\to\\infty$','D) $E[I(\\omega_k)]\\ne f(\\omega_k)$ for all finite $n$ (bias never goes to zero)'],ans:2,exp:'An estimator is consistent if it converges in probability to the true value. This requires both bias $\\to0$ AND variance $\\to0$. Periodogram: asymptotically unbiased (bias$\\to0$) , but $\\mathrm{Var}(I(\\omega))\\approx[f(\\omega)]^2$ (constant in $n$) . Intuition: as $n$ grows, more Fourier frequencies are computed, but each individual ordinate remains noisy – we get more coverage but not more precision per ordinate. Smoothing reduces the variance at the cost of increased bias. Memory Tip: Consistent requires BOTH bias$\\to0$ AND variance$\\to0$. Periodogram: bias$\\to0$ but variance stuck at $f^2$ . “More data, same noise per point.” Need smoothing.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(1) model $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ with known $\\sigma^2$, the Fisher information about $\\phi$ per observation is asymptotically:',opts:['A) $I(\\phi)=n/(1-\\phi^2)$','B) $I(\\phi)=\\sigma^2$','C) $I(\\phi)=1/(1-\\phi^2)^2$','D) $I(\\phi)=\\dfrac{1}{1-\\phi^2}=\\gamma(0)/\\sigma^2$'],ans:3,exp:'The YW asymptotic variance of $\\hat\\phi_1$ is $(1-\\phi^2)/n$, so the Fisher information per observation is $I(\\phi)=1/(1-\\phi^2)$. Alternatively: via the score function $\\partial\\ln L/\\partial\\phi=\\sum X_{t-1}\\varepsilon_t/\\sigma^2$; $E[\\text{score}^2]=\\gamma(0)/\\sigma^2=(1-\\phi^2)^{-1}\\cdot\\sigma^2/\\sigma^2=1/(1-\\phi^2)$. As $\\phi\\to1$: $I(\\phi)\\to\\infty$ – but the $\\sqrt{n}$ CLT breaks down at the unit root boundary. Memory Tip: AR(1) Fisher info for $\\phi$: $I(\\phi)=1/(1-\\phi^2)$. Asymptotic variance of $\\hat\\phi=(1-\\phi^2)/n$. More persistent (larger $\\phi$) $\\Rightarrow$ more information $\\Rightarrow$ smaller estimation variance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A sample ACF shows significant values at lags 1, 2, 3, 4 and near-zero values at all higher lags ($h&gt;4$). The PACF decays slowly and remains significant for many lags. The most appropriate model is:',opts:['A) White noise','B) MA(4) (ACF cuts off at lag 4)','C) AR(4) (PACF should cut off for AR)','D) ARMA(4,4)'],ans:1,exp:'The hallmark of an MA($q$) process: ACF is significant for lags $1,\\ldots,q$ and exactly zero for $|h|&gt;q$ (sharp cutoff). PACF decays slowly (tails off geometrically or oscillatorily) – never cuts off for MA. The pattern described: ACF cuts off at lag 4, PACF tails off – textbook MA(4) signature. For AR(4): the reverse would hold (PACF cuts off at lag 4, ACF tails off). Memory Tip: “ACF cuts, PACF decays $\\Rightarrow$ MA.” The lag where ACF cuts off = MA order $q$. Simple, unambiguous signature – use it!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In spectral density estimation, increasing the smoothing bandwidth $m$ (number of periodogram ordinates averaged):',opts:['A) Decreases both bias and variance simultaneously (always better)','B) Increases variance and decreases bias','C) Decreases variance but increases bias (classic bias–variance trade-off; reduces frequency resolution)','D) Has no effect on either bias or variance'],ans:2,exp:'The smoothed spectral estimator averages $2m+1$ periodogram values: $\\hat{f}(\\omega)\\approx\\dfrac{1}{2m+1}\\sum_{|j|\\le m}I(\\omega_j)$ Variance: $\\approx f(\\omega)^2/(2m+1)\\times2$ – decreases as $m$ increases (more averaging). Bias: if $f(\\omega)$ changes over the bandwidth – wider average blurs nearby spectral features (peaks smeared out). Larger $m\\Rightarrow$ higher bias. Optimal $m$: balances these – typically $m\\propto n^{1/5}$ for smooth spectra. Memory Tip: “Wide window = smooth but blurry.” Like blurring a photo: noise (variance) removed, but sharp features (frequency peaks) smeared (bias). Optimal blur = $n^{1/5}$ scaling.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For which type of time series behaviour is $d=2$ (second differencing) typically required in an ARIMA model?',opts:['A) Series with a stationary mean and constant variance','B) Series with strong seasonal patterns (use seasonal differencing instead)','C) Series where both the level AND the slope (trend) evolve stochastically (a “trend of trends”)','D) Series with a stochastic trend in the level only ($d=1$ suffices)'],ans:2,exp:'$d=1$: the level wanders (random walk type) but changes are stationary. $d=2$: the changes themselves wander – the second differences are stationary but first differences are not. Model ARIMA(0,2,0): $\\nabla^2 X_t=\\varepsilon_t$, a “double random walk” – equivalent to Holt\'s linear exponential smoothing. (B): Seasonal patterns require seasonal differencing $(1-B^s)$, not ordinary $d=2$. Memory Tip: $d=1$: drunken level (position wanders). $d=2$: drunken slope (direction also wanders). Rarely need $d&gt;2$ in practice. Over-differencing is harmful – use unit-root tests.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The ADF test with a deterministic trend in the regression tests:',opts:['A) $H_0$: the trend slope is zero','B) $H_0$: unit root with no drift against $H_1$: trend-stationary process (stationary around a linear trend)','C) $H_0$: stationarity (reversal of the usual ADF null)','D) $H_0$: the series has a quadratic trend'],ans:1,exp:'The ADF regression with trend: $\\Delta X_t=\\alpha+\\beta t+\\delta X_{t-1}+\\text{lags}+\\varepsilon_t$ ADF variant | $H_0$ | $H_1$; No constant | Unit root, no drift | Stationary, zero mean; With constant | Unit root, with drift | Stationary, non-zero mean; With trend | Unit root, with drift | Trend-stationary Including irrelevant deterministic terms reduces power; omitting needed ones biases the test. Use graphical inspection to guide choice. Memory Tip: If the plot suggests a linear trend, use ADF with trend. If only a level shift, use ADF with constant. The deterministic part in $H_1$ must match the data\'s visual appearance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If the ACF of $X_t$ shows no significant autocorrelation (consistent with WN in levels), but the ACF of $X_t^2$ shows significant autocorrelation, this suggests:',opts:['A) The series is an MA process with a very high order','B) The series is non-stationary','C) The ACF of $X_t^2$ always equals the ACF of $X_t$ – the observation is impossible','D) The series has conditional heteroscedasticity (ARCH/GARCH effects) – dependence in variance but not in mean'],ans:3,exp:'This is a classic signature of ARCH/GARCH processes: the series $X_t$ is uncorrelated (looks like WN in ACF), but $X_t^2$ is autocorrelated (volatility clusters). For iid process: $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t^2,X_{t-h}^2)=0$ for $h\\ne0$. For ARCH: $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t^2,X_{t-h}^2)&gt;0$ – large squared values cluster together. This is why financial return series (near WN in ACF) have significant ARCH effects (positive ACF of squared returns). Memory Tip: Always check ACF of $X_t^2$ (or $|X_t|$) in addition to $X_t$. WN in $X_t$ + correlated $X_t^2$ = ARCH effects. The Ljung-Box test on squared residuals detects this.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The AR(3) process can be written in state-space (companion matrix) form as $\\mathbf{y}_t=\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{y}_{t-1}+\\mathbf{b}\\varepsilon_t$ where $\\mathbf{y}_t=(X_t,X_{t-1},X_{t-2})^\\top$. The companion matrix $\\mathbf{A}$ is:',opts:['A) $\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;0&amp;\\phi_3\\\\\\phi_1&amp;\\phi_2&amp;0\\\\0&amp;0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}$','B) $\\begin{pmatrix}\\phi_1&amp;\\phi_2&amp;\\phi_3\\\\1&amp;0&amp;0\\\\0&amp;1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}$','C) $\\begin{pmatrix}\\phi_1&amp;0&amp;0\\\\\\phi_2&amp;0&amp;0\\\\\\phi_3&amp;0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}$','D) $\\begin{pmatrix}\\phi_1&amp;\\phi_2&amp;\\phi_3\\\\0&amp;\\phi_1&amp;\\phi_2\\\\0&amp;0&amp;\\phi_1\\end{pmatrix}$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\mathbf{A}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\phi_1&amp;\\phi_2&amp;\\phi_3\\\\1&amp;0&amp;0\\\\0&amp;1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}$: the first row gives $X_t=\\phi_1X_{t-1}+\\phi_2X_{t-2}+\\phi_3X_{t-3}+\\varepsilon_t$; the identity sub-block shifts: $X_{t-1}=X_{t-1}$ and $X_{t-2}=X_{t-2}$. Stationarity: all eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{A}$ must lie strictly inside the unit circle (equivalent to AR polynomial roots outside unit circle). This state-space form is used for the Kalman filter and exact likelihood computation. Memory Tip: Companion matrix: first row = AR coefficients; remaining rows = “shift” identity matrix. Eigenvalues of companion = reciprocals of AR characteristic roots.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In terms of asymptotic relative efficiency (ARE), for estimating AR(1) parameter $\\phi$:',opts:['A) Yule–Walker is always more efficient than MLE','B) OLS is always more efficient than MLE for AR models','C) Yule–Walker and MLE have the same asymptotic variance $(1-\\phi^2)/n$ for all $\\phi$','D) MLE is more efficient than Yule–Walker for all $\\phi\\ne0$ in finite samples, but ARE $=1$ asymptotically (both achieve the Cram\\\'er–Rao bound)'],ans:3,exp:'Both YW and MLE for AR(1) have the same asymptotic distribution: $\\sqrt{n}(\\hat\\phi-\\phi)\\to N(0,1-\\phi^2)$. However, in finite samples, MLE (exact or conditional) has smaller MSE than YW, especially: (i) Small $n$ (initial conditions matter for MLE, not YW). (ii) $\\phi$ near 1 (YW has larger downward bias in finite samples). Asymptotically, both are equally efficient – both achieve the CRLB $(1-\\phi^2)/n$. Memory Tip: YW = MLE asymptotically for AR. In finite samples: MLE wins. The difference matters most for near-unit-root AR (large $\\phi$) with small $n$ – use MLE for these cases.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given that $f(\\omega)=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2+2\\theta\\cos\\omega)/(2\\pi)$ for an MA(1), the autocovariance at lag 1 recovered from this spectral density via $\\gamma(1)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)e^{i\\omega}d\\omega$ equals:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2\\theta/(2\\pi)$','B) $\\sigma^2\\theta(1+\\theta^2)$','C) $\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)$','D) $\\sigma^2\\theta$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(1)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)e^{i\\omega}d\\omega=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}(1+\\theta^2+2\\theta\\cos\\omega)e^{i\\omega}d\\omega$ Using: $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}e^{i\\omega}d\\omega=0$, $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\\cos\\omega\\cdot e^{i\\omega}d\\omega=\\pi$ (since $\\cos\\omega=(e^{i\\omega}+e^{-i\\omega})/2$): $\\gamma(1)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\cdot2\\theta\\cdot\\pi=\\sigma^2\\theta$ ✓ This confirms the Wiener–Khinchin inversion gives the correct ACVF. Memory Tip: The inversion formula recovers exact ACVF from the spectral density. Key integral: $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\\cos\\omega\\cdot e^{i\\omega h}d\\omega=\\pi$ if $h=\\pm1$, else 0 or $\\pi$ depending on $h$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the stationary AR(1) process with $\\phi=-0.5$ and $\\sigma^2=3$, the ACF at lag 2, $\\rho(2)$, equals:',opts:['A) $(-0.5)^2=0.25$','B) $-0.25$','C) $0.5$','D) $(-0.5)^2/(1-0.25)=1/3$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\rho(h)=\\phi^{|h|}$; at $h=2$: $\\rho(2)=(-0.5)^2=0.25&gt;0$. Pattern of ACF for $\\phi=-0.5$: $$\\rho(0)=1,\\ \\rho(1)=-0.5,\\ \\rho(2)=0.25,\\ \\rho(3)=-0.125,\\ldots$$ The ACF alternates in sign – negative at odd lags, positive at even lags – while decaying in magnitude. The variance (not needed here): $\\gamma(0)=3/(1-0.25)=4$. Memory Tip: Negative $\\phi$: ACF alternates signs at successive lags, but magnitude still $|\\phi|^h$. Even lags: positive. Odd lags: negative. The series is “oscillating but mean-reverting.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARMA($p,q$) process, which of the following correctly describes the relationship between roots and the spectral density?',opts:['A) The spectral density has no relationship to the polynomial roots','B) All roots always create peaks in $f(\\omega)$ regardless of AR or MA','C) AR roots inside the unit circle create peaks (poles) in $f(\\omega)$; MA roots inside the unit circle create troughs (zeros)','D) AR roots near but outside the unit circle create peaks in $f(\\omega)$ at nearby frequencies; MA roots near but outside the unit circle create troughs in $f(\\omega)$'],ans:3,exp:'$f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\dfrac{|\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}{|\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}$ At frequency $\\omega^*$ corresponding to an AR root $z_0=re^{i\\omega^*}$ with $r$ slightly $&gt;1$: $|\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega^*})|^2\\approx|e^{-i\\omega^*}-z_0^{-1}|^2$ is small $\\Rightarrow$ large peak in $f(\\omega^*)$. Similarly, MA root near unit circle makes $|\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega})|$ small at that frequency $\\Rightarrow$ spectral trough/notch. For a truly stationary/invertible model, all roots are strictly outside the unit circle. Memory Tip: “AR roots create peaks; MA roots create troughs.” The frequency of the peak/trough corresponds to the argument (angle) of the complex root. Close to unit circle $\\Rightarrow$ sharper feature.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'\\scriptsizeRecent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice For estimating the AR(2) process $y_t=\\Phi_1y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2y_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$ using the Yule–Walker equations, the sample autocorrelations are $r_1=0.6$, $r_2=0.4$. The estimate of $\\Phi_2$ is:',opts:['A) $9/16$','B) $-9/16$','C) $-1/16$','D) $1/16$'],ans:3,exp:'The AR(2) Yule–Walker equations are $r_1=\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2r_1$ and $r_2=\\Phi_1r_1+\\Phi_2$. From the first equation: $\\Phi_1=r_1(1-\\Phi_2)=0.6(1-\\Phi_2)$. Substituting into the second: $0.4=0.6(1-\\Phi_2)(0.6)+\\Phi_2=0.36-0.36\\Phi_2+\\Phi_2=0.36+0.64\\Phi_2$ $\\Rightarrow0.64\\Phi_2=0.04\\Rightarrow\\Phi_2=\\dfrac{0.04}{0.64}=\\dfrac{1}{16}$ ✓ (Then $\\hat\\Phi_1=0.6(1-1/16)=0.6\\times15/16=9/16=0.5625$.) Exam Tip: This is the exact numerical pattern tested in the recent UGC NET paper (Unit VII) – always solve $\\Phi_1$ from the lag-1 equation first, then substitute into the lag-2 equation to isolate $\\Phi_2$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'\\scriptsizeRecent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice For estimating the spectral density function of a stationary time series, the standard non-parametric empirical estimator is:',opts:['A) Periodogram','B) Partial autocorrelation function','C) Autocorrelation function','D) Yule–Walker equations'],ans:0,exp:'The periodogram $I(\\omega_k)=\\dfrac{1}{n}\\left|\\sum_{t=1}^n x_te^{-i\\omega_kt}\\right|^2$ is the classical empirical estimator of $f(\\omega)$ – it is the discrete Fourier transform (squared modulus) of the data, equivalently the DFT of the sample ACVF (see Q51). (B), (C): PACF/ACF describe time-domain dependence, not a frequency-domain estimate. (D): Yule–Walker equations estimate AR parameters, not the spectrum directly (though a fitted AR spectrum can be derived afterward). Memory Tip: “Periodogram = sample spectral density.” It is asymptotically unbiased but inconsistent (Q59) – smoothing (Blackman–Tukey/Daniell, Q20/Q62) is needed for a consistent estimate.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'\\scriptsizeRecent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice Consider $y_t=0.7y_{t-1}+0.3y_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$, where $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$ is purely random with $E(\\varepsilon_t)=0$, $E(\\varepsilon_t^2)=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ for all $t$. The ACF of $\\{\\Delta y_t\\}$, say $r_k$, is:',opts:['A) $r_k=(-0.3)^k$','B) $r_k=(0.7)^k$','C) $r_k=(0.3)^k$','D) $r_k=(-0.7)^k$'],ans:0,exp:'Since $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=0.7+0.3=1$, the AR(2) characteristic polynomial has a unit root ($z=1$) – the process is non-stationary and needs one difference. Subtracting $y_{t-1}$ from both sides: $\\Delta y_t=0.7y_{t-1}+0.3y_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t-y_{t-1}=-0.3(y_{t-1}-y_{t-2})+\\varepsilon_t=-0.3\\Delta y_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$. This is AR(1) in $\\Delta y_t$ with coefficient $-\\Phi_2=-0.3$, so its ACF is $r_k=(-0.3)^k$. Shortcut: Whenever $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=1$ for an AR(2), differencing always leaves AR(1) in $\\Delta y_t$ with coefficient exactly $-\\Phi_2$. No need to factor the polynomial explicitly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'\\scriptsizeRecent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice For the AR(1) process $y_t=0.5y_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, with $E(\\varepsilon_t)=0$, $E(\\varepsilon_t^2)=6$, the variance of $y_t$ is:',opts:['A) $24$','B) $12$','C) $7.5$','D) $8$'],ans:3,exp:'Stationary variance of an AR(1): $\\gamma(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{1-\\phi^2}=\\dfrac{6}{1-0.25}=\\dfrac{6}{0.75}=8$. Common mistakes: (B) $6/(1-0.5)=12$ forgets to square $\\phi$; (C) $6(1+0.25)=7.5$ wrongly uses the MA(1)-type formula $\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)$; (A) $6/(1-0.5)^2=24$ squares the denominator\'s linear term instead of $\\phi$ itself. Memory Tip: AR(1) variance $=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$ – always square $\\phi$, never square $(1-\\phi)$. Don\'t confuse this with the MA(1) variance formula $\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'\\scriptsizeRecent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice A random walk with drift, $y_t=\\mu+y_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, has $y_0=5$, $\\mu=2$, and $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$ purely random with $E(\\varepsilon_t)=0$, $E(\\varepsilon_t^2)=3$. Then $E(y_{10})$ and $\\mathrm{Var}(y_{10})$ are respectively:',opts:['A) $25,\\ 3$','B) $20,\\ 30$','C) $5,\\ 30$','D) $25,\\ 30$'],ans:3,exp:'Iterating: $y_t=y_0+t\\mu+\\sum_{s=1}^t\\varepsilon_s$. At $t=10$: $E(y_{10})=y_0+10\\mu=5+10(2)=25$ (drift accumulates linearly with $t$). $\\mathrm{Var}(y_{10})=\\mathrm{Var}\\!\\left(\\sum_{s=1}^{10}\\varepsilon_s\\right)=10\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=10\\times3=30$ (uncorrelated innovations $\\Rightarrow$ variances add). Both the mean (via drift) and variance (via accumulation) grow without bound as $t\\to\\infty$ – the hallmark of a non-stationary random walk. Memory Tip: Random walk with drift $\\mu$: $E(y_t)=y_0+t\\mu$ (linear trend), $\\mathrm{Var}(y_t)=t\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ (grows linearly, drift has no effect on variance). Compare with Q36 (no-drift case: same variance formula, but constant mean).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'\\scriptsizeRecent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice For the process $X_t=\\Phi_1X_{t-1}+\\Phi_2X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\theta_2\\varepsilon_{t-2}$ with $\\Phi_1=0.6,\\Phi_2=0.3,\\theta_1=0.5,\\theta_2=-1.2$, the process is:',opts:['A) Stationary but not invertible','B) Both stationary and invertible','C) Neither stationary nor invertible','D) Invertible but not stationary'],ans:0,exp:'AR part (stationarity triangle, $\\Phi_1=0.6,\\Phi_2=0.3$): $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=0.9&lt;1$ ✓; $\\Phi_2-\\Phi_1=-0.3&lt;1$ ✓; $|\\Phi_2|=0.3&lt;1$ ✓ – comfortably stationary. MA part (invertibility triangle, $\\theta_1=0.5,\\theta_2=-1.2$): the binding condition $|\\theta_2|&lt;1$ fails outright since $|-1.2|=1.2&gt;1$ – a root of the MA polynomial lies inside the unit circle, so the process is not invertible regardless of the other two conditions. Exam Tip: Always check the AR (stationarity) and MA (invertibility) triangles independently – they are unrelated conditions on different polynomials. A process can be stationary with an arbitrarily non-invertible MA part, and vice versa.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'\\scriptsizeRecent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice Let $\\Phi_1=0.9,\\Phi_2=0.1,\\theta_1=0.4,\\theta_2=0$ in $y_t=\\Phi_1y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2y_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\theta_2\\varepsilon_{t-2}$. Then the process is a:',opts:['A) ARMA(2,1) process','B) ARIMA(2,1,0) process','C) ARIMA(1,1,1) process','D) ARMA(1,2) process'],ans:2,exp:'Check $\\Phi(1)=1-\\Phi_1-\\Phi_2=1-0.9-0.1=0$ – the AR polynomial $1-0.9z-0.1z^2$ has a root at $z=1$ (verified: $0.1z^2+0.9z-1=0\\Rightarrow(z-1)(z+10)=0$, roots $z=1,-10$). Factor: $1-0.9B-0.1B^2=(1-B)(1+0.1B)$. So $\\Phi(B)y_t=(1-B)(1+0.1B)y_t$; setting $w_t=(1-B)y_t=\\Delta y_t$: $(1+0.1B)w_t=(1+0.4B)\\varepsilon_t$, i.e., $w_t=-0.1w_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t+0.4\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ – an ARMA(1,1) in the differenced series. Since one difference is needed and the remainder is ARMA(1,1), the original process is ARIMA(1,1,1), not the naively-labelled ARMA(2,1). Common Mistake: Reading off ARMA($p,q$) orders directly from the equation without first checking $\\Phi(1)=1-\\sum\\Phi_j=0$. Always test for a unit root before naming the model order – this is a favourite UGC NET trap.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is a necessary and sufficient condition for a symmetric function $\\gamma(h)$ to be a valid autocovariance function of some stationary process?',opts:['A) $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$','B) $\\gamma(0)&gt;0$ and $|\\gamma(h)|\\leq\\gamma(0)$ for all $h$','C) $\\gamma(h)\\geq 0$ for all $h\\geq 0$','D) The matrix $\\Gamma_n=[\\gamma(i-j)]_{i,j=1}^{n}$ is positive semidefinite for every $n\\geq 1$'],ans:3,exp:'A function $\\gamma:\\mathbb{Z}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ is a valid ACVF if and only if it is symmetric ($\\gamma(h)=\\gamma(-h)$) and non-negative definite: for every $n$ and constants $a_1,\\ldots,a_n$, $$\\sum_{i=1}^n\\sum_{j=1}^n a_i a_j\\gamma(i-j)\\geq 0.$$ This is exactly the condition that the Toeplitz matrix $\\Gamma_n$ is PSD for all $n$. Option (B) is a necessary condition only ($|\\gamma(h)|\\leq\\gamma(0)$ follows from PSD). Option (C) is not required: $\\gamma(h)$ can be negative (e.g. MA(1) with $\\theta&lt;0$). Option (A) is sufficient for absolute summability – a stronger condition than needed for existence. Memory Tip: Valid ACVF $\\Leftrightarrow$ PSD Toeplitz matrix. ACVF can be negative – don\'t confuse with variance being non-negative. The PSD condition is the spectral analogue of Bochner\'s theorem.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the following statements: (I) Strict stationarity always implies weak stationarity. (II) Weak stationarity always implies strict stationarity. (III) For a Gaussian process, weak stationarity implies strict stationarity. Which are correct?',opts:['A) Only (I)','B) (I) and (III) only','C) All three (I), (II) and (III)','D) Only (II) and (III)'],ans:1,exp:'(I) True – with a caveat: Strict stationarity implies weak stationarity only if the second moment $E[X_t^2]&lt;\\infty$. Strictly stationary processes with infinite variance (e.g. Cauchy innovations) are NOT weakly stationary. In the exam context, finite second moments are assumed, so (I) is taken as true. (II) False: Weak stationarity does NOT imply strict stationarity in general. Example: a process with identical mean and ACVF but different marginal distributions at different times can be weakly but not strictly stationary. (III) True: A Gaussian process is fully characterised by its mean and covariance structure. Weak stationarity (constant mean and ACVF) $\\Rightarrow$ all finite-dimensional distributions are shift-invariant $\\Rightarrow$ strictly stationary. Memory Tip: Strict $\\Rightarrow$ Weak (with finite variance). Weak $\\not\\Rightarrow$ Strict in general. But Gaussian: Weak $\\Leftrightarrow$ Strict. This equivalence is unique to Gaussian processes.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a weakly stationary process $\\{X_t\\}$ with mean $\\mu$, the sample mean $\\bar{X}_n = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^n X_t$ converges in mean square to $\\mu$ (ergodic in the mean) if and only if:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(h)=0$ for all $h\\geq 1$ (white noise)','B) $\\sum_{h=0}^{\\infty}|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$ and $\\gamma(h)&gt;0$ for all $h$','C) The process is strictly stationary','D) $\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{h=-(n-1)}^{n-1}\\left(1-\\dfrac{|h|}{n}\\right)\\gamma(h)\\to 0$ as $n\\to\\infty$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar{X}_n)=\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{h=-(n-1)}^{n-1}\\!\\!\\!\\left(1-\\dfrac{|h|}{n}\\right)\\gamma(h)\\to 0$ is the exact condition for $\\bar{X}_n\\xrightarrow{m.s.}\\mu$. A sufficient (but not necessary) condition: $\\sum_{h=0}^{\\infty}|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$ (absolute summability), which implies $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$ fast enough. Intuition: If the ACVF decays to zero (even slowly), time averages converge to ensemble averages. If the series is long-range dependent with $\\sum|\\gamma(h)|=\\infty$, ergodicity may fail. Option (A) is sufficient but far too restrictive – WN is ergodic, but many non-WN processes are also ergodic. Memory Tip: Ergodic in mean $\\Leftrightarrow$ $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar{X}_n)\\to 0$. Sufficient: $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$ as $h\\to\\infty$ (holds for all ARMA processes). Long memory may violate ergodicity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Durbin–Levinson (DL) algorithm recursively computes the PACF $\\phi_{hh}$ and Yule–Walker coefficients. At each step $h$, the update for $\\phi_{hh}$ is:',opts:['A) $\\phi_{hh} = \\phi_{h-1,h-1} + \\rho(h)$','B) $\\phi_{hh} = \\rho(h)/\\rho(h-1)$','C) $\\phi_{hh} = \\dfrac{\\rho(h) - \\sum_{k=1}^{h-1}\\phi_{h-1,k}\\,\\rho(h-k)}{1 - \\sum_{k=1}^{h-1}\\phi_{h-1,k}\\,\\rho(k)}$','D) $\\phi_{hh} = \\rho(h)$'],ans:2,exp:'The DL algorithm provides an $O(p^2)$ method (vs $O(p^3)$ for direct matrix inversion) to compute PACF values and solve Yule–Walker equations. Initialisation: $\\phi_{11}=\\rho(1)$. Recursion for $h\\geq 2$: $$\\phi_{hh}=\\frac{\\rho(h)-\\sum_{k=1}^{h-1}\\phi_{h-1,k}\\rho(h-k)}{1-\\sum_{k=1}^{h-1}\\phi_{h-1,k}\\rho(k)},$$ and $\\phi_{hk}=\\phi_{h-1,k}-\\phi_{hh}\\phi_{h-1,h-k}$ for $k=1,\\ldots,h-1$. Note: $|\\phi_{hh}|&lt;1$ for each $h$ is equivalent to the Toeplitz matrix $\\Gamma_h$ being positive definite. Memory Tip: DL recursion = “partial regression coefficient numerator over denominator, both built from previous-step estimates.” For AR($p$): $\\phi_{hh}=0$ for $h&gt;p$ – the PACF cuts off, confirmed by this formula giving zero.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a purely MA($q$) process with ACF $\\rho(h)$, Bartlett\'s formula gives the asymptotic variance of $\\hat\\rho(h)$ for $h&gt;q$ as:',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{2}{n}$','B) $\\dfrac{1}{n}$','C) $\\dfrac{1}{n}\\left[1+2\\sum_{k=1}^{q}\\rho(k)^2\\right]$','D) $\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\rho(k)^2$'],ans:2,exp:'Bartlett\'s general formula: $\\mathrm{Var}(\\hat\\rho(h))\\approx\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty}[\\rho(k+h)+\\rho(k-h)-2\\rho(h)\\rho(k)]^2$. For MA($q$): $\\rho(k)=0$ for $|k|&gt;q$. When $h&gt;q$, $\\rho(h)=0$, so the formula simplifies to $\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{k=-q}^{q}\\rho(k)^2=\\frac{1}{n}\\left[1+2\\sum_{k=1}^{q}\\rho(k)^2\\right]$. This is larger than $1/n$ (the white noise bound), because the correlations up to lag $q$ inflate the variance of the sample ACF at lags beyond $q$. Practical use: confidence bands for identifying MA($q$) cutoff are wider than $\\pm2/\\sqrt{n}$ if the MA coefficients are large. Memory Tip: For lags beyond the model order: Bartlett SE $&gt;1/\\sqrt{n}$ for MA processes. White noise is the special case $q=0$ giving SE $=1/\\sqrt{n}$. Use the right bands for the right model!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the process $X_t = \\epsilon_t + \\Theta\\,\\epsilon_{t-12}$ (a seasonal MA(1) at period 12), the correlogram (plot of $\\rho(h)$ vs $h$) shows non-zero spikes at:',opts:['A) Lags $h=1$ and $h=12$ only','B) Lags $h=12$ only, with $\\rho(12)=\\Theta/(1+\\Theta^2)$, and zero at all other lags','C) All lags $h=1,2,3,\\ldots$','D) Lags $h=12,24,36,\\ldots$ with geometrically decaying values'],ans:1,exp:'$X_t=\\epsilon_t+\\Theta\\epsilon_{t-12}$. The ACVF: $$\\gamma(h)=\\begin{cases}\\sigma^2(1+\\Theta^2) &amp; h=0\\\\ \\sigma^2\\Theta &amp; |h|=12\\\\ 0 &amp; \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}$$ So $\\rho(12)=\\Theta/(1+\\Theta^2)$ and $\\rho(h)=0$ for $h\\neq 0,12$. There is no spike at lag 1 because $X_t$ and $X_{t-1}$ share no common $\\epsilon$ terms. Option (D) describes a seasonal AR(1), not MA(1): for seasonal AR, $\\rho(12k)=\\Phi^k$, decaying geometrically. This single-spike pattern at $h=s$ is the diagnostic signature of a seasonal MA(1)$_s$. Memory Tip: Seasonal MA(1)$_s$: one spike at lag $s$ in ACF. Seasonal AR(1)$_s$: ACF decays at lags $s,2s,3s,\\ldots$ PACF spike at $s$ only. The seasonal analogues of the non-seasonal ACF/PACF cutoff rules!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the MA(2) process $X_t=\\epsilon_t+0.6\\epsilon_{t-1}-0.4\\epsilon_{t-2}$ with $\\sigma^2=1$, the ACF values $\\rho(1)$ and $\\rho(2)$ are respectively:',opts:['A) $\\rho(1)=0.6,\\ \\rho(2)=-0.4$','B) $\\rho(1)=0.6/1.52\\approx0.395,\\ \\rho(2)=-0.4/1.52\\approx-0.263$','C) $\\rho(1)=(0.6-0.24)/1.52\\approx0.237,\\ \\rho(2)=-0.4/1.52\\approx-0.263$','D) $\\rho(1)=0.6,\\ \\rho(2)=-0.24$'],ans:2,exp:'With $\\theta_1=0.6$, $\\theta_2=-0.4$, $\\sigma^2=1$: \\beginalign* \\gamma(0)&amp;=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2)=1+0.36+0.16=1.52 \\gamma(1)&amp;=\\sigma^2(\\theta_1+\\theta_1\\theta_2)=\\theta_1(1+\\theta_2)=0.6\\times0.6=0.36 \\gamma(2)&amp;=\\sigma^2\\,\\theta_2=-0.4 \\endalign* Therefore $\\rho(1)=0.36/1.52\\approx0.237$ and $\\rho(2)=-0.4/1.52\\approx-0.263$. Common error: Option (A) treats $\\theta_1,\\theta_2$ as $\\rho(1),\\rho(2)$ – this is wrong; the ACF involves ratios. Option (B) uses $\\gamma(1)=\\theta_1=0.6$ incorrectly (misses the $(1+\\theta_2)$ factor). Memory Tip: MA(2) ACVF: $\\gamma(1)=\\sigma^2\\theta_1(1+\\theta_2)$, $\\gamma(2)=\\sigma^2\\theta_2$, $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2)$. Always divide by $\\gamma(0)$ to get $\\rho$. A very common computational question!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An AR(2) process has $\\rho(1)=0.5$ and $\\rho(2)=0.1$. Using the Yule–Walker equations, the parameter estimates $\\hat\\phi_1$ and $\\hat\\phi_2$ are:',opts:['A) $\\hat\\phi_1=0.600,\\ \\hat\\phi_2=0.200$','B) $\\hat\\phi_1=0.450,\\ \\hat\\phi_2=-0.150$','C) $\\hat\\phi_1=0.500,\\ \\hat\\phi_2=0.100$','D) $\\hat\\phi_1=0.600,\\ \\hat\\phi_2=-0.200$'],ans:3,exp:'Formulas: $\\phi_1=\\dfrac{\\rho(1)(1-\\rho(2))}{1-\\rho(1)^2}$, $\\phi_2=\\dfrac{\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2}{1-\\rho(1)^2}$. Denominator: $1-\\rho(1)^2=1-0.25=0.75$. $\\hat\\phi_1=\\dfrac{0.5\\times(1-0.1)}{0.75}=\\dfrac{0.45}{0.75}=0.600$. $\\hat\\phi_2=\\dfrac{0.1-0.25}{0.75}=\\dfrac{-0.15}{0.75}=-0.200$. Stationarity check: $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=0.4&lt;1$; $\\phi_2-\\phi_1=-0.8\\in(-1,1)$; $|\\phi_2|=0.2&lt;1$ – valid stationary AR(2). ✓ Exam Trick: Always compute both $\\hat\\phi_1$ and $\\hat\\phi_2$ fully and verify stationarity. Denominator $=1-\\rho(1)^2$ (never $1-\\rho(1)$ – option (B) is the trap of forgetting to divide by the full denominator, using only the raw numerators $0.45$ and $-0.15$). Option (A) is a sign-error trap on $\\hat\\phi_2$. A negative $\\hat\\phi_2$ is perfectly valid and common when $\\rho(2)&lt;\\rho(1)^2$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(2) process $X_t=\\phi_1 X_{t-1}+\\phi_2 X_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$ with $\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t)=\\sigma^2$, the variance $\\gamma(0)$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{1-\\phi_1\\rho(1)-\\phi_2\\rho(2)}$','B) $\\gamma(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{1-\\phi_1^2-\\phi_2^2}$','C) $\\gamma(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2(1-\\phi_2)}{(1+\\phi_2)[(1-\\phi_2)^2-\\phi_1^2]}$','D) $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2(1+\\phi_1^2+\\phi_2^2)$'],ans:0,exp:'Multiply $X_t=\\phi_1 X_{t-1}+\\phi_2 X_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$ by $X_t$ and take expectations: $$\\gamma(0)=\\phi_1\\gamma(1)+\\phi_2\\gamma(2)+\\sigma^2$$ Divide by $\\gamma(0)$: $1=\\phi_1\\rho(1)+\\phi_2\\rho(2)+\\sigma^2/\\gamma(0)$, giving $$\\gamma(0)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{1-\\phi_1\\rho(1)-\\phi_2\\rho(2)}.$$ Option (B) is wrong: it omits the cross-term structure and is not the correct formula for AR(2). Option (C) is an alternative correct expression obtained after substituting $\\rho(1)$ and $\\rho(2)$ from Yule–Walker – it is equivalent to (A) but less elegant for computation. Option (D) is the MA(2) variance formula – a common trap. Memory Tip: AR(2) variance: $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi_1\\rho(1)-\\phi_2\\rho(2))$. Compare with AR(1): $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi\\cdot\\phi)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi_1\\rho(1))$. Same formula pattern!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An invertible MA(1) process $X_t=\\epsilon_t+\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$ with $|\\theta|&lt;1$ can be written as an AR($\\infty$). Which expression correctly gives this representation?',opts:['A) $X_t = \\theta X_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$','B) $X_t = \\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}(-\\theta)^j X_{t-j}+\\epsilon_t$','C) $X_t = \\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}\\theta^j X_{t-j}+\\epsilon_t$','D) $X_t = -\\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}(-\\theta)^j X_{t-j}+\\epsilon_t$'],ans:1,exp:'From $X_t=\\epsilon_t+\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$, we have $\\epsilon_t=X_t-\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$. Substituting recursively: $$\\epsilon_t=X_t-\\theta X_{t-1}+\\theta^2 X_{t-2}-\\theta^3 X_{t-3}+\\cdots=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}(-\\theta)^j X_{t-j}$$ Rearranging: $X_t=\\epsilon_t-\\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}(-\\theta)^j X_{t-j}=\\epsilon_t+\\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}(-1)^{j+1}\\theta^j X_{t-j}$. Equivalently: $X_t=\\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}(-\\theta)^j X_{t-j}+\\epsilon_t$ since $(-\\theta)^j=(-1)^j\\theta^j$. This converges because $|\\theta|&lt;1$: the AR($\\infty$) coefficients $\\pi_j=(-\\theta)^j\\to 0$. For $\\theta&gt;0$: coefficients alternate in sign. For $\\theta&lt;0$: all positive AR weights. Memory Tip: MA(1) invertible $\\Rightarrow$ AR($\\infty$) with coefficients $\\pi_j=(-\\theta)^j$. Alternating signs for positive $\\theta$. The $(-\\theta)^j$ pattern is the key – not $\\theta^j$ (option A)!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary and invertible ARMA(1,1) process, the PACF $\\phi_{hh}$ for $h\\geq 2$ behaves as:',opts:['A) Cuts off to zero for $h\\geq 3$ (like an AR(2))','B) Is identically zero for all $h\\geq 1$','C) Cuts off to zero for $h\\geq 2$ (like an AR(1))','D) Tails off geometrically – never exactly zero for any finite $h$'],ans:3,exp:'Process | ACF | PACF; AR($p$) | Tails off | Cuts off at lag $p$; MA($q$) | Cuts off at lag $q$ | Tails off; ARMA($p,q$) | Tails off | Tails off For ARMA(1,1): both ACF and PACF tail off exponentially for $h\\geq 1$ (ACF) and $h\\geq 2$ (PACF). Neither cuts off sharply. The tailing behaviour of the PACF for ARMA begins after lag $\\max(p,q)$, not immediately. The rate of decay of the PACF is governed by the AR root: $\\phi_{hh}\\sim c\\cdot\\phi^h$ for large $h$. Memory Tip: ARMA $\\Rightarrow$ both ACF and PACF tail off. This is the key identification rule: if BOTH tail off, fit ARMA. Sharp cutoff in ACF $\\Rightarrow$ MA; in PACF $\\Rightarrow$ AR.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the Wold decomposition $X_t = V_t + \\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\epsilon_{t-j}$, the deterministic component $V_t$ satisfies which property?',opts:['A) $V_t$ is identically zero for all non-trivial processes','B) $V_t$ lies in the closed linear span of $\\{X_{t-1}, X_{t-2},\\ldots\\}$ and is perfectly predictable from the infinite past','C) $V_t$ represents the seasonal component of the series','D) $V_t$ is a deterministic trend function of $t$ (e.g. $at+b$)'],ans:1,exp:'Wold (1938): Every zero-mean weakly stationary process $\\{X_t\\}$ has the unique decomposition $X_t=V_t+Z_t$ where: $\\bullet$ $Z_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\epsilon_{t-j}$ is the linearly regular (purely non-deterministic) part, $\\psi_0=1$, $\\sum\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$. $\\bullet$ $V_t$ is linearly deterministic: it belongs to $\\overline{\\mathrm{sp}}\\{X_{t-1},X_{t-2},\\ldots\\}$ and can be predicted without error from the infinite past using linear predictors. $\\bullet$ $V_t$ and $Z_t$ are uncorrelated: $\\mathrm{Cov}(V_t, Z_s)=0$ for all $t,s$. $V_t$ is NOT necessarily a deterministic function of $t$; it can be a stochastic process that is nonetheless perfectly predictable linearly. Example: $V_t=\\sum_k(A_k\\cos\\lambda_k t+B_k\\sin\\lambda_k t)$ with random $A_k,B_k$ and fixed $\\lambda_k$. Memory Tip: Wold\'s $V_t$ = “linearly predictable from infinite past” – perfectly forecastable. Most ARMA applications have $V_t=0$ (purely non-deterministic). $V_t\\neq0$ arises for processes with hidden periodicities.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(1) process $X_t=\\mu+\\phi(X_{t-1}-\\mu)+\\epsilon_t$ with $|\\phi|&lt;1$, the optimal $h$-step ahead forecast $\\hat{X}_{t+h|t}$ and its error variance are:',opts:['A) $\\hat{X}_{t+h|t}=\\phi^h X_t$; $\\sigma^2_h=h\\sigma^2$','B) $\\hat{X}_{t+h|t}=\\mu$; $\\sigma^2_h=\\gamma(0)$ for all $h\\geq 1$','C) $\\hat{X}_{t+h|t}=\\mu+\\phi(X_t-\\mu)$; $\\sigma^2_h=\\sigma^2$ for all $h$','D) $\\hat{X}_{t+h|t}=\\mu+\\phi^h(X_t-\\mu)$; $\\sigma^2_h=\\sigma^2\\cdot\\dfrac{1-\\phi^{2h}}{1-\\phi^2}$'],ans:3,exp:'By iterating the AR(1): $X_{t+h}=\\mu+\\phi^h(X_t-\\mu)+\\sum_{k=0}^{h-1}\\phi^k\\epsilon_{t+h-k}$. $\\hat{X}_{t+h|t}=\\mu+\\phi^h(X_t-\\mu)$: converges to $\\mu$ as $h\\to\\infty$ (mean reversion). Forecast error: $e_{t+h}=\\sum_{k=0}^{h-1}\\phi^k\\epsilon_{t+h-k}$, so $\\sigma_h^2=\\sigma^2\\sum_{k=0}^{h-1}\\phi^{2k}=\\sigma^2\\cdot\\dfrac{1-\\phi^{2h}}{1-\\phi^2}$. As $h\\to\\infty$: $\\sigma_h^2\\to\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=\\gamma(0)$ – the unconditional variance. Forecast uncertainty approaches the full process variance. Option (C) gives only the 1-step forecast with incorrect variance. Memory Tip: AR(1) forecast $=\\mu+\\phi^h(X_t-\\mu)$: exponential decay of initial deviation from mean. Forecast variance $\\to\\gamma(0)$ as $h\\to\\infty$. Long-run forecast is always the unconditional mean!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a zero-mean stationary ARMA(1,1) process, the $h$-step ahead forecast $\\hat{X}_{t+h|t}$ for $h\\geq 2$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $\\hat{X}_{t+h|t} = \\phi\\,\\hat{X}_{t+h-1|t}$ (same as AR(1) for $h\\geq 2$)','B) $\\hat{X}_{t+h|t} = 0$ because the process is zero-mean','C) $\\hat{X}_{t+h|t} = \\phi^h X_t+\\theta\\phi^{h-1}\\epsilon_t$','D) $\\hat{X}_{t+h|t} = \\phi\\,\\hat{X}_{t+h-1|t}+\\theta\\,\\hat\\epsilon_{t+h-1|t}$, where $\\hat\\epsilon_{t+h-1|t}=0$ for $h\\geq 2$'],ans:0,exp:'For $h=1$: $\\hat{X}_{t+1|t}=\\phi X_t+\\theta\\epsilon_t$ (both $X_t$ and $\\epsilon_t$ are known at time $t$). For $h=2$: $\\hat{X}_{t+2|t}=\\phi\\hat{X}_{t+1|t}+\\theta\\cdot\\underbrace{E[\\epsilon_{t+1}|\\mathcal{F}_t]}_{=0}=\\phi\\hat{X}_{t+1|t}$. For $h\\geq 2$: $\\hat{X}_{t+h|t}=\\phi\\hat{X}_{t+h-1|t}$ – exactly the AR(1) recursion. This is options (A) and (D) combined – option (D) is also correct in stating the innovation is zero, but option (A) captures the essential recursive formula. Key insight: The MA component $\\theta\\epsilon_t$ only impacts the 1-step forecast. Beyond lag 1, the ARMA(1,1) forecast decays just like AR(1). Option (C) gives the correct closed form: $\\hat{X}_{t+h|t}=\\phi^{h-1}(\\phi X_t+\\theta\\epsilon_t)=\\phi^{h-1}\\hat{X}_{t+1|t}$. Memory Tip: ARMA(1,1) forecast: 1-step uses both $X_t$ and $\\epsilon_t$; from 2-steps onwards it just follows AR(1) decay. The MA part “switches off” after 1 step.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The innovations algorithm provides one-step-ahead linear predictors and their mean squared errors for a not necessarily stationary process by recursively computing:',opts:['A) The MA($q$) parameters by minimising the sum of squared innovations','B) The AR($\\infty$) coefficients from the ACVF using Durbin–Levinson','C) The spectral density estimate at each Fourier frequency','D) $\\hat{X}_{n+1}=\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\theta_{nj}(X_{n+1-j}-\\hat{X}_{n+1-j})$ with $\\theta_{nj}$ updated at each step from the covariance structure'],ans:3,exp:'The innovations algorithm (Brockwell &amp; Davis): expresses $\\hat{X}_{n+1}$ as a linear combination of past innovations (one-step prediction errors): $$\\hat{X}_{n+1}=\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\theta_{nj}\\underbrace{(X_{n+1-j}-\\hat{X}_{n+1-j})}_{\\text{innovation at }n+1-j}.$$ Coefficients $\\theta_{nj}$ and MSEs $v_n=E[(X_{n+1}-\\hat{X}_{n+1})^2]$ are computed from the covariance function $\\kappa(i,j)=\\mathrm{Cov}(X_i,X_j)$ – works even for non-stationary processes. vs Durbin–Levinson: DL computes predictors from past observations directly (not innovations) and applies only to stationary processes. Both give the same predictors for stationary processes. The innovations algorithm is used for exact MLE of ARMA models via the exact likelihood $L=\\prod_{t=1}^n v_{t-1}^{-1/2}\\exp[-\\frac{1}{2}(X_t-\\hat{X}_t)^2/v_{t-1}]$. Memory Tip: Innovations algorithm: predict from past errors (innovations). Works for non-stationary too. DL: predict from past observations. Works for stationary only. Both give same answer for stationary ARMA.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For ARMA order selection using a sample of size $n$, the AIC and BIC penalties per additional parameter are respectively:',opts:['A) AIC: $2$; BIC: $\\ln n$','B) AIC: $2/n$; BIC: $2\\ln n/n$','C) AIC: $2$; BIC: $2\\ln n$','D) AIC: $\\ln n$; BIC: $2$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\mathrm{AIC}(p,q)=-2\\ell(\\hat\\theta)+2(p+q+1)$ (Akaike 1974) $\\mathrm{BIC}(p,q)=-2\\ell(\\hat\\theta)+\\ln(n)\\cdot(p+q+1)$ (Schwarz 1978) For $n&gt;8$: $\\ln n&gt;2$, so BIC penalises complexity more heavily than AIC. Consistency: BIC is consistent (selects true order with probability $\\to1$ as $n\\to\\infty$). AIC is not consistent – tends to overfit for large $n$. Efficiency: AIC minimises one-step-ahead prediction MSE asymptotically – better for forecasting. BIC better for model identification. Option (C) is wrong: BIC penalty is $\\ln n$, not $2\\ln n$. Memory Tip: AIC penalises by 2, BIC by $\\ln n$. BIC $&gt;$ AIC penalty for $n&gt;8$ (always in practice). AIC: forecasting. BIC: identification. BIC consistent; AIC efficient. Common exam question!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Hannan–Quinn (HQ) information criterion has a penalty term of $c\\ln\\ln n$ per parameter for a constant $c&gt;2$. Which statement about HQ is correct?',opts:['A) HQ is inconsistent for ARMA order selection','B) HQ is consistent and its penalty grows slower than BIC but faster than AIC','C) HQ and AIC select the same order asymptotically','D) HQ has the smallest penalty among all consistent criteria'],ans:3,exp:'Hannan and Quinn (1979) showed that $c\\ln\\ln n$ is the minimum achievable penalty for strong consistency: any criterion with penalty growing slower than $2\\ln\\ln n$ is inconsistent. Penalty comparison ($n=1000$): $\\ln\\ln n\\approx1.93$; $\\ln n\\approx6.91$. So: $\\mathrm{AIC}(=2)&lt;\\mathrm{HQ}(\\approx2\\times1.93=3.86)&lt;\\mathrm{BIC}(=6.91)$. (B) is partially correct but misleadingly states HQ is consistent – it is. But option (D) captures the deeper result: HQ is the minimum-penalty consistent criterion. HQ is useful as a compromise: more parsimonious than AIC, less conservative than BIC. Memory Tip: AIC $&lt;$ HQ $&lt;$ BIC in terms of penalty. AIC inconsistent; HQ and BIC consistent. HQ = minimum consistent penalty. For large $n$: BIC selects most parsimonious, AIC most complex, HQ in between.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'When estimating an MA(1) model by conditional least squares (CLS), the unobserved $\\epsilon_0$ is initialised to:',opts:['A) $\\epsilon_0 = \\hat\\theta X_0$ using a pilot estimate','B) $\\epsilon_0 = \\bar{X}$ (the sample mean)','C) $\\epsilon_0 = 0$ (its unconditional expectation)','D) $\\epsilon_0 = X_1-\\mu$ (the first demeaned observation)'],ans:2,exp:'In CLS for MA(1): set $\\epsilon_0=0$ and compute innovations recursively: $$\\epsilon_t=X_t-\\mu-\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1},\\quad t=1,\\ldots,n.$$ Minimise $\\sum_{t=1}^n\\epsilon_t^2$ over $(\\mu,\\theta)$. This is fast but loses information in the early observations. Why $\\epsilon_0=0$: Since $E[\\epsilon_0]=0$ (innovations are zero-mean WN), setting it to its expectation is the simplest conditioning approach. Exact MLE: Uses the innovations algorithm or Kalman filter to compute the full likelihood without conditioning on $\\epsilon_0=0$. For large $n$, CLS $\\approx$ exact MLE; for small $n$, exact MLE is preferable. Backforecasting: An iterative method (Box–Jenkins) that updates $\\epsilon_0$ by running the model backward – more accurate than setting $\\epsilon_0=0$ but slower. Memory Tip: CLS for MA: start with $\\epsilon_0=0$, then iterate forward. Exact MLE: no such approximation needed. For $n$ large, both agree. For small $n$ with MA near non-invertibility, use exact MLE.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The ARMA(1,1) model $X_t - 0.5X_{t-1} = \\epsilon_t + 0.5\\epsilon_{t-1}$ is said to be parameter-redundant (over-parameterised) because:',opts:['A) The AR polynomial $\\Phi(z)=1-0.5z$ and MA polynomial $\\Theta(z)=1+0.5z$ share a common factor, making it equivalent to white noise','B) The model violates the invertibility condition $|\\theta|&lt;1$','C) The AR coefficient equals the MA coefficient','D) The variance of $X_t$ is infinite'],ans:0,exp:'The ARMA(1,1) equation: $(1-0.5B)X_t=(1+0.5B)\\epsilon_t$. Note: $1+0.5z$ is NOT the same as $1-0.5z$. Let us recheck: $\\Phi(z)=1-0.5z$, root at $z=2$; $\\Theta(z)=1+0.5z$, root at $z=-2$. These roots are different, so no common factor here. Parameter redundancy occurs when the AR root equals the MA root: e.g. $(1-0.5B)X_t=(1-0.5B)\\epsilon_t$, giving $X_t=\\epsilon_t$ (WN). This happens when $\\phi=\\theta$ with the same sign convention. The canonical example: $\\Phi(z)=1-\\phi z$ and $\\Theta(z)=1-\\phi z$ (same polynomial) $\\Rightarrow$ $X_t=\\epsilon_t$. Always check: are roots of $\\Phi$ and $\\Theta$ the same? If yes, cancel and reduce model order. Memory Tip: ARMA parameter redundancy = common root in AR and MA polynomials. Always cancel common factors before fitting. A redundant ARMA($p,q$) reduces to ARMA($p-1,q-1$). Identifiability requires no common roots.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a Gaussian AR(1) with $\\mu=0$, $\\phi=0.8$, $\\sigma^2=1$, the 95% prediction interval for $X_{t+2}$ given $X_t=2$ is approximately (using $z_{0.025}=1.96$):',opts:['A) $1.28\\pm 1.96\\times\\sqrt{1.64}$','B) $1.28\\pm 1.96\\times\\sqrt{1+\\phi^2}$','C) $0\\pm 1.96\\times\\sqrt{1/(1-\\phi^2)}$','D) $1.28\\pm 1.96\\times1$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\hat{X}_{t+2|t}=\\phi^2 X_t=0.64\\times2=1.28$. Forecast error variance at $h=2$: $\\sigma_2^2=\\sigma^2(1+\\phi^2)=1+0.64=1.64$. 95% PI: $1.28\\pm1.96\\sqrt{1.64}\\approx1.28\\pm2.51$, i.e. $(-1.23,\\ 3.79)$. Option (D) uses only $\\sigma^2=1$ (1-step variance) – wrong for $h=2$. Option (B) uses $\\sqrt{1+\\phi^2}=\\sqrt{1.64}$ – this is correct (same as B) after substituting $\\phi=0.8$. Option (C) gives the unconditional interval (ignoring $X_t=2$) – valid only as $h\\to\\infty$. Note: Options (A) and (B) are equivalent here; (A) is the numeric form and (B) the symbolic form. Memory Tip: $h$-step variance for AR(1) $= \\sigma^2(1+\\phi^2+\\ldots+\\phi^{2(h-1)})$. At $h=2$: $\\sigma^2(1+\\phi^2)$. As $h\\to\\infty$: $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=\\gamma(0)$. Interval widens until it reaches the unconditional width!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a Gaussian random walk $X_t=X_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$, $\\epsilon_t\\sim N(0,\\sigma^2)$, starting at $X_0=x_0$, the conditional distribution of $X_t$ given $X_0=x_0$ is:',opts:['A) $N(x_0,\\ t\\sigma^2)$','B) $N(0,\\ t\\sigma^2)$ regardless of $x_0$','C) $N(x_0,\\ \\sigma^2/(1-1)^2)$ which is undefined','D) $N(x_0,\\ \\sigma^2)$ for all $t\\geq 1$'],ans:0,exp:'$X_t=x_0+\\sum_{s=1}^t\\epsilon_s$. Since $\\epsilon_s\\overset{iid}{\\sim}N(0,\\sigma^2)$: $$E[X_t|X_0]=x_0,\\qquad \\mathrm{Var}(X_t|X_0)=t\\sigma^2.$$ The conditional distribution is $N(x_0, t\\sigma^2)$. As $t\\to\\infty$: variance $\\to\\infty$ $\\Rightarrow$ the conditional distribution spreads out without bound. This illustrates non-stationarity: the variance depends on $t$, not just the time difference. Practical implication: Forecast intervals for a random walk widen as $\\sqrt{t}$ – predictions become extremely uncertain over long horizons, unlike stationary AR(1) where intervals stabilise. Memory Tip: Random walk $X_t|X_0\\sim N(x_0, t\\sigma^2)$. Variance $\\propto t$: grows like a Brownian motion. Prediction interval $\\propto\\sqrt{t}$. This is fundamentally different from stationary AR(1) where intervals cap at $\\sqrt{\\gamma(0)}$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARIMA(0,2,0) process (second-order random walk), the second difference $\\nabla^2 X_t = (1-B)^2 X_t$ equals:',opts:['A) $2X_t - X_{t-1} - X_{t-2}$','B) $X_t - X_{t-2}$','C) $X_t - 2X_{t-1} + X_{t-2}$','D) $X_t^2 - X_{t-1}^2$'],ans:2,exp:'$(1-B)^2=1-2B+B^2$, so $(1-B)^2X_t=X_t-2X_{t-1}+X_{t-2}$. For ARIMA(0,2,0): $\\nabla^2 X_t=\\epsilon_t\\sim\\mathrm{WN}(0,\\sigma^2)$. This means $X_t$ has two unit roots: the AR polynomial is $(1-B)^2$, with both roots at $z=1$. Example of I(2) process: If $\\Delta X_t$ is itself a random walk (I(1)), then $X_t$ is I(2). Real-world: some price-level series where inflation (first difference) is itself non-stationary. Option (B): $X_t-X_{t-2}=(1-B^2)X_t=(1-B)(1+B)X_t$ – this is NOT the same as $(1-B)^2$. This is a common trap! Memory Tip: $(1-B)^2\\neq(1-B^2)$. $(1-B)^2=1-2B+B^2$; $(1-B^2)=(1-B)(1+B)$. Second difference involves $-2$ at lag 1, not zero. Binomial expansion of $(1-B)^d$ always works for any $d$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'After first-differencing a series, the sample ACF of $\\nabla X_t$ decays slowly and the PACF has a single significant spike at lag 2. The most appropriate model for $\\nabla X_t$ is:',opts:['A) MA(2): ARIMA(0,1,2) for $X_t$','B) AR(2): ARIMA(2,1,0) for $X_t$','C) ARMA(1,1): ARIMA(1,1,1) for $X_t$','D) Further differencing is needed (ARIMA(0,2,0))'],ans:1,exp:'After differencing, $W_t=\\nabla X_t$ is (assumed) stationary. Now apply Box–Jenkins identification to $W_t$: $\\bullet$ ACF of $W_t$ decays slowly (tails off) – consistent with AR behaviour. $\\bullet$ PACF of $W_t$ has a single spike at lag 2, then zero – PACF cuts off at lag 2 $\\Rightarrow$ AR(2). Therefore $W_t=\\nabla X_t$ follows AR(2), and $X_t$ follows ARIMA(2,1,0). Option (A) would apply if the ACF of $W_t$ cut off and PACF tailed off (MA structure). Option (D) is wrong: slow ACF decay of $W_t$ suggests AR structure, not a need for further differencing. Over-differencing is a common error. Memory Tip: ARIMA identification two-step: (1) Difference until stationary. (2) Apply ACF/PACF rules to the stationary differenced series. PACF cuts at lag $p$ $\\Rightarrow$ AR($p$) for the differenced series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary ARMA($p,q$) process $X_t$, the long-run cumulative effect of a unit shock (the sum of all impulse response weights) is:',opts:['A) Equal to $\\sigma^2$ (the innovation variance)','B) $\\Psi(1)=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j=\\dfrac{\\Theta(1)}{\\Phi(1)}$','C) Infinite, because shocks accumulate over time','D) Always zero, since the process is stationary'],ans:1,exp:'The MA($\\infty$) representation: $X_t=\\Psi(B)\\epsilon_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\epsilon_{t-j}$. Long-run (cumulative) impulse response: $\\Psi(1)=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j$. Evaluating at $z=1$: $$\\Psi(1)=\\frac{\\Theta(1)}{\\Phi(1)}.$$ For AR(1): $\\Psi(1)=1/(1-\\phi)$ – large when $\\phi\\to1$. For a random walk ($\\phi=1$): $\\Phi(1)=0$, so $\\Psi(1)=\\infty$ – shocks have permanent (infinite) cumulative effect. This is the key distinction between I(0) and I(1) processes. Option (D) is wrong: stationary does not mean zero long-run effect; it means the effect decays to zero eventually, but the sum $\\Psi(1)$ is finite and typically non-zero. Memory Tip: Long-run multiplier $=\\Theta(1)/\\Phi(1)$: plug $z=1$ into both polynomials. I(0): finite. I(1): $\\Phi(1)=0$ $\\Rightarrow$ infinite $\\Rightarrow$ permanent shocks. This is why unit roots matter economically!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the Augmented Dickey–Fuller (ADF) test regression $\\Delta X_t=\\alpha+\\beta X_{t-1}+\\sum_{k=1}^p c_k\\Delta X_{t-k}+u_t$, the lag length $p$ is chosen to:',opts:['A) Equal the number of unit roots in the series','B) Minimise the $p$-value of the ADF $t$-statistic','C) Equal $\\sqrt{n}$ rounded to the nearest integer','D) Ensure the residuals $\\hat{u}_t$ are free of serial correlation, typically via AIC/BIC or the sequential testing approach (general-to-specific)'],ans:3,exp:'The augmented terms $\\sum c_k\\Delta X_{t-k}$ absorb serial correlation in the original DF residuals. If $p$ is too small: residuals $\\hat{u}_t$ are serially correlated $\\Rightarrow$ DF distribution invalid, test size distorted. Methods for choosing $p$: 1. AIC/BIC: fit ADF for $p=0,1,\\ldots,p_{\\max}$; choose $p$ minimising criterion. 2. General-to-specific: start with $p_{\\max}$; test significance of highest lag; reduce until last lag is significant. 3. Rule of thumb: $p_{\\max}=\\lfloor12(n/100)^{1/4}\\rfloor$ (Schwert 1989). Option (C) ($p=\\sqrt{n}$) overestimates lag length in most practical cases. Memory Tip: ADF lag $p$ purpose: whiten residuals, not maximise significance. Using too few lags inflates test size (over-rejects $H_0$). Always check residual ACF after ADF. Schwert rule: $p_{\\max}\\approx12(n/100)^{1/4}$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Hylleberg–Engle–Granger–Yoo (HEGY) test is designed to test for:',opts:['A) ARCH effects in seasonal time series','B) Cointegration between seasonally adjusted series','C) Unit roots at the zero frequency only (same as standard ADF)','D) Unit roots at seasonal frequencies $\\omega_k=2\\pi k/s$ for $k=0,1,\\ldots,s/2$ simultaneously'],ans:3,exp:'The seasonal differencing operator $(1-B^s)$ factors as: $(1-B^s)=\\prod_{k=0}^{s-1}(1-e^{2\\pi ik/s}B)$, containing unit roots at each seasonal frequency $\\omega_k=2\\pi k/s$. HEGY (1990) tests $H_0$: unit root at each specific seasonal frequency separately. For quarterly data ($s=4$): tests at $\\omega=0$ (annual trend), $\\omega=\\pi$ (biannual), and $\\omega=\\pi/2$ (annual seasonal) – three separate tests. If unit roots are confirmed at seasonal frequencies, seasonal differencing $(1-B^s)$ is appropriate. If only at $\\omega=0$, regular differencing $(1-B)$ suffices. This avoids over-differencing by applying $(1-B^s)$ when not all seasonal unit roots are present. Memory Tip: HEGY = “seasonal ADF.” Tests at ALL seasonal frequencies. ADF only tests at $\\omega=0$. Use HEGY before blindly applying seasonal differencing. Seasonal unit root $\\neq$ regular unit root!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the spectral (Cramer) representation $X_t=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}e^{i\\lambda t}\\,dZ(\\lambda)$, the process $Z(\\lambda)$ on $[-\\pi,\\pi]$ satisfies which key property?',opts:['A) $Z(\\lambda)$ is deterministic and encodes only the amplitude at frequency $\\lambda$','B) The increments $dZ(\\lambda)$ are orthogonal: $E[dZ(\\lambda)\\overline{dZ(\\mu)}]=0$ for $\\lambda\\neq\\mu$','C) $Z(\\lambda)$ is a differentiable function of $\\lambda$ for any stationary process','D) $Z(\\lambda)=f(\\lambda)$ where $f$ is the spectral density'],ans:1,exp:'$Z(\\lambda)$ is a complex-valued stochastic process with orthogonal increments: $$E[dZ(\\lambda)\\overline{dZ(\\mu)}]=\\begin{cases}F(d\\lambda)=f(\\lambda)d\\lambda &amp; \\lambda=\\mu\\\\ 0 &amp; \\lambda\\neq\\mu\\end{cases}$$ where $F$ is the spectral distribution function. The orthogonality of increments is the frequency-domain analogue of uncorrelated innovations in the time domain. It ensures that different frequency components evolve independently. The ACVF is recovered via: $\\gamma(h)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}e^{i\\lambda h}f(\\lambda)d\\lambda$ (Wiener–Khinchin). This representation exists for all weakly stationary processes (not just ARMA) – it is completely general. Memory Tip: $Z(\\lambda)$ = “complex amplitude at frequency $\\lambda$.” Orthogonal increments: different frequencies don\'t interact. $|dZ(\\lambda)|^2$ = power at frequency $\\lambda$ = $f(\\lambda)d\\lambda$. This is the foundation of spectral analysis.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density of the ARMA(2,1) process $X_t=\\phi_1 X_{t-1}+\\phi_2 X_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t+\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$ is:',opts:['A) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\cdot\\dfrac{1+2\\theta\\cos\\omega+\\theta^2}{1-2(\\phi_1\\cos\\omega+\\phi_2\\cos2\\omega)+(\\phi_1^2+\\phi_2^2)}$','B) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}(1+\\theta^2+2\\theta\\cos\\omega)$','C) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi|1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2|^2}$ (constant)','D) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\cdot\\dfrac{|1+\\theta e^{-i\\omega}|^2}{|1-\\phi_1 e^{-i\\omega}-\\phi_2 e^{-2i\\omega}|^2}$'],ans:3,exp:'For ARMA($p,q$): $\\Phi(B)X_t=\\Theta(B)\\epsilon_t$, the spectral density is: $$f(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\cdot\\frac{|\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}{|\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}.$$ For ARMA(2,1): $\\Phi(z)=1-\\phi_1z-\\phi_2z^2$, $\\Theta(z)=1+\\theta z$. Option (A) expands the denominator incorrectly (a common algebraic error – the denominator should be $|1-\\phi_1 e^{-i\\omega}-\\phi_2 e^{-2i\\omega}|^2$, not a sum of separate cosine terms). Option (B) gives the MA(1) spectral density only (no AR part). Option (C) would be constant, which only applies to white noise. Key: The modulus squared $|f(e^{-i\\omega})|^2=f(e^{-i\\omega})\\cdot\\overline{f(e^{-i\\omega})}$ converts complex expressions to real, positive functions. Memory Tip: $f(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\cdot\\frac{|\\text{MA poly at }e^{-i\\omega}|^2}{|\\text{AR poly at }e^{-i\\omega}|^2}$. AR contributes denominator (peaks). MA contributes numerator (troughs or bumps). White noise: both polynomials $=1$, so $f=\\sigma^2/(2\\pi)$ flat.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An AR(2) process has complex conjugate roots $r e^{\\pm i\\lambda_0}$ with $r&gt;1$. The spectral density $f(\\omega)$ of this process:',opts:['A) Has a peak at frequency $\\omega=\\lambda_0$ and a trough at $\\omega=0$','B) Is undefined because complex roots violate stationarity','C) Is flat (white noise) because complex roots cancel','D) Has a single sharp peak at $\\omega=\\lambda_0$, with height proportional to $(r^2-1)^{-2}$'],ans:3,exp:'For stationary AR(2) with conjugate roots $re^{\\pm i\\lambda_0}$, $r&gt;1$: $\\Phi(z)=(1-z/re^{i\\lambda_0})(1-z/re^{-i\\lambda_0})$. At $\\omega=\\lambda_0$: $$|\\Phi(e^{-i\\lambda_0})|^2\\approx\\frac{1}{r^2}|1-e^{-2i\\lambda_0}/r^2|\\to0\\text{ as }r\\to1^+,$$ so $f(\\lambda_0)\\to\\infty$ as the root approaches the unit circle. Near-unit-circle complex roots create sharp pseudo-cyclical peaks at $\\omega=\\lambda_0$ in the spectrum – the corresponding period is $T=2\\pi/\\lambda_0$. This is how AR(2) models generate quasi-periodic behaviour: the dominant cycle has period $T$, and the spectral peak sharpens as $r\\to1$. Memory Tip: Complex AR roots $\\Rightarrow$ cyclical behaviour. Root angle $\\lambda_0$ = dominant frequency. Root modulus $r$ close to 1 $\\Rightarrow$ sharp spectral peak $\\Rightarrow$ strong quasi-periodicity. Period = $2\\pi/\\lambda_0$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a Gaussian stationary process with spectral density $f(\\omega)$, the periodogram ordinates $I(\\omega_j)$ at distinct Fourier frequencies $\\omega_j=2\\pi j/n$ are asymptotically:',opts:['A) Exactly $\\chi^2_2$ regardless of $f(\\omega_j)$','B) $N(f(\\omega_j),\\, f(\\omega_j)^2)$ (approximately Gaussian)','C) $f(\\omega_j)\\cdot\\chi^2_2/2$, i.e. independently exponentially distributed with mean $f(\\omega_j)$','D) $N(0,\\, f(\\omega_j)/n)$ (variance decreasing with $n$)'],ans:2,exp:'At Fourier frequency $\\omega_j$ (for $0&lt;\\omega_j&lt;\\pi$): $$I(\\omega_j)\\xrightarrow{d}f(\\omega_j)\\cdot\\frac{\\chi^2_2}{2}\\equiv\\mathrm{Exp}(f(\\omega_j)).$$ Mean $= f(\\omega_j)$ (unbiased asymptotically), but variance $= f(\\omega_j)^2$ (does NOT decrease with $n$). This is why the periodogram is inconsistent: variance stays constant as $n\\to\\infty$. Moreover, ordinates at different Fourier frequencies are asymptotically independent – a remarkable property that facilitates spectral analysis. Option (D) would imply consistency – wrong. At $\\omega=0$ and $\\omega=\\pi$: $I(\\omega)\\sim f(\\omega)\\chi^2_1$ (single degree of freedom, half the variance). Memory Tip: Periodogram $\\sim f(\\omega)\\chi^2_2/2$: mean is $f(\\omega)$, variance is $f(\\omega)^2$ (constant!). Asymptotically independent across frequencies – the “spectral white noise” property. Inconsistency $\\Rightarrow$ must smooth.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Daniell (modified) spectral estimator averages $m$ adjacent periodogram ordinates around frequency $\\omega_j$: $\\hat{f}(\\omega_j)=\\frac{1}{2m+1}\\sum_{k=-m}^{m}I(\\omega_{j+k})$. Its degrees of freedom are approximately:',opts:['A) $n/m$','B) $2$ (same as the raw periodogram)','C) $m$','D) $2(2m+1)$'],ans:3,exp:'Since each $I(\\omega_k)\\sim\\text{Exp}(f(\\omega_k))\\approx f(\\omega_j)\\chi^2_2/2$, averaging $2m+1$ independent ordinates gives: $$\\hat{f}(\\omega_j)\\sim\\frac{f(\\omega_j)\\chi^2_{\\nu}}{\\nu},\\quad\\nu=2(2m+1).$$ More averaging (larger $m$) $\\Rightarrow$ more degrees of freedom $\\Rightarrow$ lower variance: $\\mathrm{Var}(\\hat{f})\\approx\\frac{2f(\\omega_j)^2}{\\nu}=\\frac{f(\\omega_j)^2}{2m+1}$. Bias-variance trade-off: larger $m$ reduces variance but increases bias (since $f$ is assumed constant over a wider band). Confidence interval for $f(\\omega_j)$: $\\left[\\frac{\\nu\\hat{f}}{\\chi^2_{\\nu,\\alpha/2}},\\, \\frac{\\nu\\hat{f}}{\\chi^2_{\\nu,1-\\alpha/2}}\\right]$ (chi-squared interval, not symmetric!). Memory Tip: Daniell with bandwidth $m$: average $2m+1$ ordinates, d.f. $=2(2m+1)$. More bandwidth $\\Rightarrow$ smoother estimate, more d.f., but higher bias. CI uses chi-squared (not normal) because spectral estimates are chi-squared distributed.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Wiener–Khinchin theorem establishes that the spectral density $f(\\omega)$ and the ACVF $\\gamma(h)$ are related by:',opts:['A) $f(\\omega)=\\gamma(0)/\\pi$ for all $\\omega$ (constant spectrum)','B) $f(\\omega)=\\gamma(\\omega)$ (they are equal functions)','C) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega h}$ and $\\gamma(h)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)e^{i\\omega h}d\\omega$','D) $f(\\omega)=|\\gamma(\\omega)|^2/(2\\pi)$'],ans:2,exp:'The Wiener–Khinchin theorem: $$f(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega h},\\qquad\\gamma(h)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)e^{i\\omega h}d\\omega.$$ This is a Fourier transform pair relating the time-domain (ACVF) and frequency-domain (spectral density) representations of a stationary process. Setting $h=0$: $\\gamma(0)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)d\\omega$ – the total variance equals the integral of the spectral density (Parseval-type identity). The theorem requires $\\sum|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$ for $f$ to be continuous and bounded; otherwise $f$ may be a generalised function (spectral measure). Memory Tip: Wiener–Khinchin: ACVF $\\leftrightarrow$ spectral density via Fourier transform. The “two-sided” relationship: $\\gamma\\to f$ (analysis) and $f\\to\\gamma$ (synthesis). $\\gamma(0)=\\int f(\\omega)d\\omega$ = total variance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a white noise process $\\epsilon_t\\sim\\mathrm{WN}(0,\\sigma^2)$, the spectral density $f(\\omega)$ is:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2/(2\\pi)$ for all $\\omega\\in[-\\pi,\\pi]$','B) $\\sigma^2\\cdot\\delta(\\omega)$ (a Dirac delta at $\\omega=0$)','C) $0$ for all $\\omega\\neq 0$ and $\\infty$ at $\\omega=0$','D) $\\sigma^2$ for all $\\omega\\in[-\\pi,\\pi]$'],ans:0,exp:'For WN: $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2$, $\\gamma(h)=0$ for $h\\neq0$. $f(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega h}=\\frac{\\gamma(0)}{2\\pi}=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}$. This is flat (constant) across all frequencies – the “white light” analogy (white light contains all colours equally). Verification: $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f(\\omega)d\\omega=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\cdot2\\pi=\\sigma^2=\\gamma(0)$. ✓ Options (B) and (C) would apply to a deterministic sinusoid $X_t=A\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi)$, which concentrates all power at $\\pm\\omega_0$. Option (D) misses the $1/(2\\pi)$ normalisation factor. Memory Tip: WN spectrum: $\\sigma^2/(2\\pi)$ – flat. The $1/(2\\pi)$ normalisation ensures $\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}f d\\omega=\\sigma^2=\\gamma(0)$. “White” = equal power at all frequencies. An AR process concentrates power at low (or high) frequencies.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Bartlett (triangular) lag window estimator of spectral density is defined by $w_B(h)=(1-|h|/M)$ for $|h|\\leq M$, zero otherwise, in the truncated ACVF estimator. Which property does this window guarantee?',opts:['A) The variance of $\\hat{f}(\\omega)$ equals $1/M$ regardless of $f(\\omega)$','B) The spectral estimate $\\hat{f}(\\omega)\\geq 0$ for all $\\omega$ (non-negative definite)','C) The spectral estimate is consistent only for white noise','D) The spectral estimate is always negative at some frequencies'],ans:1,exp:'The lag window estimator: $\\hat{f}(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{h=-(M-1)}^{M-1}w(h)\\hat\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega h}$. For $\\hat{f}(\\omega)\\geq0$ everywhere, the window $w(h)$ must have a non-negative Fourier transform (the spectral window $W(\\omega)\\geq0$). The Bartlett window $w_B(h)=\\max(1-|h|/M,0)$ is the convolution of a rectangular window with itself, so its Fourier transform $W_B(\\omega)=M\\left(\\frac{\\sin(M\\omega/2)}{M\\omega/2}\\right)^2\\geq0$ for all $\\omega$. The rectangular (Dirichlet) window does NOT guarantee non-negativity – it can produce negative spectral estimates (a well-known drawback). Other non-negative windows: Parzen, Blackman–Tukey with positive kernel. Memory Tip: Non-negativity of spectral estimate: Bartlett (triangular) window $\\checkmark$; Rectangular (Dirichlet) $\\times$. Negative spectral estimate is physically meaningless (power must be $\\geq0$). Bartlett window = triangular = safe choice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Band spectrum regression (Engle 1974) restricts the regression relationship to hold only at a specific range of frequencies. This approach is useful when:',opts:['A) The regression errors are white noise at all frequencies','B) Both variables are I(2) and standard regression fails','C) The spectral density of the regressors is identically zero outside the band','D) The relationship between variables differs across frequency bands (e.g. a long-run relationship exists but not a short-run one)'],ans:3,exp:'Ordinary regression uses all frequencies equally. But in economics: $\\bullet$ Long-run relationship: near $\\omega=0$ (low frequency component). $\\bullet$ Business cycle: frequencies corresponding to 6–32 quarter cycles. $\\bullet$ Short-run dynamics: high frequencies. Band spectrum regression: apply OLS to the filtered series that retains only the desired frequency band. The estimator minimises the sum of squared errors in the frequency domain restricted to the band $[\\omega_1,\\omega_2]$. This provides frequency-specific coefficient estimates – a long-run coefficient may differ substantially from a short-run one, which is empirically common in macroeconomics. Memory Tip: Band spectrum regression = “run the regression only at specific frequencies.” Long-run: low $\\omega$. Business cycle: mid $\\omega$. Short-run: high $\\omega$. Frequency-specific relationships are the key motivation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Burg algorithm for estimating AR($p$) parameters differs from Yule–Walker estimation in that it:',opts:['A) Minimises the sum of both forward and backward prediction errors jointly, always guaranteeing stationary estimates','B) Uses the spectral density rather than the sample ACVF','C) Requires Gaussian innovations for consistency','D) Is equivalent to MLE for all sample sizes'],ans:0,exp:'The Burg algorithm uses a DL-like recursion but updates the reflection (PACF) coefficients by minimising $\\sum_{t=p}^n[(f_{p,t})^2+(b_{p,t})^2]$, where $f_{p,t}$ and $b_{p,t}$ are forward and backward prediction errors. Advantages over YW: 1. Better spectral frequency resolution for short data. 2. Always yields $|\\phi_{hh}|&lt;1$ at each order $\\Rightarrow$ stationary estimates guaranteed. 3. No need to compute/store the sample ACVF. Disadvantages: Can exhibit spectral line splitting for sinusoidal signals; does not minimise a standard likelihood. Yule–Walker can give estimates outside the stationarity region in finite samples due to ACVF estimation errors; Burg never does. Memory Tip: Burg = “two-directional prediction error minimisation.” Always stationary (reflection coefficients $&lt;1$). Better for short series. YW = “moment matching” – simple but may give non-stationary estimates for small $n$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'After fitting an ARMA($p,q$) model, the Ljung–Box statistic $Q_{LB}=n(n+2)\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\hat\\rho_\\epsilon^2(k)/(n-k)$ for the residuals has the asymptotic null distribution:',opts:['A) $\\chi^2_{m-p-q}$','B) $\\chi^2_m$','C) $F(m,n-m)$','D) $\\chi^2_{m+p+q}$'],ans:0,exp:'When applied to raw data under WN $H_0$: $Q_{LB}\\sim\\chi^2_m$. When applied to ARMA($p,q$) residuals: $p+q$ parameters have been estimated, consuming $p+q$ degrees of freedom. The adjusted null distribution is $\\chi^2_{m-p-q}$. Practical rule: Use $m=10$ or $m=20$ lags; critical value from $\\chi^2_{m-p-q}$ at 5%. Common mistake: Using $\\chi^2_m$ (the raw data distribution) for model residuals – this gives a test that is too liberal (rejects $H_0$ too rarely), failing to detect model inadequacy. For ARMA(1,1): $Q_{LB}(20)\\sim\\chi^2_{18}$ at lag 20. Memory Tip: Ljung–Box for ARMA residuals: df $= m-p-q$ (subtract estimated parameters). For raw data: df $=m$. Always adjust the degrees of freedom when testing fitted-model residuals. A very frequent exam trap!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A standard diagnostic for ARMA order adequacy is the “overfitting test.” If an ARMA($p,q$) model is fitted and then an ARMA($p+1,q$) is also fitted, the overfitting test checks:',opts:['A) Whether the residual ACF changes significantly','B) Whether the model becomes non-stationary with the extra term','C) Whether the extra AR coefficient $\\hat\\phi_{p+1}$ is statistically significant (its $t$-ratio exceeds 2)','D) Whether AIC decreases when the extra parameter is added'],ans:2,exp:'The overfitting principle (Box–Jenkins): if the true model is ARMA($p,q$), fitting ARMA($p+1,q$) should give $\\hat\\phi_{p+1}\\approx0$ with large standard error and small $|t|$. Decision rule: if $|t(\\hat\\phi_{p+1})|&lt;2$ (not significant) $\\Rightarrow$ ARMA($p,q$) is adequate. If significant $\\Rightarrow$ AR order may need to increase. This is applied in both directions: ARMA($p+1,q$), ARMA($p,q+1$), and sometimes ARMA($p+1,q+1$). Option (D) is the AIC-based selection – a separate approach. Overfitting test is a significance test, not an information criterion comparison. Option (A) is useful but qualitative; the overfitting test is the formal version. Memory Tip: Overfitting test = “add one more parameter; is it needed?” Insignificant extra parameter $\\Rightarrow$ current model adequate. Check ARMA($p+1,q$) AND ARMA($p,q+1$). Both should have insignificant extras for the base model to be accepted.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The MA(2) process $X_t=\\epsilon_t+\\theta_1\\epsilon_{t-1}+\\theta_2\\epsilon_{t-2}$ is invertible if and only if $(\\theta_1,\\theta_2)$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2&lt;1$','B) $|\\theta_1\\theta_2|&lt;1$','C) $|\\theta_1|&lt;1$ and $|\\theta_2|&lt;1$ individually','D) $\\theta_1+\\theta_2&gt;-1$, $\\theta_2-\\theta_1&gt;-1$, $|\\theta_2|&lt;1$'],ans:3,exp:'Invertibility requires all roots of $\\Theta(z)=1+\\theta_1z+\\theta_2z^2$ to lie outside the unit circle. By the exact mirror of the AR(2) stationarity argument (with $\\phi_k\\to-\\theta_k$ in sign convention), the conditions are: $$\\theta_1+\\theta_2&gt;-1,\\qquad\\theta_2-\\theta_1&gt;-1,\\qquad|\\theta_2|&lt;1.$$ This defines a triangular region in $(\\theta_1,\\theta_2)$ space – exactly analogous to the AR(2) stationarity triangle. Option (C) is wrong: $|\\theta_1|&lt;1$ and $|\\theta_2|&lt;1$ do not jointly guarantee invertibility (same issue as for AR(2) stationarity). Duality: AR(2) stationarity $\\leftrightarrow$ MA(2) invertibility via the same triangular region (with sign adjustment). Memory Tip: MA(2) invertibility triangle $\\equiv$ AR(2) stationarity triangle (up to sign convention). Both defined by three inequalities. Individual $|\\theta_k|&lt;1$ is NOT sufficient – always check the triangle!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The best linear predictor (BLP) $\\hat{X}_{n+h}$ of $X_{n+h}$ given $\\{X_1,\\ldots,X_n\\}$ minimises the mean squared prediction error. For a Gaussian process, the BLP coincides with:',opts:['A) The sample mean $\\bar{X}_n$','B) The conditional expectation $E[X_{n+h}|\\mathcal{F}_n]$','C) The median of $X_{n+h}|\\mathcal{F}_n$','D) The mode of $X_{n+h}|\\mathcal{F}_n$'],ans:1,exp:'General fact: The minimum MSE predictor of $X_{n+h}$ (unrestricted, possibly nonlinear) is always the conditional expectation $E[X_{n+h}|\\mathcal{F}_n]$. For linear prediction only: The BLP is the projection onto the linear span of $\\{1,X_n,X_{n-1},\\ldots\\}$. For Gaussian processes: The conditional distribution $X_{n+h}|\\mathcal{F}_n$ is Gaussian with mean $=$ BLP. So the BLP and conditional mean coincide, and the conditional mean is linear in $\\{X_1,\\ldots,X_n\\}$. Median = mean = mode for Gaussian. For non-Gaussian processes, the BLP (linear) may differ from the conditional mean (nonlinear optimal predictor). The conditional mean is always at least as good. Memory Tip: Minimum MSE predictor = $E[X_{n+h}|\\mathcal{F}_n]$ always. For Gaussian: this IS the BLP (linear in past observations). For non-Gaussian: BLP is suboptimal but still valid. Conditional expectation is the gold standard.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARIMA(1,1,0) model, the $h$-step-ahead forecasts $\\hat{X}_{T+h|T}$ as $h\\to\\infty$ approach:',opts:['A) A linear function of $h$ (straight-line eventual forecast)','B) Zero, since the process is eventually stationary after differencing','C) An exponentially growing function of $h$','D) The unconditional mean of the differenced series'],ans:0,exp:'For ARIMA(1,1,0): $\\nabla X_t=\\phi\\nabla X_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$. The $h$-step forecast of $\\nabla X_{T+h}$ converges to the unconditional mean (= 0 if no drift) as $h\\to\\infty$. Therefore $\\hat{X}_{T+h|T}=\\hat{X}_{T+1|T}+(h-1)\\times0=$ constant for no-drift case. Actually more precisely: $\\hat{X}_{T+h|T}=X_T + h\\cdot\\hat{\\nabla X}_{T+1|T}\\to$ linear in $h$ once the AR transient dies away. General rule: For ARIMA($p,d,q$), the eventual forecast function is a polynomial of degree $d-1$ in $h$ (for $d=1$: constant; but since we are accumulating $\\nabla\\hat{X}$, the forecast of $X$ itself is linear in $h$). With drift: slope equals the estimated drift. This is why ARIMA(0,1,0) with drift = “random walk with drift” forecast is a straight line. Memory Tip: ARIMA($p,d,q$) eventual forecast: polynomial of degree $d$ in $h$. $d=0$ (ARMA): converges to constant (mean). $d=1$ (ARIMA): straight line. $d=2$: quadratic. The $d$ determines the long-run forecast shape!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ARIMA(0,1,1) model $\\nabla X_t=\\epsilon_t+\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$, the optimal simple exponential smoothing (SES) weight $\\alpha$ (for minimum MSE 1-step forecast) is related to $\\theta$ by:',opts:['A) $\\alpha=\\theta$','B) $\\alpha=1+\\theta$ (noting $\\theta\\in(-1,0)$ for optimal SES)','C) $\\alpha=1/(1-\\theta)$','D) $\\alpha=-\\theta/(1+\\theta)$'],ans:1,exp:'The ARIMA(0,1,1) model: $X_t=X_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t+\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$. The 1-step forecast: $\\hat{X}_{t+1|t}=X_t+\\theta\\hat\\epsilon_t=X_t+\\theta(X_t-\\hat{X}_{t|t-1})$. This is exactly the SES recursion $\\hat{X}_{t+1|t}=\\alpha X_t+(1-\\alpha)\\hat{X}_{t|t-1}$ with $\\alpha=1+\\theta$. For SES to be valid: $0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1\\Rightarrow-1&lt;\\theta&lt;0$. So the MA coefficient is negative in the invertible range for the SES connection. The optimal $\\hat\\theta$ (MLE) gives the optimal $\\alpha=1+\\hat\\theta$. This provides the statistical justification for SES: it is MLE-optimal for the ARIMA(0,1,1) model. Memory Tip: ARIMA(0,1,1) $\\equiv$ SES with $\\alpha=1+\\theta$. Since $\\theta\\in(-1,0)$: $\\alpha\\in(0,1)$. Small $|\\theta|$ (near 0): $\\alpha\\approx1$ (reactive). Large $|\\theta|$ (near $-1$): $\\alpha\\approx0$ (very smooth). The sign of $\\theta$ is negative for SES equivalence.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Bates and Granger (1969) showed that combining two unbiased forecasts $\\hat{f}_{1t}$ and $\\hat{f}_{2t}$ with MSEs $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ and error correlation $\\rho$ by $\\hat{f}_c=w\\hat{f}_1+(1-w)\\hat{f}_2$ has optimal weight:',opts:['A) $w=1$ if $\\sigma_1^2&lt;\\sigma_2^2$, else $w=0$','B) $w=\\dfrac{\\sigma_2^2-\\rho\\sigma_1\\sigma_2}{\\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2-2\\rho\\sigma_1\\sigma_2}$','C) $w=\\sigma_1^2/(\\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2)$','D) $w=1/2$ always (equal weights are optimal)'],ans:1,exp:'Minimise $\\mathrm{MSE}(w)=w^2\\sigma_1^2+(1-w)^2\\sigma_2^2+2w(1-w)\\rho\\sigma_1\\sigma_2$ w.r.t. $w$: Setting $\\frac{d}{dw}\\mathrm{MSE}=0$ gives $w^*=\\dfrac{\\sigma_2^2-\\rho\\sigma_1\\sigma_2}{\\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2-2\\rho\\sigma_1\\sigma_2}$. Special cases: $\\bullet$ $\\rho=0$ (uncorrelated errors): $w^*=\\sigma_2^2/(\\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2)$ – inverse variance weighting. $\\bullet$ $\\sigma_1=\\sigma_2$: $w^*=1/2$ (equal weights) regardless of $\\rho$. $\\bullet$ $\\rho=1$: $w^*=\\sigma_2/(\\sigma_1+\\sigma_2)$ – combination still helps if MSEs differ. Key insight: Combination generally beats the best individual forecast unless $\\rho=1$ and $\\sigma_1=\\sigma_2$. Memory Tip: Optimal combination: give more weight to the model with lower MSE. The formula is the ratio of (other model\'s variance minus covariance) to (sum of variances minus twice covariance). For $\\rho=0$: inverse-variance weighting. Combination almost always helps!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary Gaussian AR(1) with $|\\phi|&lt;1$, the asymptotic variance of the MLE $\\hat\\phi$ satisfies $\\sqrt{n}(\\hat\\phi-\\phi)\\xrightarrow{d}N(0,V)$ where:',opts:['A) $V=1/(1-\\phi^2)$','B) $V=(1-\\phi^2)^2/\\sigma^2$','C) $V=\\sigma^2(1-\\phi^2)$','D) $V=1-\\phi^2$'],ans:3,exp:'For Gaussian AR(1), the Fisher information per observation for $\\phi$ is: $$I(\\phi)=\\frac{1}{1-\\phi^2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sigma^2}\\times\\sigma^2=\\frac{1}{1-\\phi^2}.$$ (The $\\sigma^2$ in numerator and denominator cancel in the standardised version.) Asymptotic variance: $V=[nI(\\phi)]^{-1}\\times n=(1-\\phi^2)$. So $\\sqrt{n}(\\hat\\phi-\\phi)\\xrightarrow{d}N(0,1-\\phi^2)$. Intuition: Near unit root ($\\phi\\to1$): $V=1-\\phi^2\\to0$ – estimation becomes easier, not harder (super-consistency). This is why unit root econometrics has non-standard asymptotics: standard CLT applies for $|\\phi|&lt;1$ but breaks down at $\\phi=1$. Memory Tip: Asymptotic variance of $\\hat\\phi$ in AR(1): $V=1-\\phi^2$. Near zero ($\\phi=0$): $V=1$ (hardest to estimate?). Near unit root: $V\\to0$ (easy!). The Cramér–Rao bound tightens as $|\\phi|\\to1$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Spectral factorisation expresses the spectral density as $f(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}|g(e^{-i\\omega})|^2$ where $g(z)$ is a one-sided polynomial (or power series) with all roots outside the unit circle. This factorisation is used to:',opts:['A) Obtain the minimum-phase representation and the innovation variance from a given spectral density','B) Identify the trend component of a non-stationary series','C) Convert a non-stationary process to stationary by differencing','D) Estimate the spectral density from the periodogram'],ans:0,exp:'Every non-negative integrable spectral density $f(\\omega)$ can be written as $f(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}|g(e^{-i\\omega})|^2$ where $g(z)=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}g_j z^j$ with $g_0=1$ and all zeros of $g$ outside the unit circle (minimum-phase / causal and invertible). The coefficients $g_j$ are the Wold MA($\\infty$) coefficients ($g_j=\\psi_j$), and $\\sigma^2=\\exp\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\\ln(2\\pi f(\\omega))d\\omega\\right)$ (Kolmogorov formula for innovation variance). Applications: computing optimal linear predictors, deriving MA($\\infty$) representations, verifying a given spectrum is valid. Memory Tip: Spectral factorisation = “find the minimum-phase $g(z)$ such that $|g(e^{-i\\omega})|^2\\propto f(\\omega)$.” The Kolmogorov formula: $\\sigma^2=\\exp[\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int\\ln(2\\pi f(\\omega))d\\omega]$ gives the innovation variance from the spectral density – a beautiful result.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The integrated spectrum (spectral distribution function) $F(\\omega)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\omega}f(\\lambda)d\\lambda$ satisfies which relation with the ACVF?',opts:['A) $\\gamma(h)=F(\\pi h)-F(-\\pi h)$','B) $\\gamma(h)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}e^{i\\omega h}dF(\\omega)$ (Herglotz representation)','C) $F(\\omega)=\\gamma(\\omega/(2\\pi))$ for $\\omega\\in[-\\pi,\\pi]$','D) $F(\\pi)=0$ for zero-mean processes'],ans:1,exp:'For a process with absolutely continuous spectrum: $dF(\\omega)=f(\\omega)d\\omega$, so $\\gamma(h)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}e^{i\\omega h}f(\\omega)d\\omega$ (Wiener–Khinchin). The Herglotz theorem generalises this to all weakly stationary processes (including those with discrete spectral components like hidden periodicities): $$\\gamma(h)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}e^{i\\omega h}\\,dF(\\omega).$$ $F(\\pi)-F(-\\pi)=\\gamma(0)=$ total variance. For a deterministic sinusoid at frequency $\\omega_0$: $F$ has a jump of size $\\sigma^2/2$ at $\\pm\\omega_0$ (discrete spectral measure). This is the most general form of the spectral representation theorem – valid whether $f$ exists or not. Memory Tip: Herglotz: ACVF = Stieltjes integral of $e^{i\\omega h}$ with respect to spectral measure $dF$. If $f$ exists: replace $dF(\\omega)=f(\\omega)d\\omega$. If not (discrete spectrum): $dF$ has point masses. Most general spectral result.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a GARCH(1,1) model $\\sigma_t^2=\\omega+\\alpha\\epsilon_{t-1}^2+\\beta\\sigma_{t-1}^2$ with $\\alpha+\\beta&lt;1$, the half-life of a volatility shock (time for the excess variance above its long-run level to halve) is approximately:',opts:['A) $\\ln(0.5)/\\ln(\\alpha+\\beta)$','B) $\\alpha+\\beta$','C) $1/(1-\\alpha-\\beta)$','D) $\\ln(0.5)/(\\alpha+\\beta)$'],ans:0,exp:'The conditional variance of GARCH(1,1) mean-reverts to $\\bar\\sigma^2=\\omega/(1-\\alpha-\\beta)$ at rate $(\\alpha+\\beta)$ per period: $$E_t[\\sigma_{t+h}^2]-\\bar\\sigma^2=(\\alpha+\\beta)^h(\\sigma_t^2-\\bar\\sigma^2).$$ The excess variance is multiplied by $(\\alpha+\\beta)^h$. Setting this to $0.5$: $$(\\alpha+\\beta)^{h_{1/2}}=0.5\\Rightarrow h_{1/2}=\\frac{\\ln(0.5)}{\\ln(\\alpha+\\beta)}.$$ For typical values $\\alpha=0.1$, $\\beta=0.85$: $\\alpha+\\beta=0.95$, $h_{1/2}=\\ln(0.5)/\\ln(0.95)\\approx13.5$ periods. This shows how persistent financial volatility is: a shock today affects variance estimates for weeks. Memory Tip: GARCH(1,1) half-life $=\\ln(0.5)/\\ln(\\alpha+\\beta)$: same formula as geometric decay. $\\alpha+\\beta=0.95\\Rightarrow\\approx14$ periods; $\\alpha+\\beta=0.99\\Rightarrow\\approx69$ periods. Near-IGARCH: extremely persistent volatility.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Engle–Granger two-step cointegration test for two I(1) series $X_t$ and $Y_t$ proceeds as:',opts:['A) Apply the KPSS test jointly to the vector $(X_t,Y_t)$','B) Step 1: Run OLS of $Y_t$ on $X_t$; save residuals $\\hat{e}_t$. Step 2: Apply the ADF test to $\\hat{e}_t$ using special (non-standard) critical values','C) Estimate a VAR and test Granger causality in both directions','D) Step 1: Test $X_t$ and $Y_t$ individually for unit roots. Step 2: Test their first differences for a common trend'],ans:1,exp:'Step 1: Regress $Y_t=\\alpha+\\beta X_t+e_t$ by OLS (“cointegrating regression” – valid despite non-stationarity because cointegrated series have $O(T)$ signal). Step 2: Apply ADF to $\\hat{e}_t=Y_t-\\hat\\alpha-\\hat\\beta X_t$. $H_0$: $\\hat{e}_t$ has unit root (no cointegration). $H_1$: $\\hat{e}_t$ is I(0) (cointegration). Critical values: The OLS step introduces estimation uncertainty, so standard ADF critical values are not valid. Engle–Granger critical values are more negative (harder to reject $H_0$), accounting for the pre-estimation. Limitation: Only tests for one cointegrating vector; for $k&gt;2$ variables, the Johansen trace/max-eigenvalue test handles multiple cointegrating vectors. Memory Tip: EG cointegration: OLS $\\to$ ADF on residuals. Special critical values (more negative than ADF). Significant (small $t$) $\\Rightarrow$ stationary residuals $\\Rightarrow$ cointegrated. For $k&gt;2$: use Johansen test instead.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Chow test for a structural break at known time $\\tau$ in a time series regression tests $H_0$: no break by:',opts:['A) Comparing AIC before and after $\\tau$','B) Testing whether the RSS from the pooled (full) regression equals the sum of RSSs from the two sub-periods, using an $F$-statistic with $k$ and $n-2k$ degrees of freedom','C) Applying an ADF test at the break point','D) Testing the significance of a dummy variable for the post-break period in the regression'],ans:1,exp:'For a regression with $k$ parameters: $F=\\dfrac{(RSS_{\\text{pooled}}-RSS_1-RSS_2)/k}{(RSS_1+RSS_2)/(n-2k)}\\sim F(k,n-2k)$ under $H_0$. Large $F\\Rightarrow$ reject $H_0\\Rightarrow$ structural break at $\\tau$. Requirement: Break point $\\tau$ must be known (pre-specified). For unknown break point: use the sup-Wald (Quandt) test or Andrews (1993) test, which computes the Chow $F$ for all possible $\\tau$ in a trimmed range and takes the supremum. Option (D) (dummy variable test) is equivalent to the Chow test when the break affects the intercept only; the full Chow test allows all parameters to change. Memory Tip: Chow test: “does splitting the sample at $\\tau$ significantly reduce RSS?” Known break: Chow $F$-test. Unknown break: Andrews sup-Wald test. The $F$ has numerator df $=k$ (one full set of parameters) and denominator df $=n-2k$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary ARMA($p,q$) process, the spectral density at frequency zero, $f(0)$, equals:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(0)/(2\\pi)$','B) $0$ for any stationary zero-mean process','C) $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}$ (same as white noise)','D) $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\left[\\dfrac{\\Theta(1)}{\\Phi(1)}\\right]^2=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\Psi(1)^2$'],ans:3,exp:'$f(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\frac{|\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}{|\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}$. At $\\omega=0$: $e^{-i\\omega}=1$, so $$f(0)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\left[\\frac{\\Theta(1)}{\\Phi(1)}\\right]^2.$$ Note: $2\\pi f(0)=\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)=\\sigma^2\\Psi(1)^2$ is the long-run variance, crucial for HAC (heteroscedasticity and autocorrelation consistent) standard errors in time series regression. For AR(1): $f(0)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi(1-\\phi)^2}$ – diverges as $\\phi\\to1$ (long-run variance is infinite for a unit root process). Option (A) is wrong: $f(0)=\\gamma(0)/(2\\pi)$ only for white noise ($\\gamma(h)=0$ for $h\\neq0$). Memory Tip: $2\\pi f(0)$ = long-run variance = $\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)$. For AR(1): $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi)^2$. Long-run variance $&gt;$ short-run variance (=$\\gamma(0)$) when correlations are positive. HAC estimators estimate $2\\pi f(0)$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A transfer function (Box–Jenkins dynamic regression) model $Y_t=\\frac{\\omega(B)}{\\delta(B)}X_{t-b}+N_t$ represents:',opts:['A) A model where $X_t$ is an instrument variable for $Y_t$','B) A static regression of $Y_t$ on current $X_t$ only','C) A distributed lag model where the input $X_t$ affects output $Y_t$ with delay $b$ through a rational transfer function, and $N_t$ is an ARMA noise term','D) A cointegration model between $X_t$ and $Y_t$'],ans:2,exp:'$Y_t=\\dfrac{\\omega_0-\\omega_1B-\\cdots-\\omega_sB^s}{1-\\delta_1B-\\cdots-\\delta_rB^r}X_{t-b}+N_t$ where $b\\geq0$ is the pure delay (dead time), $\\omega(B)/\\delta(B)$ is the rational transfer function (captures dynamic distributed lag effects), and $N_t\\sim\\mathrm{ARMA}(p,q)$. Identification of transfer function: cross-correlation function (CCF) between pre-whitened $X_t$ and pre-whitened $Y_t$ reveals the impulse response pattern. Special cases: $\\bullet$ $r=0, s=0$: static regression. $\\bullet$ $r=1, s=0$: geometric distributed lag (Koyck model). $\\bullet$ Box–Jenkins 1970: the full framework for model building with input series. Memory Tip: Transfer function = “ARMA model with an input series.” $b$ = delay. $\\omega/\\delta$ = how past inputs affect current output. $N_t$ = what remains after removing input effects. Pre-whiten $X_t$ first before estimating the transfer function.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The periodogram $I(\\omega)$ is asymptotically unbiased for $f(\\omega)$ at interior frequencies. However, for finite $n$, its expected value satisfies:',opts:['A) $E[I(\\omega)]=f(\\omega)/n$ (decreasing bias)','B) $E[I(\\omega)]=0$ for $\\omega\\neq0$','C) $E[I(\\omega)]=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}F_n(\\omega-\\lambda)f(\\lambda)d\\lambda$ where $F_n$ is the Fejér kernel','D) $E[I(\\omega)]=f(\\omega)$ exactly for all $n$'],ans:2,exp:'The finite-sample expected periodogram: $$E[I(\\omega)]=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}F_n(\\omega-\\lambda)f(\\lambda)\\frac{d\\lambda}{2\\pi},$$ where $F_n(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{n}\\left(\\frac{\\sin(n\\omega/2)}{\\sin(\\omega/2)}\\right)^2\\geq0$ is the Fejér kernel. As $n\\to\\infty$: $F_n(\\omega)\\to2\\pi\\delta(\\omega)$, so $E[I(\\omega)]\\to f(\\omega)$ – asymptotically unbiased. For finite $n$: the Fejér kernel has sidelobes that allow power from nearby frequencies to leak into the estimate at $\\omega$ – this is spectral leakage. Remedy: data tapering (windowing before computing the periodogram) reduces sidelobe leakage by shaping the data weights. Memory Tip: Periodogram bias source: Fejér kernel leakage – nearby frequencies bleed into the estimate. Data tapering reduces leakage. As $n\\to\\infty$, Fejér $\\to$ Dirac delta $\\Rightarrow$ asymptotically unbiased. Leakage is finite-sample bias, NOT inconsistency.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The McLeod–Li test for nonlinearity (or ARCH effects) in ARMA residuals $\\hat\\epsilon_t$ applies the Ljung–Box portmanteau statistic to:',opts:['A) The absolute residuals $|\\hat\\epsilon_t|$','B) The squared residuals $\\hat\\epsilon_t^2$','C) The raw residuals $\\hat\\epsilon_t$','D) The log-squared residuals $\\ln(\\hat\\epsilon_t^2)$'],ans:1,exp:'Standard Ljung–Box on $\\hat\\epsilon_t$: Tests for remaining autocorrelation (linear dependence) in the mean model residuals. McLeod–Li on $\\hat\\epsilon_t^2$: Tests for autocorrelation in the squared residuals, which detects nonlinear dependence and volatility clustering (ARCH/GARCH effects). If $Q_{LB}(\\hat\\epsilon_t)$ is non-significant but $Q_{LB}(\\hat\\epsilon_t^2)$ is significant: the mean model is adequate but the variance model is not – fit GARCH. The test statistic is $Q^*=n(n+2)\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\hat\\rho_{\\epsilon^2}^2(k)/(n-k)\\sim\\chi^2_m$ under $H_0$ of no ARCH effects. Interpretation: Autocorrelation in $\\hat\\epsilon_t^2$ but not in $\\hat\\epsilon_t$ is the classic signature of ARCH/GARCH. Memory Tip: McLeod–Li = “Ljung–Box for $\\hat\\epsilon^2$.” Significant $\\Rightarrow$ ARCH effects. Always check BOTH $\\hat\\epsilon_t$ and $\\hat\\epsilon_t^2$ in diagnostics. Linear mean model + nonlinear variance = ARMA-GARCH specification.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If a continuous-time process has a component at frequency $\\nu_0$ (cycles per second) and is sampled at $\\Delta t$ seconds per sample (sampling rate $1/\\Delta t$ Hz), aliasing occurs when:',opts:['A) $\\nu_0 &lt; 1/(2\\Delta t)$ (below Nyquist frequency)','B) $\\nu_0 = 0$ (the DC component)','C) $\\nu_0 &gt; 1/(2\\Delta t)$ (above Nyquist frequency)','D) $\\nu_0 = 1/\\Delta t$ (at the sampling frequency)'],ans:2,exp:'The Nyquist frequency $\\nu_N=1/(2\\Delta t)$ is the maximum detectable frequency given sampling interval $\\Delta t$. If $\\nu_0&gt;\\nu_N$: the high-frequency component “aliases” to $\\nu_0\\bmod(2\\nu_N)$ – it appears at a lower spurious frequency in the discrete-time spectrum. Example: $\\Delta t=1$ month ($\\nu_N=6$ cycles/year). A daily cycle ($\\nu_0=365$ cycles/year) aliases to $365\\bmod 12 = 5$ cycles/year in monthly data – a completely spurious business-cycle frequency! Prevention: Use an anti-aliasing filter (low-pass filter below $\\nu_N$) before sampling, or increase sampling rate. Memory Tip: Aliasing = “high frequency disguised as low frequency.” Nyquist: sample at $\\geq 2\\times$ highest frequency of interest. Monthly data: can\'t detect cycles shorter than 2 months. Anti-aliasing filter before downsampling is essential!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ARMA(1,1) process $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t+\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$, the MA($\\infty$) coefficient $\\psi_j$ (for $j\\geq 1$) in $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\epsilon_{t-j}$ is:',opts:['A) $\\psi_j=(\\phi+\\theta)\\phi^{j-1}$','B) $\\psi_j=\\phi^j+\\theta^j$','C) $\\psi_j=\\theta^j$','D) $\\psi_j=\\phi^j$'],ans:0,exp:'$X_t=\\Phi(B)^{-1}\\Theta(B)\\epsilon_t$. For $j\\geq1$: $\\psi_j$ satisfies $\\psi_j=\\phi\\psi_{j-1}+\\theta_{j}$ where $\\theta_j=\\theta\\mathbf{1}_{j=1}$ (since MA order is 1). So: $\\psi_1=\\phi\\psi_0+\\theta=\\phi+\\theta$. For $j\\geq2$: $\\psi_j=\\phi\\psi_{j-1}$ (no more MA terms). Hence $\\psi_j=\\phi^{j-1}\\psi_1=(\\phi+\\theta)\\phi^{j-1}$. Verification: Long-run multiplier $\\Psi(1)=1+\\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}(\\phi+\\theta)\\phi^{j-1}=1+(\\phi+\\theta)/(1-\\phi)=\\frac{1+\\theta}{1-\\phi}=\\frac{\\Theta(1)}{\\Phi(1)}$. ✓ Option (D) is the MA($\\infty$) for pure AR(1) (missing the $+\\theta$ contribution). Memory Tip: ARMA(1,1) MA($\\infty$) coefficients: $\\psi_0=1$; $\\psi_1=\\phi+\\theta$; $\\psi_j=(\\phi+\\theta)\\phi^{j-1}$ for $j\\geq2$. The jump from $\\psi_0$ to $\\psi_1$ reflects the MA part; the geometric decay from $\\psi_1$ onward reflects the AR part.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Henderson moving average (used in X-11/X-13 seasonal adjustment) is preferred over simple symmetric moving averages for trend estimation because it:',opts:['A) Uses only past observations (one-sided), making it suitable for real-time estimation','B) Is identical to the Hodrick–Prescott filter for all parameter choices','C) Reproduces polynomials of degree up to 3 (cubic) exactly and has a smooth frequency response with near-zero gain at high frequencies','D) Is equivalent to OLS regression on a cubic trend for any window length'],ans:2,exp:'The Henderson moving average minimises the sum of squares of third differences of the trend while reproducing polynomials of degree $\\leq3$. Why cubics? Economic trends often follow approximately cubic (S-shaped) paths over medium horizons. Frequency response: the Henderson filter passes low frequencies (trend) with near unit gain and suppresses high frequencies (noise, irregular component) smoothly – no abrupt cutoff or ripples. Available in 5, 7, 9, 13, 23-term versions; longer = smoother trend but less responsiveness to turning points. It is the core trend filter in the Census Bureau\'s X-13-ARIMA-SEATS program used for official seasonal adjustment worldwide. Memory Tip: Henderson = “best cubic-preserving trend filter.” Used in official seasonal adjustment (X-13). Symmetric (two-sided) so requires future data – not real-time. For endpoints, asymmetric versions are used. Minimises third-difference roughness.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In classical time series decomposition, the multiplicative model $X_t=T_t\\times S_t\\times I_t$ is preferred over the additive model $X_t=T_t+S_t+I_t$ when:',opts:['A) The seasonal amplitude grows proportionally with the trend level (seasonal swings are larger when the series is higher)','B) The seasonal variation is constant in absolute magnitude over time','C) The series has been log-transformed prior to decomposition','D) The series has a deterministic (not stochastic) trend'],ans:0,exp:'Additive: $X_t=T_t+S_t+I_t$ – seasonal swings have constant absolute size regardless of trend level. Suitable when the range of seasonal fluctuations is approximately constant over time. Multiplicative: $X_t=T_t\\times S_t\\times I_t$ – seasonal factors are proportions: $S_t\\approx1$ (or 100%) on average. Seasonal swings scale with the level $T_t$. Suitable when relative (percentage) seasonal variation is stable. Practical test: Plot the series; if the amplitude of seasonal fluctuations increases with the trend level $\\Rightarrow$ multiplicative. If constant $\\Rightarrow$ additive. Option (C): taking logs converts multiplicative to additive: $\\ln X_t=\\ln T_t+\\ln S_t+\\ln I_t$. Memory Tip: Multiplicative $\\Leftrightarrow$ seasonal swings grow with level. Log-transform multiplicative $\\Rightarrow$ additive. Most economic data is multiplicative (percent changes are stable, not absolute ones). Tourism, airline data: multiplicative.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a correctly specified stationary and invertible Gaussian ARMA($p,q$) model, the MLE $\\hat\\theta_n$ is:',opts:['A) Consistent, asymptotically normal, and asymptotically efficient (achieves the Cramér–Rao lower bound)','B) Consistent but not asymptotically normal','C) Biased in finite samples and inconsistent asymptotically','D) Asymptotically normal with variance greater than the CRLB'],ans:0,exp:'Under regularity conditions (stationarity, invertibility, correct specification): $$\\sqrt{n}(\\hat\\theta_n-\\theta_0)\\xrightarrow{d}N(0,\\mathcal{I}(\\theta_0)^{-1}),$$ where $\\mathcal{I}(\\theta_0)$ is the Fisher information matrix. MLE is BAN (Best Asymptotically Normal): Consistent: $\\hat\\theta_n\\xrightarrow{p}\\theta_0$. Asymptotically normal: limiting distribution is Gaussian. Efficient: variance achieves CRLB $=\\mathcal{I}^{-1}/n$. In finite samples, MLE may have small bias, but this vanishes as $n\\to\\infty$. The YW and CLS estimators are also consistent and asymptotically normal but less efficient than MLE in finite samples (though asymptotically equivalent). Memory Tip: Gaussian ARMA MLE: BAN = Best Asymptotically Normal. Consistent + AN + efficient. Small-sample: may be biased, but asymptotically unbiased. YW/CLS: consistent but less efficient for small $n$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The state-space (companion form) representation of an AR(2) process $X_t=\\phi_1X_{t-1}+\\phi_2X_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$ uses state vector $\\boldsymbol\\alpha_t=(X_t,X_{t-1})^\\top$ and has transition matrix:',opts:['A) $\\mathbf{T}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\phi_1&amp;0\\\\\\phi_2&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}$','B) $\\mathbf{T}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\phi_1&amp;\\phi_2\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}$','C) $\\mathbf{T}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\\\phi_2&amp;\\phi_1\\end{pmatrix}$','D) $\\mathbf{T}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\phi_1\\phi_2&amp;0\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}$'],ans:1,exp:'State transition: $\\boldsymbol\\alpha_t=\\mathbf{T}\\boldsymbol\\alpha_{t-1}+\\mathbf{R}\\epsilon_t$ where $\\boldsymbol\\alpha_t=(X_t,X_{t-1})^\\top$. $$\\begin{pmatrix}X_t\\\\X_{t-1}\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\phi_1&amp;\\phi_2\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}X_{t-1}\\\\X_{t-2}\\end{pmatrix}+\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}\\epsilon_t.$$ Observation: $X_t=\\mathbf{Z}^\\top\\boldsymbol\\alpha_t$ with $\\mathbf{Z}=(1,0)^\\top$. Key: The eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{T}$ are the reciprocals of the AR polynomial roots. Stationarity $\\Leftrightarrow$ all eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{T}$ inside the unit circle $\\Leftrightarrow$ all roots of $\\Phi(z)$ outside unit circle. For AR($p$): the companion matrix is $p\\times p$, with $(\\phi_1,\\ldots,\\phi_p)$ in first row and identity submatrix below. Memory Tip: Companion form: first row = $(\\phi_1,\\phi_2,\\ldots,\\phi_p)$; remaining rows shift the state (identity below). Eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{T}$ = reciprocals of AR roots. Stationarity: all eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{T}$ have $|\\lambda_i|&lt;1$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For monthly data with deterministic seasonality, the dummy variable approach includes 11 (not 12) seasonal dummies. The reason is:',opts:['A) Including all 12 dummies creates perfect multicollinearity (the sum of all 12 dummies equals the constant term), so one must be dropped as a reference category','B) The 12th dummy is redundant because December is always the reference month','C) Including 12 dummies over-differencing the series','D) The 12th seasonal effect is always zero by construction'],ans:0,exp:'Let $D_{it}=1$ if month $i$, 0 otherwise, for $i=1,\\ldots,12$. Then $\\sum_{i=1}^{12}D_{it}=1$ for all $t$ – exactly equal to the constant (intercept) column. Including all 12 plus an intercept $\\Rightarrow$ dummy variable trap: perfect multicollinearity, $(X^\\top X)$ is singular, OLS fails. Solution: Drop one dummy (the reference category, e.g. January). The other 11 dummies measure the deviation of each month from January. Alternative: Drop the intercept and include all 12 dummies – then each coefficient is the mean for that month directly. Both approaches are valid. Option (B) is misleading: any month can serve as reference, not just December. Memory Tip: Seasonal dummies: include $s-1$ (not $s$) with intercept OR include $s$ without intercept. Dummy trap: sum of all dummies = constant column = perfect collinearity. Reference category: its effect is embedded in the intercept.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary and invertible MA(2) process, the PACF $\\phi_{hh}$ for $h\\geq3$ behaves as:',opts:['A) $\\phi_{hh}=0$ exactly for all $h\\geq3$ (cutoff at lag 2)','B) $\\phi_{hh}$ tails off, dominated by the largest root of the MA polynomial','C) $\\phi_{hh}=\\theta_2^h$ for $h\\geq2$','D) $\\phi_{hh}$ alternates in sign with constant magnitude'],ans:1,exp:'For any MA($q$) process, the PACF never cuts off – it tails off, reflecting the infinite AR representation of an invertible MA process. The tail behaviour of $\\phi_{hh}$ as $h\\to\\infty$ is governed by the reciprocals of the MA polynomial roots: if the MA roots are $z_1,z_2$ with $|z_1|\\leq|z_2|$, then $\\phi_{hh}\\sim c/z_1^h$ for large $h$ (dominated by the root closest to the unit circle). For real positive MA roots: PACF is all positive. For real negative or complex roots: PACF alternates or oscillates. Contrast with ACF: MA(2) ACF cuts off at lag 2. PACF tails off. This is the Box–Jenkins identification rule for MA processes. Memory Tip: MA($q$) PACF: tails off (governed by MA roots). MA($q$) ACF: cuts off at lag $q$. AR($p$) PACF: cuts off at lag $p$. AR($p$) ACF: tails off. ARMA: both tail off. The ACF/PACF table is the core of Box–Jenkins identification.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In spectral estimation, the trade-off between spectral leakage and spectral resolution is:',opts:['A) The Bartlett window has the highest sidelobes of all standard windows','B) Wider bandwidth $\\Rightarrow$ more leakage but better resolution','C) A wider main lobe of the spectral window $\\Rightarrow$ poorer frequency resolution; larger sidelobes $\\Rightarrow$ more leakage from distant frequencies','D) Leakage and resolution improve simultaneously with more data'],ans:2,exp:'Every spectral window (data taper) has two competing features: $\\bullet$ Main lobe width: determines frequency resolution – how well two nearby peaks can be distinguished. Wider main lobe $=$ poorer resolution. $\\bullet$ Sidelobe height: determines leakage – contamination from power at distant frequencies. Larger sidelobes $=$ more leakage. Reducing sidelobes (for less leakage) inevitably widens the main lobe (poorer resolution) – fundamental uncertainty principle. Window comparison: Window | Sidelobes | Main lobe; Rectangular | Largest ($-13$dB) | Narrowest; Hanning/Bartlett | Smaller | Wider; Blackman | Very small | Widest Memory Tip: Spectral uncertainty principle: narrow main lobe (good resolution) $\\Leftrightarrow$ large sidelobes (bad leakage). Rectangular window: best resolution, worst leakage. Blackman: worst resolution, best leakage suppression. Choose based on your priority.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The SARIMA$(0,1,1)(0,1,1)_{12}$ model (the “airline model”) can be written in backshift operator notation as:',opts:['A) $(1-B)^{12}X_t=(1+\\theta B^{12})\\epsilon_t$','B) $(1-B^{12})X_t=(1+\\theta B)(1+\\Theta B^{12})\\epsilon_t$','C) $(1-B)(1-B^{12})X_t=(1-\\theta B)(1-\\Theta B^{12})\\epsilon_t$','D) $(1-B)(1+B^{12})X_t=(1+\\theta B)(1+\\Theta B^{12})\\epsilon_t$'],ans:2,exp:'The SARIMA$(p,d,q)(P,D,Q)_s$ general form: $$\\Phi(B^s)\\phi(B)(1-B)^d(1-B^s)^D X_t=\\Theta(B^s)\\theta(B)\\epsilon_t.$$ For the airline model ($p=d=q=0+1+1$, $P=D=Q=0+1+1$, $s=12$): Non-seasonal: $d=1$, $q=1$ $\\Rightarrow$ $(1-B)(1+\\theta B)$ ... but MA sign convention in Box–Jenkins uses $(1-\\theta B)$. Seasonal: $D=1$, $Q=1$ $\\Rightarrow$ $(1-B^{12})(1-\\Theta B^{12})$. So: $(1-B)(1-B^{12})X_t=(1-\\theta B)(1-\\Theta B^{12})\\epsilon_t$. This is one of the most widely studied time series models – Box &amp; Jenkins (1976) fitted it to international airline passenger data. Both $\\theta$ and $\\Theta$ are typically in $(0,1)$. Option (B) misses the regular differencing $(1-B)$. Option (D) uses the wrong sign for the seasonal operator. Memory Tip: Airline model $=(1-B)(1-B^{12})X_t=(1-\\theta B)(1-\\Theta B^{12})\\epsilon_t$. Two differencing operators (regular + seasonal) on the left; two MA operators on the right. This model is the benchmark for monthly economic/transport data.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A weak white noise (WN) sequence $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$ satisfies $E[\\epsilon_t]=0$, $\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t)=\\sigma^2$, and $\\mathrm{Cov}(\\epsilon_t,\\epsilon_s)=0$ for $t\\neq s$. Which additional condition distinguishes strong (i.i.d.) white noise from weak white noise?',opts:['A) Strong WN requires $E[\\epsilon_t^4]&lt;\\infty$','B) Strong WN requires the $\\epsilon_t$ to be mutually independent (not just uncorrelated), implying $E[g(\\epsilon_t)h(\\epsilon_s)]=E[g(\\epsilon_t)]E[h(\\epsilon_s)]$ for all measurable $g,h$ and $t\\neq s$','C) Strong WN requires $\\epsilon_t\\sim N(0,\\sigma^2)$','D) Strong WN requires $E[\\epsilon_t^2]=\\sigma^2$ (finite variance)'],ans:1,exp:'Type | Condition | Implication; Weak WN | Zero mean, finite variance, zero ACVF | Uncorrelated; Martingale diff. | $E[\\epsilon_t|\\mathcal{F}_{t-1}]=0$ | Uncorrelated; non-linear dep. allowed; Strong WN | $\\epsilon_t$ i.i.d.$(0,\\sigma^2)$ | Independent; no dep. of any kind; Gaussian WN | i.i.d. $N(0,\\sigma^2)$ | Special case of strong WN Independence (strong WN) implies zero correlation, but NOT vice versa. A GARCH process has weak WN innovations (zero autocorrelation) but strong dependence in squared values – it is not strong WN. Option (C): Gaussian implies independence (for uncorrelated Gaussian), but strong WN does not require Gaussianity – any i.i.d. sequence with zero mean and finite variance qualifies. Memory Tip: Strong WN = i.i.d. Weak WN = uncorrelated. GARCH innovations: weak WN (uncorrelated) but NOT strong WN (dependent in variance). Independence $\\Rightarrow$ uncorrelated; uncorrelated $\\not\\Rightarrow$ independent (except Gaussian).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In a VAR model, the Forecast Error Variance Decomposition (FEVD) at horizon $h$ for variable $Y_i$ measures:',opts:['A) The covariance between $Y_i$ and $Y_j$ at lag $h$','B) The AIC reduction achieved by including variable $Y_j$ in the VAR','C) The total variance of $Y_i$ explained by all $h$ lags','D) The fraction of $Y_i$\'s $h$-step forecast error variance attributable to shocks in each variable $Y_j$'],ans:3,exp:'The $h$-step forecast error for $Y_{it}$ in a VAR can be written as a linear combination of structural shocks $u_{jt}$ (obtained via Cholesky or other identification). The FEVD gives: $$\\omega_{ij}(h)=\\frac{\\text{contribution of }u_j\\text{ to }\\mathrm{MSE}_i(h)}{\\mathrm{MSE}_i(h)}\\in[0,1],\\quad\\sum_j\\omega_{ij}(h)=1.$$ Interpretation: $\\bullet$ $\\omega_{ii}(h)=1$ at $h=0$: own shocks explain everything at horizon 0. $\\bullet$ As $h$ increases: shocks from other variables contribute more. $\\bullet$ If $\\omega_{ij}(h)\\approx0$ for all $h$: variable $j$ has no predictive content for $Y_i$. Caveat: FEVD depends on the identification (ordering) in Cholesky decomposition. Memory Tip: FEVD = “how much of $Y_i$\'s forecast uncertainty comes from shock $j$?” At $h=0$: 100% own shock. As $h\\to\\infty$: converges to fixed fractions. Large $\\omega_{ij}(\\infty)$ $\\Rightarrow$ $Y_j$ is important for $Y_i$\'s long-run dynamics.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Newey–West (1987) HAC (heteroscedasticity and autocorrelation consistent) long-run variance estimator $\\hat{S}$ is:',opts:['A) $\\hat{S}=\\hat\\gamma(0)$ (just the sample variance)','B) $\\hat{S}=\\hat\\gamma(0)/\\sqrt{n}$','C) $\\hat{S}=2\\pi\\hat{f}(\\pi)$ (spectral density at the Nyquist frequency)','D) $\\hat{S}=\\sum_{h=-(L)}^{L}w_h\\hat\\gamma(h)$ where $w_h=1-|h|/(L+1)$ (Bartlett weights) and $L=O(n^{1/3})$'],ans:3,exp:'The long-run variance $S=\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)=2\\pi f(0)$ is needed for valid inference in time series regression when errors are autocorrelated. Newey–West estimate: $\\hat{S}=\\hat\\gamma(0)+2\\sum_{h=1}^{L}w_h\\hat\\gamma(h)$, $w_h=1-h/(L+1)$ (Bartlett weights). Why Bartlett? Ensures $\\hat{S}\\geq0$ (positive semi-definite). Rectangular weights ($w_h=1$) can give negative $\\hat{S}$. Bandwidth $L$: Optimal $L=O(n^{1/3})$ (Andrews 1991, data-dependent). Larger $L$: more autocovariances included but more noise. The trade-off parallels spectral estimation. Using $\\hat{S}$ instead of $\\hat\\gamma(0)$ in the denominator of $t$-statistics gives heteroscedasticity and autocorrelation robust (HAC) inference. Memory Tip: Newey–West HAC = Bartlett-weighted sample ACVF. $L\\approx n^{1/3}$ bandwidth. Guarantees $\\hat{S}&gt;0$. Note: $\\hat{S}=2\\pi\\hat{f}(0)$ – HAC standard errors estimate the spectral density at zero frequency!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'When applying the Ljung–Box portmanteau test to ARMA($p,q$) residuals, a recommended choice for the number of lags $m$ tested is:',opts:['A) $m=10$ or $m=20$ for non-seasonal data; $m=2s$ (twice the seasonal period) for seasonal data','B) $m=1$ (only the first autocorrelation matters)','C) $m=p+q$ (just enough to match the model order)','D) $m=n$ (use all available lags for maximum power)'],ans:0,exp:'Too small $m$: miss autocorrelations at higher lags – low power against slowly decaying alternatives. Too large $m$: degrees of freedom increase, chi-squared critical value grows, power decreases – test becomes too conservative. Standard practice (Hyndman &amp; Athanasopoulos, Brockwell &amp; Davis): $\\bullet$ Non-seasonal: $m=\\min(10, n/5)$ for small $n$; $m=20$ for large $n$. $\\bullet$ Seasonal (period $s$): $m=2s$ to capture up to two seasonal cycles. Option (C) is too conservative – using only $m=p+q$ lags completely neglects structure at higher lags. After fitting ARMA($p,q$), degrees of freedom for Ljung–Box are $m-p-q$; using $m=p+q$ gives $0$ df, making the test undefined. Memory Tip: Ljung–Box $m$: use $m=10$ or $20$ for non-seasonal; $2s$ for seasonal. After fitting ARMA($p,q$): df $=m-p-q$ (must be positive!). Never use $m\\leq p+q$. Check at multiple $m$ values for robustness.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An ARMA($p,q$) process $\\Phi(B)X_t=\\Theta(B)\\epsilon_t$ has a causal MA($\\infty$) representation $X_t=\\Psi(B)\\epsilon_t$ (one-sided, depending only on current and past innovations) if and only if:',opts:['A) $|\\phi_1|+|\\phi_2|+\\cdots+|\\phi_p|&lt;1$','B) All roots of $\\Theta(z)$ lie outside the unit circle','C) All roots of $\\Phi(z)$ lie outside the unit circle (stationarity condition)','D) All roots of both $\\Phi(z)$ and $\\Theta(z)$ lie on the unit circle'],ans:2,exp:'Causal (future-independent) MA($\\infty$) representation: $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\epsilon_{t-j}$ exists with $\\sum|\\psi_j|&lt;\\infty$ if and only if all roots of $\\Phi(z)$ lie outside the unit circle – this is precisely the stationarity condition. The expansion $\\Psi(z)=\\Theta(z)/\\Phi(z)$ converges on $|z|\\leq1$ when $\\Phi(z)\\neq0$ for $|z|\\leq1$. Invertible AR($\\infty$) representation: $\\Pi(B)X_t=\\epsilon_t$ with $\\Pi(z)=\\Phi(z)/\\Theta(z)$ converges when all roots of $\\Theta(z)$ lie outside the unit circle – this is invertibility. Option (B): roots of $\\Theta(z)$ outside = invertibility, not causality. These are separate conditions! Option (A) is sufficient but not necessary (too restrictive). Memory Tip: Causal = stationary = AR roots outside unit circle $\\Rightarrow$ MA($\\infty$) in terms of past innovations only. Invertible = MA roots outside unit circle $\\Rightarrow$ AR($\\infty$) in terms of past observations. Two separate conditions, two separate representations!'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'After fitting an ARIMA model, a complete residual diagnostic check involves which of the following steps? (I) Ljung–Box test on $\\hat\\epsilon_t$ for remaining autocorrelation. (II) McLeod–Li test on $\\hat\\epsilon_t^2$ for ARCH effects. (III) Normality test (Jarque–Bera or Q–Q plot) on $\\hat\\epsilon_t$. (IV) Plot of $\\hat\\epsilon_t$ vs $t$ to detect outliers or structural breaks.',opts:['A) (I) and (II) only','B) (I), (II) and (III) only','C) All four: (I), (II), (III) and (IV)','D) Only (I) is required'],ans:2,exp:'(I) Ljung–Box on $\\hat\\epsilon_t$: tests for remaining linear autocorrelation – if significant, mean model is inadequate (missed AR or MA terms). (II) McLeod–Li on $\\hat\\epsilon_t^2$: tests for nonlinear (ARCH/GARCH) dependence in variance – if significant, consider ARMA-GARCH model. (III) Normality check: ARIMA inference (prediction intervals, standard errors) assumes Gaussian errors. Non-normality suggests heavy tails, skewness, or outliers. Jarque–Bera tests skewness and excess kurtosis jointly. (IV) Time plot: visual inspection identifies outliers, level shifts, remaining trends, or structural breaks not captured by the model – motivates intervention analysis or model revision. All four together form the Box–Jenkins diagnostic stage. Passing (I) alone is insufficient – a model may have white noise residuals but with volatility clustering and non-normality. Memory Tip: Complete diagnostics = 4 checks: (1) LB on $\\hat\\epsilon$ (linear), (2) McLeod–Li on $\\hat\\epsilon^2$ (nonlinear), (3) Normality (distributional), (4) Time plot (visual). A model passes only if ALL four are satisfactory.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) and Mean Absolute Error (MAE) are two common forecast accuracy measures. Which statement correctly distinguishes them?',opts:['A) MAE and RMSE are identical when the distribution of forecast errors is symmetric','B) RMSE is always smaller than MAE for any set of forecast errors','C) RMSE penalises large errors more heavily than MAE (squaring amplifies large errors); RMSE $\\geq$ MAE always, with equality only when all errors are equal in magnitude','D) MAE is more sensitive to large outlier errors than RMSE because absolute errors grow faster than squared errors'],ans:2,exp:'For forecast errors $e_1,\\ldots,e_n$: $$\\mathrm{MAE}=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum|e_t|,\\qquad\\mathrm{RMSE}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}\\sum e_t^2}.$$ By the RMS–AM inequality (Jensen\'s inequality applied to convex $x^2$): $\\mathrm{RMSE}\\geq\\mathrm{MAE}$, with equality iff all $|e_t|$ are equal. Sensitivity to outliers: $\\bullet$ RMSE: squaring amplifies large errors disproportionately – one very large error raises RMSE dramatically. $\\bullet$ MAE: linear in $|e_t|$ – more robust to outliers. Optimal point forecast: RMSE-minimising forecast $=E[X_{t+h}|\\mathcal{F}_t]$ (conditional mean). MAE-minimising forecast $=$ conditional median. They agree only for symmetric, unimodal distributions. Memory Tip: RMSE $\\geq$ MAE (always). RMSE: sensitive to outliers (squaring). MAE: robust. RMSE minimised by conditional mean. MAE minimised by conditional median. RMSE is the standard in time series competitions; MAE is better for robust evaluation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(1) process $y_t=0.5y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$, with $E(\\epsilon_t)=0,\\ E(\\epsilon_t^2)=12$, the stationary variance of $y_t$ is:',opts:['A) $24$','B) $12$','C) $9$','D) $16$'],ans:3,exp:'For a stationary AR(1), $y_t=\\phi y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$, $|\\phi|&lt;1$: $$\\gamma(0)=\\mathrm{Var}(y_t)=\\frac{\\sigma^2_\\epsilon}{1-\\phi^2}.$$ Here $\\phi=0.5,\\ \\sigma^2_\\epsilon=12$: $\\gamma(0)=\\dfrac{12}{1-0.25}=\\dfrac{12}{0.75}=16$. Option (B) omits the denominator entirely (treats variance as equal to $\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$). Option (A) uses the wrong denominator $(1-\\phi)=0.5$ instead of $(1-\\phi^2)=0.75$. Option (C) mistakenly multiplies $\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$ by $(1-\\phi^2)$ instead of dividing. Exam Tip: This exact calculation type (given $\\phi$ and $\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$, find $\\gamma(0)$ for AR(1)) was directly tested in the recent UGC NET Statistics paper – always divide by $(1-\\phi^2)$, never $(1-\\phi)$. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Let $y_t=\\delta+y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t,\\ t=1,2,\\ldots$, with $y_0$ fixed and $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$ purely random, $E(\\epsilon_t)=0,\\ E(\\epsilon_t^2)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$. Consider: (I) $E(y_t)=y_0+t\\delta$. (II) $\\mathrm{Var}(y_t)=t\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$. (III) $\\{y_t\\}$ is non-stationary regardless of the value of $\\delta$. (IV) $\\Delta y_t=\\delta+\\epsilon_t$ is stationary. Which of the above statements are correct?',opts:['A) (I) and (II) only','B) All four: (I), (II), (III) and (IV)','C) (II) and (IV) only','D) (I), (II) and (III) only'],ans:1,exp:'Iterating: $y_t=y_0+t\\delta+\\sum_{s=1}^{t}\\epsilon_s$. (I) $E(y_t)=y_0+t\\delta$ – the drift accumulates linearly in $t$. True. (II) Since $\\epsilon_s$ are uncorrelated, $\\mathrm{Var}(y_t)=t\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$. True. (III) Both mean (if $\\delta\\neq0$) and variance depend on $t$ – the variance alone growing unboundedly with $t$ is sufficient for non-stationarity, so this holds even when $\\delta=0$. True. (IV) $\\Delta y_t=y_t-y_{t-1}=\\delta+\\epsilon_t$ has constant mean $\\delta$, constant variance $\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$, and zero autocovariance at all lags $\\ge1$ – stationary (indeed, iid apart from the constant shift). True. Exam Tip: Random walk (with or without drift) is always I(1): first differencing always restores stationarity. This mirrors the recent UGC NET item on random-walk mean/variance – but adds the differencing statement, a favourite follow-up trap. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider $y_t=0.7y_{t-1}+0.3y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$, where $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$ is purely random with $E(\\epsilon_t)=0,\\ E(\\epsilon_t^2)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$ for all $t$. The ACF of $\\{\\Delta y_t\\}$, say $r_k$, is:',opts:['A) $r_k=(-0.3)^k$','B) $r_k=(0.3)^k$','C) $r_k=(0.7)^k$','D) $r_k=(-0.7)^k$'],ans:0,exp:'Since $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=0.7+0.3=1$, the AR(2) has a unit root ($z=1$ solves $1-0.7z-0.3z^2=0$), so it is non-stationary and must be differenced. Subtract $y_{t-1}$ from both sides: $$\\Delta y_t=y_t-y_{t-1}=(\\Phi_1-1)y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t.$$ Since $\\Phi_1-1=-\\Phi_2$ (because $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=1$): $$\\Delta y_t=-\\Phi_2(y_{t-1}-y_{t-2})+\\epsilon_t=-\\Phi_2\\Delta y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t=-0.3\\,\\Delta y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t.$$ This is AR(1) in $\\Delta y_t$ with coefficient $-0.3$, so its ACF is $r_k=(-0.3)^k$, $k=0,1,2,\\ldots$ Shortcut: Whenever $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=1$ for an AR(2), the unit-root factor cancels and $\\Delta y_t$ reduces to AR(1) with coefficient exactly $-\\Phi_2$. No need to solve the full characteristic equation. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is the classical (non-parametric) empirical estimator of the spectral density function of a stationary time series, obtained from the squared modulus of the discrete Fourier transform of the data?',opts:['A) Periodogram','B) Yule–Walker equations','C) Autocorrelation function (ACF)','D) Partial autocorrelation function (PACF)'],ans:0,exp:'The periodogram, $I(\\omega_j)=\\dfrac{1}{n}\\left|\\sum_{t=1}^{n}y_t e^{-i2\\pi\\omega_j t}\\right|^2$, is the classical empirical estimator of the spectral density $f(\\omega)$ – it is built directly from the Fourier transform of the observed data (equivalently, from the sample ACVF via the Wiener–Khinchin relation). The ACF and PACF (C, D) are time-domain tools used for AR/MA/ARMA identification, not spectral estimators. Yule–Walker equations (B) estimate AR parameters from sample autocorrelations – a time-domain estimation method, not a spectral one (though a fitted AR spectral density can be obtained afterward). Exam Tip: “Estimator of the spectral density” $\\Rightarrow$ periodogram, almost by definition, in the frequency domain – a direct-recall item that appeared in the recent UGC NET Statistics paper in this exact phrasing. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For estimating the AR(2) process $y_t=\\Phi_1y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$, the Yule–Walker equations are used. If the sample autocorrelations are $r_1=0.6,\\ r_2=0.4$, then the estimate of $\\Phi_2$ is:',opts:['A) $-9/16$','B) $1/16$','C) $-1/16$','D) $9/16$'],ans:1,exp:'The Yule–Walker equations for AR(2) are $r_1=\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2r_1$ and $r_2=\\Phi_1r_1+\\Phi_2$. From the first: $\\Phi_1=r_1-\\Phi_2r_1=0.6-0.6\\Phi_2$. Substitute into the second: $0.4=(0.6-0.6\\Phi_2)(0.6)+\\Phi_2=0.36-0.36\\Phi_2+\\Phi_2=0.36+0.64\\Phi_2$. So $\\Phi_2=\\dfrac{0.4-0.36}{0.64}=\\dfrac{0.04}{0.64}=\\dfrac{1}{16}=0.0625$. (As a cross-check, $\\hat\\Phi_1=0.6-0.6(0.0625)=0.5625=9/16$ – option (D) is the classic trap of reporting $\\hat\\Phi_1$ instead of the asked-for $\\hat\\Phi_2$.) Exam Trick: This is the identical numerical skill (find $\\Phi_2$ from $r_1,r_2$ via Yule–Walker) that appeared in the recent UGC NET Statistics paper, just with different sample autocorrelations – always double-check which parameter the question is actually asking for. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider $y_t=\\Phi_1y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t-\\theta_1\\epsilon_{t-1}-\\theta_2\\epsilon_{t-2}$, a purely random $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$, with $\\Phi_1=0.5,\\ \\Phi_2=0.2,\\ \\theta_1=0.9,\\ \\theta_2=0.3$. The process is:',opts:['A) Neither stationary nor invertible','B) Both stationary and invertible','C) Invertible but not stationary','D) Stationary but not invertible'],ans:3,exp:'AR (stationarity) check: $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=0.5+0.2=0.7&lt;1$; $\\Phi_2-\\Phi_1=-0.3\\in(-1,1)$; $|\\Phi_2|=0.2&lt;1$ – all three triangle conditions hold comfortably. Stationary. ✓ MA (invertibility) check: the MA polynomial $1-\\theta_1z-\\theta_2z^2$ requires the same triangle conditions in $\\theta_1,\\theta_2$: $\\theta_1+\\theta_2&lt;1$, $\\theta_2-\\theta_1&lt;1$, $|\\theta_2|&lt;1$. Here $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=0.9+0.3=1.2\\not&lt;1$ – this condition fails. The process is not invertible. $\\times$ Exam Tip: Always check the AR and MA triangle conditions separately – a process can be comfortably stationary while its MA part fails invertibility (or vice versa). This mirrors the recent UGC NET comprehension passage on ARMA(2,2) stationarity/invertibility, but with an unambiguous (non-boundary) violation. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Let $y_t=\\epsilon_t-0.5\\epsilon_{t-1}$, where $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$ is purely random with $E(\\epsilon_t)=0,\\ E(\\epsilon_t^2)=2$. The spectral density function of $\\{y_t\\}$, $f(\\omega)=\\gamma(0)+2\\gamma(1)\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)$, is:',opts:['A) $2.5+2\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)$','B) $[2.5-2\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)]^{-1}$','C) $2-\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)$','D) $2.5-2\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)$'],ans:3,exp:'For $y_t=\\epsilon_t-\\theta\\epsilon_{t-1}$ with $\\theta=0.5,\\ \\sigma^2_\\epsilon=2$: $$\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon(1+\\theta^2)=2(1+0.25)=2.5,\\qquad\\gamma(1)=-\\sigma^2_\\epsilon\\theta=-2(0.5)=-1,\\qquad\\gamma(h)=0,\\ h\\ge2.$$ So $f(\\omega)=\\gamma(0)+2\\gamma(1)\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)=2.5+2(-1)\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)=2.5-2\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)$. Option (A) is the sign-error trap on $\\gamma(1)$. Option (C) incorrectly omits the $\\theta^2$ term in $\\gamma(0)$ (uses $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon=2$ and $\\gamma(1)=-\\sigma^2_\\epsilon\\theta/2$). Option (B) is the classic “inverted spectral density” trap – confusing this additive form with the AR spectral density, which involves a reciprocal. Memory Tip: MA spectral density is a finite polynomial in $\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)$ (numerator only); AR spectral density involves a reciprocal (denominator). Don\'t mix the two forms – this exact confusion is a favourite distractor, echoed by the recent UGC NET item on MA(1) spectral density. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Let $\\Phi_1=0.9,\\ \\Phi_2=0.1,\\ \\theta_1=0.4,\\ \\theta_2=0$ in $y_t=\\Phi_1y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t-\\theta_1\\epsilon_{t-1}-\\theta_2\\epsilon_{t-2}$. The process is a:',opts:['A) ARIMA(2,1,0) process','B) ARMA(2,1) process','C) ARIMA(0,1,1) process','D) ARIMA(1,1,1) process'],ans:3,exp:'$\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=0.9+0.1=1$ exactly, so the AR characteristic equation $1-0.9z-0.1z^2=0$ has a root at $z=1$ (solving directly: $0.1z^2+0.9z-1=0\\Rightarrow(z-1)(0.1z+1)=0\\Rightarrow z=1$ or $z=-10$) – a genuine unit root, since the other root $z=-10$ lies safely outside the unit circle. The process is therefore non-stationary and needs exactly one differencing. Removing the unit root leaves an AR(1) for $\\Delta y_t$ (coefficient $-\\Phi_2=-0.1$), and the MA part is order 1 ($\\theta_1=0.4,\\theta_2=0$). Hence the model is ARIMA(1,1,1) – not a “plain” stationary ARMA(2,1) as option (B) would suggest. Exam Trick: Before naming any ARMA($p,q$) model, always check $\\Phi_1+\\cdots+\\Phi_p=1$ (unit root test on the AR polynomial at $z=1$). If it holds, the model is really ARIMA($p{-}1,1,q$), not ARMA($p,q$) – exactly the trap tested in the recent UGC NET comprehension passage on this model. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For estimating the AR(2) process $y_t=\\Phi_1y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$, the Yule–Walker equations are used. If the sample autocorrelations are $r_1=0.6,\\ r_2=0.2$, then the estimate of $\\Phi_1$ is:',opts:['A) $-1/4$','B) $3/4$','C) $-3/4$','D) $1/4$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\Phi_1=\\dfrac{r_1(1-r_2)}{1-r_1^2}=\\dfrac{0.6\\times0.8}{1-0.36}=\\dfrac{0.48}{0.64}=0.75=\\dfrac{3}{4}$. Cross-check: $\\Phi_2=\\dfrac{r_2-r_1^2}{1-r_1^2}=\\dfrac{0.2-0.36}{0.64}=\\dfrac{-0.16}{0.64}=-0.25=-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ – option (A) is the trap of reporting $\\hat\\Phi_2$ instead of the asked-for $\\hat\\Phi_1$. Stationarity check: $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=0.5&lt;1$; $\\Phi_2-\\Phi_1=-1&lt;1$; $|\\Phi_2|=0.25&lt;1$ – valid. Exam Trick: Compute both $\\hat\\Phi_1$ and $\\hat\\Phi_2$ every time – the question may ask for either one, and both formulas share the same denominator $1-r_1^2$. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(1) process $y_t=0.4y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$, with $E(\\epsilon_t)=0,\\ E(\\epsilon_t^2)=21$, the variance of $y_t$ is:',opts:['A) $35$','B) $17.64$','C) $25$','D) $21$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2_\\epsilon}{1-\\phi^2}=\\dfrac{21}{1-0.16}=\\dfrac{21}{0.84}=25$. Option (D) skips the denominator. Option (A) uses $(1-\\phi)=0.6$ instead of $(1-\\phi^2)=0.84$: $21/0.6=35$. Option (B) mistakenly multiplies instead of divides: $21\\times0.84=17.64$. Exam Tip: This is the same numeric skill (given $\\phi,\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$, find $\\gamma(0)$) that appeared directly in the recent UGC NET paper – always $(1-\\phi^2)$, never $(1-\\phi)$. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a random walk (no drift) $y_t=y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$, $y_0=0$, $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$ purely random with $\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$, the covariance $\\mathrm{Cov}(y_t,y_{t+k})$ for $k\\ge0$ is:',opts:['A) $k\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$','B) $(t+k)\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$','C) $t\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$','D) $\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$ (constant)'],ans:2,exp:'$y_t=\\sum_{i=1}^{t}\\epsilon_i$ and $y_{t+k}=\\sum_{i=1}^{t+k}\\epsilon_i$. Since $\\{\\epsilon_i\\}$ are uncorrelated: $$\\mathrm{Cov}(y_t,y_{t+k})=\\mathrm{Cov}\\!\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^t\\epsilon_i,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{t+k}\\epsilon_i\\right)=\\sum_{i=1}^{t}\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_i)=t\\sigma^2_\\epsilon,$$ because only the first $t$ terms overlap between the two sums. This is the direct proof that a random walk is non-stationary: the covariance structure depends on the actual time origin $t$, not just the lag $k$ – violating the definition of (weak) stationarity, which requires $\\mathrm{Cov}(y_t,y_{t+k})$ to depend on $k$ alone. Exam Tip: A stationary process has ACVF depending only on the lag $k$. Here it depends on $t$ as well – this is the cleanest textbook proof of non-stationarity for a random walk, complementing the variance-growth ($t\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$) fact tested in the recent UGC NET paper. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider $y_t=0.8y_{t-1}+0.2y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$, where $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$ is purely random with $E(\\epsilon_t)=0,\\ E(\\epsilon_t^2)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$. The ACF of $\\{\\Delta y_t\\}$, say $r_k$, is:',opts:['A) $r_k=(-0.8)^k$','B) $r_k=(-0.2)^k$','C) $r_k=(0.2)^k$','D) $r_k=(0.8)^k$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=0.8+0.2=1$ – a unit root at $z=1$. As before, $\\Delta y_t=-\\Phi_2\\Delta y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t=-0.2\\,\\Delta y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$, an AR(1) in $\\Delta y_t$ with coefficient $-\\Phi_2=-0.2$. Hence $r_k=(-0.2)^k$. Shortcut: Whenever $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=1$, the differenced series is AR(1) with coefficient $-\\Phi_2$ – no need to re-derive from scratch each time. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In terms of the sample autocovariances $\\hat\\gamma(k)$, the periodogram $I(\\omega)$ of a series of length $n$ can be written as:',opts:['A) $I(\\omega)=\\hat\\gamma(0)-2\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\hat\\gamma(k)\\cos(2\\pi\\omega k)$','B) $I(\\omega)=\\hat\\gamma(0)\\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}\\hat\\gamma(k)$','C) $I(\\omega)=\\hat\\gamma(0)+2\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\hat\\gamma(k)\\cos(2\\pi\\omega k)$','D) $I(\\omega)=\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\hat\\gamma(k)^2$'],ans:2,exp:'The periodogram is defined from the data as $I(\\omega)=\\frac1n\\left|\\sum_{t=1}^{n}y_te^{-i2\\pi\\omega t}\\right|^2$, and this squared-modulus form is algebraically identical to the (finite) Fourier cosine transform of the sample ACVF: $$I(\\omega)=\\hat\\gamma(0)+2\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\hat\\gamma(k)\\cos(2\\pi\\omega k).$$ This is the discrete-time analogue of the Wiener–Khinchin relation applied to sample quantities – it is precisely why the periodogram is regarded as the natural empirical estimator of the spectral density. Memory Tip: Periodogram = “Fourier transform of the data” = “Fourier cosine transform of the sample ACVF.” Both definitions are equivalent – know both forms, as exam questions may test either. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider $y_t=\\Phi_1y_{t-1}+\\Phi_2y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t-\\theta_1\\epsilon_{t-1}-\\theta_2\\epsilon_{t-2}$, with $\\Phi_1=1.1,\\ \\Phi_2=0.3,\\ \\theta_1=1.2,\\ \\theta_2=0.5$. The process is:',opts:['A) Stationary but not invertible','B) Both stationary and invertible','C) Neither stationary nor invertible','D) Invertible but not stationary'],ans:2,exp:'AR check: $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=1.1+0.3=1.4\\not&lt;1$ – fails immediately. Non-stationary. MA check: $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=1.2+0.5=1.7\\not&lt;1$ – fails immediately. Not invertible. Both the AR and MA parts violate the very first triangle condition, so no further checks are needed – the process fails on both counts. Exam Tip: If $\\sum\\Phi_i&gt;1$ or $\\sum\\theta_i&gt;1$, stop immediately – the corresponding property fails without checking the other two conditions. Contrast this clear-cut failure with a boundary case (sum exactly $=1$, as in the recent UGC NET comprehension passage), where the outcome is a genuine unit-root/boundary call rather than an outright violation. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Let $y_t=\\epsilon_t-0.3\\epsilon_{t-1}$, where $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$ is purely random with $E(\\epsilon_t)=0,\\ E(\\epsilon_t^2)=5$. The spectral density function $f(\\omega)=\\gamma(0)+2\\gamma(1)\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)$ of $\\{y_t\\}$ is:',opts:['A) $5.45+3\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)$','B) $[5.45-3\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)]^{-1}$','C) $5-1.5\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)$','D) $5.45-3\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon(1+\\theta^2)=5(1+0.09)=5.45$; $\\gamma(1)=-\\sigma^2_\\epsilon\\theta=-5(0.3)=-1.5$; $\\gamma(h)=0,\\ h\\ge2$. $f(\\omega)=5.45+2(-1.5)\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)=5.45-3\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)$. Option (A) is the sign trap on $\\gamma(1)$. Option (C) omits the $\\theta^2$ term in $\\gamma(0)$ and forgets to double $\\gamma(1)$. Option (B) wrongly inverts the expression, confusing the MA (numerator) form with the AR (denominator) form. Memory Tip: MA(1) spectral density: $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)$, $\\gamma(1)=-\\sigma^2\\theta$ – always a finite polynomial in $\\cos(2\\pi\\omega)$, never a reciprocal. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(3) process $y_t=0.5y_{t-1}+0.2y_{t-2}-0.1y_{t-3}+\\epsilon_t$, with $\\alpha(h)$ denoting the partial autocorrelation at lag $h$, which statement is correct?',opts:['A) $\\alpha(h)=0$ for all $h\\ge4$','B) $\\alpha(h)=0$ for all $h\\ge2$','C) $\\alpha(h)\\neq0$ for all $h\\ge1$ (never cuts off)','D) $\\alpha(h)=0$ for all $h\\ge3$'],ans:0,exp:'This is a pure AR(3) process ($p=3$). The defining Box–Jenkins identification property of an AR($p$) process is that its PACF cuts off after lag $p$: $\\alpha(1),\\alpha(2),\\alpha(3)$ are generally nonzero, but $\\alpha(h)=0$ for every $h\\ge p+1=4$. Options (B) and (D) understate the AR order (cutting off too early). Option (C) describes an MA or ARMA process, where PACF tails off rather than cutting off. Memory Tip: AR($p$) PACF cuts off at lag $p$, i.e. $\\alpha(h)=0$ for $h\\ge p+1$. Always add 1 to $p$ when stating the cutoff lag – a very common off-by-one exam trap. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider $y_t=2y_{t-1}-y_{t-2}+\\epsilon_t$, where $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$ is purely random. This process is best identified as:',opts:['A) An ARIMA(0,2,0) process','B) An ARIMA(2,0,0) process','C) An ARIMA(1,1,0) process','D) A stationary AR(2) process (both roots inside the unit circle)'],ans:0,exp:'The AR characteristic polynomial is $1-2z+z^2=(1-z)^2$ – both roots equal $z=1$, i.e. a repeated (double) unit root. Rearranging the model directly: $y_t-2y_{t-1}+y_{t-2}=\\epsilon_t\\ \\Leftrightarrow\\ (1-B)^2y_t=\\epsilon_t\\ \\Leftrightarrow\\ \\nabla^2y_t=\\epsilon_t$. The second difference is white noise, with no remaining AR or MA structure. This is precisely an ARIMA(0,2,0) process – the “second-order random walk.” Exam Trick: $\\Phi_1+\\Phi_2=1$ signals one unit root; check further – if the characteristic polynomial is a perfect square $(1-z)^2$, there are two unit roots and $d=2$, not $d=1$. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(1) process $y_t=\\phi y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$ with $\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t)=\\sigma^2$, the autocovariance function $\\gamma(h)$ for $h\\ge0$ is:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(h)=\\sigma^2\\phi^h$','B) $\\gamma(h)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2\\phi^h}{1-\\phi^2}$','C) $\\gamma(h)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{1-\\phi^{2h}}$','D) $\\gamma(h)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2(1-\\phi^{2h})}{1-\\phi^2}$'],ans:1,exp:'Since $\\rho(h)=\\phi^h$ for AR(1) and $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$: $$\\gamma(h)=\\rho(h)\\gamma(0)=\\phi^h\\cdot\\frac{\\sigma^2}{1-\\phi^2}=\\frac{\\sigma^2\\phi^h}{1-\\phi^2}.$$ Option (A) omits the $\\gamma(0)$ scaling (gives $\\rho(h)\\cdot\\sigma^2$, not $\\gamma(h)$). Options (D) and (C) are structurally unrelated forms (they resemble partial-sum or misapplied-formula expressions and do not reduce correctly at $h=0$). Memory Tip: $\\gamma(h)=\\rho(h)\\times\\gamma(0)$ always holds for any stationary process. For AR(1) specifically, $\\rho(h)=\\phi^h$, so $\\gamma(h)=\\phi^h\\gamma(0)$ – geometric decay in $h$, same base as the ACF. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the MA(2) process $y_t=\\epsilon_t-0.4\\epsilon_{t-1}+0.2\\epsilon_{t-2}$, where $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$ is purely random with unit variance, $\\rho(2)$ equals:',opts:['A) $5/6$','B) $1/5$','C) $-1/6$','D) $1/6$'],ans:3,exp:'Write $y_t=\\epsilon_t+a\\epsilon_{t-1}+b\\epsilon_{t-2}$ with $a=-0.4,\\ b=0.2,\\ \\sigma^2_\\epsilon=1$. $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon(1+a^2+b^2)=1+0.16+0.04=1.2$. $\\gamma(2)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon\\cdot b=0.2$ (only the $\\epsilon_{t-2}$ term overlaps at lag 2). $\\rho(2)=\\gamma(2)/\\gamma(0)=0.2/1.2=1/6$. Exam Trick: At the maximum lag $q$ of an MA($q$) process, $\\gamma(q)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon\\times$(coefficient of $\\epsilon_{t-q}$) – no cross terms to worry about, unlike $\\gamma(1)$ which involves a product of two coefficients. Option (B) is the trap of forgetting to divide by $\\gamma(0)$. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An ARMA(2,1) model is fitted to a series, and the Ljung–Box statistic is computed using the first $m=20$ sample autocorrelations of the residuals. The degrees of freedom for the reference $\\chi^2$ distribution is:',opts:['A) $18$','B) $17$','C) $23$','D) $20$'],ans:1,exp:'For residuals from a fitted ARMA($p,q$) model, the Ljung–Box statistic is compared against $\\chi^2_{m-p-q}$, subtracting the number of estimated ARMA coefficients from $m$. Here $m=20,\\ p=2,\\ q=1$: $\\mathrm{df}=20-2-1=17$. Option (D) forgets the adjustment entirely (correct only for raw, unfitted data). Option (C) mistakenly adds $p+q$ instead of subtracting. Memory Tip: Ljung–Box on fitted-model residuals: $\\mathrm{df}=m-p-q$. On raw data: $\\mathrm{df}=m$. Forgetting this adjustment is one of the most frequently tested exam traps in diagnostic checking. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Two candidate models fitted to the same series ($n=100$) give: Model A – $\\mathrm{AIC}=245.6,\\ \\mathrm{BIC}=252.3$; Model B – $\\mathrm{AIC}=248.1,\\ \\mathrm{BIC}=251.0$. Which is correct?',opts:['A) Model B is preferred by both AIC and BIC','B) Model A is preferred by both AIC and BIC','C) Model B is preferred by AIC; Model A is preferred by BIC','D) Model A is preferred by AIC; Model B is preferred by BIC'],ans:3,exp:'Both AIC and BIC are minimised – the model with the smaller value is preferred. $\\mathrm{AIC}$: $245.6&lt;248.1\\ \\Rightarrow$ Model A preferred. $\\mathrm{BIC}$: $251.0&lt;252.3\\ \\Rightarrow$ Model B preferred. The two criteria disagree here because BIC\'s heavier penalty ($k\\ln n$ vs $2k$) favours the more parsimonious Model B, even though Model A fits marginally better (lower AIC). Exam Tip: When AIC and BIC disagree, it is almost always because BIC penalises extra parameters more heavily for large $n$ – BIC tends to select the simpler model. In practice, many practitioners report both and let context (forecasting vs interpretation) decide. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(1) process $y_t=\\phi y_{t-1}+\\epsilon_t$, $|\\phi|&lt;1$, the spectral density function $f(\\omega)$, for $\\omega\\in(-\\pi,\\pi]$, is:',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2_\\epsilon}{2\\pi(1-2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2)}$','B) $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2_\\epsilon}{2\\pi(1+2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2)}$','C) $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2_\\epsilon}{2\\pi(1-\\phi^2)}$ (constant in $\\omega$)','D) $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2_\\epsilon(1-2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2)}{2\\pi}$'],ans:0,exp:'General formula: $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2_\\epsilon}{2\\pi}\\dfrac{|\\Theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}{|\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}$. For AR(1), $\\Theta=1$ and $\\Phi(z)=1-\\phi z$, so $$|\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2=(1-\\phi e^{-i\\omega})(1-\\phi e^{i\\omega})=1-2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2.$$ Hence $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2_\\epsilon}{2\\pi(1-2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2)}$. Option (B) has a sign error. Option (D) wrongly inverts the AR spectral density into a numerator form (that would be correct for an MA process, not AR). Option (C) evaluates only $\\omega=0$ (using $\\cos0=1$, i.e. $1-2\\phi+\\phi^2=(1-\\phi)^2\\neq1-\\phi^2$) and ignores the $\\omega$-dependence entirely. Memory Tip: AR spectral density $=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\times\\dfrac{1}{|\\Phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}$ – AR contributes to the denominator (creates peaks near roots), while MA contributes to the numerator. Never confuse the two forms. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the sample correlogram (ACF plot) of a random walk series, the sample autocorrelations $r_k$ typically:',opts:['A) Oscillate rapidly between strongly positive and negative values','B) Are exactly zero for all $k\\ge1$','C) Remain close to 1 and decay only very slowly, even at large lags','D) Decay rapidly to zero after lag 1'],ans:2,exp:'For a random walk, successive values are highly persistent ($y_t$ and $y_{t-k}$ share almost all of their accumulated shocks even for large $k$), so the theoretical correlation between $y_t$ and $y_{t-k}$ approaches 1 as $t\\to\\infty$ for fixed $k$ small relative to $t$. In practice, this shows up in the sample correlogram as autocorrelations that stay high (close to 1) and decline only very gradually and almost linearly – a hallmark visual signature used by practitioners to flag non-stationarity/a unit root before running a formal test like ADF. Exam Tip: “Slowly decaying, persistently high correlogram” $\\Rightarrow$ suspect a unit root $\\Rightarrow$ difference the series and re-examine. This is one of the most practically important diagnostic skills in the entire unit. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(1) process with $\\phi=0.5$ and $\\sigma^2_\\epsilon=4$, the variance of the 3-step-ahead forecast error, $\\mathrm{Var}(e_3)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon(1+\\phi^2+\\phi^4)$, is:',opts:['A) $5.33$','B) $5.25$','C) $4.75$','D) $4$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\mathrm{Var}(e_3)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon(1+\\phi^2+\\phi^4)=4(1+0.25+0.0625)=4\\times1.3125=5.25$. Option (D) is just $\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$ (the 1-step-ahead value only). Option (A) is the unconditional variance $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon/(1-\\phi^2)=4/0.75=5.333$ – correct only as $h\\to\\infty$, not at $h=3$ – a very common confusion. Option (C) is an arithmetic slip. Memory Tip: $\\mathrm{Var}(e_h)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\phi^{2j}$, increasing with $h$ and converging to $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon/(1-\\phi^2)$ only in the limit $h\\to\\infty$. Never substitute the unconditional variance for a finite-$h$ forecast error variance. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the MA(2) process $y_t=\\epsilon_t-\\theta_1\\epsilon_{t-1}-\\theta_2\\epsilon_{t-2}$ with $\\theta_1=0.5,\\ \\theta_2=-0.8$, is the process invertible?',opts:['A) No, because $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=-0.3$ is negative','B) Cannot be determined without more information','C) No, because $\\theta_2$ is negative','D) Yes – all three invertibility triangle conditions ($\\theta_1+\\theta_2&lt;1$, $\\theta_2-\\theta_1&lt;1$, $|\\theta_2|&lt;1$) hold'],ans:3,exp:'Checking all three triangle conditions directly: $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=0.5-0.8=-0.3&lt;1$ ✓ $\\theta_2-\\theta_1=-0.8-0.5=-1.3&lt;1$ ✓ (the condition is a one-sided upper bound; a very negative value easily satisfies it) $|\\theta_2|=0.8&lt;1$ ✓ All three conditions hold, so the process is invertible. Options (C) and (A) are common misconceptions – a negative coefficient, or a negative sum, does not by itself violate any of the inequalities, which are all stated as “$&lt;1$” (satisfied automatically by negative numbers). Exam Trick: Always check all three inequalities explicitly rather than relying on intuition about signs – negative coefficients are frequently invertible/stationary, and this “looks risky but isn\'t” pattern is a favourite exam trap. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the general linear process $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\epsilon_{t-j}$ (with $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$ white noise, $\\psi_0=1$), which condition on $\\{\\psi_j\\}$ guarantees $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)&lt;\\infty$, so that $X_t$ is well-defined as a mean-square limit?',opts:['A) $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j&lt;\\infty$ (absolute summability)','B) $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$ (square summability)','C) $\\psi_j\\to0$ as $j\\to\\infty$','D) $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}|\\psi_j|=1$'],ans:1,exp:'Since $\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$ are uncorrelated with common variance $\\sigma^2_\\epsilon$: $$\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2_\\epsilon\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j^2,$$ which is finite if and only if $\\sum\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$. This is exactly the condition required by Wold\'s decomposition theorem for the linear representation of a purely non-deterministic stationary process. Absolute summability (A) is a stronger, sufficient-but-not-necessary condition (it also guarantees the ACVF is absolutely summable, useful for spectral density existence, but is not itself required merely for finite variance). Mere convergence to zero (C) is too weak on its own. Memory Tip: Finite variance $\\Leftrightarrow$ square-summable $\\psi_j$ ($\\ell^2$). Absolutely summable $\\psi_j$ ($\\ell^1$) is a stronger condition, additionally ensuring a continuous, well-behaved spectral density. All stationary ARMA processes satisfy both. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A series shows a sample ACF that decays extremely slowly (still above 0.9 at lag 20), while the ACF of its first difference decays rapidly to near zero after lag 2. The appropriate order of differencing $d$ for an ARIMA($p,d,q$) model is:',opts:['A) $d=2$','B) $d=0$','C) $d=1$','D) $d$ cannot be determined from the ACF alone'],ans:2,exp:'A slowly decaying original ACF is the classic symptom of non-stationarity (a unit root). Differencing once already produces an ACF that decays rapidly (near zero after lag 2) – the hallmark of a stationary series. Since one round of differencing is sufficient to achieve (apparent) stationarity, $d=1$. There is no need for a second differencing ($d=2$), which would risk over-differencing – introducing spurious negative autocorrelation and unnecessarily inflating the variance of $\\Delta^2y_t$. Exam Tip: Difference just enough to make the ACF decay quickly – stop as soon as this happens. Over-differencing is a common practical mistake: it does not just fail to help, it actively harms the model (creates a unit root in the MA polynomial). Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The sample PACF of a (stationary) series shows significant spikes at lags 1 and 2 (both outside the $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt n$ bounds), and is statistically insignificant for all lags $\\ge3$. Based on the Box–Jenkins identification rules, the appropriate tentative model is:',opts:['A) AR(2)','B) MA(2)','C) ARMA(1,1)','D) AR(3)'],ans:0,exp:'The Box–Jenkins identification rule: an AR($p$) process has a PACF that cuts off sharply after lag $p$ (significant spikes up to lag $p$, then negligible beyond). Here, significant spikes occur at exactly lags 1 and 2, with nothing significant afterward – this is textbook AR(2) behaviour. (An MA(2) process would instead show its ACF cutting off at lag 2, with the PACF tailing off gradually; ARMA processes show both ACF and PACF tailing off, not cutting off sharply.) Memory Tip: PACF cuts off $\\Rightarrow$ read the order directly off the last significant spike $\\Rightarrow$ AR($p$). ACF cuts off $\\Rightarrow$ MA($q$). Both tail off $\\Rightarrow$ ARMA. This table is the single most exam-relevant fact in Box–Jenkins identification. Recent UGC NET Concept-Based Practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A time series is best described as:',opts:['A) A purely deterministic mathematical function of time','B) A realization of a stochastic process indexed by time','C) A random sample drawn without regard to order','D) A set of independent and identically distributed random variables'],ans:1,exp:'A time series $\\{x_1,x_2,\\dots,x_n\\}$ is the single observed sequence generated by an underlying stochastic process $\\{X_t,t\\in T\\}$ – exactly as an observed value $x$ is a realization of a random variable $X$. Why (D), (A), (C) are wrong: (D) is false because successive time series values are typically dependent (autocorrelated), not i.i.d. (A) is false because real time series contain a random component and cannot be perfectly predicted by a fixed function. (C) is false because time order is essential to a time series, unlike an ordinary random sample. Common mistake: Students often equate “time series” with “the stochastic process” – remember the series is only one realization of the process.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The monthly sales figures (in Rs. lakh) of a single company, recorded every month for five years, form a time series that is best classified as:',opts:['A) Discrete, multivariate','B) Continuous, univariate','C) Discrete, univariate','D) Continuous, multivariate'],ans:2,exp:'Observations are recorded at specific, equally spaced time points (monthly) $\\Rightarrow$ discrete; only a single variable (sales) is tracked $\\Rightarrow$ univariate. Why others are wrong: (D) and (B) wrongly call the series continuous. (D) and (A) wrongly call it multivariate – multivariate requires two or more variables observed jointly. Tip: If the question mentions “monthly/quarterly/annual” data on a single quantity, default to discrete + univariate.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement correctly distinguishes a deterministic time series from a stochastic time series?',opts:['A) Future values of a deterministic series can be exactly predicted from a known mathematical function; future values of a stochastic series can only be described probabilistically','B) A deterministic series is always discrete; a stochastic series is always continuous','C) There is no meaningful distinction between the two','D) A deterministic series has no trend, while a stochastic series always has a trend'],ans:0,exp:'If $X_t = f(t)$ for some known function $f$, the series is deterministic. Real-world series contain an unpredictable/random component, making them stochastic. Why (D), (B), (C) are wrong: (D) confuses trend presence with predictability. (B) wrongly links determinism to the discrete/continuous classification. (C) is wrong since the distinction is fundamental to modelling choice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'GDP, inflation rate, and unemployment rate of a country, each recorded quarterly and analysed jointly, together constitute a:',opts:['A) Deterministic time series','B) Multivariate time series','C) Cross-sectional data set','D) Univariate stochastic time series'],ans:1,exp:'Three variables observed jointly over the same time points form a multivariate time series $\\{\\mathbf{X}_t\\}=\\{(X_{1t},X_{2t},X_{3t})\\}$. Why others are wrong: (D) is wrong since more than one variable is involved. (C) is wrong because the data is time-ordered. (A) is wrong because macroeconomic series are stochastic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is NOT one of the standard objectives of time series analysis?',opts:['A) Randomization','B) Description','C) Forecasting','D) Explanation'],ans:0,exp:'The four standard objectives are Description, Explanation, Forecasting/Prediction, and Control. “Randomization” is a design-of-experiments concept, not a time series objective. Tip: Remember “D-E-F-C”: Describe, Explain, Forecast, Control.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The stochastic process notation $\\{X_t, t\\in T\\}$ refers to:',opts:['A) A probability distribution function','B) A single random variable','C) A fixed sequence of numbers','D) A family of random variables indexed by time'],ans:3,exp:'$\\{X_t,t\\in T\\}$ denotes a collection (family) of random variables, one for each time point $t$ in the index set $T$, all defined on a common probability space. Common mistake: Do not confuse the process (family of random variables) with a single observed realization (a fixed sequence of numbers).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is an example of a continuous time series?',opts:['A) Annual rainfall total','B) An ECG (electrocardiogram) trace recorded continuously','C) Quarterly GDP','D) Monthly sales figures'],ans:1,exp:'A continuous time series has observations (conceptually) available at every instant of time – an ECG trace is a classic example. The other options are all discrete, recorded at fixed intervals (annual, monthly, quarterly).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Standard NET-level time series methods most commonly assume that observations are recorded at:',opts:['A) Random, unordered time points','B) Continuous recording only','C) Equally spaced time intervals','D) Unequally spaced, arbitrary time points'],ans:2,exp:'Most classical and modern time series techniques (trend fitting, seasonal indices, ACF-based models) are developed for discrete series observed at equally spaced intervals (daily, monthly, quarterly, annually).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The process $X_t = 5 + 3t$ (with no random error term) is an example of a:',opts:['A) Deterministic time series','B) Seasonal time series','C) Multivariate time series','D) Stochastic time series'],ans:0,exp:'Since $X_t$ is an exact, known function of $t$ with no random component, every future value can be predicted with certainty – this is the defining property of a deterministic series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following pairs correctly matches an approach to its typical associated technique?',opts:['A) Frequency domain – autocorrelation function','B) Frequency domain – trend fitting by least squares','C) Time domain – spectral analysis','D) Time domain – autocorrelation function'],ans:3,exp:'The time domain approach studies a series through its behaviour over time, using tools such as the autocorrelation function and ARIMA-type models. The frequency domain approach uses spectral analysis and the periodogram. Common mistake: A common mix-up is pairing spectral analysis with the time domain – spectral analysis belongs to the frequency domain.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Cross-sectional data differs from time series data primarily because:',opts:['A) Cross-sectional data always includes seasonal effects','B) Cross-sectional data has no natural ordering, while time series data is naturally time-ordered','C) Cross-sectional data is always larger in sample size','D) Time series data cannot be numeric'],ans:1,exp:'The defining structural difference is the presence of a natural time ordering (and consequent autocorrelation) in time series data, which cross-sectional data lacks.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Daily closing prices of a single stock over one year form a:',opts:['A) Continuous, univariate time series','B) Discrete, multivariate time series','C) Continuous, multivariate time series','D) Discrete, univariate time series'],ans:3,exp:'Prices are recorded once per trading day (discrete, equally spaced) and only one variable (price) is tracked (univariate).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is an example of a multivariate time series?',opts:['A) Temperature, humidity, and rainfall recorded jointly every day','B) Only rainfall recorded monthly','C) A single company\'s monthly profit','D) A student\'s marks in one subject over four semesters'],ans:0,exp:'Multivariate time series require two or more related variables observed jointly over the same time index – exactly the case when temperature, humidity, and rainfall are recorded together.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The primary reason time series methods differ fundamentally from ordinary cross-sectional regression methods is that:',opts:['A) Time series data is always categorical','B) Time series observations are typically autocorrelated, violating the independence assumption','C) Time series data cannot be plotted','D) There is no real difference between the two'],ans:1,exp:'Classical regression assumes independent observations; time series observations are ordered in time and usually exhibit serial (auto) correlation, requiring specialised methods that account for this dependence.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The classical decomposition of a time series recognises how many distinct components?',opts:['A) Five','B) Three','C) Six','D) Four'],ans:3,exp:'The four classical components are Trend ($T_t$), Seasonal ($S_t$), Cyclical ($C_t$), and Irregular ($I_t$) – remembered as T-S-C-I. Common mistake: A very common error is to say “three components,” forgetting the cyclical component. Tip: “Trendy Seasons Cycle Irregularly” – four words, four components.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which component of a time series is characterised by a wave-like movement with a period exceeding one year, of variable and non-fixed length?',opts:['A) Trend','B) Irregular variation','C) Seasonal variation','D) Cyclical variation'],ans:3,exp:'Cyclical variation refers to oscillations around the trend with period $&gt;1$ year and variable length, typically linked to business cycles. Shortcut: Whenever a question mentions “business cycle” or “period longer than a year but not fixed,” the answer is always cyclical variation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Seasonal variation in a time series is best characterised by:',opts:['A) A period longer than one year that varies unpredictably','B) A permanent shift in the long-run level of the series','C) A completely random, non-repeating pattern','D) A fixed, predictable period of one year or less'],ans:3,exp:'Seasonal variation recurs with a fixed periodicity of one year or less, driven by climate, festivals, or customs. Why others are wrong: (A) describes cyclical variation. (C) describes irregular variation. (B) describes a structural change in level, not seasonality.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Unpredictable, one-off fluctuations in a time series caused by events such as floods, strikes, or wars are attributed to:',opts:['A) Trend','B) Irregular (random) variation','C) Seasonal variation','D) Cyclical variation'],ans:1,exp:'Irregular (residual/noise) variation captures erratic, non-recurring movements left after removing trend, seasonal, and cyclical effects.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the classical description of a business cycle, the correct sequence of phases is:',opts:['A) Depression $\\to$ Prosperity $\\to$ Recession $\\to$ Recovery','B) Prosperity $\\to$ Recession $\\to$ Depression $\\to$ Recovery','C) Recovery $\\to$ Depression $\\to$ Prosperity $\\to$ Recession','D) Recession $\\to$ Prosperity $\\to$ Recovery $\\to$ Depression'],ans:1,exp:'The classical cycle moves Prosperity $\\to$ Recession $\\to$ Depression $\\to$ Recovery, after which it repeats. Tip: “People Rarely Dislike Recovering” gives P-R-D-R.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which method is commonly used to smooth out short-term fluctuations and estimate the trend in a time series?',opts:['A) Autocorrelation function','B) Cauchy–Schwarz inequality','C) Moving averages','D) Stationarity testing'],ans:2,exp:'The method of moving averages smooths a series by averaging over a fixed window of consecutive observations, damping out seasonal and irregular fluctuations to reveal the trend.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The method of least squares, when used to fit a trend line, minimizes:',opts:['A) The sum of the trend values','B) The seasonal index','C) The sum of squared deviations between actual and fitted trend values','D) The autocovariance'],ans:2,exp:'Least-squares trend fitting chooses parameters that minimise $\\sum_t (x_t - T_t)^2$, the sum of squared residuals from the fitted trend line/curve.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A trend of the form $T_t = a + bt + ct^2$ is called a:',opts:['A) Quadratic trend','B) Exponential trend','C) Logistic trend','D) Linear trend'],ans:0,exp:'The presence of a $t^2$ term identifies this as a quadratic (second-degree polynomial) trend model.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An exponential trend is typically written as:',opts:['A) $T_t = a/(1+be^{-ct})$','B) $T_t = a+bt+ct^2$','C) $T_t = a+bt$','D) $T_t = ab^t$'],ans:3,exp:'$T_t=ab^t$ represents constant proportional (percentage) growth per period, the defining property of an exponential trend. Option (A) is a logistic curve, not exponential.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which trend curve is S-shaped and often used to model growth that saturates over time (e.g., market penetration)?',opts:['A) Logistic curve','B) Quadratic trend','C) Exponential trend','D) Linear trend'],ans:0,exp:'The logistic curve $T_t = \\dfrac{a}{1+be^{-ct}}$ rises slowly, accelerates, then flattens near a saturation level, giving its characteristic S-shape.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Seasonal indices are most commonly measured using:',opts:['A) The Cauchy–Schwarz inequality','B) The autocorrelation function','C) The Gaussian equivalence theorem','D) The ratio-to-moving-average method'],ans:3,exp:'The ratio-to-moving-average method computes seasonal indices by expressing each observation as a ratio (or percentage) of its corresponding centred moving average, then averaging across years for each period.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the ratio-to-moving-average method, seasonal indices are typically expressed as:',opts:['A) Percentages fluctuating around 100','B) Negative numbers only','C) Z-scores','D) Absolute deviations'],ans:0,exp:'Seasonal indices are normalised so that their average equals 100%; an index of 120 means the period is typically 20% above the trend-cycle level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best describes the “link relative method” used in measuring seasonal variation?',opts:['A) It applies only to irregular variation','B) It links successive period values as ratios/percentages of consecutive periods to isolate seasonal effects','C) It ignores seasonal variation entirely','D) It is used exclusively to measure cyclical variation'],ans:1,exp:'The link relative method expresses each observation as a percentage of the immediately preceding observation (a “link relative”), and these ratios are then adjusted to isolate the seasonal pattern.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The typical duration of one full cyclical variation (a business cycle) is:',opts:['A) Exactly 5 years, always','B) Less than one month','C) Exactly one year','D) Generally 2 to 10+ years, of variable length'],ans:3,exp:'Unlike seasonal variation, a cyclical swing has no fixed length – business cycles commonly range from about 2 to more than 10 years.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Irregular variation is also commonly referred to as:',opts:['A) Systematic variation','B) Noise or residual component','C) Trend component','D) Seasonal index'],ans:1,exp:'Because it has no predictable pattern, irregular variation is often called the noise or residual component – what remains after trend, seasonal, and cyclical effects are removed.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In most classical decomposition models, the irregular component $I_t$ is assumed to have:',opts:['A) A large, non-zero constant mean','B) Perfect correlation with the trend','C) Mean approximately zero','D) A fixed one-year period'],ans:2,exp:'The irregular component is modelled as a random (noise) term with mean approximately zero, representing unsystematic deviations from the other three components.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is a method for estimating trend using the averages of two halves of the data?',opts:['A) Cauchy–Schwarz method','B) Ratio-to-moving-average method','C) Link relative method','D) Method of semi-averages'],ans:3,exp:'The method of semi-averages splits the series into two equal halves, computes the average of each half, and joins these two points to obtain a straight-line trend.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A freehand curve fitted to a time plot to represent the trend is:',opts:['A) Used only to compute seasonal indices','B) A subjective, graphical method','C) Identical to the method of least squares','D) A rigorous mathematical method'],ans:1,exp:'Freehand curve fitting relies on visual judgement to draw a smooth trend line through the plotted points – simple but subjective, unlike the objective least-squares method.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following scenarios best illustrates cyclical, not seasonal, variation?',opts:['A) School admissions peaking every June','B) Ice-cream sales peaking every summer','C) Retail sales peaking every December','D) A national economy moving through boom and recession phases roughly every 6–8 years'],ans:3,exp:'Options (B), (C), and (A) all have a fixed, $\\le1$ year periodicity – seasonal. Option (D) has a period $&gt;1$ year and variable length – the hallmark of cyclical variation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If, after deseasonalizing and detrending a series, no further systematic (periodic or cyclical) pattern remains, the remaining part represents:',opts:['A) A new cyclical component','B) Additional seasonal effect','C) Additional trend','D) Irregular/random variation'],ans:3,exp:'By definition, whatever remains after trend, seasonal, and cyclical effects are removed – with no further systematic structure – is classified as irregular (random) variation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the multiplicative model $X_t = T_t\\,S_t\\,C_t\\,I_t$, taking logarithms on both sides transforms the model into:',opts:['A) A quadratic trend model','B) A model with no seasonal component','C) An additive model in the logarithms of the components','D) A purely deterministic model'],ans:2,exp:'$\\log X_t = \\log T_t + \\log S_t + \\log C_t + \\log I_t$ – logarithms convert products into sums, turning the multiplicative model into an additive one in the log scale. Shortcut: Whenever asked how to “linearise” a multiplicative model, the answer is always: take logarithms.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The multiplicative decomposition model is generally preferred over the additive model when:',opts:['A) The seasonal amplitude remains constant regardless of the trend level','B) There is no trend present in the series','C) The seasonal amplitude grows or shrinks proportionally with the trend level','D) The series has no seasonal component at all'],ans:2,exp:'When seasonal/cyclical swings scale with the trend level (percentage effects), the multiplicative model $X_t=T_tS_tC_tI_t$ fits better than the additive model.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A quarterly sales series has average sales of 50 units with a seasonal swing of 5 units, and later average sales of 200 units with a seasonal swing of 20 units. Which decomposition model is most appropriate?',opts:['A) Multiplicative model, since the swing-to-level ratio is constant at $0.1$','B) Neither model is appropriate','C) Additive model, since the swing increased','D) Additive model, since the swing-to-level ratio is constant'],ans:0,exp:'$5/50=0.1$ and $20/200=0.1$ – a constant ratio indicates the swing is proportional to the level, favouring the multiplicative model. Common mistake: Do not conclude “additive” just because the absolute swing increased – always check the ratio.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is NOT an assumption of the additive decomposition model $X_t=T_t+S_t+C_t+I_t$?',opts:['A) The magnitude of seasonal variation increases proportionally with the level of the trend','B) The components combine by simple addition','C) The magnitude of seasonal variation does not depend on the level of the trend','D) The four components act independently of one another'],ans:0,exp:'Statement (A) describes the multiplicative model\'s assumption, not the additive model\'s.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In a mixed decomposition model such as $X_t = T_t S_t C_t + I_t$, the irregular component is combined:',opts:['A) Only with the seasonal component','B) Multiplicatively with all other components','C) It is ignored','D) Additively, after the other three components are multiplied together'],ans:3,exp:'A mixed model applies multiplicative combination to $T_t, S_t, C_t$ and then adds the irregular term separately, combining features of both classical models.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Deseasonalization of a time series refers to:',opts:['A) Removing the trend component only','B) Converting the additive model into a multiplicative model','C) Removing the seasonal component to reveal trend-cycle-irregular movements','D) Removing the irregular component only'],ans:2,exp:'Deseasonalized data (e.g., dividing by the seasonal index in a multiplicative model) isolates the trend-cycle-irregular part of the series, which is essential for comparing periods fairly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the multiplicative model, a seasonal index of 120 for a given month means:',opts:['A) The month\'s value is typically about 20% above the trend-cycle level','B) The seasonal index has no meaningful interpretation','C) The month\'s value is typically about 20% below the trend-cycle level','D) The month\'s value is 120 units above the yearly average'],ans:0,exp:'Since seasonal indices average to 100 (i.e., 100%), an index of 120 means the period\'s value is, on average, 20% higher than the underlying trend-cycle level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which model is generally problematic to apply directly when a time series contains zero or negative values (without adjustment)?',opts:['A) Additive model','B) Both are equally unsuitable','C) Multiplicative model (due to the log transform used with it)','D) Neither model is ever affected by this'],ans:2,exp:'The multiplicative model is often linearised via logarithms, which are undefined for zero or negative values – requiring a shift or an alternative approach in such cases.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For monthly data, if the twelve seasonal indices do not average to 100, what adjustment is typically required?',opts:['A) The indices should be normalised (rescaled) so that they average to 100','B) Convert the series to an additive model','C) Multiply all indices by 2','D) No adjustment is needed'],ans:0,exp:'A standard convention requires the seasonal indices (for monthly data) to average to 100; if they do not, each index is rescaled by a common adjustment factor so the average is exactly 100.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is a key advantage of the multiplicative model for typical economic time series?',opts:['A) It eliminates the need for a trend component','B) It cannot be logarithmically transformed','C) It requires no seasonal adjustment','D) It naturally captures percentage/proportional seasonal effects common in economic data'],ans:3,exp:'Economic and business series often show seasonal swings that scale with the level of activity – exactly the proportional structure the multiplicative model represents.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The classical additive decomposition model assumes which relationship among $T_t, S_t, C_t, I_t$?',opts:['A) A simple additive combination with no interaction between components','B) No relationship exists among the components','C) An exponential combination','D) A multiplicative interaction'],ans:0,exp:'The additive model $X_t=T_t+S_t+C_t+I_t$ assumes the components combine by simple addition and act independently of one another.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement about the mixed additive-multiplicative decomposition model is TRUE?',opts:['A) It only applies to univariate series and never to real economic data','B) It is never used in practice','C) It requires taking square roots of the data','D) It can combine multiplicative trend-seasonal-cyclical effects with an additive random term'],ans:3,exp:'The mixed model, e.g., $X_t=T_tS_tC_t+I_t$, is used in practice when the systematic components interact multiplicatively but the irregular/noise term is better modelled as additive.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which visual check on a time plot helps decide between an additive and a multiplicative decomposition model?',opts:['A) Checking whether the seasonal amplitude changes proportionally with the level of the series','B) There is no way to check this visually','C) Checking only the number of data points','D) Checking the colour used in the plot'],ans:0,exp:'If the size of seasonal swings visibly grows or shrinks with the level of the series, multiplicative is indicated; if the swing size stays roughly constant, additive is indicated.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A key limitation of the additive decomposition model is that it:',opts:['A) Cannot handle negative values','B) Always requires logarithms','C) Cannot be represented graphically','D) Assumes seasonal amplitude stays constant, which may not hold for many real economic series'],ans:3,exp:'Many real series show seasonal swings that grow with the trend level, violating the additive model\'s constant-amplitude assumption – in such cases the multiplicative model is preferred.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Taking the natural logarithm of a multiplicative model $X_t = T_t S_t C_t I_t$ yields an additive model on which scale?',opts:['A) The logarithmic (log) scale','B) The percentage scale','C) The squared scale','D) The original scale'],ans:0,exp:'$\\log X_t=\\log T_t+\\log S_t+\\log C_t+\\log I_t$ – the resulting additive relationship holds on the log scale, not the original scale.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If a time series is perfectly explained by trend and seasonal components alone (with no cyclical or irregular effects), then under the additive model, $I_t$ equals:',opts:['A) Zero for all $t$','B) Equal to the trend value','C) A large positive constant','D) Undefined'],ans:0,exp:'If $X_t=T_t+S_t$ exactly (no cyclical or irregular effect), then comparing with $X_t=T_t+S_t+C_t+I_t$ forces $C_t+I_t=0$ for all $t$; with no cyclical effect, $I_t=0$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The first and most essential step recommended before applying any formal time series technique is to:',opts:['A) Test for a unit root','B) Fit an ARIMA model directly','C) Compute the sample autocorrelation function only','D) Construct and visually inspect the time plot'],ans:3,exp:'“Always plot the data first” – the time plot reveals trend, seasonality, outliers, level shifts, and variance behaviour, guiding every subsequent modelling choice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If a time plot shows that the seasonal fluctuations grow larger as the overall level of the series rises, this visually suggests that:',opts:['A) The series has no trend','B) A multiplicative decomposition model is appropriate','C) An additive decomposition model is appropriate','D) The series is stationary'],ans:1,exp:'Growing seasonal amplitude with rising level is the visual signature of proportional (percentage) seasonal effects – exactly what the multiplicative model captures.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A monthly time series shows a steadily rising trend, a clear spike every December, and an unusually large, one-time drop in a single year traced to a factory fire. Which components best explain this behaviour?',opts:['A) Seasonal and cyclical variation only','B) Trend and cyclical variation only','C) Irregular variation only','D) Trend, seasonal, and irregular variation'],ans:3,exp:'The steady rise is trend; the repeating December spike is seasonal; the one-off factory-fire drop is irregular variation. No multi-year wave pattern is mentioned, so cyclical variation is not indicated.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A sudden, permanent jump in the level of a time plot (not returning to the previous level) most likely indicates:',opts:['A) A structural break or level shift','B) Seasonal variation','C) Cyclical variation','D) Random noise only'],ans:0,exp:'A one-time, persistent change in level is typically caused by an external event (policy change, structural shift) and is classified as a structural break, not routine seasonal, cyclical, or irregular variation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If the variance of a time series visibly increases as its level increases over time, this is evidence of:',opts:['A) Perfect stationarity','B) Heteroscedasticity (non-constant variance), often addressed using a log or multiplicative model','C) Homoscedasticity (constant variance)','D) Zero autocorrelation'],ans:1,exp:'A variance that grows with the level indicates heteroscedasticity; a log transform (linked to the multiplicative model) often stabilises such variance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In a time plot, an isolated point far from the general pattern of the series is called:',opts:['A) A cyclical trough','B) A seasonal peak','C) A trend','D) An outlier'],ans:3,exp:'An outlier is an unusual, isolated observation that deviates sharply from the surrounding pattern, often signalling a data error or a genuine but rare event.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following CANNOT typically be determined directly, with numerical precision, from a simple visual time plot alone?',opts:['A) Presence of outliers','B) The exact numerical value of the autocovariance function at every lag','C) Presence of trend','D) Presence of obvious seasonality'],ans:1,exp:'A time plot gives strong qualitative/visual evidence of trend, seasonality, and outliers, but exact numerical values of the autocovariance function require formal computation, not visual inspection alone.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A time plot that oscillates around a constant mean with roughly constant variance and no visible trend or seasonality is suggestive of:',opts:['A) A multivariate series','B) A (weakly) stationary series','C) A non-stationary series','D) A deterministic series'],ans:1,exp:'Constant mean, constant variance, and no obvious trend or seasonality in the time plot are visual hallmarks consistent with (weak) stationarity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'When interpreting a time series, a slowly changing, smooth-looking time plot (values change gradually rather than jump erratically) suggests:',opts:['A) Zero variance','B) Pure white noise','C) Strong persistence/high positive autocorrelation between successive values','D) Independence between observations'],ans:2,exp:'Smooth, gradually evolving series typically have high positive lag-1 (and higher lag) autocorrelation – successive values are strongly linearly related.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'When a time plot reveals an obvious upward trend, which is the most appropriate first action before fitting a stationary model?',opts:['A) Detrend or difference the series to remove the trend','B) Ignore the trend and proceed directly','C) Discard the data entirely','D) Immediately compute the autocorrelation function without any adjustment'],ans:0,exp:'Since stationary models require a constant mean, detrending (e.g., by fitting and subtracting a trend) or differencing is the standard first step to remove a visible trend before applying stationary modelling techniques.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The mean function of a stochastic process $\\{X_t\\}$ is defined as:',opts:['A) $\\mu_t = \\operatorname{E}(X_t)$','B) $\\mu_t = \\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_t)$','C) $\\mu_t = \\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$','D) $\\mu_t = \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^n X_t$ always'],ans:0,exp:'By definition, $\\mu_t=\\operatorname{E}(X_t)$ is the expected value of the process at time $t$; it may vary with $t$ in general. Why others are wrong: (C) confuses mean with variance. (B) is a variance in disguise. (D) is only the sample mean estimator, valid solely under stationarity in mean.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The following are five successive observations of a time series: $12,\\ 15,\\ 18,\\ 20,\\ 25$. The sample mean $\\bar x$ equals:',opts:['A) $18$','B) $17$','C) $19$','D) $20$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\bar x=\\dfrac{12+15+18+20+25}{5}=\\dfrac{90}{5}=18$. Shortcut: Pair values: $(12+25)=37$, $(15+20)=35$, plus middle value $18$: total $=90$, divided by $5$ gives $18$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly gives the sample variance (divisor $n$) of a time series $x_1,\\dots,x_n$?',opts:['A) $s^2=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}(x_t-\\bar x)^2$','B) $s^2=\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{t=1}^{n}x_t^2$','C) $s^2=\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^{n}(x_t-\\bar x)$','D) $s^2=\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^{n}(x_t-\\bar x)^2$'],ans:3,exp:'$s^2=\\frac1n\\sum(x_t-\\bar x)^2$, the average squared deviation from the mean. Why others are wrong: (C) sums unsquared deviations, which always equal zero. (B) has the wrong divisor and no mean-correction. (A) omits division by $n$ altogether.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If a process is stationary in mean, the mean function $\\mu_t$:',opts:['A) Varies with $t$','B) Is constant, $\\mu_t=\\mu$ for all $t$','C) Equals the sample variance','D) Equals zero always'],ans:1,exp:'Stationarity in mean means $\\operatorname{E}(X_t)$ does not change with $t$; it settles at a single constant value $\\mu$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The sample mean $\\bar x$ is generally an appropriate estimator of $\\mu_t$ only when:',opts:['A) The series is multivariate','B) The mean is constant over time (stationary in mean)','C) The series has strong seasonal variation','D) The series has a strong trend'],ans:1,exp:'If $\\mu_t$ genuinely varies with $t$ (e.g., due to trend), a single overall sample mean is a poor summary; it is appropriate only when the underlying mean is constant.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given the observations $10,\\ 12,\\ 14,\\ 16,\\ 18$, the sample mean equals:',opts:['A) $13$','B) $15$','C) $16$','D) $14$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\bar x=\\dfrac{10+12+14+16+18}{5}=\\dfrac{70}{5}=14$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The variance function $\\sigma_t^2$ measures:',opts:['A) The average value of the process at time $t$','B) The correlation between two time points','C) The trend slope','D) The dispersion of the process about its mean at time $t$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\sigma_t^2=\\operatorname{E}[(X_t-\\mu_t)^2]$ quantifies how spread out the values of $X_t$ are around its mean $\\mu_t$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the observations $2,\\ 4,\\ 6,\\ 8,\\ 10$, the sample variance (divisor $n$) equals:',opts:['A) $6$','B) $10$','C) $8$','D) $40$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\bar x=6$. Deviations: $-4,-2,0,2,4$; squared: $16,4,0,4,16$; sum $=40$; $s^2=40/5=8$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$ changes systematically over time, the process is said to be:',opts:['A) Seasonal','B) Stationary in variance','C) Deterministic','D) Non-stationary in variance (heteroscedastic)'],ans:3,exp:'A time-varying variance violates the constant-variance condition of weak stationarity and is termed heteroscedasticity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The unbiased sample variance estimator uses which divisor?',opts:['A) $n-1$','B) $n+1$','C) $n-k$','D) $n$'],ans:0,exp:'Dividing by $n-1$ (instead of $n$) corrects the bias that arises from estimating $\\mu$ with $\\bar x$, yielding the unbiased sample variance $s^2=\\frac{1}{n-1}\\sum(x_t-\\bar x)^2$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is true about the relationship between the mean function and trend?',opts:['A) They are entirely unrelated concepts','B) When the mean function varies systematically with $t$, it essentially reflects the trend in the series','C) The mean function is always zero when trend is present','D) Trend has no effect on the mean function'],ans:1,exp:'A systematically changing $\\mu_t=\\operatorname{E}(X_t)$ (e.g., $\\mu_t=a+bt$) is precisely how a trend manifests in the mean function of the underlying process.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A process with $\\operatorname{E}(X_t) = 5 + 2t$ is:',opts:['A) Strictly stationary','B) Non-stationary in mean, since the mean depends on $t$','C) Stationary in mean','D) Weakly stationary'],ans:1,exp:'Since $\\operatorname{E}(X_t)=5+2t$ changes with $t$, condition (i) of weak stationarity (constant mean) is violated, so the process is non-stationary in mean – and hence cannot be weakly or strictly stationary either.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The autocovariance function $\\gamma(t,s)=\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_s)$ reduces to the variance function $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$ precisely when:',opts:['A) $s=0$','B) $t=s$','C) $t=-s$','D) $t=0$'],ans:1,exp:'When $t=s$, $\\gamma(t,t)=\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_t)=\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$ – variance is the “autocovariance at lag zero.” Tip: Whenever you see $\\gamma(t,t)$ or $\\gamma(0)$, immediately read it as “variance.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following properties does the autocovariance function $\\gamma(t,s)$ always satisfy?',opts:['A) $\\gamma(t,s) &gt; 0$ for all $t,s$','B) $\\gamma(t,s) = \\gamma(s,t)$','C) $\\gamma(t,s)$ is undefined when $t\\ne s$','D) $\\gamma(t,s) = -\\gamma(s,t)$'],ans:1,exp:'Since $\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_s)=\\operatorname{Cov}(X_s,X_t)$ by definition, the autocovariance function is always symmetric. Common mistake: Do not assume asymmetry just because $t$ and $s$ appear in a specific order in the notation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'By the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality, the autocovariance function satisfies:',opts:['A) $|\\gamma(t,s)| \\le \\sqrt{\\gamma(t,t)\\,\\gamma(s,s)}$','B) $|\\gamma(t,s)| \\le \\gamma(t,t)+\\gamma(s,s)$','C) $\\gamma(t,s) = \\sqrt{\\gamma(t,t)\\,\\gamma(s,s)}$','D) $|\\gamma(t,s)| \\ge \\sqrt{\\gamma(t,t)\\,\\gamma(s,s)}$'],ans:0,exp:'$|\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_s)|\\le\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)\\operatorname{Var}(X_s)}$, i.e.\\ $|\\gamma(t,s)|\\le\\sqrt{\\gamma(t,t)\\gamma(s,s)}$, directly implying $-1\\le\\rho(t,s)\\le1$ for the autocorrelation function.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an observed series $x_1,\\dots,x_n$, the correct divisor used in the sample autocovariance function at lag $k$, $\\hat\\gamma(k)=\\dfrac{1}{?}\\sum_{t=1}^{n-k}(x_t-\\bar x)(x_{t+k}-\\bar x)$, is:',opts:['A) $n-1$','B) $n+k$','C) $n-k$','D) $n$'],ans:3,exp:'Although only $(n-k)$ product terms are summed, the conventional sample autocovariance divides by the full sample size $n$. Common mistake: One of the most common numerical slip-ups: dividing by the number of terms summed ($n-k$) instead of $n$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given the series $4,\\ 6,\\ 5,\\ 9,\\ 8,\\ 10$ ($n=6$), the sample autocovariance at lag $1$, $\\hat\\gamma(1)$, equals:',opts:['A) $1.2$','B) $2.0$','C) $0.5$','D) $1.0$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\bar x=7$. Deviations: $-3,-1,-2,2,1,3$. $$\\hat\\gamma(1)=\\frac{1}{6}\\big[(-3)(-1)+(-1)(-2)+(-2)(2)+(2)(1)+(1)(3)\\big]=\\frac{6}{6}=1.$$'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The autocorrelation at lag $0$, $\\rho(0)$, always equals:',opts:['A) $1$','B) $0$','C) It depends on the process','D) $-1$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\rho(0)=\\gamma(0)/\\gamma(0)=1$ for every process – a series is always perfectly correlated with itself at lag zero.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a (weakly) stationary process, the autocovariance function $\\gamma(t,s)$ depends on $t$ and $s$ only through:',opts:['A) Their product, $ts$','B) Neither – it remains a function of both $t$ and $s$ separately','C) The lag, $k=|t-s|$','D) Their sum, $t+s$'],ans:2,exp:'Under stationarity, $\\gamma(t,s)=\\gamma(|t-s|)=\\gamma(k)$ – the autocovariance depends only on the time gap between observations, not their absolute positions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given a stationary process with $\\gamma(0)=25$ and $\\gamma(1)=10$, the autocorrelation $\\rho(1)$ equals:',opts:['A) $0.2$','B) $0.4$','C) $0.5$','D) $2.5$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\rho(1)=\\gamma(1)/\\gamma(0)=10/25=0.4$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary process, if $\\gamma(k)\\to 0$ as $k$ increases, this indicates:',opts:['A) Increasing dependence at large lags','B) The process must be non-stationary','C) Diminishing linear dependence between observations far apart in time','D) $\\gamma(0)$ must also be zero'],ans:2,exp:'An autocovariance function that decays toward zero at large lags indicates that observations widely separated in time become nearly linearly uncorrelated.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following values is an invalid autocorrelation coefficient at some lag $k$ ($k\\ne 0$)?',opts:['A) $0.5$','B) $1.5$','C) $-0.7$','D) $0$'],ans:1,exp:'Since $-1\\le\\rho(k)\\le1$ always, a value of $1.5$ is mathematically impossible for a valid autocorrelation coefficient.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The autocovariance matrix formed from $\\gamma(0),\\gamma(1),\\dots,\\gamma(n-1)$ for a stationary process must be:',opts:['A) Singular, always','B) Negative definite','C) Diagonal only','D) Positive semi-definite'],ans:3,exp:'Any valid autocovariance function generates a covariance matrix that must be positive semi-definite – a fundamental mathematical requirement, which is also why the sample version divides by $n$ rather than $n-k$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $X_t$ and $X_{t+k}$ are uncorrelated for a particular lag $k$, then:',opts:['A) $\\rho(k) = 1$','B) $\\gamma(k) = 0$','C) The process cannot be stationary','D) $\\gamma(0) = 0$'],ans:1,exp:'Zero correlation between $X_t$ and $X_{t+k}$ means $\\gamma(k)=\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+k})=0$ by definition of the autocovariance function at lag $k$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given the series $3,\\ 5,\\ 4,\\ 7,\\ 6$ ($n=5$), the sample autocovariance at lag $1$, $\\hat\\gamma(1)$, equals:',opts:['A) $1$','B) $-0.4$','C) $0$','D) $0.4$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\bar x=5$. Deviations: $-2,0,-1,2,1$. $$\\hat\\gamma(1)=\\frac{1}{5}\\big[(-2)(0)+(0)(-1)+(-1)(2)+(2)(1)\\big] =\\frac{0+0-2+2}{5}=\\frac{0}{5}=0.$$ Common mistake: Do not skip zero-valued terms in the sum – include every consecutive product, even when a deviation is zero.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly relates the autocorrelation and autocovariance functions at time points $t$ and $s$?',opts:['A) $\\rho(t,s) = \\gamma(t,s) \\times \\gamma(t,t)$','B) $\\rho(t,s) = \\gamma(t,s) + 1$','C) $\\rho(t,s) = \\gamma(t,t) - \\gamma(s,s)$','D) $\\rho(t,s) = \\gamma(t,s)/\\sqrt{\\gamma(t,t)\\gamma(s,s)}$'],ans:3,exp:'The autocorrelation function standardises the autocovariance by the standard deviations at each time point: $\\rho(t,s)=\\gamma(t,s)/\\sqrt{\\gamma(t,t)\\gamma(s,s)}$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For lag $k=0$, the sample autocovariance function $\\hat\\gamma(0)$ equals:',opts:['A) The sample variance $s^2$ (with divisor $n$)','B) Always $0$','C) The sample mean','D) Always $1$'],ans:0,exp:'At lag $0$, $\\hat\\gamma(0)=\\frac1n\\sum(x_t-\\bar x)^2=s^2$, the sample variance with divisor $n$ – consistent with $\\gamma(0)=\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$ at the population level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In a purely random (white noise) process with mean zero and constant variance $\\sigma^2$, the autocovariance at any non-zero lag $k$ is:',opts:['A) Equal to $1$','B) Undefined','C) Equal to zero','D) Equal to $\\sigma^2$'],ans:2,exp:'White noise, by definition, consists of uncorrelated random variables, so $\\gamma(k)=0$ for all $k\\ne0$, while $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is NOT one of the conditions required for a process to be weakly (covariance) stationary?',opts:['A) $\\operatorname{E}(X_t)=\\mu$, a constant free of $t$','B) $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2&lt;\\infty$, a constant free of $t$','C) $\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+k})$ depends only on the lag $k$','D) The joint distribution of $(X_{t_1},\\dots,X_{t_k})$ is invariant under any time shift, for all $k$'],ans:3,exp:'(A), (B), (C) are exactly the three defining conditions of weak stationarity. (D) describes strict stationarity – a stronger requirement on the entire joint distribution. Tip: “Weak needs only three; Strict needs the whole distribution.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Weak stationarity and strict stationarity are equivalent for which class of processes?',opts:['A) Binomial processes','B) Poisson processes','C) Uniformly distributed processes','D) Gaussian (Normal) processes'],ans:3,exp:'A Gaussian process\'s entire joint distribution is completely determined by its mean vector and covariance structure, so weak stationarity (shift-invariant first two moments) automatically implies strict stationarity for such processes. Common mistake: This equivalence does not hold in general for non-Gaussian processes.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\operatorname{E}(X_t)$ genuinely changes with $t$ (e.g., the process has a trend), the process is:',opts:['A) Strictly stationary','B) Necessarily deterministic','C) Non-stationary in the mean','D) Weakly stationary'],ans:2,exp:'A time-varying mean directly violates condition (i) of weak stationarity, so the process is non-stationary in the mean – this is precisely why trending series must typically be detrended or differenced first.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A random walk process $X_t = X_{t-1} + e_t$ (where $e_t$ is white noise) is:',opts:['A) Deterministic','B) Strictly stationary','C) Non-stationary, since its variance grows with $t$','D) Stationary'],ans:2,exp:'For a random walk starting at $X_0$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=t\\sigma_e^2$, which grows without bound as $t$ increases – violating the constant variance condition of stationarity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A white noise process with $\\operatorname{E}(X_t)=0$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2$ constant, and $\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+k})=0$ for $k\\ne0$ is an example of:',opts:['A) A trending process','B) A deterministic process','C) A weakly stationary process','D) A non-stationary process'],ans:2,exp:'All three weak-stationarity conditions – constant mean, constant variance, lag-only (here, zero) autocovariance – are satisfied, so white noise is weakly stationary.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is required, in addition to weak stationarity, to establish strict stationarity in general (non-Gaussian case)?',opts:['A) Only the mean needs to be checked again','B) Invariance of the entire joint distribution under time shifts, not just the first two moments','C) Nothing more is required','D) Only the variance needs to be checked again'],ans:1,exp:'Weak stationarity concerns only the first two moments; strict stationarity is a stronger property requiring the entire joint distribution (all orders) to be shift-invariant.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Strict stationarity implies weak stationarity, provided that:',opts:['A) The process has finite first and second moments','B) The process is discrete','C) The process is multivariate','D) The process has a trend'],ans:0,exp:'If the finite-dimensional distributions are shift-invariant (strict stationarity) and the first two moments exist and are finite, those moments are automatically shift-invariant too, giving weak stationarity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A deterministic linear trend series $X_t = a + bt$ ($b\\ne0$, no random component) is:',opts:['A) Strictly stationary','B) Stationary','C) White noise','D) Non-stationary, since $\\operatorname{E}(X_t)$ depends on $t$'],ans:3,exp:'Since $\\operatorname{E}(X_t)=a+bt$ changes with $t$ whenever $b\\ne0$, the constant-mean condition of stationarity is violated.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Differencing a non-stationary series (e.g., computing $Y_t = X_t - X_{t-1}$) is a common technique used to:',opts:['A) Achieve stationarity by removing trend','B) Convert an additive model into a multiplicative model','C) Increase the degree of non-stationarity','D) Compute the mean function directly'],ans:0,exp:'Differencing removes a linear (or higher-order, with repeated differencing) trend, often transforming a non-stationary series into a stationary one – a key preliminary step before ARIMA modelling (covered in later parts of this unit).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is generally the weakest (least restrictive) condition among these stationarity-related concepts?',opts:['A) Strict stationarity','B) The two conditions are not comparable','C) Both are equally restrictive','D) Weak (second-order) stationarity'],ans:3,exp:'Weak stationarity requires only the first two moments to be shift-invariant, a strictly weaker requirement than strict stationarity\'s demand on the entire joint distribution – hence weak stationarity is the less restrictive condition.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A researcher observes that a quarterly time series has: (i) a rising long-run level, (ii) seasonal peaks every fourth quarter whose size grows proportionally as the series level rises, and (iii) a constant, lag-independent variance after removing (i) and (ii). Which statement is correct?',opts:['A) The series has no seasonal component','B) An additive model should be used, and the residual series is stationary in variance','C) The series is strictly stationary','D) A multiplicative model should be used, and the residual series shows constant variance consistent with (weak) stationarity of the residuals'],ans:3,exp:'Seasonal amplitude proportional to level $\\Rightarrow$ multiplicative model. Constant, lag-independent residual variance is consistent with (at least) weak stationarity of the residuals. Why others are wrong: (B) picks the wrong model. (A) contradicts the stated seasonal peaks. (C) overstates the result – constant variance alone establishes only weak, not strict, stationarity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A time series is observed to have constant mean, constant variance, and an autocovariance function that depends only on the lag between observations, but its full joint distribution is unknown and cannot be verified as shift-invariant. Based on this information alone, the process can be said to be:',opts:['A) Weakly stationary','B) Non-stationary','C) Deterministic','D) Strictly stationary'],ans:0,exp:'The three stated properties are exactly the definition of weak stationarity. Without verifying the full joint distribution, strict stationarity cannot be claimed – only weak stationarity is established by the given information.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The autocovariance function at lag $k$ is defined as:',opts:['A) $\\operatorname{Corr}(X_t,X_{t+k})$','B) $\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+k})$','C) $E(X_t)$','D) $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$'],ans:1,exp:'By definition, $\\gamma(k)=\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+k})=E[(X_t-\\mu)(X_{t+k}-\\mu)]$. Why other options are wrong: A defines the ACF, not ACVF; D, C are unrelated single-time quantities. Shortcut: "Covariance $\\to$ ACVF; Correlation $\\to$ ACF." Tip: This exact definitional distinction is tested in nearly every cycle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The autocorrelation function $\\rho(k)$ always lies in the range:',opts:['A) $(-\\infty,\\infty)$','B) $[-1,1]$','C) $(0,\\infty)$','D) $[0,1]$'],ans:1,exp:'As a correlation coefficient, $\\rho(k)$ is bounded between $-1$ and $1$ inclusive. Why other options are wrong: C, A wrongly suggest unbounded ranges; C wrongly excludes negative autocorrelation. Shortcut: ACF is always a genuine correlation, so the standard $[-1,1]$ bound applies. Tip: A one-line recall question, occasionally embedded in longer multi-part questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'$\\gamma(0)$ equals:',opts:['A) $\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+1})$','B) $1$','C) $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$','D) $0$'],ans:2,exp:'At lag 0, $\\gamma(0)=\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_t)=\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$. Why other options are wrong: D, A are unrelated quantities; B is instead the value of $\\rho(0)$, not $\\gamma(0)$. Shortcut: Do not confuse $\\gamma(0)$ (variance) with $\\rho(0)$ (always 1). Tip: A classic distractor pairs $\\gamma(0)$ and $\\rho(0)$ to test this exact distinction.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'$\\phi_{11}$, the PACF at lag 1, always equals:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(0)$','B) $\\gamma(1)$','C) $0$','D) $\\rho(1)$'],ans:3,exp:'With no intervening lags to control for at lag 1, the partial and ordinary autocorrelations coincide: $\\phi_{11}=\\rho(1)$. Why other options are wrong: A gives the unstandardized covariance, not PACF; A, C are unrelated/incorrect. Shortcut: "Lag 1 PACF is always free – just equals ACF(1)." Tip: One of the single most repeated one-line facts on this topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which function is used to identify the order of an AR process via a "cutoff" pattern?',opts:['A) None of these','B) ACVF','C) PACF','D) ACF'],ans:2,exp:'The PACF theoretically cuts off (becomes zero) after lag $p$ for a true AR($p$) process. Why other options are wrong: A instead tails off for AR processes; C is not typically used for this cutoff identification directly; D is incorrect since B is valid. Shortcut: "AR order $\\to$ look at PACF." Tip: Foundational identification fact underlying most correlogram-based MCQs.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The sample ACVF formula uses which divisor?',opts:['A) $n-1$','B) $n$','C) $k$','D) $n-k$'],ans:1,exp:'Despite summing only $n-k$ product terms, the standard sample ACVF formula divides by the full sample size $n$, which guarantees the resulting sequence is non-negative definite. Why other options are wrong: D, A, C would break the non-negative definiteness guarantee or are simply incorrect conventions. Shortcut: "Always $n$ on the bottom, regardless of how many terms are summed on top." Tip: A frequently tested numerical trap – many students mistakenly use $n-k$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which property must any valid ACVF sequence satisfy?',opts:['A) Symmetric about lag $p$','B) Non-negative definiteness','C) Always positive at every lag','D) Strictly increasing'],ans:1,exp:'Non-negative definiteness is both a necessary and (by a converse result) sufficient condition for a function to be a valid ACVF of some stationary process. Why other options are wrong: D, C are not generally true properties; D is not a defined or required property. Shortcut: "Valid ACVF $\\Leftrightarrow$ non-negative definite," a foundational theoretical fact. Tip: Frequently linked to why the sample ACVF divisor is chosen as $n$ (Q6).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The correlogram is a plot of:',opts:['A) $\\rho(k)$ (or $\\hat\\rho(k)$) vs. $k$','B) $\\gamma(0)$ vs. $n$','C) $X_t$ vs. $X_{t-1}$','D) $X_t$ vs. $t$'],ans:0,exp:'A correlogram plots the (sample) autocorrelation function against the lag $k$. Why other options are wrong: A describes a raw time series plot; C describes a scatter/lag plot; D is not a standard plot. Shortcut: "Correlogram = ACF vs. lag." Tip: Elementary but essential terminology question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Under the white-noise null hypothesis and large $n$, the approximate variance of $\\hat\\rho(k)$ is:',opts:['A) $n^2$','B) $n$','C) $1/n^2$','D) $1/n$'],ans:3,exp:'Bartlett\'s approximation gives $\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat\\rho(k))\\approx1/n$ under the white-noise null for large $n$. Why other options are wrong: B, A, C do not match the standard Bartlett large-sample result. Shortcut: Remember the resulting band directly: $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt n$ (square root of $1/n$ times 1.96). Tip: Foundational for nearly every significance-testing MCQ on this topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of these best describes the PACF at lag $k$?',opts:['A) Correlation between $X_t$ and $X_{t+k}$ after removing the linear effect of $X_{t+1},\\ldots,X_{t+k-1}$','B) Correlation between $X_t$ and $X_{t+k}$ ignoring all intervening structure','C) Covariance between $X_t$ and $X_{t+k}$','D) The $k$-th moment of $X_t$'],ans:0,exp:'PACF is precisely the correlation between $X_t$ and $X_{t+k}$ after controlling for the linear effect of the intervening observations. Why other options are wrong: A misdescribes PACF as identical to ACF; C describes ACVF, not PACF; D is unrelated. Shortcut: "PACF = direct correlation, with the \'middlemen\' lags partialled out." Tip: The precise wording "intervening lags" (not "all observations") is often the crux of trap questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following pairs is correctly matched?',opts:['A) MA($q$) $\\to$ ACF cuts off at $q$','B) MA($q$) $\\to$ PACF cuts off at $q$','C) AR($p$) $\\to$ ACF cuts off at $p$','D) AR($p$) $\\to$ ACF cuts off at $p$, PACF tails off'],ans:0,exp:'The correct identification rule is MA($q$) $\\to$ ACF cuts off after lag $q$ (while PACF tails off). Why other options are wrong: C, D wrongly assign the cutoff property to ACF for AR processes; D wrongly assigns cutoff to PACF for MA processes. Shortcut: "M for MA, A for ACF – both start differently but MA pairs with ACF cutoff." Tip: The single most repeated identification fact in this entire topic area.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For white noise, the theoretical ACF and PACF at all lags $k\\geq1$ are:',opts:['A) Both equal to 1','B) Undefined','C) ACF is 1, PACF is 0','D) Both equal to 0'],ans:3,exp:'Since white noise has zero correlation across time by definition, both the ACF and PACF are zero at every non-zero lag. Why other options are wrong: A, C wrongly assign nonzero values; D is incorrect, since both are well-defined and simply zero. Shortcut: "White noise: flat zero correlogram beyond lag 0, in both ACF and PACF." Tip: Useful baseline recognized instantly in correlogram-description questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\hat\\gamma(0)=16$ and $\\hat\\gamma(2)=4$, find $\\hat\\rho(2)$.',opts:['A) $0.5$','B) $0.25$','C) $16$','D) $4$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\hat\\rho(2)=\\hat\\gamma(2)/\\hat\\gamma(0)=4/16=0.25$. Why other options are wrong: B repeats a raw ACVF value; A, C are arithmetic slips. Shortcut: Always simply divide the given lag-$k$ ACVF by the lag-0 ACVF. Tip: A guaranteed fast numerical mark whenever ACVF values are given directly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For $n=25$, the approximate 95% significance band for the sample ACF is:',opts:['A) $\\pm1.96$','B) $\\pm0.04$','C) $\\pm0.392$','D) $\\pm0.196$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{25}=\\pm1.96/5=\\pm0.392$. Why other options are wrong: B corresponds to $n=100$; C omits the division by $\\sqrt n$; D is an arithmetic error. Shortcut: Compute $\\sqrt n$ first, then divide 1.96 by it directly. Tip: A very frequently repeated numerical significance-band question across NET cycles.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is an even function of lag $k$?',opts:['A) $n$','B) $\\gamma(k)$','C) $X_t$','D) $\\bar X$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\gamma(-k)=\\gamma(k)$ for any stationary process, i.e., the ACVF is symmetric (even) in lag. Why other options are wrong: C, D, A are not functions of lag $k$ at all. Shortcut: "ACVF/ACF: symmetric plot around lag 0." Tip: Useful for quickly eliminating asymmetric-looking distractor claims.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement about $\\hat\\gamma(0)$ is correct?',opts:['A) It equals $\\hat\\rho(1)$','B) It is always zero','C) It equals the sample mean','D) It equals the (biased, divide-by-$n$) sample variance'],ans:3,exp:'$\\hat\\gamma(0)=\\frac1n\\sum_{t=1}^n(X_t-\\bar X)^2$, the biased sample variance using divisor $n$ (not $n-1$). Why other options are wrong: C, B, A each misidentify $\\hat\\gamma(0)$ with unrelated quantities. Shortcut: Recognize $\\hat\\gamma(0)$ as the $k=0$ special case of the general sample ACVF formula. Tip: A recurring subtlety: this differs from the standard $(n-1)$-divisor sample variance taught in basic statistics.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which pair correctly completes: "ACVF is to ___ as ACF is to ___"?',opts:['A) mean; variance','B) variance; mean','C) covariance; correlation','D) correlation; covariance'],ans:2,exp:'ACVF is the covariance-based (unstandardized) quantity, and ACF is its standardized, correlation-based counterpart. Why other options are wrong: B reverses the correct pairing; A, B are unrelated statistical concepts. Shortcut: The names themselves state the answer – "covariance" and "correlation." Tip: A simple analogy-style question occasionally used as a quick screening MCQ.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best describes the asymptotic variance of $\\hat\\rho(k)$ for $k&gt;q$ under a true MA($q$) process (not simple white noise)?',opts:['A) $\\frac1n\\left(1+2\\sum_{j=1}^q\\rho(j)^2\\right)$','B) $\\rho(k)^2/n$','C) $1/n$ exactly','D) $n$'],ans:0,exp:'Bartlett\'s general formula accounts for the true MA($q$) autocorrelation structure through the summed $\\rho(j)^2$ terms, reducing to the simple $1/n$ white-noise case only when $q=0$. Why other options are wrong: A applies only to the pure white-noise special case; D, B are not valid forms of this asymptotic variance. Shortcut: "General MA($q$) null formula reduces to simple $1/n$ exactly when $q=0$" – use this as a consistency check. Tip: A frequently confused JRF-level formula – many students default to the simpler $1/n$ bound even when an MA($q$) null is explicitly stated.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.5$, find $\\phi_{22}$ (PACF at lag 2).',opts:['A) $0$','B) $-0.5$','C) $0.5$','D) $0.25$'],ans:0,exp:'By the PACF cutoff theorem for AR($p$) processes, $\\phi_{kk}=0$ for all $k&gt;p$; here $p=1$, so $\\phi_{22}=0$. Why other options are wrong: C, D, B would apply if a nonzero PACF were expected, contradicting the cutoff theorem for AR(1). Shortcut: For AR(1), everything beyond $\\phi_{11}$ in the PACF is automatically zero. Tip: A guaranteed easy/moderate mark whenever the process is explicitly AR(1).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of these correctly gives $\\phi_{22}$ in terms of $\\rho(1),\\rho(2)$?',opts:['A) $\\phi_{22}=\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2$','B) $\\phi_{22}=\\dfrac{\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2}{1-\\rho(1)^2}$','C) $\\phi_{22}=\\rho(2)/\\rho(1)$','D) $\\phi_{22}=\\rho(1)\\rho(2)$'],ans:1,exp:'This is the standard Durbin-Levinson recursion result at $k=1\\to2$, matching the AR(2) Yule-Walker shortcut for $\\phi_2$. Why other options are wrong: A omits the necessary denominator normalization; C, D are not valid derived forms. Shortcut: Memorize this formula exactly as the fast route to $\\phi_{22}$ without a full matrix recursion. Tip: A guaranteed formula-recall or direct-application question in most cycles.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of these values is impossible for $\\rho(3)$?',opts:['A) $0$','B) $-0.3$','C) $0.5$','D) $1.5$'],ans:3,exp:'Since $\\rho(k)\\in[-1,1]$ always, a value of $1.5$ is impossible for any valid autocorrelation. Why other options are wrong: C, B, A all lie within the valid $[-1,1]$ range. Shortcut: Instantly flag any $|\\rho(k)|&gt;1$ as invalid. Tip: A quick "spot the impossible value" screening question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement about the Ljung-Box statistic is correct?',opts:['A) It requires $n&lt;30$','B) It tests a single lag\'s significance only','C) It is used only for AR models','D) It jointly tests whether $\\rho(1)=\\cdots=\\rho(m)=0$'],ans:3,exp:'The Ljung-Box (portmanteau) statistic tests the joint null hypothesis that the first $m$ autocorrelations are all zero. Why other options are wrong: A misdescribes it as single-lag (that role belongs to individual $t$-type tests against Bartlett bands); C wrongly restricts scope; D is an arbitrary, incorrect sample-size restriction. Shortcut: "Ljung-Box = joint test; Bartlett band = single-lag test." Tip: This ACF-testing distinction (joint vs. individual) is a common conceptual MCQ theme.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) with $\\theta=0.4$, the ACF cuts off after which lag?',opts:['A) Lag 0','B) Lag 2','C) Lag 1','D) Never cuts off'],ans:2,exp:'MA(1) has $\\rho(k)=0$ for all $k\\geq2$, i.e., the ACF cuts off after lag 1 (matching $q=1$). Why other options are wrong: A is too early (ACF is nonzero at lag 1); C is one lag too late; D contradicts the finite-memory MA property. Shortcut: MA($q$) ACF cutoff lag always equals $q$ exactly. Tip: Direct generalization pattern – expect similar MA(2)/MA(3) variants.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best explains why the sample ACVF divisor is chosen as $n$ rather than $n-k$?',opts:['A) It guarantees the resulting sample ACVF sequence remains non-negative definite','B) It matches the population ACVF formula exactly','C) It has no particular reason; either divisor works equally well','D) It simplifies arithmetic only'],ans:0,exp:'Using $n$ as a uniform divisor (rather than the varying $n-k$) ensures the empirical sequence retains the non-negative definiteness property required of any valid ACVF, unlike the $n-k$ alternative which can violate this property. Why other options are wrong: A understates a deep theoretical justification as mere convenience; C is factually incorrect, since the divisors are not interchangeable without consequence; D is not the primary justification (the population formula uses expectation, not any specific $n$-based divisor). Shortcut: "Same divisor for every lag $\\to$ guaranteed valid (PSD) empirical structure." Tip: A conceptually deep JRF-level question testing understanding beyond rote formula memorization.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'PACF at lag 0 is typically:',opts:['A) Always $\\gamma(0)$','B) Undefined/not conventionally used','C) Always $0.5$','D) Always $-1$'],ans:1,exp:'PACF is conventionally defined starting from lag 1 ($\\phi_{11}$); lag 0 is not a meaningful "partial" correlation since there is nothing to partial out with zero intervening lags and no second variable. Why other options are wrong: D, C, A assign specific incorrect numeric values to an conventionally undefined/trivial quantity. Shortcut: PACF discussions and formulas almost always begin explicitly at $k=1$. Tip: A subtle convention-based question occasionally used to test careful reading.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly completes the Durbin-Levinson updating relation?',opts:['A) $\\hat\\phi_{k+1,j}=\\hat\\phi_{kj}-\\hat\\phi_{k+1,k+1}\\hat\\phi_{k,k-j+1}$','B) $\\hat\\phi_{k+1,j}=\\hat\\rho(j)$','C) $\\hat\\phi_{k+1,j}=\\hat\\phi_{kj}\\times\\hat\\phi_{k+1,k+1}$','D) $\\hat\\phi_{k+1,j}=\\hat\\phi_{kj}+\\hat\\phi_{k+1,k+1}$'],ans:0,exp:'This is the exact standard Durbin-Levinson updating formula for the intermediate coefficients at each recursive step. Why other options are wrong: D, C, B are not valid forms of the recursive update. Shortcut: Recognize the structure: new coefficient = old coefficient minus (new PACF times a "mirrored" old coefficient). Tip: A formula-recall question aimed squarely at JRF-level depth on the recursion mechanics.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following functions is scale-invariant (unaffected by a linear rescaling of $X_t$)?',opts:['A) $\\gamma(k)$ only','B) Neither','C) $\\rho(k)$ and $\\phi_{kk}$','D) $n$ only'],ans:2,exp:'Both the ACF and PACF are correlation-type (standardized) quantities and hence unaffected by linear rescaling; the ACVF, being a raw covariance, is not scale-invariant. Why other options are wrong: A wrongly attributes scale-invariance to ACVF; B, D are incorrect/irrelevant. Shortcut: "Anything called a \'correlation\' is automatically scale-invariant." Tip: Useful for quickly eliminating ACVF-based distractors in "which is scale-invariant" questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best describes the Inverse Autocorrelation Function (IACF)?',opts:['A) The reciprocal of each $\\rho(k)$ value','B) $\\quad$','C) The ACF computed on differenced data','D) Identical to PACF in all cases'],ans:1,exp:'The IACF is defined via the "inverse" ARMA process formed by exchanging the AR and MA polynomials, providing an alternative order-identification tool especially useful when both AR and MA components are present. Why other options are wrong: A misdescribes a differencing-based operation, not the IACF; C is not the correct mathematical definition; D wrongly equates two genuinely distinct concepts. Shortcut: "IACF: swap $\\phi$ and $\\theta$, then take the ACF of that swapped process." Tip: An advanced JRF concept occasionally tested as a research-oriented conceptual question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of these is NOT a required property of a valid ACF sequence?',opts:['A) $|\\rho(k)|\\leq1$','B) $\\rho(-k)=\\rho(k)$','C) $\\rho(0)=1$','D) $\\rho(k)$ strictly decreasing in $k$'],ans:3,exp:'There is no requirement that $\\rho(k)$ be strictly decreasing; ACF can oscillate, alternate sign, or even show complex damped-sinusoidal patterns while remaining perfectly valid. Why other options are wrong: C, A, B are all genuine, universally required properties of any valid ACF. Shortcut: Only D imposes an unnecessary monotonicity constraint not found in the actual definition. Tip: A classic "identify the false property" elimination-style question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In Box-Jenkins methodology, correlogram-based ACF/PACF analysis primarily supports which stage?',opts:['A) Forecasting','B) Diagnostic checking only, never identification','C) Estimation','D) Identification'],ans:3,exp:'ACF/PACF correlogram analysis is the principal tool used at the identification stage to select tentative ARMA orders, though it is also reused at the diagnostic stage for residual checking. Why other options are wrong: C, A occur later and use different tools/outputs; C wrongly excludes the (correct) primary identification role. Shortcut: Remember the Box-Jenkins order: Identify $\\to$ Estimate $\\to$ Diagnose $\\to$ Forecast; correlograms appear at both Identify and Diagnose stages. Tip: A recurring "connections to other topics" style question linking ACF/PACF theory to the broader modeling framework.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best explains why $\\phi_{22}=0$ for a true AR(1) process?',opts:['A) Because $X_{t+2}$ is fully explained by $X_{t+1}$ alone under the AR(1) recursion, leaving no additional explanatory power for $X_t$ once $X_{t+1}$ is included','B) Because PACF is undefined for AR(1)','C) Because AR(1) is never stationary','D) Because $\\rho(2)=0$ always for AR(1)'],ans:0,exp:'Since $X_{t+2}=\\phi X_{t+1}+\\varepsilon_{t+2}$ already captures the full AR(1) dependence using only $X_{t+1}$, adding $X_t$ to the regression contributes no additional explanatory power, giving $\\phi_{22}=0$. Why other options are wrong: B is factually false (AR(1) can be stationary under $|\\phi|&lt;1$); C is incorrect since $\\rho(2)=\\phi^2\\neq0$ generally; D is false, PACF is well-defined and simply zero at this lag. Shortcut: Directly connects to the AR(1) PACF-cutoff proof from the theory notes – review that derivation. Tip: A conceptual explanation question testing whether the derivation (not just the result) is understood.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Derive the general form of $\\phi_{33}$ using the Durbin-Levinson recursion structure (conceptually). Which best describes the correct approach?',opts:['A) Use the order-2 solution ($\\phi_{21},\\phi_{22}$) to construct $\\phi_{33}$ via the recursive numerator/denominator formula involving $\\rho(1),\\rho(2),\\rho(3)$','B) Directly apply $\\phi_{33}=\\rho(3)/\\rho(1)$','C) Set $\\phi_{33}=\\rho(3)$ directly without using lower-order results','D) $\\phi_{33}$ cannot be derived without $\\sigma^2$'],ans:0,exp:'The Durbin-Levinson recursion is inherently sequential: computing $\\phi_{33}$ requires the previously obtained order-2 coefficients $\\phi_{21},\\phi_{22}$ as inputs into the next recursive step, not a direct formula skipping intermediate orders. Why other options are wrong: B, C propose invalid shortcuts that bypass the necessary recursive structure; D wrongly claims a dependency on $\\sigma^2$, which is not required for this purely correlation-based recursion. Shortcut: Always build up PACF order-by-order; never attempt to jump directly to a higher-order coefficient. Tip: This sequential-dependency understanding is essential for full-mark long-answer Durbin-Levinson derivation questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(2) process with $\\rho(1)=0.6,\\rho(2)=0.4$, compute $\\phi_{22}$.',opts:['A) $0.4$','B) $-0.0625$','C) $0.6$','D) $0.0625$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\phi_{22}=\\dfrac{\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2}{1-\\rho(1)^2}=\\dfrac{0.4-0.36}{1-0.36}=\\dfrac{0.04}{0.64}=0.0625$. Why other options are wrong: A, C directly restate given $\\rho$ values; D has an incorrect sign. Shortcut: Compute $\\rho(1)^2$ first, then apply the numerator/denominator formula directly. Tip: If this is truly an AR(2) process, this computed $\\phi_{22}$ equals the true $\\phi_2$ coefficient exactly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly describes the relationship between $\\phi_{22}$ (from PACF/Durbin-Levinson) and $\\phi_2$ (the true AR(2) Yule-Walker coefficient), when the true process IS AR(2)?',opts:['A) $\\phi_{22}=\\phi_2$ exactly','B) $\\phi_{22}=\\phi_2^2$','C) $\\phi_{22}=-\\phi_2$','D) They are unrelated'],ans:0,exp:'For a true AR(2) process, the Durbin-Levinson recursion\'s order-2 last coefficient $\\phi_{22}$ coincides exactly with the true second Yule-Walker AR coefficient $\\phi_2$, consistent with the general PACF cutoff theorem. Why other options are wrong: A denies a well-established, exact identity; C, B propose incorrect algebraic relationships. Shortcut: "PACF at the true AR order = the true highest-order AR coefficient," always. Tip: This identity connects the PACF/Durbin-Levinson framework directly to Yule-Walker theory – a favorite cross-topic JRF link.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best describes the necessary degrees-of-freedom adjustment when applying the Ljung-Box test to the residuals of a fitted ARMA($p,q$) model (rather than raw data)?',opts:['A) No adjustment needed; use $\\chi^2_m$ as with raw data','B) Use $\\chi^2_{m-p-q}$ to account for the estimated parameters','C) Use $\\chi^2_{m+p+q}$','D) The Ljung-Box test cannot be applied to residuals'],ans:1,exp:'When testing residuals from a fitted model with $p+q$ estimated parameters, the degrees of freedom must be reduced accordingly to $m-p-q$ to correctly reflect the reduced residual degrees of freedom. Why other options are wrong: A ignores the necessary correction (leading to an overly conservative test); C incorrectly increases rather than decreases the degrees of freedom; D wrongly denies a standard and very common diagnostic application. Shortcut: "Subtract the number of estimated ARMA parameters from $m$ before consulting the chi-square table." Tip: A commonly overlooked but frequently tested JRF-level diagnostic-checking subtlety.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best explains the one-to-one correspondence result between valid ACVF sequences and valid PACF sequences?',opts:['A) Only AR processes have this correspondence','B) The correspondence holds only for finite sequences of length 2','C) There is no such correspondence','D) Any sequence of $\\phi_{kk}$ values, each strictly in $(-1,1)$, corresponds to exactly one valid non-negative definite ACVF sequence (up to $\\gamma(0)$ scaling), and vice versa'],ans:3,exp:'This deep parametrization result (used in Bayesian AR modeling and time series simulation) shows that freely choosing PACF values in $(-1,1)$ at each lag automatically guarantees a valid (non-negative definite) ACVF/ACF sequence, and conversely. Why other options are wrong: B denies an established theoretical result; C wrongly restricts scope; D wrongly limits generality to an arbitrary finite case. Shortcut: "PACF values are \'unconstrained\' in $(-1,1)$ individually, unlike ACF values which must jointly satisfy non-negative definiteness." Tip: A genuinely advanced research-level fact, occasionally referenced in the hardest JRF conceptual questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best describes the practical implication of a "slowly decaying, non-vanishing" sample ACF observed over many lags?',opts:['A) A common informal warning sign of possible non-stationarity, motivating formal unit root testing','B) Definitive proof of an MA(1) process','C) Strong evidence the series is white noise','D) Irrelevant to model identification'],ans:0,exp:'A slowly decaying, persistent ACF (rather than the geometric/exponential decay typical of stationary AR/ARMA processes) is a classic informal red flag suggesting the series may be non-stationary, warranting formal tests such as the Dickey-Fuller test. Why other options are wrong: A directly contradicts the expected flat correlogram of white noise; C is an unjustified, overly specific conclusion; D understates the genuine diagnostic value of this pattern. Shortcut: "Slow ACF decay = suspect a unit root; fast decay = suspect genuine stationarity." Tip: A key applied cross-topic link between correlogram interpretation and stationarity/unit-root testing.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which condition guarantees that a proposed finite sequence $\\rho(0)=1,\\rho(1),\\ldots,\\rho(p)$ corresponds to a genuine, valid stationary process\'s ACF?',opts:['A) No condition is required; any sequence in $[-1,1]$ is automatically valid','B) $\\sum \\rho(k)=1$','C) The corresponding $(p+1)\\times(p+1)$ autocorrelation (Toeplitz) matrix must be non-negative definite','D) $\\rho(k)$ must be monotonically decreasing'],ans:2,exp:'The full Toeplitz autocorrelation matrix formed from the proposed sequence must be non-negative definite; this is the precise, necessary and sufficient condition for validity as a genuine ACF sequence. Why other options are wrong: B imposes an unnecessary and false monotonicity requirement; C is an arbitrary, unrelated condition; D wrongly claims validity is automatic merely from the $[-1,1]$ range of individual values, ignoring joint consistency requirements. Shortcut: Individual values in $[-1,1]$ are necessary but NOT sufficient; joint (matrix-level) non-negative definiteness is the real requirement. Tip: Connects directly to Q87-style AR(2) validity-checking questions from prior ARMA material – a recurring JRF theme.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'True or False: "PACF at lag $k$ removes the linear effect of ALL other observations in the entire sample."',opts:['A) True','B) False – only the intervening lags $X_{t+1},\\ldots,X_{t+k-1}$ are controlled for','C) True only for AR processes','D) True only for large $n$'],ans:1,exp:'PACF\'s regression definition controls only for the specific intervening lags between $t$ and $t+k$, not every observation in the entire dataset. Why other options are wrong: A, C, D each wrongly validate or conditionally validate the overstated "all observations" claim. Shortcut: Always mentally insert "only the intervening lags" whenever PACF\'s controlling role is described. Tip: One of the most common and consequential trap statements on this topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A student claims: "Since $\\rho(k)\\in[-1,1]$, $\\gamma(k)$ must also lie in $[-1,1]$." Is this correct?',opts:['A) Correct only if $\\gamma(0)=1$','B) Correct','C) Incorrect – $\\gamma(k)$ is unit-dependent and unbounded in general (only bounded by $\\gamma(0)$, itself unrestricted)','D) Correct only for stationary processes'],ans:2,exp:'$\\gamma(k)$ carries the (squared) units of $X_t$ and is bounded only by $\\pm\\gamma(0)$, which itself can be any non-negative value depending on the scale of the data – there is no universal $[-1,1]$ bound on $\\gamma(k)$ itself. Why other options are wrong: A wrongly extends the ACF\'s bound to the ACVF; C correctly identifies the special case where the bound would coincide numerically but wrongly frames it as the general condition needed; D is an unrelated, insufficient condition. Shortcut: "Only the standardized version (ACF) has the universal $[-1,1]$ bound." Tip: A very common trap exploiting the superficial similarity between $\\gamma(k)$ and $\\rho(k)$ notation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Is this statement correct: "Since the sample ACVF uses divisor $n$, it is always an unbiased estimator of the population ACVF"?',opts:['A) Incorrect – the divisor-$n$ sample ACVF is actually a biased (though asymptotically unbiased/consistent) estimator, chosen deliberately for the non-negative definiteness property rather than unbiasedness','B) Correct','C) Correct only for white noise processes','D) Correct only for $k=0$'],ans:0,exp:'The divisor-$n$ sample ACVF is a deliberately biased (finite-sample) estimator; the bias vanishes asymptotically as $n\\to\\infty$, but the choice of $n$ over $n-k$ trades off unbiasedness for the crucial non-negative definiteness guarantee. Why other options are wrong: A wrongly claims unbiasedness as the reason/property; D, C falsely narrow this general bias property to special cases. Shortcut: "Biased but consistent, and deliberately so – for a good structural reason (PSD guarantee)." Tip: A subtle but important statistical-properties trap frequently tested at JRF level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Is the statement true: "A sample ACF spike crossing the $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt n$ band at a single lag definitively proves genuine autocorrelation at that lag"?',opts:['A) True','B) True only if $n&gt;100$','C) True only for the first lag','D) False – with multiple lags tested, some spikes are expected to cross the band purely by chance (multiple testing); joint tests like Ljung-Box should be considered too'],ans:3,exp:'Testing many lags individually at a 5% significance level means roughly 1 in 20 lags is expected to cross the band purely by chance, even under a true white-noise null; a single crossing is suggestive, not definitive. Why other options are wrong: A overstates the strength of evidence from an isolated spike; B, C falsely narrow this general multiple-testing caveat to specific conditions. Shortcut: "One spike out of many lags $\\to$ interpret cautiously; use Ljung-Box for the full picture." Tip: A statistically sophisticated trap testing awareness of multiple-testing issues in applied correlogram interpretation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A question states: "For an MA(2) process with $\\theta_1=0.5,\\theta_2=0.5$, is $\\rho(2)$ necessarily equal to $\\rho(1)$?" Evaluate this trap.',opts:['A) Not necessarily – $\\rho(1)$ and $\\rho(2)$ depend on different combinations of $\\theta_1,\\theta_2$ via the MA(2) ACVF formulas, and equal coefficients do not generally imply equal autocorrelations at different lags','B) Yes, because MA(2) always has constant ACF','C) Yes, always, since the coefficients are equal','D) Cannot be determined without $\\sigma^2$'],ans:0,exp:'The MA(2) ACVF formulas involve different combinations of $\\theta_1,\\theta_2$ at lag 1 versus lag 2 (e.g., $\\gamma(1)$ involves $\\theta_1+\\theta_1\\theta_2$-type terms while $\\gamma(2)$ involves $\\theta_2$ alone), so equal raw coefficients do not automatically produce equal autocorrelations; this must be checked via the actual formulas, not assumed by symmetry of coefficients. Why other options are wrong: C, B wrongly assume a symmetry/constancy that isn\'t generally guaranteed by equal coefficients; D unnecessarily invokes $\\sigma^2$, which actually cancels out in the ratio $\\rho(k)=\\gamma(k)/\\gamma(0)$ and is not needed. Shortcut: Never assume autocorrelation symmetry across lags just because underlying MA coefficients look symmetric – always verify via the actual ACVF formula structure. Tip: Tests whether students blindly pattern-match coefficient symmetry versus correctly applying the underlying formulas.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) $\\gamma(k)$ is scale-invariant. (II) $\\rho(k)$ is scale-invariant. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Both','B) Neither','C) Only I','D) Only II'],ans:3,exp:'Only the standardized ACF $\\rho(k)$ is scale-invariant; the raw ACVF $\\gamma(k)$ changes under rescaling of $X_t$. Why other options are wrong: C, A wrongly attribute scale-invariance to the ACVF; D wrongly denies the (true) scale-invariance of the ACF. Shortcut: "Correlation-type quantities are scale-invariant; covariance-type quantities are not." Tip: A very frequently repeated I/II pairing format on this exact distinction.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) The sample ACVF uses divisor $n$. (II) The sample ACVF using divisor $n$ guarantees non-negative definiteness of the estimated sequence. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only II','B) Neither','C) Both','D) Only I'],ans:2,exp:'Both statements are correct and causally linked: the choice of divisor $n$ (Statement I) is precisely what produces the non-negative definiteness guarantee (Statement II). Why other options are wrong: D, A each capture only part of this correct, linked pair of facts. Shortcut: Recognize this as a cause-and-effect pairing, not two independent claims. Tip: A "both true, and linked" pairing – avoid assuming only one can be correct.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) PACF cuts off after lag $p$ for AR($p$) processes. (II) ACF cuts off after lag $p$ for AR($p$) processes. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only I','B) Both','C) Only II','D) Neither'],ans:0,exp:'Statement I correctly describes the PACF cutoff property of AR($p$); Statement II is false – the ACF of an AR($p$) process tails off gradually rather than cutting off cleanly. Why other options are wrong: C, B wrongly validate the false ACF-cutoff claim for AR processes; D wrongly rejects the correct Statement I. Shortcut: Only PACF (not ACF) has a clean cutoff property for AR processes. Tip: Reinforces the core AR/MA identification rule tested in nearly every cycle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) Bartlett\'s simple $1/n$ variance formula applies exactly for any stationary process. (II) Bartlett\'s general formula reduces to $1/n$ specifically under the white-noise null. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only II','B) Neither','C) Both','D) Only I'],ans:0,exp:'The simple $1/n$ formula is a special case valid specifically under a white-noise (or, more precisely, $q=0$) null; it does not apply exactly for a general stationary process with genuine autocorrelation structure. Why other options are wrong: A wrongly overgeneralizes the simple formula\'s applicability; C, B each misjudge the true validity of the two statements. Shortcut: Always check which null hypothesis (white noise vs. general MA($q$)) is being tested before selecting the correct Bartlett variance formula. Tip: Reinforces the important distinction tested earlier in Q18.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Assertion (A): The Durbin-Levinson recursion simultaneously computes PACF values and solves the Yule-Walker equations. Reason (R): The recursion\'s final coefficient at each order $k$, $\\phi_{kk}$, is by definition the PACF at lag $k$, and the full set of coefficients at order $p$ solves the AR($p$) Yule-Walker system.',opts:['A) A false, R true','B) Both A and R true, R does not explain A','C) Both A and R true, R correctly explains A','D) A true, R false'],ans:2,exp:'Both statements are true, and R directly and correctly explains why A holds: the recursive structure inherently produces both outputs simultaneously by construction. Why other options are wrong: B wrongly denies the explanatory connection; D, A incorrectly mark one of the two true statements as false. Shortcut: When R explains the precise mechanism behind A\'s claim, and both are independently verifiable as true, the answer is almost always "both true, R explains A." Tip: Assertion-Reason questions on this dual-purpose recursion property are increasingly common in recent JRF papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match the quantity (Column I) with its defining property (Column II). @ll@ Column I | Column II; (a) $\\gamma(k)$ | (i) Bounded in $[-1,1]$, scale-free; (b) $\\rho(k)$ | (ii) Unit-dependent, unbounded in general; (c) $\\phi_{kk}$ | (iii) Controls for intervening lags only; (d) $\\hat\\gamma(0)$ | (iv) Biased sample variance (divisor $n$)',opts:['A) a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii','B) a-i, b-ii, c-iii, d-iv','C) a-ii, b-i, c-iii, d-iv','D) a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv'],ans:2,exp:'$\\gamma(k)$ is unit-dependent/unbounded (ii); $\\rho(k)$ is scale-free and bounded (i); $\\phi_{kk}$ controls only for intervening lags (iii); $\\hat\\gamma(0)$ is the biased sample variance (iv). Why other options are wrong: B, D, A each contain at least one swapped or mismatched pair. Shortcut: Match the most distinctive keyword in each description first (e.g., "bounded in $[-1,1]$" uniquely identifies $\\rho(k)$). Tip: This exact table structure is a strong candidate for direct match-the-following NET questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match the process (Column I) with its correlogram behavior (Column II). @ll@ Column I | Column II; (a) AR($p$) | (i) ACF cuts off at $q$, PACF tails off; (b) MA($q$) | (ii) Both ACF and PACF zero beyond lag 0; (c) ARMA($p,q$) | (iii) PACF cuts off at $p$, ACF tails off; (d) White noise | (iv) Both ACF and PACF tail off',opts:['A) a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii','B) a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii','C) a-ii, b-i, c-iii, d-iv','D) a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii'],ans:0,exp:'AR($p$)$\\to$PACF cuts, ACF tails (iii); MA($q$)$\\to$ACF cuts, PACF tails (i); ARMA($p,q$)$\\to$both tail (iv); white noise$\\to$both zero beyond lag 0 (ii). Why other options are wrong: D, B, C each scramble at least one correct pairing. Shortcut: Re-derive using the standard AR/PACF, MA/ACF cutoff mnemonic before matching. Tip: A direct application of the master identification table – memorize this table structure exactly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match the concept (Column I) with its correct description (Column II). @ll@ Column I | Column II; (a) Bartlett\'s formula | (i) Joint significance test across $m$ lags; (b) Ljung-Box statistic | (ii) Asymptotic variance of $\\hat\\rho(k)$; (c) IACF | (iii) Swap $\\phi(B)$ and $\\theta(B)$ roles; (d) Non-negative definiteness | (iv) Necessary/sufficient condition for valid ACVF',opts:['A) a-ii, b-i, c-iii, d-iv','B) a-i, b-ii, c-iv, d-iii','C) a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i','D) a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii'],ans:0,exp:'Bartlett\'s formula gives the asymptotic variance of $\\hat\\rho(k)$ (ii); Ljung-Box tests joint significance across lags (i); IACF swaps AR/MA polynomial roles (iii); non-negative definiteness is the necessary/sufficient ACVF validity condition (iv). Why other options are wrong: B, D, C each scramble at least two pairings incorrectly. Shortcut: Isolate the most distinctive keyword in each Column II description first (e.g., "swap" uniquely identifies IACF). Tip: JRF-level matching questions often combine advanced concepts – review the "Advanced JRF Concepts" section before attempting.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\hat\\gamma(0)=10,\\hat\\gamma(1)=5$, find $\\hat\\rho(1)$.',opts:['A) $0.5$','B) $10$','C) $5$','D) $2$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\hat\\rho(1)=\\hat\\gamma(1)/\\hat\\gamma(0)=5/10=0.5$. Why other options are wrong: B inverts the ratio; C, B restate raw ACVF values. Shortcut: Divide lag-1 ACVF by lag-0 ACVF directly. Tip: The most basic and frequently repeated numerical calculation on this topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For $n=400$, compute the approximate 95% significance band.',opts:['A) $\\pm0.049$','B) $\\pm0.196$','C) $\\pm0.098$','D) $\\pm1.96$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{400}=\\pm1.96/20=\\pm0.098$. Why other options are wrong: B corresponds to $n=100$; A, D result from arithmetic slips. Shortcut: $\\sqrt{400}=20$ is a common, easily recalled perfect square – use it directly. Tip: A guaranteed 10-second calculation if $\\sqrt n$ is computed carefully.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given $\\rho(1)=0.7$ for a process believed to be AR(1), what is the expected value of $\\hat\\phi_{22}$ (approximately, in a large sample from a genuine AR(1))?',opts:['A) $0.49$','B) $1.4$','C) Approximately $0$','D) $0.7$'],ans:2,exp:'If the true process is genuinely AR(1), the PACF cutoff theorem implies $\\phi_{22}=0$ in the population, so the sample estimate $\\hat\\phi_{22}$ should be close to zero (within sampling variability) in a large sample. Why other options are wrong: B restates $\\rho(1)$ itself, not $\\phi_{22}$; A, B are unrelated computed values not matching the cutoff expectation. Shortcut: Recall the cutoff theorem directly rather than attempting an unnecessary computation. Tip: Tests conceptual application of the cutoff theorem within a numerically-styled question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For $\\rho(1)=0.5,\\rho(2)=0.3,\\rho(3)=0.2$, compute $\\phi_{22}$ and comment on whether an AR(1) or higher-order model seems more appropriate (informally).',opts:['A) $\\phi_{22}=\\dfrac{0.3-0.25}{1-0.25}=\\dfrac{0.05}{0.75}\\approx0.067$, a small value suggesting AR(1) may be adequate (pending significance testing)','B) $\\phi_{22}=0.3$, indicating strong AR(2) structure','C) Cannot be computed from the given information','D) $\\phi_{22}=0.5$, matching $\\rho(1)$ exactly'],ans:0,exp:'Direct substitution gives $\\phi_{22}\\approx0.067$, a relatively small value that, if found statistically insignificant against the appropriate band, would support a simpler AR(1) specification over a higher AR(2) model. Why other options are wrong: B, D misapply or restate given values without correct computation; D wrongly claims insufficient information, when the formula is directly computable from the given $\\rho$ values. Shortcut: Always compute $\\phi_{22}$ numerically first, then compare its magnitude against the significance band before making an order-selection judgment. Tip: A realistic applied JRF-style question combining numerical computation with practical model-selection reasoning.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\hat\\rho(1)=0.4$ and $n=64$, is this ACF value significant at the 5% level under the white-noise null?',opts:['A) Band is $\\pm0.04$; significant','B) Band is $\\pm0.245$; since $0.4&gt;0.245$, it is significant','C) Band is $\\pm0.5$; not significant','D) Cannot be determined'],ans:1,exp:'$\\sqrt{64}=8$, so the band is $\\pm1.96/8=\\pm0.245$; since $|0.4|&gt;0.245$, the spike is statistically significant. Why other options are wrong: C, A use incorrect band calculations; D wrongly claims insufficient information despite $n$ being given. Shortcut: $\\sqrt{64}=8$ is another easily recalled perfect square for quick band computation. Tip: A standard significance-testing numerical question format seen repeatedly across NET cycles.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An MA(2) process has $\\theta_1=0.4,\\theta_2=0.3$. Using the general MA($q$) Bartlett formula, find the approximate variance of $\\hat\\rho(3)$ for large $n=225$, given $\\rho(1)\\approx0.44,\\rho(2)\\approx0.24$ (values computed from the MA(2) ACVF formulas).',opts:['A) $\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat\\rho(3))\\approx\\frac{1}{225}(1+2(0.44^2+0.24^2))=\\frac{1}{225}(1+2(0.1936+0.0576))=\\frac{1}{225}(1.5024)\\approx0.00668$','B) $0.24$','C) $0.44$','D) $1/225\\approx0.00444$'],ans:0,exp:'Applying Bartlett\'s general MA($q$)-null formula with $q=2$ at $k=3&gt;q$: $\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat\\rho(3))\\approx\\frac1n(1+2\\sum_{j=1}^2\\rho(j)^2)$, substituting the given $\\rho(1),\\rho(2)$ values gives approximately $0.00668$. Why other options are wrong: B uses the simpler white-noise-only formula, ignoring the genuine MA(2) structure; C, B simply restate given $\\rho$ values without applying the variance formula. Shortcut: Always identify $q$ correctly and confirm $k&gt;q$ before applying the general (not simple) Bartlett formula. Tip: A comprehensive, multi-step JRF numerical question combining MA process knowledge with Bartlett\'s general formula application.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'How does the ACF of a long-memory (e.g., ARFIMA) process typically differ from that of a standard stationary ARMA process?',opts:['A) It decays geometrically, identical to ARMA','B) It is exactly zero beyond a finite lag','C) It decays hyperbolically (much more slowly than the geometric decay of ARMA processes)','D) It is undefined for long-memory processes'],ans:2,exp:'Long-memory (fractionally integrated) processes exhibit a hyperbolically decaying ACF, contrasting sharply with the exponential/geometric decay characteristic of standard stationary ARMA processes. Why other options are wrong: A incorrectly equates long-memory decay with standard ARMA decay; C describes MA-type finite memory, the opposite of long memory; D wrongly claims the ACF is undefined, when it is well-defined, just slowly decaying. Shortcut: "Long memory = slow (hyperbolic) decay; short memory (ARMA) = fast (geometric) decay." Tip: An emerging research-level topic increasingly referenced in advanced JRF conceptual awareness questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'What is the primary advantage of the Extended ACF (EACF, Tsay-Tiao) approach over simple ACF/PACF cutoff-based identification?',opts:['A) It replaces the need for stationarity','B) It requires no data','C) It can jointly identify both $p$ and $q$ for mixed ARMA models using a two-dimensional array, beyond what simple cutoff logic can reliably achieve','D) It is identical to the simple ACF/PACF approach'],ans:2,exp:'EACF provides a systematic two-dimensional table-based procedure specifically designed to identify mixed ARMA($p,q$) orders, addressing situations where simple ACF/PACF cutoff patterns are ambiguous (as in genuinely mixed processes where both tail off). Why other options are wrong: A is nonsensical, since EACF is data-driven like any ACF-based method; C is unrelated and false; D denies the genuine methodological advance EACF represents. Shortcut: "EACF: built specifically for the hard case (mixed ARMA) where simple cutoffs fail." Tip: An advanced identification technique occasionally referenced in the hardest JRF research-oriented questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The relationship between the ACVF and the spectral density function is best described as:',opts:['A) Unrelated quantities','B) Only applicable to MA processes','C) A Fourier transform pair: $f(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_k\\gamma(k)e^{-ik\\omega}$','D) $f(\\omega)=\\gamma(0)$ for all $\\omega$ always'],ans:2,exp:'The spectral density is defined as the (normalized) Fourier transform of the ACVF sequence, providing the frequency-domain counterpart to the time-domain ACVF. Why other options are wrong: A denies a fundamental, well-established relationship; C wrongly claims a constant spectral density (true only for white noise); D wrongly restricts applicability. Shortcut: "ACVF and spectral density: same information, two domains (time vs. frequency)." Tip: A key cross-topic connection frequently tested linking this topic to the Spectral Analysis unit.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which criterion is typically combined with correlogram-based (ACF/PACF) identification for final, formal ARMA order confirmation?',opts:['A) AIC/BIC information criteria','B) White noise variance alone','C) Sample size alone','D) Mean alone'],ans:0,exp:'While correlograms give an informal visual/tentative order suggestion, formal information criteria like AIC and BIC are used to confirm or refine the final model order via a penalized-likelihood comparison across candidate models. Why other options are wrong: D, C, B are each insufficient standalone criteria for formal order confirmation. Shortcut: "Correlogram suggests, AIC/BIC confirms" – the standard two-step practical workflow. Tip: A frequently tested "expected future question" style link between classical and modern model-selection tools.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'$\\rho(0)$ always equals:',opts:['A) $1$','B) $0$','C) Undefined','D) $\\gamma(0)$'],ans:0,exp:'By definition, $\\rho(0)=\\gamma(0)/\\gamma(0)=1$ for any non-degenerate process. Why other options are wrong: B, C contradict the trivial identity; C confuses $\\rho(0)$ with $\\gamma(0)$ itself. Shortcut: $\\rho(0)=1$ is a tautology, never needs derivation. Tip: A guaranteed easy mark, useful for matrix-diagonal setup questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which quantity is measured in the squared units of the original series?',opts:['A) $\\gamma(k)$','B) None of these','C) $\\phi_{kk}$','D) $\\rho(k)$'],ans:0,exp:'As a covariance, $\\gamma(k)$ inherits the squared units of $X_t$; ACF and PACF are unit-free correlation-type quantities. Why other options are wrong: D, C are dimensionless by construction; D is incorrect since B is a valid answer. Shortcut: "Covariance = squared units; correlation = no units." Tip: Useful for quickly identifying which quantity a "units" question refers to.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The correlogram plots which axis pairing?',opts:['A) Time vs. value','B) Frequency vs. spectral density','C) Lag vs. autocorrelation','D) Lag vs. autocovariance always'],ans:2,exp:'The standard correlogram plots lag $k$ on the horizontal axis against $\\hat\\rho(k)$ on the vertical axis. Why other options are wrong: A describes a raw time series plot; C describes an (uncommon) ACVF-based variant, not the standard correlogram; D describes a periodogram, a different plot entirely. Shortcut: "Correlogram = lag vs. ACF," the standard convention. Tip: Do not confuse the correlogram with the periodogram (spectral analysis plot).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of these is NOT typically required for computing the population ACVF?',opts:['A) Finite second moments','B) A well-defined mean $\\mu$','C) Stationarity','D) Normality of $X_t$'],ans:3,exp:'Normality is not required for the ACVF\'s basic definition (though it may be assumed for certain inference procedures); stationarity, finite second moments, and a well-defined mean are the genuine definitional requirements. Why other options are wrong: C, A, B are all genuine prerequisites for the ACVF to be well-defined and meaningful. Shortcut: "Normality is an inference convenience, not a definitional requirement" – same logic as in the ARMA topic. Tip: A recurring distinction between model definition and estimation/inference assumptions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(2), if the true process has $\\phi_2=0$, what happens to $\\phi_{22}$?',opts:['A) $\\phi_{22}=1$','B) $\\phi_{22}=\\phi_1$','C) $\\phi_{22}=0$, consistent with the process reducing to AR(1)','D) $\\phi_{22}$ is undefined'],ans:2,exp:'If $\\phi_2=0$, the AR(2) process reduces to AR(1), and by the PACF cutoff theorem for AR(1), $\\phi_{22}=0$ exactly, consistent with the identity $\\phi_{22}=\\phi_2$ for a true AR(2) process. Why other options are wrong: A, D, B are inconsistent with the reduction to AR(1) and the cutoff theorem. Shortcut: Setting $\\phi_2=0$ should always recover the AR(1) PACF pattern as a consistency check. Tip: A useful self-verification technique for PACF-related derivations.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best explains why $\\phi_{kk}$ for $k&gt;p$ is exactly zero (not just approximately small) for a true AR($p$) process?',opts:['A) Because AR($p$) processes are never stationary','B) Because higher lags $X_{t+k}$ for $k&gt;p$ are exactly linearly determined (in the regression sense) by only the $p$ immediately preceding values, leaving zero additional explanatory power for $X_t$','C) Because $\\rho(k)=0$ for $k&gt;p$','D) Because $\\phi_{kk}$ is not defined beyond lag $p$'],ans:1,exp:'The AR($p$) structure means $X_{t+k}$ for $k&gt;p$ is fully explained (in expectation) by its $p$ immediately preceding lags, so including $X_t$ in the regression at greater distance contributes exactly zero additional explanatory power once those $p$ lags are included. Why other options are wrong: B is factually false; C is incorrect, as $\\rho(k)$ for AR processes generally remains nonzero (tailing, not cutting off); D wrongly claims non-existence rather than a well-defined zero value. Shortcut: This is the general-$p$ extension of the AR(1) proof seen in Q31. Tip: A conceptual explanation frequently expected as a full derivation in JRF long-answer questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For $n=1600$, at what approximate $|\\hat\\rho(k)|$ value does an ACF spike become significant at the 5% level?',opts:['A) $0.0049$','B) $0.196$','C) $0.049$','D) $1.96$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\sqrt{1600}=40$, so the band is $\\pm1.96/40=\\pm0.049$. Why other options are wrong: B corresponds to $n=100$; C omits division by $\\sqrt n$ entirely; D is an arithmetic error. Shortcut: $\\sqrt{1600}=40$ is another easily recognized perfect square. Tip: Reinforces speed with perfect-square sample sizes, a recurring numerical question style.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If a correlogram shows PACF significant at lags 1 and 2 only (with lag 3 onward within bounds), and ACF gradually tailing off, the best tentative model is:',opts:['A) White noise','B) ARMA(1,1)','C) AR(2)','D) MA(2)'],ans:2,exp:'PACF significant only through lag 2 (cutoff at $p=2$) combined with a tailing ACF is the classic empirical signature of an AR(2) process. Why other options are wrong: A would instead show ACF (not PACF) cutting off; C requires both functions to tail off, not one to cut off; D would show no significant spikes anywhere. Shortcut: Match directly against the standard identification table: PACF cutoff $\\to$ AR order equals the cutoff lag. Tip: A very common applied, description-based identification question style.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following ACVF sequences (given as $\\gamma(0),\\gamma(1),\\gamma(2)$) is NOT valid (i.e., not non-negative definite)?',opts:['A) $1, 0.3, 0.1$','B) $1, 0.9, -0.9$','C) $1, 0.5, 0.2$','D) $1, 0, 0$'],ans:1,exp:'Converting to correlations ($\\rho(1)=0.9,\\rho(2)=-0.9$) and checking the $2\\times2$ determinant $1-\\rho(1)^2=1-0.81=0.19&gt;0$ passes, but the full $3\\times3$ Toeplitz matrix check (including the joint constraint linking $\\rho(1)$ and $\\rho(2)$ at these extreme, oppositely-signed values) fails non-negative definiteness – such an abrupt sign flip at this magnitude is inconsistent with any genuine stationary process. Why other options are wrong: C, A, D all represent plausible, mild, non-negative-definite-consistent sequences typical of valid AR/MA-type processes. Shortcut: Extreme, abruptly sign-flipping correlations at adjacent, high-magnitude lags are a strong red flag for an invalid (non-PSD) proposed sequence – always verify via the full matrix check rather than individual bounds alone. Tip: A sophisticated "spot the invalid ACVF" JRF question requiring genuine matrix-level verification, not just individual $[-1,1]$ range checking.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement about the relationship between PACF and multiple regression is most accurate?',opts:['A) $\\phi_{kk}$ is the coefficient on the $k$-th lag in a regression of $X_{t+k}$ on its $k$ immediately preceding lags','B) PACF is the $R^2$ of a regression','C) PACF has no connection to regression','D) PACF requires a regression on all past and future values'],ans:0,exp:'This is the precise regression-based definition of PACF used throughout the theory – $\\phi_{kk}$ is the last (highest-lag) coefficient in the order-$k$ linear regression. Why other options are wrong: A denies a fundamental, standard definitional connection; C misidentifies PACF as a goodness-of-fit measure rather than a coefficient; D wrongly includes future values, which are not part of the standard regression definition. Shortcut: "PACF = last coefficient in a $k$-lag regression," the core definitional anchor. Tip: Essential for understanding (and deriving) every PACF-related theorem in this topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of these best describes $\\hat\\rho(k)$ for large $k$ relative to $n$ (e.g., $k$ close to $n$)?',opts:['A) Always reliable regardless of $k$','B) Undefined for any $k&gt;1$','C) Increasingly unreliable, since fewer observation pairs ($n-k$) are used in its computation','D) Always exactly zero'],ans:2,exp:'As $k$ approaches $n$, the number of available product terms ($n-k$) shrinks, making $\\hat\\rho(k)$ increasingly unreliable and high-variance at large lags relative to sample size. Why other options are wrong: A wrongly claims uniform reliability; D, B are factually incorrect general claims. Shortcut: "Large $k$ relative to $n$ = few data points = unreliable estimate," a general statistical principle applied here. Tip: A practical caveat often mentioned in applied correlogram interpretation questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A time series shows PACF cutting off sharply after lag 3 and ACF tailing off gradually. The best tentative model is:',opts:['A) MA(3)','B) White noise','C) AR(3)','D) ARMA(3,3)'],ans:2,exp:'PACF cutoff at lag $p=3$ with tailing ACF is the textbook signature of an AR(3) process. Why other options are wrong: B requires the opposite pattern (ACF cutoff, PACF tailing); C requires both to tail off; D would show no structure in either function. Shortcut: Directly apply the ACF/PACF decision tree from the revision notes. Tip: A direct application-style question testing pattern recognition under exam time pressure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best explains the fact that PACF values $\\phi_{kk}$ can be interpreted as "partial regression coefficients" analogous to those in multiple linear regression?',opts:['A) Just as a partial regression coefficient measures the unique contribution of a predictor after controlling for others, $\\phi_{kk}$ measures the unique contribution of $X_t$ to explaining $X_{t+k}$ after controlling for the intervening lags','B) There is no valid analogy','C) The analogy only holds for MA processes','D) PACF measures total (not partial) variance explained'],ans:0,exp:'This is precisely the statistical interpretation: PACF generalizes the concept of a partial regression coefficient to the time series (own-lag) regression setting. Why other options are wrong: A denies a well-established, pedagogically useful analogy; C misattributes a variance-explained interpretation instead of a coefficient interpretation; D wrongly restricts the analogy\'s applicability. Shortcut: "PACF = time series version of a partial regression coefficient." Tip: A conceptual bridge-question connecting time series theory to general regression theory – valuable for JRF-level depth.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly ranks the reliability of $\\hat\\rho(k)$ estimates as $k$ increases, holding $n$ fixed?',opts:['A) Reliability depends only on $\\gamma(0)$','B) Reliability is constant across all $k$','C) Reliability increases with $k$','D) Reliability generally decreases with $k$, due to fewer usable observation pairs and compounding estimation variance'],ans:3,exp:'As established in Q72, larger lags use fewer effective data pairs and are subject to greater sampling variability, generally reducing estimation reliability. Why other options are wrong: A reverses the correct relationship; B, A ignore the genuine lag-dependent reliability pattern. Shortcut: Reinforces Q72\'s key practical caveat from a different angle. Tip: A conceptual companion question to the numerical significance-testing questions in this bank.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In deriving Bartlett\'s simple formula $\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat\\rho(k))\\approx1/n$ under the white-noise null, which key assumption is essential?',opts:['A) The process must be non-stationary','B) The process must be exactly Gaussian with known mean','C) $n$ must be small','D) The true process has no autocorrelation at any nonzero lag (white noise), so cross-product terms in the variance expansion vanish, leaving only the leading term'],ans:3,exp:'Under the white-noise null, all true $\\rho(j)=0$ for $j\\neq0$, causing the general Bartlett variance formula\'s summation terms to vanish entirely, leaving the simplified $1/n$ result. Why other options are wrong: B directly contradicts the required stationarity assumption; C is backward, as the approximation specifically requires large (not small) $n$; D overstates the requirement – approximate normality of $\\hat\\rho(k)$ follows asymptotically without needing exact Gaussian innovations or known mean. Shortcut: Always connect the simple formula back to its white-noise-null origin when asked to justify or derive it. Tip: This is the core derivation technique examinable in long-answer JRF questions – practice explicitly connecting the general and simple Bartlett formulas.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of these is a valid theoretical ACF sequence (values at lags 0,1,2)?',opts:['A) $1, 1.2, 0.8$','B) $1, -1.5, 0.5$','C) $0.5, 0.3, 0.1$','D) $1, 0.6, 0.36$'],ans:3,exp:'$1,0.6,0.36$ is consistent with an AR(1)-type geometric decay pattern ($\\rho(k)=\\phi^k$ with $\\phi=0.6$) and satisfies all basic bounds. Why other options are wrong: A, B have values exceeding the valid $[-1,1]$ range; D incorrectly starts from $\\rho(0)=0.5\\neq1$, violating the fundamental $\\rho(0)=1$ identity. Shortcut: Always check $\\rho(0)=1$ first, then verify all subsequent values lie within $[-1,1]$. Tip: A quick "spot the valid sequence" screening question testing basic property recall.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary process, $\\gamma(-k)$ equals:',opts:['A) $-\\gamma(k)$','B) $\\gamma(k)$','C) $0$','D) $1$'],ans:1,exp:'Stationarity implies the ACVF is symmetric: $\\gamma(-k)=\\gamma(k)$, since covariance depends only on the lag magnitude, not direction. Why other options are wrong: A wrongly claims anti-symmetry; C, D are not generally true. Shortcut: "ACVF is an even function of lag $k$." Tip: A foundational symmetry property assumed implicitly throughout correlogram interpretation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best describes how the sample PACF is used, in conjunction with the sample ACF, during residual diagnostic checking after fitting an ARMA model?',opts:['A) Neither is used post-estimation; only visual inspection of the raw series is used','B) Only the sample ACF of residuals is examined; PACF is irrelevant post-estimation','C) PACF of residuals is examined but never the ACF','D) Both the sample ACF and PACF of the residuals are examined for any remaining significant structure, alongside the (adjusted) Ljung-Box test, to confirm model adequacy'],ans:3,exp:'Standard diagnostic practice examines both the residual ACF and PACF correlograms (plus the joint Ljung-Box test) to check that no significant structure remains, confirming the fitted model has adequately captured the dependence. Why other options are wrong: B, C each arbitrarily exclude one of the two complementary diagnostic tools; D wrongly dismisses the standard, essential diagnostic procedure entirely. Shortcut: "Diagnostic checking = residual ACF + residual PACF + Ljung-Box, together, not in isolation." Tip: Connects this topic\'s identification tools to their equally important diagnostic-checking role in the full modeling cycle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\hat\\gamma(1)=-3$ and $\\hat\\gamma(0)=12$, find $\\hat\\rho(1)$.',opts:['A) $-0.25$','B) $4$','C) $-4$','D) $0.25$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\hat\\rho(1)=\\hat\\gamma(1)/\\hat\\gamma(0)=-3/12=-0.25$. Why other options are wrong: B drops the negative sign; C, B invert the ratio incorrectly. Shortcut: Always carry the sign of $\\hat\\gamma(k)$ through directly into the ratio. Tip: Reinforces that ACF can be negative, testing careful sign-handling under time pressure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The theoretical PACF of a pure MA(1) process:',opts:['A) Cuts off after lag 1','B) Tails off gradually, never exactly cutting off','C) Equals the ACF exactly at every lag','D) Is always zero'],ans:1,exp:'Consistent with the general MA($q$) rule, the PACF of MA(1) tails off (decays gradually) rather than cutting off cleanly, mirroring the AR(1) ACF\'s tailing behavior. Why other options are wrong: A wrongly assigns the ACF\'s cutoff property to the PACF; D, C are factually incorrect general claims. Shortcut: "MA processes: ACF cuts, PACF tails" – apply consistently regardless of specific order. Tip: Direct extension of the master identification rule to the simplest MA case.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $\\phi_{kk}\\to0$ as $k$ increases for a stationary AR($p$) process where $k&gt;p$?',opts:['A) $\\phi_{kk}$ is undefined for $k&gt;p$','B) The process becomes non-stationary at large lags','C) The PACF is exactly (not just approximately) zero for every $k&gt;p$, by the cutoff theorem','D) The PACF gradually decays to zero'],ans:2,exp:'Unlike the ACF\'s gradual tailing decay, the PACF cutoff for AR($p$) is exact – $\\phi_{kk}=0$ precisely (in the population) for every $k&gt;p$, not merely approaching zero asymptotically. Why other options are wrong: A misapplies the ACF\'s tailing behavior to the PACF; B, A misunderstand the well-defined, exact cutoff property. Shortcut: "PACF cutoff is exact and immediate; ACF decay is gradual," a key distinguishing feature between the two functions. Tip: A subtle but important conceptual distinction tested in interpretation-focused MCQs.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement correctly connects correlogram-based seasonality detection to the ACF?',opts:['A) Seasonality only affects the PACF, never the ACF','B) Seasonality always appears only at lag 1','C) Seasonality is undetectable via ACF','D) Seasonality produces periodic spikes in the ACF at the seasonal lag and its multiples (e.g., lag 12, 24 for monthly data)'],ans:3,exp:'A seasonal pattern manifests as recurring significant ACF spikes at the seasonal period and its integer multiples, a well-known and widely used diagnostic signal in applied time series analysis. Why other options are wrong: C, A wrongly deny the ACF\'s genuine and standard role in seasonality detection; C wrongly restricts the seasonal signal to lag 1 only, ignoring the periodic multiple-lag pattern. Shortcut: "Seasonal period $s$ $\\to$ expect ACF spikes at $s, 2s, 3s,\\ldots$" Tip: A key practical application frequently tested in applied/interpretation-based correlogram questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A researcher observes that the sample ACF is significant only at lag 1, with all other lags within the confidence band, and the PACF also shows only a single significant spike at lag 1. The tentatively suggested model is:',opts:['A) Definitely MA(1) only','B) White noise','C) Definitely AR(1) only','D) AR(1) or MA(1) (ambiguous from this pattern alone at low order; both are consistent with a single significant spike at lag 1 in their respective functions)'],ans:3,exp:'A single significant spike at lag 1 in isolation is consistent with either an AR(1) (PACF cutoff at 1) or MA(1) (ACF cutoff at 1) process; the full correlogram pattern across both functions (not just presence of one spike) is needed to distinguish between the two, and here both showing a single spike could occur under either hypothesis in some finite samples. Why other options are wrong: C, A prematurely commit to one specific model without sufficient distinguishing evidence; D ignores the genuine significant spikes present. Shortcut: Always examine the full pattern in both ACF and PACF together, not just the presence of isolated spikes, before concluding a specific model. Tip: Tests careful, non-hasty interpretation of correlogram evidence – a valuable applied judgment skill.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best justifies why $\\phi_{kk}$ (not $\\rho(k)$) is the preferred diagnostic for AR order selection, rather than using $\\rho(k)$ directly?',opts:['A) Because $\\rho(k)$ is undefined for AR processes','B) Because $\\rho(k)$ requires more data to compute','C) Because $\\phi_{kk}$ and $\\rho(k)$ are always identical','D) Because $\\rho(k)$ for AR processes tails off gradually without a clean cutoff, making it difficult to pinpoint an exact order, whereas $\\phi_{kk}$ provides an exact theoretical cutoff at the true order $p$'],ans:3,exp:'The key practical advantage of PACF over ACF for AR order selection is precisely its exact cutoff property, in contrast to the ACF\'s gradual, order-ambiguous tailing decay for AR processes. Why other options are wrong: B is factually false; C is only true at lag 1 ($\\phi_{11}=\\rho(1)$), not generally; D is an irrelevant, incorrect data-requirement claim. Shortcut: Tie this directly back to the "AR $\\to$ PACF cuts" master identification rule and its underlying justification. Tip: A synthesis-style question connecting multiple concepts – common in the hardest JRF-tier questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following pairs correctly matches identification function to model type?',opts:['A) Both $\\to$ MA order only','B) PACF $\\to$ MA order; ACF $\\to$ AR order','C) PACF $\\to$ AR order; ACF $\\to$ MA order','D) Both $\\to$ AR order only'],ans:2,exp:'This is the fundamental, repeatedly emphasized identification rule of this entire topic. Why other options are wrong: B reverses the correct pairing; D, A each ignore one of the two genuine identification roles. Shortcut: If in doubt, return to this exact sentence as the anchor fact. Tip: Possibly the single most repeated fact across the entire topic – guaranteed to appear in some form.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\hat\\rho(1)=0.9$ and $\\hat\\rho(2)=0.85$, is this pair automatically a valid (consistent) autocorrelation pair?',opts:['A) Must check positive/non-negative definiteness of the implied $2\\times2$ (or larger) autocorrelation matrix before concluding validity','B) No, never valid','C) Validity cannot be checked without $n$','D) Yes, always valid for any values within $(-1,1)$'],ans:0,exp:'As with any candidate ACF pair, positive definiteness of the corresponding autocorrelation matrix must be verified before accepting the pair as consistent with a genuine stationary process. Why other options are wrong: A wrongly assumes automatic validity without any check; C wrongly presumes invalidity without verification; D incorrectly claims $n$ is needed for this purely theoretical/correlational check. Shortcut: Always verify $|\\rho(1)|&lt;1$ and check the determinant condition $1-\\rho(1)^2&gt;0$ (and higher-order conditions if more lags are given) before proceeding. Tip: An important "don\'t just accept – verify" reminder frequently rewarded in JRF-level long-answer questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A key distinction between the sample ACVF/ACF and the sample PACF in terms of computational approach is:',opts:['A) Neither requires any computation beyond raw data collection','B) Sample PACF uses a direct formula while ACVF/ACF require recursion','C) Sample ACVF/ACF use a direct closed-form summation formula, while sample PACF requires a recursive algorithm (Durbin-Levinson) building up order by order','D) They are computed identically'],ans:2,exp:'ACVF/ACF have simple, direct closed-form sample formulas, whereas PACF genuinely requires the sequential Durbin-Levinson recursive procedure to compute correctly at each order. Why other options are wrong: B ignores this clear computational distinction; C reverses the correct assignment; D trivializes the well-defined computational procedures involved. Shortcut: "ACVF/ACF: direct formula. PACF: recursive algorithm." A key computational distinction. Tip: A comparative computational fact occasionally tested in advanced JRF conceptual questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of these best describes the practical role of the correlogram in exploratory time series analysis, even before any formal model is proposed?',opts:['A) It is completely unused in practice','B) It serves purely a decorative/reporting purpose with no analytical value','C) It is used only after model fitting, never before','D) It provides an immediate visual first impression of the dependence structure, informing whether the series might be white noise, stationary with structure, or potentially non-stationary'],ans:3,exp:'As one of the very first exploratory steps in time series analysis, the correlogram gives an immediate, informal sense of the data\'s dependence structure, guiding all subsequent modeling decisions. Why other options are wrong: B, A understate its genuine, foundational analytical value; C wrongly restricts its use to only post-estimation diagnostics, ignoring its equally important pre-modeling exploratory role. Shortcut: "Correlogram: useful both before (exploration/identification) and after (diagnostics) model fitting." Tip: A very practical, applications-oriented fact frequently tested in applied questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An observed correlogram with all ACF and PACF values essentially zero (within bounds) at every lag suggests:',opts:['A) A strongly autocorrelated MA process','B) A non-stationary series','C) A strongly autocorrelated AR process','D) A series consistent with white noise (no significant linear dependence)'],ans:3,exp:'A flat correlogram (no significant spikes in either function) is the classic signature of a white-noise (uncorrelated) series. Why other options are wrong: C, A both require some significant structure in at least one function, contrary to the flat pattern described; D is an unrelated and unjustified conclusion from this pattern. Shortcut: "Flat correlogram = white noise," the simplest possible identification case. Tip: A quick baseline-recognition question, useful as a confidence-builder.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best explains why $\\hat\\gamma(0)$ (using divisor $n$) is described as a "biased" estimator of the true variance $\\gamma(0)$?',opts:['A) It is not actually biased; this is a common misconception','B) Because white noise has $\\hat\\gamma(0)=0$','C) Because $E[\\hat\\gamma(0)] \\neq \\gamma(0)$ exactly in finite samples (though the bias vanishes as $n\\to\\infty$), unlike the standard $n-1$-divisor unbiased sample variance','D) Because $\\hat\\gamma(0)$ is always negative'],ans:2,exp:'Using divisor $n$ (rather than the unbiased $n-1$) introduces a small, systematic finite-sample bias in $\\hat\\gamma(0)$ as an estimator of the true variance, though this bias is asymptotically negligible and is a deliberate trade-off for the non-negative definiteness property across the full ACVF sequence. Why other options are wrong: B contradicts the fact that variance estimates are non-negative; C is factually false; D wrongly denies a genuine, well-documented finite-sample statistical property. Shortcut: Connect directly back to Q41\'s fuller explanation of this same bias-vs-PSD tradeoff. Tip: A recurring theme testing genuine statistical understanding rather than superficial formula recall.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a hypothetical process with $\\rho(1)=0.6,\\rho(2)=0.36,\\rho(3)=0.216$, what pattern is evident, and what does it suggest about $\\phi_{kk}$ for $k\\geq2$?',opts:['A) $\\phi_{22}=0.36$','B) The pattern indicates an MA(3) process','C) $\\phi_{22}=0.6$','D) The pattern $\\rho(k)=0.6^k$ is exactly the AR(1) geometric decay signature; consistent with this, $\\phi_{22}=\\phi_{33}=\\cdots=0$ (verified via the Durbin-Levinson formula, e.g., $\\phi_{22}=\\frac{0.36-0.36}{1-0.36}=0$)'],ans:3,exp:'Recognizing $\\rho(k)=(0.6)^k$ as the exact AR(1) ACF signature, and directly verifying via the $\\phi_{22}$ formula that it indeed evaluates to zero, confirms consistency with an AR(1) process and its associated PACF cutoff. Why other options are wrong: A, C misapply or restate given $\\rho$ values without correctly computing $\\phi_{22}$ via the proper formula; D misidentifies a clearly AR(1)-signature geometric decay pattern as an MA process. Shortcut: Recognizing geometric decay patterns ($\\rho(k)=\\phi^k$) instantly signals AR(1) without needing further computation, though verifying via the formula (as shown) reinforces the conclusion rigorously. Tip: A comprehensive pattern-recognition-plus-verification question representative of thorough JRF-level correlogram analysis.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is TRUE regarding the number of "free" PACF values needed to fully parametrize an AR($p$) process\'s autocorrelation structure?',opts:['A) Infinitely many nonzero PACF values','B) $p^2$ PACF values','C) Exactly $p$ nonzero PACF values ($\\phi_{11},\\ldots,\\phi_{pp}$), with all subsequent values exactly zero','D) Exactly 1 PACF value regardless of $p$'],ans:2,exp:'The AR($p$) cutoff theorem guarantees that only the first $p$ PACF values are (generally) nonzero, with everything beyond exactly zero, so exactly $p$ values are needed to characterize the process\'s partial-correlation structure. Why other options are wrong: A, B overstate the required parametrization; C understates it except in the trivial $p=1$ case. Shortcut: Directly ties the cutoff theorem to a parameter-counting interpretation. Tip: Relevant background fact connecting PACF cutoff theory to AR model order/parameter counting.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement about the boundary case $\\rho(1)=1$ (perfect lag-1 correlation) is correct?',opts:['A) This is a perfectly valid, common scenario for any stationary process','B) This automatically implies white noise','C) $\\rho(1)=1$ implies a degenerate, perfectly (deterministically) linearly predictable process at lag 1, a boundary/limiting case inconsistent with genuine non-degenerate stationary variability','D) This is impossible under any circumstances'],ans:2,exp:'A perfect correlation of exactly 1 at any lag implies an exact deterministic linear relationship between $X_t$ and $X_{t+1}$, a degenerate boundary case (e.g., approached only in the non-stationary limit $\\phi\\to1$ for AR(1)) rather than a typical genuine stationary process outcome. Why other options are wrong: A overstates the commonality of this boundary/degenerate case; C directly contradicts the definition of white noise (which requires zero, not perfect, correlation); D is too absolute, as the value is mathematically approachable as a limiting/boundary case, just not typical of well-behaved genuine stationary processes. Shortcut: Treat $\\rho(k)=\\pm1$ values as red-flag boundary/degenerate cases requiring careful scrutiny, not routine values. Tip: Tests whether students recognize boundary values as special, noteworthy cases rather than routine inputs.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statistician\'s name is most closely associated with the large-sample distribution theory of the sample ACF?',opts:['A) Box','B) Durbin','C) Fisher','D) Bartlett'],ans:3,exp:'Bartlett (1946) derived the foundational large-sample distributional results for the sample ACF underlying the standard significance bands. Why other options are wrong: A, C, B are all significant statisticians, but this specific asymptotic ACF theory is attributed to Bartlett. Shortcut: "Bartlett\'s formula/bound" – the name is directly embedded in the standard terminology. Tip: Occasionally tested historical/attribution-based easy question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following most accurately completes: "The PACF cutoff theorem for AR($p$) states that..."?',opts:['A) ...$\\phi_{kk}=0$ for all $k&gt;p$, with $\\phi_{pp}\\neq0$','B) ...the process has zero mean','C) ...$\\rho(k)=0$ for all $k&gt;p$','D) ...$\\gamma(k)=0$ for all $k$'],ans:0,exp:'This is the precise statement of the theorem central to AR order identification via PACF. Why other options are wrong: B misattributes the (false, for AR) cutoff property to the ACF instead; D, B are unrelated general conditions not specific to this theorem. Shortcut: Match "PACF cutoff theorem" directly with "$\\phi_{kk}$" every time. Tip: A direct definitional/theorem recall question, useful as a fast-scoring opportunity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A JRF question asks to prove that $1-\\rho(1)^2&gt;0$ is required for a valid, non-degenerate AR(1)-type $2\\times2$ autocorrelation matrix $P_2=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;\\rho(1)\\\\\\rho(1)&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}$. Which condition is this equivalent to?',opts:['A) $\\rho(1)=1$','B) $|\\rho(1)|&lt;1$ (strict inequality, excluding the boundary $\\pm1$)','C) $\\rho(1)=0$ only','D) $\\rho(1)&gt;1$'],ans:1,exp:'The determinant condition $1-\\rho(1)^2&gt;0$ directly rearranges to $|\\rho(1)|&lt;1$, the strict (non-degenerate) validity condition for a genuinely positive definite (not merely semi-definite) $2\\times2$ correlation matrix. Why other options are wrong: B gives a zero determinant (degenerate boundary, positive semi-definite only, consistent with Q94\'s discussion); C is overly restrictive (sufficient but far from necessary); D violates the valid correlation range entirely. Shortcut: For $2\\times2$ correlation matrices, strict positive definiteness reduces to simply checking $|\\rho(1)|&lt;1$. Tip: A clean, self-contained proof frequently asked as a full derivation in JRF long-answer sections, directly connecting back to the $\\phi_{22}$ denominator structure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best explains why the denominator $1-\\phi_{11}\\rho(1)$ appears in the Durbin-Levinson formula for $\\phi_{22}$?',opts:['A) It represents the residual variance (relative to $\\gamma(0)$) remaining after regressing out the first-order dependence, ensuring the recursion remains well-defined as long as the process is non-degenerate','B) It is an arbitrary normalization constant with no deeper meaning','C) It always equals 1','D) It is unrelated to the regression interpretation of PACF'],ans:0,exp:'This denominator reflects the proportion of variance not yet explained by the lower-order (lag-1) regression, tying directly into the sequential, order-building regression interpretation underlying the entire Durbin-Levinson recursion. Why other options are wrong: A dismisses a meaningful, well-justified structural term; C is false in general (it varies with $\\rho(1)$); D denies the recursion\'s fundamental regression-based grounding. Shortcut: "Denominator = leftover variance after the previous regression step," a recurring structural theme in sequential regression-based recursions. Tip: A useful comparison point connecting to broader recursive estimation methodology questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the notation $\\phi_{kk}$, the double subscript $kk$ indicates:',opts:['A) Multiplication of two different lags','B) A typo; it should be a single subscript','C) An error term index','D) The lag $k$ and the order-$k$ regression from which this coefficient is the final (highest-lag) term'],ans:3,exp:'The notation reflects that $\\phi_{kk}$ is specifically the $k$-th (final, highest-lag) coefficient from the order-$k$ regression, distinguishing it from lower-order coefficients like $\\phi_{k1},\\ldots,\\phi_{k,k-1}$ within that same regression. Why other options are wrong: B wrongly dismisses meaningful, standard notation as an error; A, C misinterpret the notation\'s actual meaning. Shortcut: Recall the general regression definition: $\\phi_{kj}$ for $j=1,\\ldots,k$, with $\\phi_{kk}$ being the special "last/own-order" case defining the PACF. Tip: A notation-clarification question, useful for correctly interpreting Durbin-Levinson recursion formulas.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A researcher computes the sample PACF for lags 1 through 5 and finds $\\hat\\phi_{44}$ statistically indistinguishable from zero using the significance band, while $\\hat\\phi_{11},\\hat\\phi_{22},\\hat\\phi_{33}$ are all significant. The recommended next step is:',opts:['A) Immediately switch to a pure MA model','B) Report AR(4) or higher regardless','C) Consider AR(3) as the tentative order, since PACF cutoff appears to occur after lag 3','D) Ignore the non-significant $\\hat\\phi_{44}$ and test higher lags before drawing any conclusion'],ans:2,exp:'A non-significant PACF value at lag 4, following three significant values, is standard evidence for a PACF cutoff after lag 3, supporting a tentative AR(3) specification (subject to further diagnostic confirmation, e.g., via AIC/BIC and residual checking). Why other options are wrong: A ignores the clear non-significance evidence at lag 4; C is an unjustified model-family switch without supporting ACF evidence; D unnecessarily delays a reasonable, well-supported tentative conclusion, though checking a couple of further lags for robustness is not unreasonable in very careful practice – the primary, most direct conclusion remains AR(3). Shortcut: "Significant through lag $p$, then non-significant $\\Rightarrow$ tentative AR($p$) cutoff." Tip: An applied, judgment-based question style increasingly common in recent JRF papers testing practical model-building sense using PACF patterns.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of these correctly states the relationship between the sample ACF/PACF and the periodogram in spectral analysis?',opts:['A) The periodogram can be computed from the sample ACVF via a discrete Fourier transform, providing a frequency-domain counterpart to the correlogram\'s time-domain view','B) They are entirely unrelated','C) The periodogram exists only for MA models','D) The periodogram equals $\\hat\\gamma(0)$ for all frequencies'],ans:0,exp:'The periodogram is closely linked to the sample ACVF through a Fourier-transform-type relationship, offering a complementary frequency-domain perspective to the correlogram\'s time-domain (lag-based) view. Why other options are wrong: B denies a fundamental, well-established relationship; C wrongly claims a constant value (true only for a degenerate case); D wrongly restricts applicability to MA models only. Shortcut: "Correlogram (time domain) and periodogram (frequency domain) are two views of the same underlying dependence structure." Tip: Connects this ACVF/ACF/PACF topic to the Spectral Analysis unit, a common cross-unit JRF question source.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(1) process, as $\\phi\\to0$, the PACF at lag 1 approaches:',opts:['A) $-1$','B) $0$','C) Undefined','D) $1$'],ans:1,exp:'Since $\\phi_{11}=\\rho(1)=\\phi$ for AR(1), as $\\phi\\to0$, $\\phi_{11}\\to0$ as well, consistent with the process approaching pure white noise. Why other options are wrong: D, A are incorrect boundary values not approached in this limit; D wrongly claims non-existence of a well-defined, simple limiting value. Shortcut: $\\phi=0$ is the trivial boundary case recovering white noise exactly, with all ACF/PACF values at nonzero lags going to zero. Tip: A useful limiting-case sanity check frequently used to verify understanding of parameter roles.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best describes the effect of using too few PACF lags (under-testing) when attempting to identify a true AR(4) process?',opts:['A) The analyst risks concluding a lower, incorrect AR order (e.g., AR(2) or AR(3)) if the correlogram is truncated before the true cutoff lag is reached, since the genuine cutoff at lag 4 would never be observed','B) No effect on identification','C) The model becomes automatically invertible','D) Under-testing always improves identification accuracy'],ans:0,exp:'If the analyst only examines PACF up to, say, lag 3, they will never observe the true cutoff occurring at lag 4, risking a premature and incorrect conclusion about a lower AR order. Why other options are wrong: A dismisses a genuine, well-documented practical risk; C is an unrelated, false claim (invertibility is an MA/ARMA concept, not directly relevant to pure AR truncation); D is generally false, as under-testing typically worsens rather than improves identification accuracy. Shortcut: "Always examine PACF out to a reasonably generous maximum lag, well beyond any suspected order, to avoid missing the true cutoff." Tip: Connects PACF cutoff theory to practical correlogram examination strategy – a valuable applied JRF insight.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of these correctly describes the typical number of lags $m$ recommended for the Ljung-Box test in practice?',opts:['A) $m$ has no accepted guideline and is arbitrary','B) Always exactly $m=1$','C) Always exactly $m=n$','D) A commonly used rule of thumb is approximately $\\ln(n)$ or a similarly modest fraction of $n$, avoiding both too few (missing genuine structure) and too many (diluting test power with many uninformative high-order lags) lags'],ans:3,exp:'Practical guidelines suggest choosing $m$ as a modest function of $n$ (such as $\\ln n$ or similar), balancing sufficient coverage of potentially significant lags against the dilution of test power from including too many uninformative high-order lags. Why other options are wrong: C, B are impractical/extreme choices not reflecting standard practice; D wrongly denies the existence of widely used practical guidelines. Shortcut: "Neither too few nor too many lags – a modest, growing-with-$n$ choice is standard." Tip: A recurring computational-methodology fact tested in JRF-level questions on practical diagnostic testing procedures.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best summarizes the overall relationship between ACVF, ACF, PACF, and their role in the broader time series modeling cycle?',opts:['A) Only ACF is used in practice; ACVF and PACF are purely historical/theoretical constructs','B) These concepts apply only to non-stationary processes','C) ACVF provides the raw covariance structure; ACF standardizes this into a scale-free, bounded measure of dependence; PACF isolates the direct, own-lag contribution after removing intervening effects; together, their sample counterparts and the resulting correlogram form the primary tool for both initial ARMA order identification and post-estimation residual diagnostic checking, all grounded in Bartlett\'s asymptotic distribution theory for formal significance assessment','D) ACVF, ACF, and PACF are three unrelated, independently developed concepts with no shared theoretical foundation'],ans:2,exp:'This synthesis correctly integrates the entire chapter\'s core themes: the hierarchical relationship between the three functions, their joint role (via the correlogram) in both identification and diagnostic checking, and the underlying asymptotic (Bartlett) theory enabling formal statistical inference. Why other options are wrong: B wrongly denies the clear, deliberate theoretical connections between the three concepts; C dismisses the genuine, ongoing practical relevance of both ACVF and PACF; D incorrectly restricts applicability, when these tools are fundamentally defined for and applied to stationary processes. Shortcut: Use this synthesis statement itself as a one-paragraph chapter summary for rapid final revision. Tip: A capstone-style synthesis question – exactly the kind of "big picture" question that rewards conceptual (not just formula) mastery in JRF papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A stochastic process $\\{X_t\\}$ is said to be strictly stationary if',opts:['A) The mean of $X_t$ is zero for all $t$','B) Only the variance of $X_t$ is constant over time','C) The joint distribution of $(X_{t_1},\\dots,X_{t_n})$ is identical to that of $(X_{t_1+h},\\dots,X_{t_n+h})$ for all $t_1,\\dots,t_n$, $h$ and $n$','D) The process has no autocorrelation'],ans:2,exp:'Strict (also called strong) stationarity requires the entire joint probability distribution of any finite collection of the process to remain invariant under a shift of time origin. It is a distributional property, not merely a moment condition. Why other options are wrong: (A) concerns only the mean, a much weaker first-order condition; (B) refers only to variance stationarity, one of many second-order conditions; (D) describes zero autocorrelation, which is neither necessary nor sufficient for strict stationarity. Shortcut: Remember: “Strict = whole distribution shifts, not just moments.” Tip: NET frequently tests this exact definition verbatim – memorise the phrase “joint distribution is invariant under time shift.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The terms strict stationarity and strong stationarity are:',opts:['A) Strict stationarity applies only to Gaussian processes','B) Strong stationarity is a weaker version of strict stationarity','C) Completely different concepts','D) Synonyms referring to the same concept'],ans:3,exp:'Different textbooks use “strict stationarity” and “strong stationarity” interchangeably to denote invariance of the complete joint distribution under time translation. There is no distinction in standard usage. Why other options are wrong: (C) is false since they denote the same property; (B) wrongly implies a hierarchy that does not exist; (A) is false because strict stationarity is defined for any process, Gaussian or not. Shortcut: If a question tries to separate “strict” from “strong,” treat it as a distractor – they are the same. Tip: A classic examiner trap: options claim strict and strong stationarity differ. Both mean full-distribution invariance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A process is weakly stationary (covariance stationary) if:',opts:['A) The joint distribution is invariant under time shifts','B) $E(X_t)=\\mu$ (constant), $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2&lt;\\infty$ (constant), and $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h})$ depends only on $h$','C) $E(X_t)$ depends on $t$, $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ is constant','D) Only the covariance function is zero for all lags'],ans:1,exp:'Weak stationarity (also called covariance/second-order/wide-sense stationarity) requires three conditions: constant mean, finite constant variance, and an autocovariance function that depends only on the lag $h$, not on absolute time $t$. Why other options are wrong: (C) contradicts the constant-mean requirement; (A) is the definition of strict stationarity, a stronger condition; (D) describes white noise, a special case, not the general definition. Shortcut: Remember the acronym M-V-C: constant Mean, constant Variance, Covariance depends only on lag. Tip: Weak stationarity is the most frequently tested definition in NET – write all three conditions explicitly when asked.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Mean stationarity of a process $\\{X_t\\}$ means:',opts:['A) $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ is constant','B) The process is Gaussian','C) $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h})=0$','D) $E(X_t)=\\mu$, a constant independent of $t$'],ans:3,exp:'Mean stationarity, also called first-order stationarity, requires only that the expected value of the process does not change with time; it says nothing about variance or covariance. Why other options are wrong: (A) is variance stationarity (a separate second-order condition); (C) describes an uncorrelated process, unrelated to the mean; (B) is a distributional assumption independent of stationarity. Shortcut: “Mean stationarity” $\\to$ look only at $E(X_t)$; ignore variance/covariance clauses in the option. Tip: NET often mixes mean, variance, and covariance stationarity in the same question to test precise recall – read each option\'s exact moment condition carefully.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Variance stationarity requires that:',opts:['A) $E(X_t)$ is constant','B) $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h})$ depends only on $h$','C) The autocorrelation function decays to zero','D) $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2$, a finite constant not depending on $t$'],ans:3,exp:'Variance stationarity requires the variance of the process to remain the same finite value at every time point, independent of $t$. Why other options are wrong: (A) is mean stationarity; (B) is covariance stationarity, a distinct (though related) condition; (C) describes a mixing/ergodic-type property, not variance stationarity. Shortcut: Match the word in the question stem (“variance”) directly to the moment mentioned in the option. Tip: A process like the random walk fails variance stationarity because $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=t\\sigma^2$ grows with $t$ – a favourite NET counter-example.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following statements about the relationship between strict and weak stationarity is correct?',opts:['A) Strict stationarity always implies weak stationarity, regardless of moment existence','B) Weak stationarity always implies strict stationarity','C) Strict and weak stationarity are unrelated concepts','D) Strict stationarity implies weak stationarity only if the second moments exist (finite variance)'],ans:3,exp:'If a process is strictly stationary and its second moments are finite, then the mean, variance, and autocovariance automatically become time-invariant, so weak stationarity follows. If the variance is infinite (e.g. certain heavy-tailed processes), weak stationarity cannot even be defined. Why other options are wrong: (A) ignores the necessary finite-variance caveat; (B) is false in general – weak stationarity is a much weaker condition and does not fix higher moments or the full distribution; (C) is false since a clear implication exists under regularity conditions. Shortcut: “Strict $\\Rightarrow$ Weak, provided variance is finite” – always check the finite second-moment condition before ticking this implication. Tip: This is one of the most repeated JRF-level conceptual questions – the finite-variance caveat is often the missing detail in wrong options.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a Gaussian process, weak stationarity and strict stationarity are:',opts:['A) Unrelated, since Gaussian processes are always non-stationary','B) Equivalent, because a Gaussian distribution is fully characterised by its first two moments','C) Equivalent only if the process is also ergodic','D) Never equivalent'],ans:1,exp:'A multivariate Gaussian distribution is completely determined by its mean vector and covariance matrix. Hence, if the mean and autocovariance structure are time-invariant (weak stationarity), the entire joint Gaussian distribution is automatically time-invariant, giving strict stationarity as well. Why other options are wrong: (D) contradicts the well-known equivalence result; (C) incorrectly introduces ergodicity, which is a separate concept unrelated to this equivalence; (A) is simply false. Shortcut: “Gaussian + Weakly Stationary = Strictly Stationary.” This is a standard one-line NET fact. Tip: This equivalence is a very high-probability NET/JRF fact – expect it in Assertion-Reason format.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Ergodicity of a stationary process is primarily concerned with whether:',opts:['A) The process is Gaussian','B) Time averages computed from a single realisation converge to the corresponding ensemble (population) averages','C) The variance is finite','D) The process has a constant mean'],ans:1,exp:'Ergodicity addresses whether statistics computed by averaging over time along one observed sample path consistently estimate the theoretical (ensemble) moments of the process, allowing inference from a single realisation. Why other options are wrong: (D) is a stationarity condition, not ergodicity; (C) is a moment-existence requirement, not the defining idea of ergodicity; (A) is a distributional assumption unrelated to ergodicity. Shortcut: “Ergodic = one long realisation is as good as many independent realisations.” Tip: NET often frames this as “time average = ensemble average” – memorise this phrase precisely.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A stationary process $\\{X_t\\}$ with mean $\\mu$ is said to be mean ergodic if:',opts:['A) The autocovariance function is identically zero','B) $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=0$ for all $t$','C) The sample mean $\\bar{X}_T=\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}X_t$ converges (in mean square/probability) to $\\mu$ as $T\\to\\infty$','D) The process is strictly stationary'],ans:2,exp:'Mean ergodicity requires the time-average of the observed series to converge to the true (ensemble) mean $\\mu$ as the sample size grows, typically established via mean-square convergence: $E[(\\bar{X}_T-\\mu)^2]\\to 0$. Why other options are wrong: (B) is an unrealistic degenerate condition; (A) describes white noise, a sufficient but far from necessary condition; (D) is a distributional property, not a convergence statement about the sample mean. Shortcut: Mean ergodic $\\to$ sample mean $\\bar X_T \\xrightarrow{m.s.} \\mu$. Tip: A must-know one-line definition; JRF often asks for the precise mean-square convergence condition.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The standard sufficient condition for mean ergodicity of a weakly stationary process with autocovariance function $\\gamma(h)$ is:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(h)\\to\\infty$ as $h\\to\\infty$','B) $\\dfrac{1}{T}\\sum_{h=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\\gamma(h)\\to 0$ as $T\\to\\infty$','C) $\\mu=0$','D) $\\gamma(h)=0$ for all $h\\neq 0$'],ans:1,exp:'Using $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T)=\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{h=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\\left(1-\\frac{|h|}{T}\\right)\\gamma(h)$, mean ergodicity in the mean-square sense holds if this Cesaro-type average of autocovariances vanishes as $T\\to\\infty$; this is implied whenever $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$ as $h\\to\\infty$ (short-memory/weakly dependent processes). Why other options are wrong: (D) is a much stronger, unnecessary condition (white noise only); (A) would make the variance of $\\bar X_T$ diverge, violating ergodicity; (C) is irrelevant to the convergence of the sample mean\'s variance. Shortcut: “Average of autocovariances $\\to 0$” is the key phrase to recall for mean ergodicity. Tip: This Cesaro-sum condition is a favourite JRF numerical/theoretical question – practice deriving $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T)$ for AR(1).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The term covariance stationarity is best regarded as:',opts:['A) Another name for weak (second-order/wide-sense) stationarity','B) A synonym for strict stationarity','C) A completely separate concept from weak stationarity','D) A property only white noise processes can satisfy'],ans:0,exp:'Covariance stationarity, weak stationarity, second-order stationarity and wide-sense stationarity are all names used by different authors for the same three-condition definition: constant mean, constant finite variance, and lag-dependent-only autocovariance. Why other options are wrong: (C) is false since they are identical; (B) confuses a weaker with a stronger concept; (D) is false because many processes besides white noise (e.g. stable AR/MA models) satisfy covariance stationarity. Shortcut: Build a synonym list: Weak = Covariance = Second-order = Wide-sense stationarity. Tip: NET loves testing terminology synonyms as distractors – prepare a mental synonym map before the exam.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is a classic example of a strictly (and weakly) stationary process?',opts:['A) A deterministic linear trend $X_t = a+bt$','B) A pure random walk $X_t = X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$','C) $X_t = t\\cdot\\varepsilon_t$ where $\\varepsilon_t$ is i.i.d. $N(0,1)$','D) An i.i.d. white noise sequence $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$ with $E(\\varepsilon_t)=0$, $\\mathrm{Var}(\\varepsilon_t)=\\sigma^2$'],ans:3,exp:'An i.i.d. sequence has an identical marginal and joint distribution at every time shift (since the variables are independent and identically distributed), so it is strictly stationary; finite constant variance makes it weakly stationary too. Why other options are wrong: (B) has variance $t\\sigma^2$, growing with $t$; (A) has a time-varying mean $a+bt$; (C) has variance $t^2$, which depends on $t$. Shortcut: i.i.d. sequences are always the safest “stationary” answer choice in NET MCQs. Tip: White noise is the benchmark stationary (and ergodic) process – know its properties cold.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The random walk process $X_t = X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, $X_0=0$, with $\\varepsilon_t$ i.i.d. $(0,\\sigma^2)$, is non-stationary primarily because:',opts:['A) It is not a Gaussian process','B) Its variance, $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=t\\sigma^2$, grows with $t$ and is not constant','C) Its mean is non-zero','D) Its autocovariance is always zero'],ans:1,exp:'By repeated substitution $X_t=\\sum_{i=1}^{t}\\varepsilon_i$, so $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=t\\sigma^2$, which increases without bound as $t$ increases – violating the constant-variance requirement of stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (C) is false, since $E(X_t)=0$ for all $t$ here; (A) is irrelevant – a Gaussian random walk is still non-stationary; (D) is false, since $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h})=t\\sigma^2\\neq 0$ and depends on $t$, not just $h$. Shortcut: Random walk $\\to$ variance grows linearly in $t$ $\\to$ automatically non-stationary. Tip: The random walk is THE standard non-stationary counter-example in NET – always check variance growth first.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which hierarchy correctly orders the stationarity concepts from strongest (most restrictive) to weakest?',opts:['A) All three are equally restrictive','B) Strict stationarity $\\to$ Weak (covariance) stationarity $\\to$ Mean stationarity','C) Mean stationarity $\\to$ Weak stationarity $\\to$ Strict stationarity','D) Weak stationarity $\\to$ Strict stationarity $\\to$ Mean stationarity'],ans:1,exp:'Strict stationarity constrains the entire joint distribution, the strongest requirement. Weak stationarity constrains only the first two moments. Mean stationarity constrains only the first moment, making it the weakest of the three. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (C) reverse the correct order; (A) is false, since each condition is progressively weaker. Shortcut: Think of nested requirements: Strict $\\supset$ Weak $\\supset$ Mean stationarity (each imposes fewer restrictions). Tip: Draw this hierarchy as a simple funnel diagram for quick last-minute revision.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A process is covariance ergodic if:',opts:['A) The process is Gaussian and mean ergodic','B) $\\gamma(h)=0$ for all $h$','C) The variance of $X_t$ is zero','D) The sample autocovariance $\\hat\\gamma(h)=\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T-h}(X_t-\\bar X)(X_{t+h}-\\bar X)$ converges to the true autocovariance $\\gamma(h)$ as $T\\to\\infty$'],ans:3,exp:'Covariance ergodicity extends the idea of mean ergodicity to second moments: sample (time-average) autocovariances computed from a single long realisation must converge to the corresponding population autocovariances for every lag $h$. Why other options are wrong: (B) describes white noise, a special case, not the general definition; (A) unnecessarily restricts to Gaussian processes and conflates two distinct ergodicity notions; (C) is a degenerate, unrealistic condition. Shortcut: Mean ergodic $\\to$ sample mean $\\to \\mu$; Covariance ergodic $\\to$ sample autocovariance $\\to \\gamma(h)$. Same idea, applied to different moments. Tip: JRF may ask you to state both mean and covariance ergodicity together – practice writing both definitions side by side.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(NET-style) If $\\{X_t\\}$ is strictly stationary and $E(X_t^2)&lt;\\infty$, then $\\{X_t\\}$ is necessarily:',opts:['A) Gaussian','B) Weakly stationary','C) Ergodic','D) A white noise process'],ans:1,exp:'With finite second moments, invariance of the joint distribution under strict stationarity automatically forces the mean, variance, and autocovariance to be time-invariant, giving weak stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (C) ergodicity requires additional mixing/dependence conditions, not implied by stationarity alone; (A) strict stationarity does not require normality; (D) white noise is only one special stationary process, not implied in general. Shortcut: “Strict + finite variance $\\Rightarrow$ Weak” is the go-to implication chain. Tip: This exact implication has repeated across multiple NET cycles – a guaranteed-marks fact.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(NET-style) For a weakly stationary process, the autocovariance function $\\gamma(h)$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(h)$ is always negative for $h\\neq 0$','B) $\\gamma(h)$ depends on both $t$ and $h$','C) $\\gamma(h)$ increases without bound as $h\\to\\infty$','D) $\\gamma(h) = \\gamma(-h)$ (symmetry in lag)'],ans:3,exp:'A basic property of the autocovariance function of any weakly stationary process is symmetry: $\\gamma(h)=\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h})=\\mathrm{Cov}(X_{t+h},X_t)=\\gamma(-h)$. Why other options are wrong: (B) contradicts the defining property that $\\gamma$ depends only on $h$; (A) is false – sign depends on the process; (C) violates the boundedness property $|\\gamma(h)|\\le\\gamma(0)$. Shortcut: Autocovariance functions are always even functions of lag: $\\gamma(h)=\\gamma(-h)$. Tip: Also remember $|\\gamma(h)| \\le \\gamma(0) = \\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ – both properties are frequently tested together.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(NET-style) Which of the following processes is weakly stationary?',opts:['A) $X_t = X_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$','B) $X_t = \\varepsilon_1 + \\varepsilon_2 + \\cdots + \\varepsilon_t$','C) $X_t = \\mu + \\varepsilon_t$, $\\varepsilon_t \\sim$ i.i.d. $(0,\\sigma^2)$','D) $X_t = \\mu_t + \\varepsilon_t$, where $\\mu_t$ changes with $t$'],ans:2,exp:'Here $E(X_t)=\\mu$ (constant), $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2$ (constant), and $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h})=0$ for $h\\neq 0$ (depends only on $h$) – all three weak stationarity conditions hold. Why other options are wrong: (D) has a time-varying mean; (A) and (B) are both random walks (identical processes) with variance growing linearly in $t$. Shortcut: Constant-mean-plus-noise models are the safest “stationary” choice unless stated otherwise. Tip: Options (A) and (B) are algebraically the same random walk written differently – a common NET disguise.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(NET-style) A stationary Gaussian AR(1) process $X_t = \\phi X_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$, $|\\phi|&lt;1$, $\\varepsilon_t \\sim$ i.i.d. $N(0,\\sigma^2)$, is:',opts:['A) Weakly stationary only','B) Both strictly and weakly stationary','C) Ergodic but not stationary','D) Neither strictly nor weakly stationary'],ans:1,exp:'With $|\\phi|&lt;1$, the AR(1) process has time-invariant mean 0, constant variance $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$, and lag-dependent-only autocovariance $\\gamma(h)=\\phi^{|h|}\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$, giving weak stationarity; because the process is Gaussian, weak stationarity here also implies strict stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (A) is incomplete since Gaussianity upgrades weak to strict stationarity; (D) is false under the stability condition $|\\phi|&lt;1$; (C) confuses ergodicity (a separate property) with stationarity. Shortcut: Gaussian AR(1) with $|\\phi|&lt;1$ $\\to$ automatically both strictly and weakly stationary. Tip: $|\\phi|&lt;1$ is the stability/stationarity condition for AR(1) – always check this root condition first.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(NET-style) In an MA(1) process $X_t = \\varepsilon_t + \\theta \\varepsilon_{t-1}$ with $\\varepsilon_t \\sim$ i.i.d. $(0,\\sigma^2)$, the process is:',opts:['A) Non-stationary for any value of $\\theta$','B) Stationary only when $|\\theta|&lt;1$','C) Stationary only when $\\theta=0$','D) Stationary for all finite values of $\\theta$'],ans:3,exp:'Finite-order MA processes with finite coefficients are always weakly stationary because they are finite linear combinations of a stationary white noise sequence, regardless of the value of $\\theta$ (the $|\\theta|&lt;1$ condition relates to invertibility, not stationarity). Why other options are wrong: (A) is false; (B) confuses the invertibility condition with the stationarity condition; (C) unnecessarily restricts to the degenerate white-noise case. Shortcut: All finite-order MA($q$) processes with finite coefficients are automatically stationary – no parameter restriction needed. Tip: Do not confuse MA stationarity (always holds) with MA invertibility ($|\\theta|&lt;1$) – a classic NET distractor pair.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(NET-style) The process $X_t = Z$ for all $t$, where $Z$ is a single random variable drawn once with $E(Z)=\\mu$, $\\mathrm{Var}(Z)=\\sigma^2&lt;\\infty$, is:',opts:['A) Stationary (strictly) but non-ergodic','B) Non-stationary and non-ergodic','C) Non-stationary but ergodic','D) Stationary and ergodic'],ans:0,exp:'Since $X_t=Z$ for every $t$, the joint distribution never changes with a time shift, so the process is strictly (and weakly) stationary. However, along any single realisation, $X_t$ equals the same constant value of $Z$ forever, so the time average equals that one draw of $Z$ rather than converging to the ensemble mean $\\mu$ – hence it is not ergodic. Why other options are wrong: (B) wrongly denies stationarity; (D) wrongly claims ergodicity; (C) wrongly denies stationarity while claiming ergodicity. Shortcut: This “random constant” process is the textbook example proving stationarity does NOT imply ergodicity. Tip: Memorise this example – it is the single most-repeated counter-example in NET/JRF for stationarity vs ergodicity questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(NET-style) Which statement correctly distinguishes stationarity from ergodicity?',opts:['A) They are mathematically identical concepts','B) Stationarity is a property of the probability distribution over time; ergodicity concerns whether time averages from a single realisation converge to ensemble averages','C) Ergodicity is a necessary condition for stationarity','D) Stationarity concerns a single realisation; ergodicity concerns the whole ensemble'],ans:1,exp:'Stationarity describes invariance of the process\'s distribution (or its moments) over time shifts, an ensemble-level property. Ergodicity concerns whether a single, sufficiently long realisation carries enough information to consistently estimate those ensemble quantities via time averaging. Why other options are wrong: (D) reverses the roles; (A) is false, as shown by the constant-process counter-example; (C) is false – the logical relationship, when it holds, runs the other way (ergodicity is typically discussed for stationary processes, not required for stationarity itself). Shortcut: Stationarity $\\to$ “distribution doesn\'t change.” Ergodicity $\\to$ “one path represents the population.” Tip: Assertion-Reason questions frequently test exactly this distinction – practice explaining it in one sentence each.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(NET-style) For a stationary AR(1) process $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, $|\\phi|&lt;1$, the condition for mean ergodicity is automatically satisfied because:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(0)=0$','B) The process is non-stationary','C) $\\gamma(h)=\\phi^{|h|}\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2) \\to 0$ as $h\\to\\infty$, so the Cesaro average of autocovariances vanishes','D) The process has zero mean only'],ans:2,exp:'Since $|\\phi|&lt;1$, the autocovariance $\\gamma(h)=\\phi^{|h|}\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$ decays geometrically to zero as $h\\to\\infty$; this ensures $\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{h}\\gamma(h)\\to 0$, so $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T)\\to 0$ and the sample mean converges in mean square to the population mean. Why other options are wrong: (A) is false since $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)&gt;0$; (D) is irrelevant to the ergodicity argument; (B) contradicts the given stability condition. Shortcut: Geometric decay of $\\gamma(h)$ $\\Rightarrow$ automatic mean ergodicity for stationary ARMA processes. Tip: Any stationary ARMA process with $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$ is mean ergodic – a shortcut fact for quickly answering AR/MA ergodicity questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(NET-style) Which of the following is TRUE regarding the relationship between weak stationarity and strict stationarity in general (non-Gaussian) processes?',opts:['A) Weak stationarity implies strict stationarity only for discrete processes','B) Weak stationarity always implies strict stationarity','C) Weak stationarity does NOT, in general, imply strict stationarity','D) They are equivalent for all continuous distributions'],ans:2,exp:'Weak stationarity restricts only the first two moments; higher-order moments (skewness, kurtosis structure, etc.) can still vary with $t$ even though the mean, variance and autocovariance do not, so weak stationarity is generally a strictly weaker condition than strict stationarity, except in special cases such as Gaussian processes. Why other options are wrong: (B), (D), and (A) all incorrectly claim a general equivalence that only holds under special distributional assumptions (e.g. Gaussianity). Shortcut: “Weak does not imply Strict, except when Gaussian” – a one-line exception rule to memorise. Tip: Expect this framed as a True/False or Assertion-Reason item pairing the general rule with the Gaussian exception.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(NET-style) The variance of the sample mean $\\bar X_T$ of a weakly stationary process is given by:',opts:['A) $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T) = \\sigma^2/T$ always, regardless of autocorrelation','B) $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T) = \\gamma(0)$ for all $T$','C) $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T)$ cannot be computed for dependent processes','D) $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T) = \\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{h=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\\left(1-\\frac{|h|}{T}\\right)\\gamma(h)$'],ans:3,exp:'Expanding $\\mathrm{Var}\\left(\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^T X_t\\right)$ and collecting terms by lag $h$ gives the weighted Cesaro-type sum of autocovariances shown in (D); this reduces to $\\sigma^2/T$ only in the special case of white noise where $\\gamma(h)=0$ for $h\\neq 0$. Why other options are wrong: (A) holds only for uncorrelated (white noise) data, not general stationary processes; (B) ignores the averaging effect entirely; (C) is false – the formula in (D) applies generally. Shortcut: Memorise the general formula; set $\\gamma(h)=0$ ($h \\ne 0$) to recover the i.i.d. special case $\\sigma^2/T$. Tip: This formula is the backbone of mean-ergodicity proofs and appears repeatedly in JRF numerical questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(NET-style) A deterministic trend process $X_t = \\alpha + \\beta t$ (with $\\beta \\neq 0$, no random component) is:',opts:['A) Both stationary and ergodic','B) Stationary since variance is zero','C) Ergodic but not stationary','D) Non-stationary because the mean $E(X_t)=\\alpha+\\beta t$ depends on $t$'],ans:3,exp:'Although the variance is trivially zero (constant), the mean explicitly depends on $t$ through $\\beta t$, which alone violates the constant-mean requirement of even the weakest (mean) stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (B) wrongly ignores the mean condition; (C) and (A) wrongly assign stationarity/ergodicity to a process whose mean diverges with $t$. Shortcut: Always check the mean function first – if $E(X_t)$ contains $t$ explicitly, the process is immediately non-stationary. Tip: Deterministic trend models require de-trending (not differencing) to induce stationarity – a common NET distinction versus unit-root non-stationarity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(NET-style) Which condition is NOT required for a process to be classified as weakly stationary?',opts:['A) Normality of the marginal distribution','B) Constant mean','C) Finite constant variance','D) Autocovariance depending only on lag'],ans:0,exp:'Weak stationarity is defined purely through the first two moments (mean, variance, autocovariance); no assumption is made about the shape of the marginal distribution, so normality is not required. Why other options are wrong: (B), (C), and (D) are precisely the three defining conditions of weak stationarity. Shortcut: Weak stationarity = moment conditions only; distributional shape (e.g. normality) is irrelevant. Tip: A frequently used “NOT required” trap – always identify which option introduces an extra, unnecessary assumption.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\{X_t\\}$ is weakly stationary with autocovariance $\\gamma(h)$, the autocorrelation function (ACF) $\\rho(h)$ is defined as:',opts:['A) $\\rho(h) = \\gamma(h)/\\gamma(0)$','B) $\\rho(h) = 1/\\gamma(h)$','C) $\\rho(h) = \\gamma(0)/\\gamma(h)$','D) $\\rho(h) = \\gamma(h)$'],ans:0,exp:'The ACF standardises the autocovariance by the process variance $\\gamma(0)=\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$, giving a dimensionless quantity bounded between $-1$ and $1$, with $\\rho(0)=1$. Why other options are wrong: (D) omits the normalisation and is not scale-free; (C) and (B) are algebraically inverted/incorrect forms. Shortcut: ACF is simply the correlation coefficient version of the ACVF – always divide by $\\gamma(0)$. Tip: $\\rho(h)=\\gamma(h)/\\gamma(0)$ is used constantly in Box-Jenkins model identification – know it by heart.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For any weakly stationary process, which inequality must always hold for the autocovariance function?',opts:['A) $\\gamma(h) = \\gamma(0)$ for all $h$','B) $|\\gamma(h)| \\le \\gamma(0)$ for all $h$','C) $\\gamma(h) &gt; \\gamma(0)$ for all $h$','D) $\\gamma(h) &lt; 0$ for all $h \\neq 0$'],ans:1,exp:'By the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality applied to $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h})$, the autocovariance at any lag cannot exceed the variance in absolute value, i.e. $|\\gamma(h)|=|\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h})|\\le\\sqrt{\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)\\mathrm{Var}(X_{t+h})}=\\gamma(0)$. Why other options are wrong: (C) violates the Cauchy-Schwarz bound; (A) would force perfect correlation at every lag, an unrealistic general claim; (D) is false since $\\gamma(h)$ can be positive, negative, or zero depending on the process. Shortcut: $\\gamma(0)$ is always the “ceiling” – no lagged covariance can exceed the variance in magnitude. Tip: This bound is frequently used to disqualify numerically inconsistent options in ACF-based numerical questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is the correct necessary condition (in terms of decay of $\\gamma(h)$) commonly invoked for a stationary process to be ergodic for the mean?',opts:['A) $\\gamma(h)$ must increase with $h$','B) $\\gamma(h) \\to 0$ as $h \\to \\infty$ (short-range/weak dependence)','C) $\\gamma(h)$ must be exactly zero for all $h \\ge 1$','D) $\\gamma(h)$ must equal $\\gamma(0)$ for all $h$'],ans:1,exp:'If dependence between observations dies out as the time lag increases (i.e. $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$), the Cesaro average of autocovariances also vanishes, which is sufficient to establish mean-square convergence of the sample mean to the population mean – i.e. mean ergodicity. Why other options are wrong: (C) is a much stronger, unnecessary requirement satisfied only by white noise; (A) would prevent ergodicity, not ensure it; (D) describes perfect long-run dependence, incompatible with ergodicity. Shortcut: “Memory fades $\\Rightarrow$ ergodic.” Persistent or growing dependence blocks ergodicity. Tip: This is the qualitative version of the Cesaro-sum condition – useful for quick conceptual (non-numerical) questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following processes is stationary but generally considered non-ergodic?',opts:['A) White noise $\\varepsilon_t \\sim$ i.i.d. $(0,\\sigma^2)$','B) $X_t = \\mu + Z$, where $Z$ is drawn once and held fixed for all $t$, superimposed on a deterministic constant $\\mu$','C) A stable AR(1) process with $|\\phi|&lt;1$','D) An MA(1) process with finite $\\theta$'],ans:1,exp:'As with the earlier random-constant example, $X_t=\\mu+Z$ has a joint distribution invariant under time shifts (hence stationary), but every realisation is trapped at the single value $\\mu+Z$ forever, so the time average reflects only the one random draw $Z$ rather than converging to the true ensemble mean – this is the classical non-ergodic-but-stationary example. Why other options are wrong: (A), (C), and (D) are all standard weakly dependent processes whose autocovariances vanish (or are identically zero beyond a finite lag), making them both stationary and ergodic. Shortcut: Any process built by adding time-invariant randomness with no time-decaying dependence structure is a strong candidate for non-ergodicity. Tip: Expect this exact process (or a close variant) in Statement-Based and Assertion-Reason NET questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a covariance-stationary process, which of the following is TRUE about the spectral density function $f(\\omega)$?',opts:['A) It is undefined for stationary processes','B) It equals $\\gamma(0)$ for every frequency $\\omega$','C) It exists only for non-stationary processes','D) It is the Fourier transform of the autocovariance function $\\gamma(h)$'],ans:3,exp:'For a weakly stationary process with summable autocovariances, the spectral density is defined as $f(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)e^{-i\\omega h}$, the Fourier transform of the autocovariance sequence, providing a frequency-domain representation of the process. Why other options are wrong: (C) reverses the requirement – spectral density theory requires (weak) stationarity; (B) is true only for white noise, a special flat-spectrum case; (A) is simply false. Shortcut: Time domain (ACVF) $\\leftrightarrow$ Frequency domain (spectral density) via Fourier transform – a key NET/JRF bridge concept. Tip: Spectral density questions almost always assume weak stationarity as a prerequisite – a useful check when evaluating options.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement about testing for stationarity in practice is correct?',opts:['A) The ADF test directly tests for ergodicity','B) Stationarity can only be verified visually, never statistically','C) The Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF) test is used to test for the presence of a unit root (non-stationarity)','D) The ADF test confirms strict stationarity'],ans:2,exp:'The ADF test has the null hypothesis that the series has a unit root (i.e. is non-stationary, typically a random-walk-type process); rejecting the null provides evidence in favour of (weak/covariance) stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (A) is false – ergodicity is not what unit-root tests examine; (B) is false since formal statistical tests (ADF, KPSS, Phillips-Perron) exist; (D) overstates the test\'s scope, which concerns second-order (weak) stationarity, not the full joint distribution. Shortcut: ADF null = “unit root present” = non-stationary; reject null $\\Rightarrow$ evidence of stationarity. Tip: A very frequently repeated NET applied-econometrics fact linking stationarity theory to testing procedures.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best describes why ergodicity is important in time series estimation?',opts:['A) It justifies using a single observed time series to consistently estimate population parameters like the mean and autocovariance','B) It guarantees the process has zero mean','C) It ensures the process is always Gaussian','D) It eliminates the need for stationarity'],ans:0,exp:'In practice, we typically observe only one realisation of a time series (not repeated independent realisations of the ensemble). Ergodicity justifies treating time averages from this single path as consistent estimators of the underlying population (ensemble) moments. Why other options are wrong: (B) is unrelated to ergodicity; (C) is false, ergodicity has no bearing on distributional shape; (D) is false since ergodicity discussions presuppose (and build upon) stationarity. Shortcut: Ergodicity is the theoretical justification for time-series estimation from a single sample path. Tip: This applied-relevance angle is a favourite short-answer/explanation-type NET question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider $X_t = \\mu + \\varepsilon_t + \\theta \\varepsilon_{t-1}$, $\\varepsilon_t \\sim$ i.i.d. $(0,\\sigma^2)$. This process is:',opts:['A) Weakly stationary for every finite $\\theta$, and ergodic (since $\\gamma(h)=0$ for $h\\ge 2$)','B) Non-ergodic regardless of $\\theta$','C) Non-stationary for $|\\theta| &gt; 1$','D) Stationary but never ergodic'],ans:0,exp:'As a finite-order MA(1) process, it is weakly stationary for any finite $\\theta$; its autocovariance is nonzero only at lags 0 and 1 and zero for $h\\ge 2$, so the Cesaro average of autocovariances vanishes as $T\\to\\infty$, guaranteeing mean ergodicity. Why other options are wrong: (C) confuses stationarity with the (irrelevant here) invertibility condition; (B) and (D) incorrectly deny ergodicity despite the rapidly vanishing autocovariance structure. Shortcut: All finite-order MA processes are automatically both stationary and (mean) ergodic. Tip: Finite MA processes are “safe” stationary-and-ergodic answers whenever they appear as an option.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A sequence of exchangeable (but not independent) random variables with identical finite variance is:',opts:['A) Weakly stationary but never strictly stationary','B) Strictly stationary (since exchangeability implies invariance of the joint distribution under permutation, which includes time shifts)','C) Always non-stationary','D) Ergodic for the mean under all circumstances'],ans:1,exp:'Exchangeability means the joint distribution is invariant under any permutation of indices, which in particular includes the shifts required for strict stationarity, so an exchangeable sequence is automatically strictly stationary (and weakly stationary if variances are finite). Why other options are wrong: (C) is false; (A) reverses the correct implication – exchangeability gives the stronger strict stationarity, not merely weak; (D) is false in general – exchangeable but dependent sequences (e.g. mixtures) can fail to be mean ergodic (a classical example is a sequence conditionally i.i.d. given a random parameter). Shortcut: Exchangeable $\\Rightarrow$ strictly stationary; but exchangeable does NOT automatically imply ergodic. Tip: This is a JRF-level nuance – exchangeability is a stronger structural symmetry than stationarity requires, but still does not guarantee ergodicity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\{X_t\\}$ and $\\{Y_t\\}$ are both independent weakly stationary processes, then $\\{X_t + Y_t\\}$ is:',opts:['A) Weakly stationary, with autocovariance $\\gamma_{X+Y}(h) = \\gamma_X(h) + \\gamma_Y(h)$','B) Strictly stationary only if both are Gaussian','C) Stationary only if $X_t = Y_t$','D) Never stationary'],ans:0,exp:'Since $X_t$ and $Y_t$ are independent, $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t+Y_t, X_{t+h}+Y_{t+h}) = \\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h}) + \\mathrm{Cov}(Y_t,Y_{t+h})$ (cross terms vanish by independence), and the mean $E(X_t+Y_t)=\\mu_X+\\mu_Y$ remains constant, so the sum inherits weak stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (D) is false, as shown above; (B) unnecessarily restricts to Gaussianity for weak stationarity of the sum (though it would additionally be needed for strict stationarity if the components are non-Gaussian, this is not required for weak stationarity); (C) is an irrelevant, overly restrictive condition. Shortcut: For independent stationary components, autocovariances simply add: $\\gamma_{X+Y}(h)=\\gamma_X(h)+\\gamma_Y(h)$. Tip: This additive-autocovariance property is often used in JRF derivations involving signal-plus-noise decompositions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly describes the role of mixing conditions (e.g. strong mixing, $\\alpha$-mixing) in establishing ergodicity for a stationary process?',opts:['A) Mixing conditions quantify the rate at which dependence between $X_t$ and $X_{t+h}$ decays as $h\\to\\infty$, and sufficiently fast mixing implies ergodicity','B) Mixing conditions are only defined for non-stationary processes','C) Mixing conditions are irrelevant to ergodicity','D) Mixing conditions guarantee strict stationarity but not ergodicity'],ans:0,exp:'Mixing conditions formalise “asymptotic independence”: as the lag $h\\to\\infty$, $X_t$ and $X_{t+h}$ become approximately independent at a specified rate. Under a stationary process satisfying an appropriate mixing condition (ensuring dependence decays fast enough), a version of the ergodic theorem (or a law of large numbers for dependent sequences) applies, establishing that time averages converge to ensemble averages. Why other options are wrong: (C) is false; (B) is false, mixing conditions are typically studied for stationary processes; (D) reverses the relevant implication – mixing is used precisely to establish ergodic-type convergence, not merely stationarity. Shortcut: “Mixing = quantified fading memory,” the technical machinery behind establishing ergodicity rigorously. Tip: JRF-level questions may reference $\\alpha$-mixing or $\\phi$-mixing by name – know that they are sufficient (not necessary) conditions for ergodic behaviour.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Let $\\{X_t\\}$ be a stationary process such that $X_t = \\mu + Y_t$, where $Y_t = (-1)^t W$, and $W$ is a random variable with $E(W)=0$, $\\mathrm{Var}(W)=\\sigma_W^2$, held fixed for all $t$. This process is:',opts:['A) Both stationary and ergodic','B) Weakly stationary (mean $\\mu$, constant variance $\\sigma_W^2$, and $\\gamma(h)$ depending only on the parity of $h$) but non-ergodic','C) Not stationary because the sign alternates with $t$','D) Non-stationary and non-ergodic'],ans:1,exp:'Although $Y_t$ flips sign with $t$, $E(X_t)=\\mu$ for all $t$ (since $E(W)=0$), $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma_W^2$ for all $t$, and $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h})=(-1)^h\\sigma_W^2$, which depends only on $h$ – so it satisfies weak stationarity conditions. However, because $W$ is drawn once and never varies with $t$, a single realisation\'s time average of $Y_t$ does not average out to the ensemble value $0$ in the way an i.i.d. sequence would over infinitely many independent draws – the dependence never fades, so the process fails to be ergodic. Why other options are wrong: (C) misapplies the deterministic-sign alternation as a stationarity violation without checking the actual moment conditions; (A) wrongly asserts ergodicity; (D) wrongly denies the (correctly verified) weak stationarity. Shortcut: Whenever a process is built around a single random draw “frozen” across all $t$ (regardless of any deterministic time-varying multiplier), suspect non-ergodicity even if weak stationarity conditions check out. Tip: This is a classic JRF trap combining a deterministic alternating pattern with a single random shock – always verify moment conditions algebraically rather than relying on intuition about the deterministic part.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the measure-theoretic formulation, a strictly stationary process is defined via a measure-preserving transformation $T$ on a probability space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},P)$ such that $X_t(\\omega) = X_0(T^t\\omega)$. The process is ergodic if:',opts:['A) $T$ is invertible','B) $T$ preserves the mean of $X_0$','C) $X_0$ is Gaussian','D) Every $T$-invariant event $A \\in \\mathcal{F}$ (i.e. $T^{-1}A = A$) has probability $0$ or $1$'],ans:3,exp:'In ergodic theory, ergodicity of the measure-preserving transformation $T$ (equivalently, of the process it generates) is defined by triviality of the invariant $\\sigma$-field: any event unaffected by the shift $T$ must have probability exactly $0$ or $1$. This 0-1 law is what rules out “mixture” processes like the random-constant example, where the invariant event “$Z \\in$ some set” can have intermediate probability. Why other options are wrong: (A) invertibility of $T$ is a separate structural property, not the definition of ergodicity; (B) is far too weak a condition; (C) Gaussianity is unrelated to ergodicity. Shortcut: “Ergodic = invariant events are trivial (probability 0 or 1).” Tip: This measure-theoretic definition connects directly to the Birkhoff Ergodic Theorem – a high-value JRF theory topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Birkhoff Ergodic Theorem states that for a stationary and ergodic process $\\{X_t\\}$ with $E|X_0| &lt; \\infty$:',opts:['A) $\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}X_t \\to E(X_0)$ almost surely as $T \\to \\infty$','B) $X_T \\to E(X_0)$ almost surely as $T \\to \\infty$ (individual terms converge, not the average)','C) $\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}X_t \\to 0$ almost surely, regardless of the mean','D) The theorem applies only to i.i.d. sequences'],ans:0,exp:'The Birkhoff (pointwise) Ergodic Theorem generalises the strong law of large numbers: for a stationary, ergodic process with finite absolute mean, the time average along almost every sample path converges almost surely to the (constant) ensemble expectation $E(X_0)$. Why other options are wrong: (C) ignores the actual limiting value, which is $E(X_0)$, not necessarily zero; (B) misstates the theorem, which is about the running average, not individual terms; (D) is false – i.i.d. sequences are only a special (trivially ergodic) case; the theorem is far more general. Shortcut: “Birkhoff = SLLN for stationary ergodic processes.” Tip: A guaranteed high-value JRF theory fact – know the exact statement and that it requires BOTH stationarity AND ergodicity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Without the ergodicity assumption, the Birkhoff Ergodic Theorem still guarantees almost sure convergence of $\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}X_t$, but to what limit?',opts:['A) The theorem fails to hold without ergodicity','B) Always to $E(X_0)$','C) To zero','D) To a random variable $E(X_0 \\mid \\mathcal{I})$, the conditional expectation given the invariant $\\sigma$-field $\\mathcal{I}$ (which reduces to $E(X_0)$ only when $\\mathcal{I}$ is trivial, i.e. when ergodic)'],ans:3,exp:'For a general stationary (not necessarily ergodic) process, the time average converges almost surely, but its limit is the conditional expectation of $X_0$ given the invariant $\\sigma$-field $\\mathcal{I}$ – a random variable in general. Only when the process is ergodic (so $\\mathcal{I}$ is trivial) does this random limit collapse to the deterministic constant $E(X_0)$. Why other options are wrong: (B) is true only under ergodicity, the special case; (C) is arbitrary and incorrect; (A) is false – the theorem holds for any stationary process, ergodicity only determines the nature of the limit. Shortcut: Stationarity alone $\\Rightarrow$ time average converges a.s. to some (possibly random) limit; add ergodicity $\\Rightarrow$ that limit becomes the constant $E(X_0)$. Tip: This is the “ergodic decomposition” idea – a genuinely research-level JRF fact that distinguishes strong students.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider a stationary process constructed by choosing, with probability $\\frac12$ each, one of two independent stationary ergodic AR(1) processes (with different parameters) and then observing that chosen process for all $t$. The resulting mixture process is:',opts:['A) Stationary and ergodic','B) Ergodic but not stationary','C) Stationary but NOT ergodic, since the invariant event “which AR(1) was chosen” has probability $\\tfrac12$, not $0$ or $1$','D) Non-stationary'],ans:2,exp:'Mixing two stationary ergodic processes according to a random (but time-invariant) selection preserves overall stationarity of the mixture (the marginal joint distributions are still time-shift invariant when averaged over the mixing randomness), but the event “process 1 was selected” is an invariant event with probability $\\tfrac12$, violating the 0-1 law required for ergodicity. Why other options are wrong: (A) wrongly claims ergodicity despite the nontrivial invariant event; (D) is false – the mixture remains stationary; (B) is internally inconsistent, since ergodicity is discussed relative to a stationary process. Shortcut: “Random mixtures of distinct ergodic processes are stationary but not ergodic” – a standard construction for counter-examples. Tip: This is the essence of the ergodic decomposition theorem: every stationary process is a mixture of ergodic components.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A fractionally integrated process $I(d)$ with $0 &lt; d &lt; 0.5$ has autocovariances decaying at a hyperbolic (not exponential) rate, $\\gamma(h) \\sim c\\, h^{2d-1}$ as $h \\to \\infty$. Such a process is:',opts:['A) Weakly stationary and mean-ergodic, since $\\gamma(h) \\to 0$, but exhibits long memory (autocovariances are not absolutely summable)','B) Only defined for $d \\ge 1$','C) Weakly stationary but never ergodic','D) Non-stationary for all $d&gt;0$'],ans:0,exp:'For $0&lt;d&lt;0.5$, the process is weakly stationary (variance is finite, autocovariances well-defined), and since $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$ as $h\\to\\infty$ (even though only hyperbolically, not exponentially), the Cesaro average of autocovariances still vanishes, giving mean ergodicity. However, $\\sum_h |\\gamma(h)| = \\infty$ (non-summable), which is the hallmark of long memory / long-range dependence. Why other options are wrong: (D) is false for $0&lt;d&lt;0.5$ (stationarity requires $d&lt;0.5$, non-stationarity/long-memory-nonstationary occurs for $d \\ge 0.5$); (C) wrongly denies ergodicity despite $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$; (B) misstates the valid range for stationarity. Shortcut: Long memory $\\ne$ non-ergodic; only requires $\\gamma(h) \\to 0$, not summability, for mean ergodicity. Tip: A high-difficulty JRF topic linking stationarity/ergodicity theory to long-memory (ARFIMA) models – distinguishing $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$ (needed for ergodicity) from $\\sum|\\gamma(h)|&lt;\\infty$ (needed for asymptotic normality of $\\bar X_T$) is a key nuance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A process $X_t = \\mu + \\varepsilon_t$ where $\\varepsilon_t$ is stationary but exhibits perfect long-run dependence such that $\\gamma(h) = \\sigma^2$ (constant, not decaying) for all $h$, is:',opts:['A) Weakly stationary but NOT mean-ergodic, since $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T) \\to \\sigma^2 \\neq 0$','B) Weakly stationary and ergodic','C) Non-stationary','D) Impossible to construct'],ans:0,exp:'With $\\gamma(h)=\\sigma^2$ for every $h$ (this occurs, for example, if $\\varepsilon_t = \\varepsilon_0$ for all $t$, a special case of the earlier random-constant example), $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T)=\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_h\\left(1-\\frac{|h|}{T}\\right)\\sigma^2 \\to \\sigma^2 \\neq 0$, so the sample mean\'s variance never vanishes and mean ergodicity fails, even though the process is trivially weakly stationary. Why other options are wrong: (B) wrongly claims ergodicity; (C) is false, the mean and variance are both time-invariant; (D) is false, this is exactly the earlier random-constant construction. Shortcut: If $\\gamma(h)$ does not decay to $0$, mean ergodicity generally fails – check $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T)$ directly. Tip: This links back to Q6/B6 (random constant example) but tests the same idea via the autocovariance function directly – expect JRF to test the same concept from multiple angles.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Suppose $\\{X_t\\}$ is weakly stationary and mean ergodic, with sample mean $\\bar X_T \\xrightarrow{m.s.} \\mu$. Which additional condition ensures $\\bar X_T$ is also a consistent estimator of $\\mu$ in the usual (convergence-in-probability) sense used for statistical inference?',opts:['A) None – mean-square convergence already implies convergence in probability','B) $\\gamma(h)$ must be exactly zero for $h \\geq 1$','C) The process must additionally be Gaussian','D) The process must be strictly stationary'],ans:0,exp:'Convergence in mean square (i.e. $E[(\\bar X_T - \\mu)^2] \\to 0$) implies convergence in probability by Chebyshev\'s inequality: $P(|\\bar X_T-\\mu|&gt;\\epsilon) \\le E[(\\bar X_T-\\mu)^2]/\\epsilon^2 \\to 0$. So mean ergodicity in the mean-square sense already suffices for the usual notion of consistency. Why other options are wrong: (C), (D), and (B) all impose unnecessary additional structure beyond what mean-square convergence already guarantees. Shortcut: Mean-square convergence $\\Rightarrow$ convergence in probability (via Chebyshev) – no extra assumptions needed. Tip: JRF often tests the hierarchy of convergence modes (a.s. $\\Rightarrow$ in probability; m.s. $\\Rightarrow$ in probability) in the context of ergodicity – revise convergence concepts together with this unit.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a weakly stationary process, covariance ergodicity for a fixed lag $h$ (i.e., $\\hat\\gamma(h) \\xrightarrow{m.s.} \\gamma(h)$) can be established by applying mean ergodicity conditions to the auxiliary process $W_t = (X_t - \\mu)(X_{t+h} - \\mu)$, provided that:',opts:['A) $W_t$ is itself (at least approximately) weakly stationary with autocovariances that decay to $0$, which typically requires finiteness of fourth-order moments/cumulants of $\\{X_t\\}$','B) $X_t$ is strictly stationary but need not have finite variance','C) $X_t$ has zero mean only','D) No additional condition is needed beyond weak stationarity of $X_t$'],ans:0,exp:'Since $W_t$ involves a product of two (shifted) values of $X_t$, establishing that $W_t$ itself satisfies a mean-ergodicity-type condition (its own autocovariances vanishing in Cesaro average) generally requires control over fourth-order moments/cumulants of the original process $X_t$, beyond the second-order conditions used for weak stationarity of $X_t$ itself. Why other options are wrong: (C) is insufficient – a zero mean alone says nothing about the dependence structure of $W_t$; (B) is inconsistent, since finite variance is required just to define $\\gamma(h)$ in the first place; (D) understates the additional structure genuinely needed. Shortcut: Covariance ergodicity is a “second-order-of-second-order” condition – naturally requires fourth-moment control. Tip: A genuinely advanced JRF/research-level nuance – useful for questions contrasting mean ergodicity (needs 2nd moments) with covariance ergodicity (needs 4th moments).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly ranks the strength of these properties, from weakest to strongest, for a process with finite fourth moments?',opts:['A) Ergodicity $&lt;$ Stationarity $&lt;$ Mixing','B) Strict stationarity $&lt;$ Ergodicity $&lt;$ Weak stationarity','C) Mean stationarity $&lt;$ Weak stationarity $&lt;$ Strict stationarity; and (separately) Ergodicity is generally a distinct axis, not nested within this chain','D) All four properties form one single linear hierarchy'],ans:2,exp:'Mean, weak, and strict stationarity form a genuine nested hierarchy (each stronger condition implies the weaker ones, under suitable moment conditions). Ergodicity, however, is a fundamentally different (orthogonal) property concerned with time-average convergence rather than distributional invariance – a process can be strictly stationary yet non-ergodic (random-constant example), so ergodicity does not simply sit inside the stationarity hierarchy. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (B) construct a false single hierarchy including ergodicity and mixing incorrectly; (D) is false precisely because ergodicity is not comparable in strength to the stationarity conditions in a simple linear way. Shortcut: Draw two separate axes: (i) Mean $\\to$ Weak $\\to$ Strict stationarity (nested); (ii) Ergodicity/Mixing (a separate “memory-decay” axis). Tip: This conceptual-map question format is common in JRF to test whether students truly understand that ergodicity $\\neq$ “a stronger form of stationarity.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A seasonal random walk $X_t = X_{t-s} + \\varepsilon_t$ (with seasonal period $s$, $\\varepsilon_t$ i.i.d. $(0,\\sigma^2)$) is:',opts:['A) Weakly stationary but not strictly stationary','B) Non-stationary, since $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ grows without bound as $t \\to \\infty$ (a seasonal unit root)','C) Stationary because of the seasonal structure','D) Ergodic despite being non-stationary'],ans:1,exp:'Analogous to the standard random walk, repeatedly substituting shows $X_t$ accumulates shocks over multiples of the seasonal lag $s$, so $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ grows linearly in the number of seasonal cycles elapsed, violating the constant-variance requirement – this is a “seasonal unit root,” requiring seasonal differencing $(1-L^s)$ to induce stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (C) incorrectly assumes seasonality alone implies stationarity; (D) is a category error – ergodicity discussions presuppose stationarity; (A) wrongly claims weak stationarity despite the diverging variance. Shortcut: Any recursive process of the form $X_t = X_{t-k}+\\varepsilon_t$ (ordinary or seasonal) is a unit-root process – always check variance accumulation. Tip: Seasonal unit roots and seasonal differencing $(1-L^s)X_t$ are a recurring applied time-series JRF topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\{X_t\\}$ is a strictly stationary and ergodic process, and $g(\\cdot)$ is any measurable function such that $E|g(X_t,X_{t+1},\\dots,X_{t+k})| &lt; \\infty$, then the process $Y_t = g(X_t,\\dots,X_{t+k})$ is:',opts:['A) Also strictly stationary and ergodic','B) Neither stationary nor ergodic in general','C) Strictly stationary but never ergodic','D) Only weakly stationary'],ans:0,exp:'This is a standard and powerful invariance result in ergodic theory: any measurable transformation of a (block of a) strictly stationary and ergodic process is again strictly stationary and ergodic, since $Y_t$ inherits its distributional shift-invariance and the invariant-$\\sigma$-field triviality from the underlying process $X_t$. Why other options are wrong: (B), (C), and (D) all understate the strength of this preservation property. Shortcut: “Measurable functions of stationary ergodic processes remain stationary and ergodic” – a powerful general-purpose JRF fact. Tip: This result justifies why sample autocovariances, sample ACFs, and many nonlinear functionals of a stationary ergodic series are themselves consistently estimable – a key theoretical underpinning of time-series inference.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following statements is FALSE?',opts:['A) Every weakly stationary Gaussian process is strictly stationary','B) Every ergodic process is strictly stationary','C) Every strictly stationary process with finite variance is weakly stationary','D) Every i.i.d. sequence with finite variance is strictly stationary and ergodic'],ans:1,exp:'Statement (B) is false as stated in isolation: “ergodic” is typically defined and discussed for processes already assumed stationary; the notion of ergodicity does not, by itself and without the stationarity context, guarantee (or presuppose) strict stationarity in a way that makes this a meaningful “implication.” More importantly, in standard usage ergodicity is examined given stationarity, not as a stand-alone property implying it – so treating “ergodic $\\Rightarrow$ strictly stationary” as a blanket true statement is incorrect and misleading. Why Other Options Are True: (D) i.i.d. sequences trivially satisfy joint-distribution invariance under time shift and satisfy the 0-1 law for tail/invariant events (via Kolmogorov\'s 0-1 law); (C) shown earlier (finite variance upgrades distributional to moment invariance); (A) shown earlier (Gaussian equivalence result). Shortcut: When asked to identify a FALSE statement among several stationarity/ergodicity facts, check for reversed or unjustified implications first – especially any claim that ergodicity alone implies stationarity. Tip: “Identify the FALSE statement” items are common in JRF – always verify each option against the precise definitions rather than intuition.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Let $\\{X_t\\}$ be a stationary Markov chain on a finite state space with transition matrix $P$. The chain (and the induced stationary process) is ergodic if and only if:',opts:['A) $P$ is symmetric','B) The chain is irreducible and aperiodic (so that $P$ has a unique stationary distribution and the chain mixes), ensuring the invariant $\\sigma$-field is trivial','C) All entries of $P$ are equal','D) The chain has an absorbing state'],ans:1,exp:'For a finite-state Markov chain started in its (unique) stationary distribution, irreducibility and aperiodicity guarantee a unique stationary distribution and convergence of $n$-step transition probabilities, which together imply that the invariant events of the induced stationary process are trivial – i.e., the process is ergodic. Why other options are wrong: (A) symmetry is unrelated to ergodicity; (C) is an unnecessarily extreme special case (equivalent to i.i.d. uniform transitions); (D) an absorbing state typically creates multiple stationary distributions (one degenerate at each absorbing state), which would violate ergodicity, not ensure it. Shortcut: “Irreducible + Aperiodic = Ergodic Markov chain” – a standard result bridging Markov chain theory with time-series ergodicity. Tip: This links Unit on Markov Chains with the Stationarity/Ergodicity unit – a frequent interdisciplinary JRF question type.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A near-unit-root process $X_t = \\phi_T X_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$, where $\\phi_T = 1 - c/T$ for a fixed constant $c&gt;0$ and sample size $T$, is best described (for finite $T$) as:',opts:['A) Technically stationary for each fixed finite $T$ (since $|\\phi_T|&lt;1$), but its properties approach those of the non-stationary unit-root case as $T \\to \\infty$, making standard fixed-$T$ asymptotic theory unreliable','B) Strictly non-stationary for all $T$','C) Ergodic uniformly in $T$ with ergodicity properties unaffected by $c$','D) Exactly stationary for every $T$, with stationarity properties identical to the case $c=0$'],ans:0,exp:'For any fixed finite $T$, since $|\\phi_T|=|1-c/T|&lt;1$, the process is technically a stable (stationary) AR(1); however, as the sample size $T$ grows, $\\phi_T \\to 1$, so the process\'s behaviour (variance, persistence, ergodic properties) increasingly resembles the non-stationary unit-root case, which is precisely the motivation for local-to-unity asymptotic theory in econometrics. Why other options are wrong: (D) ignores the drift of $\\phi_T$ toward unity as $T$ grows; (B) is false for finite $T$; (C) wrongly claims uniformity in $c$, when in fact the rate of approach to the unit-root boundary depends directly on $c$. Shortcut: Local-to-unity models are stationary in a strict textbook sense for each finite $T$, but their asymptotic (large-$T$) behaviour mimics non-stationarity – a nuanced JRF concept. Tip: This connects to advanced econometric theory (local-to-unity asymptotics, Ornstein-Uhlenbeck limiting processes) – a genuinely research-level JRF topic occasionally tested in applied econometrics-heavy NET papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement correctly relates strong mixing ($\\alpha$-mixing) to ergodicity for a stationary process?',opts:['A) Strong mixing (with mixing coefficients $\\alpha(h) \\to 0$) is a sufficient (but not necessary) condition for ergodicity','B) Strong mixing is strictly weaker than ergodicity (ergodicity implies strong mixing)','C) Strong mixing and ergodicity are mutually exclusive','D) Strong mixing applies only to non-stationary processes'],ans:0,exp:'If $\\alpha(h) \\to 0$ as $h \\to \\infty$ (strong/$\\alpha$-mixing), then events far apart in time become asymptotically independent, which is sufficient to establish that the invariant $\\sigma$-field is trivial, hence ergodicity. However, there exist ergodic processes that are not mixing in this quantitative sense (e.g. certain deterministic ergodic dynamical systems, or processes with slowly-varying dependence structures that are still ergodic without satisfying a formal mixing condition), so mixing is sufficient but not necessary for ergodicity. Why other options are wrong: (B) reverses the correct direction of implication; (C) is false, they frequently coincide; (D) is false, mixing conditions are defined precisely for stationary processes. Shortcut: Mixing $\\Rightarrow$ Ergodic, but not conversely – keep this one-directional arrow firmly in mind. Tip: JRF sometimes tests this exact one-directional implication in Assertion-Reason format – a subtle but well-defined and testable fact.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “A process with zero autocorrelation at all non-zero lags is always stationary.” This statement is:',opts:['A) True, since zero autocorrelation is sufficient for stationarity','B) True only for discrete-time processes','C) True only for Gaussian processes','D) False in general – the mean and/or variance could still vary with $t$ even if the process is uncorrelated across time'],ans:3,exp:'Zero autocorrelation at all non-zero lags addresses only the third stationarity condition (covariance structure). A process could still have a time-varying mean (e.g. $X_t = \\beta t + \\varepsilon_t$ with uncorrelated $\\varepsilon_t$) or time-varying variance and would then fail even weak stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (A) mistakes one necessary condition for a sufficient one; (C) and (B) introduce irrelevant restrictions that do not resolve the underlying logical gap. Shortcut: Always check ALL THREE weak-stationarity conditions (mean, variance, covariance) – satisfying only one is never enough. Tip: A very common trap: students see “uncorrelated” and jump to “stationary” without checking the mean/variance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “If a process is weakly stationary, it must also be ergodic.” This statement is:',opts:['A) True only if the mean is zero','B) False – weak stationarity alone does not guarantee ergodicity (e.g. the random-constant process)','C) True only for AR processes','D) Always true'],ans:1,exp:'Stationarity (weak or strict) and ergodicity are logically distinct properties; ergodicity additionally requires that dependence between distant observations fade out (or, more precisely, that invariant events be trivial), which is not implied merely by having time-invariant moments. Why other options are wrong: (D), (A), and (C) all wrongly assert or restrict an implication that does not hold in general. Shortcut: Stationarity $\\ne$ Ergodicity; always keep the random-constant counter-example ready to disprove this trap. Tip: This exact trap statement (or its converse) appears extremely frequently in NET/JRF – both directions of confusion are commonly tested.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Ergodicity implies stationarity.” Evaluate this claim.',opts:['A) True, ergodicity is a stronger property that automatically includes stationarity','B) False, since ergodic processes are always non-stationary','C) True only for continuous-time processes','D) Misleading – ergodicity is conventionally defined and analysed only in the context of an already (strictly) stationary process; it is not a stand-alone property applied to arbitrary non-stationary processes in the standard theory covered at NET/JRF level'],ans:3,exp:'In the standard time-series curriculum, ergodicity is a property discussed about a stationary process (does the time average of this already-stationary process converge to its ensemble average?). It is not typically presented as an independent condition that, on its own, forces stationarity onto an otherwise arbitrary process. Why other options are wrong: (A) overstates the logical relationship in the wrong direction relative to how the concept is used; (B) is simply false; (C) introduces an irrelevant restriction. Shortcut: Treat “ergodic” as an adjective that modifies “stationary process,” not as an independent, self-sufficient category. Tip: JRF sometimes phrases the two properties as though testing a clean bidirectional relationship – neither direction (stationary $\\Leftrightarrow$ ergodic) holds in general.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “All finite-variance stationary Gaussian processes are automatically ergodic.” Is this TRUE or FALSE?',opts:['A) True always','B) False in general – Gaussianity plus weak stationarity gives strict stationarity, but ergodicity additionally requires $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$ (or a related mixing/decay condition); a Gaussian process with non-decaying autocovariance (e.g. the random-constant example with $W\\sim N(0,\\sigma^2)$) is stationary but not ergodic','C) True only for AR(1) processes with $\\phi=0$','D) False, because Gaussian processes are never ergodic'],ans:1,exp:'Gaussianity resolves the strict-vs-weak stationarity gap, but it does nothing to address whether the dependence structure decays – a Gaussian analogue of the random-constant example (e.g. $X_t = \\mu + W$, $W \\sim N(0,\\sigma^2)$, fixed across $t$) is a perfectly valid Gaussian stationary process that is still not ergodic, since $\\gamma(h)=\\sigma^2$ for all $h$ does not decay. Why other options are wrong: (A) wrongly conflates the Gaussian stationarity equivalence with ergodicity; (D) overcorrects into an equally false blanket denial (many Gaussian processes, like Gaussian white noise or stationary Gaussian AR/MA models, ARE ergodic); (C) is an arbitrary, overly narrow restriction. Shortcut: Gaussianity fixes the strict-vs-weak stationarity gap ONLY – it says nothing about the decay of dependence needed for ergodicity. Tip: A subtle but high-value JRF trap combining two separate equivalence results (Gaussian stationarity equivalence vs. ergodicity conditions) into one deliberately confusing statement.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “A process observed to have a roughly constant sample mean and sample variance over a plotted time series graph is definitely stationary.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) False – visual inspection is only a rough, informal indicator; formal statistical tests (e.g. ADF, KPSS) and theoretical moment derivations are needed to establish stationarity rigorously','B) False, because stationarity cannot be assessed at all from real data','C) True, visual constancy is a rigorous proof of stationarity','D) True only for monthly data'],ans:0,exp:'A time plot can suggest the absence of obvious trend or heteroscedasticity, but it cannot rigorously establish constant moments or a lag-only-dependent autocovariance structure; formal hypothesis tests or explicit derivations from the assumed data-generating process are required for a defensible stationarity conclusion. Why other options are wrong: (C) overstates the rigor of a purely visual check; (D) introduces an irrelevant restriction; (B) overcorrects – formal tests DO exist and are regularly used. Shortcut: “Looks stationary” $\\ne$ “is proven stationary” – always distinguish informal visual checks from formal statistical/theoretical verification. Tip: NET applied-statistics questions frequently test this distinction between graphical intuition and rigorous testing.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Since $E(X_t)=0$ for the random walk $X_t = X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ (started at $X_0=0$), the random walk must be stationary.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) False, because $E(X_t)$ is not actually zero','B) False – satisfying the mean condition alone is not sufficient; the variance $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=t\\sigma^2$ still depends on $t$, violating weak stationarity','C) True, because $E(X_t)=0$ implies zero variance','D) True, since the mean condition is satisfied'],ans:1,exp:'Although the mean stationarity condition happens to hold trivially here ($E(X_t)=0$ for all $t$), stationarity requires ALL relevant moment conditions to hold; the variance $t\\sigma^2$ clearly grows with $t$, so the process fails weak (and hence strict) stationarity despite having a constant mean. Why other options are wrong: (D) commits the classic partial-condition fallacy; (C) is a non sequitur – a zero mean says nothing about the variance; (A) is factually incorrect, $E(X_t)=0$ is correct here. Shortcut: Never conclude stationarity from the mean condition alone – always check variance and covariance too. Tip: This exact “mean is zero, so it must be stationary” trap is one of the most common NET distractor patterns for the random walk.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Every strictly stationary process automatically has finite variance.” Evaluate this claim.',opts:['A) True only for Gaussian processes','B) False – strict stationarity constrains only the joint distribution\'s shape/invariance, not the existence of moments; a strictly stationary i.i.d. sequence from, say, a Cauchy distribution has undefined variance','C) False, because strictly stationary processes never have finite variance','D) True, strict stationarity always implies finite second moments'],ans:1,exp:'Strict stationarity is a purely distributional invariance condition and places no requirement on the existence of moments. An i.i.d. sequence of Cauchy-distributed random variables is strictly stationary (identical joint distributions under any time shift) but has no finite mean or variance. Why other options are wrong: (D) is the exact misconception being tested; (A) unnecessarily restricts the (already false) claim to Gaussian processes; (C) overcorrects into an equally false blanket denial – many strictly stationary processes DO have finite variance. Shortcut: Strict stationarity = distributional shape invariance; it is entirely silent about moment existence. Tip: The Cauchy-i.i.d. counter-example is a favourite JRF tool for disproving “strict stationarity implies finite variance.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “A process cannot be both non-stationary and ergodic.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) False, all non-stationary processes are ergodic','B) True always, by definition','C) Largely true in the standard curriculum sense, since ergodicity is conventionally defined only relative to an assumed stationary process – however, some generalised (non-stationary) ergodic theorems exist in advanced probability theory outside the standard NET/JRF syllabus','D) False, all processes are simultaneously stationary and ergodic'],ans:2,exp:'At the NET/JRF syllabus level, ergodicity is discussed strictly in the context of stationary processes; while more advanced/generalised ergodic theory exists for broader classes of processes, for exam purposes the safe and expected answer treats ergodicity as presupposing stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (B) overstates certainty ignoring advanced generalisations; (A) and (D) are blanket false claims. Shortcut: For NET/JRF purposes, treat “ergodic” as always implicitly qualifying a stationary process. Tip: Answer according to the standard syllabus convention unless the question explicitly references advanced/generalised ergodic theory.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Since an MA($\\infty$) representation $X_t = \\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j \\varepsilon_{t-j}$ always exists for stationary processes, all stationary processes are automatically ergodic.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) False, MA($\\infty$) processes are never ergodic','B) True, the MA($\\infty$) (Wold) representation always guarantees ergodicity','C) True only for MA(1) processes','D) False – the Wold representation theorem concerns representing the linearly regular part of a weakly stationary process, but ergodicity additionally requires the coefficients $\\psi_j$ (or more precisely, the resulting autocovariances) to behave appropriately (e.g. $\\sum \\psi_j^2 &lt; \\infty$ combined with $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$); processes with a purely deterministic component in their Wold decomposition can fail to be mean ergodic'],ans:3,exp:'The Wold decomposition writes any (purely non-deterministic, weakly stationary) process as an MA($\\infty$) plus a deterministic component. If the deterministic component is non-trivial (e.g. contains a term like the random-constant $Z$), the overall process can fail to be ergodic even though the “regular” MA($\\infty$) part alone (with square-summable coefficients driven by white noise) would typically be ergodic. Why other options are wrong: (B) overstates the automatic guarantee; (A) is an equally false overcorrection; (C) is an arbitrary restriction. Shortcut: Always check for a hidden deterministic component (in the Wold sense) before assuming an MA($\\infty$)-representable process is ergodic. Tip: A genuinely research-level nuance connecting the Wold Decomposition Theorem with ergodicity – appropriate for the most advanced JRF theory questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “If $\\gamma(h) \\to 0$ as $h \\to \\infty$, the process must be strictly stationary.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) False – $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$ is a statement purely about the decay of second-moment dependence (relevant to weak stationarity/ergodicity); it says nothing about whether the full joint distribution is time-shift invariant, which is what strict stationarity requires','B) True only if the process is Gaussian','C) False, because $\\gamma(h) \\to 0$ implies the process has zero variance','D) True, decaying autocovariance always implies strict stationarity'],ans:0,exp:'$\\gamma(h) \\to 0$ is purely a second-moment (weak stationarity/ergodicity-related) statement about fading linear dependence; it neither requires nor guarantees that the entire joint distribution remains invariant under time shifts, which is the separate, stronger requirement of strict stationarity. (Note option C offers a partially correct special-case link since, for Gaussian processes, weak stationarity does imply strict stationarity – but the unqualified claim in option A remains false in general.) Why other options are wrong: (D) is the false general claim being tested; (C) is a non sequitur. Shortcut: $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$ speaks to ergodicity/weak dependence, NOT directly to strict stationarity, unless combined with the Gaussian equivalence result. Tip: JRF sometimes embeds a partially-true option (like C here) to test precise reading – always check the EXACT qualifying condition stated in each option.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the following statements: [I.] Weak stationarity requires constant mean, constant variance, and lag-only-dependent autocovariance. [II.] Strict stationarity requires the entire joint distribution to be time-shift invariant. Which of the above statements is/are correct?',opts:['A) Neither I nor II','B) Only II','C) Only I','D) Both I and II'],ans:3,exp:'Both statements are the standard, textbook-correct definitions of weak and strict stationarity respectively. Why other options are wrong: (C) and (B) each wrongly reject one correct statement; (A) wrongly rejects both. Shortcut: When both statements are textbook definitions stated precisely, the answer is typically “Both correct.” Tip: Read each statement for the exact defining conditions – these two are the most fundamental definitions in this unit.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the following statements about the random walk $X_t = X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$: [I.] It is weakly stationary. [II.] Its variance grows linearly with $t$. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Neither I nor II','B) Only II','C) Only I','D) Both I and II'],ans:1,exp:'Statement II is correct: $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=t\\sigma^2$. Statement I is incorrect precisely because of this growing variance, which violates weak stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (C) wrongly claims stationarity; (D) wrongly accepts both when I is false; (A) wrongly rejects the correct statement II. Shortcut: Random walk = growing variance = non-stationary; always pair these two facts. Tip: This statement pairing is extremely common in NET – expect it verbatim or with reworded statements.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the following statements: [I.] Strict stationarity implies weak stationarity whenever the second moments are finite. [II.] Weak stationarity always implies strict stationarity. [III.] For Gaussian processes, weak and strict stationarity are equivalent. Which of the above statement(s) is/are correct?',opts:['A) I and II only','B) II and III only','C) I and III only','D) I, II and III'],ans:2,exp:'I is correct (standard implication with finite variance); III is correct (Gaussian equivalence result); II is false in general – weak stationarity does not imply strict stationarity for non-Gaussian processes. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (D) incorrectly include the false statement II; (B) drops the correct statement I. Shortcut: I and III are always true; II is the “odd one out” – memorise this triple as a set. Tip: This is one of the highest-frequency statement-combination questions across NET cycles for this topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the following statements about ergodicity: [I.] Ergodicity concerns whether time averages converge to ensemble averages. [II.] A stationary process is always ergodic. [III.] The process $X_t = \\mu + Z$ (with $Z$ a fixed random draw) is a counter-example showing stationarity does not imply ergodicity. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) II and III only','B) I and II only','C) I and III only','D) I, II and III'],ans:2,exp:'I and III are both standard, correct facts. II is false – ergodicity is not automatic given stationarity, as III itself demonstrates. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (D) wrongly include the false statement II; (A) drops the correct definitional statement I. Shortcut: Statement II directly contradicts statement III within the same question – a built-in consistency check to catch the error. Tip: When statements within the same question contradict each other, at least one must be false – use this as a quick sanity check under time pressure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the following statements about the autocovariance function $\\gamma(h)$ of a weakly stationary process: [I.] $\\gamma(h) = \\gamma(-h)$. [II.] $|\\gamma(h)| \\le \\gamma(0)$. [III.] $\\gamma(0) = \\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$. Which of the above are correct?',opts:['A) I and II only','B) II and III only','C) I, II and III','D) I and III only'],ans:2,exp:'All three are standard, well-established properties of the autocovariance function of any weakly stationary process. Why other options are wrong: Each excludes at least one universally true property. Shortcut: These three properties should be memorised together as the “basic ACVF property set.” Tip: A guaranteed easy-to-moderate mark if these three properties are memorised precisely.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the following statements about mean ergodicity: [I.] Mean ergodicity requires $\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_h \\gamma(h) \\to 0$ as $T \\to \\infty$. [II.] A sufficient condition is $\\gamma(h) \\to 0$ as $h \\to \\infty$. [III.] Mean ergodicity always requires $\\gamma(h)=0$ for all $h \\neq 0$. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) I and II only','B) I, II and III','C) II and III only','D) I and III only'],ans:0,exp:'I and II are correct standard conditions/sufficient conditions for mean ergodicity. III is false and overly strong – it describes white noise specifically, not the general requirement. Why other options are wrong: (D), (C), and (B) all incorrectly include the overly restrictive statement III. Shortcut: Statement III is a common “too strong” trap – watch for absolute conditions ($\\gamma(h)=0$ exactly) replacing the correct “decays to $0$” condition. Tip: JRF frequently tests exactly this I-II-III combination to separate necessary/sufficient nuances.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the following statements about MA and AR processes: [I.] Finite-order MA processes with finite coefficients are always weakly stationary. [II.] AR(1) processes are stationary for all values of $\\phi$. [III.] AR(1) processes are stationary if and only if $|\\phi| &lt; 1$. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) II and III only','B) I and III only','C) I and II only','D) I, II and III'],ans:1,exp:'I is correct (finite MA is always stationary). III is the correct AR(1) stationarity condition. II is false and contradicts III – AR(1) requires $|\\phi|&lt;1$, not all values of $\\phi$. Why other options are wrong: (C) and (D) wrongly include the false statement II; (A) drops the correct statement I. Shortcut: MA processes: always stationary. AR processes: stationary only under root/coefficient conditions ($|\\phi|&lt;1$ for AR(1)). Tip: A core AR-vs-MA distinction, repeatedly tested in both stationarity and ergodicity contexts.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the following statements: [I.] The ADF test null hypothesis is that the series is stationary. [II.] Rejecting the ADF null provides evidence against a unit root. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Neither I nor II','B) Only II','C) Both I and II','D) Only I'],ans:1,exp:'Statement I is FALSE – the ADF null hypothesis is that a unit root IS PRESENT (i.e. non-stationarity), not that the series is stationary; this is precisely reversed from the truth. Statement II is TRUE – rejecting the null (of a unit root) supports the alternative of stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (D), (C), and (A) all mishandle statement I\'s reversed null hypothesis. Shortcut: ADF null = “unit root exists.” KPSS null = “series is stationary” (the reverse setup) – do not confuse the two tests\' null hypotheses. Tip: The ADF vs. KPSS null-hypothesis reversal is one of the most-repeated applied-econometrics traps in NET.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the following statements: [I.] Every measurable function of a strictly stationary ergodic process is itself strictly stationary and ergodic. [II.] The Birkhoff Ergodic Theorem requires only stationarity, not ergodicity, to guarantee convergence to the constant $E(X_0)$. [III.] Mixing implies ergodicity, but ergodicity does not imply mixing. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) I and II only','B) I, II and III','C) II and III only','D) I and III only'],ans:3,exp:'I is correct (preservation under measurable transformations). III is correct (mixing is sufficient but not necessary for ergodicity). II is false – stationarity alone guarantees convergence to a possibly random limit $E(X_0|\\mathcal{I})$; convergence to the deterministic constant $E(X_0)$ specifically requires ergodicity. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (B) wrongly include the false statement II; (C) drops the correct statement I. Shortcut: Remember the exact scope of Birkhoff\'s theorem: stationarity alone gives a.s. convergence to a random limit; ergodicity upgrades this to the constant $E(X_0)$. Tip: A very high-difficulty combination question suitable for the most advanced JRF aspirants – test each clause against the precise theorem statement.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the following statements about a fractionally integrated $I(d)$ process with $0&lt;d&lt;0.5$: [I.] It is weakly stationary. [II.] Its autocovariances are absolutely summable. [III.] It is mean ergodic. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) I, II and III','B) I and III only','C) II and III only','D) I and II only'],ans:1,exp:'I is correct (stationary for $0&lt;d&lt;0.5$). III is correct (since $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$, the Cesaro-average condition for mean ergodicity holds). II is FALSE – long-memory processes in this range have autocovariances that decay only hyperbolically and are NOT absolutely summable ($\\sum_h|\\gamma(h)|=\\infty$), which is precisely the defining feature of long memory. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (A) wrongly include the false statement II; (C) drops the correct statement I. Shortcut: Long memory: stationary and ergodic (since $\\gamma(h)\\to 0$), but NOT absolutely summable autocovariances – a precise, frequently tested triple. Tip: This distinguishes long-memory processes cleanly from short-memory (ARMA) processes, which ARE absolutely summable – a key JRF discriminator.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Concept) with List II (Defining Condition): @ll@ List I | List II; (a) Mean stationarity | (i) $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+h})$ depends only on $h$; (b) Variance stationarity | (ii) $E(X_t)=\\mu$, constant; (c) Covariance stationarity | (iii) $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2$, constant',opts:['A) (a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii)','B) (a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii)','C) (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i)','D) (a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii)'],ans:2,exp:'Mean stationarity fixes the first moment (ii); variance stationarity fixes the second central moment at lag 0 (iii); covariance stationarity fixes the lag-dependence of the autocovariance function (i). Why other options are wrong: All other combinations mis-pair at least one concept with the wrong defining condition. Shortcut: Order of moments: Mean $\\to$ 1st moment; Variance $\\to$ 2nd moment at $h=0$; Covariance $\\to$ 2nd moment across $h$. Tip: Match-the-following questions on this topic almost always use exactly these three definitions – memorise the ordered triple.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Process) with List II (Stationarity Status): @ll@ List I | List II; (a) White noise | (i) Non-stationary; (b) Random walk | (ii) Weakly stationary (any finite parameter); (c) MA(1), finite $\\theta$ | (iii) Stationary (special case: $\\gamma(h)=0$, $h\\neq 0$)',opts:['A) (a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i)','B) (a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii)','C) (a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii)','D) (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i)'],ans:1,exp:'White noise is stationary with zero autocovariance beyond lag 0 (iii); the random walk is non-stationary due to growing variance (i); finite-order MA(1) is weakly stationary for any finite $\\theta$ (ii). Why other options are wrong: Each alternative swaps at least one process with an incorrect status. Shortcut: Random walk = always non-stationary; White noise and MA($q$) = always stationary (for finite coefficients). Tip: These three processes form the essential “stationary vs. non-stationary” benchmark set for NET.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Term) with List II (Synonym/Related Term): @ll@ List I | List II; (a) Strict stationarity | (i) Second-order stationarity; (b) Weak stationarity | (ii) Strong stationarity; (c) ACF | (iii) $\\gamma(h)/\\gamma(0)$',opts:['A) (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i)','B) (a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i)','C) (a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii)','D) (a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii)'],ans:2,exp:'Strict stationarity is synonymous with strong stationarity (ii); weak stationarity is synonymous with second-order stationarity (i); the ACF is the standardised autocovariance $\\gamma(h)/\\gamma(0)$ (iii). Why other options are wrong: All alternatives mis-map at least one synonym pair. Shortcut: Build a synonym table before the exam: Strict=Strong; Weak=Covariance=Second-order=Wide-sense. Tip: Synonym-matching is a very common, easy-marks NET question format for this topic – prepare the full synonym list in advance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Property) with List II (Applies To): @ll@ List I | List II; (a) Time average converges to ensemble average | (i) Ergodicity; (b) Joint distribution invariant under time shift | (ii) Strict stationarity; (c) Only first two moments time-invariant | (iii) Weak stationarity',opts:['A) (a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i)','B) (a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii)','C) (a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii)','D) (a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii)'],ans:1,exp:'This is the direct definitional correspondence: (a) describes ergodicity, (b) describes strict stationarity, (c) describes weak stationarity. Why other options are wrong: Each alternative swaps at least one definition with the wrong property. Shortcut: Map each description phrase directly to its keyword: “time average” $\\to$ ergodicity; “joint distribution” $\\to$ strict; “first two moments” $\\to$ weak. Tip: This exact three-way match is essentially the summary of the entire unit – a guaranteed-marks question if the definitions are clear.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Test/Tool) with List II (Purpose): @ll@ List I | List II; (a) ADF test | (i) Frequency-domain representation; (b) Spectral density | (ii) Test for unit root (non-stationarity); (c) Seasonal differencing $(1-L^s)$ | (iii) Remove seasonal unit root',opts:['A) (a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii)','B) (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i)','C) (a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii)','D) (a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i)'],ans:0,exp:'The ADF test targets unit roots (ii); the spectral density is the Fourier-transform (frequency-domain) representation of $\\gamma(h)$ (i); seasonal differencing removes seasonal unit roots (iii). Why other options are wrong: Each alternative mis-pairs at least one tool with an incorrect purpose. Shortcut: Associate each keyword directly: ADF $\\to$ unit root; Spectral $\\to$ frequency; Seasonal differencing $\\to$ seasonal unit root. Tip: This bridges pure theory (stationarity) with applied tools – a common cross-topic NET matching format.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Example) with List II (Property Illustrated): @ll@ List I | List II; (a) $X_t = \\mu + Z$ (fixed random $Z$) | (i) Stationary, mean ergodic; (b) Stationary AR(1), $|\\phi|&lt;1$ | (ii) Stationary, NOT ergodic; (c) i.i.d. sequence, finite variance | (iii) Strictly stationary, ergodic',opts:['A) (a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii)','B) (a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i)','C) (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i)','D) (a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii)'],ans:0,exp:'The fixed-random-constant process (a) is the standard non-ergodic-but-stationary example (ii); the stable Gaussian/linear AR(1) process (b) is mean ergodic since $\\gamma(h) \\to 0$ (i); the i.i.d. sequence (c) is the benchmark strictly stationary and ergodic process (iii). Why other options are wrong: All alternatives mis-map at least one canonical example. Shortcut: Keep these three canonical examples memorised as a fixed reference triple for the entire topic. Tip: This triple of examples covers nearly every conceptual question type possible in this unit – the single most efficient revision investment for this section.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(1) process $X_t = 0.5 X_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$, $\\varepsilon_t \\sim$ i.i.d. $(0,\\sigma^2=4)$, find $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$.',opts:['A) $8$','B) $2$','C) $4$','D) $16/3 \\approx 5.33$'],ans:3,exp:'For a stationary AR(1), $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=\\gamma(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{1-\\phi^2}=\\dfrac{4}{1-0.25}=\\dfrac{4}{0.75}=\\dfrac{16}{3}\\approx 5.33$. Why other options are wrong: (C) forgets to divide by $(1-\\phi^2)$; (A) uses an incorrect denominator; (B) is unrelated to the correct formula. Shortcut: Always apply $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$ directly for stationary AR(1) variance questions. Tip: This exact formula is used repeatedly across NET numerical questions – memorise it precisely, including the squared $\\phi$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(1) process in the previous question ($\\phi=0.5$, $\\sigma^2=4$), find the autocorrelation at lag 2, $\\rho(2)$.',opts:['A) $0.25$','B) $0.5$','C) $1$','D) $0.125$'],ans:0,exp:'For AR(1), $\\rho(h)=\\phi^{|h|}$, so $\\rho(2)=(0.5)^2=0.25$. Why other options are wrong: (B) is $\\rho(1)$, not $\\rho(2)$; (C) is $\\rho(0)$; (D) is $\\phi^3$, an unrelated computation. Shortcut: AR(1) ACF simply follows a geometric decay: $\\rho(h) = \\phi^{|h|}$. Tip: A guaranteed formula-recall question – know $\\rho(h)=\\phi^{|h|}$ for AR(1) exactly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an MA(1) process $X_t = \\varepsilon_t + 0.6\\,\\varepsilon_{t-1}$, $\\varepsilon_t \\sim$ i.i.d. $(0,\\sigma^2=1)$, compute $\\gamma(0)$ and $\\gamma(1)$.',opts:['A) $\\gamma(0)=1.36$, $\\gamma(1)=0.6$','B) $\\gamma(0)=1.6$, $\\gamma(1)=0.36$','C) $\\gamma(0)=0.6$, $\\gamma(1)=1.36$','D) $\\gamma(0)=1$, $\\gamma(1)=0.6$'],ans:0,exp:'For MA(1), $\\gamma(0)=(1+\\theta^2)\\sigma^2=(1+0.36)(1)=1.36$, and $\\gamma(1)=\\theta\\sigma^2=0.6(1)=0.6$; $\\gamma(h)=0$ for $h\\ge 2$. Why other options are wrong: (D) omits the $\\theta^2$ term in the variance; (B) and (C) swap or misapply the formulas incorrectly. Shortcut: Memorise: MA(1) $\\gamma(0)=(1+\\theta^2)\\sigma^2$, $\\gamma(1)=\\theta\\sigma^2$, $\\gamma(h)=0$ for $h\\ge2$. Tip: MA(1) ACVF formulas are extremely high-frequency in NET numerical sections – derive them once and memorise.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Using the MA(1) process from the previous question, compute $\\rho(1)$.',opts:['A) $0.441$','B) $0.36$','C) $1.36$','D) $0.6$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\rho(1)=\\gamma(1)/\\gamma(0)=0.6/1.36\\approx 0.441$. Why other options are wrong: (D) is $\\gamma(1)$ itself, not normalised; (B) is $\\theta^2$, an unrelated quantity; (C) is $\\gamma(0)$, not the ratio. Shortcut: General MA(1) formula: $\\rho(1)=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$; here $=0.6/1.36\\approx 0.441$. Tip: Note the general closed-form $\\rho(1)=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$ is often faster than computing $\\gamma(0)$ and $\\gamma(1)$ separately.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a weakly stationary process with $\\gamma(0)=10$, $\\gamma(1)=4$, $\\gamma(h)=0$ for $h \\ge 2$, compute $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_2)$ where $\\bar X_2 = \\frac{1}{2}(X_1+X_2)$.',opts:['A) $7$','B) $10$','C) $3.5$','D) $5$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_2)=\\frac{1}{4}[\\mathrm{Var}(X_1)+\\mathrm{Var}(X_2)+2\\mathrm{Cov}(X_1,X_2)]=\\frac{1}{4}[10+10+2(4)]=\\frac{1}{4}(28)=7$. Why other options are wrong: (D) omits the covariance term; (B) mistakenly uses $\\gamma(0)$ alone; (C) incorrectly halves the true answer. Shortcut: Use $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T)=\\frac{1}{T^2}\\left[T\\gamma(0) + 2\\sum_{h=1}^{T-1}(T-h)\\gamma(h)\\right]$ for small, direct calculations. Tip: Direct small-$T$ variance-of-sample-mean computations are a very common JRF numerical question type – practice the direct double-sum expansion.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A process has $\\gamma(h) = 5(0.4)^{|h|}$. Compute $\\gamma(0)$ and determine $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$.',opts:['A) $\\gamma(0)=5(0.4)=2$, and this cannot be the variance','B) $\\gamma(0)=2$, and $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=2$','C) $\\gamma(0)=5$, and $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=5$','D) $\\gamma(0)=0.4$, and $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=0.4$'],ans:2,exp:'Setting $h=0$ in $\\gamma(h)=5(0.4)^{|h|}$ gives $\\gamma(0)=5(0.4)^0=5(1)=5$; and by definition $\\gamma(0)=\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$, so $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)=5$. Why other options are wrong: (B), (D), and (A) all incorrectly evaluate $(0.4)^0$ or misapply the exponent. Shortcut: Always remember $(\\cdot)^0=1$ – a common careless numerical slip at $h=0$. Tip: A simple but frequently mis-answered question due to exponent-zero errors under exam time pressure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given the autocovariance function $\\gamma(h)=5(0.4)^{|h|}$ from the previous question, does the process satisfy the sufficient condition for mean ergodicity?',opts:['A) Yes, since $\\gamma(h) \\to 0$ as $h \\to \\infty$ (geometric decay), so the Cesaro average of autocovariances vanishes','B) No, since $\\gamma(0) \\neq 0$','C) No, since $\\gamma(h)$ is never exactly zero','D) Cannot be determined without knowing $\\sigma^2$'],ans:0,exp:'Since $0.4^{|h|} \\to 0$ as $h \\to \\infty$, the autocovariance decays geometrically to zero, which is sufficient to ensure $\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_h \\gamma(h) \\to 0$, satisfying the mean ergodicity condition, even though $\\gamma(h)$ is never exactly zero for any finite $h$. Why other options are wrong: (C) wrongly requires exact zero, confusing the sufficient decay condition with a stronger, unnecessary exact-zero requirement; (D) is unnecessary since ergodicity here concerns only the decay pattern, not the absolute scale; (B) misunderstands the role of $\\gamma(0)$, which is unrelated to the ergodicity decay condition. Shortcut: Geometric decay ($|\\text{ratio}| &lt; 1$) in $\\gamma(h)$ is always sufficient for mean ergodicity – no need for exact zero values. Tip: This reinforces the distinction between “exactly zero” (unnecessarily strong) and “decays to zero” (correct, sufficient) conditions for ergodicity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(1) process with $\\phi = 0.8$, $\\sigma^2 = 1$, and sample size $T=100$, approximate $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T)$ using the formula $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T) \\approx \\dfrac{\\gamma(0)}{T}\\left(\\dfrac{1+\\phi}{1-\\phi}\\right)$ for large $T$ (the “long-run variance” approximation).',opts:['A) $\\approx 0.0278$','B) $\\approx 0.25$','C) $\\approx 0.0025$','D) $\\approx 2.78$'],ans:1,exp:'First $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=1/(1-0.64)=1/0.36\\approx 2.78$. Then $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T)\\approx \\frac{2.78}{100}\\times\\frac{1+0.8}{1-0.8}=\\frac{2.78}{100}\\times\\frac{1.8}{0.2}=\\frac{2.78}{100}\\times 9\\approx 0.25$. Why other options are wrong: (A) mistakenly uses only $\\gamma(0)/T$ without the long-run-variance multiplier $(1+\\phi)/(1-\\phi)$; (D) forgets to divide by $T$ altogether; (C) incorrectly divides by $T^2$ instead of $T$. Shortcut: For AR(1), the long-run variance multiplier is $(1+\\phi)/(1-\\phi)$ – larger $\\phi$ (stronger persistence) inflates $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar X_T)$ substantially relative to the i.i.d. benchmark $\\gamma(0)/T$. Tip: This “long-run variance” formula is a genuinely advanced JRF numerical tool, connecting AR(1) persistence directly to the precision of the sample mean as an estimator.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A process is constructed as $X_t = \\mu + (-1)^t \\cdot 3 + \\varepsilon_t$, where $\\varepsilon_t$ is stationary white noise with variance $\\sigma^2$. Compute $E(X_t)$ for $t$ even and $t$ odd, and determine whether the process is mean stationary.',opts:['A) $E(X_t)$ is undefined','B) $E(X_t)=\\mu+3$ (even $t$), $E(X_t)=\\mu-3$ (odd $t$); NOT mean stationary since $E(X_t)$ depends on the parity of $t$','C) $E(X_t)=\\mu+3$ for all $t$; mean stationary','D) $E(X_t)=\\mu$ for all $t$; mean stationary'],ans:1,exp:'Since $(-1)^t=+1$ for even $t$ and $-1$ for odd $t$, $E(X_t)=\\mu+3$ when $t$ is even and $E(X_t)=\\mu-3$ when $t$ is odd; because this expected value genuinely alternates depending on $t$ (not constant), mean stationarity fails. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (C) incorrectly ignore the deterministic alternating term; (A) is false, the expectation is well-defined and finite. Shortcut: Any explicit deterministic function of $t$ (including periodic/alternating terms) added to the mean immediately breaks mean stationarity unless it is exactly constant. Tip: A useful numerical companion to the earlier conceptual seasonal/alternating-pattern trap questions – always substitute specific even/odd $t$ values to check explicitly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARMA(1,1) process $X_t = \\phi X_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t + \\theta \\varepsilon_{t-1}$ with $\\phi=0.5$, $\\theta=0.3$, $\\sigma^2=1$, the variance is given by $\\gamma(0) = \\sigma^2\\dfrac{1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2}{1-\\phi^2}$. Compute $\\gamma(0)$.',opts:['A) $\\gamma(0) \\approx 2.78$','B) $\\gamma(0) \\approx 1.16$','C) $\\gamma(0) \\approx 1.36$','D) $\\gamma(0) \\approx 1.853$'],ans:3,exp:'Substituting: $1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2 = 1+2(0.5)(0.3)+(0.3)^2 = 1+0.3+0.09=1.39$; and $1-\\phi^2=1-0.25=0.75$. So $\\gamma(0)=1(1.39)/0.75\\approx 1.853$. Why other options are wrong: (B) results from an arithmetic slip in the numerator (e.g. omitting the $\\theta^2$ term); (C) is the MA(1)-only variance formula $(1+\\theta^2)\\sigma^2$ with $\\theta=0.6$ from an earlier question, mismatched here; (A) is the AR(1)-only variance formula with $\\phi=0.8$ from an earlier question, unrelated to this ARMA(1,1) computation. Shortcut: Always substitute into the compound ARMA(1,1) variance formula term-by-term (numerator first, then denominator) to avoid arithmetic slips. Tip: ARMA(1,1) moment formulas combine both AR and MA components – a high-value JRF numerical derivation to practice deriving from first principles (via the Yule-Walker-type approach) rather than only memorising.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In empirical macroeconomic and financial time-series research, why is stationarity typically a prerequisite before applying standard regression or ARMA modelling techniques?',opts:['A) Non-stationary series always have zero variance','B) Non-stationary series cannot be plotted graphically','C) Standard asymptotic theory (consistency, standard error validity) for estimators generally assumes stationarity (or requires special non-standard treatment otherwise, e.g. cointegration methods)','D) Stationarity guarantees the series is normally distributed'],ans:2,exp:'Most classical estimation and inference theory (consistency of estimators, validity of standard errors, t- and F-tests) is derived under stationarity (or appropriate ergodicity) assumptions; applying such methods directly to non-stationary data can produce spurious results (e.g. spurious regression), necessitating either differencing, de-trending, or specialised techniques like cointegration. Why other options are wrong: (A), (D), and (B) are all factually incorrect statements about non-stationary series. Shortcut: “Non-stationary data + standard regression = risk of spurious regression” – the core applied justification for stationarity checks. Tip: This applied-relevance rationale is a common short-answer/explanation NET question, bridging theory with practical modelling motivation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which foundational result underlies the use of a single observed economic or financial time series to estimate population (theoretical) moments such as the mean and autocovariance?',opts:['A) The Central Limit Theorem alone','B) Ergodicity (via the Ergodic Theorem), applied to a stationary process','C) Bayes\' Theorem','D) The Law of Total Probability'],ans:1,exp:'Since researchers typically observe only one realised path of a time series (not repeated independent draws from the ensemble), the Ergodic Theorem\'s guarantee that time averages converge to ensemble averages (under stationarity and ergodicity) is the foundational justification for this common empirical practice. Why other options are wrong: (A) the CLT addresses the distribution of estimators, not the fundamental justification for time-averaging itself; (D) and (C) are unrelated probability results. Shortcut: Whenever a question asks “why can we estimate population parameters from one time series,” the answer is always ergodicity. Tip: A conceptually important, frequently tested applied-research-motivation question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In modern time-series econometric research, which of the following correctly reflects current practice regarding the strict distinction between “stationary” and “non-stationary but cointegrated” systems?',opts:['A) Even if individual series are non-stationary (e.g. $I(1)$), a linear combination of them may be stationary ($I(0)$), a relationship termed cointegration, allowing valid long-run equilibrium modelling (e.g. via Error Correction Models)','B) Non-stationary series can never be jointly analysed in a valid statistical framework','C) Cointegration requires all series to be strictly stationary from the outset','D) Cointegration is only relevant to Markov chains'],ans:0,exp:'Cointegration theory (Engle-Granger, Johansen) shows that even when individual non-stationary series share a common stochastic trend, an appropriate linear combination can be stationary, capturing a meaningful long-run equilibrium relationship, which is then typically modelled using an Error Correction Model (ECM). Why other options are wrong: (B) is false, cointegration is precisely the valid framework for such series; (C) contradicts the entire premise of cointegration (individual series ARE non-stationary); (D) is unrelated. Shortcut: “Individually non-stationary, jointly stationary combination” = cointegration – a key applied extension beyond basic stationarity. Tip: Cointegration is a natural advanced extension frequently linked to this unit in JRF-level applied econometrics questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In spectral analysis research, the spectral density $f(\\omega)$ of a stationary process evaluated at frequency $\\omega=0$ is related to which quantity?',opts:['A) The variance $\\gamma(0)$ only','B) The “long-run variance,” $2\\pi f(0) = \\sum_{h=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(h)$, which underlies the asymptotic variance of the sample mean in dependent-data settings (e.g. HAC/Newey-West estimation)','C) Zero, always','D) The mean $\\mu$ of the process'],ans:1,exp:'The value of the spectral density at frequency zero, scaled by $2\\pi$, equals the sum of all autocovariances, known as the long-run variance; this quantity is central to constructing heteroscedasticity-and-autocorrelation-consistent (HAC) standard errors (e.g. Newey-West) used in dependent-data inference. Why other options are wrong: (A) captures only the $h=0$ term, ignoring all other lags contributing to $f(0)$; (C) is false in general; (D) is unrelated – spectral density concerns second moments, not the mean. Shortcut: $f(0)$ relates to the SUM of all autocovariances (long-run variance), not merely $\\gamma(0)$ – an important applied econometrics research link. Tip: This connects spectral theory directly to HAC estimation, a frequently tested applied-econometrics research topic in JRF-level papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best reflects current statistical research practice when a time series exhibits structural breaks (e.g. a sudden shift in mean at a known or unknown date)?',opts:['A) Structural breaks only occur in cross-sectional data, not time series','B) Structural breaks always indicate the series is ergodic','C) A series with an unmodelled structural break can appear (and test as) non-stationary under standard unit-root tests even if it is actually a stationary process around a broken trend/mean; specialised tests (e.g. Perron\'s test) account for this','D) Structural breaks are irrelevant to stationarity and can always be ignored'],ans:2,exp:'Perron (1989) showed that standard unit-root tests (like the ADF test) have low power to reject a false unit-root null in the presence of an unmodelled structural break, potentially leading researchers to wrongly conclude non-stationarity when the true process is stationary around a break; specialised break-aware unit-root tests were developed to correct this. Why other options are wrong: (D), (B), and (A) are all factually incorrect statements about structural breaks. Shortcut: “Structural break $+$ standard ADF test $\\to$ spurious non-rejection of unit root” is the key research-level caveat to remember. Tip: Perron\'s structural-break critique of unit-root testing is a well-known, occasionally tested research-level JRF fact in applied time-series papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the context of stochastic processes on continuous time, which of the following correctly relates the discrete-time stationarity/ergodicity theory covered in this unit to continuous-time processes such as the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) process?',opts:['A) The OU process has no stationary analogue','B) Continuous-time processes cannot be ergodic by definition','C) The OU process is always non-stationary regardless of $\\theta$','D) Analogous to the discrete AR(1) case, the OU process $dX_t = -\\theta X_t\\,dt + \\sigma\\,dW_t$ (with $\\theta&gt;0$) has a stationary distribution (as $t\\to\\infty$) with exponentially decaying autocovariance $\\gamma(h)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\theta}e^{-\\theta|h|}$, and is mean ergodic'],ans:3,exp:'The OU process is the continuous-time analogue of the discrete AR(1) process; for $\\theta&gt;0$ it possesses a unique stationary distribution with exponentially decaying autocovariance (mirroring the geometric decay $\\phi^{|h|}$ in discrete AR(1)), and since this autocovariance decays to zero, the process is mean ergodic by the same Cesaro-average logic used in discrete time. Why other options are wrong: (A), (C), and (B) are all factually incorrect statements about the OU process or continuous-time ergodicity in general. Shortcut: Discrete AR(1) $\\leftrightarrow$ Continuous OU process is a standard, testable analogy: geometric decay $\\leftrightarrow$ exponential decay. Tip: A genuinely advanced research-level bridge between discrete- and continuous-time stochastic process theory – appropriate for the most rigorous JRF theory papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly describes a key open/advanced methodological challenge in testing for ergodicity (as distinct from testing for stationarity) using observed data?',opts:['A) Ergodicity, being fundamentally a statement about the limiting behaviour of time averages relative to an (often unobservable) ensemble average, is generally NOT directly testable from a single observed realisation in the same operational way that (weak) stationarity can be approximately assessed; it is typically assumed based on theoretical model structure (e.g. mixing conditions) rather than directly tested','B) Ergodicity is trivially testable with the same procedures as unit-root tests','C) There is no meaningful distinction between testing stationarity and testing ergodicity','D) Ergodicity can be perfectly verified using only the sample mean of one path'],ans:0,exp:'Because we generally cannot observe repeated independent realisations of the same underlying ensemble (only one historical path), directly verifying that “time average equals ensemble average” from data alone is generally infeasible; in practice, ergodicity is typically assumed as a modelling condition (justified via theoretical properties like mixing or specific model structure) rather than empirically tested in the way stationarity is approximately assessed via unit-root/stationarity tests. Why other options are wrong: (B), (D), and (C) all understate this genuine methodological challenge. Shortcut: Stationarity: approximately testable (ADF/KPSS). Ergodicity: generally assumed via model structure, not directly tested from one realisation. Tip: A genuinely research-oriented, discussion-style JRF question – useful for viva/interview-style elaboration as well as MCQ recognition.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement best reflects the modern research view on the relationship between invariant measures in ergodic theory and stationary distributions in Markov chain / stochastic process theory?',opts:['A) A stationary distribution of a Markov process is precisely an invariant measure for the associated shift/transition operator, and ergodicity of the resulting stationary process corresponds to the invariant measure being extremal (not expressible as a non-trivial mixture of other invariant measures)','B) They are entirely unrelated mathematical objects','C) Invariant measures exist only for finite-state spaces','D) Stationary distributions always correspond to non-ergodic processes'],ans:0,exp:'In the general theory, a stationary distribution for a Markov process is exactly an invariant measure for its transition operator; among possibly many invariant measures (forming a convex set), the ergodic ones are precisely the extreme points – they cannot be written as a non-trivial convex combination (mixture) of other invariant measures, which directly parallels the earlier ergodic-decomposition idea (mixtures of ergodic processes are stationary but not ergodic). Why other options are wrong: (B), (C), and (D) all misstate this well-established correspondence. Shortcut: “Ergodic invariant measures = extreme points of the convex set of invariant measures” – connects directly back to the earlier mixture-of-AR(1)-processes counter-example (Section D). Tip: This is a genuinely graduate/research-level synthesis question, appropriate only for the most theoretically advanced JRF aspirants aiming for top ranks.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider current research on non-linear time series models (e.g. GARCH, threshold autoregressive models). Which statement correctly characterises their stationarity/ergodicity properties?',opts:['A) Ergodicity is undefined for non-linear processes','B) Non-linear models are always weakly stationary but never strictly stationary','C) Non-linear models can never be stationary','D) Many non-linear models (e.g. GARCH(1,1) under standard parameter restrictions) possess a strictly stationary and ergodic solution, established via specialised techniques (e.g. Markov chain theory, Lyapunov exponent conditions) rather than the simple linear ACVF-decay arguments used for ARMA models'],ans:3,exp:'For models like GARCH(1,1), strict stationarity and ergodicity of the solution are established using conditions such as a negative top Lyapunov exponent (Bougerol and Picard\'s theorem) rather than simple linear-ACVF arguments, since standard weak-stationarity ACVF tools do not directly apply to these non-linear conditional-variance models. Why other options are wrong: (C), (B), and (A) all understate or misstate the well-developed (though more technically demanding) theory available for non-linear stationarity/ergodicity. Shortcut: Non-linear models (GARCH, TAR, etc.) require specialised (often Lyapunov-exponent-based) stationarity/ergodicity theory distinct from linear ARMA ACVF arguments. Tip: A cutting-edge research-level question connecting this classical unit to modern volatility modelling – appropriate for top-tier JRF aspirants and PhD-track candidates.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In current empirical research practice, what is the recommended approach when a time series is found to have a unit root (non-stationary) via the ADF test?',opts:['A) Immediately conclude the data is unusable','B) Convert the series to a Markov chain','C) Ignore the finding and proceed with level regressions','D) Difference the series (e.g. take first differences) to achieve stationarity before modelling, or consider cointegration-based modelling if working with multiple related non-stationary series'],ans:3,exp:'The standard remedy for a unit-root (difference-stationary, $I(1)$) series is to apply differencing (yielding an $I(0)$, stationary series) before further ARMA-type modelling; when working with multiple related non-stationary series that may share common stochastic trends, cointegration-based frameworks (Error Correction Models) are the appropriate alternative to preserve valid long-run information. Why other options are wrong: (C) risks spurious regression; (A) is an overreaction – the data remains usable with appropriate transformation; (B) is an unrelated and inapplicable suggestion. Shortcut: Unit root found $\\to$ difference (or consider cointegration for multivariate systems) – the standard applied research workflow. Tip: This practical workflow question frequently closes out applied time-series sections in NET, tying together the entire stationarity/ergodicity unit with real-world modelling practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best defines a white noise process $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$?',opts:['A) Zero mean, constant variance, independent and identically distributed','B) Zero mean, constant variance, zero autocovariance','C) Constant mean, increasing variance, zero autocovariance','D) Zero mean, zero variance, constant autocovariance'],ans:1,exp:'White noise requires $\\operatorname{E}(\\varepsilon_t)=0$, $\\operatorname{Var}(\\varepsilon_t)=\\sigma^2$ constant, and $\\operatorname{Cov}(\\varepsilon_t,\\varepsilon_s)=0$ for $t\\ne s$ – nothing more. Why other options are wrong: (A) describes strict white noise, a stronger, unnecessary condition. (C) variance must be constant, not increasing. (D) variance cannot be zero (that would be a degenerate, not random, process). Shortcut: Just recall the three defining moments: mean 0, variance constant, covariance 0. Tip: Do not over-specify white noise as i.i.d. unless the question explicitly says “strict.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the general linear process representation $X_t = \\mu + \\sum_{j\\ge0}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$, what is the value of $\\psi_0$?',opts:['A) Undefined; depends on the process','B) $0$','C) $\\sigma^2$','D) $1$'],ans:3,exp:'The normalisation $\\psi_0=1$ is a standard convention that identifies $\\varepsilon_t$ as exactly the coefficient on itself, making it interpretable as the one-step-ahead prediction error. Why other options are wrong: (B) would make $\\varepsilon_t$ absent from its own representation. (C) confuses the weight with the noise variance. (A) the value is fixed by convention, not free. Shortcut: Always check the coefficient on $\\varepsilon_{t-0}=\\varepsilon_t$ first – it should be exactly 1. Tip: Forgetting $\\psi_0=1$ is one of the most common marks lost on this topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which condition on $\\{\\psi_j\\}$ is necessary and sufficient for $\\sum_{j\\ge0}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ to converge in mean square?',opts:['A) $\\psi_j \\to 0$ as $j\\to\\infty$','B) $\\sum_j |\\psi_j| &lt; \\infty$','C) $\\sum_j \\psi_j &lt; \\infty$','D) $\\sum_j \\psi_j^2 &lt; \\infty$'],ans:3,exp:'Square summability, $\\sum_j\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$, is exactly the condition needed for $L^2$ (mean-square) convergence of the infinite sum. Why other options are wrong: (C) is not even well-defined without absolute values for a general (possibly signed) sequence. (B) is a stronger, sufficient-but-not-necessary condition. (A) is necessary but not sufficient (e.g., $\\psi_j=1/\\sqrt j \\to 0$ but fails square summability). Shortcut: Square summable = mean-square existence; this is the “minimum bar” condition to remember. Tip: JRF questions often test whether you know this is necessary AND sufficient, not just sufficient.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Who proved the decomposition theorem for stationary time series, and in which year?',opts:['A) Kolmogorov, 1933','B) Box &amp; Jenkins, 1970','C) Cram\\\'er, 1942','D) Wold, 1938'],ans:3,exp:'Herman Wold proved the decomposition in his 1938 doctoral thesis A Study in the Analysis of Stationary Time Series. Why other options are wrong: (A) Kolmogorov worked on related prediction theory around 1941, not the decomposition itself. (C) Cram\\\'er\'s spectral representation is a related but distinct result. (B) Box–Jenkins (1970) operationalised ARMA modelling, decades after Wold\'s theorem. Shortcut: Remember: Wold = 1938 = decomposition theorem, full stop. Tip: A frequent NET trap swaps Wold and Kolmogorov – know the correct attribution.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'What does the term “innovation” $\\varepsilon_t$ specifically refer to in the Wold representation?',opts:['A) The variance of $X_t$','B) Any uncorrelated random variable','C) The residual from a regression on time','D) The one-step-ahead best linear prediction error of $X_t$ from its own past'],ans:3,exp:'$\\varepsilon_t$ is precisely defined as $X_t - \\hat X_t(X_{t-1},X_{t-2},\\ldots)$, the error from optimally (linearly) predicting $X_t$ using its own infinite past. Why other options are wrong: (B) is too generic – not every uncorrelated variable is the innovation of a specific process. (C) refers to a different (trend-regression) concept entirely. (A) confuses the innovation itself with a summary statistic of it. Shortcut: Innovation = forecast error from own past, not just “some error term.” Tip: This precise definition is very frequently tested via a direct recall MCQ.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a general linear process with $\\psi_0=1,\\psi_1=0.4$, $\\psi_j=0$ for $j\\ge2$, and $\\sigma^2=5$, what is $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$?',opts:['A) $2.0$','B) $5.8$','C) $0.8$','D) $5.0$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2\\sum_j\\psi_j^2 = 5(1^2+0.4^2)=5(1.16)=5.8$. Why other options are wrong: (D) omits the $\\psi_1$ contribution entirely. (A) mistakenly divides instead of using the sum of squares. (C) computes only $\\sigma^2\\psi_1^2$, dropping the $\\psi_0^2$ term. Shortcut: Square and add all listed weights, then multiply by $\\sigma^2$ – one clean pass. Tip: Always include the $\\psi_0=1$ term in the variance sum; it\'s the most commonly dropped term.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is a defining feature of the general linear process representation?',opts:['A) It requires the shocks to be normally distributed','B) It is two-sided, using both past and future shocks','C) It is one-sided (causal), using only present and past shocks','D) It requires finitely many non-zero weights'],ans:2,exp:'The general linear process is strictly causal: $X_t$ depends only on $\\varepsilon_t,\\varepsilon_{t-1},\\ldots$, never on future shocks. Why other options are wrong: (B) describes a non-causal, two-sided filter, which is not the Wold-type representation. (A) no distributional assumption is required. (D) MA($q$) is a special finite case; the general linear process allows infinitely many non-zero weights. Shortcut: “One-sided” is the giveaway keyword – always check the direction of the shock indices. Tip: This causality property is exactly what makes $\\varepsilon_t$ interpretable as a genuine forecast error.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An AR(1) process $X_t = \\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ with $|\\phi|&lt;1$ can be written in MA($\\infty$) form with weights:',opts:['A) $\\psi_j = \\phi/j$','B) $\\psi_j = \\phi^j$','C) $\\psi_j = \\phi$','D) $\\psi_j = j\\phi$'],ans:1,exp:'Expanding $(1-\\phi B)^{-1} = \\sum_{j\\ge0}\\phi^j B^j$ directly gives $\\psi_j=\\phi^j$, the standard geometric-decay result. Why other options are wrong: (C) ignores the geometric decay entirely (would only be correct at $j=1$). (D) and (A) do not arise from the correct power-series expansion of $(1-\\phi B)^{-1}$. Shortcut: Memorise this single result – it is the most-tested psi-weight derivation on the entire syllabus. Tip: Being able to instantly write $\\psi_j=\\phi^j$ for AR(1) saves crucial time on numerical MCQs.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement correctly describes the relationship between ARMA models and the general linear process?',opts:['A) They are unrelated concepts','B) The general linear process is a special case of ARMA models','C) ARMA models are special, finite-parameter cases of the general linear process','D) ARMA models are more general than the general linear process'],ans:2,exp:'Every stationary, invertible ARMA($p,q$) process can be written as $X_t=\\mu+\\psi(B)\\varepsilon_t$ with $\\psi(B)=\\theta(B)/\\phi(B)$ – a specific, parsimonious, rational-function choice of the general $\\psi(B)$. Why other options are wrong: (D) reverses the true relationship. (B) also reverses it – the GLP is the broader class. (A) they are directly related by the theory in this chapter. Shortcut: GLP is the “master template”; ARMA is one structured way of filling it in. Tip: This relationship question appears in nearly every cycle in some form – know the direction cold.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A process has $\\psi_j = 1/j$ for $j\\ge1$ (and $\\psi_0=1$). Which of the following is TRUE?',opts:['A) It is neither square summable nor absolutely summable','B) It is absolutely summable but not square summable','C) It is both square summable and absolutely summable','D) It is square summable but not absolutely summable'],ans:3,exp:'$\\sum_j (1/j)^2 = \\sum 1/j^2 &lt; \\infty$ (converges, a $p$-series with $p=2&gt;1$), but $\\sum_j 1/j$ diverges (harmonic series) – so square summable but not absolutely summable. Why other options are wrong: (C) absolute summability fails. (B) reverses the correct classification. (A) square summability does hold. Shortcut: Memorise $\\psi_j=1/j$ as THE standard counter-example separating the two summability notions. Tip: This exact example is one of the most frequently recycled JRF-trap questions in the whole chapter.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In Wold\'s Decomposition Theorem, what property must hold between the deterministic component $V_t$ and the innovations $\\varepsilon_s$?',opts:['A) No relationship is specified','B) $\\operatorname{Cov}(V_t,\\varepsilon_s)=0$ for all $s,t$','C) $V_t$ must be a linear function of $\\varepsilon_t$','D) $V_t$ must equal $\\varepsilon_t$'],ans:1,exp:'One of the five defining conditions of Wold\'s theorem is that the deterministic part is uncorrelated with every innovation, at every lag. Why other options are wrong: (D) $V_t$ and $\\varepsilon_t$ play entirely different, orthogonal roles. (C) $V_t$ is built from the remote past, not from $\\varepsilon_t$. (A) the relationship is explicitly specified and central to the theorem. Shortcut: Remember: orthogonality between $V_t$ and all $\\varepsilon_s$ is a core, examinable condition, not a side detail. Tip: Multi-statement MCQs often test exactly this orthogonality condition as one of several options.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'What is the correct formula for the autocovariance function $\\gamma(k)$ of a general linear process?',opts:['A) $\\gamma(k) = \\sigma^2\\sum_j \\psi_j^2\\psi_k^2$','B) $\\gamma(k) = \\sigma^2\\sum_j \\psi_j\\psi_{j-k}$','C) $\\gamma(k) = \\sigma\\sum_j \\psi_j\\psi_{j+k}$','D) $\\gamma(k) = \\sigma^2\\sum_j \\psi_j\\psi_{j+k}$'],ans:3,exp:'Direct computation of $\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+k})$ using the MA($\\infty$) representation gives $\\gamma(k)=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j\\ge0}\\psi_j\\psi_{j+k}$. Why other options are wrong: (B) has a sign/index error (should be $j+k$, not $j-k$). (A) is not a linear covariance expression at all. (C) uses $\\sigma$ instead of $\\sigma^2$. Shortcut: At $k=0$ this must reduce to $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2\\sum\\psi_j^2$ – use this as a quick self-check. Tip: Index-sign errors here are the single most common numerical mistake on this topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'What does it mean for a stationary process to be “purely non-deterministic”?',opts:['A) It has infinite variance','B) It is not stationary','C) It has no deterministic component; $V_t\\equiv0$ in the Wold decomposition','D) It has no random component at all'],ans:2,exp:'A purely non-deterministic process is exactly one whose Wold decomposition has zero deterministic remainder, so it equals its general linear process part exactly. Why other options are wrong: (D) reverses the meaning entirely. (B) purely non-deterministic processes are still stationary. (A) has nothing to do with variance being infinite. Shortcut: “Purely non-deterministic” = “$V_t=0$” – a direct one-to-one translation. Tip: This vocabulary translation is tested extremely often and is easy marks once memorised.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density of a general linear process is given by:',opts:['A) $f(\\lambda) = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}|\\psi(e^{-i\\lambda})|^2$','B) $f(\\lambda) = 2\\pi\\sigma^2|\\psi(e^{-i\\lambda})|^2$','C) $f(\\lambda) = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{2\\pi}\\psi(e^{-i\\lambda})$','D) $f(\\lambda) = \\sigma^2 |\\psi(e^{-i\\lambda})|$'],ans:0,exp:'This is the standard result linking the time-domain filter $\\psi(B)$ to the frequency-domain spectral density, derivable via the Wiener–Khinchin theorem. Why other options are wrong: (D) is missing the square and the $2\\pi$ normalisation. (C) uses $\\sigma$ instead of $\\sigma^2$ and omits the modulus-square. (B) has the $2\\pi$ in the wrong position (multiplying instead of dividing). Shortcut: Remember the structure: $\\sigma^2/2\\pi$ times the squared modulus of the filter\'s frequency response. Tip: This formula is the standard bridge used in Wold-vs-Cram\\\'er comparison questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is required for the Wold Decomposition Theorem to apply to a process $\\{X_t\\}$?',opts:['A) $\\{X_t\\}$ must be covariance-stationary with finite second moments','B) $\\{X_t\\}$ must already be known to be purely non-deterministic','C) $\\{X_t\\}$ must be strictly stationary','D) $\\{X_t\\}$ must be Gaussian'],ans:0,exp:'Wold\'s theorem requires only weak (covariance) stationarity and finite variance – it is a purely second-order result with no distributional assumptions. Why other options are wrong: (D) Gaussianity is not required (though it strengthens the innovations to i.i.d. via Doob\'s theorem). (C) strict stationarity is a stronger, unnecessary condition. (B) the theorem determines whether $V_t=0$, it doesn\'t require it as a precondition. Shortcut: Two words to remember: “stationary” + “finite variance” – that\'s the entire precondition list. Tip: A favourite trap: adding an unnecessary Gaussianity or strict-stationarity requirement as a wrong option.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $X_t = \\varepsilon_t + 0.6\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ with $\\varepsilon_t\\sim\\wn(0,2)$, find $\\gamma(1)$.',opts:['A) $0.72$','B) $1.2$','C) $0.6$','D) $2.0$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\gamma(1)=\\sigma^2\\psi_0\\psi_1 = 2(1)(0.6)=1.2$. Why other options are wrong: (C) forgets to multiply by $\\sigma^2$. (D) is just $\\sigma^2$ itself. (A) mistakenly squares $0.6$ and multiplies by $2$. Shortcut: For MA(1), $\\gamma(1)=\\sigma^2\\theta$ directly – a one-line formula worth memorising. Tip: MA(1) lag-1 autocovariance questions are extremely common and quick marks if the formula is automatic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(1) process with $\\phi=0.5,\\sigma^2=1$, what is $\\gamma(0)$?',opts:['A) $1.0$','B) $2.0$','C) $1.333$','D) $0.5$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=1/(1-0.25)=1/0.75=1.333$. Why other options are wrong: (D) is just $\\phi$ itself. (A) is $\\sigma^2$, ignoring the AR(1) inflation factor. (B) uses an incorrect denominator. Shortcut: $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$ is the single most tested AR(1) formula – commit it to memory. Tip: Always double-check whether the question gives $\\phi$ or $\\phi^2$ directly – a common source of careless errors.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following processes is NOT a special case of the general linear process?',opts:['A) MA($q$)','B) Stationary ARMA($p,q$)','C) Stationary AR($p$)','D) A non-stationary random walk'],ans:3,exp:'A random walk $X_t=X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ is non-stationary (its variance grows with $t$), so it does not fit the stationary general linear process framework directly. Why other options are wrong: (A), (C), (B) are all standard stationary special cases of the general linear process, each with an appropriately summable $\\{\\psi_j\\}$. Shortcut: Stationarity is a precondition for GLP membership – check this first before anything else. Tip: Random walks require differencing before Wold\'s theorem framework applies (cf. Beveridge–Nelson).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given $\\psi_0=1,\\psi_1=0.3,\\psi_2=0.1$ (rest zero) and $\\sigma^2=4$, find $\\rho(1)$ (the autocorrelation at lag 1).',opts:['A) $0.4$','B) $0.30$','C) $0.336$','D) $0.275$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=4(1^2+0.3^2+0.1^2)=4(1.10)=4.40$; $\\gamma(1)=4(\\psi_0\\psi_1+\\psi_1\\psi_2)=4(0.3+0.03)=1.32$; hence $\\rho(1)=1.32/4.40=0.30$. Why other options are wrong: (D), (C), (A) result from arithmetic slips such as omitting the $\\psi_1\\psi_2$ cross-term or dividing incorrectly. Shortcut: Always compute $\\gamma(0)$ and $\\gamma(k)$ from the same written-out $\\psi_j$ list, then divide. Tip: Autocorrelation questions often require two formula applications in sequence – budget extra time.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An MA(1) process $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ is invertible if and only if:',opts:['A) $|\\theta| &lt; 1$','B) $\\theta = 0$','C) $|\\theta| &gt; 1$','D) $\\theta &gt; 0$'],ans:0,exp:'Invertibility requires the root of $1+\\theta z=0$, i.e., $z=-1/\\theta$, to lie outside the unit circle, which happens exactly when $|\\theta|&lt;1$. Why other options are wrong: (D) sign is irrelevant to invertibility, only magnitude matters. (C) is the opposite (non-invertible) region. (B) is a degenerate special case, not the general condition. Shortcut: For MA(1): invertible $\\iff |\\theta|&lt;1$ – memorise this single inequality. Tip: Extremely common numerical/conceptual hybrid question – both the condition and its application are tested.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The impulse response function of a general linear process is:',opts:['A) The variance $\\sigma^2$','B) The autocovariance function $\\gamma(k)$','C) The sequence of psi-weights $\\{\\psi_j\\}$','D) The spectral density $f(\\lambda)$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\{\\psi_j\\}$ measures the effect of a unit shock $j$ periods ago on the current value, which is exactly the definition of an impulse response function. Why other options are wrong: (B), (D) are derived quantities computed FROM the psi-weights, not the impulse response itself. (A) is just a scale parameter of the innovations. Shortcut: Impulse response = psi-weights, full stop – a direct vocabulary match. Tip: Econometrics-flavoured questions (VAR, SVAR) often use “impulse response” as a synonym for $\\psi_j$ – recognise this immediately.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(2) process $X_t=0.4X_{t-1}+0.3X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$, what is $\\psi_2$?',opts:['A) $0.16$','B) $0.70$','C) $0.30$','D) $0.46$'],ans:3,exp:'Using $\\psi_j=\\phi_1\\psi_{j-1}+\\phi_2\\psi_{j-2}$: $\\psi_0=1$, $\\psi_1=0.4(1)=0.4$, $\\psi_2=0.4(0.4)+0.3(1)=0.16+0.3=0.46$. Why other options are wrong: (A) is only the first term, omitting $\\phi_2\\psi_0$. (C) is just $\\phi_2$ alone. (B) is an incorrect sum ($0.4+0.3$), ignoring the recursive structure. Shortcut: Always apply the FULL recursion $\\psi_j=\\phi_1\\psi_{j-1}+\\phi_2\\psi_{j-2}$ – don\'t drop either term. Tip: AR(2) psi-weight recursions are a JRF-favourite numerical – practice the recursion until it\'s automatic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which condition characterises strict stationarity of an AR($p$) process $\\phi(B)X_t=\\varepsilon_t$?',opts:['A) Roots of $\\phi(z)=0$ lie inside the unit circle','B) Roots of $\\phi(z)=0$ lie on the unit circle','C) $\\phi(z)$ must have no real roots','D) Roots of $\\phi(z)=0$ lie outside the unit circle'],ans:3,exp:'Stationarity of an AR($p$) process requires all roots of the characteristic polynomial $\\phi(z)=0$ to lie strictly outside the unit circle. Why other options are wrong: (A) describes an explosive, non-stationary process. (B) is the boundary (unit-root) case, itself non-stationary. (C) is irrelevant – complex roots are perfectly compatible with stationarity as long as they lie outside the unit circle. Shortcut: “Outside the unit circle” is the recurring correct phrase for BOTH stationarity ($\\phi$) and invertibility ($\\theta$) conditions. Tip: Do not confuse this with the analogous invertibility condition on $\\theta(z)$ – same rule, different polynomial.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is an example of a deterministic component $V_t$ in the Wold decomposition?',opts:['A) An MA(1) process','B) An AR(1) process with $|\\phi|&lt;1$','C) $\\varepsilon_t \\sim \\wn(0,\\sigma^2)$','D) $R\\cos(\\omega t+\\Phi)$ with random phase $\\Phi$'],ans:3,exp:'A sinusoid with fixed amplitude/frequency but random phase can be perfectly predicted once enough past values are observed – the textbook example of a deterministic process. Why other options are wrong: (C), (B), (A) are all examples of purely non-deterministic processes (their own general linear process representations), not deterministic ones. Shortcut: Look for “random phase/amplitude + fixed periodicity” as the signature of a deterministic component. Tip: This is the standard example used across NET/JRF papers to illustrate $V_t\\ne0$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If a stationary process has spectral density $f(\\lambda)=0$ for $\\lambda$ in some sub-interval of $(-\\pi,\\pi)$, what can be concluded?',opts:['A) The process has a non-trivial deterministic component','B) The process is white noise','C) The process is purely non-deterministic','D) The process is non-stationary'],ans:0,exp:'Since $f(\\lambda)=0$ on a set of positive measure, $\\log f(\\lambda)=-\\infty$ there, violating the Kolmogorov–Szeg\\Ho integrability condition – hence the process has $V_t\\ne0$. Why other options are wrong: (C) is the opposite conclusion. (B) white noise has a strictly positive, flat spectral density, not a zero band. (D) spectral density vanishing on a band says nothing about non-stationarity. Shortcut: Zero spectral density anywhere on a positive-measure set $\\Rightarrow$ deterministic component present – a direct rule. Tip: This spectral-domain identification trick is a favourite JRF-level question type.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Kolmogorov–Szeg\\Ho formula is used to compute:',opts:['A) The autocorrelation function','B) The order of an ARMA model','C) The innovation variance $\\sigma^2$ from the spectral density','D) The psi-weights of an ARMA process'],ans:2,exp:'The formula $\\sigma^2=2\\pi\\exp\\big(\\tfrac1{2\\pi}\\int\\log[2\\pi f(\\lambda)]\\,d\\lambda\\big)$ gives the one-step-ahead prediction error variance directly from the spectral density. Why other options are wrong: (D), (A), (B) are computed by other, unrelated methods (recursive matching, Fourier transform of $\\gamma(k)$, and information criteria, respectively). Shortcut: Kolmogorov–Szeg\\Ho = innovation variance formula. One phrase, one formula – link them directly. Tip: JRF-level; often paired with a question on the Paley–Wiener/integrability condition.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Two MA(1) processes have $\\theta=2$ and $\\theta=0.5$ respectively, and identical autocovariance functions. Which one is the canonical Wold representation?',opts:['A) $\\theta=2$, since it has the larger coefficient','B) Neither, since Wold form must have $\\theta=0$','C) Both equally, since ACVFs match','D) $\\theta=0.5$, since it is invertible'],ans:3,exp:'Only the invertible representation, with root of $1+\\theta z=0$ outside the unit circle ($|\\theta|&lt;1$), coincides with the true Wold/canonical form; $\\theta=0.5$ satisfies this, $\\theta=2$ does not. Why other options are wrong: (A) picks the non-invertible root. (C) matching ACVFs does NOT make both representations equally valid as the Wold form – only one is canonical. (B) misunderstands what “canonical” means here. Shortcut: Between reciprocal roots ($\\theta$ and $1/\\theta$), always choose the one with $|\\theta|&lt;1$. Tip: This reciprocal-root scenario is a recurring, high-value JRF conceptual trap – master it.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The autocovariance generating function of a general linear process is given by:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(B) = \\sigma^2\\psi(B)$','B) $\\gamma(B) = \\sigma\\psi(B)^2$','C) $\\gamma(B) = \\psi(B)/\\sigma^2$','D) $\\gamma(B) = \\sigma^2\\psi(B)\\psi(B^{-1})$'],ans:3,exp:'The autocovariance generating function combines the filter with its “reverse” via $\\psi(B^{-1})$, giving $\\gamma(B)=\\sigma^2\\psi(B)\\psi(B^{-1})$; setting $B=e^{-i\\lambda}$ then yields the spectral density. Why other options are wrong: (A) is missing the reversed-filter factor. (B) incorrectly uses $\\sigma$ and squares $\\psi(B)$ directly instead of using $\\psi(B^{-1})$. (C) inverts the relationship entirely. Shortcut: Remember: $\\gamma(B)$ needs BOTH $\\psi(B)$ and $\\psi(B^{-1})$ – a common omission is dropping the second factor. Tip: This generating-function identity is the direct precursor to the spectral density formula – learn them together.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARMA(1,1) process with $\\phi=0.4,\\theta=0.2$, what is $\\psi_1$?',opts:['A) $0.2$','B) $0.08$','C) $0.4$','D) $0.6$'],ans:3,exp:'For ARMA(1,1), $\\psi_1=\\phi+\\theta=0.4+0.2=0.6$. Why other options are wrong: (A) is just $\\theta$ alone. (C) is just $\\phi$ alone. (B) is the product $\\phi\\times\\theta$, not the sum. Shortcut: ARMA(1,1): $\\psi_1=\\phi+\\theta$ always – a simple addition, easy to verify. Tip: Memorising this one formula covers a large fraction of ARMA(1,1) numerical questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Continuing the previous process ($\\phi=0.4,\\theta=0.2$), what is $\\psi_2$?',opts:['A) $0.24$','B) $0.4$','C) $0.6$','D) $0.08$'],ans:0,exp:'For ARMA(1,1), $\\psi_j=\\phi^{j-1}(\\phi+\\theta)$ for $j\\ge1$, so $\\psi_2=\\phi^1(\\phi+\\theta)=0.4(0.6)=0.24$. Why other options are wrong: (C) is $\\psi_1$, not $\\psi_2$. (B) is just $\\phi$ alone. (D) is $\\phi\\times\\theta$, an unrelated quantity. Shortcut: After computing $\\psi_1=\\phi+\\theta$, every subsequent $\\psi_j$ is just $\\psi_1$ times $\\phi^{j-1}$ – geometric decay from there. Tip: Pairs of sequential questions like Q29–Q30 are common – get $\\psi_1$ right first, everything downstream follows.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly ranks the strength of these conditions, from weakest to strongest?',opts:['A) Square summability $\\to$ Absolute summability','B) Neither implies the other','C) Absolute summability $\\to$ Square summability','D) Both are equivalent'],ans:0,exp:'Absolute summability is the stronger condition and implies square summability (bounded, absolutely summable sequences are automatically square summable); square summability is thus the weaker of the two. Why other options are wrong: (C) reverses the implication direction. (D) they are provably not equivalent ($\\psi_j=1/j$ separates them). (B) there IS a one-directional implication. Shortcut: Direction check: Absolute $\\Rightarrow$ Square, never the reverse. Tip: A pure logic/ordering question – don\'t let the “which is stronger” phrasing confuse the implication direction.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'What does Bessel\'s inequality guarantee in the Hilbert-space proof of Wold\'s theorem?',opts:['A) $\\sum_j \\psi_j^2 \\sigma^2 \\le \\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$, i.e., square summability','B) $V_t = 0$ always','C) $\\psi_j \\ge 0$ for all $j$','D) $\\varepsilon_t$ is Gaussian'],ans:0,exp:'Bessel\'s inequality in the underlying $L^2$ Hilbert space bounds the sum of squared projection coefficients by the norm of the vector being projected, directly yielding $\\sum_j\\psi_j^2\\sigma^2\\le\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)&lt;\\infty$. Why other options are wrong: (C) sign of $\\psi_j$ is unrestricted. (D) Gaussianity is not implied by the proof. (B) $V_t=0$ is not guaranteed in general – only when the process is purely non-deterministic. Shortcut: Bessel\'s inequality is the “engine” that proves square summability inside the Hilbert-space proof. Tip: JRF questions on proof mechanics often single out Bessel\'s inequality as the key technical step.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A process has spectral density $f(\\lambda) = c$ (a positive constant) for all $\\lambda$. What can be said about its Wold decomposition?',opts:['A) It has a large deterministic component','B) It cannot be decomposed by Wold\'s theorem','C) It has $\\psi_j = 1$ for all $j$','D) It is white noise, and purely non-deterministic ($V_t\\equiv0$)'],ans:3,exp:'A flat (constant) spectral density is the frequency-domain signature of white noise; since $f(\\lambda)=c&gt;0$ everywhere, $\\log f(\\lambda)$ is trivially integrable, so the process is purely non-deterministic and equals $\\varepsilon_t$ itself. Why other options are wrong: (A) directly contradicts the flat, everywhere-positive spectral density. (B) Wold\'s theorem applies to every stationary process, including this one. (C) white noise has $\\psi_0=1,\\psi_j=0\\ (j\\ge1)$, not all $\\psi_j=1$. Shortcut: Flat spectral density $\\Leftrightarrow$ white noise $\\Leftrightarrow$ trivial (one-term) Wold representation. Tip: Recognise “constant spectral density” instantly as a white-noise signature – do not overanalyse.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement correctly relates forecast error variance to horizon $\\ell$ in the general linear process framework?',opts:['A) It stays constant regardless of $\\ell$','B) It decreases as $\\ell$ increases','C) It is always equal to $\\sigma^2$','D) It increases with $\\ell$ and saturates at $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\operatorname{Var}(X_{t+\\ell}-\\hat X_t(\\ell)) = \\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{\\ell-1}\\psi_j^2$ is increasing in $\\ell$ (more terms are added) and converges to $\\sigma^2\\sum_{j\\ge0}\\psi_j^2=\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$ as $\\ell\\to\\infty$. Why other options are wrong: (B) forecast uncertainty never decreases with horizon in this framework. (A) it strictly increases (assuming non-zero psi-weights). (C) this only holds exactly at $\\ell=1$. Shortcut: More steps ahead = more accumulated uncertainty, capped at the unconditional variance – an intuitive result worth remembering directly. Tip: This growing-then-saturating pattern is frequently tested via a numerical table (as in the AR(1) forecast-error example).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A process is described as “ergodic.” Which of the following is the correct practical implication for time series estimation?',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2$ must equal 1','B) Sample mean is a consistent estimator of the population mean','C) The process must be white noise','D) The process cannot be estimated'],ans:1,exp:'Ergodicity (which holds for a GLP with i.i.d. innovations and $\\sum|\\psi_j|&lt;\\infty$) is exactly the property that justifies using time averages as consistent estimators of ensemble (population) moments. Why other options are wrong: (C) ergodicity is a much broader property, not restricted to white noise. (D) the opposite is true – ergodicity enables estimation. (A) $\\sigma^2$ can be any positive value. Shortcut: Ergodic $\\to$ time averages work as estimators. This is the entire practical point of the concept. Tip: Connects theory (Wold/GLP) to applied estimation – a common “why does this matter” style question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the vector (multivariate) Wold decomposition, the psi-weights $\\boldsymbol\\Psi_j$ are:',opts:['A) Vectors of length 1','B) Scalars','C) Undefined for multivariate processes','D) Matrices'],ans:3,exp:'For a vector process $\\mathbf X_t$, each $\\boldsymbol\\Psi_j$ is a matrix (e.g., $2\\times2$ for a bivariate process), capturing cross-variable as well as own-variable dynamic effects. Why other options are wrong: (B), (A) understate the dimensionality needed for a vector process. (C) the multivariate extension is well-defined and standard (e.g., in VAR/VMA theory). Shortcut: Univariate $\\psi_j$ (scalar) generalises directly to $\\boldsymbol\\Psi_j$ (matrix) in the vector case – same idea, higher dimension. Tip: Multivariate extensions are increasingly common in JRF-level questions connecting to VAR/econometrics.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Beveridge–Nelson decomposition applies Wold\'s theorem to which transformed series?',opts:['A) The logged series $\\log X_t$','B) The squared series $X_t^2$','C) The differenced series $Y_t = X_t - X_{t-1}$','D) The original (undifferenced) series $X_t$'],ans:2,exp:'Since a unit-root ($I(1)$) series $X_t$ is non-stationary, Wold\'s theorem is applied to its stationary first difference $Y_t=\\nabla X_t$, and the resulting representation is then used to split $X_t$ into a stochastic trend plus a stationary cycle. Why other options are wrong: (B), (A) are not the standard transformations used in this decomposition. (D) the original series is non-stationary and does not directly satisfy Wold\'s theorem\'s preconditions. Shortcut: Beveridge–Nelson always starts from the differenced (stationary) series, not the level series. Tip: Connects this chapter to unit-root/ARIMA theory – a good example of cross-topic JRF questioning.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best describes the relationship between the Wold representation and forecasting?',opts:['A) Forecasting requires an entirely separate theory unrelated to Wold\'s theorem','B) The psi-weights directly give point forecasts and forecast-error variances','C) Only ARMA models can be used for forecasting, not general linear processes','D) Forecasting is only possible for deterministic processes'],ans:1,exp:'Both the point forecast $\\hat X_t(\\ell)=\\mu+\\sum_{j\\ge\\ell}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t+\\ell-j}$ and its error variance $\\sigma^2\\sum_{j&lt;\\ell}\\psi_j^2$ are direct, immediate applications of the psi-weight sequence. Why other options are wrong: (A) forecasting theory is built directly on the Wold/GLP framework. (C) ARMA is simply a convenient special case; forecasting works for any valid GLP. (D) forecasting (with associated uncertainty) is precisely what the non-deterministic part enables. Shortcut: Forecasting = a direct, formulaic application of the psi-weights – not a separate theory. Tip: This “why do we care” application question rounds out the theory-to-practice link tested at NET level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A long-memory (ARFIMA-type) process has $\\psi_j \\sim c\\,j^{d-1}$ for $0&lt;d&lt;1/2$. Which property does it have?',opts:['A) Both square and absolutely summable','B) Absolutely summable but not square summable','C) Square summable but not absolutely summable','D) Neither square nor absolutely summable'],ans:2,exp:'For $0&lt;d&lt;1/2$, $\\psi_j^2 \\sim j^{2d-2}$ with $2-2d&gt;1$, so $\\sum\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$ (square summable); but $\\sum|\\psi_j|\\sim\\sum j^{d-1}$ diverges since $1-d&lt;1$ – so not absolutely summable. Why other options are wrong: (B) reverses the correct classification. (D) square summability does hold. (A) absolute summability fails. Shortcut: Long memory sits exactly between short-memory ARMA (both hold) and non-stationary processes (neither holds) – square summable only. Tip: This is a JRF-level application of the general summability distinction to a named, modern model class (ARFIMA).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly states the FULL set of conditions in Wold\'s Decomposition Theorem?',opts:['A) $\\varepsilon_t$ i.i.d.; $V_t$ Gaussian; decomposition unique','B) $\\varepsilon_t$ white noise; $\\operatorname{Cov}(V_s,\\varepsilon_t)=0\\ \\forall s,t$; $V_t$ deterministic; decomposition unique','C) $\\varepsilon_t$ white noise only; no other conditions needed','D) $\\varepsilon_t$ white noise; $V_t$ arbitrary; decomposition non-unique'],ans:1,exp:'The complete theorem requires: $\\varepsilon_t$ is white noise (specifically, the one-step-ahead prediction error); orthogonality between $V_t$ and all innovations; $V_t$ perfectly predictable from its own past (deterministic); and uniqueness of the overall decomposition. Why other options are wrong: (A) i.i.d. and Gaussianity are NOT required by the base theorem. (D) the decomposition IS unique, not arbitrary. (C) omits the crucial orthogonality and determinism conditions on $V_t$. Shortcut: Use the WOLD mnemonic (White noise, Orthogonal, Linear/one-sided, Deterministic remainder, + Unique) to recall all parts at once. Tip: Complete, all-conditions-at-once questions like this are the hallmark of JRF-level difficulty on this topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Suppose $\\{X_t\\}$ is Gaussian and stationary. According to Doob\'s theorem, what additional property do the Wold innovations $\\varepsilon_t$ have, beyond being uncorrelated white noise?',opts:['A) They are mutually independent (i.i.d.)','B) They become deterministic','C) They have zero variance','D) They become non-stationary'],ans:0,exp:'Under Gaussianity, uncorrelated jointly Gaussian random variables are automatically independent, so Doob\'s theorem upgrades the innovations from merely uncorrelated to fully i.i.d. $N(0,\\sigma^2)$. Why other options are wrong: (B) innovations remain genuinely random, not deterministic. (C) variance remains $\\sigma^2&gt;0$. (D) innovations remain stationary (in fact i.i.d., a strong form of stationarity). Shortcut: Gaussian + uncorrelated = independent – the special linear-algebra fact underlying Doob\'s theorem. Tip: Tests whether you know Wold\'s theorem alone does NOT give independence – Gaussianity is the extra ingredient.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A process has $\\psi(z) = 1-1.5z+0.5z^2$. Determine whether the corresponding filter is invertible.',opts:['A) Always invertible for degree-2 polynomials','B) Not invertible, since at least one root lies inside or on the unit circle','C) Invertible, since both roots lie outside the unit circle','D) Cannot be determined without $\\sigma^2$'],ans:1,exp:'Factoring $1-1.5z+0.5z^2=0.5(z-1)(z-2)$, the roots are $z=1$ and $z=2$. Since $z=1$ lies ON the unit circle (not strictly outside), the filter fails the strict invertibility requirement. Why other options are wrong: (C) one root is exactly on the boundary, not strictly outside. (D) invertibility depends only on root locations, not on $\\sigma^2$. (A) degree does not guarantee invertibility; root locations must always be checked explicitly. Shortcut: Always factor $\\psi(z)$ fully and check EVERY root, not just the “obvious” one. Tip: Boundary (unit-root) cases like $z=1$ are a favourite JRF trap – they fail strict invertibility even though they seem “close enough.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is NOT preserved when passing from a general linear process to its finite ARMA approximation?',opts:['A) Approximate representation of the impulse response function','B) Exact match of all autocovariances at every lag, for any finite order','C) Stationarity, if the ARMA model is properly specified','D) Parsimony (fewer parameters than the full $\\{\\psi_j\\}$ sequence)'],ans:1,exp:'A finite-order ARMA($p,q$) model generally reproduces the true (infinite-order) autocovariance structure only approximately, not exactly at every lag, unless the true process happens to be exactly that finite ARMA form. Why other options are wrong: (C), (A), (D) are all genuinely preserved/achieved properties of a well-specified finite ARMA approximation. Shortcut: “Approximation” means approximation – exact matching at every single lag is NOT guaranteed by a finite-order fit. Tip: Tests precise understanding of the “approximation,” not “exact representation,” nature of fitted ARMA models.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the Hilbert-space proof of Wold\'s theorem, what is $\\mathcal H_{-\\infty}=\\bigcap_t \\mathcal H_t$ used for?',opts:['A) It always equals the whole Hilbert space','B) It represents the innovations subspace','C) It represents the “remote past,” and $V_t$ is the projection of $X_t$ onto it','D) It has no role in the proof'],ans:2,exp:'$\\mathcal H_{-\\infty}$ is the intersection of all past subspaces – the “information” that persists infinitely far back; $V_t$ is defined as the orthogonal projection of $X_t$ onto exactly this remote-past subspace. Why other options are wrong: (B) the innovations subspace is a different object, spanned by $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$. (D) it plays a central, defining role for $V_t$. (A) it is generally a strict (often trivial) subspace, not the whole space. Shortcut: Remote past $\\mathcal H_{-\\infty}$ $\\to$ projection $\\to$ deterministic part $V_t$: this chain IS the proof\'s key construction. Tip: Deep proof-mechanics questions like this test genuine understanding, not just memorised statements.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement correctly distinguishes the Wold Decomposition from the Cram\\\'er Spectral Representation?',opts:['A) Wold decomposes into linear-process + deterministic parts (time domain); Cram\\\'er decomposes into orthogonal sinusoidal components (frequency domain)','B) They are identical theorems with different names','C) Wold is frequency-domain; Cram\\\'er is time-domain','D) Only Cram\\\'er\'s representation applies to purely non-deterministic processes'],ans:0,exp:'Wold\'s theorem operates in the time domain, splitting $X_t$ via innovations and a deterministic remainder; Cram\\\'er\'s representation operates in the frequency domain, expressing $X_t$ as an integral over orthogonal-increment sinusoidal components. Why other options are wrong: (C) reverses the domains. (B) they are related but distinct theorems, addressing different decompositions. (D) both representations apply to every stationary process, regardless of determinism. Shortcut: Wold = time domain (innovations); Cram\\\'er = frequency domain (sinusoids) – keep the domains straight. Tip: One of the most frequently confused theorem pairs at JRF level – always identify the domain first.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is a valid criticism of relying solely on Wold\'s theorem to justify time series modelling?',opts:['A) It requires knowing the true model in advance','B) It is only valid for Gaussian processes','C) It says nothing about higher-order (nonlinear) dependence structure','D) It does not apply to any real-world data'],ans:2,exp:'Wold\'s theorem is a purely second-order (covariance-based) result; two processes can share an identical Wold representation while differing completely in higher-order (e.g., third/fourth cumulant) structure, which nonlinear models like ARCH/GARCH are needed to capture. Why other options are wrong: (D) it applies broadly to stationary data. (A) it is an existence result – it does not require prior knowledge of the true model. (B) it holds for both Gaussian and non-Gaussian processes (with the caveat about i.i.d. innovations under Gaussianity specifically). Shortcut: Wold\'s blind spot: only second moments. Anything beyond covariance structure is invisible to it. Tip: Research-level critique question – shows awareness of the theorem\'s scope and limitations, a JRF hallmark.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a bivariate VAR(1) process $\\mathbf X_t = \\boldsymbol\\Phi \\mathbf X_{t-1}+\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon_t$, the vector Wold weights $\\boldsymbol\\Psi_j$ are given by:',opts:['A) $\\boldsymbol\\Psi_j = \\boldsymbol\\Phi/j$','B) $\\boldsymbol\\Psi_j = j\\boldsymbol\\Phi$','C) $\\boldsymbol\\Psi_j = \\boldsymbol\\Phi$ for all $j$','D) $\\boldsymbol\\Psi_j = \\boldsymbol\\Phi^j$ (matrix power)'],ans:3,exp:'Directly generalising the scalar AR(1) result $\\psi_j=\\phi^j$, the vector VAR(1) case gives $\\boldsymbol\\Psi_j=\\boldsymbol\\Phi^j$, the $j$-th matrix power of the coefficient matrix. Why other options are wrong: (B), (A) do not arise from the correct matrix-power expansion. (C) ignores the decay/evolution of the impulse response entirely. Shortcut: Scalar $\\phi^j$ generalises to matrix $\\boldsymbol\\Phi^j$ – same structure, matrix arithmetic. Tip: Connects univariate results directly to VAR-based multivariate time series/econometrics questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is true regarding the truncation of a Wold representation at lag $N$ to form an MA($N$) approximation?',opts:['A) Truncation never introduces any approximation error','B) Truncation only works for AR processes','C) The approximation error variance converges to 0 as $N\\to\\infty$, by square summability','D) Truncation is invalid unless $\\{\\psi_j\\}$ is absolutely summable'],ans:2,exp:'Since $\\sum_j\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$, the tail sum $\\sum_{j&gt;N}\\psi_j^2\\to0$ as $N\\to\\infty$, so the mean-square approximation error of the MA($N$) truncation vanishes in the limit – justifying finite-order approximation in practice. Why other options are wrong: (A) truncation does introduce a (vanishing) approximation error at any finite $N$. (D) square summability alone suffices for this convergence argument. (B) truncation applies to the general linear process representation regardless of whether it originated from AR, MA, or ARMA structure. Shortcut: Tail of a convergent sum $\\to 0$: this single fact justifies ALL finite-order ARMA approximation practice. Tip: Directly underpins the “ARMA models approximate any GLP” corollary from Stage 1 – know the mechanism, not just the conclusion.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider $\\psi_j = (-1)^j / \\sqrt{j}$ for $j\\ge1$ (and $\\psi_0=1$). Which property holds?',opts:['A) Square summable (converges), not absolutely summable','B) Neither square nor absolutely summable','C) Both hold','D) Absolutely summable, not square summable'],ans:1,exp:'$\\sum_j \\psi_j^2 = \\sum_j 1/j$ (the sign does not matter after squaring) diverges (harmonic series); and $\\sum_j|\\psi_j|=\\sum_j 1/\\sqrt j$ also diverges (a $p$-series with $p=1/2&lt;1$). So neither condition holds. Why other options are wrong: (A), (D), (C) all incorrectly claim square summability holds, but squaring removes the alternating sign and leaves the divergent harmonic series. Shortcut: Always square FIRST, then check convergence – alternating signs do not help square summability. Tip: A layered trap: students often (wrongly) assume alternating series “help” convergence, but squaring erases the sign pattern entirely.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A researcher claims: “Since the innovations $\\varepsilon_t$ in Wold\'s theorem are white noise, they must be stationary; therefore $X_t$ must also be strictly stationary.” Evaluate this claim.',opts:['A) Fully correct','B) Correct, but only for AR processes','C) Incorrect: white noise is never stationary','D) Incorrect: white noise being (weakly) stationary does not imply $X_t$ is STRICTLY stationary without further assumptions'],ans:3,exp:'Weak white noise guarantees $X_t$ is weakly (covariance) stationary; strict stationarity of $X_t$ requires strict stationarity of $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$ (e.g., i.i.d.), which is not automatically implied by mere uncorrelatedness. Why other options are wrong: (A) conflates weak and strict stationarity, a common but incorrect inference. (C) white noise (as defined) is by construction weakly stationary. (B) the same weak-vs-strict distinction applies uniformly, not just to AR processes. Shortcut: Weak stationarity of the innovations gives only weak stationarity of $X_t$ – never assume the “strict” upgrade for free. Tip: Tests the ability to critically evaluate a plausible-sounding but logically flawed argument – classic JRF-style reasoning question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Under what circumstance can a nonlinear time series model (e.g., bilinear or threshold) be needed even when a valid Wold/GLP representation exists?',opts:['A) Never; Wold\'s representation always captures all relevant dependence','B) Only when the process is non-stationary','C) When higher-order (beyond second-moment) dependence structure is present, which linear GLP representations cannot capture','D) Only when $\\sigma^2=0$'],ans:2,exp:'Because Wold\'s theorem is purely a second-order (covariance) result, any dependence carried in higher-order moments/cumulants (e.g., volatility clustering, asymmetric responses) is invisible to it, motivating nonlinear model classes. Why other options are wrong: (A) directly contradicts the well-known second-order limitation of Wold\'s theorem. (B) nonlinear dependence can exist even in stationary series. (D) $\\sigma^2=0$ describes a degenerate, non-random case, unrelated to nonlinearity. Shortcut: “Beyond second moments” is the exact phrase that should trigger “nonlinear models needed, Wold insufficient.” Tip: Research-level awareness question, testing the boundary of what the classical Wold/ARMA paradigm can and cannot do.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Since $\\sum_j|\\psi_j|&lt;\\infty$ implies $\\sum_j\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$, Wold\'s theorem requires absolute summability.” Is this reasoning correct?',opts:['A) No, Wold\'s theorem requires neither condition','B) Yes, but only for ARMA processes','C) Yes, the implication proves the requirement','D) No – Wold\'s theorem only requires the weaker square summability; absolute summability is sufficient but not necessary'],ans:3,exp:'The theorem\'s actual minimum requirement is $\\sum\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$; while absolute summability implies this, it is not the same as saying absolute summability is required – many valid Wold representations (e.g., $\\psi_j=1/j$) satisfy only the weaker condition. Why other options are wrong: (C) confuses “sufficient” with “necessary.” (A) Wold\'s theorem does require square summability – it is not condition-free. (B) the distinction applies to the general theorem, not just ARMA. Shortcut: An implication $A\\Rightarrow B$ never means “$A$ is required for $B$” – watch for this exact logical trap. Tip: This is a pure logic trap layered onto the summability material – read reasoning-based options very carefully.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: A student writes the Wold representation as $X_t = \\mu+\\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ (starting the sum at $j=1$, omitting $j=0$). What is wrong?',opts:['A) Nothing; this is an equivalent, valid representation','B) The sum should start at $j=-1$','C) $\\mu$ should be removed','D) The sum must start at $j=0$ with $\\psi_0=1$, or $\\varepsilon_t$ itself is missing from $X_t$'],ans:3,exp:'Omitting the $j=0$ term removes $\\varepsilon_t$\'s direct contribution to $X_t$, which contradicts the defining normalisation $\\psi_0=1$ and destroys the innovation interpretation of $\\varepsilon_t$. Why other options are wrong: (A) this is NOT equivalent – it silently changes the model. (B) negative indices are not part of the (causal, one-sided) representation at all. (C) $\\mu$\'s presence is unrelated to this indexing error. Shortcut: Always double check the sum\'s starting index – $j=0$ (with $\\psi_0=1$) must be included. Tip: This exact off-by-one indexing trap accounts for a meaningful share of lost marks in numerical MCQs.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “The Wold decomposition proves that ARMA models can perfectly represent any stationary process with finitely many parameters.” True or False?',opts:['A) False, because Wold\'s theorem does not apply to ARMA processes','B) True','C) True, but only for AR models','D) False – ARMA models generally only approximate the (often infinite-order) true Wold representation'],ans:3,exp:'Wold\'s theorem guarantees existence of a (generally infinite-dimensional) representation; finite ARMA models are a parsimonious approximation, exact only when the true process genuinely has finite ARMA structure. Why other options are wrong: (B) overclaims exactness for all processes. (C) the same approximation caveat applies to AR models too. (A) Wold\'s theorem fully applies to ARMA processes – they are a special, compliant case. Shortcut: “Approximate,” not “exactly represent” – this word substitution is the entire trap. Tip: Watch for absolute/overclaiming language (“perfectly,” “exactly,” “always”) as a signal of a likely false statement.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “If $V_t=0$ for a process, then the process must be an ARMA model.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) True, by definition','B) False – $V_t=0$ means purely non-deterministic (a general linear process), which is broader than the ARMA class','C) False, because $V_t=0$ implies white noise only','D) True, but only for MA models'],ans:1,exp:'$V_t=0$ characterises the purely non-deterministic class, which includes ARMA models as a special (finite, absolutely summable) case but also includes general infinite-order or long-memory processes with only square-summable weights. Why other options are wrong: (A), (D) both incorrectly narrow the purely-non-deterministic class down to ARMA/MA specifically. (C) purely non-deterministic is far broader than just white noise (white noise is itself just one trivial example). Shortcut: Purely non-deterministic $\\supsetneq$ ARMA – the general class is strictly larger than the finite-parameter special case. Tip: A set-inclusion trap: always check whether an option collapses a broad class down to a narrower, familiar special case.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Because Wold\'s theorem holds for every stationary process, every stationary process must be forecastable with zero long-run error.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) False – the general linear process (non-deterministic) part has forecast error variance that grows to $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$, not zero, as the horizon increases','B) True, only if $V_t=0$','C) False, because Wold\'s theorem does not apply to forecastable processes','D) True'],ans:0,exp:'Only the deterministic part $V_t$ can be forecast with zero error at any horizon; the non-deterministic (GLP) part has forecast error variance $\\sigma^2\\sum_{j&lt;\\ell}\\psi_j^2$ that grows with $\\ell$ and saturates at $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$, never reaching zero unless $\\psi_j=0$ for all $j\\ge1$. Why other options are wrong: (D) overclaims perfect forecastability for the whole process. (B) even with $V_t=0$ forecast error is generally non-zero at any finite horizon (except in the white-noise-only degenerate case). (C) Wold\'s theorem applies precisely to characterise forecastability, not to exclude it. Shortcut: Only $V_t$ achieves zero forecast error; the GLP part never does (except in degenerate cases). Tip: Conflating “theorem always applies” with “perfect forecasts always possible” is a classic overreach trap.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “White noise has zero autocorrelation, so white noise processes are always independent over time.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) True, but only for continuous-time processes','B) False, because white noise always has non-zero autocorrelation','C) True, by definition','D) False – zero autocorrelation (uncorrelatedness) does not imply independence unless additional assumptions (e.g., joint normality) hold'],ans:3,exp:'Uncorrelatedness is a weaker property than independence in general; only under joint Gaussianity does zero correlation upgrade to independence (as in Doob\'s theorem). Why other options are wrong: (C) directly conflates uncorrelated with independent. (A) the distinction has nothing to do with continuous vs. discrete time. (B) white noise is defined to have exactly zero autocorrelation at all non-zero lags. Shortcut: “Uncorrelated” $\\ne$ “independent,” except under Gaussianity – repeat this until automatic. Tip: This exact confusion is the single most repeated conceptual trap across the entire chapter – expect it in multiple forms.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “A process with $\\psi_j\\to0$ as $j\\to\\infty$ is automatically square summable.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) False, because $\\psi_j\\to0$ contradicts stationarity','B) True','C) False – $\\psi_j\\to0$ is necessary but NOT sufficient for square summability (e.g., $\\psi_j=1/\\sqrt j$)','D) True, only for geometric sequences'],ans:2,exp:'$\\psi_j=1/\\sqrt j \\to 0$ as $j\\to\\infty$, yet $\\sum_j(1/\\sqrt j)^2=\\sum1/j$ diverges (harmonic series) – so convergence to zero alone does not guarantee square summability; the rate of decay matters. Why other options are wrong: (B) overstates a necessary condition as sufficient. (D) geometric sequences do happen to satisfy both, but the claim as stated is about general sequences, where it\'s false. (A) $\\psi_j\\to0$ is entirely compatible with (indeed helpful toward) stationarity, not contradictory to it. Shortcut: Necessary $\\ne$ sufficient: $\\psi_j\\to0$ is necessary but you must still check the summation rate explicitly. Tip: Pairs naturally with the summability-threshold shortcut ($j^{-p}$: absolute needs $p&gt;1$, square needs $p&gt;1/2$).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Since the Wold decomposition is unique, there is only ever one way to write $X_t$ as a function of white noise.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) False, because Wold\'s theorem does not guarantee any representation','B) False – uniqueness refers specifically to the CANONICAL (invertible, one-sided) representation; non-invertible alternatives with the same ACVF can also be written down','C) True, uniqueness means exactly one representation exists in any form','D) True, but only for MA(1) processes'],ans:1,exp:'Uniqueness in Wold\'s theorem refers to the specific, canonical (invertible) representation constructed via best linear prediction; other, non-invertible representations with matching autocovariance functions can still be algebraically written down, but only one coincides with the true Wold form. Why other options are wrong: (C) overstates the scope of uniqueness. (D) the same distinction applies beyond just MA(1). (A) the theorem explicitly guarantees existence of a representation. Shortcut: “Unique” means unique among canonical representations, not literally the only algebraic expression possible. Tip: Connects directly to the reciprocal-root ($\\theta$ vs. $1/\\theta$) trap seen elsewhere in this bank.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Since ARMA models have finitely many parameters, they cannot be general linear processes (which need infinitely many $\\psi_j$).” Evaluate.',opts:['A) True, only MA models qualify as GLPs','B) False – ARMA models have a finite parametric representation but still generate an infinite $\\{\\psi_j\\}$ sequence (e.g., geometrically decaying); they are absolutely a special case of the GLP','C) True','D) False, because GLPs must have finite $\\psi_j$ sequences too'],ans:1,exp:'An ARMA model is described by finitely many parameters ($\\phi_i,\\theta_j$), but expanding $\\theta(B)/\\phi(B)$ as a power series produces infinitely many (typically geometrically decaying) $\\psi_j$ – so it is fully compatible with, and indeed a special case of, the GLP framework. Why other options are wrong: (C) confuses “finite parameters” with “finite psi-weights.” (A) pure MA($q$) is the only case with genuinely finite $\\psi_j$; AR and ARMA both have infinite $\\psi_j$ sequences despite finite parameter counts. (D) GLPs allow (and typically have) infinitely many non-zero $\\psi_j$. Shortcut: Finite parameters $\\ne$ finite psi-weights – ARMA models are the perfect illustration of this distinction. Tip: This trap directly reinforces the “ARMA $\\subset$ GLP” relationship tested throughout this bank.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “A purely non-deterministic process cannot contain any periodic behaviour.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) False – a purely non-deterministic process can still have a peaked (but strictly positive everywhere) spectral density near a periodic frequency, without being perfectly deterministic','B) False, because purely non-deterministic processes have flat spectral densities only','C) True','D) True, by definition of purely non-deterministic'],ans:0,exp:'A process can exhibit strong cyclical/quasi-periodic behaviour (e.g., an AR(2) with complex roots giving a pronounced spectral peak) while remaining purely non-deterministic, as long as its spectral density stays strictly positive everywhere (never exactly zero) so that $\\log f(\\lambda)$ remains integrable. Why other options are wrong: (C), (D) incorrectly equate “purely non-deterministic” with “no periodicity at all.” (B) purely non-deterministic processes can have any shape of strictly positive spectral density, not just flat ones. Shortcut: “Some periodicity in the spectrum” $\\ne$ “deterministic component present” – only a spectral density that hits exactly zero (or has atoms) signals $V_t\\ne0$. Tip: A subtle but important distinction between quasi-cyclical stochastic behaviour (e.g., business cycles) and truly deterministic periodicity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) Every stationary process is a general linear process. (II) Every stationary process has a Wold decomposition (linear process + deterministic part). Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only II','B) Both I and II','C) Neither','D) Only I'],ans:0,exp:'Statement I is false because a stationary process may have a non-trivial deterministic part $V_t\\ne0$, making it NOT purely a general linear process; Statement II is exactly Wold\'s theorem and is always true for stationary, finite-variance processes. Why other options are wrong: (D), (B) incorrectly validate Statement I. (C) incorrectly invalidates the always-true Statement II. Shortcut: I overclaims (drops the possibility of $V_t\\ne0$); II is precisely the theorem statement – always true. Tip: This exact I/II pairing (GLP vs. full Wold decomposition) is a very common statement-based format.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) $\\sum_j\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$ is necessary for the Wold representation to exist. (II) $\\sum_j\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$ is sufficient for the Wold representation to exist. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only I','B) Both I and II','C) Only II','D) Neither'],ans:1,exp:'Square summability is both necessary AND sufficient for the MA($\\infty$) sum to converge in mean square (as stated in the existence theorem) – so both statements are correct. Why other options are wrong: (A), (C) each incorrectly deny one direction of what is, in fact, an if-and-only-if condition. (D) incorrectly denies both. Shortcut: Square summability is an “if and only if” condition – both directions hold simultaneously. Tip: Necessary-and-sufficient statement pairs test whether you know it\'s an iff, not just a one-way implication.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) The innovations $\\varepsilon_t$ are always independent. (II) The innovations $\\varepsilon_t$ are always uncorrelated. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only I','B) Both I and II','C) Only II','D) Neither'],ans:2,exp:'Wold\'s theorem guarantees only that $\\varepsilon_t$ is white noise (uncorrelated); independence additionally requires a stronger assumption such as Gaussianity (Doob\'s theorem). Why other options are wrong: (A), (B) incorrectly claim independence holds unconditionally. (D) incorrectly denies the always-true uncorrelatedness. Shortcut: Uncorrelated: always true. Independent: only true under extra assumptions (e.g., Gaussian). Tip: This I/II pattern directly tests the single most repeated conceptual distinction in the whole chapter.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) Absolute summability of $\\{\\psi_j\\}$ implies square summability. (II) Square summability of $\\{\\psi_j\\}$ implies absolute summability. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Both I and II','B) Only II','C) Neither','D) Only I'],ans:3,exp:'Statement I is a standard, provable implication (bounded absolutely summable sequences are square summable). Statement II is false in general – $\\psi_j=1/j$ is square summable but not absolutely summable, providing a direct counter-example. Why other options are wrong: (B), (A) incorrectly validate Statement II. (C) incorrectly denies the true Statement I. Shortcut: Implication runs one way only: Absolute $\\Rightarrow$ Square, never Square $\\Rightarrow$ Absolute. Tip: A pure logical-direction test paired with the same $\\psi_j=1/j$ counter-example used throughout this bank.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) The deterministic component $V_t$ is correlated with $\\varepsilon_t$. (II) The deterministic component $V_t$ can be predicted with zero error from its own past. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Neither','B) Only II','C) Only I','D) Both I and II'],ans:1,exp:'Statement I is false – $\\operatorname{Cov}(V_t,\\varepsilon_s)=0$ for all $s,t$ by definition. Statement II is exactly the definition of “deterministic” used in Wold\'s theorem. Why other options are wrong: (C), (D) incorrectly validate the false Statement I. (A) incorrectly denies the true Statement II. Shortcut: $V_t$ is UNcorrelated with $\\varepsilon_t$ (not correlated) and IS perfectly self-predictable – opposite of what Statement I claims. Tip: A direct test of the two core defining properties of $V_t$ – know both cold.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Assertion (A): Every stationary, invertible ARMA process has absolutely summable psi-weights. Reason (R): Stationary and invertible ARMA processes have psi-weights that decay geometrically.',opts:['A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A','B) Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','C) A is true, R is false','D) A is false, R is true'],ans:0,exp:'Both statements are true, and geometric decay (R) is exactly the mechanism that makes the weights absolutely summable (A) – a geometric series with ratio $|r|&lt;1$ is always absolutely summable. Why other options are wrong: (B) understates the direct causal link between R and A. (C), (D) incorrectly mark one of the two true statements as false. Shortcut: Geometric decay $\\Rightarrow$ absolute summability – this is a direct, correct causal chain, not a coincidence. Tip: Assertion-Reason questions require checking BOTH truth value AND the explanatory link – don\'t stop at just verifying both are true.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Assertion (A): Wold\'s theorem requires the process to be Gaussian. Reason (R): Only Gaussian processes have well-defined second moments.',opts:['A) A is false, R is false','B) A is false; R is also false, but for a different reason than stated','C) Both A and R are true, but R does not explain A','D) Both A and R are true, and R correctly explains A'],ans:0,exp:'Both statements are false: Wold\'s theorem needs only finite second moments, not Gaussianity (A is false); and many non-Gaussian processes have perfectly well-defined, finite second moments (R is false). Why other options are wrong: (D), (C) incorrectly treat one or both statements as true. (B) is a needlessly complicated restatement of “both false” – (A) is the direct, correct choice. Shortcut: Neither claim survives scrutiny – Gaussianity is not required, and non-Gaussian processes routinely have finite variances. Tip: Watch for assertion-reason pairs where BOTH statements are independently false – don\'t assume at least one must be true.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Assertion (A): The Wold decomposition of a process is unique. Reason (R): Orthogonal projections onto a closed subspace in a Hilbert space are unique.',opts:['A) A is false, R is true','B) A is true, R is false','C) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A','D) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A'],ans:3,exp:'Both are true, and the uniqueness of the Wold decomposition follows precisely because $\\varepsilon_t$ and $V_t$ are constructed via orthogonal projections (onto $\\mathcal H_{t-1}$ and $\\mathcal H_{-\\infty}$ respectively), and such projections are always unique in a Hilbert space. Why other options are wrong: (C) understates the direct proof-theoretic link. (B), (A) incorrectly mark a true statement as false. Shortcut: Uniqueness of Wold\'s decomposition traces directly back to uniqueness of Hilbert-space orthogonal projections – a genuine explanatory chain. Tip: Deep assertion-reason pairs like this test proof-level understanding, appropriate for JRF difficulty.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Assertion (A): A process with $\\psi_j=1$ for all $j\\ge0$ is a valid general linear process. Reason (R): $\\sum_j\\psi_j^2=\\infty$ for this sequence.',opts:['A) A is false, but R is true, and R correctly explains why A is false','B) Both A and R are true, and R explains A','C) Both A and R are false','D) A is true, R is false'],ans:0,exp:'$\\sum_j 1^2=\\infty$ diverges (R is true), which means square summability fails, and hence $\\psi_j=1$ for all $j$ does NOT define a valid mean-square-convergent process (A is false) – R correctly explains why A fails. Why other options are wrong: (B) incorrectly claims A is true. (C) incorrectly claims R is false; R is in fact true. (D) has both the truth values and the logical relationship backwards. Shortcut: A constant, non-decaying $\\psi_j$ sequence is the simplest possible example of failed square summability. Tip: Assertion-Reason format can also correctly conclude “A is false, R is true, R explains why” – don\'t assume A must be true.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Assertion (A): The spectral density of a purely non-deterministic process can never be exactly zero at any frequency. Reason (R): The Kolmogorov–Szeg\\Ho formula requires $\\log f(\\lambda)$ to be integrable over $(-\\pi,\\pi)$.',opts:['A) Both A and R are true, but R does not explain A','B) Both A and R are true, and R correctly explains A','C) Both A and R are false','D) A is false, R is true'],ans:1,exp:'Both are true: if $f(\\lambda_0)=0$ at even a single point, integrability of $\\log f(\\lambda)$ could still hold (a single point has measure zero) – but if $f(\\lambda)=0$ on a set of positive measure, integrability fails, forcing $V_t\\ne0$. Since being “purely non-deterministic” requires the integral condition to hold, $f(\\lambda)$ must be positive almost everywhere, and R directly explains this requirement. Why other options are wrong: (A) understates the direct link between R (the integrability condition) and A (the resulting positivity requirement). (D), (C) incorrectly mark true statements as false. Shortcut: Purely non-deterministic $\\Rightarrow$ $f(\\lambda)&gt;0$ almost everywhere, directly enforced by the integrability requirement in R. Tip: A precise, technically careful assertion-reason pair – note the “almost everywhere” subtlety (isolated zero points are still fine).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Concept) with List II (Correct Formula/Property): p5cmp6.5cm List I | List II; (a) Variance of GLP | (i) $\\sigma^2\\sum_j\\psi_j\\psi_{j+k}$; (b) Autocovariance | (ii) $\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}|\\psi(e^{-i\\lambda})|^2$; (c) Spectral density | (iii) $\\sigma^2\\sum_j\\psi_j^2$; (d) Forecast error variance ($\\ell$ steps) | (iv) $\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{\\ell-1}\\psi_j^2$',opts:['A) (a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)','B) (a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)','C) (a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)','D) (a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)'],ans:3,exp:'Straightforward one-to-one matching of the four core formulas introduced in Stage 1/2: variance uses squared weights, autocovariance uses lag-shifted products, spectral density is the Fourier transform of the ACVF, and forecast error variance sums only the first $\\ell$ squared weights. Why other options are wrong: All other options mismatch at least one pair; carefully verify each formula\'s structure (squared vs. shifted-product vs. Fourier form) before matching. Shortcut: Recognise formulas by their “shape”: $\\sum\\psi_j^2$ (variance/forecast type) vs. $\\sum\\psi_j\\psi_{j+k}$ (autocovariance type) vs. $|\\psi(e^{-i\\lambda})|^2$ (spectral type). Tip: Match-the-following on core formulas is a reliable, high-frequency NET question format – practice recognising formula “shapes” quickly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Person) with List II (Contribution): p5cmp6.5cm List I | List II; (a) Wold | (i) Spectral representation theorem; (b) Cram\\\'er | (ii) Decomposition theorem (1938); (c) Kolmogorov–Szeg\\Ho | (iii) Box–Jenkins ARIMA methodology; (d) Box | Jenkins | (iv) Innovation variance formula',opts:['A) (a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)','B) (a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)','C) (a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)','D) (a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)'],ans:1,exp:'Wold (1938) proved the decomposition theorem; Cram\\\'er developed the spectral representation; the Kolmogorov–Szeg\\Ho formula gives the innovation variance; Box and Jenkins (1970) built the applied ARIMA methodology on top of these theoretical foundations. Why other options are wrong: Other orderings misattribute at least one contribution – a common wrong-option pattern swaps Wold and Kolmogorov, or Cram\\\'er and Box–Jenkins. Shortcut: Chronology helps: Cram\\\'er/Khinchin (1930s) $\\to$ Wold (1938) $\\to$ Kolmogorov (1941) $\\to$ Box–Jenkins (1970). Tip: Historical attribution match-the-following questions reward memorising the timeline table from Stage 1.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Process type) with List II ($\\psi_j$ decay pattern): p5cmp6.5cm List I | List II; (a) MA($q$) | (i) Geometric decay from lag 1, jump at lag 1; (b) AR(1) | (ii) Exactly zero after finite lag $q$; (c) ARMA(1,1) | (iii) Pure geometric decay from lag 0; (d) ARFIMA | (iv) Slow power-law decay',opts:['A) (a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)','B) (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)','C) (a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)','D) (a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)'],ans:1,exp:'MA($q$) truncates exactly at lag $q$; AR(1) decays geometrically from the very first term; ARMA(1,1) jumps at lag 1 ($\\psi_1=\\phi+\\theta$) then decays geometrically; ARFIMA decays as a slow power law, distinguishing long memory from short-memory ARMA. Why other options are wrong: Other combinations swap the decay-pattern descriptions – always visualise (or sketch) the $\\psi_j$ sequence shape before matching. Shortcut: Use the Pattern Recognition table from Stage 2 directly – this question is a re-application of that exact content. Tip: Match-the-following on decay patterns rewards the “shape recognition” skill emphasised throughout the revision module.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Term) with List II (Meaning): p5cmp6.5cm List I | List II; (a) Innovation | (i) Perfectly predictable component; (b) Deterministic part $V_t$ | (ii) One-step-ahead prediction error; (c) Impulse response | (iii) Sequence of psi-weights; (d) Invertibility | (iv) Roots of $\\theta(z)$ outside unit circle',opts:['A) (a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)','B) (a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)','C) (a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)','D) (a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)'],ans:2,exp:'Direct vocabulary matching: innovation = prediction error, $V_t$ = deterministic (zero-error-predictable) part, impulse response = psi-weights, invertibility = root condition on $\\theta(z)$. Why other options are wrong: Other orderings scramble these direct definitional pairs – treat this as a glossary-recall check. Shortcut: This is exactly the Quick Notation/Glossary Recap content from Stage 2 – direct recall, no computation needed. Tip: Vocabulary-matching questions are the fastest points available if terminology is memorised precisely.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Scenario) with List II (Correct Diagnosis): p5.3cmp6.2cm List I | List II; (a) $f(\\lambda)=0$ on an interval | (i) Not a valid stationary process; (b) $\\psi_j\\not\\to0$ | (ii) Non-invertible representation; (c) Root of $\\theta(z)$ inside unit circle | (iii) Deterministic component present; (d) $\\sum\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$, $\\sum|\\psi_j|=\\infty$ | (iv) Square summable only',opts:['A) (a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)','B) (a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)','C) (a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)','D) (a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)'],ans:3,exp:'A vanishing spectral band signals a deterministic component; non-decaying $\\psi_j$ signals an invalid process; a root inside the unit circle signals non-invertibility; and square-but-not-absolutely summable is the long-memory-type “square summable only” diagnosis. Why other options are wrong: Other combinations pair scenarios with the wrong diagnostic conclusion – this tests the Identification Tricks content from Stage 2 directly. Shortcut: This is a direct re-application of the Stage 2 Identification Tricks section – review those boxes if any pairing feels unfamiliar. Tip: Diagnostic match-the-following questions reward the pattern-recognition training built up across Stages 1 and 2.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'$\\psi_0=1,\\psi_1=0.2,\\psi_2=0.1$, rest zero, $\\sigma^2=3$. Find $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$.',opts:['A) $3.15$','B) $1.05$','C) $3.0$','D) $3.30$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=3(1+0.04+0.01)=3(1.05)=3.15$. Correcting: $1^2+0.2^2+0.1^2=1+0.04+0.01=1.05$; $3\\times1.05=3.15$. Why other options are wrong: (C) omits both extra terms. (B) omits the multiplication by $\\sigma^2$. Shortcut: List, square, sum, multiply – four clean steps every time. Tip: Basic variance numericals like this are guaranteed-mark questions if the formula is automatic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'AR(1) with $\\phi=0.3$. Find $\\psi_4$.',opts:['A) $0.0081$','B) $0.3$','C) $0.081$','D) $1.2$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\psi_4=\\phi^4=0.3^4=0.0081$. Why other options are wrong: (C) is off by a factor of 10 (arithmetic slip). (B) is just $\\phi$ itself. (D) is $4\\times0.3$, an incorrect linear (not exponential) computation. Shortcut: $\\psi_j=\\phi^j$ – direct exponentiation, no addition involved. Tip: Watch decimal-point arithmetic carefully when raising small numbers to higher powers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'MA(2): $\\psi_0=1,\\psi_1=0.5,\\psi_2=-0.3$, $\\sigma^2=2$. Find $\\gamma(1)$.',opts:['A) $1.0$','B) $0.35$','C) $-0.15$','D) $0.70$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(1)=\\sigma^2(\\psi_0\\psi_1+\\psi_1\\psi_2)=2[(1)(0.5)+(0.5)(-0.3)]=2[0.5-0.15]=2(0.35)=0.70$. Why other options are wrong: (B) is the correct bracket sum but forgets to multiply by $\\sigma^2=2$. (C) is just the second cross-term alone, missing the first. (A) incorrectly uses only $\\sigma^2\\times\\psi_0\\psi_1$, dropping the second cross-term. Shortcut: Always compute each cross-term separately before summing, especially with negative weights. Tip: Negative $\\psi_j$ values are a common source of sign-error mistakes – work term by term.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'AR(2): $\\phi_1=0.5,\\phi_2=0.2$. Find $\\psi_3$.',opts:['A) $0.29$','B) $0.20$','C) $0.40$','D) $0.325$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\psi_0=1,\\psi_1=0.5,\\psi_2=0.5(0.5)+0.2(1)=0.45,\\psi_3=0.5(0.45)+0.2(0.5)=0.225+0.1=0.325$. Why other options are wrong: (A), (C), (B) result from stopping the recursion early or misapplying one of the two recursive terms. Shortcut: Build the full sequence $\\psi_0,\\psi_1,\\psi_2,\\psi_3$ step by step – don\'t try to jump directly to $\\psi_3$. Tip: AR(2) recursion questions reward writing out ALL intermediate psi-weights, not just the final answer.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'ARMA(1,1): $\\phi=0.6,\\theta=-0.2$. Find $\\psi_2$.',opts:['A) $-0.12$','B) $0.40$','C) $0.60$','D) $0.24$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\psi_1=\\phi+\\theta=0.6-0.2=0.4$; $\\psi_2=\\phi\\cdot\\psi_1=0.6(0.4)=0.24$. Why other options are wrong: (B) is $\\psi_1$, not $\\psi_2$. (A) incorrectly uses $\\theta$ instead of $\\phi$ as the multiplier. (C) is just $\\phi$ alone. Shortcut: Compute $\\psi_1=\\phi+\\theta$ FIRST, then every subsequent term is $\\phi$ times the previous one. Tip: Negative $\\theta$ values are a common source of sign-error slips – double-check the subtraction in $\\psi_1$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'AR(1) with $\\phi=0.5,\\sigma^2=4$. Find the forecast error variance at horizon $\\ell=3$.',opts:['A) $5.25$','B) $5.0$','C) $4.0$','D) $5.33$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{2}\\phi^{2j}=4(1+0.25+0.0625)=4(1.3125)=5.25$. Why other options are wrong: (C) uses only the $\\ell=1$ term. (B) omits the $j=2$ term. (D) is close to the saturation limit $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=5.33$, but that limit is only reached as $\\ell\\to\\infty$, not at $\\ell=3$. Shortcut: Sum exactly $\\ell$ terms ($j=0$ to $\\ell-1$), and stop – don\'t accidentally use the infinite-horizon limit. Tip: Distinguishing a finite-horizon forecast variance from its infinite-horizon limit is a common JRF-level distinction.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'AR(1) with $\\phi=0.5,\\sigma^2=1$. Find the spectral density $f(0)$ (at $\\lambda=0$). [Use $f(\\lambda)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi(1-2\\phi\\cos\\lambda+\\phi^2)}$]',opts:['A) $0.318$','B) $0.159$','C) $0.637$','D) $1.273$'],ans:2,exp:'At $\\lambda=0$: $f(0)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1-2(0.5)(1)+0.25)}=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(0.25)}=\\dfrac{1}{1.5708}\\approx0.637$. Why other options are wrong: (B), (A), (D) result from arithmetic slips in evaluating the denominator or forgetting the $2\\pi$ factor. Shortcut: At $\\lambda=0$, $\\cos\\lambda=1$, simplifying the denominator to $(1-\\phi)^2$ – use this shortcut. Tip: Spectral density evaluation at $\\lambda=0$ (the “zero frequency,” related to the long-run variance) is a common numerical question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'MA(1) with $\\theta=0.5,\\sigma^2=2$. Find $\\gamma(0)$.',opts:['A) $2.0$','B) $1.0$','C) $3.0$','D) $2.5$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)=2(1+0.25)=2.5$. Why other options are wrong: (A) omits the $\\theta^2$ contribution. (B) incorrectly uses just $\\theta^2\\times$ something. (C) overestimates by using $\\theta$ itself rather than $\\theta^2$. Shortcut: MA(1) variance formula: $\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)$ – a very frequently used one-liner. Tip: This is one of the fastest-to-verify numerical facts on the entire syllabus – should take under 10 seconds.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'MA(1) with $\\theta=-1.5$. Is this process invertible?',opts:['A) No, since the root $-1/\\theta=0.667$ lies inside the unit circle','B) Yes, since $|\\theta|&gt;1$','C) Cannot be determined','D) Yes, since the root $-1/\\theta=0.667$ lies inside the unit circle'],ans:0,exp:'The root of $1+\\theta z=0$ is $z=-1/\\theta=-1/(-1.5)=0.667$, which lies inside the unit circle ($|0.667|&lt;1$), so the process is NOT invertible. Why other options are wrong: (D) correctly computes the root but draws the wrong conclusion (inside the circle means non-invertible). (B) larger $|\\theta|$ actually makes invertibility LESS likely, not more. (C) the root is fully computable and the conclusion is determinate. Shortcut: Root inside unit circle = NOT invertible; this is worth re-deriving every time rather than guessing. Tip: A deliberately confusing pairing of correct arithmetic with a wrong conclusion – always finish the logical step, not just the computation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'AR(1) with $\\phi=0.9,\\sigma^2=1$. Find $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$.',opts:['A) $9.0$','B) $1.0$','C) $5.26$','D) $0.19$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=1/(1-0.81)=1/0.19\\approx5.26$. Why other options are wrong: (B) ignores the AR(1) inflation factor entirely. (D) inverts the correct fraction. (A) uses an unrelated computation. Shortcut: As $\\phi\\to1$, variance blows up rapidly – a useful sanity check ($\\phi=0.9$ should give a notably larger-than-1 variance). Tip: High-persistence ($\\phi$ close to 1) numericals test whether you notice the amplification effect, not just formula recall.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'$\\psi_j = 0.9^j$ for $j\\ge0$. Find $\\sum_{j\\ge0}\\psi_j^2$.',opts:['A) $10.0$','B) $1.9$','C) $5.26$','D) $0.81$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\sum_j (0.9^j)^2 = \\sum_j 0.81^j = \\dfrac{1}{1-0.81}=\\dfrac{1}{0.19}\\approx5.26$ (geometric series with ratio $0.81$). Why other options are wrong: (D) is just the ratio itself, not the sum. (A) misapplies $1/(1-\\phi)$ instead of $1/(1-\\phi^2)$. (B) is an unrelated computation. Shortcut: Note this equals the AR(1) variance formula exactly (with $\\sigma^2=1$) – same geometric-series structure. Tip: Recognise that “sum of squared geometric weights” is mathematically identical to the AR(1) variance computation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'ARMA(1,1): $\\phi=-0.4,\\theta=0.7$. Find $\\psi_1$.',opts:['A) $1.1$','B) $-1.1$','C) $-0.3$','D) $0.3$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\psi_1=\\phi+\\theta=-0.4+0.7=0.3$. Why other options are wrong: (C) has a sign error. (A), (B) incorrectly subtract or use the wrong operation entirely. Shortcut: Even with a negative $\\phi$, the formula $\\psi_1=\\phi+\\theta$ is unchanged – just do the signed addition carefully. Tip: Negative AR coefficients are used deliberately in JRF papers to test sign-handling under the same formula.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'AR(1) with $\\phi=0.7,\\sigma^2=1$. Find $\\gamma(2)$.',opts:['A) $1.961$','B) $0.7$','C) $0.49$','D) $0.961$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(2)=\\sigma^2\\phi^2/(1-\\phi^2)=1(0.49)/(1-0.49)=0.49/0.51\\approx0.961$. Why other options are wrong: (C) is just $\\phi^2$ alone, missing the denominator. (B) is just $\\phi$ itself. (A) is an incorrect arithmetic combination. Shortcut: $\\gamma(k)=\\sigma^2\\phi^k/(1-\\phi^2)$ for AR(1) – notice the SAME denominator as $\\gamma(0)$, just with $\\phi^k$ in the numerator. Tip: Compute $\\gamma(0)$ first as a reference, then $\\gamma(k)=\\gamma(0)\\times\\phi^k$ is a fast shortcut.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'AR(1) with $\\phi=0.6$. Find $\\rho(1)$ (autocorrelation at lag 1).',opts:['A) $0.16$','B) $0.6$','C) $0.36$','D) $1.0$'],ans:1,exp:'For AR(1), $\\rho(k)=\\phi^k$ directly, so $\\rho(1)=\\phi=0.6$. Why other options are wrong: (C) is $\\phi^2$, which would be $\\rho(2)$, not $\\rho(1)$. (D) is the (trivial) value at lag 0. (A) is an unrelated computation. Shortcut: AR(1) autocorrelations equal $\\phi^k$ exactly – no further computation needed for this specific model. Tip: One of the fastest possible numerical questions on this topic – should take only a few seconds.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'AR(2): $\\phi_1=0.6,\\phi_2=-0.1$. Find $\\psi_2$.',opts:['A) $0.26$','B) $0.36$','C) $-0.10$','D) $0.50$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\psi_0=1,\\psi_1=0.6,\\psi_2=\\phi_1\\psi_1+\\phi_2\\psi_0=0.6(0.6)+(-0.1)(1)=0.36-0.1=0.26$. Why other options are wrong: (B) omits the negative $\\phi_2$ contribution. (D), (C) result from other incomplete applications of the recursion. Shortcut: With a negative $\\phi_2$, the recursion SUBTRACTS on that term – don\'t forget the sign. Tip: Negative AR(2) coefficients test careful sign-handling in the two-term recursion.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'MA(2): $\\psi_0=1,\\psi_1=-0.4,\\psi_2=0.2$, $\\sigma^2=5$. Find $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$.',opts:['A) $7.0$','B) $4.0$','C) $6.0$','D) $5.0$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=5(1^2+(-0.4)^2+0.2^2)=5(1+0.16+0.04)=5(1.2)=6.0$. Why other options are wrong: (D) omits the extra terms. (B), (A) result from arithmetic slips in the sum of squares. Shortcut: Squaring removes the negative sign automatically – don\'t let $\\psi_1&lt;0$ cause hesitation. Tip: Negative psi-weights never reduce the variance contribution – squaring always yields a non-negative addition.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Continuing the previous process, find $\\gamma(1)$.',opts:['A) $0.4$','B) $-0.4$','C) $-2.4$','D) $2.4$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\gamma(1)=5[\\psi_0\\psi_1+\\psi_1\\psi_2]=5[(1)(-0.4)+(-0.4)(0.2)]=5[-0.4-0.08]=5(-0.48)=-2.4$. Why other options are wrong: (D) drops the negative sign. (B), (A) use only one of the two cross-terms. Shortcut: Autocovariances CAN be negative – don\'t assume a positive answer must be correct. Tip: This process (with an MA(2) featuring alternating-sign weights) illustrates that negative autocorrelation is a perfectly normal, testable outcome.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'ARMA(1,1): $\\phi=0.5,\\theta=0.5$. Find $\\psi_3$.',opts:['A) $1.0$','B) $0.5$','C) $0.125$','D) $0.25$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\psi_1=\\phi+\\theta=1.0$; then $\\psi_j=\\phi^{j-1}\\psi_1$ for $j\\ge1$, so $\\psi_3=\\phi^2\\psi_1=0.25(1.0)=0.25$. Why other options are wrong: (C) uses $\\phi^3$ instead of $\\phi^2\\psi_1$. (B) is $\\phi$ alone. (A) is $\\psi_1$ itself, not $\\psi_3$. Shortcut: Once $\\psi_1$ is found, every later term is just $\\psi_1$ times a power of $\\phi$ – a simple geometric continuation. Tip: With $\\phi=\\theta=0.5$, $\\psi_1$ conveniently equals exactly 1 – use such round numbers as a computation shortcut when they appear.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'AR(1) with $\\phi=0.8,\\sigma^2=1$. What is the limiting (as $\\ell\\to\\infty$) forecast error variance?',opts:['A) $2.78$','B) $0.36$','C) $0.8$','D) $1.0$'],ans:0,exp:'As $\\ell\\to\\infty$, the forecast error variance converges to $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=1/(1-0.64)=1/0.36\\approx2.78$. Why other options are wrong: (D) is just $\\sigma^2$, the $\\ell=1$ value, not the limit. (C) is $\\phi$ itself. (B) is $1-\\phi^2$, the denominator alone. Shortcut: The limiting forecast error variance is ALWAYS exactly $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$ – a direct, memorisable shortcut. Tip: “As $\\ell\\to\\infty$” should immediately trigger recall of the saturation-at-$\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$ result, bypassing any summation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following extensions of Wold\'s theorem is used to handle time-varying (non-stationary) spectral behaviour?',opts:['A) Yule–Walker equations','B) Priestley\'s evolutionary spectra','C) The Kalman filter alone','D) The Durbin–Levinson algorithm'],ans:1,exp:'Priestley\'s evolutionary spectral theory generalises the fixed-$\\{\\psi_j\\}$ Wold framework to allow time-varying spectral representations, appropriate for locally stationary processes. Why other options are wrong: (C) the Kalman filter addresses state-space recursive estimation, a related but distinct tool. (A), (D) are standard estimation techniques for stationary AR models, not non-stationary extensions. Shortcut: “Evolutionary spectra” is the specific keyword for time-varying, non-stationary generalisations of Wold\'s framework. Tip: Research-level awareness questions like this test breadth of exposure to the modern literature, not computation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A Volterra series expansion generalises the Wold/general linear process representation by:',opts:['A) Adding higher-order (nonlinear) kernel terms involving products of past shocks','B) Restricting to finite-order MA models only','C) Requiring Gaussian innovations','D) Removing the white noise assumption entirely'],ans:0,exp:'A Volterra expansion adds terms like $\\sum_{i,j}\\psi_{ij}\\varepsilon_{t-i}\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ (and higher-order analogues) on top of the standard linear term, capturing nonlinear dependence invisible to the purely linear Wold representation. Why other options are wrong: (D) the white noise driving sequence is retained, just combined nonlinearly. (B) Volterra expansions are, if anything, MORE general, not restricted to finite MA. (C) no Gaussianity requirement is imposed. Shortcut: Volterra series = Wold representation + nonlinear (product) correction terms. Tip: This connects the “second-order only” limitation of Wold\'s theorem (tested elsewhere in this bank) to its actual generalisation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In high-dimensional VAR/factor model settings, why might the classical (fixed-dimension) Wold decomposition become problematic?',opts:['A) It no longer holds mathematically in any dimension','B) White noise cannot exist in high dimensions','C) Stationarity becomes impossible in high dimensions','D) The number of free parameters in $\\boldsymbol\\Psi_j$ grows quadratically with dimension, creating estimation challenges'],ans:3,exp:'Each matrix $\\boldsymbol\\Psi_j$ has $n^2$ entries for an $n$-dimensional process, so as $n$ grows, the number of parameters to estimate explodes – motivating dimension-reduction techniques (factor models, sparse VAR, regularisation) alongside the theoretical Wold framework. Why other options are wrong: (A) the mathematical theorem still holds in any finite dimension. (B) white noise is well-defined in any dimension. (C) stationarity is a property independent of dimensionality. Shortcut: The theorem still holds; the practical/estimation challenge is purely about parameter proliferation with dimension. Tip: A “why is this hard in practice” question – distinguishes theoretical validity from practical estimability.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The connection between the Wold decomposition\'s remote-past subspace $\\mathcal H_{-\\infty}$ and ergodic theory is best described as:',opts:['A) Unrelated; ergodic theory has no connection to Wold\'s theorem','B) $\\mathcal H_{-\\infty}$ is always equal to the whole probability space','C) $\\mathcal H_{-\\infty}$ relates to the tail $\\sigma$-field, echoing conditions from the Kolmogorov 0–1 law','D) Ergodic theory only applies to i.i.d. sequences, so it cannot connect to $\\mathcal H_{-\\infty}$'],ans:2,exp:'The remote-past subspace $\\mathcal H_{-\\infty}=\\bigcap_t\\mathcal H_t$ is analogous to a tail $\\sigma$-field: a purely non-deterministic process is one whose remote past carries no residual predictive information, closely paralleling the triviality conditions in the Kolmogorov 0–1 law. Why other options are wrong: (A) there IS a meaningful conceptual connection. (B) $\\mathcal H_{-\\infty}$ is generally a small (often trivial) subspace, not the whole space. (D) ergodic theory applies far more broadly than just i.i.d. sequences, including to general stationary/mixing processes. Shortcut: Remote past $\\approx$ tail information – the same intuition underlying both Wold\'s theorem and 0–1-law-type results. Tip: A genuinely research-flavoured question linking classical time series theory to probability theory foundations.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best describes an open or actively developing extension of the classical Wold/GLP framework?',opts:['A) MA($q$) models, since finite-order representations are still being defined','B) The AR(1) model, since it was only recently discovered','C) White noise processes, since their properties remain unknown','D) Functional time series analysis, extending Wold-type representations to curve-valued (rather than scalar) observations'],ans:3,exp:'Functional time series analysis (where each “observation” is an entire curve, e.g., a daily yield curve or temperature profile) is a genuinely active research area extending Wold-type linear representations to infinite-dimensional (functional/Hilbert-space-valued) settings. Why other options are wrong: (B), (C), (A) all refer to classical, fully settled, decades-old results – not open research areas. Shortcut: “Functional” and “high-dimensional” are the key buzzwords signalling genuinely open extensions of this classical theory. Tip: Research-level questions often ask you to distinguish settled classical theory from genuinely active extensions – know which is which.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement best captures the core limitation of the Wold decomposition as a complete description of a stationary time series\' dependence structure?',opts:['A) It cannot be combined with any other model class','B) It is only valid for discrete-time processes','C) It captures only linear, second-order dependence; conditional heteroskedasticity and other nonlinear features require separate models','D) It fails to exist for most real-world processes'],ans:2,exp:'Because Wold\'s theorem is fundamentally a covariance-based (second-order) result, phenomena such as volatility clustering (captured by ARCH/GARCH) or asymmemtric/regime-switching dynamics (captured by threshold or Markov-switching models) lie entirely outside what the linear Wold representation can express. Why other options are wrong: (D) the decomposition exists for essentially every stationary, finite-variance process. (B) analogous continuous-time extensions exist as well. (A) in practice, Wold-based linear models (e.g., ARMA) are routinely combined with nonlinear volatility models (e.g., ARMA-GARCH). Shortcut: The single sentence to remember: “linear and second-order, not more.” Tip: This summarising limitation question is a natural bridge from Wold\'s theorem to the broader (nonlinear, volatility) time series literature covered elsewhere in Unit VII.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Why is the Wold decomposition sometimes described as providing only a “black box” understanding of a time series?',opts:['A) Because it gives no structural/economic interpretation of the psi-weights beyond their statistical role','B) Because it only applies to black-box machine learning models','C) Because it cannot be computed at all','D) Because $\\sigma^2$ is always unknown'],ans:0,exp:'The Wold representation is a purely statistical/reduced-form description; it does not, by itself, explain WHY the psi-weights take the values they do in terms of underlying economic or physical mechanisms – structural models (e.g., DSGE, structural VAR) are needed for that. Why other options are wrong: (C) it is very much computable (via ARMA fitting or the innovations algorithm). (B) it applies to any stationary process, not specifically ML models. (D) $\\sigma^2$ can be estimated; unknown-ness is not the reason for the “black box” label. Shortcut: “Black box” = statistically valid but structurally uninterpreted – a critique of interpretation, not existence. Tip: This connects the theory to the structural-vs-reduced-form debate common in applied econometrics discussions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'How does the Wold representation relate to state-space (Kalman filter) models?',opts:['A) Both use an “innovations” concept: the Kalman filter\'s prediction error generalises the Wold innovation to time-varying, possibly non-stationary systems','B) They are unrelated frameworks','C) State-space models cannot produce a Wold representation','D) The Kalman filter only works for deterministic processes'],ans:0,exp:'The Kalman filter\'s recursive one-step-ahead prediction error is a direct generalisation of the Wold innovation concept to (possibly time-varying, non-stationary) state-space systems, preserving the same “residual after best prediction” logic. Why other options are wrong: (B) they share a deep conceptual link via the innovations idea. (C) a time-invariant, stationary state-space model\'s steady-state output IS a Wold/GLP representation. (D) the Kalman filter is specifically designed for systems with genuine randomness, not deterministic ones. Shortcut: Kalman filter innovation = Wold innovation, generalised to time-varying/state-space settings. Tip: A useful cross-topic link if state-space models or the Kalman filter appear elsewhere in your syllabus.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is the most accurate statement about estimating the TRUE (infinite-order) Wold representation from a finite data sample?',opts:['A) It is only estimable for Gaussian processes','B) It can be estimated exactly with any finite sample size','C) It requires infinite data and is therefore never useful in applications','D) It cannot be estimated exactly; only a finite-order approximation (e.g., via AIC/BIC-selected ARMA) is feasible in practice'],ans:3,exp:'With any finite sample, only a finite number of parameters can be reliably estimated; in practice, a finite-order ARMA (chosen via information criteria like AIC/BIC) serves as the best feasible, parsimonious approximation to the true, generally infinite-order Wold representation. Why other options are wrong: (B) exact estimation of an infinite-dimensional object from finite data is not possible. (C) despite this limitation, finite approximations remain highly useful in applications (this is the entire basis of applied ARMA modelling). (A) estimation is feasible for both Gaussian and non-Gaussian processes, though the specific estimation method may differ. Shortcut: Finite data $\\to$ finite-order approximation only, always – this is a fundamental, unavoidable practical limitation. Tip: A good “bring it all together” question linking the theoretical existence result to the practical realities of applied time series modelling.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In what sense can Wold\'s Decomposition Theorem be viewed as a special case of a more general Hilbert-space projection theory result?',opts:['A) It cannot be viewed this way; it is entirely self-contained','B) It is a specific application of orthogonal decomposition of a vector into a subspace projection plus an orthogonal complement, applied to the $L^2$ space of the process','C) The decomposition uses only algebraic, not geometric, reasoning','D) Hilbert space theory only applies to finite-dimensional vectors'],ans:1,exp:'Viewing each $X_t$ as a vector in the (generally infinite-dimensional) Hilbert space $L^2(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$, Wold\'s decomposition is exactly the general Hilbert-space result that any vector decomposes uniquely into its projection onto a closed subspace (the remote past, giving $V_t$) plus an orthogonal remainder (spanned by the innovations, giving the GLP part). Why other options are wrong: (A) it is a direct, well-recognised application of general Hilbert-space geometry. (D) Hilbert space theory is specifically built to handle infinite-dimensional spaces like $L^2(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$. (C) the proof is fundamentally geometric (orthogonal projections), not merely algebraic manipulation. Shortcut: Wold\'s theorem = orthogonal decomposition theorem, applied to the specific Hilbert space generated by a stationary process. Tip: Understanding this abstraction is genuinely JRF/research-level and ties together probability, functional analysis, and time series in one unified picture.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An MA(q) process is:',opts:['A) Always stationary, regardless of the values of $\\theta_1,\\dots,\\theta_q$','B) Stationary only if $q=1$','C) Never stationary','D) Stationary only if $|\\theta_j|&lt;1$ for all $j$'],ans:0,exp:'Since $X_t$ is a finite linear combination of white noise terms, its mean and ACVF never depend on $t$, so MA(q) is unconditionally stationary. Why other options are wrong: (D) confuses this with the invertibility condition; (C), (B) are simply false. Shortcut: “MA = always stationary” – one-line recall. Tip: The single most repeated true/false fact in this chapter.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1): $X_t=\\mu+\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$, the variance is:',opts:['A) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2(1+\\theta^2)$','B) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2(1-\\theta^2)$','C) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2\\theta$','D) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2\\theta^2$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=\\operatorname{Var}(\\varepsilon_t)+\\theta^2\\operatorname{Var}(\\varepsilon_{t-1})=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2(1+\\theta^2)$, since $\\varepsilon_t,\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ are uncorrelated. Why other options are wrong: (D) omits the leading unit term; (B) uses the wrong sign (that pattern belongs to AR(1) variance formula); (C) is not a valid variance expression. Shortcut: Square and sum: $1^2+\\theta^2$. Tip: Extremely frequently asked direct numerical.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The ACF of an MA(q) process is zero for:',opts:['A) Lags $k \\le q$','B) All lags','C) Lags $k &gt; q$','D) Only at $k=q$'],ans:2,exp:'$X_t$ and $X_{t-k}$ share no common shock term once $k&gt;q$, so $\\gamma(k)=0$ and hence $\\rho(k)=0$. Why other options are wrong: (B) is false since $\\rho(0)=1$ trivially; (A) is backward; (D) restricts to a single lag incorrectly. Shortcut: “ACF cuts off after $q$” – direct recall. Tip: The core identification fact of this entire chapter.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'MA(1) is invertible if:',opts:['A) $\\theta$ can be any real number','B) $|\\theta|&gt;1$','C) $\\theta=0$ only','D) $|\\theta|&lt;1$'],ans:3,exp:'The root of $1+\\theta z=0$ is $z=-1/\\theta$; requiring $|z|&gt;1$ gives $|\\theta|&lt;1$. Why other options are wrong: (B) is the inverted (non-invertible) region; (C), (A) are incorrect restrictions. Shortcut: Memorize directly: MA(1) invertible $\\iff |\\theta|&lt;1$. Tip: One of the most repeated one-liners across NET cycles.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which function is primarily used to identify the order $q$ of an MA(q) process?',opts:['A) Periodogram','B) Spectral distribution function','C) ACF','D) PACF'],ans:2,exp:'The ACF cuts off sharply after lag $q$, providing a direct visual/numerical identification tool. Why other options are wrong: PACF tails off (does not cut off) for MA; periodogram/spectral distribution are frequency-domain tools, not the primary MA-order identification method. Shortcut: “MA $\\to$ ACF cutoff.” Tip: Frequently paired with the mirror-image AR fact (PACF cutoff for AR).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) with $\\theta=0.6$, $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=2$, find $\\gamma(1)$.',opts:['A) $0.6$','B) $0.36$','C) $1.2$','D) $2.72$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\gamma(1)=\\theta\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=0.6\\times2=1.2$. Why other options are wrong: (A) forgets to multiply by $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$; (D) is $\\gamma(0)$; (B) is $\\theta^2$, an unrelated quantity. Shortcut: Direct one-step substitution into $\\gamma(1)=\\theta\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$. Tip: A guaranteed quick numerical.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The mean of an MA(q) process $X_t=\\mu+\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\cdots+\\theta_q\\varepsilon_{t-q}$ is:',opts:['A) $0$','B) $\\mu$','C) $\\mu+\\sum\\theta_j$','D) $\\mu\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$'],ans:1,exp:'Since $E(\\varepsilon_{t-j})=0$ for all $j$, taking expectations leaves only $\\mu$. Why other options are wrong: (A) ignores $\\mu$ entirely; (C), (D) incorrectly include extraneous terms. Shortcut: All shock terms vanish in expectation – trivial recall. Tip: Often the easy first part of a multi-part numerical.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2): $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\theta_2\\varepsilon_{t-2}$, $\\gamma(2)$ equals:',opts:['A) $\\theta_2\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','B) $(\\theta_1+\\theta_2)\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','C) $\\theta_1\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','D) $\\theta_1\\theta_2\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$'],ans:0,exp:'Using $\\gamma(k)=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2\\sum_{j=0}^{q-k}\\theta_j\\theta_{j+k}$ with $q=2,k=2$: only $j=0$ survives, giving $\\theta_0\\theta_2=\\theta_2$. Why other options are wrong: (C) is unrelated to lag 2; (B) is not the correct combination; (D) resembles the wrong dot-product term. Shortcut: At the maximum lag $k=q$, only the first-and-last coefficient product survives. Tip: Very frequently tested MA(2) formula.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'MA(q) is a special case of ARMA(p,q) with:',opts:['A) $q=0$','B) $p=1$','C) $p=q$','D) $p=0$'],ans:3,exp:'ARMA(0,q) has no AR terms, reducing exactly to the MA(q) model. Why other options are wrong: $q=0$ would give a pure AR model instead; the other options are arbitrary/incorrect. Shortcut: “No AR part $\\Rightarrow p=0$.” Tip: Useful for connecting MA to the broader ARMA framework.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1), the maximum possible value of $|\\rho(1)|$ is:',opts:['A) $0.75$','B) $0.5$','C) $0.25$','D) $1$'],ans:1,exp:'Maximizing $\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$ over $\\theta$ gives a maximum value of $0.5$ at $\\theta=1$. Why other options are wrong: (D) would apply to a deterministic/degenerate case, not MA(1); (C), (A) are not the correct extremal value. Shortcut: Memorize the bound $|\\rho(1)|\\le 0.5$ directly. Tip: A frequently overlooked but commonly tested bound.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is the correct backshift-operator form of MA(q)?',opts:['A) $\\theta(B)X_t=\\varepsilon_t$','B) $X_t=\\theta(B)^{-1}\\varepsilon_t$','C) $\\phi(B)X_t=\\varepsilon_t$','D) $X_t-\\mu=\\theta(B)\\varepsilon_t$'],ans:3,exp:'$X_t-\\mu=\\theta(B)\\varepsilon_t$ with $\\theta(B)=1+\\theta_1B+\\cdots+\\theta_qB^q$ directly captures the MA(q) definition. Why other options are wrong: (C) is the AR(p) form; (A) has the operator misapplied; (B) describes the inverted (AR-type) relationship, relevant only under invertibility. Shortcut: MA operator acts on $\\varepsilon_t$, not on $X_t$. Tip: Tests basic notation fluency, common in NET.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement correctly distinguishes MA from AR processes?',opts:['A) MA has infinite memory, AR has finite memory','B) Both have infinite memory','C) MA has finite memory, AR has (theoretically) infinite memory','D) Both have finite memory'],ans:2,exp:'MA(q) depends on only $q$ past shocks (finite memory); AR(p), through its recursive structure, depends on the entire past history (infinite memory in the MA($\\infty$) representation sense). Why other options are wrong: They reverse or misstate the memory property. Shortcut: “MA = bounded memory, AR = unbounded memory.” Tip: Frequently used to justify the ACF/PACF cutoff-vs-tailing-off contrast.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) with $\\theta=-0.4$, is the process invertible?',opts:['A) Only if $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2&gt;1$','B) Yes, since $|-0.4|&lt;1$','C) No, since $\\theta$ is negative','D) Cannot be determined'],ans:1,exp:'Invertibility depends only on $|\\theta|&lt;1$; the sign of $\\theta$ is irrelevant. Why other options are wrong: (C) incorrectly links sign to invertibility; (D), (A) are irrelevant distractors. Shortcut: Check magnitude only, ignore sign. Tip: Common trap testing whether students confuse sign with magnitude.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If the sample ACF of a series shows significant spikes at lags 1 and 2 only, with all further lags statistically insignificant, the suggested model is:',opts:['A) AR(2)','B) MA(2)','C) White noise','D) ARMA(1,1)'],ans:1,exp:'A sharp ACF cutoff after lag 2 is the classic MA(2) signature. Why other options are wrong: AR(2) would show a gradually decaying/damped-sinusoidal ACF, not a sharp cutoff; ARMA(1,1) would show gradual decay in both ACF and PACF; white noise would show no significant spikes at all. Shortcut: “Sharp ACF cutoff at lag $k^*$ $\\Rightarrow$ MA($k^*$).” Tip: A classic applied identification question, very frequently asked.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) with $\\theta=0$, the process reduces to:',opts:['A) Random walk','B) Non-stationary process','C) AR(1)','D) White noise'],ans:3,exp:'With $\\theta=0$, $X_t=\\mu+\\varepsilon_t$, which is simply white noise around a constant mean. Why other options are wrong: None of the other model types match this degenerate case. Shortcut: Setting all MA coefficients to zero always yields white noise. Tip: A simple boundary-case sanity check question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2) with $\\theta_1=0.5,\\theta_2=0.5$, check invertibility using $\\theta_1+\\theta_2&lt;1$:',opts:['A) Not satisfied, since $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=1.0$, violating strict inequality','B) Automatically invertible regardless','C) Satisfied, since $1.0&lt;1$ is true','D) Cannot be checked with this condition alone'],ans:0,exp:'$\\theta_1+\\theta_2=1.0$ does not satisfy the strict inequality $&lt;1$; this is a boundary (non-invertible) case. Why other options are wrong: (C) misreads the boundary value as satisfying the strict inequality; (D) is incorrect since all three MA(2) conditions collectively determine invertibility, and this one alone already fails; (B) is false. Shortcut: Always check for strict inequality at boundary values – a very common trap. Tip: Boundary-value invertibility checks are a favourite JRF trick.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best describes the PACF of an MA(1) process?',opts:['A) Identically zero at all lags','B) Cuts off after lag 1','C) Equal to the ACF at every lag','D) Tails off gradually'],ans:3,exp:'MA processes have a PACF that decays gradually (in a damped exponential/oscillatory manner), mirroring AR\'s ACF behaviour. Why other options are wrong: Cutting off after lag 1 is the AR(1) PACF pattern, not MA(1); PACF is not identically zero nor equal to ACF in general. Shortcut: “MA $\\to$ PACF tails off” – the mirror-image rule. Tip: Frequently tested alongside the ACF cutoff fact as a pair.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an MA(1) process with $\\theta=0.8$, is it invertible?',opts:['A) Cannot say without $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','B) Only if $\\mu=0$','C) No','D) Yes'],ans:3,exp:'$|\\theta|=0.8&lt;1$, so the process is invertible. Why other options are wrong: Invertibility does not depend on $\\mu$ or $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$, only on $\\theta$. Shortcut: Direct magnitude check. Tip: A quick, direct confidence-building numerical.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The variance of an MA(q) process is always:',opts:['A) Equal to $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','B) Less than $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','C) Independent of $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','D) Greater than or equal to $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2\\sum_{j=0}^q\\theta_j^2 \\ge \\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ since $\\theta_0=1$ alone already contributes $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$, and all other terms are non-negative. Why other options are wrong: (B), (A) are not generally true (equality only when all $\\theta_j=0,\\ j\\ge1$); (C) is false since $\\gamma(0)$ is directly proportional to $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$. Shortcut: Recognize $\\sum\\theta_j^2\\ge1$ always (since $\\theta_0^2=1$). Tip: A useful inequality-based conceptual question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A non-invertible MA(1) process with parameter $\\theta=2$ has an observationally equivalent invertible representation with parameter:',opts:['A) $0.5$','B) $4$','C) $2$','D) $-2$'],ans:0,exp:'The observationally equivalent invertible twin has parameter $1/\\theta=1/2=0.5$ (with appropriately rescaled $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$). Why other options are wrong: (C) is the original non-invertible value; (D), (B) do not correspond to the reciprocal relationship. Shortcut: Always take the reciprocal $1/\\theta$ to find the equivalent invertible parameterization. Tip: A frequently tested application of the non-uniqueness property.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2), which of the following is NOT one of the three invertibility conditions?',opts:['A) $\\theta_2-\\theta_1&lt;1$','B) $|\\theta_2|&lt;1$','C) $\\theta_1+\\theta_2&lt;1$','D) $\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2&lt;1$'],ans:3,exp:'The three correct invertibility conditions are (C), (A), (B); (D) is not part of the standard triangle-region conditions. Why other options are wrong: (C), (A), (B) are each genuine, standard invertibility conditions for MA(2). Shortcut: Memorize exactly three conditions – any fourth/different-looking inequality is likely a distractor. Tip: Tests precise recall of the MA(2) invertibility region, a common JRF-level requirement.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'MA processes are most naturally associated with which methodology for practical model building?',opts:['A) Box–Jenkins methodology','B) Principal component analysis','C) Bayesian hierarchical modelling','D) Discriminant analysis'],ans:0,exp:'MA models, along with AR and ARIMA, are core components of the classical Box–Jenkins time series modelling framework. Why other options are wrong: The other methodologies are unrelated to standard MA model building. Shortcut: “MA/AR/ARIMA = Box–Jenkins.” Tip: Basic contextual/historical recall question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) with $\\theta=0.5$, $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=1$, compute $\\rho(1)$.',opts:['A) $0.2$','B) $0.25$','C) $0.4$','D) $0.5$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\rho(1)=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)=0.5/(1.25)=0.4$. Why other options are wrong: (D) forgets to divide by $(1+\\theta^2)$; (B), (A) are arithmetic slips. Shortcut: Direct substitution into the memorized MA(1) ACF formula. Tip: Very frequently tested exact numerical.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2) with $\\theta_1=0.4,\\theta_2=-0.2$, $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=1$, compute $\\gamma(0)$.',opts:['A) $1.2$','B) $0.8$','C) $1.0$','D) $1.16$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=1+0.4^2+(-0.2)^2=1+0.16+0.04=1.2$... recompute carefully: $1+0.16+0.04=1.20$. Correct value is $1.20$, matching option (A); please select (A) $1.2$. Why other options are wrong: (C) omits both squared terms; (B) uses only one term. Shortcut: Sum $1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2$ directly. Tip: A pure plug-in numerical – double-check arithmetic carefully under time pressure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Continuing Q24, compute $\\gamma(1)$.',opts:['A) $0.4\\times(-0.2)=-0.08$','B) $0.4-0.2=0.2$','C) $-0.2$','D) $0.4+(0.4)(-0.2) = 0.32$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(1)=\\theta_0\\theta_1+\\theta_1\\theta_2 = (1)(0.4)+(0.4)(-0.2)=0.4-0.08=0.32$. Why other options are wrong: (B) incorrectly subtracts coefficients directly; (C) picks out only $\\theta_2$; (A) computes only the second product term, omitting the first. Shortcut: Use the shifted dot-product: $\\gamma(1)=\\theta_0\\theta_1+\\theta_1\\theta_2$. Tip: A very standard MA(2) numerical building block.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Continuing Q24–Q25, compute $\\gamma(2)$.',opts:['A) $0.4$','B) $0.2$','C) $0$','D) $-0.2$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(2)=\\theta_0\\theta_2=(1)(-0.2)=-0.2$. Why other options are wrong: (A) uses $\\theta_1$ instead of $\\theta_2$; (B) drops the sign; (C) would apply only for $k&gt;q$. Shortcut: At $k=q$, only $\\theta_0\\theta_q$ survives. Tip: Completes the standard three-part MA(2) moment-computation sequence.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Continuing Q24–Q26, what is $\\gamma(3)$?',opts:['A) $-0.08$','B) $0.08$','C) Cannot be determined','D) $0$'],ans:3,exp:'For MA(2), $\\gamma(k)=0$ for all $k&gt;2$; $\\gamma(3)=0$ exactly. Why other options are wrong: Any nonzero value contradicts the fundamental cutoff property of finite-order MA processes. Shortcut: “Beyond order $q$, ACVF is always exactly zero.” Tip: Reinforces the cutoff property using a direct numerical follow-up.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'MA parameters are typically estimated using:',opts:['A) Maximum likelihood or the innovations algorithm','B) Only graphical methods','C) Ordinary least squares (OLS) directly','D) Simple averaging of observed values'],ans:0,exp:'Because $\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ are unobserved, OLS cannot be applied directly; ML or the innovations algorithm (or conditional/exact likelihood via state-space methods) is used instead. Why other options are wrong: OLS applies directly to AR models (linear in observed regressors), not MA; simple averaging and pure graphical methods are insufficient for estimation. Shortcut: “Unobserved regressors $\\Rightarrow$ no direct OLS $\\Rightarrow$ ML/innovations needed.” Tip: Important conceptual contrast with AR estimation, frequently tested.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which theorem establishes that any purely non-deterministic stationary process has an MA($\\infty$) representation?',opts:['A) Slutsky\'s Theorem','B) Central Limit Theorem','C) Cramér–Rao Theorem','D) Wold\'s Decomposition Theorem'],ans:3,exp:'Wold\'s theorem (1938) is the foundational result establishing the MA($\\infty$) representation of stationary processes. Why other options are wrong: The other named theorems belong to unrelated statistical areas. Shortcut: “Wold = MA($\\infty$) representation” direct recall. Tip: A guaranteed historical/theoretical recall question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) with $\\theta=0.3$, $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=5$, find $\\gamma(0)$.',opts:['A) $5.0$','B) $0.45$','C) $1.5$','D) $5.45$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=5(1+0.09)=5(1.09)=5.45$. Why other options are wrong: (A) ignores the $\\theta^2$ term; (C), (B) are partial/incorrect computations. Shortcut: $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2(1+\\theta^2)$ direct substitution. Tip: Standard quick numerical, very frequently seen.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density of an MA(q) process is always:',opts:['A) Unbounded','B) Identically zero','C) Bounded','D) Negative for some $\\omega$'],ans:2,exp:'Since $f(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}|\\theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2$ is a fixed-degree trigonometric polynomial, it is always bounded on $[-\\pi,\\pi]$. Why other options are wrong: Unboundedness is a feature of AR spectra near unit-root boundaries, not MA; spectral density is never negative; it is not identically zero for a non-degenerate MA process. Shortcut: “MA = bounded spectrum” – link to the finite-memory property. Tip: Connects this chapter to spectral theory, a common cross-topic question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1), the 2-step-ahead forecast $\\hat X_{t+2|t}$ equals:',opts:['A) Cannot be determined','B) $\\mu+\\theta\\varepsilon_t$','C) $\\mu$','D) $\\mu+\\theta\\hat\\varepsilon_{t+1}$'],ans:2,exp:'For MA(1) ($q=1$), any forecast horizon $h&gt;q=1$ collapses exactly to the unconditional mean $\\mu$. Why other options are wrong: (D), (B) incorrectly retain shock terms beyond the point where they contribute; (A) is false since the value is fully determined. Shortcut: “$h&gt;q \\Rightarrow$ forecast $=\\mu$, no exceptions.” Tip: A very frequently tested forecasting fact for MA processes.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an invertible MA(1) process with $\\theta=0.4$, derive the coefficient of $X_{t-2}$ in the AR($\\infty$) representation $\\varepsilon_t=\\sum_{j=0}^\\infty(-\\theta)^j(X_{t-j}-\\mu)$.',opts:['A) $(-0.4)^2=0.16$','B) $-0.4$','C) $(-0.4)^3=-0.064$','D) $0.4$'],ans:0,exp:'The coefficient of $(X_{t-j}-\\mu)$ in the AR($\\infty$) expansion is $(-\\theta)^j$; for $j=2$, this is $(-0.4)^2=0.16$. Why other options are wrong: (B) is the $j=1$ coefficient; (D) has an incorrect sign for even $j$; (C) is the $j=3$ coefficient. Shortcut: Just raise $(-\\theta)$ to the appropriate power $j$ matching the desired lag. Tip: Tests genuine understanding of the back-substitution derivation, not just formula recall.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2) with $\\theta_1=0.3,\\theta_2=0.2$, verify whether the process is invertible.',opts:['A) Cannot be determined without $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','B) Not invertible since $\\theta_2-\\theta_1$ is negative','C) Invertible: $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=0.5&lt;1$, $\\theta_2-\\theta_1=-0.1&lt;1$, $|\\theta_2|=0.2&lt;1$ – all hold','D) Not invertible since $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=0.5$ is too small'],ans:2,exp:'All three invertibility conditions hold comfortably, so the process is invertible. Why other options are wrong: (D) incorrectly treats a small sum as problematic; (B) incorrectly treats a negative value as automatically failing (the condition only requires $&lt;1$, negative values easily satisfy this); (A) is false since invertibility depends only on $\\theta_1,\\theta_2$. Shortcut: Plug numbers directly into all three inequalities; don\'t be misled by sign alone. Tip: Tests careful, correct application of the full triangle-region condition set.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2) with $\\theta_1=1.5,\\theta_2=-0.7$, is the process invertible?',opts:['A) No – $\\theta_1+\\theta_2=0.8&lt;1$ but $\\theta_2-\\theta_1=-2.2&lt;1$, and $|\\theta_2|=0.7&lt;1$; check again: all conditions numerically appear satisfied, so it IS invertible','B) Yes – all three conditions hold','C) No, since $\\theta_1&gt;1$','D) Cannot be determined'],ans:1,exp:'$\\theta_1+\\theta_2=0.8&lt;1$ ✓; $\\theta_2-\\theta_1=-2.2&lt;1$ ✓; $|\\theta_2|=0.7&lt;1$ ✓ – all three hold, so the process is invertible, despite $\\theta_1$ itself exceeding 1 in magnitude. Why other options are wrong: (A) reaches the right numeric conclusion but is worded confusingly as if it were a “No”; (C) incorrectly assumes $|\\theta_1|&lt;1$ is required (it is not, for MA(2) – only the joint triangle conditions matter); (D) is false since a direct check is possible. Shortcut: Never judge MA(2) invertibility from $\\theta_1$ or $\\theta_2$ individually exceeding 1 – always check the joint triangle conditions. Tip: A genuinely tricky JRF-level question exposing the common misconception that each $|\\theta_i|&lt;1$ individually is required.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1), the forecast error variance at horizon $h=1$ is:',opts:['A) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2(1+\\theta^2)$','B) $\\theta^2\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','C) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','D) $0$'],ans:2,exp:'Using $\\operatorname{Var}(e_t(h))=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2\\sum_{j=0}^{\\min(h,q)-1}\\theta_j^2$ with $h=1,q=1$: sum runs over $j=0$ only, giving $\\theta_0^2\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$. Why other options are wrong: (A) is the full unconditional variance $\\gamma(0)$, correct only for $h\\ge q+1=2$; (B), (D) are incorrect partial computations. Shortcut: At $h=1$, forecast error variance is always just $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ (the one-step-ahead innovation itself is entirely unpredictable). Tip: A subtle but important forecasting-variance question, frequently confused with the full unconditional variance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1), the forecast error variance at horizon $h=2$ (with $q=1$) is:',opts:['A) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','B) $\\theta^2\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','C) $2\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','D) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2(1+\\theta^2) = \\gamma(0)$'],ans:3,exp:'At $h=2&gt;q=1$, the forecast error variance equals the full unconditional variance $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2(1+\\theta^2)$, since the entire process becomes unpredictable beyond the memory horizon. Why other options are wrong: (A) is only correct for $h=1$; (B), (C) are incorrect partial/incorrect computations. Shortcut: “Once $h&gt;q$, forecast error variance = full $\\gamma(0)$, always.” Tip: Completes the two-part forecast-variance question sequence (Q36–Q37), a realistic full JRF-difficulty pairing.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly gives the number of distinct parameter combinations (choices of roots inside/outside the unit circle per factor) yielding the same ACVF for a general MA(q) process?',opts:['A) $2^q$','B) $q^2$','C) $q$','D) $q!$'],ans:0,exp:'Each of the $q$ roots of $\\theta(z)=0$ can independently be “flipped” inside or outside the unit circle while preserving the ACVF, giving $2^q$ observationally equivalent parameterizations; the invertible one (all roots outside) is the canonical choice. Why other options are wrong: They do not match the correct combinatorial count arising from binary in/out choices per root. Shortcut: “Each root: 2 choices, $q$ roots $\\Rightarrow 2^q$ total.” Tip: A genuinely advanced JRF fact about non-uniqueness, rarely known but occasionally tested.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In a VMA (vector moving average) model, the invertibility condition generalizes the scalar MA condition to require:',opts:['A) No condition is needed for VMA invertibility','B) The trace of $\\Theta$ is less than $q$','C) Roots of $\\det[\\Theta(z)]=0$ lie outside the unit circle','D) All diagonal elements of $\\Theta$ less than 1'],ans:2,exp:'The multivariate generalization replaces the scalar polynomial condition with a determinant condition on the matrix MA polynomial $\\Theta(z)$. Why other options are wrong: Diagonal-element or trace-based conditions do not correctly generalize the invertibility requirement; a condition is indeed needed. Shortcut: “Scalar root condition $\\to$ determinant root condition” when moving from univariate to multivariate. Tip: Research-level extension question, occasionally seen in advanced JRF sets.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly explains why MA parameters cannot generally be estimated by simple OLS regression of $X_t$ on lagged $\\varepsilon_{t-j}$?',opts:['A) Because $\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ are unobserved and must themselves be estimated recursively','B) Because MA models have no error term','C) Because MA models are non-stationary','D) Because $\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ are perfectly collinear'],ans:0,exp:'Since the regressors $\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ are latent (not directly observed), standard OLS – which requires observed regressors – cannot be applied directly; iterative/likelihood-based methods that recursively estimate the $\\varepsilon_t$ are required instead. Why other options are wrong: Collinearity is not the fundamental issue; MA models are stationary, not non-stationary; MA models do have an error term (indeed, only error terms). Shortcut: “Latent regressors $\\Rightarrow$ no direct OLS.” Tip: A conceptually deep estimation-theory question, testing genuine understanding rather than formula recall.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'True or False: “An MA(1) process with $\\theta=5$ is not a valid stationary process.”',opts:['A) True – large $\\theta$ violates stationarity','B) True, but only because $\\theta&gt;1$','C) False, because $\\theta=5$ implies invertibility','D) False – MA(1) is stationary for any $\\theta$, including $\\theta=5$; only invertibility fails here'],ans:3,exp:'MA(1) is always stationary regardless of $\\theta$; $\\theta=5$ only violates invertibility ($|\\theta|&lt;1$ fails), not stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (A), (B) incorrectly conflate invertibility with stationarity; (C) incorrectly claims invertibility holds when it clearly fails ($|5|&gt;1$). Shortcut: Always separate the two conditions mentally: stationarity (always true for MA) vs invertibility (parameter-dependent). Tip: The single most exploited conceptual trap in this entire chapter.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'True or False: “The ACF of MA(q) gradually decays to zero as lag increases, similar to AR processes.”',opts:['A) True, only for $q&gt;2$','B) True, only if $\\theta_j&gt;0$ for all $j$','C) False – it cuts off exactly at lag $q$, not a gradual decay','D) True'],ans:2,exp:'The MA(q) ACF is exactly zero beyond lag $q$ – a sharp cutoff, fundamentally different from AR\'s gradual decay. Why other options are wrong: None of the qualified “True” options correctly describe MA(q) ACF behaviour. Shortcut: “Cutoff, not decay” – a firm distinction to hold onto. Tip: Frequently confused with AR\'s gradual decay pattern – classic trap.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'True or False: “Two different sets of MA(1) parameters can produce identical autocovariance functions.”',opts:['A) True, but only if $\\theta=0$','B) True – $\\theta$ and $1/\\theta$ (with rescaled variance) give the same ACVF','C) False, unless the process is non-stationary','D) False – MA parameters are always uniquely identified by the ACVF'],ans:1,exp:'This is the well-known non-uniqueness/observational-equivalence property of MA(1) parameterizations. Why other options are wrong: (D), (C) incorrectly claim uniqueness; (A) incorrectly restricts to a trivial case. Shortcut: “ACVF alone cannot distinguish $\\theta$ from $1/\\theta$.” Tip: Tests deep understanding of why invertibility is imposed as an additional selection criterion.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'True or False: “An MA(q) process always has a well-defined PACF that cuts off after some finite lag, just like its ACF.”',opts:['A) False, because the PACF is undefined for MA processes','B) True, only for MA(1)','C) False – the PACF of MA(q) generally tails off (decays gradually), it does not cut off','D) True'],ans:2,exp:'Unlike the ACF, the PACF of a genuine MA(q) process (with $q\\ge1$) tails off gradually rather than cutting off at a finite lag. Why other options are wrong: (D), (B) incorrectly claim cutoff behaviour for the PACF; (A) is false since the PACF is perfectly well-defined for MA processes. Shortcut: “ACF cuts off, PACF tails off” for MA – always keep these separate. Tip: A carefully worded trap testing precise ACF/PACF distinction at JRF level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'True or False: “Since MA(q) always has finite memory, its spectral density must also be exactly zero beyond some frequency.”',opts:['A) True','B) False, because MA processes have no spectral density','C) True, only for even $q$','D) False – finite memory in the time domain (ACVF cutoff) does not imply the spectral density vanishes at any frequency; it only implies boundedness'],ans:3,exp:'Finite-order ACVF cutoff translates to a bounded (not zero-valued) spectral density across all frequencies; the spectral density of MA(q) is generally strictly positive everywhere on $[-\\pi,\\pi]$. Why other options are wrong: (A), (C) incorrectly infer a frequency-domain cutoff from a time-domain cutoff – these are different domains with different behaviours; (B) is false since MA processes do have a well-defined spectral density. Shortcut: “Time-domain cutoff $\\ne$ frequency-domain cutoff” – do not conflate the two domains. Tip: A sophisticated cross-topic trap connecting MA theory with spectral analysis.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) MA(q) processes are always stationary. (II) MA(q) processes are always invertible. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Neither','B) Both I and II','C) Only II','D) Only I'],ans:3,exp:'I is always true; II is false since invertibility depends on the specific parameter values (roots outside unit circle condition). Why other options are wrong: (C), (B) incorrectly accept the false statement II; (A) incorrectly rejects the true statement I. Shortcut: “Stationary: always. Invertible: conditional.” Tip: The most fundamental statement-pair distinction in this chapter.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) The ACF of MA(q) is exactly zero for lags beyond $q$. (II) The PACF of MA(q) is also exactly zero for lags beyond $q$. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Neither','B) Both I and II','C) Only II','D) Only I'],ans:3,exp:'I is the defining ACF cutoff property; II is false – PACF tails off gradually rather than becoming exactly zero. Why other options are wrong: They incorrectly accept or reject the wrong combination of statements. Shortcut: Never assume PACF mirrors ACF cutoff behaviour for MA. Tip: A guaranteed appearance in some phrasing across NET/JRF cycles.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) Every invertible MA(q) process has a convergent AR($\\infty$) representation. (II) Every stationary AR(p) process has a convergent MA($\\infty$) representation (Wold decomposition). Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only II','B) Neither','C) Only I','D) Both I and II'],ans:3,exp:'Both statements are true and jointly describe the fundamental MA-AR duality in linear time series theory. Why other options are wrong: Excluding either statement is incorrect, since both directions of the duality independently hold. Shortcut: Remember the duality as a complete package: MA $\\leftrightarrow$ AR via invertibility and Wold decomposition respectively. Tip: A both-true statement pairing testing the complete duality concept.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) MA(1) parameter $\\theta$ and $1/\\theta$ give identical ACVFs (with appropriately rescaled $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$). (II) Only the invertible representation ($|\\theta|&lt;1$) is conventionally selected in practice. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only II','B) Both I and II','C) Neither','D) Only I'],ans:1,exp:'Both statements are accurate and complementary – the first describes the non-uniqueness fact, the second describes the conventional resolution. Why other options are wrong: Excluding either statement misses a true, standard fact about MA(1) parameterization. Shortcut: These two ideas always travel together: non-uniqueness fact + invertibility as the tie-breaking convention. Tip: Reinforces the non-uniqueness/invertibility-selection concept from a statement-based angle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Assertion (A): MA($q$) models cannot be estimated by ordinary least squares in the same direct way as AR($p$) models. Reason (R): The regressors $\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ in the MA representation are unobserved.',opts:['A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A','B) A is true, R is false','C) A is false, R is true','D) Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A'],ans:0,exp:'R directly and correctly explains why A is true – unobserved regressors preclude direct OLS application. Why other options are wrong: Both statements are individually true and causally linked, ruling out the other three combinations. Shortcut: “Unobserved regressors $\\Rightarrow$ no OLS” is a direct, single-step causal chain. Tip: A well-constructed Assertion-Reason question testing the estimation-theory contrast with AR models.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Process feature) with List II (MA(q) property): List I: (a) ACF (b) PACF (c) Stationarity (d) Invertibility List II: (1) Always holds regardless of parameters (2) Cuts off after lag $q$ (3) Depends on roots of $\\theta(z)$ (4) Tails off gradually',opts:['A) a-2, b-4, c-1, d-3','B) a-4, b-2, c-3, d-1','C) a-1, b-2, c-3, d-4','D) a-2, b-1, c-4, d-3'],ans:0,exp:'ACF cuts off (2); PACF tails off (4); stationarity always holds (1); invertibility depends on roots (3). Why other options are wrong: All other options mis-assign at least one pairing. Shortcut: Memorize the four-way mapping as a single unit. Tip: Tests the complete property set of MA(q) in one efficient question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Model) with List II (ACF/PACF signature): List I: (a) MA(q) (b) AR(p) (c) ARMA(p,q) (d) White noise List II: (1) Both ACF and PACF decay gradually (2) ACF cuts off after $q$, PACF tails off (3) All ACF/PACF values statistically zero (4) PACF cuts off after $p$, ACF tails off',opts:['A) a-4, b-2, c-3, d-1','B) a-3, b-2, c-1, d-4','C) a-2, b-1, c-4, d-3','D) a-2, b-4, c-1, d-3'],ans:3,exp:'MA(q): ACF cuts off, PACF tails off (2); AR(p): PACF cuts off, ACF tails off (4); ARMA(p,q): both tail off (1); white noise: all values statistically zero (3). Why other options are wrong: Each distractor swaps at least one correct pairing. Shortcut: Recall the full four-way identification table from the revision notes. Tip: A comprehensive identification-table question, very efficient for testing the whole ACF/PACF framework at once.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (MA(2) quantity) with List II (Formula): List I: (a) $\\gamma(0)$ (b) $\\gamma(1)$ (c) $\\gamma(2)$ (d) $\\gamma(3)$ List II: (1) $0$ (2) $\\theta_2\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ (3) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2(1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2)$ (4) $(\\theta_1+\\theta_1\\theta_2)\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$',opts:['A) a-3, b-4, c-2, d-1','B) a-4, b-3, c-2, d-1','C) a-3, b-2, c-4, d-1','D) a-3, b-4, c-1, d-2'],ans:0,exp:'$\\gamma(0)$ is the variance (3); $\\gamma(1)$ is the lag-1 formula (4); $\\gamma(2)$ is $\\theta_2\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ (2); $\\gamma(3)=0$ since $3&gt;q=2$ (1). Why other options are wrong: Each distractor misassigns at least one lag to the wrong formula. Shortcut: Work through lags in increasing order, matching each to its shifted-dot-product formula. Tip: Full formula-recall match question for MA(2), efficient revision tool.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Person/Concept) with List II (Contribution): List I: (a) Slutsky/Yule (b) Wold (c) Box/Jenkins (d) Invertibility List II: (1) Formalized MA(q) within ARIMA framework (2) Showed moving averages can mimic cycles (3) Ensures unique AR($\\infty$) representation (4) MA($\\infty$) decomposition theorem',opts:['A) a-2, b-1, c-4, d-3','B) a-4, b-2, c-1, d-3','C) a-2, b-4, c-1, d-3','D) a-1, b-2, c-4, d-3'],ans:2,exp:'Slutsky/Yule showed the cyclical-mimicry effect (2); Wold proved the MA($\\infty$) decomposition (4); Box/Jenkins formalized MA(q) practically (1); invertibility ensures the AR($\\infty$) representation (3). Why other options are wrong: Each distractor misassigns at least one historical/conceptual contribution. Shortcut: Build a mental timeline: Slutsky/Yule (1927) $\\to$ Wold (1938) $\\to$ Box/Jenkins (1970). Tip: History-based match question, occasionally appears in NET.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) with $\\theta=-0.5$, $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=4$, compute $\\gamma(1)$.',opts:['A) $-0.5$','B) $5$','C) $-2$','D) $2$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\gamma(1)=\\theta\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=(-0.5)(4)=-2$. Why other options are wrong: (D) drops the sign; (A), (B) are incorrect computations. Shortcut: Direct substitution, carry the sign through. Tip: Sign-handling numerical, common source of avoidable error.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Continuing Q55, compute $\\rho(1)$.',opts:['A) $-0.5$','B) $0.4$','C) $-0.25$','D) $-0.4$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=4(1+0.25)=5$; $\\rho(1)=\\gamma(1)/\\gamma(0)=-2/5=-0.4$. Why other options are wrong: (B) drops the sign; (A), (C) are incorrect partial computations. Shortcut: Compute $\\gamma(0)$ and $\\gamma(1)$ first, then divide. Tip: Two-part numerical, very standard JRF combination.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2): $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+0.7\\varepsilon_{t-1}+0.1\\varepsilon_{t-2}$, $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=2$, compute $\\gamma(0)$.',opts:['A) $2$','B) $2(1+0.49+0.01)=3.0$','C) $2(0.7+0.1)=1.6$','D) $2(1+0.7+0.1)=3.6$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2(1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2)=2(1+0.49+0.01)=2(1.5)=3.0$. Why other options are wrong: (C), (D) sum coefficients directly instead of squaring; (A) ignores the MA structure entirely. Shortcut: Always square each coefficient (including implicit $\\theta_0=1$) before summing. Tip: A very standard MA(2) variance numerical.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Continuing Q57, compute $\\rho(1)$.',opts:['A) $0.7/1.5\\approx0.467$','B) $(0.7+0.7\\times0.1)/1.5 = 0.77/1.5 \\approx 0.513$','C) $0.1/1.5\\approx0.067$','D) $0.77/3.0\\approx0.257$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(1)=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2(\\theta_1+\\theta_1\\theta_2)=2(0.7+0.07)=2(0.77)=1.54$; and $\\gamma(0)=3.0$ from Q57; so $\\rho(1)=1.54/3.0\\approx0.513$. Note: since $\\rho(k)=\\gamma(k)/\\gamma(0)$ is scale-free in $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$, the numerically correct value is $\\approx0.513$, matching the unsimplified numerator/denominator ratio in option (B); please select (B) as the intended correct choice given the matching final value $\\approx0.513$. Why other options are wrong: (A), (C) use only one term of the numerator; (D)\'s arithmetic (dividing the $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=1$-scaled numerator by the $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=2$-scaled denominator) is inconsistent. Shortcut: Since $\\rho(k)$ is scale-invariant in $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$, you may compute it using the coefficient-only numerator and denominator (ignoring $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ entirely) to save time: $\\rho(1)=(\\theta_1+\\theta_1\\theta_2)/(1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2)$. Tip: A great time-saving realization: $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ always cancels out of $\\rho(k)$ – never waste time carrying it through ACF (as opposed to ACVF) computations.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1), if $\\gamma(0)=10$ and $\\gamma(1)=3$, find $\\rho(1)$.',opts:['A) $10/3$','B) $0.7$','C) $3.33$','D) $0.3$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\rho(1)=\\gamma(1)/\\gamma(0)=3/10=0.3$. Why other options are wrong: (C) inverts the ratio; (B), (A) are unrelated computations. Shortcut: Direct division, no further formula needed once $\\gamma(0),\\gamma(1)$ are known. Tip: A simple “given ACVF, find ACF” numerical.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1), if $\\rho(1)=0.3$, solve for $\\theta$ (choosing the invertible root).',opts:['A) $\\theta=0.7$','B) $\\theta=1/0.3\\approx3.33$','C) Solve $\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)=0.3 \\Rightarrow 0.3\\theta^2-\\theta+0.3=0 \\Rightarrow \\theta\\approx0.345$ (invertible root)','D) $\\theta=0.3$ directly'],ans:2,exp:'Solving the quadratic $0.3\\theta^2-\\theta+0.3=0$ gives two roots, $\\theta\\approx0.345$ and $\\theta\\approx2.90$ (reciprocals of each other); the invertible one satisfying $|\\theta|&lt;1$ is $\\theta\\approx0.345$. Why other options are wrong: (D) incorrectly assumes $\\theta=\\rho(1)$ directly (true only approximately for very small $\\theta$, not exactly); (A), (B) do not solve the correct quadratic. Shortcut: Recognize this as an inverse problem requiring the quadratic formula, then select the root with $|\\theta|&lt;1$ as the invertible (canonical) solution. Tip: A genuinely challenging JRF-level inverse-problem numerical, testing the two-roots/invertibility-selection concept in a computational context.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) with $\\theta=1$ (boundary case), compute $\\rho(1)$.',opts:['A) $0.25$','B) $1$','C) $0$','D) $0.5$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\rho(1)=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)=1/2=0.5$ – this confirms the maximum bound is attained exactly at $\\theta=1$ (a non-invertible boundary case, since $|\\theta|&lt;1$ is required strictly). Why other options are wrong: (B), (C), (A) do not match the correct substitution. Shortcut: Direct substitution confirms the theoretical maximum bound derived earlier. Tip: Connects the numerical computation directly to the theoretical $|\\rho(1)|\\le0.5$ bound.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2) with $\\theta_1=0.6,\\theta_2=0.4$, compute the forecast error variance at horizon $h=1$.',opts:['A) $0$','B) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2(1+0.36+0.16)$','C) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','D) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2(1+0.36)$'],ans:2,exp:'At $h=1$, the sum in $\\operatorname{Var}(e_t(h))=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2\\sum_{j=0}^{\\min(h,q)-1}\\theta_j^2$ runs only over $j=0$, giving $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ regardless of $q$. Why other options are wrong: (D) incorrectly includes the $j=1$ term (relevant only at $h=2$); (B) is the full unconditional variance (relevant only at $h\\ge3=q+1$); (A) is false. Shortcut: At $h=1$, forecast error variance is always exactly $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$, for ANY MA(q) model. Tip: Generalizes the MA(1) forecast-variance fact (Q36) to MA(2) – tests whether the pattern is truly understood, not just memorized for $q=1$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In modern computational practice, exact likelihood estimation of MA($q$) models is typically achieved via:',opts:['A) Graphical curve-fitting','B) Simple method of moments only','C) State-space representation and the Kalman filter','D) Direct matrix inversion of an infinite-dimensional covariance matrix'],ans:2,exp:'Representing the MA($q$) model in state-space form allows the Kalman filter to compute the exact likelihood efficiently, superseding older conditional-likelihood approximations. Why other options are wrong: Method of moments and graphical methods are not exact-likelihood approaches; infinite-dimensional direct inversion is computationally infeasible. Shortcut: “State-space + Kalman filter = modern exact MA/ARMA likelihood computation.” Tip: Research-level computational fact, occasionally tested in the hardest JRF sets.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In certain structural macroeconomic models involving anticipated (news) shocks, which of the following may deliberately arise?',opts:['A) A non-invertible MA representation','B) Only AR(1) processes','C) Only white noise processes','D) Models with no moving average component at all'],ans:0,exp:'News/anticipated-shock models can generate non-invertible MA representations, an active and important area of applied macro-econometric research, since agents\' information sets differ from the econometrician\'s. Why other options are wrong: The other options do not correctly describe this research phenomenon. Shortcut: “Anticipated shocks $\\Rightarrow$ possible non-invertibility, deliberately.” Tip: A genuinely research-level, applied macro-econometrics connection, appropriate for the most advanced JRF questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'How does the finite-memory property of MA(q) processes relate to long-memory (ARFIMA) processes in the research literature?',opts:['A) MA(q) and ARFIMA are mathematically identical','B) MA(q) processes are themselves examples of long memory','C) There is no connection between the two','D) MA(q) components are combined with fractional differencing to jointly model short-run and long-run dynamics'],ans:3,exp:'In ARFIMA($p,d,q$) models, finite-order MA (and AR) components capture short-run dynamics, while the fractional differencing parameter $d$ captures long-run persistence – a combined modelling strategy. Why other options are wrong: MA(q) alone is emphatically short-memory, not long-memory; the two model classes are related but not identical; there is indeed a well-established connection. Shortcut: “ARFIMA = fractional-$d$ (long memory) + ARMA (short memory), working together.” Tip: Connects this chapter\'s finite-memory theme to the long-memory literature – a favourite advanced cross-topic JRF question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'From a research perspective, why is the invertibility condition particularly important for forecasting applications?',opts:['A) It ensures forecasts can be computed as a convergent function of the observed past, rather than requiring unobservable future information','B) It ensures the process has finite variance','C) It has no real importance beyond mathematical elegance','D) It ensures the ACF is always positive'],ans:0,exp:'Invertibility guarantees that the innovations $\\varepsilon_t$ (and hence forecasts) can be expressed as a convergent function of currently and previously observed data, which is essential for practical, implementable forecasting. Why other options are wrong: It has genuine practical (not just aesthetic) importance; finite variance and ACF positivity are unrelated to invertibility. Shortcut: “Invertibility = forecasts computable from the observable past.” Tip: Synthesizes the practical rationale behind invertibility – an excellent concluding conceptual question for this chapter.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(1) process $X_t = \\phi X_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$ with $\\varepsilon_t \\sim WN(0,\\sigma^2)$, the condition for weak (covariance) stationarity is:',opts:['A) $|\\phi| &gt; 1$','B) $\\phi \\geq 1$','C) $|\\phi| &lt; 1$','D) $\\phi = 0$'],ans:2,exp:'An AR(1) process can be written as an infinite MA process $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\phi^j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ only if $\\phi^j \\to 0$ as $j\\to\\infty$, which requires $|\\phi|&lt;1$. This ensures a finite, time-invariant variance and ACVF. Why other options are wrong: (B) $\\phi \\geq 1$ gives an explosive or unit-root (non-stationary) process. (D) $\\phi = 0$ is a special case (white noise), not the general stationarity boundary. (A) $|\\phi|&gt;1$ makes the process explosive, variance diverges. Remember: for AR(1), stationarity $\\Leftrightarrow$ root of $1-\\phi z=0$, i.e. $z=1/\\phi$, lies outside the unit circle $\\Leftrightarrow |\\phi|&lt;1$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The mean of a stationary zero-intercept AR(1) process $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ is:',opts:['A) $\\phi/(1-\\phi)$','B) $\\mu/(1-\\phi)$','C) $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$','D) $0$'],ans:3,exp:'Taking expectation of both sides under stationarity, $E(X_t)=\\phi E(X_{t-1})+0 \\Rightarrow \\mu=\\phi\\mu \\Rightarrow \\mu(1-\\phi)=0$. Since $\\phi \\neq 1$ (stationary case), $\\mu=0$. Why other options are wrong: (C) This is the variance formula, not the mean. (A) Not a standard AR(1) mean expression. (B) Applies when an intercept $c$ is present: $\\mu=c/(1-\\phi)$, but no intercept is given here. If the model has an intercept $X_t=c+\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, mean shifts to $\\mu=c/(1-\\phi)$ – a very common NET trap.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The variance of a stationary AR(1) process is given by:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2 \\phi^2$','B) $\\sigma^2(1-\\phi^2)$','C) $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi)$','D) $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$'],ans:3,exp:'From $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, under stationarity $\\gamma(0)=\\phi^2\\gamma(0)+\\sigma^2$ (since $X_{t-1}$ and $\\varepsilon_t$ are uncorrelated), giving $\\gamma(0)(1-\\phi^2)=\\sigma^2$, so $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$. Why other options are wrong: (B) Inverts the algebra; would give negative/absurd values for $\\phi$ close to 1. (C) Missing the square on $\\phi$. (A) Confuses variance with the lag-1 autocovariance component. As $|\\phi|\\to 1$, variance $\\to \\infty$: a quick sanity check for correctness of the formula.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The autocorrelation function (ACF) of a stationary AR(1) process at lag $k$ is:',opts:['A) $\\rho(k)=1-\\phi^k$','B) $\\rho(k)=\\phi^{k}/(1-\\phi^2)$','C) $\\rho(k)=\\phi^k$','D) $\\rho(k)=k\\phi$'],ans:2,exp:'The Yule-Walker recursion for AR(1) gives $\\gamma(k)=\\phi\\gamma(k-1)$, so $\\gamma(k)=\\phi^k\\gamma(0)$, hence $\\rho(k)=\\gamma(k)/\\gamma(0)=\\phi^k$. Why other options are wrong: (D) Linear decay is not the correct functional form. (B) Confuses ACF with ACVF (which does carry the $1/(1-\\phi^2)$ term via $\\gamma(0)$). (A) Not derivable from the AR(1) recursion. For $0&lt;\\phi&lt;1$, ACF decays geometrically to 0; for $-1&lt;\\phi&lt;0$, ACF alternates in sign while decaying – a favourite NET graph-identification question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The partial autocorrelation function (PACF) of an AR(1) process:',opts:['A) Oscillates indefinitely','B) Decays exponentially for all lags','C) Is zero at lag 1 and nonzero beyond','D) Cuts off (becomes zero) after lag 1'],ans:3,exp:'For AR(1), $\\phi_{11}=\\rho(1)=\\phi$ and $\\phi_{kk}=0$ for all $k \\geq 2$, since lag-1 value fully explains the dependence; this “cut-off” behaviour is the hallmark identification signature of AR($p$) models at lag $p$. Why other options are wrong: (B) Exponential decay is the ACF\'s behaviour, not PACF\'s, for AR(1). (C) Backwards – PACF is nonzero exactly at lag 1 and zero after. (A) Oscillation without decay describes neither ACF nor PACF of a stationary AR(1). Golden rule: AR($p$) $\\Rightarrow$ PACF cuts off after lag $p$; MA($q$) $\\Rightarrow$ ACF cuts off after lag $q$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) model $X_t=\\phi_1 X_{t-1}+\\phi_2 X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$, which of the following is NOT part of the stationarity (triangle) conditions?',opts:['A) $\\phi_2-\\phi_1&lt;1$','B) $|\\phi_2|&lt;1$','C) $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&lt;1$','D) $\\phi_1\\phi_2&lt;1$'],ans:3,exp:'The standard stationarity/triangle region for AR(2) requires all three conditions $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&lt;1$, $\\phi_2-\\phi_1&lt;1$, and $-1&lt;\\phi_2&lt;1$ simultaneously (equivalent to both roots of the characteristic equation lying outside the unit circle). $\\phi_1\\phi_2&lt;1$ is not one of the standard conditions. Why other options are wrong: (C), (A), (B) are each genuine, textbook-standard parts of the AR(2) triangular stationarity region. Visualise the “stationarity triangle” in the $(\\phi_1,\\phi_2)$-plane with vertices at $(-2,-1),(2,-1),(0,1)$ – a very frequently tested diagram.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The characteristic equation of the AR($p$) process $X_t=\\sum_{i=1}^p \\phi_i X_{t-i}+\\varepsilon_t$ is obtained by setting:',opts:['A) $1-\\sum_{i=1}^p \\phi_i z^i=0$','B) $\\sum_{i=1}^p \\phi_i z^i=1$, treated identically to (A)','C) $z^p-\\sum_{i=1}^p\\phi_i=0$','D) $\\sum_{i=1}^p \\phi_i=0$'],ans:0,exp:'Writing the AR($p$) model using the backshift operator $B$ as $(1-\\phi_1B-\\phi_2B^2-\\cdots-\\phi_pB^p)X_t=\\varepsilon_t$, the characteristic (auxiliary) equation replaces $B$ by $z$ and sets the AR polynomial to zero. Why other options are wrong: (B) Superficially similar but option (A) is the conventionally stated and internally consistent form used in stationarity analysis (roots outside unit circle). (C) Wrong placement of the powers of $z$ relative to coefficients. (D) Ignores the polynomial structure entirely. Stationarity $\\Leftrightarrow$ all roots $z_i$ of $1-\\phi_1z-\\cdots-\\phi_pz^p=0$ satisfy $|z_i|&gt;1$ (lie strictly outside the unit circle).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which method is used to identify the order $p$ of an AR($p$) model from sample data?',opts:['A) ACF is exactly zero for all lags','B) ACF cuts off after lag $p$','C) PACF decays exponentially with no cut-off','D) PACF cuts off after lag $p$'],ans:3,exp:'In the Box-Jenkins identification methodology, a sample PACF that is significant up to lag $p$ and (statistically) negligible thereafter is the classical signature of an AR($p$) process; the ACF instead tails off (decays) gradually. Why other options are wrong: (B) This describes MA($q$) identification via the ACF, not AR($p$). (A) An all-zero ACF describes pure white noise, not AR($p$). (C) Exponential decay with no cut-off is the ACF pattern for AR processes, not PACF. Memory aid: “AR $\\to$ PACF cuts, ACF tails”; “MA $\\to$ ACF cuts, PACF tails”.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.6$ and $\\sigma^2=4$, the value of $\\gamma(1)$ (lag-1 autocovariance) is:',opts:['A) $6.25$','B) $2.4$','C) $3.75$','D) $1.44$'],ans:2,exp:'First compute $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=4/(1-0.36)=4/0.64=6.25$. Then $\\gamma(1)=\\phi\\,\\gamma(0)=0.6\\times6.25=3.75$. Why other options are wrong: (B) $2.4=0.6\\times4$ mistakenly uses $\\sigma^2$ instead of $\\gamma(0)$. (A) $6.25$ is $\\gamma(0)$, not $\\gamma(1)$. (D) $1.44=0.6^2\\times4$, an incorrect combination of terms. Quick path: always get $\\gamma(0)$ first via $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$, then $\\gamma(k)=\\phi^k\\gamma(0)$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In an AR(2) process, the Yule-Walker equations for $\\rho(1)$ and $\\rho(2)$ in terms of $\\phi_1,\\phi_2$ are:',opts:['A) $\\rho(1)=\\phi_1\\rho(2)$, $\\rho(2)=\\phi_2\\rho(1)$','B) $\\rho(1)=\\phi_2$, $\\rho(2)=\\phi_1$','C) $\\rho(1)=\\phi_1+\\phi_2\\rho(1)$, $\\rho(2)=\\phi_1\\rho(1)+\\phi_2$','D) $\\rho(1)=\\phi_1-\\phi_2$, $\\rho(2)=\\phi_1+\\phi_2$'],ans:2,exp:'Multiplying $X_t=\\phi_1X_{t-1}+\\phi_2X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$ by $X_{t-k}$ and taking expectations yields the Yule-Walker recursion $\\rho(k)=\\phi_1\\rho(k-1)+\\phi_2\\rho(k-2)$. Setting $k=1$ (using $\\rho(-1)=\\rho(1)$) gives $\\rho(1)=\\phi_1+\\phi_2\\rho(1)$; setting $k=2$ gives $\\rho(2)=\\phi_1\\rho(1)+\\phi_2$. Why other options are wrong: (A), (D), (B) do not follow from the correct Yule-Walker recursion and misassign the coefficients. Solving (C) gives the classic closed form $\\rho(1)=\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)$ – frequently asked directly in NET.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If the roots of the AR(2) characteristic equation are complex conjugates, the ACF of the process:',opts:['A) Decays monotonically to zero','B) Remains constant at all lags','C) Diverges to infinity','D) Exhibits a damped (pseudo-periodic) sinusoidal pattern'],ans:3,exp:'Complex conjugate roots outside the unit circle correspond to a pair of complex reciprocal roots inside; the general solution to the homogeneous difference equation involves terms like $r^k\\cos(k\\theta+\\text{phase})$, producing oscillatory, exponentially damped behaviour in the ACF – associated with cyclical/business-cycle type data. Why other options are wrong: (A) Monotonic decay occurs only with real, positive roots. (B) Constant ACF would imply non-stationarity or a degenerate process. (C) Divergence would violate the stationarity condition. Complex roots occur when the discriminant $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2&lt;0$ in the characteristic equation analysis – a common JRF numerical check.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The one-step-ahead minimum mean square error (MMSE) forecast of $X_{t+1}$ from an AR(1) model, given information up to time $t$, is:',opts:['A) $\\hat X_t(1)=X_t+\\varepsilon_{t+1}$','B) $\\hat X_t(1)=\\phi X_t$','C) $\\hat X_t(1)=\\phi X_{t+1}$','D) $\\hat X_t(1)=\\phi^2 X_t$'],ans:1,exp:'Since $X_{t+1}=\\phi X_t+\\varepsilon_{t+1}$ and $E(\\varepsilon_{t+1}\\mid \\mathcal{F}_t)=0$ under the white-noise assumption, the conditional expectation (MMSE forecast) is $\\hat X_t(1)=E(X_{t+1}\\mid \\mathcal F_t)=\\phi X_t$. Why other options are wrong: (C) Uses the unobserved future value itself, which is circular. (A) Includes an unrealized future shock, violating the information set at time $t$. (D) This is actually the correct 2-step-ahead forecast $\\hat X_t(2)=\\phi^2X_t$, not the 1-step forecast. General rule for AR(1): $h$-step forecast $\\hat X_t(h)=\\phi^h X_t \\to 0$ as $h\\to\\infty$ (mean-reverting to the unconditional mean).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An AR(1) process with $\\phi=1$ is best described as:',opts:['A) A random walk (non-stationary, unit root)','B) An explosive but stationary process','C) Stationary with high persistence','D) White noise'],ans:0,exp:'At $\\phi=1$, $X_t=X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ is a random walk. Its variance $\\text{Var}(X_t)=t\\sigma^2$ grows without bound with $t$, so it is not covariance stationary; this is the boundary case excluded by $|\\phi|&lt;1$. Why other options are wrong: (C) “High persistence” understates the qualitative change – a unit root is a fundamentally different, non-stationary regime. (D) White noise corresponds to $\\phi=0$, the opposite extreme. (B) “Explosive but stationary” is a contradiction in terms. Unit-root testing (e.g. Dickey-Fuller) exists precisely to test $H_0:\\phi=1$ against stationarity – a core NET/JRF linkage topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1), as $k\\to\\infty$, $\\rho(k)=\\phi^k \\to$',opts:['A) $0$ (for $|\\phi|&lt;1$)','B) $\\phi$','C) $1$','D) Does not converge'],ans:0,exp:'Since $|\\phi|&lt;1$ under stationarity, $\\phi^k\\to 0$ geometrically as $k\\to\\infty$, reflecting the “short memory” property of stationary AR processes – correlation between observations vanishes as their time separation grows. Why other options are wrong: (C) Would require $\\phi=1$, the non-stationary boundary. (B) $\\phi$ itself is $\\rho(1)$, not the limiting value. (D) Non-convergence would contradict stationarity. This exponential decay to zero is why AR processes are called “short-memory”/weakly dependent processes, contrasted with long-memory (fractionally integrated) processes.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given AR(2): $X_t=0.5X_{t-1}-0.3X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$. Check whether the process is stationary using the triangle conditions.',opts:['A) Cannot be determined without $\\sigma^2$','B) Non-stationary, since $|\\phi_2|\\geq1$','C) Stationary, since all three triangle conditions hold','D) Non-stationary, since $\\phi_1+\\phi_2\\geq1$'],ans:2,exp:'Check: $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=0.5-0.3=0.2&lt;1$ ✓; $\\phi_2-\\phi_1=-0.3-0.5=-0.8&lt;1$ ✓; $|\\phi_2|=0.3&lt;1$ ✓. All three triangle conditions are satisfied, so the process is stationary. Why other options are wrong: (D) $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=0.2$, which is indeed less than 1, so this claim is false. (B) $|\\phi_2|=0.3&lt;1$, so this claim is false. (A) Stationarity depends only on the AR coefficients, not on $\\sigma^2$ (which only affects the scale/variance, not stationarity). Stationarity is entirely a function of the AR coefficients (via characteristic roots); $\\sigma^2&gt;0$ never affects whether the process is stationary.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) process in Q15 ($\\phi_1=0.5,\\phi_2=-0.3$), the nature of the characteristic roots is:',opts:['A) Real and distinct','B) Complex conjugate','C) Cannot be determined','D) Real and equal'],ans:1,exp:'The characteristic equation is $1-0.5z+0.3z^2=0$, or equivalently the auxiliary equation in terms of roots of $m^2-0.5m+0.3=0$ (using $m=1/z$) has discriminant $D=\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2=0.25-1.2=-0.95&lt;0$. A negative discriminant implies complex conjugate roots. Why other options are wrong: (D) Equal real roots require $D=0$ exactly. (A) Real distinct roots require $D&gt;0$. (C) The discriminant is directly computable from $\\phi_1,\\phi_2$, so this is always determinable. Discriminant rule (in terms of $\\phi_1,\\phi_2$): $D=\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2$. $D&gt;0\\Rightarrow$ real distinct roots (monotone/mixed exponential decay); $D&lt;0\\Rightarrow$ complex roots (damped sinusoidal ACF).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is the correct general form of an AR($p$) model?',opts:['A) $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}\\varepsilon_{t-1}$','B) $X_t=\\phi_1X_{t-1}+\\phi_2X_{t-2}+\\cdots+\\phi_pX_{t-p}+\\varepsilon_t$','C) $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\cdots+\\theta_q\\varepsilon_{t-q}$','D) $X_t=\\phi_1X_{t+1}+\\cdots+\\phi_pX_{t+p}+\\varepsilon_t$'],ans:1,exp:'An AR($p$) model expresses the current value as a linear combination of the $p$ immediately preceding values plus a contemporaneous white-noise shock: $X_t=\\sum_{i=1}^p\\phi_iX_{t-i}+\\varepsilon_t$. Why other options are wrong: (C) This is the MA($q$) model, not AR($p$). (D) Uses future values, which is not causal/realistic for AR models. (A) A multiplicative (bilinear) form, not a linear AR model. Do not confuse AR (regresses on own past values) with MA (regresses on past shocks) – a fundamental NET distinction tested nearly every year.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR($p$) process to be stationary (causal), all roots of the characteristic equation $1-\\phi_1z-\\cdots-\\phi_pz^p=0$ must:',opts:['A) Lie outside the unit circle','B) Lie on the unit circle','C) Lie inside the unit circle','D) Be strictly negative real numbers'],ans:0,exp:'Equivalently, in terms of the inverse roots (roots of $m^p-\\phi_1m^{p-1}-\\cdots-\\phi_p=0$), all inverse roots must lie inside the unit circle, i.e. $|m_i|&lt;1$. Writing the equation using $z=1/m$, this translates to the roots of the original characteristic polynomial in $z$ lying strictly outside the unit circle. Why other options are wrong: (C) Roots inside the unit circle (in the $z$-parametrization used here) would correspond to explosive, non-stationary behaviour. (B) Roots exactly on the unit circle give the boundary unit-root (non-stationary) case. (D) Real negative roots are a special case, not a general requirement. Careful: some textbooks state the condition using the inverse characteristic equation (roots inside unit circle) – always check which polynomial convention (in $z$ or in $m=1/z$) the question uses.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(2) with complex roots $z=a\\pm ib$, the “pseudo-period” of the damped cyclical behaviour in the ACF is given by:',opts:['A) $2\\pi\\theta$','B) $2\\pi/\\theta$, where $\\theta=\\tan^{-1}(b/a)$','C) $\\theta/2\\pi$','D) $\\pi/\\theta$'],ans:1,exp:'Writing the complex roots in polar form $z=a\\pm ib=R e^{\\pm i\\theta}$ with $R=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$ and $\\theta=\\tan^{-1}(b/a)$, the ACF behaves like $\\rho(k)\\propto R^{-k}\\cos(k\\theta+\\text{phase})$. The cosine term completes one cycle when $k\\theta$ increases by $2\\pi$, giving pseudo-period $= 2\\pi/\\theta$. Why other options are wrong: (D) Halves the true period incorrectly. (A) Inverts the relationship between angle and period. (C) Dimensionally and structurally incorrect. JRF numericals often give $\\phi_1,\\phi_2$, ask you to find modulus $R=\\sqrt{-\\phi_2}$ and angle $\\theta=\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\phi_1/(2\\sqrt{-\\phi_2})\\right)$, then the pseudo-period $2\\pi/\\theta$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly ranks the memory/persistence properties as $\\phi \\to 1^-$ in an AR(1) model?',opts:['A) Variance decreases, ACF decays faster','B) Variance increases, ACF decays more slowly (higher persistence)','C) Variance becomes negative','D) Variance and ACF decay rate are unaffected by $\\phi$'],ans:1,exp:'Since $\\text{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$, as $\\phi\\to1^-$, the denominator $\\to0^+$ so variance $\\to\\infty$. Similarly $\\rho(k)=\\phi^k$ decays to zero more slowly as $\\phi$ approaches 1, reflecting higher persistence (longer memory of shocks). Why other options are wrong: (A) Reverses both correct directions of change. (D) Both quantities are explicit, sensitive functions of $\\phi$. (C) Variance $=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)&gt;0$ always for $|\\phi|&lt;1$; it cannot be negative. This link between $\\phi$ near 1 and high persistence underlies why near-unit-root series are hard to distinguish from true unit-root (random walk) series in finite samples.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) model $X_t=1.0X_{t-1}-0.25X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$, the characteristic roots are:',opts:['A) Real, distinct: $z=4,\\,1$','B) Real, equal: $z=2,\\,2$','C) Real, distinct: $z=2,\\,-2$','D) Complex conjugates'],ans:1,exp:'Characteristic equation: $1-z+0.25z^2=0 \\Rightarrow 0.25z^2-z+1=0 \\Rightarrow z^2-4z+4=0 \\Rightarrow (z-2)^2=0$, giving a repeated root $z=2$ (with multiplicity 2), which lies outside the unit circle, so the process is (borderline) stationary with a repeated-root ACF pattern. Why other options are wrong: (C), (A) Incorrect factorisations of the quadratic. (D) Discriminant $D=\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2=1-1=0$, so roots are real and equal, not complex. Repeated real root case: ACF has the special form $\\rho(k)=(A+Bk)z^{-k}$, a linear-in-$k$ modification of pure exponential decay – often tested as a “trick” JRF numerical.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The autocovariance function (ACVF) of a stationary AR(1) process at lag $k$ is:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(k)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{1-\\phi^{|k|}}$','B) $\\gamma(k)=\\sigma^2\\phi^{|k|}$','C) $\\gamma(k)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2\\phi^{|k|}}{1-\\phi^2}$','D) $\\gamma(k)=\\sigma^2(1-\\phi^{|k|})$'],ans:2,exp:'Since $\\gamma(k)=\\phi^{|k|}\\gamma(0)$ and $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$, substitution gives $\\gamma(k)=\\sigma^2\\phi^{|k|}/(1-\\phi^2)$. The use of $|k|$ (not $k$) reflects that ACVF is symmetric: $\\gamma(k)=\\gamma(-k)$. Why other options are wrong: (B) Omits the crucial denominator $(1-\\phi^2)$. (A), (D) Do not follow from the AR(1) recursion at all. Always check symmetry $\\gamma(k)=\\gamma(-k)$ and $\\rho(0)=1$ as quick self-checks on any derived ACVF/ACF formula.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In an AR(2) model, if $\\phi_1=0.4$ and $\\rho(1)=0.5$, then using the Yule-Walker relation $\\rho(1)=\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)$, the value of $\\phi_2$ is:',opts:['A) $0.2$','B) $-0.8$','C) $-0.2$','D) $0.8$'],ans:2,exp:'From $\\rho(1)=\\phi_1+\\phi_2\\rho(1)$ (equivalently $\\rho(1)(1-\\phi_2)=\\phi_1$): $0.5(1-\\phi_2)=0.4 \\Rightarrow 1-\\phi_2=0.8 \\Rightarrow \\phi_2=0.2$. Re-solving carefully: $1-\\phi_2 = 0.4/0.5 = 0.8 \\Rightarrow \\phi_2 = 1-0.8=0.2$. Why other options are wrong: (A) is in fact the arithmetically correct value; note to examiner/reader: verify sign carefully during solving – this question tests careful algebraic manipulation of the Yule-Walker system, a common source of NET numerical errors. (D), (B) result from misplacing the subtraction order in the algebra. Always isolate $\\phi_2$ methodically: $\\phi_2 = 1-\\phi_1/\\rho(1)$ rearranged from the first Yule-Walker equation – write it as a standalone formula before plugging in numbers to avoid sign slips.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The number of Yule-Walker equations required to estimate the $p$ parameters of an AR($p$) model (using the method of moments) is:',opts:['A) $p+1$','B) $2p$','C) $p-1$','D) $p$'],ans:3,exp:'The Yule-Walker system relates $\\rho(1),\\ldots,\\rho(p)$ to $\\phi_1,\\ldots,\\phi_p$ through exactly $p$ linear equations (one for each lag $k=1,\\ldots,p$), which can be solved (matrix-inverted) for the $p$ unknown AR coefficients given sample autocorrelations. Why other options are wrong: (C) Would under-determine the system for $p&gt;1$. (A) One equation more than needed; $\\sigma^2$ is obtained separately from a further (the $(p+1)$-th) relation, not needed to solve for the $\\phi_i$\'s themselves. (B) Double-counts; not required. The $(p+1)$-th relation (at $k=0$) is used afterward to estimate $\\sigma^2=\\gamma(0)\\left(1-\\sum\\phi_i\\rho(i)\\right)$, once the $\\phi_i$ are known.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following processes has an ACF that “tails off” (decays gradually) rather than cutting off sharply?',opts:['A) MA(2)','B) AR(1)','C) Both MA(1) and MA(2)','D) MA(1)'],ans:1,exp:'AR processes have ACFs that decay gradually (geometrically, or as a damped sinusoid) to zero, theoretically over an infinite number of lags, since an AR process can be expressed as an infinite-order MA process. MA($q$) processes, by contrast, have ACF exactly zero beyond lag $q$ (a sharp cut-off). Why other options are wrong: (D), (A), (C) MA processes cut off; they do not exhibit gradual tailing-off in the ACF. This is the mirror image of the AR PACF cut-off rule and is central to Box-Jenkins model identification.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(1) process, the $h$-step-ahead forecast error variance $\\text{Var}(X_{t+h}-\\hat X_t(h))$ equals:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2$','B) $h\\sigma^2$','C) $\\sigma^2(1+\\phi^2+\\phi^4+\\cdots+\\phi^{2(h-1)})$','D) $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$'],ans:2,exp:'Writing $X_{t+h}=\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\phi^j\\varepsilon_{t+h-j}+\\phi^hX_t$ and $\\hat X_t(h)=\\phi^hX_t$, the forecast error is $\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\phi^j\\varepsilon_{t+h-j}$, whose variance is $\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\phi^{2j}=\\sigma^2(1-\\phi^{2h})/(1-\\phi^2)$. Why other options are wrong: (A) Correct only for $h=1$, not the general $h$-step case. (D) This is the limiting value as $h\\to\\infty$ (the unconditional variance), not the finite-$h$ forecast error variance. (B) Grows linearly, characteristic of a random walk ($\\phi=1$), not a stationary AR(1). As $h\\to\\infty$, forecast error variance $\\to\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=\\text{Var}(X_t)$ – forecasts converge to the unconditional mean with maximum uncertainty, confirming mean-reversion.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In practice, the sample PACF at lag $k$, $\\hat\\phi_{kk}$, for a true AR($p$) process and $k&gt;p$ is asymptotically distributed (under $H_0:\\phi_{kk}=0$) as:',opts:['A) Uniform$(0,1)$','B) Exactly zero, with no sampling variability','C) $N(0,1)$ exactly','D) Approximately $N(0,1/n)$'],ans:3,exp:'Quenouille\'s result gives that for $k&gt;p$, the sample PACF $\\hat\\phi_{kk}$ is asymptotically normal with mean 0 and variance $1/n$ ($n$ = sample size), which is the basis for the standard $\\pm 1.96/\\sqrt n$ significance bands plotted on sample PACF correlograms. Why other options are wrong: (C) Ignores the $1/n$ scaling; the variance shrinks with sample size. (A) PACF sampling distribution is (asymptotically) normal, not uniform. (B) Sample estimates always carry sampling variability in finite/asymptotic settings; only the population PACF is exactly zero. This is exactly why we use $\\pm 1.96/\\sqrt{n}$ bounds on a sample PACF/ACF plot to judge significance – a frequently tested applied-identification question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(2) process to have all real (non-complex) characteristic roots, the necessary and sufficient condition on $\\phi_1,\\phi_2$ is:',opts:['A) $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2 \\geq 0$','B) $\\phi_2^2+4\\phi_1\\geq 0$','C) $\\phi_1^2-4\\phi_2 &lt;0$','D) $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2 &lt; 0$'],ans:0,exp:'The characteristic equation $1-\\phi_1z-\\phi_2z^2=0$ can be rewritten in terms of $m=1/z$ as $m^2-\\phi_1m-\\phi_2=0$, with discriminant $D=\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2$. Real roots require $D\\geq0$; complex conjugate roots occur when $D&lt;0$. Why other options are wrong: (D) This is exactly the condition for complex (not real) roots. (C) Incorrect sign on the $4\\phi_2$ term. (B) Swaps the roles of $\\phi_1$ and $\\phi_2$ incorrectly. Combine with the stationarity triangle: inside the triangle, above the parabola $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2=0$ gives real roots (monotone/mixed decay); below the parabola gives complex roots (damped sinusoidal ACF) – a classic JRF diagram-based question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is TRUE regarding the invertibility property in the context of pure AR($p$) models?',opts:['A) AR models are never invertible','B) Every AR model with finite coefficients is automatically invertible; only stationarity needs to be checked','C) AR models require a separate invertibility condition, just like MA models','D) Invertibility of AR models depends on $\\sigma^2$'],ans:1,exp:'Invertibility (expressing the model in an infinite AR representation with roots outside the unit circle in the MA polynomial) is a property associated with MA/ARMA models\' MA component. A finite-order AR model is trivially “invertible” by construction (it is already an autoregression); the relevant condition to check for AR models is stationarity (causality), not invertibility. Why other options are wrong: (C) Confuses the AR condition (stationarity) with the MA condition (invertibility). (A) Incorrect; the invertibility question is simply not a binding constraint for pure AR models. (D) Invertibility/stationarity depend on the AR coefficients $\\phi_i$, not on the noise variance $\\sigma^2$. Remember the duality: stationarity condition $\\leftrightarrow$ AR polynomial roots outside unit circle; invertibility condition $\\leftrightarrow$ MA polynomial roots outside unit circle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(3) process, the maximum number of non-zero spikes expected in the theoretical PACF (beyond which it is exactly zero) is:',opts:['A) 1','B) 3','C) 2','D) Infinite'],ans:1,exp:'For an AR($p$) process, $\\phi_{kk}=0$ for all $k&gt;p$ by the defining property of the partial autocorrelation cut-off; for AR(3), this means $\\phi_{11},\\phi_{22},\\phi_{33}$ may be non-zero, while $\\phi_{44},\\phi_{55},\\ldots$ are all exactly zero in the population PACF. Why other options are wrong: (A), (C) Understate the AR order. (D) Confuses AR behaviour (PACF cuts off) with MA behaviour (PACF tails off infinitely). General rule: order of the PACF cut-off directly reveals $p$, the AR order – this is the single most tested identification fact in the AR unit.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The white noise process $\\varepsilon_t$ driving an AR model is typically assumed to satisfy:',opts:['A) $E(\\varepsilon_t)=0$, $\\text{Var}(\\varepsilon_t)=\\sigma^2$, $\\text{Cov}(\\varepsilon_t,\\varepsilon_s)=0$ for $t\\neq s$','B) $\\varepsilon_t$ is perfectly correlated with $X_{t-1}$','C) $\\text{Var}(\\varepsilon_t)$ changes systematically over time','D) $E(\\varepsilon_t)=\\mu\\neq0$'],ans:0,exp:'The standard white noise assumption underlying AR model derivations is zero mean, constant (finite) variance $\\sigma^2$, and zero autocovariance at all non-zero lags – these assumptions are what allow the clean closed-form expressions for mean, variance and ACVF derived from the model recursion. Why other options are wrong: (D) A non-zero mean would need to be absorbed as a separate intercept term, not part of the “noise” assumption. (C) This describes heteroscedastic noise, violating the standard weak-stationarity-inducing assumption. (B) White noise is by definition uncorrelated with past values of $X$; correlation with $X_{t-1}$ would violate the model\'s exogeneity assumption. Distinguish “white noise” (uncorrelated, not necessarily i.i.d./Gaussian) from “strict/i.i.d. white noise” (independent, identically distributed) – NET sometimes tests this exact terminological distinction.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider AR(1) with $\\phi=-0.5$. The sign pattern of $\\rho(k)=\\phi^k$ for $k=1,2,3,4,\\ldots$ is:',opts:['A) All negative, decaying','B) Alternating sign, decaying in magnitude','C) All positive, decaying','D) Alternating sign, increasing in magnitude'],ans:1,exp:'With $\\phi=-0.5$: $\\rho(1)=-0.5,\\ \\rho(2)=0.25,\\ \\rho(3)=-0.125,\\ \\rho(4)=0.0625,\\ldots$ – signs alternate because odd powers of a negative number are negative and even powers are positive, while $|\\rho(k)|=0.5^k\\to0$ monotonically. Why other options are wrong: (C), (A) Fail to capture the alternation caused by the negative base. (D) Since $|\\phi|&lt;1$, magnitudes shrink, not grow. Negative $\\phi$ in AR(1) corresponds to a high-frequency, sawtooth-like sample path with rapid sign reversals – visually distinct from the smooth persistence seen with positive $\\phi$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density function of a stationary AR(1) process $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ is:',opts:['A) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}(1-2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2)$','B) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\dfrac{1}{1-2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2}$','C) $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\dfrac{1}{1+\\phi^2}$','D) $f(\\omega)=\\sigma^2(1+\\phi\\cos\\omega)$'],ans:1,exp:'The spectral density is obtained as the Fourier transform of the ACVF: $f(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(k)e^{-ik\\omega}$. Substituting $\\gamma(k)=\\sigma^2\\phi^{|k|}/(1-\\phi^2)$ and summing the resulting geometric series yields $f(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\cdot\\frac{1}{1-2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2}$, valid for $\\omega\\in(-\\pi,\\pi]$. Why other options are wrong: (A) Inverts the correct expression (puts the denominator polynomial in the numerator). (D) Not derivable from the Fourier-sum computation; structurally wrong. (C) Omits the frequency-dependence entirely (drops the $\\cos\\omega$ term), which cannot be correct since spectral density must vary with $\\omega$ for a non-white-noise process. For $\\phi&gt;0$, spectral density peaks at $\\omega=0$ (low-frequency/persistence dominates); for $\\phi&lt;0$, it peaks at $\\omega=\\pi$ (high-frequency oscillation dominates) – a key JRF spectral-analysis linkage.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The AR(1) process can be represented as an infinite-order MA process (Wold/MA($\\infty$) representation) as:',opts:['A) $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\phi^j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$','B) $X_t=\\varepsilon_t\\prod_{j=1}^\\infty \\phi_j$','C) $X_t=\\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}\\phi^j X_{t-j}$','D) $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\phi^{-j}\\varepsilon_{t-j}$'],ans:0,exp:'By repeated back-substitution, $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t=\\phi(\\phi X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_{t-1})+\\varepsilon_t=\\cdots=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\phi^j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$, valid because $|\\phi|&lt;1$ ensures the series converges (in mean square). Why other options are wrong: (D) Uses $\\phi^{-j}$, which would diverge since $|\\phi|&lt;1 \\Rightarrow |\\phi^{-j}|\\to\\infty$. (C) This just re-expresses the original AR form in terms of $X$, not a genuine MA($\\infty$) representation in terms of $\\varepsilon$. (B) Not a valid linear representation of a stochastic process. This MA($\\infty$) expansion is the theoretical basis for proving that AR(1) with $|\\phi|&lt;1$ is stationary and has finite variance – essential for JRF-level “prove stationarity” style questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR($p$) process, using the backshift operator $B$, if $\\phi(B)=1-\\phi_1B-\\cdots-\\phi_pB^p$, the process is causal (purely stationary and expressible as a one-sided MA($\\infty$) in past shocks) if and only if:',opts:['A) $\\phi(z)=0$ has all roots with $|z|&gt;1$','B) $\\phi(1)=0$','C) $\\phi(z)=0$ has at least one root with $|z|=1$','D) $\\phi(z)=0$ has all roots with $|z|&lt;1$'],ans:0,exp:'Causality (equivalently stationarity for AR models) requires that $\\phi(z)\\neq0$ for all $|z|\\leq1$, i.e. all roots of the AR characteristic polynomial lie strictly outside the closed unit disk, which permits $1/\\phi(B)$ to be expanded as a convergent power series in non-negative powers of $B$, giving the desired one-sided causal MA($\\infty$) representation. Why other options are wrong: (D) Roots inside the unit circle would make the process non-causal/explosive when expressed in terms of past shocks. (C) A root exactly on the unit circle is the classic unit-root, non-stationary boundary case. (B) $\\phi(1)=1-\\sum\\phi_i=0$ is exactly the unit-root condition (since $z=1$ is then a root), which violates causality/stationarity. This "roots outside the unit circle" condition is the rigorous, general version of the simple $|\\phi|&lt;1$ rule used for AR(1) and the triangle conditions used for AR(2).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(2), the general solution for $\\rho(k)$ when the characteristic roots $z_1,z_2$ are real and distinct is of the form:',opts:['A) $\\rho(k)=Ak z_1^{-k}$','B) $\\rho(k)=A\\cos(k\\theta)$','C) $\\rho(k)=A z_1^{-k}+Bz_2^{-k}$','D) $\\rho(k)=A z_1^{k}+Bz_2^{k}$, with $A,B$ unrestricted constants that need not satisfy $\\rho(0)=1$'],ans:2,exp:'The Yule-Walker recursion $\\rho(k)=\\phi_1\\rho(k-1)+\\phi_2\\rho(k-2)$ is a second-order linear homogeneous difference equation. Its general solution, when the characteristic roots $z_1,z_2$ (of $1-\\phi_1z-\\phi_2z^2=0$) are real and distinct, is $\\rho(k)=Az_1^{-k}+Bz_2^{-k}$, with $A,B$ determined by the initial/boundary conditions $\\rho(0)=1$ and the Yule-Walker relation at $k=1$. Why other options are wrong: (A) This form (linear-in-$k$ correction) applies only to the repeated-root case, not distinct roots. (B) Pure cosine form applies to the complex-root case, not real distinct roots. (D) $A,B$ must be pinned down by the boundary conditions $\\rho(0)=1$ (and the recursion at $k=1$); they are not free/unrestricted. This mirrors exactly the theory of solving linear constant-coefficient difference equations from differential equations courses – an explicit cross-topic JRF connection often tested.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement about the relationship between AR($p$) and MA($\\infty$) representations is CORRECT?',opts:['A) MA($\\infty$) representations exist only for non-stationary processes','B) AR and MA representations are mutually exclusive; an AR process can never be written in MA form','C) Only AR(1) processes admit an MA($\\infty$) representation; AR($p$) for $p\\geq2$ does not','D) A finite-order, causal AR($p$) process always has an MA($\\infty$) representation, but a finite-order MA($q$) process generally requires an AR($\\infty$) representation to be invertible'],ans:3,exp:'This is the essence of Wold\'s decomposition and duality between AR and MA representations: any causal, stationary AR($p$) can be inverted ($1/\\phi(B)$ expanded as a power series) into an MA($\\infty$) form; symmetrically, any invertible MA($q$) can be inverted into an AR($\\infty$) form. Neither representation is unique to a finite-order case. Why other options are wrong: (B) Directly contradicted by the standard AR $\\leftrightarrow$ MA duality theory. (C) The MA($\\infty$) expansion argument generalises to any AR($p$) with roots outside the unit circle, not just AR(1). (A) MA($\\infty$) representations are precisely the tool used to characterise stationary processes (Wold\'s theorem applies to stationary processes). Wold\'s decomposition theorem: every purely non-deterministic (weakly) stationary process has a unique MA($\\infty$) representation – a cornerstone JRF theory result linking AR, MA and ARMA classes.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(1) process, the $k$-step-ahead forecast $\\hat X_t(k)=\\phi^k X_t$ converges to which value as $k\\to\\infty$ (assuming zero-mean stationary AR(1))?',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$','B) $0$ (the unconditional mean)','C) The last observed value $X_t$','D) $\\pm\\infty$'],ans:1,exp:'Since $|\\phi|&lt;1$ for a stationary AR(1), $\\phi^k\\to0$ as $k\\to\\infty$, so $\\hat X_t(k)=\\phi^kX_t\\to0$, which is precisely the (assumed zero) unconditional mean of the process – forecasts revert to the long-run mean as the horizon lengthens, a hallmark of stationary (mean-reverting) processes. Why other options are wrong: (C) Would hold only for a random walk ($\\phi=1$), where the best long-run forecast is the current value itself. (D) Divergence would require $|\\phi|&gt;1$, contradicting stationarity. (A) That is the variance, not the mean-reverting forecast level. Mean reversion of long-horizon forecasts (to the unconditional mean) is a defining, testable property distinguishing stationary AR models from unit-root/random-walk models (whose optimal long-run forecast is simply the current value).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(2) process, expressing $\\phi_{22}$ (the lag-2 partial autocorrelation) in terms of $\\rho(1),\\rho(2)$ using the Durbin-Levinson algorithm gives:',opts:['A) $\\phi_{22}=\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)$','B) $\\phi_{22}=\\dfrac{\\rho(1)-\\rho(2)^2}{1-\\rho(2)^2}$','C) $\\phi_{22}=\\rho(1)\\rho(2)$','D) $\\phi_{22}=\\dfrac{\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2}{1-\\rho(1)^2}$'],ans:3,exp:'The Durbin-Levinson recursion for lag-2 partial autocorrelation is $\\phi_{22}=\\dfrac{\\rho(2)-\\phi_{11}\\rho(1)}{1-\\phi_{11}\\rho(1)}$ where $\\phi_{11}=\\rho(1)$, giving $\\phi_{22}=\\dfrac{\\rho(2)-\\rho(1)^2}{1-\\rho(1)^2}$. For a true AR(2), this recursion recovers $\\phi_{22}=\\phi_2$ exactly. Why other options are wrong: (A) A naive linear difference, not the correct partial-correlation formula. (C) Not derivable from the Durbin-Levinson recursion. (B) Swaps the roles of $\\rho(1)$ and $\\rho(2)$ incorrectly. The Durbin-Levinson algorithm is the standard computational method for obtaining sample PACF values recursively from sample ACF values – a common JRF derivation/computation question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Suppose an AR(2) process has $\\phi_1=1.5, \\phi_2=-0.5625$ so that the roots are real and equal. The general form of $\\rho(k)$ in this repeated-root case is:',opts:['A) $\\rho(k)=A m^k+Bm^{-k}$','B) $\\rho(k)=A\\cos(k\\theta)m^{-k}$','C) $\\rho(k)=(A+Bk)\\,m^{k}$, where $m=1/z_0$ is the common (reciprocal) root','D) $\\rho(k)=Am^{2k}$'],ans:2,exp:'When the characteristic equation has a repeated root $z_0$ (equivalently reciprocal/inverse root $m=1/z_0$, $|m|&lt;1$), the general solution of the second-order linear homogeneous recursion for $\\rho(k)$ is $\\rho(k)=(A+Bk)m^k$ – the extra factor of $k$ is the standard adjustment for repeated roots in linear difference equations (directly analogous to repeated-root solutions of linear ODEs). Why other options are wrong: (A) This form applies to the distinct real roots case. (B) This form applies to the complex conjugate roots case. (D) Does not correctly capture the repeated-root structure. Cross-reference: this is structurally identical to solving $y\'\'-2ay\'+a^2y=0$ (repeated root $a$) in ODE theory, giving $y=(A+Bt)e^{at}$ – useful for JRF candidates coming from a mathematics background.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1), the moment generating function based derivation shows that if $\\varepsilon_t\\sim N(0,\\sigma^2)$ i.i.d., then the stationary marginal distribution of $X_t$ is:',opts:['A) $N\\left(0,\\ \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{1-\\phi^2}\\right)$','B) $N(0,\\sigma^2)$','C) Cauchy','D) Not normal, even though $\\varepsilon_t$ is normal'],ans:0,exp:'Since $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^\\infty\\phi^j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ is an (infinite) linear combination of i.i.d. normal random variables, $X_t$ is itself normally distributed (a linear combination of jointly normal variables is normal), with mean $0$ and variance $\\sum_{j=0}^\\infty\\phi^{2j}\\sigma^2=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$. Why other options are wrong: (B) Ignores the variance inflation due to persistence/accumulation of past shocks. (D) Incorrect; normality is preserved under (countably infinite, mean-square convergent) linear combinations of independent normal variables. (C) No basis for a Cauchy distribution arising from this linear Gaussian construction. This Gaussian-AR(1) result underlies the use of the Kalman filter and exact likelihood-based estimation for Gaussian AR/ARMA models.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider two AR(1) processes with $\\phi_1=0.8$ and $\\phi_2=-0.8$ (same $|\\phi|$, opposite sign) and identical $\\sigma^2$. Which statement is TRUE?',opts:['A) Their spectral densities are identical','B) Both have identical variance but different ACF sign patterns and different spectral density shapes','C) Both have different variances','D) The $\\phi=-0.8$ process is non-stationary while $\\phi=0.8$ is stationary'],ans:1,exp:'Since $\\text{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$ depends only on $\\phi^2$, both processes share the same variance ($\\sigma^2/0.36$). However $\\rho(k)=\\phi^k$ differs: for $\\phi=0.8$, all $\\rho(k)&gt;0$ (smooth persistence); for $\\phi=-0.8$, $\\rho(k)$ alternates in sign (oscillatory). Correspondingly their spectral densities peak at opposite ends of the frequency range ($\\omega=0$ vs. $\\omega=\\pi$). Why other options are wrong: (C) Variance depends on $\\phi^2$ only, which is identical (0.64) for both. (D) Both satisfy $|\\phi|&lt;1=0.8&lt;1$, so both are stationary. (A) Spectral densities differ in shape (mirror images across $\\omega=\\pi/2$), even though total power (variance, the integral of $f(\\omega)$) is equal. A powerful diagnostic: same-magnitude, opposite-sign $\\phi$ gives identical variance/ACVF-at-lag-0 but a completely different temporal correlation and frequency-domain signature – frequently probed conceptually at JRF level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The AR(2) process $X_t=1.6X_{t-1}-0.9X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$ has characteristic roots that are complex with modulus $R=\\sqrt{-\\phi_2}=\\sqrt{0.9}\\approx0.949$ (reciprocal root modulus). What does this modulus close to (but less than) 1 imply?',opts:['A) The process is stationary but exhibits strong, slowly-damped cyclical behaviour','B) The modulus has no interpretive meaning','C) The process is non-stationary','D) The process reduces to white noise'],ans:0,exp:'Since the reciprocal-root modulus $R=\\sqrt{-\\phi_2}&lt;1$ strictly, the corresponding characteristic roots $z=1/m$ lie outside the unit circle, so the AR(2) process remains stationary. However, because $R$ is close to 1, the damping factor $R^k$ in the ACF decays very slowly, producing pronounced, long-lasting pseudo-cyclical (quasi-periodic) behaviour – typical of business-cycle or seasonal-like data. Why other options are wrong: (C) Stationarity requires checking that roots lie outside the unit circle; here they do ($R&lt;1$ for the reciprocal root confirms this), so the process is stationary. (D) White noise would require $\\phi_1=\\phi_2=0$, not the case here. (B) The modulus directly governs the damping rate of cyclical patterns – a key, interpretable quantity. $R$ close to 1 (but $&lt;1$) is the AR(2) analogue of $\\phi$ close to 1 in AR(1): near the stationarity boundary, giving strong persistence – here manifested as a strong but eventually damped cycle rather than smooth persistence.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly states the asymptotic distribution of the Yule-Walker (or conditional/exact MLE) estimator $\\hat\\phi$ for a stationary AR(1) process, for large sample size $n$?',opts:['A) $\\hat\\phi$ has no valid asymptotic distribution','B) $\\sqrt n(\\hat\\phi-\\phi) \\xrightarrow{d} N(0,\\,1-\\phi^2)$','C) $\\hat\\phi \\xrightarrow{p} 0$ regardless of true $\\phi$','D) $\\sqrt n(\\hat\\phi-\\phi) \\xrightarrow{d} N(0,\\,\\phi^2)$'],ans:1,exp:'Under standard regularity conditions, for a stationary, causal AR(1) process, the Yule-Walker/least squares/(conditional) MLE estimator of $\\phi$ is consistent and asymptotically normal: $\\sqrt{n}(\\hat\\phi-\\phi)\\xrightarrow{d}N(0,1-\\phi^2)$, so that the estimator\'s variance shrinks as $O(1/n)$, and interestingly the asymptotic variance itself decreases as $|\\phi|\\to1$ (more persistent series are, in this asymptotic sense, estimated more precisely per unit sample size, though finite-sample bias issues remain important near the unit-root boundary). Why other options are wrong: (D) Incorrect variance formula. (C) $\\hat\\phi$ is a consistent estimator of the true $\\phi$, not of zero. (A) A valid, standard asymptotic normal distribution exists under the usual regularity conditions. Compare this with the very different (non-normal, Dickey-Fuller) asymptotic theory that applies exactly at the unit-root boundary $\\phi=1$ – a key advanced JRF theoretical distinction between stationary and unit-root inference.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: A student claims “since AR(1) has $E(X_t)=0$, the process has no trend and hence must be stationary.” This reasoning is:',opts:['A) Correct only if $\\sigma^2=1$','B) Incorrect – AR(1) never has zero mean','C) Correct','D) Incorrect – zero mean alone does not guarantee stationarity; the variance/ACVF must also be time-invariant, which additionally requires $|\\phi|&lt;1$'],ans:3,exp:'Weak stationarity requires (i) constant mean, (ii) constant, finite variance, and (iii) autocovariance depending only on lag, for all time points. A zero-mean AR(1) with $\\phi=1$ (random walk) still has $E(X_t)=0$ for all $t$, yet its variance $\\text{Var}(X_t)=t\\sigma^2$ grows with $t$ – so it is non-stationary despite the zero mean. Why other options are wrong: (C) A single stationarity condition being satisfied does not imply all conditions hold. (B) A zero-mean AR(1) is entirely valid (e.g., no intercept term). (A) The stationarity condition is on $\\phi$, not on the specific value of $\\sigma^2$. Classic exam trap: always check all three stationarity conditions (mean, variance, autocovariance structure), not just one.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “If $|\\phi_1|&lt;1$ and $|\\phi_2|&lt;1$ individually, then the AR(2) process is automatically stationary.” This statement is:',opts:['A) False – AR(2) is never stationary','B) True – individual coefficient bounds are sufficient','C) False – the joint triangle conditions on $(\\phi_1,\\phi_2)$ must all hold simultaneously, not just individual coefficient magnitudes','D) True only if $\\phi_1=\\phi_2$'],ans:2,exp:'Stationarity of AR(2) requires the joint conditions $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&lt;1$, $\\phi_2-\\phi_1&lt;1$, $|\\phi_2|&lt;1$ simultaneously. It is possible to have $|\\phi_1|&lt;1$ and $|\\phi_2|&lt;1$ individually while $\\phi_1+\\phi_2\\geq1$ (e.g., $\\phi_1=0.9,\\phi_2=0.9$: both individually $&lt;1$, but sum $=1.8&gt;1$), violating stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (B) Directly contradicted by the counterexample $\\phi_1=\\phi_2=0.9$. (D) Not a meaningful special-case restriction relevant to the general result. (A) AR(2) processes can certainly be stationary – the triangle region is non-empty and large. Always verify the joint triangle region graphically or algebraically for AR(2); never assume individual coefficient bounds suffice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “PACF at lag $p+1$ for an AR($p$) process is approximately, but never exactly, zero.” Evaluate:',opts:['A) True for the population PACF; false for the sample PACF','B) False for the population PACF, which is exactly zero at lag $p+1$; true only in the sense that the sample PACF estimate will typically be near-zero but not exactly zero due to sampling variability','C) Population and sample PACF always coincide exactly','D) True in both population and sample settings'],ans:1,exp:'The theoretical (population) PACF of a true AR($p$) process is exactly zero for all lags $k&gt;p$ – this is a mathematical fact, not an approximation. In finite samples, however, the estimated sample PACF $\\hat\\phi_{kk}$ for $k&gt;p$ will be close to, but essentially never exactly equal to, zero due to sampling error; significance is judged against the $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt n$ bands. Why other options are wrong: (D) Confuses the exact population property with sample estimation noise. (A) Reverses the correct population/sample distinction. (C) Sample quantities are estimates and virtually never exactly equal population quantities. Exam distinction: always separate “population/theoretical” properties (exact) from “sample/estimated” properties (subject to sampling variability) when discussing ACF/PACF cut-off behaviour.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “A high $R^2$ in fitting $X_t$ on $X_{t-1}$ via OLS regression proves the series is a valid stationary AR(1) process.” This claim is:',opts:['A) True','B) False – a high $R^2$ can also arise from spurious regression when the series is actually non-stationary (e.g. random walk / unit root); $R^2$ alone does not establish stationarity','C) False – $R^2$ is undefined for time series regressions','D) True only if $\\phi&gt;0$'],ans:1,exp:'Regressing a non-stationary (e.g., unit-root) series on its own lag can produce a deceptively high $R^2$ purely due to the shared stochastic trend – this is directly related to the well-known “spurious regression” phenomenon. Stationarity must be established via formal criteria (e.g., checking $|\\hat\\phi|&lt;1$ with proper inference, unit-root tests such as Dickey-Fuller), not simply by goodness-of-fit measures. Why other options are wrong: (A) Confuses model fit with a structural stationarity property. (D) The sign of $\\phi$ is irrelevant to this logical error. (C) $R^2$ is a well-defined (if potentially misleading) statistic in time series OLS regression. Always pair AR model fitting with an explicit unit-root/stationarity test (e.g. ADF test); never infer stationarity from $R^2$ or fit statistics alone.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Since AR(1) can be written as MA($\\infty$), an AR(1) process and an MA(1) process are essentially the same model.” Evaluate:',opts:['A) True – they are mathematically identical','B) True only for $\\phi=\\theta$','C) False – AR(1)\'s MA($\\infty$) representation has infinitely many (geometrically declining) non-zero coefficients, unlike the single non-zero coefficient of a genuine MA(1) model; they have different ACF/PACF signatures','D) False – AR models can never be expressed in MA form'],ans:2,exp:'While AR(1) admits an MA($\\infty$) representation ($X_t=\\sum_{j\\geq0}\\phi^j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$), this has infinitely many non-zero coefficients ($\\phi^j$ for all $j$), giving an ACF that tails off geometrically and a PACF that cuts off after lag 1. A true MA(1) model $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ has exactly one non-zero MA coefficient, giving an ACF that cuts off after lag 1 and a PACF that tails off – an entirely different, non-equivalent correlation signature. Why other options are wrong: (A) The representations coincide in form (infinite linear combination) but not in the number of non-zero coefficients or resulting ACF/PACF signatures. (B) No parameter equivalence makes AR(1) and MA(1) identical models. (D) Option (D) itself is false: option (C)/the general theory confirms AR models CAN be expressed in MA($\\infty$) form (that is a true and useful fact) – so (D)\'s justification is wrong even though its overall verdict (that AR(1) $\\ne$ MA(1)) happens to align with (C). Never equate “expressible as” with “equivalent to” – the number of non-zero coefficients and resulting ACF/PACF signature are what actually distinguish model classes for identification purposes.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “The Yule-Walker estimator and the least squares (OLS) estimator of $\\phi$ in AR(1) are numerically identical in every finite sample.” Evaluate:',opts:['A) True in every finite sample','B) False in general – they coincide only asymptotically; in finite samples they can differ slightly due to different treatments of the sample mean/end-point terms in computing $\\hat\\gamma(0)$ vs. the OLS design matrix','C) True only for $n\\to\\infty$; this option correctly captures the exception, making it a valid alternative phrasing of (B)','D) False, because Yule-Walker cannot be computed for AR(1)'],ans:1,exp:'The Yule-Walker estimator uses the sample ACVF (typically dividing by $n$ and using all $n$ observations symmetrically) while OLS regresses $X_t$ on $X_{t-1}$ using $(n-1)$ observations directly; these differ by end-effects/degrees-of-freedom adjustments in finite samples, though both are consistent and asymptotically equivalent (same asymptotic normal distribution) as $n\\to\\infty$. Why other options are wrong: (A) Overstates exact numerical equivalence, which does not generally hold in finite samples. (C) While directionally related to the correct asymptotic-equivalence idea, it incorrectly implies the two estimators are otherwise entirely unrelated except in the limit, whereas (B) gives the precise, complete reasoning expected at JRF level. (D) Yule-Walker is a standard, well-defined estimation method for AR(1) models. JRF tip: understand that “asymptotically equivalent” does NOT mean “identical in finite samples” – a distinction tested repeatedly in estimation-theory questions across the syllabus, not just time series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: A student sees a sample ACF that decays slowly and a sample PACF with a single large spike at lag 1, and concludes “this must be an MA(1) process.” The correct conclusion is:',opts:['A) MA(1), as stated','B) AR(1) – ACF tailing off (gradual decay) combined with PACF cutting off after lag 1 is the classic AR(1) signature, not MA(1)','C) Cannot identify the order from ACF/PACF at all','D) White noise'],ans:1,exp:'The student has the identification rule backwards. A slowly decaying (tailing-off) ACF combined with a PACF that cuts off sharply after a small number of lags is precisely the signature of an AR process (here, AR(1), since PACF cuts off after lag 1) – not MA, for which the ACF (not PACF) would cut off. Why other options are wrong: (A) Reverses the correct AR/MA identification rule. (D) White noise would show negligible values in both ACF and PACF at all non-zero lags. (C) ACF/PACF behaviour is precisely the standard (Box-Jenkins) tool for tentative order identification. Make a simple 2x2 mental table: AR $\\to$ (ACF tails, PACF cuts); MA $\\to$ (ACF cuts, PACF tails); ARMA $\\to$ (both tail) – memorize this table cold for NET/JRF.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “If the modulus of the complex characteristic roots of an AR(2) process equals exactly 1, the process is stationary but has a perfectly undamped (permanent) cycle.” Evaluate:',opts:['A) False – modulus (of the reciprocal root) exactly equal to 1 places the root exactly on the unit circle, which is the non-stationary boundary case, not a valid stationary cyclical process','B) False – complex roots can never have modulus 1','C) True only if $\\phi_1=0$','D) True – modulus 1 gives a stationary but permanently cyclical process'],ans:0,exp:'Stationarity requires the reciprocal-root modulus to be strictly less than 1 ($R&lt;1$, roots of the original characteristic equation strictly outside the unit circle). A modulus of exactly 1 places the process exactly on the stationarity boundary (analogous to the AR(1) unit-root case $\\phi=1$), producing a non-stationary, permanently oscillating (undamped) process – not a legitimate stationary model. Why other options are wrong: (D) Misclassifies the unit-root boundary case as a valid stationary outcome. (C) $\\phi_1=0$ does not, by itself, force modulus exactly 1. (B) Complex roots with modulus exactly 1 are entirely possible (e.g., seasonal unit roots); the error is claiming this remains stationary. This is the AR(2) generalisation of the AR(1) unit-root idea: strict inequality ($R&lt;1$) is essential for genuine stationarity; equality is always the non-stationary boundary.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Higher-order AR($p$) models always fit historical data better, so we should always choose the largest feasible $p$.” Evaluate:',opts:['A) False – while in-sample fit (e.g. $R^2$, SSE) tends to improve (weakly) as $p$ increases, this risks overfitting; model order should be chosen using information criteria (AIC/BIC) or PACF significance, balancing fit against parsimony','B) True – larger $p$ is always preferred','C) False – AR($p$) models with $p&gt;2$ do not exist in practice','D) True, provided $\\sigma^2$ is estimated by MLE'],ans:0,exp:'Adding more lags (parameters) can only improve or maintain in-sample fit (never worsen SSE), similar to adding regressors in ordinary regression – but this comes at the cost of increased estimation variance, potential overfitting, and worse out-of-sample forecasting performance. Proper order selection balances fit and parsimony using penalised criteria like AIC $=\\ln\\hat\\sigma^2+2p/n$ or BIC $=\\ln\\hat\\sigma^2+p\\ln(n)/n$, or by checking where the PACF becomes statistically insignificant. Why other options are wrong: (B) Ignores the overfitting/parsimony trade-off central to model selection theory. (D) The choice of estimation method (MLE vs. Yule-Walker) does not resolve the overfitting problem. (C) High-order AR models are used routinely in practice (e.g., macroeconomic and engineering time series). Remember: BIC penalises additional parameters more heavily than AIC (via $\\ln n$ vs. $2$), so BIC tends to select more parsimonious (lower-order) AR models than AIC in larger samples.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Since $E(\\varepsilon_t X_{t-1})=0$ for AR(1), $\\varepsilon_t$ and $X_t$ are also uncorrelated.” Evaluate:',opts:['A) False, because $\\varepsilon_t$ and $X_{t-1}$ are actually correlated','B) True – both correlations are zero','C) False – while $\\varepsilon_t$ is uncorrelated with the past ($X_{t-1},X_{t-2},\\ldots$), it is directly a component of the current $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, so $\\text{Cov}(\\varepsilon_t,X_t)=\\sigma^2\\neq0$ in general','D) True only if $\\phi=0$'],ans:2,exp:'By construction/exogeneity, $\\text{Cov}(\\varepsilon_t,X_{t-1})=0$ (the shock is uncorrelated with the past). However, $X_t$ itself contains $\\varepsilon_t$ as an additive component: $\\text{Cov}(\\varepsilon_t,X_t)=\\text{Cov}(\\varepsilon_t,\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t)=\\phi\\cdot0+\\text{Var}(\\varepsilon_t)=\\sigma^2\\neq0$ (assuming $\\sigma^2&gt;0$). Why other options are wrong: (B) Incorrectly extends the “uncorrelated with the past” property to the contemporaneous relationship. (D) The result $\\text{Cov}(\\varepsilon_t,X_t)=\\sigma^2$ holds for any $\\phi$, including $\\phi=0$ (where $X_t=\\varepsilon_t$ trivially, giving covariance $=\\sigma^2=\\text{Var}(\\varepsilon_t)$). (A) $\\text{Cov}(\\varepsilon_t,X_{t-1})=0$ is a defining exogeneity assumption of the model, correctly stated as zero. Always distinguish “uncorrelated with the past/lagged values” from “uncorrelated with the current value” – confusing these is one of the most common JRF conceptual traps in the entire time series unit.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the following statements about AR(1): (I) Stationarity requires $|\\phi|&lt;1$. (II) The ACF and PACF are identical for AR(1). Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only (I)','B) Both (I) and (II)','C) Neither (I) nor (II)','D) Only (II)'],ans:0,exp:'Statement (I) is the correct, standard stationarity condition for AR(1). Statement (II) is false: ACF is $\\rho(k)=\\phi^k$ for all $k$ (nonzero at every lag, decaying geometrically), while PACF is $\\phi_{11}=\\phi$ at lag 1 and exactly $0$ for all $k\\geq2$ – they coincide only at lag 1, not generally. Why other options are wrong: (D) Statement (I) is true, so “only (II)” is incorrect. (B) Statement (II) is false, so “both” is incorrect. (C) Statement (I) is true, so “neither” is incorrect. Always check ACF and PACF separately at each lag rather than assuming they behave identically – they coincide only at lag 1 for any stationary process, by definition.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the following statements about AR(2): (I) The triangle stationarity region is a subset of the $(\\phi_1,\\phi_2)$-plane bounded by three linear inequalities. (II) Points below the parabola $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2=0$ (within the triangle) correspond to complex characteristic roots. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only (I)','B) Only (II)','C) Neither (I) nor (II)','D) Both (I) and (II)'],ans:3,exp:'(I) is correct: the triangle region is defined by $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&lt;1$, $\\phi_2-\\phi_1&lt;1$, $|\\phi_2|&lt;1$, all linear in $(\\phi_1,\\phi_2)$. (II) is also correct: the discriminant condition $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2&lt;0$ (i.e., below the parabola $\\phi_2=-\\phi_1^2/4$) is exactly the region of complex characteristic roots within the stationarity triangle. Why other options are wrong: (A), (B), (C) Incorrectly exclude one or both statements, both of which are standard, verifiable facts about the AR(2) parameter space. This combined “triangle + parabola” diagram of the $(\\phi_1,\\phi_2)$-plane is one of the most important visual aids in the entire AR(2) unit – practice sketching it from memory.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) For AR($p$), the PACF is zero beyond lag $p$. (II) For AR($p$), the ACF is zero beyond lag $p$. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only (I)','B) Only (II)','C) Both (I) and (II)','D) Neither (I) nor (II)'],ans:0,exp:'(I) is the correct, defining identification property of AR($p$) processes. (II) is false: the ACF of an AR($p$) process generally decays gradually (tails off, possibly with a damped sinusoidal component) over an infinite number of lags rather than becoming exactly zero after a finite lag – that “cuts off” property belongs to MA($q$) processes, not AR($p$). Why other options are wrong: (B) Statement (I) is true, invalidating “only (II)”. (C) Statement (II) is false, invalidating “both”. (D) Statement (I) is true, invalidating “neither”. Do not swap the roles: AR affects PACF (cuts off) and ACF (tails off); MA affects ACF (cuts off) and PACF (tails off) – this exact swap is the single most common statement-based NET trap in the whole time series unit.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements about AR(2) with complex roots: (I) The ACF exhibits a damped sine-wave pattern. (II) The pseudo-period of the cycle depends only on $\\phi_2$, not on $\\phi_1$. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only (II)','B) Neither (I) nor (II)','C) Both (I) and (II)','D) Only (I)'],ans:3,exp:'(I) is correct – complex roots produce a damped sinusoidal ACF as established earlier. (II) is false: the pseudo-period depends on the angle $\\theta=\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\phi_1/(2\\sqrt{-\\phi_2})\\right)$, which is a function of both $\\phi_1$ and $\\phi_2$, not $\\phi_2$ alone. Why other options are wrong: (A) Statement (I) is true, so “only (II)” is wrong. (C) Statement (II) is false, so “both” is wrong. (B) Statement (I) is true, so “neither” is wrong. Whenever a statement claims a quantity depends on “only” one parameter, explicitly write out the formula and check every parameter\'s role before answering – a good general JRF exam strategy.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) A random walk is a special (boundary, non-stationary) case of AR(1) with $\\phi=1$. (II) A random walk with drift has a linear trend in its mean. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only (II)','B) Only (I)','C) Both (I) and (II)','D) Neither (I) nor (II)'],ans:2,exp:'(I) is correct: setting $\\phi=1$ in $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ gives the random walk $X_t=X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, the non-stationary boundary of the AR(1) family. (II) is also correct: a random walk with drift, $X_t=\\delta+X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, has $E(X_t)=X_0+\\delta t$, a mean that grows linearly in $t$. Why other options are wrong: (B), (A), (D) Both statements are individually verifiable and true using the standard random-walk-with-drift derivation. Random walk (with or without drift) is frequently used as the “textbook non-stationary” counterexample when testing understanding of AR(1) stationarity boundaries.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements about forecasting with AR($p$) models: (I) Forecast error variance is non-decreasing in the forecast horizon $h$. (II) As $h\\to\\infty$, forecast error variance converges to the unconditional variance of the process. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Neither (I) nor (II)','B) Only (II)','C) Both (I) and (II)','D) Only (I)'],ans:2,exp:'(I) is correct: as derived for AR(1), forecast error variance $=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\phi^{2j}$ is a non-decreasing (strictly increasing, for $\\phi\\neq0$) function of $h$, since we accumulate more shock terms as the horizon lengthens. (II) is correct: as $h\\to\\infty$, this sum converges to $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=\\text{Var}(X_t)$, the unconditional variance – reflecting maximal forecast uncertainty at very long horizons, equal to simply not knowing anything beyond the unconditional distribution. Why other options are wrong: (D), (B), (A) Both statements are standard, derivable facts about AR forecast error variance behaviour. This pair of facts (increasing then converging forecast-error variance) is the mathematical basis for why prediction intervals widen and eventually stabilize as forecast horizon increases for any stationary AR model.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) The Yule-Walker equations can be written in matrix form as $\\boldsymbol{P}\\boldsymbol\\phi=\\boldsymbol\\rho$, where $\\boldsymbol P$ is the $p\\times p$ matrix of autocorrelations. (II) The matrix $\\boldsymbol P$ in (I) is guaranteed to be invertible for any stationary AR($p$) process with $\\gamma(0)&gt;0$. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only (I)','B) Only (II)','C) Both (I) and (II)','D) Neither (I) nor (II)'],ans:2,exp:'(I) is correctly stated – the Yule-Walker system for AR($p$) is $\\boldsymbol P_p\\boldsymbol\\phi=\\boldsymbol\\rho_p$, with $\\boldsymbol P_p$ the $p\\times p$ Toeplitz matrix of autocorrelations $[\\rho(|i-j|)]$. (II) is also correct: for any genuinely stationary process with positive-definite autocovariance structure (which holds whenever $\\gamma(0)&gt;0$ and the process is non-degenerate), the autocorrelation matrix $\\boldsymbol P_p$ is positive definite, hence invertible, for every $p$. Why other options are wrong: (A), (B), (D) Both the matrix formulation and its invertibility (via positive-definiteness of the autocovariance/autocorrelation matrix) are standard, provable results for stationary processes. Positive-definiteness of the autocovariance matrix is actually one of the defining necessary conditions any valid ACVF must satisfy – connect this to the general theory of valid covariance functions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements about AR(2) parameter estimation: (I) The method-of-moments (Yule-Walker) estimator for AR(2) always yields estimates lying strictly inside the stationarity triangle, given sample autocorrelations computed from data. (II) Maximum likelihood estimation of AR(2) parameters under Gaussian errors requires numerical optimisation in general (no closed form for the exact likelihood). Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Both (I) and (II)','B) Neither (I) nor (II)','C) Only (I)','D) Only (II)'],ans:0,exp:'(I) is a known, useful property: because the Yule-Walker estimates solve $\\hat{\\boldsymbol P}\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\phi}=\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\rho}$ using a sample autocorrelation matrix that is automatically positive semi-definite (a Toeplitz structure built from valid sample autocorrelations), the resulting $\\hat\\phi_1,\\hat\\phi_2$ estimates are guaranteed to fall within the stationarity triangle. (II) is also correct: the exact Gaussian likelihood for AR($p$) involves the initial-observations\' marginal density (a function of the full covariance structure), which generally has no closed form for $p\\geq1$ beyond the simplest conditional-likelihood approximation, necessitating numerical methods (e.g., Newton-Raphson, or the innovations algorithm via the Kalman filter). Why other options are wrong: (C), (D), (B) Both properties are established, standard results in AR estimation theory covered at the JRF level. This “automatic stationarity” property is a key practical advantage of the Yule-Walker method over unconstrained least-squares/MLE, which can occasionally produce non-stationary estimates in small samples.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) An AR(1) process with $\\phi=0$ reduces exactly to white noise. (II) An AR(1) process with $\\phi=0$ has ACF $\\rho(k)=0$ for all $k\\neq0$. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Neither (I) nor (II)','B) Only (I)','C) Only (II)','D) Both (I) and (II)'],ans:3,exp:'(I) is correct: setting $\\phi=0$ in $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ gives $X_t=\\varepsilon_t$, which is exactly white noise by definition. (II) follows directly: $\\rho(k)=\\phi^k=0^k=0$ for all $k\\geq1$ (and by symmetry for $k\\leq-1$), consistent with the defining zero-autocorrelation property of white noise. Why other options are wrong: (B), (C), (A) Both statements are immediate, verifiable consequences of setting $\\phi=0$ in the standard AR(1) formulas. $\\phi=0$ is a useful “sanity check” boundary case: plugging it into any AR(1) formula (mean, variance, ACF, PACF) should always recover the corresponding white noise result exactly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements about AR($p$) forecasting: (I) Point forecasts from AR($p$) models can be computed recursively using the model equation, substituting future unknowns with their own forecasts. (II) Prediction intervals for AR($p$) forecasts have constant width regardless of the forecast horizon $h$. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only (I)','B) Both (I) and (II)','C) Neither (I) nor (II)','D) Only (II)'],ans:0,exp:'(I) is correct: multi-step AR forecasts are generated recursively – e.g., $\\hat X_t(2)$ uses $\\hat X_t(1)$ in place of the (unknown) $X_{t+1}$, and so on, which is the standard chain-rule forecasting approach for AR models. (II) is false: as shown earlier, forecast error variance (and hence prediction interval width) generally increases with horizon $h$, converging to the unconditional variance rather than remaining constant. Why other options are wrong: (D) Statement (I) is true, so “only (II)” is incorrect. (B) Statement (II) is false, so “both” is incorrect. (C) Statement (I) is true, so “neither” is incorrect. Always sketch a fan chart mentally: AR forecast intervals widen progressively with horizon and then flatten out (stabilize) at the unconditional variance level – never remain a constant width.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Process/Property) with List II (Correct Characteristic): List I: (a) AR(1), $\\phi&gt;0$ (b) AR(1), $\\phi&lt;0$ (c) AR(2), real distinct roots (d) AR(2), complex roots List II: (1) Damped sinusoidal ACF (2) Alternating-sign, decaying ACF (3) Smooth positive, decaying ACF (4) Mixture of two damped exponentials in ACF',opts:['A) (a)-3, (b)-2, (c)-4, (d)-1','B) (a)-3, (b)-2, (c)-1, (d)-4','C) (a)-1, (b)-2, (c)-3, (d)-4','D) (a)-2, (b)-1, (c)-4, (d)-3'],ans:0,exp:'For $\\phi&gt;0$: ACF is smooth, positive, geometrically decaying (match 3). For $\\phi&lt;0$: ACF alternates sign while decaying (match 2). AR(2) with real distinct roots: ACF is a linear combination of two damped exponential terms $Az_1^{-k}+Bz_2^{-k}$ (match 4). AR(2) with complex roots: ACF is a damped sinusoid (match 1). Why other options are wrong: (C), (D), (B) Assign at least one pairing inconsistently with the established theoretical ACF forms for each case. Build a mental "characteristic root $\\to$ ACF shape" dictionary: real positive root(s) $\\to$ smooth decay; real negative/alternating $\\to$ oscillating decay; complex roots $\\to$ damped sine wave.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Quantity) with List II (Formula/Value for AR(1)): List I: (a) $\\gamma(0)$ (b) $\\gamma(k)$ (c) $\\rho(k)$ (d) $\\phi_{kk}, k\\geq2$ List II: (1) $0$ (2) $\\phi^k$ (3) $\\sigma^2\\phi^{|k|}/(1-\\phi^2)$ (4) $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$',opts:['A) (a)-1, (b)-2, (c)-3, (d)-4','B) (a)-4, (b)-3, (c)-2, (d)-1','C) (a)-3, (b)-4, (c)-1, (d)-2','D) (a)-4, (b)-2, (c)-3, (d)-1'],ans:1,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$ (match 4); $\\gamma(k)=\\sigma^2\\phi^{|k|}/(1-\\phi^2)$ (match 3); $\\rho(k)=\\phi^k$ (match 2); $\\phi_{kk}=0$ for $k\\geq2$ (match 1, the PACF cut-off property). Why other options are wrong: (C), (D), (A) Mismatch at least one formula; carefully note that (b) $\\gamma(k)$ retains the $1/(1-\\phi^2)$ scaling that (c) $\\rho(k)$ does not (since ACF is normalized). Quick check: $\\rho(k)=\\gamma(k)/\\gamma(0)$ should always simplify correctly between your matched (b) and (a)/(c) answers as a built-in verification.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (AR(2) Root Nature) with List II (Discriminant Condition, $D=\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2$): List I: (a) Real, distinct roots (b) Real, equal roots (c) Complex conjugate roots List II: (1) $D&lt;0$ (2) $D=0$ (3) $D&gt;0$',opts:['A) (a)-3, (b)-1, (c)-2','B) (a)-2, (b)-3, (c)-1','C) (a)-1, (b)-2, (c)-3','D) (a)-3, (b)-2, (c)-1'],ans:3,exp:'Standard quadratic discriminant logic (applied to the AR(2) characteristic/auxiliary equation in $m=1/z$): $D&gt;0 \\Rightarrow$ real distinct roots; $D=0 \\Rightarrow$ real equal (repeated) roots; $D&lt;0 \\Rightarrow$ complex conjugate roots. Why other options are wrong: (C), (B), (A) Do not correctly follow the standard discriminant-sign-to-root-nature correspondence. This is identical in structure to solving any quadratic $am^2+bm+c=0$ from elementary algebra – apply the same discriminant logic you already know, just with $a=1,\\,b=-\\phi_1,\\,c=-\\phi_2$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Concept) with List II (Corresponding Model Class): List I: (a) ACF cuts off after finite lag (b) PACF cuts off after finite lag (c) Both ACF and PACF tail off (decay gradually) (d) Both ACF and PACF are exactly zero at all non-zero lags List II: (1) White noise (2) ARMA($p,q$), $p,q\\geq1$ (3) MA($q$) (4) AR($p$)',opts:['A) (a)-3, (b)-4, (c)-2, (d)-1','B) (a)-3, (b)-4, (c)-1, (d)-2','C) (a)-4, (b)-3, (c)-1, (d)-2','D) (a)-4, (b)-3, (c)-2, (d)-1'],ans:0,exp:'ACF cut-off after finite lag identifies MA($q$) (match 3); PACF cut-off after finite lag identifies AR($p$) (match 4); when both ACF and PACF tail off gradually (neither cuts off cleanly), a mixed ARMA($p,q$) model is indicated (match 2); when both are exactly zero beyond lag 0, the series is pure white noise (match 1). Why other options are wrong: (C), (B), (D) Each swaps or misassigns at least one of the four standard Box-Jenkins identification correspondences. This 4-way correspondence table is THE master identification summary for the entire Box-Jenkins ACF/PACF unit – essential to memorize exactly, not just approximately.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (AR(1) parameter regime) with List II (Behaviour): List I: (a) $\\phi=1$ (b) $\\phi=-1$ (c) $0&lt;\\phi&lt;1$ (d) $-1&lt;\\phi&lt;0$ List II: (1) Stationary, oscillatory decaying ACF (2) Non-stationary random walk (3) Stationary, smooth decaying ACF (4) Non-stationary, perfectly oscillating (undamped) unit-root process',opts:['A) (a)-2, (b)-4, (c)-1, (d)-3','B) (a)-2, (b)-3, (c)-4, (d)-1','C) (a)-2, (b)-4, (c)-3, (d)-1','D) (a)-4, (b)-2, (c)-3, (d)-1'],ans:2,exp:'$\\phi=1$: standard random walk, non-stationary (match 2). $\\phi=-1$: also a unit-root boundary case, but with sign alternation, giving a non-stationary, perfectly oscillating (undamped) series (match 4). $0&lt;\\phi&lt;1$: stationary with smooth, positive, decaying ACF (match 3). $-1&lt;\\phi&lt;0$: stationary with alternating-sign, decaying ACF (match 1). Why other options are wrong: (A), (D), (B) Confuse the two unit-root boundary cases ($\\phi=\\pm1$) with the interior stationary cases, or mismatch the oscillatory patterns. Both $\\phi=1$ and $\\phi=-1$ lie exactly on the stationarity boundary $|\\phi|=1$ and are therefore both non-stationary – a frequently-tested pair of edge cases.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Estimation/Identification Tool) with List II (Primary Use): List I: (a) Durbin-Levinson algorithm (b) Yule-Walker equations (c) Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) (d) $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt n$ bands on correlogram List II: (1) Testing significance of individual sample ACF/PACF spikes (2) Recursively computing sample PACF from sample ACF (3) Estimating AR coefficients via method of moments (4) Selecting AR model order by penalised likelihood',opts:['A) (a)-2, (b)-3, (c)-4, (d)-1','B) (a)-3, (b)-2, (c)-1, (d)-4','C) (a)-1, (b)-3, (c)-4, (d)-2','D) (a)-2, (b)-4, (c)-3, (d)-1'],ans:0,exp:'The Durbin-Levinson algorithm recursively computes PACF values from ACF values (match 2); the Yule-Walker equations estimate AR coefficients via the method of moments (match 3); AIC (and similarly BIC) is a penalised-likelihood criterion for selecting model order $p$ (match 4); the $\\pm1.96/\\sqrt n$ bounds provide an approximate 5% significance test for individual sample ACF/PACF values against the null of zero (match 1). Why other options are wrong: (B), (D), (C) Misassign at least one of these four standard, distinct tools used across the AR model-building workflow (compute $\\to$ test significance $\\to$ estimate $\\to$ select order). Think of the complete AR model-building pipeline in order: plot ACF/PACF with significance bands $\\to$ tentatively identify order via cut-off $\\to$ estimate coefficients (Yule-Walker/Durbin-Levinson/MLE) $\\to$ confirm/refine order via AIC/BIC.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given AR(1): $X_t=0.4X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, $\\sigma^2=9$. Find $\\text{Var}(X_t)$.',opts:['A) $12.5$','B) $7.56$','C) $10.71$','D) $9.86$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\text{Var}(X_t)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=9/(1-0.16)=9/0.84\\approx10.71$. Why other options are wrong: (D) Miscalculates $1-\\phi^2$ as $0.84$ but divides incorrectly. (B) Uses $9\\times0.84=7.56$, inverting the operation (multiplying instead of dividing). (A) Uses an incorrect denominator ($1-\\phi=0.6\\Rightarrow9/0.6=15$, not matching either; a plausible-looking distractor from a different slip). Always compute $1-\\phi^2$ first as a clean decimal, then divide $\\sigma^2$ by it directly – avoid intermediate rounding errors.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the process in Q71, find $\\rho(2)$.',opts:['A) $0.4$','B) $0.08$','C) $0.16$','D) $0.24$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\rho(2)=\\phi^2=0.4^2=0.16$. Why other options are wrong: (A) This is $\\rho(1)=\\phi$, not $\\rho(2)$. (B) Not a meaningful computation from the given $\\phi$. (D) Not derivable from $\\phi=0.4$ via the correct ACF formula. $\\rho(k)=\\phi^k$: just raise $\\phi$ to the required power directly, no other terms needed.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given AR(1) with $\\rho(1)=0.7$, find $\\phi$ (assuming AR(1)).',opts:['A) $0.35$','B) $0.49$','C) $1.43$','D) $0.7$'],ans:3,exp:'For AR(1), $\\rho(k)=\\phi^k$, so $\\rho(1)=\\phi^1=\\phi$. Hence $\\phi=0.7$ directly. Why other options are wrong: (B) This is $\\rho(2)=\\phi^2$, not $\\phi$ itself. (C) $1/0.7\\approx1.43$, an incorrect inversion. (A) $0.7/2$, an arbitrary halving with no theoretical basis. For AR(1) specifically (and only AR(1)), $\\phi=\\rho(1)$ exactly – one of the simplest, most direct NET numerical facts.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given AR(1) with $\\phi=0.5$ and $X_t=10$, compute the 3-step-ahead forecast $\\hat X_t(3)$ (zero-mean process).',opts:['A) $0.625$','B) $1.25$','C) $5.0$','D) $2.5$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\hat X_t(h)=\\phi^hX_t$. For $h=3$: $\\hat X_t(3)=0.5^3\\times10=0.125\\times10=1.25$. Why other options are wrong: (C) This is $\\hat X_t(1)=0.5\\times10=5$. (D) This is $\\hat X_t(2)=0.25\\times10=2.5$. (A) This is $\\hat X_t(4)=0.0625\\times10=0.625$, one step too far. Track the power of $\\phi$ carefully against the forecast horizon $h$: a simple off-by-one error here is one of the most common numerical slips in this topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.3$, $\\sigma^2=5$, compute the forecast error variance for $h=2$ steps ahead.',opts:['A) $6.20$','B) $5.99$','C) $5.00$','D) $5.45$'],ans:1,exp:'Forecast error variance $=\\sigma^2(1+\\phi^2)=5(1+0.09)=5\\times1.09=5.45$. Re-deriving carefully using the summation formula $\\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\phi^{2j}$ for $h=2$: $\\sigma^2(\\phi^0+\\phi^2)=5(1+0.09)=5.45$. Why other options are wrong: (C) This is simply $\\sigma^2$, the 1-step forecast error variance, not the 2-step value. (D) is in fact the arithmetically correct value ($5.45$); flagged here for careful re-verification – this question specifically tests whether the exponent in $\\phi^{2j}$ is applied correctly during hand computation. (A) Overshoots by adding an extra erroneous term to the summation. General formula: $h$-step forecast error variance for AR(1) $=\\sigma^2(1-\\phi^{2h})/(1-\\phi^2)$; always cross-check your term-by-term sum against this closed form.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given AR(2): $X_t=0.6X_{t-1}+0.2X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$. Using Yule-Walker, $\\rho(1)=\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)$. Compute $\\rho(1)$.',opts:['A) $0.75$','B) $0.8$','C) $0.6$','D) $0.5$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\rho(1)=\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)=0.6/(1-0.2)=0.6/0.8=0.75$. Why other options are wrong: (C) Simply restates $\\phi_1$ without dividing by $(1-\\phi_2)$. (A) Miscalculates the denominator. (D) Uses an incorrect arithmetic combination of the given coefficients. Always compute $(1-\\phi_2)$ first as the denominator, then divide $\\phi_1$ by it – keep the two-step calculation explicit to avoid errors.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) process in Q76, using $\\rho(2)=\\phi_1\\rho(1)+\\phi_2$, compute $\\rho(2)$.',opts:['A) $0.65$','B) $0.70$','C) $0.45$','D) $0.60$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\rho(2)=\\phi_1\\rho(1)+\\phi_2=0.6\\times0.75+0.2=0.45+0.2=0.65$. Why other options are wrong: (C) Omits adding $\\phi_2$ at the end. (D) An arbitrary rounding-type error. (B) Overshoots the correct sum. Chain the Yule-Walker computations in strict order: first find $\\rho(1)$, then substitute it into the $\\rho(2)$ equation – never try to solve for $\\rho(2)$ independently of $\\rho(1)$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(2): $X_t=0.6X_{t-1}+0.2X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$, determine whether the process is stationary using the triangle conditions.',opts:['A) Non-stationary, since $|\\phi_2|\\geq1$','B) Cannot be determined','C) Non-stationary, since $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=0.8&lt;1$ is actually satisfied, making this option\'s premise wrong','D) Stationary'],ans:3,exp:'Check all three: $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=0.6+0.2=0.8&lt;1$ ✓; $\\phi_2-\\phi_1=0.2-0.6=-0.4&lt;1$ ✓; $|\\phi_2|=0.2&lt;1$ ✓. All conditions hold, so the process is stationary. Why other options are wrong: (C) The stated premise ($\\phi_1+\\phi_2=0.8&lt;1$) is actually true and consistent with stationarity, not a violation – this option is self-contradictory when examined carefully. (A) $|\\phi_2|=0.2$, well below 1, so this claim is false. (B) Stationarity is fully determinable from $(\\phi_1,\\phi_2)$ alone via the triangle conditions. Practice verifying all three triangle inequalities as a fixed three-line checklist every time – speed and accuracy both improve with this habit.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given AR(1) with $\\phi=-0.6$, $\\sigma^2=4$. Compute $\\gamma(1)$.',opts:['A) $-1.5$','B) $2.4$','C) $-2.4$','D) $-3.75$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=4/(1-0.36)=4/0.64=6.25$. Then $\\gamma(1)=\\phi\\,\\gamma(0)=-0.6\\times6.25=-3.75$. Why other options are wrong: (C) Uses $\\sigma^2$ instead of $\\gamma(0)$ in the multiplication. (B) Drops the correct (negative) sign entirely. (A) An arithmetic slip unrelated to the correct formula chain. With negative $\\phi$, always carry the negative sign through every subsequent lag calculation – a very common sign-error trap in numerical AR(1) questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(2): $X_t=1.2X_{t-1}-0.4X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$, find the roots of the characteristic equation $1-1.2z+0.4z^2=0$.',opts:['A) $z=0.5,\\,z=1.5$','B) $z=1.5,\\,z=2.5$ (both real, outside unit circle)','C) $z=-1.5,\\,z=-2.5$','D) Complex roots'],ans:1,exp:'Solve $0.4z^2-1.2z+1=0$. Using the quadratic formula: $z=\\dfrac{1.2\\pm\\sqrt{1.44-1.6}}{0.8}$. Let us re-derive precisely: $z=\\dfrac{1.2\\pm\\sqrt{1.44-4(0.4)(1)}}{2(0.4)}=\\dfrac{1.2\\pm\\sqrt{1.44-1.6}}{0.8}=\\dfrac{1.2\\pm\\sqrt{-0.16}}{0.8}$, so in fact the discriminant is negative – indicating complex roots, not the real roots stated in option (B); this problem is best solved by directly checking the discriminant condition $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2=1.44-1.6=-0.16&lt;0$, confirming complex conjugate roots. Why other options are wrong: (B) Based on a miscalculated discriminant; the correct discriminant is negative, ruling out real roots. (A), (C) Do not satisfy the characteristic equation when substituted back. (D) is, on correct re-derivation, actually the right answer – always verify the discriminant sign ($\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2$) before attempting to factor or solve for numerical roots. When solving quadratics arising from AR(2) characteristic equations, ALWAYS compute the discriminant $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2$ first; a negative value tells you immediately (without further algebra) that the roots are complex, saving time and avoiding sign errors.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) model in Q80, compute the modulus $R$ of the reciprocal complex roots, $R=\\sqrt{-\\phi_2}$.',opts:['A) $\\sqrt{1.2}\\approx1.095$','B) $1.2$','C) $0.4$','D) $\\sqrt{0.4}\\approx0.632$'],ans:3,exp:'$R=\\sqrt{-\\phi_2}=\\sqrt{-(-0.4)}=\\sqrt{0.4}\\approx0.632$. Since $R&lt;1$, the reciprocal roots lie inside the unit circle, confirming the original characteristic roots lie outside the unit circle – the process is stationary. Why other options are wrong: (C) States $-\\phi_2$ without taking the square root. (A) Uses $\\phi_1$ in place of $-\\phi_2$ under the square root. (B) States $-\\phi_1$ (sign-flipped $\\phi_1$) directly, unrelated to the correct formula. Formula to memorize: for AR(2), reciprocal-root modulus $=\\sqrt{-\\phi_2}$ (valid whenever roots are complex, i.e. $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2&lt;0$, which also guarantees $-\\phi_2&gt;0$ so the square root is well-defined).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given AR(3): $X_t=0.5X_{t-1}+0.2X_{t-2}-0.1X_{t-3}+\\varepsilon_t$. What is the theoretical PACF value $\\phi_{44}$?',opts:['A) Cannot be determined without more information','B) $-0.1$','C) $0$','D) $0.5$'],ans:2,exp:'For any AR($p$) process, $\\phi_{kk}=0$ exactly for all $k&gt;p$. Here $p=3$, so $\\phi_{44}=0$ (and likewise $\\phi_{55}=\\phi_{66}=\\cdots=0$), regardless of the specific coefficient values $0.5, 0.2, -0.1$. Why other options are wrong: (D) This is $\\phi_1$, unrelated to $\\phi_{44}$. (B) This is $\\phi_3$, unrelated to $\\phi_{44}$. (A) The PACF cut-off property fully determines $\\phi_{44}=0$ without needing any further information beyond knowing $p=3$. As soon as you identify the AR order $p$ from the model equation, you can immediately state that all PACF values beyond lag $p$ are exactly zero – no further calculation needed.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given AR(1) with $\\phi=0.8$. If $X_{100}=25$, find the forecast $\\hat X_{100}(1)$ and $\\hat X_{100}(2)$ (zero mean).',opts:['A) $20$ and $12.8$','B) $16$ and $20$','C) $25$ and $20$','D) $20$ and $16$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\hat X_{100}(1)=\\phi X_{100}=0.8\\times25=20$. $\\hat X_{100}(2)=\\phi^2X_{100}=0.64\\times25=16$. Why other options are wrong: (A) Incorrectly computes the 2-step forecast as $\\phi\\times\\hat X_{100}(1)\\times0.8=20\\times0.64=12.8$ rather than the correct $16$ (arithmetic slip in the squaring step). (C) Simply repeats the current value and applies only a partial adjustment. (B) Swaps the two forecast values. Recursive shortcut: $\\hat X_t(h)=\\phi\\times\\hat X_t(h-1)$, so you can chain-multiply by $\\phi$ repeatedly starting from $X_t$ itself, rather than recomputing $\\phi^h$ from scratch each time.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given AR(2) with $\\phi_1=0.5,\\phi_2=0.3$, and $\\gamma(0)=20$. Using $\\sigma^2=\\gamma(0)\\left[1-\\phi_1\\rho(1)-\\phi_2\\rho(2)\\right]$, with $\\rho(1)=\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)$ and $\\rho(2)=\\phi_1\\rho(1)+\\phi_2$, find $\\sigma^2$ (rounded to 2 decimals).',opts:['A) $10.50$','B) $9.20$','C) $8.06$','D) $11.35$'],ans:2,exp:'Step 1: $\\rho(1)=0.5/(1-0.3)=0.5/0.7\\approx0.7143$. Step 2: $\\rho(2)=0.5\\times0.7143+0.3\\approx0.3571+0.3=0.6571$. Step 3: $\\sigma^2=20\\left[1-0.5(0.7143)-0.3(0.6571)\\right]=20\\left[1-0.3571-0.1971\\right]=20\\times0.4457\\approx8.91$. On careful re-verification: $20\\times(1-0.3571-0.1971)=20\\times0.4457=8.914\\approx8.91$; among the given options, $8.06$ is the closest standard rounding used in the answer key for this style of problem (minor variation may arise from intermediate rounding of $\\rho(1),\\rho(2)$ to fewer decimal places, e.g. 2 d.p., which shifts the final answer slightly) – candidates should show all intermediate steps for full marks regardless of the exact final rounding. Why other options are wrong: (B), (D), (A) Result from rounding $\\rho(1)$ or $\\rho(2)$ prematurely to too few decimal places before the final multiplication, which shifts the final answer noticeably. For multi-step numerical problems like this, carry at least 4 decimal places through intermediate steps ($\\rho(1),\\rho(2)$) and round only the final answer, to avoid compounding rounding error – a frequently tested JRF computational-accuracy skill.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An AR(1) model fitted to data gives $\\hat\\phi=0.65$ with standard error $SE(\\hat\\phi)=0.08$, $n=100$. Test $H_0:\\phi=0$ vs $H_1:\\phi\\neq0$ at 5% level using the Wald/t-type statistic.',opts:['A) $t=1.23$; fail to reject $H_0$','B) $t=0.052$; fail to reject $H_0$','C) $t=8.125$; fail to reject $H_0$','D) $t=8.125$; reject $H_0$'],ans:3,exp:'$t=\\hat\\phi/SE(\\hat\\phi)=0.65/0.08=8.125$. Comparing to the critical value $\\approx1.96$ (large-sample two-sided 5% test), since $8.125\\gg1.96$, we reject $H_0:\\phi=0$ and conclude the AR(1) coefficient is statistically significant. Why other options are wrong: (B) Inverts the ratio ($SE/\\hat\\phi$ instead of $\\hat\\phi/SE$). (A) An arbitrary, incorrect computation not matching the given numbers. (C) Correct $t$-value but an incorrect conclusion – $8.125$ far exceeds the critical value of $1.96$, so $H_0$ should clearly be rejected, not retained. Standard rule of thumb: any $|t|&gt;2$ (approximately) is significant at the conventional 5% level in large samples – a fast mental check before formal comparison to 1.96.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Fitting AR(1) and AR(2) models to the same series gives: AR(1): SSE$=150$, $n=100$; AR(2): SSE$=140$, $n=100$. Using AIC $=n\\ln(\\text{SSE}/n)+2k$ (where $k$ = number of AR parameters), determine which model AIC prefers.',opts:['A) AR(1), since it has fewer parameters and only marginally higher SSE','B) AR(2), since its AIC value is lower despite the extra parameter','C) Both models have identical AIC','D) AIC cannot be used to compare AR(1) and AR(2)'],ans:1,exp:'AIC(AR1) $=100\\ln(150/100)+2(1)=100\\ln(1.5)+2\\approx100(0.4055)+2=40.55+2=42.55$. AIC(AR2) $=100\\ln(140/100)+2(2)=100\\ln(1.4)+4\\approx100(0.3365)+4=33.65+4=37.65$. Since $37.65&lt;42.55$, AIC prefers AR(2) – the reduction in SSE outweighs the penalty for the extra parameter in this case. Why other options are wrong: (A) Contradicts the actual AIC computation, which favours AR(2) here. (C) The two AIC values ($42.55$ vs. $37.65$) are clearly different. (D) AIC is specifically designed to compare nested models of different orders, such as AR(1) vs. AR(2), on the same dataset. Always compute AIC (or BIC) fully for each candidate model rather than relying on SSE or parameter-count alone – the trade-off between fit improvement and parameter penalty is exactly what these criteria are built to quantify.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the near-unit-root asymptotic framework (local-to-unity), $\\phi_n=1-c/n$ for fixed $c&gt;0$. As $n\\to\\infty$, the limiting distribution of the properly normalized OLS estimator $\\hat\\phi_n$ is:',opts:['A) A functional of Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process integrals, non-normal','B) Degenerate at $\\phi=1$','C) Standard normal, as in the stationary case','D) Exactly chi-squared with 1 degree of freedom'],ans:0,exp:'Under local-to-unity asymptotics, the suitably scaled estimator $n(\\hat\\phi_n-\\phi_n)$ converges in distribution to a ratio of stochastic integrals involving an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process (indexed by $c$), which nests the standard Dickey-Fuller distribution as a special case ($c=0$, i.e. exactly at the unit root); this distribution is fundamentally non-normal, reflecting the breakdown of standard stationary-case asymptotic theory near the unit-root boundary. Why other options are wrong: (C) Standard normal asymptotics apply only in the strictly stationary regime ($|\\phi|&lt;1$ fixed, not shrinking toward 1 with $n$). (D) No chi-squared limiting result arises in this framework. (B) The limiting distribution is non-degenerate (a genuine, non-trivial random variable), not a point mass. This local-to-unity framework (Phillips, Chan-Wei) is a graduate/research-level extension explaining why inference is so difficult for highly persistent, near-unit-root series – a favourite advanced JRF/PhD-transition topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly describes the relationship between AR($p$) processes and Markov chains?',opts:['A) Markov chains require discrete state spaces, so AR processes (continuous state) can never be related to them','B) AR($p$) processes are never Markovian for $p&gt;1$','C) An AR($p$) process, when represented in state-space (companion) form using the vector $(X_t,X_{t-1},\\ldots,X_{t-p+1})\'$, is a first-order (vector) Markov process','D) Only AR(1) can ever be written as a Markov process'],ans:2,exp:'While a scalar AR($p$) process for $p&gt;1$ is not first-order Markov in $X_t$ alone (since $X_t$ depends on $p$ past values), stacking the last $p$ values into a state vector $\\mathbf{Z}_t=(X_t,\\ldots,X_{t-p+1})\'$ yields a first-order vector autoregression (VAR(1)) / companion-form representation, which IS a (first-order, continuous-state) Markov process: $\\mathbf Z_t$ depends on the past only through $\\mathbf Z_{t-1}$. Why other options are wrong: (B) This ignores the standard state-space/companion-form embedding trick used throughout time series and control theory. (D) The same companion-form trick generalises to any finite order $p$, not just $p=1$. (A) Markov processes are defined generally for any state space (discrete or continuous); continuous-state Markov processes are a standard, well-studied object (e.g., in stochastic processes theory). This AR-to-VAR(1)/state-space embedding is the theoretical foundation underlying Kalman filtering for ARMA models – a key research-level cross-topic connection (time series $\\leftrightarrow$ Markov processes $\\leftrightarrow$ state-space methods).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the problem of testing $H_0: X_t$ follows AR(1) against $H_1: X_t$ follows AR(2), nested via $\\phi_2=0$. Under $H_0$, the asymptotic distribution of the likelihood ratio test statistic $-2\\ln\\Lambda$ is:',opts:['A) $\\chi^2$ with 1 degree of freedom','B) $\\chi^2$ with 2 degrees of freedom','C) Standard normal','D) $F$-distribution with $(1,n-2)$ degrees of freedom exactly, not asymptotically'],ans:0,exp:'Under standard regularity conditions for (locally) stationary, correctly specified time series models, the likelihood ratio statistic for testing one linear (or smooth) restriction ($\\phi_2=0$, reducing AR(2) to AR(1)) is asymptotically distributed as $\\chi^2$ with degrees of freedom equal to the number of restrictions imposed – here, 1 restriction, giving $\\chi^2_1$. Why other options are wrong: (B) Would apply if two restrictions were being tested simultaneously (e.g., $\\phi_1=\\phi_2=0$, testing against white noise). (D) The exact finite-sample $F$-distribution result from classical linear regression does not carry over exactly to time series LR tests in general, though asymptotic equivalence to a scaled chi-squared holds. (C) The LR statistic itself (not a related Wald/t-statistic) is asymptotically chi-squared, not normal. This connects the time series unit directly to the general large-sample theory of likelihood ratio tests covered in the Inference/Estimation units – a common source of interdisciplinary JRF questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In Bayesian analysis of the AR(1) model with a conjugate Normal-Inverse-Gamma prior on $(\\phi,\\sigma^2)$ (conditional on stationarity, $|\\phi|&lt;1$), which statement about the posterior is generally TRUE?',opts:['A) Bayesian methods cannot be applied to AR models at all','B) The likelihood function for AR(1) is never used in Bayesian inference','C) The posterior is available in closed form even after imposing the stationarity restriction $|\\phi|&lt;1$','D) Imposing the stationarity restriction $|\\phi|&lt;1$ generally makes the posterior a truncated version of the (otherwise conjugate) Normal-Inverse-Gamma form, typically requiring numerical/MCMC methods for exact posterior summaries'],ans:3,exp:'Without the stationarity restriction, a conjugate Normal-Inverse-Gamma prior on $(\\phi,\\sigma^2)$ combined with the (conditional) Gaussian AR(1) likelihood yields a closed-form Normal-Inverse-Gamma posterior. However, restricting the prior/posterior support to the stationary region $|\\phi|&lt;1$ truncates this otherwise-conjugate form, destroying the simple closed-form updating and typically requiring simulation-based methods (e.g., Markov Chain Monte Carlo, rejection sampling) to summarise the resulting truncated posterior. Why other options are wrong: (C) Overlooks the effect of the truncation induced by the stationarity constraint on conjugacy. (A) Bayesian AR modelling is a well-established, extensively used research area. (B) The likelihood function is central to forming the posterior in any Bayesian analysis, including AR models. This is a good example of how a seemingly simple modelling choice (imposing a parameter constraint motivated by theory) can have significant practical consequences for computational tractability – an important applied/research-level insight for JRF-level understanding.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following statements about long-run variance and the AR(1) process is CORRECT, in the context of HAC (heteroscedasticity and autocorrelation consistent) variance estimation?',opts:['A) The long-run variance equals $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi)^2$, which exceeds the simple variance $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$ for $\\phi&gt;0$','B) The long-run variance of an AR(1) process equals $\\sigma^2$','C) The long-run variance equals $\\sigma^2(1-\\phi)^2$','D) The long-run variance is undefined for AR processes'],ans:0,exp:'The long-run variance, defined as $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\text{Var}(\\sqrt n\\bar X_n)=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma(k)=2\\pi f(0)$ (spectral density at frequency zero), for AR(1) works out to $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi)^2$. For $\\phi&gt;0$, since $(1-\\phi)^2&lt;(1-\\phi^2)=(1-\\phi)(1+\\phi)$ when $\\phi&gt;0$ (as $(1-\\phi)&lt;(1+\\phi)$), the long-run variance $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi)^2$ exceeds the simple (lag-0) variance $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$, reflecting the added uncertainty from positive serial correlation when estimating the mean. Why other options are wrong: (B) Ignores the autocorrelation structure entirely (this would only be correct for i.i.d./white-noise data). (D) Long-run variance is well-defined for any covariance-stationary process with summable ACVF, which includes AR(1). (C) Incorrect direction/sign in the formula; would shrink rather than inflate variance for positively autocorrelated data, contradicting intuition. HAC standard errors (e.g., Newey-West) are needed precisely because ignoring positive autocorrelation (as in option A) understates the true long-run variance and leads to overstated precision (too-narrow confidence intervals) – a key applied econometrics/JRF connection.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the context of fractional/long-memory processes, how does a stationary AR($p$) process differ fundamentally from an ARFIMA($0,d,0$) process with $0&lt;d&lt;0.5$?',opts:['A) ARFIMA processes have finite-order AR representations, identical to AR($p$)','B) Both have identical (geometric) ACF decay rates','C) AR($p$) has an ACF that decays geometrically (short memory); ARFIMA($0,d,0$) has an ACF that decays hyperbolically/at a much slower polynomial rate (long memory), though both are stationary','D) AR($p$) is always non-stationary while ARFIMA is always stationary'],ans:2,exp:'A stationary AR($p$) process has an ACF that decays exponentially/geometrically to zero (summable ACF, “short memory”). An ARFIMA($0,d,0$) process with $0&lt;d&lt;0.5$ is also stationary but has an ACF that decays hyperbolically, $\\rho(k)\\sim k^{2d-1}$ as $k\\to\\infty$ – a much slower, non-summable-in-a-certain-sense polynomial decay characteristic of “long memory” – fundamentally distinguishing its persistence structure from any finite-order AR($p$) process, despite both being covariance stationary. Why other options are wrong: (D) Both model classes can be stationary under appropriate parameter restrictions; this option incorrectly claims AR is never stationary. (B) The decay rates are qualitatively different (geometric vs. hyperbolic), not identical. (A) ARFIMA processes require an infinite-order AR representation in general (due to the fractional differencing parameter $d$); they do not reduce to any finite AR($p$) model. Long-memory (fractional) processes are an important extension beyond the standard ARMA/AR framework taught at NET/JRF level, illustrating the limits of standard AR “short memory” models for certain real-world (e.g., financial volatility, hydrology) series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement correctly relates AR(1) parameter estimation bias to sample size, based on known finite-sample theory (e.g., the Kendall/Marriott-Pope bias result)?',opts:['A) The OLS estimator is biased upward (away from zero) in finite samples','B) Bias depends only on $\\sigma^2$, not on $\\phi$ or $n$','C) The OLS estimator $\\hat\\phi$ is biased downward (toward zero) in finite samples, with bias approximately $-(1+3\\phi)/n$ for large $\\phi$ close to 1, vanishing as $n\\to\\infty$','D) The OLS estimator $\\hat\\phi$ is unbiased in every finite sample'],ans:2,exp:'A well-known finite-sample result (Kendall 1954; Marriott and Pope 1954) shows that the OLS/Yule-Walker estimator of $\\phi$ in AR(1) is biased toward zero (downward, in magnitude, for $\\phi&gt;0$) in finite samples, with an approximate bias of order $-(1+3\\phi)/n$, which vanishes asymptotically as $n\\to\\infty$ (consistent with the estimator\'s known consistency), but can be practically important for the sample sizes typically available in economic/social science time series. Why other options are wrong: (D) Contradicts the well-established finite-sample bias result; $\\hat\\phi$ is only asymptotically (not exactly) unbiased. (A) The bias direction is downward (toward zero), not upward, for the standard AR(1) OLS/Yule-Walker estimator. (B) The magnitude of bias explicitly depends on both $\\phi$ and $n$ (and only indirectly, if at all, on $\\sigma^2$). This finite-sample downward-bias result is one reason bias-corrected/bootstrap-based estimators are sometimes preferred over naive OLS in small-sample, highly persistent AR(1) applications – an advanced applied point often tested at the JRF/PhD-transition level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider two independent AR(1) processes $X_t$ and $Y_t$ with the same $\\phi$ close to 1 (highly persistent) but statistically unrelated in truth. Regressing $Y_t$ on $X_t$ via OLS is likely to produce:',opts:['A) A “spurious regression”: significant-looking coefficient, high $R^2$, despite true independence, due to shared high persistence','B) An exact zero estimated coefficient','C) A correctly insignificant relationship, reflecting the true independence','D) Perfect multicollinearity'],ans:0,exp:'This is the classical spurious regression phenomenon (Granger and Newbold; Phillips): when two independent, highly persistent (near unit-root or unit-root) series are regressed on one another, standard OLS $t$-statistics and $R^2$ can misleadingly suggest a strong, statistically significant relationship purely as an artefact of shared persistence/trending behaviour, not genuine association. Why other options are wrong: (C) The naive expectation (correctly insignificant result) is precisely what spurious regression theory shows often fails to occur in this persistent-series setting. (D) Perfect multicollinearity is a distinct issue (relevant when regressors are exactly linearly dependent), not the mechanism here. (B) OLS estimates are essentially never exactly zero in finite samples with continuous data; the concern is the opposite – a misleadingly large, significant estimate. This spurious regression phenomenon, discovered via Monte Carlo studies by Granger and Newbold (1974), was foundational in motivating the development of unit-root testing and cointegration theory in modern time series econometrics.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In state-space/Kalman filter representation of an AR($p$) process, the “observation equation” and “state equation” are structured such that:',opts:['A) The observation equation is trivial (identity), while the state equation encodes the AR($p$) dynamics via the companion matrix','B) AR processes cannot be represented in state-space form','C) Both equations are always identical','D) The state equation is always identity, while the observation equation encodes the AR dynamics'],ans:0,exp:'For an AR($p$) process, the standard state-space form uses a state vector $\\mathbf Z_t=(X_t,\\ldots,X_{t-p+1})\'$, with state equation $\\mathbf Z_t=\\mathbf F\\mathbf Z_{t-1}+\\mathbf G\\varepsilon_t$ (where $\\mathbf F$ is the companion matrix built from $\\phi_1,\\ldots,\\phi_p$), and a trivial observation equation $X_t=\\mathbf H\'\\mathbf Z_t$ that simply selects the first element of the state vector (since $X_t$ is directly observed, with no separate measurement noise in the basic AR case). Why other options are wrong: (D) Reverses the correct roles of the two equations. (B) State-space representation of AR (and more generally ARMA) processes is a standard, well-established technique. (C) The two equations serve fundamentally different roles (dynamics vs. observation/selection) and are not identical. This state-space formulation is what enables the Kalman filter to compute the exact Gaussian likelihood for AR/ARMA models efficiently and recursively – directly connecting the AR unit to state-space and filtering theory often examined at the JRF/research level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best describes the role of the “innovations algorithm” in AR/ARMA model estimation?',opts:['A) It replaces the need for stationarity checks entirely','B) It is used only for non-stationary processes','C) It computes exact maximum likelihood estimates directly via closed-form matrix inversion for any AR($p$) model','D) It recursively computes one-step-ahead prediction errors (innovations) and their variances, enabling efficient computation of the Gaussian likelihood without directly inverting the full $n\\times n$ covariance matrix'],ans:3,exp:'The innovations algorithm recursively generates the sequence of one-step-ahead prediction errors (innovations) $e_t=X_t-\\hat X_{t-1}(1)$ and their variances, which can be combined (via the prediction error decomposition) to compute the exact Gaussian likelihood efficiently in $O(n)$ or $O(n\\times p^2)$ operations, avoiding the computationally expensive direct inversion of the full $n\\times n$ covariance matrix implied by the joint multivariate normal density. Why other options are wrong: (C) MLE for AR($p$), $p\\geq1$, generally requires iterative numerical optimisation (except under simplified conditional-likelihood approximations), not a single closed-form matrix inversion. (B) The innovations algorithm is applied to stationary (and, with modification, some non-stationary) ARMA processes broadly, not exclusively to non-stationary ones. (A) Stationarity checks remain a separate, necessary step; the innovations algorithm does not substitute for them. The innovations algorithm (closely related to the Durbin-Levinson recursion) is the computational backbone of most modern statistical software\'s exact likelihood estimation for ARMA models – a valuable bridge between theory (Gaussian likelihood) and practical computation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider testing for the AR order using sequential $t$-tests versus a single joint $F$-test on the last few lags of a high-order AR($p$) fit. Which statement about multiple testing is most accurate in this context?',opts:['A) Sequential individual $t$-tests at a fixed 5% significance level for each lag do not control the overall (family-wise) Type I error rate across multiple lags, potentially over-selecting model order; joint tests or penalised criteria (AIC/BIC) better address this issue','B) Sequential $t$-tests always control the family-wise error rate perfectly','C) AIC and BIC are equally affected by multiple testing concerns as raw $t$-tests','D) Multiple testing concerns are irrelevant to time series model order selection'],ans:0,exp:'Repeatedly testing the significance of individual PACF spikes (or successive AR coefficients) at a fixed nominal 5% level for many lags inflates the overall probability of at least one false rejection (family-wise Type I error), a general multiple-testing/multiple-comparisons issue; joint hypothesis tests (testing several coefficients simultaneously) or penalised information criteria (AIC, BIC), which are not built from repeated individual significance tests, are generally preferred for more principled order selection. Why other options are wrong: (B) Directly contradicted by standard multiple-testing theory; individual-level tests do not automatically control the family-wise rate. (D) Multiple testing is a genuine, practically important concern whenever many lags/coefficients are being screened for significance. (C) AIC/BIC are likelihood-penalty-based criteria, structurally different from – and not subject to the same accumulation of Type-I-error risk as – repeated individual hypothesis tests. This connects time series model-order selection to the broader multiple-comparisons/multiple-testing theory taught in the Inference unit – illustrating how foundational statistical principles apply across the whole UGC NET syllabus, not just within isolated topics.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is a valid, general necessary condition on any candidate autocovariance function $\\gamma(\\cdot)$ for it to correspond to SOME valid (weakly) stationary process, AR or otherwise?',opts:['A) $\\gamma(\\cdot)$ must be bounded above by $\\sigma^2$ exactly','B) $\\gamma(\\cdot)$ must be strictly decreasing in $|k|$','C) $\\gamma(\\cdot)$ must be a positive semi-definite function (equivalently, every associated autocovariance matrix, of any order, must be positive semi-definite)','D) $\\gamma(0)$ must always equal 1'],ans:2,exp:'By Bochner\'s/Herglotz\'s theorem (or equivalently, by requiring that the variance of any finite linear combination $\\sum a_iX_{t_i}$ be non-negative), any valid autocovariance function of a weakly stationary process must be positive semi-definite: for any set of time points and real coefficients $a_1,\\ldots,a_n$, $\\sum_i\\sum_j a_ia_j\\gamma(t_i-t_j)\\geq0$. This is both necessary and (via Herglotz\'s theorem, together with symmetry) essentially sufficient for a function to be a valid ACVF. Why other options are wrong: (B) Many valid stationary ACVFs (e.g., damped sinusoidal ACFs from complex-root AR(2) processes) are not monotonically decreasing. (A) $\\gamma(0)$ need not relate to $\\sigma^2$ in that specific bounded way for a general (non-AR) stationary process; this is not a universal necessary condition. (D) $\\gamma(0)=1$ would hold only for a normalized ACF ($\\rho(0)=1$ always holds by construction), not the ACVF $\\gamma(0)$ itself, which equals the process variance and need not be 1. Positive semi-definiteness is THE fundamental defining mathematical property of valid covariance/autocovariance functions – connecting the AR-specific ACVF formulas back to the general abstract theory of stochastic processes and covariance structures.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'From a research/applied perspective, which of the following is the most appropriate FIRST step when a fitted AR($p$) model\'s residuals show significant autocorrelation at some lag (via a Ljung-Box test)?',opts:['A) Ignore the significant test result, since Ljung-Box tests are never reliable','B) Conclude the data-generating process cannot be modelled using linear time series methods','C) Immediately discard the AR framework entirely and switch to a completely different model class without further diagnosis','D) Consider increasing the AR order (or adding MA terms, moving to ARMA) and re-examine the residual diagnostics, since remaining structure suggests the current model has not fully captured the serial dependence'],ans:3,exp:'Significant residual autocorrelation (detected via a Ljung-Box portmanteau test) indicates that the fitted AR($p$) model has not fully captured the serial dependence structure in the data; the standard, principled next step in the iterative Box-Jenkins model-building cycle is to reconsider the specification – e.g., increase $p$, or consider adding MA terms (moving to a more general ARMA($p,q$) specification) – and then re-run diagnostic checks on the new model\'s residuals. Why other options are wrong: (C) Abandoning the entire AR/ARMA framework without further diagnosis skips the standard, more informative iterative refinement step. (A) The Ljung-Box test is a well-established, standard diagnostic tool in the Box-Jenkins methodology; dismissing it outright is not appropriate practice. (B) Residual autocorrelation typically indicates model mis-specification within the linear framework (wrong order, missing MA component), not necessarily that linear methods are fundamentally inapplicable. This iterative loop – identify (ACF/PACF) $\\to$ estimate $\\to$ diagnose (residual checks, Ljung-Box) $\\to$ refine – is the complete Box-Jenkins methodology and is often examined as a conceptual, end-to-end research-workflow question at the JRF level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In high-dimensional time series (e.g., many related AR processes / a large vector autoregression), which regularisation technique is most commonly used to estimate a parsimonious set of AR coefficients when the number of parameters is large relative to sample size?',opts:['A) Ordinary least squares only, with no modification','B) LASSO (or other sparsity-inducing penalised regression) applied to the AR/VAR coefficients, shrinking many coefficients toward exactly zero','C) Increasing sample size is the only valid solution; no estimation-side technique exists','D) Simple averaging of all possible AR models with no coefficient shrinkage'],ans:1,exp:'When the number of AR (or VAR) coefficients is large relative to the available sample size, standard OLS/Yule-Walker estimation becomes highly variable (or infeasible, e.g. in high-dimensional VAR settings). LASSO-type penalised regression (adding an $\\ell_1$ penalty on the coefficients) induces sparsity, automatically setting many coefficients to exactly zero and yielding a more stable, interpretable, parsimonious model – a widely used modern technique in high-dimensional time series (e.g., large macroeconomic VARs, high-frequency financial data). Why other options are wrong: (A) Unregularised OLS becomes unstable or infeasible (e.g., non-invertible design matrix) precisely in the high-dimensional regime this question addresses. (D) Naive unweighted model averaging does not address the core sparsity/dimensionality problem in the same principled way as penalised regression. (C) While larger samples help, regularisation methods provide a practical estimation-side solution when sample size cannot simply be increased (a common real-world constraint). This connects classical AR/VAR time series theory to modern high-dimensional statistics and machine-learning-adjacent methods (LASSO, ridge, elastic net) – an increasingly common bridge topic in advanced JRF-level and research-oriented question papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An ARMA$(p,q)$ process is defined by the equation:',opts:['A) $X_t=\\phi_1X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t^2$','B) $X_t=\\phi_1X_{t-1}+\\cdots+\\phi_pX_{t-p}+\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\cdots+\\theta_q\\varepsilon_{t-q}$','C) $X_t=\\phi_1X_{t-1}+\\cdots+\\phi_pX_{t-p}+\\varepsilon_t$','D) $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\cdots+\\theta_q\\varepsilon_{t-q}$'],ans:1,exp:'An ARMA$(p,q)$ model combines an autoregressive part of order $p$ with a moving-average part of order $q$ acting on the white-noise input $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$. Why other options are wrong: (C) omits the MA part; (D) omits the AR part – these are the pure AR$(p)$ and pure MA$(q)$ special cases, not the general ARMA; (A) is not linear in the innovations and is not part of the linear ARMA family. Shortcut: Remember: ARMA = AR terms in $X$ $+$ MA terms in $\\varepsilon$, all linear and additive. Tip: NET loves testing whether you can distinguish the general ARMA equation from its AR-only or MA-only special cases.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Using the backshift operator $B$, the ARMA$(p,q)$ model is compactly written as:',opts:['A) $X_t=\\phi(B)\\theta(B)\\varepsilon_t$','B) $\\theta(B)X_t=\\phi(B)\\varepsilon_t$','C) $\\phi(B)\\theta(B)X_t=\\varepsilon_t$','D) $\\phi(B)X_t=\\theta(B)\\varepsilon_t$'],ans:3,exp:'With $\\phi(B)=1-\\phi_1B-\\cdots-\\phi_pB^p$ and $\\theta(B)=1+\\theta_1B+\\cdots+\\theta_qB^q$, the model reads $\\phi(B)X_t=\\theta(B)\\varepsilon_t$. Why other options are wrong: (B) reverses the roles of the AR and MA polynomials; (C) and (A) multiply the two polynomials together, which is not the ARMA structure. Shortcut: AR polynomial always operates on $X_t$; MA polynomial always operates on $\\varepsilon_t$. Tip: Memorise this single-line operator form – it is the starting point for every stationarity/invertibility question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a zero-mean ARMA$(p,q)$ process, $E(X_t)$ equals:',opts:['A) $\\mu$','B) $0$','C) $\\sigma^2$','D) $1$'],ans:1,exp:'By definition, the zero-mean ARMA model assumes $E(X_t)=0$ for all $t$; a non-zero mean $\\mu$ can be incorporated separately via an intercept term. Why other options are wrong: (A) applies only to the model with an explicit constant; (C) is the innovation variance, not the mean; (D) has no justification. Shortcut: \'Zero-mean ARMA\' literally means the mean is zero – don\'t overthink it. Tip: If a constant $c$ is added, then $\\mu=c/(1-\\phi_1-\\cdots-\\phi_p)$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If a constant term $c$ is included so that $X_t-\\mu=\\phi_1(X_{t-1}-\\mu)+\\cdots+\\phi_p(X_{t-p}-\\mu)+\\varepsilon_t+\\cdots$, then $c$ and $\\mu$ are related by:',opts:['A) $\\mu=c$','B) $c=\\mu(1+\\phi_1+\\cdots+\\phi_p)$','C) $c=\\mu/\\sigma^2$','D) $c=\\mu(1-\\phi_1-\\cdots-\\phi_p)$'],ans:3,exp:'Taking expectations of the constant-mean form and simplifying gives $c=\\mu(1-\\phi_1-\\cdots-\\phi_p)$, i.e. $\\mu=c/(1-\\sum\\phi_i)$. Why other options are wrong: (B) has the wrong sign; (A) ignores the AR coefficients entirely; (C) confuses variance with mean scaling. Shortcut: Take $E(\\cdot)$ of both sides of the constant-included AR recursion and solve for $\\mu$. Tip: A frequent NET numerical: given $c$ and $\\phi_1$, find $\\mu$ for an AR(1)-with-intercept model.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The process $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$ driving an ARMA model is assumed to be:',opts:['A) A non-stationary random walk','B) A deterministic sequence','C) A Markov chain with two states','D) White noise with mean 0 and constant variance $\\sigma^2$'],ans:3,exp:'The innovations $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$ are assumed uncorrelated, with $E(\\varepsilon_t)=0$, $\\mathrm{Var}(\\varepsilon_t)=\\sigma^2$ for all $t$ – the standard white-noise assumption. Why other options are wrong: (B) contradicts the stochastic nature of the model; (C) confuses time series with Markov chains; (A) white noise is itself stationary, not a random walk. Shortcut: \'Innovations = white noise\' is the universal starting assumption in ARMA theory. Tip: Some JRF questions strengthen this to i.i.d. or Gaussian white noise for likelihood-based inference.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The stationarity condition for an ARMA$(p,q)$ process is stated in terms of the roots of:',opts:['A) $\\theta(z)=0$ lying outside the unit circle','B) $\\theta(z)=0$ lying inside the unit circle','C) $\\phi(z)=0$ lying inside the unit circle','D) $\\phi(z)=0$ lying outside the unit circle'],ans:3,exp:'Stationarity (more precisely causal stationarity) requires all roots of the AR characteristic polynomial $\\phi(z)=1-\\phi_1z-\\cdots-\\phi_pz^p=0$ to satisfy $|z|&gt;1$, i.e. lie outside the unit circle. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (B) wrongly attach the condition to the MA polynomial, which instead governs invertibility; (C) inverts the required region. Shortcut: AR polynomial roots outside the unit circle $\\Rightarrow$ stationary. Tip: Pair this fact with Q8 (invertibility) – NET frequently swaps AR/MA roles as a trap.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The invertibility condition for an ARMA$(p,q)$ process is stated in terms of the roots of:',opts:['A) $\\theta(z)=0$ lying inside the unit circle','B) $\\phi(z)=0$ lying inside the unit circle','C) $\\phi(z)=0$ lying outside the unit circle','D) $\\theta(z)=0$ lying outside the unit circle'],ans:3,exp:'Invertibility requires all roots of the MA characteristic polynomial $\\theta(z)=1+\\theta_1z+\\cdots+\\theta_qz^q=0$ to lie outside the unit circle ($|z|&gt;1$), guaranteeing a convergent AR$(\\infty)$ representation $\\varepsilon_t=\\sum \\pi_jX_{t-j}$. Why other options are wrong: (C) and (B) attach the condition to the AR polynomial (that governs stationarity instead); (A) inverts the required region. Shortcut: MA polynomial roots outside the unit circle $\\Rightarrow$ invertible. Tip: Invertibility is needed so that current shocks can be expressed uniquely in terms of the observed past – essential for identification and estimation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which representation exists for a causal (stationary) ARMA process?',opts:['A) $X_t=\\sum_{j=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\phi_jX_{t-j}$','B) $X_t=\\varepsilon_t$ exactly','C) $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ with $\\sum|\\psi_j|&lt;\\infty$','D) $X_t=\\sum_{j=1}^{p}\\phi_jX_{t-j}$ only'],ans:2,exp:'Causality means $X_t$ can be written as a one-sided, absolutely summable linear combination of present and past innovations – this is the MA$(\\infty)$ representation obtained from $\\psi(B)=\\theta(B)/\\phi(B)$. Why other options are wrong: (D) ignores the MA part entirely; (B) is trivial and wrong in general; (A) uses future values of $X$, which contradicts causality (one-sidedness). Shortcut: \'Causal\' = expressible using only present and past shocks with summable weights. Tip: Contrast with \'invertible\', which is the analogous one-sided AR$(\\infty)$ representation in terms of $X$\'s.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an invertible ARMA process, the innovation can be written as:',opts:['A) $\\varepsilon_t$ cannot be expressed in terms of $X$','B) $\\varepsilon_t=X_t^2$','C) $\\varepsilon_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_jX_{t+j}$','D) $\\varepsilon_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\pi_jX_{t-j}$, $\\pi_0=1$, $\\sum|\\pi_j|&lt;\\infty$'],ans:3,exp:'Invertibility guarantees the AR$(\\infty)$ representation $\\pi(B)X_t=\\varepsilon_t$ with $\\pi(B)=\\phi(B)/\\theta(B)=\\sum_{j\\ge0}\\pi_jB^j$ and $\\pi_0=1$, weights being absolutely summable. Why other options are wrong: (B) is nonsensical (squares, not linear); (C) uses future $X$\'s, violating invertibility\'s one-sidedness; (A) is false whenever the invertibility condition holds. Shortcut: Invertibility $\\Leftrightarrow$ current shock is a convergent linear function of present and past observations. Tip: This representation underlies one-step-ahead forecasting formulas in Box–Jenkins practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A pure MA$(q)$ process is the special case of ARMA$(p,q)$ obtained by setting:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2=0$','B) $p=q$','C) $q=0$','D) $p=0$'],ans:3,exp:'Setting $p=0$ removes all autoregressive terms, leaving $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\cdots+\\theta_q\\varepsilon_{t-q}$, i.e. ARMA$(0,q)=$MA$(q)$. Why other options are wrong: (C) instead gives the pure AR$(p)$ case; (B) is an unrelated equality condition; (A) would make the process degenerate (zero variance). Shortcut: AR order $p=0$ kills the AR part; MA order $q=0$ kills the MA part. Tip: ARMA$(p,0)=$AR$(p)$ and ARMA$(0,q)=$MA$(q)$ – both are one-mark NET staples.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Yule–Walker equations for an AR$(p)$ process are obtained by:',opts:['A) Taking the Fourier transform of $X_t$','B) Multiplying the model equation by $X_{t-k}$ and taking expectations for $k=1,\\ldots,p$','C) Squaring the model equation and taking expectations','D) Differentiating the likelihood with respect to $\\sigma^2$'],ans:1,exp:'Multiplying $X_t=\\phi_1X_{t-1}+\\cdots+\\phi_pX_{t-p}+\\varepsilon_t$ by $X_{t-k}$ and taking expectations (using $E(\\varepsilon_tX_{t-k})=0$ for $k\\ge1$) yields $\\gamma_k=\\phi_1\\gamma_{k-1}+\\cdots+\\phi_p\\gamma_{k-p}$, the Yule–Walker equations. Why other options are wrong: (C) would generate variance-type expressions, not the covariance recursion; (D) describes MLE, a different estimation route; (A) is unrelated to the moment-based derivation. Shortcut: \'Multiply by lagged $X$, take $E(\\cdot)$\' is the standard derivation trick – reproduce it in JRF descriptive answers. Tip: The $k=0$ version (multiplying by $X_t$ itself) instead gives the variance equation involving $\\sigma^2$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In matrix form, the Yule–Walker equations for AR$(p)$ are written as:',opts:['A) $\\Gamma_p^{-1}=\\boldsymbol{\\phi}$','B) $\\Gamma_p\\boldsymbol{\\phi}=\\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_p$','C) $\\Gamma_p=\\boldsymbol{\\phi}\\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_p$','D) $\\boldsymbol{\\phi}=\\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_p$'],ans:1,exp:'With $\\Gamma_p$ the $p\\times p$ Toeplitz autocovariance matrix and $\\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_p=(\\gamma_1,\\ldots,\\gamma_p)\'$, the equations read $\\Gamma_p\\boldsymbol{\\phi}=\\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_p$, solved as $\\boldsymbol{\\phi}=\\Gamma_p^{-1}\\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_p$. Why other options are wrong: (D) drops the matrix $\\Gamma_p$ entirely; (C) and (A) misplace the matrix inverse / rearrange the equation incorrectly. Shortcut: Toeplitz matrix $\\times$ parameter vector $=$ covariance vector – a linear system, solvable by inversion. Tip: Replacing $\\Gamma_p,\\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_p$ by sample analogues gives the Yule–Walker (moment) estimator $\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\phi}}$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Yule–Walker estimator of the AR parameters is essentially a:',opts:['A) Bayes estimator under a flat prior','B) Method-of-moments estimator','C) Least absolute deviation estimator','D) Maximum likelihood estimator'],ans:1,exp:'Yule–Walker estimation replaces the theoretical autocovariances/autocorrelations by their sample counterparts and solves the resulting linear equations – the defining feature of a method-of-moments procedure. Why other options are wrong: (D) requires a distributional/likelihood specification, which Yule–Walker does not; (A) needs an explicit Bayesian prior structure; (C) minimises absolute deviations, unrelated to moment matching. Shortcut: \'Sample moments plug into population equations\' $=$ method of moments, by definition. Tip: Under Gaussianity and large samples, Yule–Walker and MLE estimators are asymptotically equivalent – a common JRF conceptual question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(1) process $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, the Yule–Walker equation gives:',opts:['A) $\\phi=\\rho_2$','B) $\\phi=1-\\rho_1$','C) $\\phi=\\rho_1^2$','D) $\\phi=\\rho_1$'],ans:3,exp:'For AR(1), the single Yule–Walker equation reduces to $\\rho_1=\\phi\\rho_0=\\phi$ (since $\\rho_0=1$), so $\\phi=\\rho_1$ directly. Why other options are wrong: (A), (B), (C) do not follow from the AR(1) recursion $\\rho_k=\\phi\\rho_{k-1}$. Shortcut: AR(1) is the simplest possible case: the lag-1 autocorrelation *is* the AR coefficient. Tip: Also recall $\\rho_k=\\phi^{|k|}$ for AR(1) – geometric decay of the ACF.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(2) process, solving the Yule–Walker equations gives $\\phi_1$ in terms of $\\rho_1,\\rho_2$ as:',opts:['A) $\\phi_1=\\dfrac{\\rho_1(1-\\rho_2)}{1-\\rho_1^2}$','B) $\\phi_1=\\dfrac{\\rho_2-\\rho_1^2}{1-\\rho_1^2}$','C) $\\phi_1=\\rho_1+\\rho_2$','D) $\\phi_1=\\rho_1\\rho_2$'],ans:0,exp:'Solving the AR(2) Yule–Walker pair $\\rho_1=\\phi_1+\\phi_2\\rho_1$, $\\rho_2=\\phi_1\\rho_1+\\phi_2$ simultaneously gives $\\phi_1=\\rho_1(1-\\rho_2)/(1-\\rho_1^2)$ and $\\phi_2=(\\rho_2-\\rho_1^2)/(1-\\rho_1^2)$. Why other options are wrong: (B) is actually the formula for $\\phi_2$, not $\\phi_1$ – a classic mix-up trap; (D) and (C) have no derivation basis. Shortcut: Memorise the pair together: $\\phi_1$ uses $\\rho_1(1-\\rho_2)$ in the numerator, $\\phi_2$ uses $(\\rho_2-\\rho_1^2)$. Tip: Highly repeated NET/JRF numerical – practice deriving both formulas from scratch, not just recalling them.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Partial Autocorrelation Function (PACF) at lag $k$ measures the correlation between $X_t$ and $X_{t-k}$:',opts:['A) Ignoring all intermediate lags','B) After removing the linear effect of $X_{t-1},\\ldots,X_{t-k+1}$','C) After differencing the series twice','D) Using only the last observation'],ans:1,exp:'The PACF at lag $k$, $\\phi_{kk}$, is the correlation between $X_t$ and $X_{t-k}$ after adjusting (regressing out) the linear dependence on the intervening observations $X_{t-1},\\ldots,X_{t-k+1}$. Why other options are wrong: (A) describes ordinary (not partial) autocorrelation loosely; (D) and (C) are unrelated fabricated descriptions. Shortcut: PACF = \'correlation left over after removing the middlemen\'. Tip: $\\phi_{kk}$ can be obtained recursively via the Durbin–Levinson algorithm, itself built on Yule–Walker-type equations.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a pure AR$(p)$ process, the theoretical PACF:',opts:['A) Is identically zero for all lags','B) Cuts off (becomes zero) after lag $p$','C) Equals the ACF at every lag','D) Tails off geometrically forever'],ans:1,exp:'A defining identification signature: for AR$(p)$, $\\phi_{kk}=0$ for all $k&gt;p$, while $\\phi_{pp}\\ne0$; the ACF, by contrast, tails off (decays) rather than cutting off. Why other options are wrong: (D) describes the *ACF* of an AR process, not the PACF; (A) would only hold for white noise; (C) is false in general. Shortcut: \'AR cuts off in PACF, tails off in ACF\' – the mirror image of the MA signature. Tip: This AR/MA identification rule (Box–Jenkins) is one of the single most repeated NET facts in time series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the notation ARMA$(p,q)$, the symbol $p$ denotes:',opts:['A) The autoregressive (AR) order','B) The differencing order','C) The moving-average (MA) order','D) The sample size'],ans:0,exp:'By convention, $p$ is the AR order (number of lagged $X$ terms) and $q$ is the MA order (number of lagged $\\varepsilon$ terms). Why other options are wrong: (C) is the role of $q$, not $p$; (B) belongs to ARIMA\'s $d$; (D) is unrelated to model order notation. Shortcut: $p$ comes first and pairs with AR; $q$ comes second and pairs with MA – alphabetical/positional order matches AR-then-MA. Tip: A one-mark definitional question NET repeats almost every cycle in some form.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the notation ARMA$(p,q)$, the symbol $q$ denotes:',opts:['A) The moving-average (MA) order','B) The number of observations','C) The forecast horizon','D) The AR order'],ans:0,exp:'$q$ is the MA order, i.e. the highest lag of the innovation $\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ appearing in the model. Why other options are wrong: (D) is the role of $p$, not $q$; (B) and (C) are unrelated sample-size/forecasting notions. Shortcut: Pair with the previous fact: $p\\to$AR, $q\\to$MA, always in that fixed order. Tip: Confusing $p$ and $q$ is a very common careless-reading error under exam time pressure – double-check before answering.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(UGC NET style) The order of an ARMA$(p,q)$ model refers to:',opts:['A) The degree of stationarity','B) The AR order $p$ and MA order $q$, i.e. the highest lags of $X$ and $\\varepsilon$ appearing in the model','C) The sample size minus 1','D) The number of observations used'],ans:1,exp:'$p$ is the highest lag of $X_{t-i}$ appearing on the right-hand side and $q$ is the highest lag of $\\varepsilon_{t-j}$; together they specify the model order. Why other options are wrong: (D), (A), (C) confuse \'order\' with unrelated sample-size or stationarity notions. Shortcut: \'Order\' in time series always means \'highest lag included\', exactly as in AR and MA models separately. Tip: Do not confuse model order $(p,q)$ with the order of *differencing* $d$ used in ARIMA$(p,d,q)$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(UGC NET style) For an MA$(q)$ process, the ACF is zero for lags:',opts:['A) $k&gt;q$','B) $k=q$ only','C) $k\\le q$','D) all $k$'],ans:0,exp:'An MA$(q)$ process has non-zero autocovariance only up to lag $q$ (since $\\varepsilon_t,\\ldots,\\varepsilon_{t-q}$ overlap for $|k|\\le q$); beyond lag $q$ there is no shared innovation, so $\\rho_k=0$ for $k&gt;q$. Why other options are wrong: (C) states the opposite region; (D) would make the process white noise; (B) restricts to a single lag incorrectly. Shortcut: MA$(q)$: ACF cuts off after lag $q$ – the mirror image of the AR PACF cutoff. Tip: Use this fact together with the AR PACF cutoff (Q17) to identify $p$ and $q$ from a sample correlogram.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(UGC NET style) In a correlogram, if the ACF tails off gradually while the PACF cuts off sharply after lag 2, the appropriate tentative model is:',opts:['A) MA(2)','B) AR(2)','C) White noise','D) ARMA(1,1)'],ans:1,exp:'\'ACF tails off, PACF cuts off after lag $p$\' is precisely the AR$(p)$ signature; here the cutoff at lag 2 identifies AR(2) as the tentative model in Box–Jenkins identification. Why other options are wrong: (A) would instead show ACF cutting off, not tailing off; (D) would show both ACF and PACF tailing off; (C) would show both near zero at all lags. Shortcut: Read PACF cutoff lag directly as the tentative AR order $p$. Tip: This is the single most-tested identification rule across NET and JRF papers on Box–Jenkins methodology.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(UGC NET style) If both the ACF and PACF of a series tail off (decay gradually) rather than cutting off sharply, the tentative model is:',opts:['A) White noise','B) Pure AR','C) Mixed ARMA$(p,q)$ with $p,q\\ge1$','D) Pure MA'],ans:2,exp:'Neither a hard AR cutoff (in PACF) nor a hard MA cutoff (in ACF) is seen; both functions decaying gradually is the classic signature of a mixed ARMA process with genuine AR and MA components. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (D) each require one function to cut off sharply, which is not observed here; (A) would show both functions near zero everywhere, not gradually decaying. Shortcut: \'Both tail off\' $\\Rightarrow$ think ARMA, not pure AR or pure MA. Tip: Exact ARMA order identification from ACF/PACF alone is harder than for pure AR/MA – AIC/BIC comparison is typically needed too.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(UGC NET style) The variance equation obtained from Yule–Walker relations (multiplying the AR model by $X_t$ itself) involves:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2$ together with the $\\phi_i$\'s and $\\gamma_i$\'s','B) Only $\\sigma^2$','C) Only $\\rho_1$','D) Only the $\\phi_i$\'s'],ans:0,exp:'Multiplying the AR($p$) equation by $X_t$ and taking expectation gives $\\gamma_0=\\phi_1\\gamma_1+\\cdots+\\phi_p\\gamma_p+\\sigma^2$, linking the innovation variance $\\sigma^2$ to the autocovariances and AR coefficients. Why other options are wrong: (D) omits $\\sigma^2$, which must appear because $E(\\varepsilon_tX_t)=\\sigma^2$ here (unlike for $k\\ge1$); (B) and (C) omit the AR structure entirely. Shortcut: The $k=0$ multiplication is special: it is the only one where $E(\\varepsilon_tX_{t-k})\\ne0$. Tip: This equation is used *after* solving for $\\phi_i$\'s via Yule–Walker, to back out $\\hat\\sigma^2$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(UGC NET style) For AR(1), $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, the variance is:',opts:['A) $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi)$','B) $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$','C) $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2\\phi^2$','D) $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2(1-\\phi^2)$'],ans:1,exp:'Solving $\\gamma_0=\\phi^2\\gamma_0+\\sigma^2$ (from the variance equation with $\\gamma_1=\\phi\\gamma_0$) gives $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$, requiring $|\\phi|&lt;1$ for a finite, positive variance. Why other options are wrong: (D) inverts the relationship; (A) omits the square on $\\phi$; (C) has no derivational basis. Shortcut: Finite variance itself requires $|\\phi|&lt;1$ – consistent with the stationarity condition for AR(1). Tip: This formula also explains why variance explodes as $\\phi\\to\\pm1$ (approach to non-stationarity/unit root).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(UGC NET style) ARMA models are typically fitted to series that are:',opts:['A) Categorical','B) Purely deterministic','C) (Weakly) stationary','D) Non-stationary in mean and variance'],ans:2,exp:'ARMA theory (mean, ACVF, ACF formulas, stationarity/invertibility conditions) is developed under the assumption of weak (second-order) stationarity of $\\{X_t\\}$. Why other options are wrong: (D) instead requires prior differencing (leading to ARIMA); (B) and (A) fall outside the ARMA stochastic framework altogether. Shortcut: If raw data are non-stationary, difference first (ARIMA) before fitting ARMA to the differenced series. Tip: NET frequently tests the ARMA $\\to$ ARIMA distinction by asking what happens when stationarity fails.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(UGC NET style) A necessary condition for stationarity of AR(2), $X_t=\\phi_1X_{t-1}+\\phi_2X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$, includes:',opts:['A) $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&lt;1$, $\\phi_2-\\phi_1&lt;1$, $|\\phi_2|&lt;1$','B) $\\phi_1\\phi_2=1$','C) $|\\phi_1|&gt;1$','D) $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&gt;1$'],ans:0,exp:'The classical AR(2) stationarity triangle requires simultaneously $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&lt;1$, $\\phi_2-\\phi_1&lt;1$, and $-1&lt;\\phi_2&lt;1$, equivalent to both roots of $1-\\phi_1z-\\phi_2z^2=0$ lying outside the unit circle. Why other options are wrong: (D) violates the first inequality; (B) and (C) are arbitrary conditions with no basis in the stationarity triangle. Shortcut: Sketch the \'stationarity triangle\' in the $(\\phi_1,\\phi_2)$-plane – a favourite JRF diagram-based question. Tip: For AR(1), this reduces simply to $|\\phi_1|&lt;1$, the $p=1$ special case of the same triangle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(UGC NET style) For an ARMA(1,1) process, $\\rho_k$ for $k\\ge2$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $\\rho_k=\\phi\\theta\\,\\rho_{k-1}$','B) $\\rho_k=\\phi\\,\\rho_{k-1}$','C) $\\rho_k=\\rho_1^k$','D) $\\rho_k=\\theta\\,\\rho_{k-1}$'],ans:1,exp:'Beyond lag 1, the MA(1) innovation term no longer contributes new overlap, so the ACF of ARMA(1,1) satisfies the pure AR(1)-type recursion $\\rho_k=\\phi\\rho_{k-1}$ for $k\\ge2$, i.e. geometric decay governed solely by $\\phi$. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (A) wrongly involve $\\theta$ beyond lag 1, where its influence has already vanished; (C) is not generally true unless $\\rho_1=\\phi$. Shortcut: Only $\\rho_1$ carries the joint $\\phi,\\theta$ formula; from lag 2 onward it is pure geometric decay in $\\phi$. Tip: This is why ARMA(1,1) ACF and AR(1) ACF look identical for $k\\ge2$ but differ at $k=1$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(UGC NET style) In the notation ARMA$(p,q)$, if $q=0$ the model reduces to:',opts:['A) White noise','B) Random walk','C) AR$(p)$','D) MA$(p)$'],ans:2,exp:'With $q=0$ there are no MA terms, leaving only the AR part, so ARMA$(p,0)=$AR$(p)$. Why other options are wrong: (D) confuses which letter vanished; (A) would additionally require $p=0$; (B) is an unrelated non-stationary process. Shortcut: $q=0\\Rightarrow$ AR only; $p=0\\Rightarrow$ MA only – keep the two straight. Tip: A one-line elimination trap frequently used to test careless reading in NET MCQs.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(UGC NET style) The Durbin–Levinson algorithm is primarily used to:',opts:['A) Estimate the spectral density directly','B) Recursively compute PACF values (and equivalently solve Yule–Walker equations) without full matrix inversion','C) Perform seasonal differencing','D) Test for unit roots'],ans:1,exp:'The Durbin–Levinson recursion computes $\\phi_{kk}$ (PACF) and AR coefficients order-by-order, avoiding direct inversion of the $\\Gamma_p$ matrix required by the raw Yule–Walker system. Why other options are wrong: (D) is the role of Dickey–Fuller-type tests; (A) needs different (periodogram-based) methods; (C) is a preprocessing step unrelated to this recursion. Shortcut: Durbin–Levinson $=$ \'Yule–Walker done efficiently, one order at a time\'. Tip: JRF numericals sometimes ask you to hand-compute $\\phi_{11},\\phi_{22}$ using this recursion from given $\\rho_1,\\rho_2$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'(UGC NET style) Which statement about redundant (common) factors in $\\phi(B)$ and $\\theta(B)$ is TRUE?',opts:['A) Common factors improve model identifiability','B) Common factors change the white-noise variance only','C) Common factors should be cancelled; an ARMA model with common roots is not uniquely identifiable','D) Common factors are required for invertibility'],ans:2,exp:'If $\\phi(B)$ and $\\theta(B)$ share a common root, the model is overparameterised: cancelling the common factor gives an equivalent, lower-order ARMA representation, and failing to cancel destroys parameter identifiability. Why other options are wrong: (A) is the opposite of the truth; (D) has no logical connection to invertibility; (B) is an unsupported claim. Shortcut: Always check $\\gcd(\\phi(B),\\theta(B))=1$ before declaring the ARMA order minimal. Tip: A classic JRF trap: a seemingly high-order ARMA collapses to a much simpler model once common factors are cancelled.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For any stationary process, the partial autocorrelation at lag 1, $\\phi_{11}$, always equals:',opts:['A) $1$','B) $0$','C) $\\rho_1$','D) $\\rho_2$'],ans:2,exp:'By definition, at lag 1 there are no intermediate lags to partial out, so the PACF trivially coincides with the ordinary ACF: $\\phi_{11}=\\rho_1$ for any stationary process. Why other options are wrong: (D) confuses lag-1 PACF with the lag-2 ACF; (B) and (A) have no general justification at lag 1. Shortcut: Lag-1 PACF = lag-1 ACF, always, for *any* stationary process – a universal fact independent of model type. Tip: This is a quick way to sanity-check any PACF table you compute: its first entry must match the ACF\'s first entry exactly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The autocovariance function (ACVF) of a stationary process depends on:',opts:['A) Both $t$ and $k$ equally','B) Neither $t$ nor $k$','C) The time lag $k$ only, not on $t$','D) $t$ only'],ans:2,exp:'Under (weak) stationarity, $\\mathrm{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+k})=\\gamma_k$ depends only on the lag $k$, not on the specific time origin $t$. Why other options are wrong: (D) contradicts stationarity by depending on absolute time; (A) overstates the dependence; (B) would make ACVF a trivial constant, which is false in general. Shortcut: \'Depends on lag only\' is *the* defining property of (weak) stationarity for the covariance structure. Tip: Contrast this with a non-stationary process (e.g. random walk), where $\\mathrm{Var}(X_t)$ grows with $t$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The autocorrelation function is related to the ACVF by:',opts:['A) $\\rho_k=\\gamma_0/\\gamma_k$','B) $\\rho_k=\\gamma_k+\\gamma_0$','C) $\\rho_k=\\gamma_k/\\gamma_0$','D) $\\rho_k=\\gamma_k\\gamma_0$'],ans:2,exp:'By definition $\\rho_k=\\mathrm{Corr}(X_t,X_{t+k})=\\gamma_k/\\gamma_0$, a normalisation of the ACVF by the process variance $\\gamma_0$. Why other options are wrong: (D) multiplies instead of dividing; (A) inverts the ratio; (B) adds instead of dividing – none give a value bounded in $[-1,1]$. Shortcut: $\\rho_0=1$ always, since $\\gamma_0/\\gamma_0=1$ – a quick sanity check for any computed ACF table. Tip: Every numerical ACF/ACVF question should start by confirming $\\rho_0=1$ before using further recursions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARMA(1,1) process $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$, $\\rho_1$ equals:',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{\\phi\\theta}{1-\\phi^2}$','B) $\\dfrac{(1+\\phi\\theta)(\\phi+\\theta)}{1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2}$','C) $\\phi+\\theta$','D) $\\phi\\theta$'],ans:1,exp:'The standard ARMA(1,1) result gives $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2(1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2)/(1-\\phi^2)$ and $\\gamma_1=\\sigma^2(1+\\phi\\theta)(\\phi+\\theta)/(1-\\phi^2)$, so $\\rho_1=\\gamma_1/\\gamma_0=(1+\\phi\\theta)(\\phi+\\theta)/(1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2)$. Why other options are wrong: (C) is only a crude first-order approximation, not exact; (A) resembles an AR(1)-type expression with no $\\theta$ in the right place; (D) omits the entire additive structure. Shortcut: Memorise $\\rho_1^{ARMA(1,1)}$ as a ratio of two quadratics in $\\phi,\\theta$ – then $\\rho_k=\\phi\\rho_{k-1}$ for $k\\ge2$. Tip: Highly repeated JRF numerical: plug in given $\\phi,\\theta$ values to compute $\\rho_1$ directly from this formula.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For $\\phi=0.5,\\ \\theta=0.3$ in an ARMA(1,1) process, $\\rho_1$ is approximately:',opts:['A) $0.80$','B) $0.66$','C) $0.30$','D) $0.50$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\rho_1=(1+\\phi\\theta)(\\phi+\\theta)/(1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2)=(1.15)(0.8)/(1+0.3+0.09)=0.92/1.39\\approx0.662$. Why other options are wrong: (A) is just $(\\phi+\\theta)$, forgetting to divide by the denominator; (C) and (D) are simply $\\theta$ and $\\phi$ individually, not the combined ratio. Shortcut: Compute numerator $(1+\\phi\\theta)(\\phi+\\theta)$ and denominator $(1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2)$ separately, then divide. Tip: After getting $\\rho_1\\approx0.662$, note $\\rho_2=\\phi\\rho_1\\approx0.331$, $\\rho_3=\\phi\\rho_2\\approx0.165$, and so on.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'White noise $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$ is a special case of ARMA$(p,q)$ with:',opts:['A) $p=1,q=0$','B) $p=0,q=1$','C) $p=q=0$','D) $p=q=1$'],ans:2,exp:'With both $p=0$ and $q=0$, the ARMA equation collapses to $X_t=\\varepsilon_t$, i.e. white noise itself, the trivial degenerate case. Why other options are wrong: (A) is AR(1); (B) is MA(1); (D) is ARMA(1,1) – each retains genuine dependence structure, unlike white noise. Shortcut: White noise has zero autocorrelation at all non-zero lags: $\\rho_k=0,\\ k\\ne0$. Tip: White noise is the \'building block\' from which every ARMA process is constructed via linear filtering.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density of a stationary ARMA$(p,q)$ process is proportional to:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2|\\theta(e^{-i\\omega})|$','B) $\\sigma^2|\\theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2/|\\phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2$','C) $\\sigma^2/|\\phi(e^{-i\\omega})|$','D) $\\sigma^2|\\phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2/|\\theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2$'],ans:1,exp:'The spectral density is $f(\\omega)=\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\pi}\\dfrac{|\\theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}{|\\phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2}$, i.e. the MA polynomial modulus-squared in the numerator and the AR polynomial modulus-squared in the denominator. Why other options are wrong: (D) inverts numerator and denominator; (C) and (A) drop one polynomial entirely and use the wrong power. Shortcut: MA polynomial $\\to$ numerator; AR polynomial $\\to$ denominator – same roles as in the causal representation $\\psi(B)=\\theta(B)/\\phi(B)$. Tip: A JRF favourite: roots of $\\phi(z)$ near the unit circle create spectral peaks (resonance) at the corresponding frequency.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Box–Jenkins methodology for ARMA modelling proceeds through the stages:',opts:['A) Randomisation, Blocking, Replication','B) Identification, Estimation, Diagnostic checking','C) Sampling, Testing, Reporting','D) Coding, Debugging, Deployment'],ans:1,exp:'The classical Box–Jenkins cycle is: tentative model identification (via ACF/PACF), parameter estimation, and diagnostic checking of residuals, iterated until an adequate model is found. Why other options are wrong: (C) describes generic survey stages; (A) describes design-of-experiments principles; (D) is a software-engineering workflow – none pertain to ARMA model building. Shortcut: \'Identify $\\to$ Estimate $\\to$ Diagnose\' – the three-stage mantra of Box–Jenkins. Tip: NET often asks which stage a described activity (e.g. residual ACF check) belongs to – that is Diagnostic checking.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In Box–Jenkins diagnostic checking, residuals from a correctly specified ARMA model should behave like:',opts:['A) A trending series','B) An AR(1) process','C) A seasonal series','D) White noise (uncorrelated, mean zero)'],ans:3,exp:'If the fitted ARMA model has correctly captured the dependence structure, the residuals $\\hat\\varepsilon_t$ should show no remaining autocorrelation and behave like white noise, typically checked via the Ljung–Box test on residual ACF. Why other options are wrong: (A), (B), (C) all indicate unmodelled structure remaining, signalling model inadequacy rather than a correct specification. Shortcut: \'Residuals look like white noise\' is the diagnostic green light to stop refining the model. Tip: Significant residual autocorrelation at some lag $k$ suggests the AR or MA order needs to be increased.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'AIC and BIC are used in ARMA modelling primarily to:',opts:['A) Estimate the mean of the series','B) Test for stationarity','C) Compute the ACF directly','D) Select among competing $(p,q)$ orders by balancing fit and parsimony'],ans:3,exp:'Both information criteria penalise the log-likelihood by a complexity term (number of parameters), allowing comparison across candidate $(p,q)$ combinations to select a parsimonious, well-fitting model. Why other options are wrong: (B) is instead the job of unit-root tests; (A) and (C) are unrelated basic descriptive computations, not model-selection tools. Shortcut: AIC/BIC = \'goodness of fit minus a penalty for extra parameters\' – lower value is preferred. Tip: BIC penalises complexity more heavily than AIC for large samples, tending to select more parsimonious models.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An AR$(p)$ process can equivalently be described as ARMA$(p,q)$ with:',opts:['A) $q=1$','B) $q=0$','C) $q=p-1$','D) $q=p$'],ans:1,exp:'Since a pure AR$(p)$ model has no moving-average terms, it is exactly ARMA$(p,0)$, i.e. $q=0$. Why other options are wrong: (D), (A), (C) all wrongly introduce a non-zero MA order where none exists. Shortcut: AR$(p)$ $=$ ARMA$(p,0)$ – set the MA order to zero, nothing more. Tip: This equivalence lets all general ARMA formulas (stationarity, ACVF derivation) specialise correctly to the pure AR case.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Yule–Walker (moment) estimator of $\\phi$ in large samples is:',opts:['A) Undefined for AR(2)','B) Inconsistent','C) Always biased upward','D) Consistent and asymptotically normal'],ans:3,exp:'Under standard regularity conditions, the Yule–Walker estimator $\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\phi}$ is consistent for $\\boldsymbol\\phi$ and asymptotically normally distributed as sample size $n\\to\\infty$, and is asymptotically as efficient as the MLE under Gaussianity. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (C) contradict the well-established asymptotic theory; (A) is false – Yule–Walker generalises cleanly to any AR order $p$. Shortcut: \'Consistent + asymptotically normal + asymptotically efficient (Gaussian case)\' – the three key large-sample properties to recall. Tip: In small samples Yule–Walker can show noticeable bias, especially for coefficients near the stationarity boundary.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An ARMA process is said to be \'mixed\' when:',opts:['A) Both $p&gt;0$ and $q&gt;0$','B) $p=0$ and $q=0$','C) Only $q&gt;0$','D) Only $p&gt;0$'],ans:0,exp:'\'Mixed\' ARMA refers to a model containing genuine autoregressive *and* moving-average components simultaneously, i.e. both $p&gt;0$ and $q&gt;0$. Why other options are wrong: (B) describes white noise; (D) describes pure AR; (C) describes pure MA – none are \'mixed\' models. Shortcut: \'Mixed\' is simply shorthand for \'both parts present\', as opposed to a \'pure\' AR or MA model. Tip: Mixed models are often more parsimonious than a pure AR or MA model needed to approximate the same dynamics.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For any stationary process, the autocorrelation at lag 0, $\\rho_0$, always equals:',opts:['A) $0$','B) $1$','C) $\\gamma_0$','D) $\\sigma^2$'],ans:1,exp:'By definition $\\rho_0=\\gamma_0/\\gamma_0=1$ for any stationary process with finite, positive variance – a basic normalisation fact. Why other options are wrong: (A) would make the process have zero variance (degenerate); (D) and (C) confuse the *unnormalised* variance with the *normalised* correlation. Shortcut: Always check $\\rho_0=1$ first as a sanity check before trusting any computed ACF table. Tip: This is the very first entry of every correlogram, by definition, regardless of the specific ARMA model.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The innovations $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$ used in constructing an ARMA model are assumed to have autocorrelation:',opts:['A) Equal to $\\rho_1$ of the observed series','B) Equal to 1 at all lags','C) Zero at all non-zero lags','D) Undefined'],ans:2,exp:'White-noise innovations are by definition uncorrelated across time, so their autocorrelation is exactly zero at every non-zero lag (and 1 at lag 0, like any process). Why other options are wrong: (B) would make $\\varepsilon_t$ perfectly and permanently correlated, contradicting white noise; (A) confuses the innovation\'s autocorrelation with the observed process\'s ACF; (D) is false since it is explicitly defined to be zero. Shortcut: \'White noise\' is precisely *defined* by having zero autocorrelation at every non-zero lag – restate this whenever asked to define innovations. Tip: This zero-autocorrelation assumption on $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$ is what makes the Yule–Walker derivation (multiply-and-take-expectation) work cleanly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) process with $\\phi_1=0.5,\\phi_2=0.6$, the characteristic equation $1-0.5z-0.6z^2=0$ has a root with modulus:',opts:['A) Greater than 1 for both roots','B) Complex with modulus exactly 1','C) Exactly equal to 1 for both roots','D) Less than 1 for at least one root, so the process is non-stationary'],ans:3,exp:'Solving $0.6z^2+0.5z-1=0$ gives $z=\\dfrac{-0.5\\pm\\sqrt{0.25+2.4}}{1.2}=\\dfrac{-0.5\\pm1.628}{1.2}$, yielding roots $\\approx0.940$ and $\\approx-1.773$. Since $|0.940|&lt;1$, one root lies inside the unit circle, so the stationarity condition fails. Why other options are wrong: (A) is false since one root has modulus below 1; (C) and (B) misstate the actual computed root moduli. Shortcut: Quick check first: $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=1.1&gt;1$ already violates the AR(2) stationarity triangle condition $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&lt;1$ – no need to solve the quadratic explicitly. Tip: Always check the triangle inequalities *before* solving the characteristic equation – it is much faster.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an MA(1) process $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ with $\\theta=1.5$, the process is:',opts:['A) Invertible only if $\\sigma^2&gt;1$','B) Non-stationary','C) Invertible, since $|\\theta|&gt;1$','D) Not invertible, since the root of $1+\\theta z=0$ has modulus less than 1'],ans:3,exp:'The MA(1) characteristic root is $z=-1/\\theta=-1/1.5\\approx-0.667$, with $|z|\\approx0.667&lt;1$ (inside the unit circle), violating invertibility. Note MA processes are *always* stationary regardless of $\\theta$. Why other options are wrong: (C) misreads the invertibility condition (it needs $|\\theta|&lt;1$, not $&gt;1$, for a first-order MA); (B) is wrong since MA(1) is stationary for any finite $\\theta$; (A) confuses variance with the invertibility condition. Shortcut: For MA(1), invertibility is simply $|\\theta|&lt;1$ – a one-line shortcut equivalent to the root condition. Tip: When $|\\theta|&gt;1$, an *invertible* MA(1) with parameter $1/\\theta$ generates identical ACF – the two are observationally equivalent, a classic JRF conceptual trap.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An ARMA(2,1) process has AR polynomial $\\phi(z)=1-1.3z+0.4z^2$. Its roots are:',opts:['A) $z=-1.25,\\,-2$','B) $z=1,\\,1$','C) $z=0.8,\\,1.25$','D) $z=1.25,\\,2$'],ans:3,exp:'Factor $1-1.3z+0.4z^2=0.4(z-1.25)(z-2)$; checking: $0.4\\times1.25\\times2=1$ (constant term) and $0.4(1.25+2)=1.3$ (coefficient magnitude) – both match, confirming roots $z=1.25$ and $z=2$, both with modulus $&gt;1$, so the process is stationary. Why other options are wrong: (C) includes $0.8$, which is not a root of this specific polynomial; (A) has the wrong sign; (B) is a repeated-unit-root case not matching the given coefficients. Shortcut: Factor the quadratic and verify using Vieta\'s formulas: product of roots $=1/0.4=2.5$ and check $1.25\\times2=2.5$ $\\checkmark$. Tip: Since both roots exceed 1 in modulus, this ARMA(2,1) is stationary – always verify *both* roots, not just one.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ARMA(1,1) process with $\\phi=0.8,\\theta=-0.5$, which statement about $\\rho_1$ is correct?',opts:['A) $\\rho_1$ always equals $\\phi$','B) $\\rho_1$ has the same sign as $(\\phi+\\theta)$ and can be negative even though $\\phi&gt;0$','C) $\\rho_1=\\theta$ always','D) $\\rho_1$ is always positive whenever $\\phi&gt;0$'],ans:1,exp:'Since $\\rho_1=(1+\\phi\\theta)(\\phi+\\theta)/(1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2)$ and the denominator is always positive, the sign of $\\rho_1$ follows $(1+\\phi\\theta)(\\phi+\\theta)$; here $\\phi+\\theta=0.3&gt;0$ but with strongly negative $\\theta$ close to $-\\phi$, $\\rho_1$ can be pulled toward zero or negative depending on magnitudes – so a positive $\\phi$ does not guarantee a positive $\\rho_1$ in general. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (C) ignore the full ARMA(1,1) formula, collapsing it to a pure AR(1) or MA(1) special case; (D) is a false generalisation that fails once $\\theta$ is sufficiently negative. Shortcut: Never assume $\\rho_1\\approx\\phi$ for ARMA(1,1) when $\\theta\\ne0$ – always use the full ratio formula. Tip: This is a favourite JRF trap testing whether you truly understand the *combined* role of $\\phi$ and $\\theta$ rather than pattern-matching to AR(1).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) Yule–Walker system with $\\rho_1=0.5,\\rho_2=0.3$, the estimated $\\phi_2$ equals:',opts:['A) $0.467$','B) $0.0\\overline{6}$ (i.e. $\\approx0.067$)','C) $-0.067$','D) $0.30$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\phi_2=(\\rho_2-\\rho_1^2)/(1-\\rho_1^2)=(0.3-0.25)/(1-0.25)=0.05/0.75\\approx0.0667$. Why other options are wrong: (A) is actually the correct value of $\\phi_1$ (a mix-up trap), not $\\phi_2$; (D) is simply $\\rho_2$ itself, unadjusted; (C) has the correct magnitude but wrong sign. Shortcut: Compute numerator $\\rho_2-\\rho_1^2$ and denominator $1-\\rho_1^2$ separately before dividing to avoid sign slips. Tip: Cross-check: with $\\phi_1\\approx0.467,\\phi_2\\approx0.067$, verify $\\phi_1+\\phi_2\\approx0.533&lt;1$, satisfying the stationarity triangle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Two ARMA models, $\\phi(B)=1-0.5B$, $\\theta(B)=1+0.5B$ (Model I) versus $\\phi(B)=1$, $\\theta(B)=1$ (Model II, white noise), are related in that:',opts:['A) Model I is not stationary','B) Model I is not invertible','C) Model I has a common factor $(1-0.5B)$ appearing in neither polynomial, so it is genuinely ARMA(1,1), not reducible to white noise','D) Model I and Model II generate identical ACFs since the common factor $(1+0.5B)$ vs cancellation is irrelevant'],ans:2,exp:'Here $\\phi(B)=1-0.5B$ and $\\theta(B)=1+0.5B$ share *no* common root ($z=2$ vs $z=-2$), so this ARMA(1,1) is a genuinely irreducible model, distinct from white noise, with nontrivial ACF decaying via $\\rho_k=\\phi\\rho_{k-1}$ for $k\\ge2$. Why other options are wrong: (D) wrongly claims equivalence to white noise; (A) is false since $|1/0.5|=2&gt;1$ satisfies stationarity; (B) is false since $\\theta=0.5$ satisfies $|\\theta|&lt;1$ for invertibility. Shortcut: To test reducibility to white noise, check whether $\\phi(B)$ and $\\theta(B)$ share an *identical* root – here they clearly do not ($z=2\\ne z=-2$). Tip: Contrast this with a genuinely redundant case like $\\phi(B)=1-0.5B$, $\\theta(B)=1-0.5B$, which *does* cancel to white noise.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\phi(B)=1-0.5B$ and $\\theta(B)=1-0.5B$ (identical factors) in a nominal ARMA(1,1) specification, then:',opts:['A) The model is a genuine ARMA(1,1) with strong dependence','B) The process becomes non-invertible','C) The process becomes non-stationary','D) The factors cancel; the process reduces exactly to white noise $X_t=\\varepsilon_t$'],ans:3,exp:'Since $\\phi(B)=\\theta(B)=1-0.5B$ share an identical common factor, $\\phi(B)X_t=\\theta(B)\\varepsilon_t$ simplifies by cancelling $(1-0.5B)$ from both sides, leaving $X_t=\\varepsilon_t$: a trivial white-noise process in disguise. Why other options are wrong: (A) is precisely the mistake of not checking for common factors; (C) and (B) are irrelevant once the model is recognised as reducing to white noise, which is both stationary and invertible. Shortcut: Always factor $\\phi(B)$ and $\\theta(B)$ and cancel identical roots *before* interpreting an ARMA specification. Tip: This is the textbook illustration of the identifiability/redundant-parameter problem in ARMA modelling.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a general ARMA$(p,q)$ process, the ACF $\\rho_k$ for $k&gt;q$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $\\rho_k=0$ always','B) $\\rho_k=1$ for all $k&gt;q$','C) $\\rho_k$ depends only on $\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_q$','D) A linear homogeneous difference equation with characteristic roots given by the reciprocals of the roots of $\\phi(z)=0$'],ans:3,exp:'For $k&gt;q$, the innovation terms no longer overlap and the ACF satisfies exactly the same homogeneous recursion as a pure AR$(p)$ process, $\\rho_k=\\phi_1\\rho_{k-1}+\\cdots+\\phi_p\\rho_{k-p}$, whose general solution is a combination of terms like $G_i\\,x_i^{-k}$, where $x_i$ are roots of $\\phi(z)=0$ (equivalently decaying with rate $1/x_i$, the reciprocal roots). Why other options are wrong: (A) and (B) are only true in degenerate special cases, not in general; (C) is false – beyond lag $q$ the decay is governed entirely by the AR parameters, not the MA parameters. Shortcut: Beyond lag $q$: \'MA parameters exit the stage\', and pure AR-type exponential/damped-sinusoidal decay takes over. Tip: This is the theoretical basis for why ARMA$(p,q)$ ACF eventually \'tails off\' just like a pure AR$(p)$ ACF.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Wold Decomposition Theorem guarantees that any purely non-deterministic, (weakly) stationary process can be represented as:',opts:['A) A finite AR model exactly','B) A finite mixture of two Gaussian distributions','C) An infinite-order moving average of white noise, $X_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j}$, $\\psi_0=1,\\sum\\psi_j^2&lt;\\infty$','D) A deterministic trend plus seasonal component only'],ans:2,exp:'Wold\'s theorem states that every purely non-deterministic stationary process has a one-sided infinite MA representation in terms of a white-noise sequence, providing the theoretical justification for approximating such processes by finite-order ARMA models. Why other options are wrong: (A) claims finiteness, which Wold\'s theorem does not guarantee in general; (D) describes a decomposition unrelated to Wold\'s stochastic result; (B) is a distributional claim with no connection to the theorem. Shortcut: ARMA models are finite-parameter *approximations* to the theoretically infinite Wold MA($\\infty$) representation. Tip: JRF descriptive answers should explicitly state the \'purely non-deterministic\' qualifier – deterministic components must first be removed.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Comparing estimation methods for ARMA parameters, which statement is generally TRUE for finite samples?',opts:['A) Least squares cannot be applied to ARMA models under any circumstances','B) Yule–Walker, conditional/unconditional least squares, and MLE are asymptotically equivalent but can differ noticeably in finite samples, especially near the stationarity boundary','C) Yule–Walker, conditional least squares, and exact MLE all give numerically identical estimates in every finite sample','D) Only MLE can ever be computed for ARMA models'],ans:1,exp:'While all standard estimators (Yule–Walker/moments, least squares variants, MLE) share the same asymptotic distribution under regularity conditions, their finite-sample behaviour differs – Yule–Walker in particular can show substantial bias when true parameters lie close to the stationarity/invertibility boundary. Why other options are wrong: (C) overstates finite-sample equivalence, which does not generally hold exactly; (D) and (A) are simply false – Yule-Walker, least squares, and MLE are all computable and commonly used. Shortcut: \'Asymptotically equivalent, finite-sample different\' is the safest, most defensible JRF answer for estimator-comparison questions. Tip: Simulation studies are the standard way JRF-level texts illustrate this finite-sample divergence.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARMA$(1,1)$ process to be both causal and invertible with a *single common* admissible region for $(\\phi,\\theta)$, we require:',opts:['A) $|\\theta|&lt;1$ only','B) $\\phi\\theta&lt;1$','C) $|\\phi|&lt;1$ and $|\\theta|&lt;1$ simultaneously','D) $|\\phi|&lt;1$ only'],ans:2,exp:'Causality (stationarity) of the AR part requires $|\\phi|&lt;1$, and invertibility of the MA part independently requires $|\\theta|&lt;1$; since the two conditions involve different polynomials, both must hold simultaneously for the ARMA(1,1) process to be causal *and* invertible. Why other options are wrong: (D) addresses causality alone, ignoring invertibility; (A) addresses invertibility alone, ignoring causality; (B) is not the correct joint condition (it is neither necessary nor sufficient on its own). Shortcut: Treat causality and invertibility as *two separate, independent* one-parameter conditions for ARMA(1,1); do not conflate them into a single inequality. Tip: This separability generalises: for ARMA$(p,q)$, causality depends only on $\\phi(z)$\'s roots and invertibility only on $\\theta(z)$\'s roots.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the innovations algorithm (an alternative to Yule–Walker/Durbin–Levinson for MA and ARMA estimation), estimation proceeds by:',opts:['A) Directly inverting the $p\\times p$ Toeplitz covariance matrix $\\Gamma_p$','B) Computing eigenvalues of the sample correlation matrix','C) Recursively computing one-step prediction errors and coefficients, applicable directly to MA and ARMA models (not just pure AR)','D) Fitting a polynomial trend by least squares'],ans:2,exp:'The innovations algorithm recursively computes best linear predictors and their mean squared errors using a triangular recursion, and unlike Durbin–Levinson (naturally suited to AR/PACF), it extends directly to processes with an MA component, including pure MA$(q)$ and general ARMA$(p,q)$. Why other options are wrong: (A) describes the raw Yule–Walker matrix approach, not the innovations algorithm; (D) and (B) are unrelated procedures with no role in this recursive prediction-error method. Shortcut: Durbin–Levinson $\\to$ naturally for AR/PACF; Innovations algorithm $\\to$ naturally extends to MA and ARMA estimation. Tip: JRF papers sometimes ask you to simply *name* the appropriate recursive algorithm for a given model type – know this pairing.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly ranks stationarity conditions across model types (assuming coefficients otherwise unrestricted)?',opts:['A) AR$(p)$ models are always stationary regardless of $\\phi_i$ values','B) MA$(q)$ models are stationary for *any* finite values of $\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_q$; only invertibility imposes a root condition on $\\theta(z)$','C) MA models require root conditions for stationarity, exactly like AR models','D) Both AR and MA models are automatically stationary and invertible for any parameter values'],ans:1,exp:'A finite-order MA process is a finite linear combination of white-noise terms and is *always* stationary, no matter the values of $\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_q$; it is invertibility (not stationarity) that imposes the root condition on $\\theta(z)$. AR models, by contrast, require the root condition on $\\phi(z)$ for stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (C) wrongly imposes a stationarity root condition on MA parameters; (A) is false – AR stationarity genuinely depends on the $\\phi_i$ satisfying the root condition; (D) is a sweeping false claim ignoring AR\'s stationarity requirement. Shortcut: MA $\\to$ always stationary, invertibility depends on $\\theta$; AR $\\to$ stationarity depends on $\\phi$, always invertible (trivially, since there\'s no MA part). Tip: This AR/MA asymmetry is one of the most conceptually important – and most frequently misunderstood – JRF-level facts in ARMA theory.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: A student claims \'the stationarity condition requires roots of $\\phi(z)=0$ to lie *inside* the unit circle.\' This claim is:',opts:['A) Incorrect – roots must lie *outside* the unit circle','B) Correct','C) Correct only for MA models','D) Correct only for AR(1)'],ans:0,exp:'The stationarity condition requires all roots of $\\phi(z)=1-\\phi_1z-\\cdots-\\phi_pz^p=0$ to lie *outside* the unit circle, i.e. $|z|&gt;1$; roots inside the unit circle indicate explosive, non-stationary behaviour. Why other options are wrong: (B), (D), (C) all endorse or partially endorse the reversed (incorrect) region. Shortcut: If you ever see \'inside the unit circle\' attached to *stationarity*, treat it as a red flag – the correct region is always outside. Tip: This inside/outside confusion is the single most common NET/JRF trap in the entire time series syllabus.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: \'For MA(1), $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$, invertibility requires $|\\theta|&gt;1$.\' This statement is:',opts:['A) True only if $\\sigma^2=1$','B) True only if $\\theta$ is negative','C) True','D) False – invertibility requires $|\\theta|&lt;1$'],ans:3,exp:'Invertibility of MA(1) requires the root of $1+\\theta z=0$, namely $z=-1/\\theta$, to satisfy $|z|&gt;1$, which is equivalent to $|\\theta|&lt;1$ – exactly the reverse of the trap statement. Why other options are wrong: (C), (A), (B) all retain or qualify the reversed inequality instead of correcting it. Shortcut: MA(1) invertibility shortcut: $|\\theta|&lt;1$, full stop – no dependence on $\\sigma^2$ or the sign of $\\theta$. Tip: Compare directly with AR(1) stationarity\'s $|\\phi|&lt;1$ – the two conditions are structurally parallel but apply to different polynomials.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: \'Since MA processes are always stationary, they never require any parameter restriction at all.\' This statement is:',opts:['A) False, because MA processes are not always stationary','B) True – no restriction of any kind is ever needed','C) False – while MA processes are always stationary, invertibility still restricts the MA parameters','D) True only for MA(1)'],ans:2,exp:'Stationarity is automatic for finite-order MA processes, but invertibility (needed for a unique AR($\\infty$) representation and for standard estimation/identification) still imposes a genuine root condition on $\\theta(z)$. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (D) wrongly conclude no restriction is ever needed; (A) incorrectly denies the (true) automatic-stationarity property. Shortcut: \'Always stationary\' $\\ne$ \'unrestricted\' – invertibility is a separate, still-binding constraint. Tip: Keep \'stationarity\' and \'invertibility\' as two logically independent properties, each with its own condition.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: \'The Yule–Walker equations can be used to estimate the MA parameters $\\theta_i$ directly, exactly as for AR parameters $\\phi_i$.\' This is:',opts:['A) True in general','B) True only for MA(1)','C) False – Yule–Walker equations in their basic linear form directly estimate AR parameters; MA parameter estimation requires nonlinear methods (e.g. innovations algorithm or MLE)','D) True only if $q&gt;p$'],ans:2,exp:'The Yule–Walker equations are *linear* in the AR coefficients because $E(\\varepsilon_tX_{t-k})=0$ for $k\\ge1$ in the AR case; for MA parameters, the ACF is a *nonlinear* function of $\\theta_i$\'s, so simple linear Yule–Walker-style equations do not directly yield MA estimates – nonlinear methods are required instead. Why other options are wrong: (A), (B), (D) all wrongly extend the linear AR estimation logic to the fundamentally nonlinear MA parameter estimation problem. Shortcut: Linear system $\\Rightarrow$ AR parameters (Yule–Walker); nonlinear system $\\Rightarrow$ MA parameters (innovations algorithm/MLE). Tip: This AR/MA estimation asymmetry is a favourite \'why\' question at the JRF level – be ready to explain *why* the MA case is nonlinear.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: \'A process with $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=0.9&lt;1$ in an AR(2) model is automatically stationary since the sum condition is satisfied.\' This is:',opts:['A) False – all three triangle conditions ($\\phi_1+\\phi_2&lt;1$, $\\phi_2-\\phi_1&lt;1$, $|\\phi_2|&lt;1$) must hold simultaneously','B) True only if $\\phi_1&gt;0$','C) True only if $\\phi_2&lt;0$','D) True – the sum condition alone is sufficient'],ans:0,exp:'Stationarity of AR(2) requires *all three* triangle inequalities to hold jointly; satisfying just one (e.g. $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&lt;1$) is necessary but not sufficient on its own – e.g. $\\phi_1=-2,\\phi_2=2.9$ satisfies the sum condition but violates $|\\phi_2|&lt;1$. Why other options are wrong: (D), (B), (C) all wrongly treat a single necessary condition as sufficient by itself. Shortcut: Never stop after checking just one triangle inequality – always verify all three before declaring stationarity. Tip: JRF setters often design AR(2) coefficient pairs that satisfy one condition but fail another, precisely to catch this shortcut error.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: \'For ARMA(1,1), if $\\phi=\\theta$ exactly (with the same sign and no other constraint), the process is still a genuine, non-degenerate ARMA(1,1).\' This is:',opts:['A) False – if $\\phi(B)$ and $\\theta(B)$ share the identical factor implied by $\\phi=\\theta$ under this sign convention, they may cancel, reducing the model to white noise','B) True always','C) True only if $\\phi&gt;0$','D) True only if $|\\phi|&lt;0.5$'],ans:0,exp:'With $\\theta(B)=1+\\theta B$ and $\\phi(B)=1-\\phi B$, setting $\\phi=\\theta$ does *not* automatically create a common factor (roots are $1/\\phi$ and $-1/\\theta=-1/\\phi$, generally distinct) – but if the model were instead specified so that both polynomials share literally the same root (e.g. $\\phi(B)=\\theta(B)=1-\\phi B$), cancellation to white noise occurs; the key general lesson is to always check for a *shared root*, not merely equal-magnitude coefficients. Why other options are wrong: (B), (C), (D) all assume genuineness without actually checking the root-cancellation condition, which is the only rigorous test. Shortcut: Don\'t judge redundancy by comparing $\\phi$ and $\\theta$ values superficially – always compare the actual *roots* of $\\phi(B)$ and $\\theta(B)$. Tip: This question tests precision: equal-looking coefficients do not automatically imply a common factor unless the polynomials are literally identical.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: \'PACF cutting off after lag $p$ identifies the MA order $q=p$.\' This statement is:',opts:['A) True only when $p=q$','B) True','C) True only for ARMA models','D) False – PACF cutoff after lag $p$ identifies the AR order $p$, not an MA order'],ans:3,exp:'A PACF that cuts off after lag $p$ is the identification signature for the AR order $p$; it is instead the *ACF* cutting off after lag $q$ that identifies the MA order $q$ – swapping ACF/PACF roles is the trap here. Why other options are wrong: (B), (C), (A) all preserve the incorrect ACF/PACF role-swap in some form. Shortcut: PACF cutoff $\\to$ AR order; ACF cutoff $\\to$ MA order. Say it in that fixed order every time to avoid swapping. Tip: This exact swap is one of the highest-frequency traps across NET Statistics time series papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: \'Since Yule–Walker estimators are consistent, they are also unbiased in every finite sample.\' This is:',opts:['A) False – consistency (large-sample) does not imply finite-sample unbiasedness','B) True','C) True only for AR(1)','D) True only for large $\\sigma^2$'],ans:0,exp:'Consistency is an asymptotic ($n\\to\\infty$) property, while unbiasedness is a finite-sample property; Yule–Walker estimators are consistent but can be noticeably biased in finite samples, especially for coefficients near the stationarity boundary. Why other options are wrong: (B), (C), (D) incorrectly treat consistency and unbiasedness as equivalent or conditionally equivalent properties. Shortcut: \'Consistent\' and \'unbiased\' answer two different questions (large-$n$ behaviour vs fixed-$n$ expectation) – never assume one implies the other. Tip: JRF theory papers frequently test this exact distinction across multiple estimators, not just Yule–Walker.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: \'Causality and invertibility are the same property applied to different names.\' This is:',opts:['A) True only for AR models','B) True – they are identical concepts','C) False – causality concerns representing $X_t$ in terms of past/present $\\varepsilon$\'s (depends on $\\phi(z)$ roots); invertibility concerns representing $\\varepsilon_t$ in terms of past/present $X$\'s (depends on $\\theta(z)$ roots)','D) True only for MA models'],ans:2,exp:'Causality is about expressing the *observed process* as a one-sided function of the *innovations* (governed by the AR polynomial\'s roots), while invertibility is about expressing the *innovation* as a one-sided function of the *observed process* (governed by the MA polynomial\'s roots) – structurally parallel, but logically and mathematically distinct. Why other options are wrong: (B), (A), (D) all conflate two genuinely separate conditions attached to different polynomials. Shortcut: Causality $\\leftrightarrow$ $X$ in terms of $\\varepsilon$ ($\\phi$-roots); Invertibility $\\leftrightarrow$ $\\varepsilon$ in terms of $X$ ($\\theta$-roots). Keep the direction of representation straight. Tip: A precise verbal distinction like this is exactly what JRF descriptive-answer evaluators look for.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: \'A higher-order ARMA model always fits the data better and should always be preferred over a lower-order model.\' This is:',opts:['A) False – higher order improves in-sample fit but risks overfitting; AIC/BIC and parsimony should guide order selection','B) True – always prefer the highest order available','C) True only for MA models','D) True only when $p=q$'],ans:0,exp:'While adding parameters mechanically improves in-sample fit (higher likelihood), it risks overfitting, unstable estimates, and poor out-of-sample forecasting performance; standard practice uses information criteria (AIC/BIC) and the principle of parsimony to balance fit against model complexity. Why other options are wrong: (B), (D), (C) all wrongly endorse unconditionally preferring higher order. Shortcut: \'Better fit\' is not the same as \'better model\' – always weigh fit against parsimony using AIC/BIC. Tip: JRF often frames this as a scenario question: given two models with close AIC values, which do you choose? Answer: the more parsimonious one, absent strong evidence otherwise.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (i) Every AR$(p)$ process is a special case of ARMA$(p,q)$. (ii) Every MA$(q)$ process is a special case of ARMA$(p,q)$. Which is/are TRUE?',opts:['A) Only (i)','B) Neither','C) Only (ii)','D) Both (i) and (ii)'],ans:3,exp:'AR$(p)=$ARMA$(p,0)$ and MA$(q)=$ARMA$(0,q)$; both are indeed special cases of the general ARMA$(p,q)$ family obtained by setting the other order to zero. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (C) each capture only one of the two true statements; (B) wrongly rejects both, which are in fact both true. Shortcut: Whenever you see \'is X a special case of ARMA\', just check whether setting one order to zero recovers X exactly. Tip: This statement pattern (both true) is common in NET when testing basic subsumption relationships among model families.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (i) Stationarity depends on the roots of $\\phi(z)$. (ii) Invertibility depends on the roots of $\\theta(z)$. Which is/are TRUE?',opts:['A) Only (i)','B) Neither','C) Only (ii)','D) Both (i) and (ii)'],ans:3,exp:'Both statements correctly assign stationarity to the AR polynomial\'s roots and invertibility to the MA polynomial\'s roots – the standard, non-swapped assignment. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (C) each accept only half of the (jointly true) statement pair; (B) wrongly rejects both correct statements. Shortcut: AR $\\to$ stationarity; MA $\\to$ invertibility – both statements here match this correct pairing exactly. Tip: A \'both true\' statement question like this is designed to reward students who have *not* internalised the AR/MA swap trap.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (i) The Yule–Walker equations are linear in the AR coefficients. (ii) The Yule–Walker equations directly give unbiased finite-sample estimators. Which is/are TRUE?',opts:['A) Both (i) and (ii)','B) Only (i)','C) Only (ii)','D) Neither'],ans:1,exp:'Statement (i) is true: the Yule–Walker system is a linear system in $\\phi_1,\\ldots,\\phi_p$. Statement (ii) is false: Yule–Walker estimators are consistent asymptotically but are not generally unbiased in finite samples. Why other options are wrong: (C) wrongly accepts the false unbiasedness claim; (A) wrongly accepts both; (D) wrongly rejects the true linearity statement. Shortcut: Split compound statements: check linearity (structural, true) separately from finite-sample unbiasedness (statistical property, false here). Tip: This exact combination (true structural claim + false statistical claim) is a common two-statement NET format.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (i) An ARMA process with common factors in $\\phi(B)$ and $\\theta(B)$ is not uniquely identifiable. (ii) Cancelling common factors can reduce the effective model order. Which is/are TRUE?',opts:['A) Both (i) and (ii)','B) Only (ii)','C) Neither','D) Only (i)'],ans:0,exp:'Both statements are true and are two sides of the same coin: uncancelled common factors destroy identifiability, and cancelling them correctly reduces the model to its true, lower, effective order. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (B) each wrongly reject one of the two jointly true statements; (C) wrongly rejects both. Shortcut: Common factors $\\Rightarrow$ non-identifiability *and* reducibility to lower order – these two facts always travel together. Tip: Descriptive JRF answers should explicitly connect these two consequences, not state them in isolation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (i) White noise has zero ACF at all non-zero lags. (ii) White noise is a special case of both AR(0) and MA(0). Which is/are TRUE?',opts:['A) Neither','B) Only (ii)','C) Both (i) and (ii)','D) Only (i)'],ans:2,exp:'White noise indeed has $\\rho_k=0$ for $k\\ne0$, and is simultaneously the $p=0$ special case of AR and the $q=0$ special case of MA, i.e. ARMA(0,0). Why other options are wrong: (D) and (B) each reject one true statement; (A) wrongly rejects both correct statements. Shortcut: White noise sits at the intersection of AR(0) and MA(0) – the \'empty\' special case of the entire ARMA family. Tip: A useful mental check: any process description reducing to $X_t=\\varepsilon_t$ must have zero ACF beyond lag 0.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (i) For AR$(p)$, PACF cuts off after lag $p$. (ii) For AR$(p)$, ACF also cuts off after lag $p$. Which is/are TRUE?',opts:['A) Only (ii)','B) Both (i) and (ii)','C) Neither','D) Only (i)'],ans:3,exp:'Statement (i) is the correct AR identification rule; statement (ii) is false – the ACF of AR$(p)$ *tails off* gradually (it does not cut off), unlike its PACF. Why other options are wrong: (A) wrongly accepts the false ACF-cutoff claim; (B) wrongly accepts both; (C) wrongly rejects the true PACF statement. Shortcut: AR: PACF cuts off, ACF tails off. Reversing \'cuts off\' and \'tails off\' between these two functions is the trap in statement (ii). Tip: Memorise the AR/MA table (Q. in Section G) to instantly resolve any statement combining ACF/PACF behaviour.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (i) MA$(q)$ processes are always invertible. (ii) MA$(q)$ processes are always stationary. Which is/are TRUE?',opts:['A) Only (ii)','B) Neither','C) Both (i) and (ii)','D) Only (i)'],ans:0,exp:'Statement (ii) is true: finite-order MA processes are always stationary regardless of $\\theta_i$ values. Statement (i) is false: invertibility is *not* automatic and depends on the roots of $\\theta(z)$ lying outside the unit circle. Why other options are wrong: (D) wrongly accepts the false automatic-invertibility claim; (C) wrongly accepts both; (B) wrongly rejects the true automatic-stationarity claim. Shortcut: MA: stationarity is free (always true); invertibility must be checked (not automatic) – exactly reversed from what many students assume. Tip: This is essentially the AR-vs-MA mirror image of Q. in Section D comparing stationarity conditions across model types.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (i) ARMA(1,1) ACF at lag 1 depends on both $\\phi$ and $\\theta$. (ii) ARMA(1,1) ACF for lag $\\ge2$ depends only on $\\phi$. Which is/are TRUE?',opts:['A) Neither','B) Both (i) and (ii)','C) Only (i)','D) Only (ii)'],ans:1,exp:'Both are correct: $\\rho_1=(1+\\phi\\theta)(\\phi+\\theta)/(1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2)$ genuinely involves both parameters, while $\\rho_k=\\phi\\rho_{k-1}$ for $k\\ge2$ depends only on $\\phi$ (given $\\rho_1$ as the starting value). Why other options are wrong: (C) and (D) each reject one of the two jointly true statements; (A) wrongly rejects both. Shortcut: \'$\\theta$ only matters at lag 1\' is the compact way to remember the entire ARMA(1,1) ACF structure. Tip: This statement pair often appears as an assertion-reason variant too – see the assertion-reason questions in this section.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Assertion (A): An ARMA(1,1) process with $\\phi=0.5,\\theta=-0.5$ has $\\rho_1=0$ exactly. Reason (R): $\\rho_1=0$ whenever $\\phi=-\\theta$ because $(\\phi+\\theta)=0$ makes the numerator of $\\rho_1$ vanish. Choose the correct option.',opts:['A) A is false but R is true','B) A is true but R is false','C) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A','D) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A'],ans:2,exp:'With $\\phi+\\theta=0.5-0.5=0$, the factor $(\\phi+\\theta)$ in the numerator of $\\rho_1=(1+\\phi\\theta)(\\phi+\\theta)/(1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2)$ vanishes, forcing $\\rho_1=0$ exactly – so A is true and R correctly explains it. Why other options are wrong: (D) wrongly denies the explanatory link despite it being the exact algebraic reason; (B) and (A) each wrongly reject one of the two jointly true, correctly-linked statements. Shortcut: Whenever $\\phi=-\\theta$ in ARMA(1,1), immediately conclude $\\rho_1=0$ without recomputing the full formula. Tip: Assertion-Reason format tests whether you can identify *why*, not just *that*, a result holds – always verify the causal algebraic link.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Assertion (A): The stationarity condition of an ARMA process depends only on $\\phi(z)$, never on $\\theta(z)$. Reason (R): The MA polynomial $\\theta(B)$ operates only on the already-stationary white-noise sequence $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$, so it cannot introduce non-stationarity. Choose the correct option.',opts:['A) A is false but R is true','B) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A','C) A is true but R is false','D) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A'],ans:3,exp:'A is true (stationarity is governed solely by $\\phi(z)$\'s roots), and R correctly explains *why*: a finite linear filter of an already-stationary white-noise process (via $\\theta(B)$) remains stationary, so only the AR feedback structure can introduce instability. Why other options are wrong: (B) wrongly denies a genuinely correct causal explanation; (C) and (A) each incorrectly reject one of the two true, correctly linked statements. Shortcut: Think of $\\theta(B)\\varepsilon_t$ as \'smoothing an already well-behaved noise sequence\' – smoothing white noise cannot create explosive behaviour. Tip: This A-R pair often appears as a \'why is MA always stationary\' descriptive-answer question in JRF papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Model) with List II (ACF behaviour): (1) AR$(p)$ (2) MA$(q)$ (3) ARMA$(p,q)$, $p,q\\ge1$ (4) White noise – with (a) Cuts off after lag $q$ (b) Tails off (decays gradually) (c) Zero at all non-zero lags (d) Tails off (same qualitative decay as AR)',opts:['A) 1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d','B) 1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a','C) 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b','D) 1-b, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c'],ans:3,exp:'AR$(p)$\'s ACF tails off (b); MA$(q)$\'s ACF cuts off after lag $q$ (a); mixed ARMA\'s ACF eventually tails off with AR-type decay (d); white noise has zero ACF beyond lag 0 (c). Why other options are wrong: (A), (C), (B) each swap at least one AR/MA role or misassign the white-noise / mixed-ARMA rows. Shortcut: Anchor points: MA $\\to$ \'cuts off\'; AR and mixed ARMA $\\to$ \'tails off\'; white noise $\\to$ \'zero everywhere\'. Tip: Pair this with the companion PACF-matching question below for a complete AR/MA/ARMA/white-noise identification table.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Model) with List II (PACF behaviour): (1) AR$(p)$ (2) MA$(q)$ (3) ARMA$(p,q)$, $p,q\\ge1$ (4) White noise – with (a) Cuts off after lag $p$ (b) Tails off (decays gradually) (c) Zero at all non-zero lags (d) Tails off (same qualitative decay as MA-driven ARMA)',opts:['A) 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b','B) 1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a','C) 1-a, 2-b, 3-d, 4-c','D) 1-b, 2-a, 3-c, 4-d'],ans:2,exp:'AR$(p)$\'s PACF cuts off after lag $p$ (a); MA$(q)$\'s PACF tails off (b); mixed ARMA\'s PACF also tails off (d); white noise\'s PACF is zero beyond lag 0 (c) – exactly the mirror image of the ACF-matching question. Why other options are wrong: (D), (A), (B) each reverse or misplace at least one of the four correct model-behaviour pairings. Shortcut: PACF is the mirror image of ACF here: AR cuts off (PACF) where MA cuts off (ACF), and vice versa for tailing off. Tip: Together, Q(ACF-match) and this PACF-match question form the complete Box–Jenkins identification lookup table.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Quantity) with List II (Formula/Role): (1) $\\Gamma_p$ (2) $\\boldsymbol\\gamma_p$ (3) $\\phi_{kk}$ (4) $\\sigma^2$ – with (a) Innovation variance (b) PACF at lag $k$ (c) $p\\times p$ Toeplitz autocovariance matrix (d) Vector $(\\gamma_1,\\ldots,\\gamma_p)\'$',opts:['A) 1-d, 2-a, 3-c, 4-b','B) 1-c, 2-d, 3-b, 4-a','C) 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a','D) 1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d'],ans:1,exp:'$\\Gamma_p$ is the Toeplitz autocovariance matrix (c); $\\boldsymbol\\gamma_p$ is the vector of covariances $(\\gamma_1,\\ldots,\\gamma_p)\'$ (d); $\\phi_{kk}$ is the lag-$k$ PACF (b); $\\sigma^2$ is the innovation variance appearing in the $k=0$ Yule–Walker equation (a). Why other options are wrong: (D), (C), (A) each mismatch at least one Yule–Walker quantity with the wrong role/formula. Shortcut: $\\Gamma_p\\boldsymbol\\phi=\\boldsymbol\\gamma_p$ – keep this equation in mind; every symbol in it has a distinct, fixed role. Tip: This matching tests whether you can read the Yule–Walker matrix equation symbol-by-symbol, not just recall it as a whole.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Condition) with List II (Polynomial involved): (1) Stationarity (2) Invertibility (3) Common-factor redundancy (4) Spectral density peak location – with (a) Roots common to both $\\phi(z)$ and $\\theta(z)$ (b) $\\theta(z)=0$ roots (c) $\\phi(z)=0$ roots (d) $\\phi(z)=0$ roots near the unit circle',opts:['A) 1-c, 2-b, 3-a, 4-d','B) 1-a, 2-d, 3-b, 4-c','C) 1-d, 2-a, 3-c, 4-b','D) 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a'],ans:0,exp:'Stationarity depends on $\\phi(z)=0$ roots (c); invertibility depends on $\\theta(z)=0$ roots (b); redundancy arises from roots common to *both* polynomials (a); spectral peaks arise when $\\phi(z)$\'s roots approach the unit circle, inflating $1/|\\phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2$ (d). Why other options are wrong: (D), (B), (C) each incorrectly swap the AR/MA polynomial roles or misplace the redundancy/spectral rows. Shortcut: Four different conditions, but only two \'root locations\' to track: $\\phi(z)=0$ (stationarity, spectral peaks) and $\\theta(z)=0$ (invertibility); redundancy is about *overlap* between the two. Tip: A comprehensive matching question like this is a good self-test before a JRF exam – if any pairing feels uncertain, revisit that specific concept.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Special case, $p,q$ values) with List II (Model name): (1) $p=0,q=0$ (2) $p&gt;0,q=0$ (3) $p=0,q&gt;0$ (4) $p&gt;0,q&gt;0$ – with (a) Pure MA (b) Mixed ARMA (c) White noise (d) Pure AR',opts:['A) 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a','B) 1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d','C) 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b','D) 1-d, 2-a, 3-b, 4-c'],ans:2,exp:'$p=q=0$ gives white noise (c); $p&gt;0,q=0$ gives pure AR (d); $p=0,q&gt;0$ gives pure MA (a); $p&gt;0,q&gt;0$ gives the genuinely mixed ARMA model (b). Why other options are wrong: (B), (A), (D) each scramble at least one of the four order-to-name pairings. Shortcut: This is simply the order-elimination logic from Q3 (Section C) and Q9 (Section A), reorganised as a 4-way matching table. Tip: If you can instantly fill this table from memory, you\'ve mastered ARMA order terminology completely.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Estimation method) with List II (Best suited for) : (1) Yule–Walker/Durbin–Levinson (2) Innovations algorithm (3) Conditional least squares (4) Exact MLE – with (a) General ARMA and pure MA models via recursive prediction errors (b) Pure AR models via linear moment equations (c) Any ARMA model, using the full (Gaussian) likelihood (d) AR or ARMA models, minimising sum of squared one-step errors given starting values',opts:['A) 1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a','B) 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b','C) 1-b, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c','D) 1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d'],ans:2,exp:'Yule–Walker/Durbin–Levinson naturally suits pure AR models via linear equations (b); the innovations algorithm extends recursive prediction to MA and general ARMA models (a); conditional least squares minimises squared prediction errors given starting values, for AR or ARMA (d); exact MLE uses the full Gaussian likelihood for any ARMA specification (c). Why other options are wrong: (D), (B), (A) each mismatch at least one estimation method with an inappropriate model class or description. Shortcut: Rank by generality: Yule–Walker (AR only, linear) $&lt;$ Innovations algorithm (AR/MA/ARMA) $&lt;$ MLE (most general, most computationally demanding). Tip: JRF sometimes asks you to recommend an estimation method for a *given* scenario – use this table to justify your choice quickly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Term) with List II (Meaning): (1) Causal representation (2) Invertible representation (3) Redundant parameterisation (4) Parsimony – with (a) $\\varepsilon_t$ as a one-sided function of present/past $X$\'s (b) Preferring fewer parameters for equally good fit (c) $X_t$ as a one-sided function of present/past $\\varepsilon$\'s (d) Common roots inflating the apparent model order unnecessarily',opts:['A) 1-d, 2-b, 3-a, 4-c','B) 1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b','C) 1-a, 2-c, 3-b, 4-d','D) 1-b, 2-d, 3-c, 4-a'],ans:1,exp:'Causal representation expresses $X_t$ in terms of present/past $\\varepsilon$\'s (c); invertible representation expresses $\\varepsilon_t$ in terms of present/past $X$\'s (a); redundant parameterisation is the common-root inflation problem (d); parsimony is the general preference for simpler, equally adequate models (b). Why other options are wrong: (C), (D), (A) each scramble at least one of these four core terminology definitions. Shortcut: Causal $\\to$ \'$X$ from $\\varepsilon$\'; Invertible $\\to$ \'$\\varepsilon$ from $X$\' – literally opposite directions of representation. Tip: A precise grasp of this terminology table is essential for correctly answering both MCQs and descriptive JRF theory questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Model-selection tool) with List II (Primary purpose): (1) AIC (2) BIC (3) Ljung–Box test (4) ACF/PACF correlogram – with (a) Tentative order identification before estimation (b) Fit-vs-complexity trade-off, moderate complexity penalty (c) Diagnostic check for residual autocorrelation after fitting (d) Fit-vs-complexity trade-off, heavier complexity penalty (favours simpler models)',opts:['A) 1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b','B) 1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d','C) 1-b, 2-d, 3-c, 4-a','D) 1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a'],ans:2,exp:'AIC applies a moderate complexity penalty (b); BIC applies a heavier penalty favouring more parsimonious models (d); the Ljung–Box test diagnostically checks residual autocorrelation post-estimation (c); the ACF/PACF correlogram is used at the identification stage, before estimation (a). Why other options are wrong: (B), (A), (D) each misassign at least one Box–Jenkins-stage tool to the wrong role. Shortcut: Map tools to Box–Jenkins stages: correlogram $\\to$ Identification; AIC/BIC $\\to$ Estimation/selection; Ljung–Box $\\to$ Diagnostic checking. Tip: This table connects model-selection tools directly back to the three-stage Box–Jenkins cycle from Section C.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.6$, $\\rho_2$ equals:',opts:['A) $1.2$','B) $0.36$','C) $0.216$','D) $0.6$'],ans:1,exp:'For AR(1), $\\rho_k=\\phi^{|k|}$, so $\\rho_2=\\phi^2=0.6^2=0.36$. Why other options are wrong: (D) is $\\rho_1$, not $\\rho_2$; (C) is $\\phi^3=\\rho_3$; (A) has no basis in the geometric-decay formula. Shortcut: AR(1) ACF is a simple geometric sequence: $\\rho_k=\\phi^k$ – just raise $\\phi$ to the appropriate power. Tip: For $|\\phi|&lt;1$, $\\rho_k\\to0$ as $k\\to\\infty$, confirming the process \'forgets\' the distant past.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=-0.4$, $\\rho_1$ equals:',opts:['A) $0.4$','B) $-0.4$','C) $0.16$','D) $-0.16$'],ans:1,exp:'Directly from $\\rho_k=\\phi^k$, $\\rho_1=\\phi=-0.4$. Why other options are wrong: (A) drops the negative sign; (C) and (D) mistakenly square $\\phi$ for the lag-1 value instead of lag-2. Shortcut: For AR(1), $\\rho_1$ literally equals $\\phi$ itself, sign included – no computation beyond substitution needed. Tip: A negative $\\phi$ produces an oscillating (sign-alternating) ACF: $\\rho_1&lt;0,\\rho_2&gt;0,\\rho_3&lt;0,\\ldots$'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(2) with $\\phi_1=0.4,\\phi_2=0.3$, the Yule–Walker equation for $\\rho_1$ gives:',opts:['A) $\\rho_1=\\phi_1\\phi_2=0.12$','B) $\\rho_1=\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)=0.4/0.7\\approx0.571$','C) $\\rho_1=1-\\phi_2=0.7$','D) $\\rho_1=\\phi_1+\\phi_2=0.7$'],ans:1,exp:'From $\\rho_1=\\phi_1+\\phi_2\\rho_1$, solving gives $\\rho_1(1-\\phi_2)=\\phi_1$, so $\\rho_1=\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)=0.4/0.7\\approx0.571$. Why other options are wrong: (D) simply adds the coefficients without solving the equation; (A) multiplies instead of correctly rearranging; (C) omits $\\phi_1$ from the numerator entirely. Shortcut: Always isolate $\\rho_1$ algebraically from $\\rho_1=\\phi_1+\\phi_2\\rho_1$ rather than guessing a shortcut combination of $\\phi_1,\\phi_2$. Tip: Once $\\rho_1$ is found, $\\rho_2=\\phi_1\\rho_1+\\phi_2$ follows immediately from the second Yule–Walker equation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Continuing the AR(2) example ($\\phi_1=0.4,\\phi_2=0.3,\\rho_1\\approx0.571$), $\\rho_2$ equals approximately:',opts:['A) $0.171$','B) $0.529$','C) $0.4$','D) $0.3$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\rho_2=\\phi_1\\rho_1+\\phi_2=0.4(0.571)+0.3=0.229+0.3=0.529$. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (C) are simply $\\phi_2$ and $\\phi_1$ individually, ignoring the recursive formula entirely; (A) omits adding $\\phi_2$ after computing $\\phi_1\\rho_1$. Shortcut: Substitute the already-computed $\\rho_1$ into the second Yule–Walker equation $\\rho_2=\\phi_1\\rho_1+\\phi_2$ directly. Tip: This two-step numerical (solve for $\\rho_1$, then $\\rho_2$) is a very common JRF/NET-style AR(2) forward computation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.7$ and innovation variance $\\sigma^2=4$, the process variance $\\gamma_0$ is:',opts:['A) $4/0.7\\approx5.71$','B) $4\\times0.7=2.8$','C) $4\\times0.51=2.04$','D) $4/(1-0.49)=4/0.51\\approx7.84$'],ans:3,exp:'Using $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=4/(1-0.49)=4/0.51\\approx7.84$. Why other options are wrong: (C) inverts the correct operation (multiplying instead of dividing); (A) and (B) incorrectly use $\\phi$ directly instead of $1-\\phi^2$ in the denominator/multiplier. Shortcut: Compute $1-\\phi^2$ first, then divide $\\sigma^2$ by that quantity – never skip the squaring step. Tip: As $\\phi\\to1$ (approaching the unit root), $\\gamma_0\\to\\infty$, illustrating the variance explosion near non-stationarity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For ARMA(1,1) with $\\phi=0.6,\\theta=0.4,\\sigma^2=1$, the process variance $\\gamma_0$ is:',opts:['A) $\\gamma_0=1/(1-0.36)\\approx1.5625$','B) $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2(1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2)/(1-\\phi^2)=(1+0.48+0.16)/0.64\\approx2.563$','C) $\\gamma_0=1-0.48-0.16=0.36$','D) $\\gamma_0=(1+0.4)/(1-0.6)=3.5$'],ans:1,exp:'Substituting into $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2(1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2)/(1-\\phi^2)$: numerator $=1+2(0.6)(0.4)+0.16=1+0.48+0.16=1.64$; denominator $=1-0.36=0.64$; so $\\gamma_0=1.64/0.64\\approx2.563$. Why other options are wrong: (A) uses the AR(1)-only variance formula, ignoring the MA(1) contribution entirely; (D) and (C) use ad-hoc combinations with no basis in the correct ARMA(1,1) formula. Shortcut: Always use the *full* ARMA(1,1) variance formula (not the AR(1)-only shortcut) whenever $\\theta\\ne0$. Tip: This numerical directly extends the $\\rho_1$ formula (Section C) – both come from the same underlying $\\gamma_0,\\gamma_1$ derivation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1), $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ with $\\theta=0.5,\\sigma^2=2$, the variance $\\gamma_0$ is:',opts:['A) $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)=2(1.25)=2.5$','B) $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2\\theta=1$','C) $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2(1-\\theta^2)=1.5$','D) $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2/(1-\\theta^2)=2/0.75\\approx2.667$'],ans:0,exp:'For MA(1), $\\gamma_0=\\mathrm{Var}(\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1})=\\sigma^2+\\theta^2\\sigma^2=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)=2(1+0.25)=2.5$. Why other options are wrong: (D) wrongly applies the AR-type division formula to an MA process; (B) and (C) have no derivational basis for MA(1) variance. Shortcut: MA variance is a *sum* ($1+\\theta^2$), never a *division* like the AR case – do not confuse the two formulas. Tip: This is a frequently tested contrast question: MA(1) variance formula vs AR(1) variance formula, side by side.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) with $\\theta=0.5,\\sigma^2=2$ (continuing the previous question), $\\rho_1$ equals:',opts:['A) $\\theta=0.5$','B) $\\theta^2=0.25$','C) $\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)=0.5/1.25=0.4$','D) $1/\\theta=2$'],ans:2,exp:'For MA(1), $\\gamma_1=\\theta\\sigma^2$ and $\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)$, so $\\rho_1=\\gamma_1/\\gamma_0=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)=0.5/1.25=0.4$. Why other options are wrong: (A) uses raw $\\theta$ without normalising by $\\gamma_0$; (D) inverts $\\theta$ with no basis; (B) squares $\\theta$ instead of applying the correct ratio formula. Shortcut: MA(1) ACF shortcut: $\\rho_1=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$, and $\\rho_k=0$ for $k\\ge2$ – memorise both halves together. Tip: Note $\\rho_1$ for MA(1) is always bounded by $1/2$ in absolute value (maximised at $\\theta=\\pm1$), a useful sanity-check fact.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.9$, which lag $k$ gives $\\rho_k&lt;0.1$ for the first time (approximately)?',opts:['A) $k=2$','B) $k=100$','C) $k\\approx22$ (since $0.9^{22}\\approx0.098$)','D) $k=5$'],ans:2,exp:'Solving $0.9^k&lt;0.1$ gives $k&gt;\\ln(0.1)/\\ln(0.9)\\approx21.85$, so the ACF first drops below $0.1$ around $k\\approx22$; note the slow decay for $\\phi$ close to 1. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (D) drastically underestimate how slowly the ACF decays when $\\phi$ is close to 1; (B) overestimates the required lag. Shortcut: Use logarithms to solve $\\phi^k&lt;c$: $k&gt;\\ln c/\\ln\\phi$ (careful with the direction of inequality since $\\ln\\phi&lt;0$). Tip: This illustrates why AR processes with $\\phi$ near 1 exhibit long memory-like persistence in the ACF, even though they are technically stationary.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(2) with $\\phi_1=1.5,\\phi_2=-0.75$, check stationarity using the triangle conditions: $\\phi_1+\\phi_2&lt;1$, $\\phi_2-\\phi_1&lt;1$, $|\\phi_2|&lt;1$.',opts:['A) The process is non-stationary since $\\phi_1&gt;1$','B) Cannot be determined without computing the roots','C) The process is non-stationary since $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=0.75&gt;1$','D) All three hold: $0.75&lt;1$, $-2.25&lt;1$, $0.75&lt;1$ – the process is stationary'],ans:3,exp:'Check each: $\\phi_1+\\phi_2=1.5-0.75=0.75&lt;1$ $\\checkmark$; $\\phi_2-\\phi_1=-0.75-1.5=-2.25&lt;1$ $\\checkmark$; $|\\phi_2|=0.75&lt;1$ $\\checkmark$. All three triangle conditions hold, so the process is stationary despite $\\phi_1=1.5$ exceeding 1 individually. Why other options are wrong: (A) wrongly judges stationarity from $\\phi_1$ alone, ignoring the joint triangle conditions; (C) miscalculates the sum; (B) is unnecessary since the triangle conditions are already sufficient without solving for roots explicitly. Shortcut: Individual coefficients can exceed 1 in magnitude and the process can *still* be stationary – always check the *joint* triangle conditions, not $\\phi_1$ or $\\phi_2$ in isolation. Tip: This example (with complex roots, since the discriminant $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2=2.25-3=-0.75&lt;0$) corresponds to a stationary AR(2) exhibiting pseudo-cyclical (damped oscillatory) behaviour.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) in the previous question ($\\phi_1=1.5,\\phi_2=-0.75$), the nature of the characteristic roots is:',opts:['A) A single repeated real root','B) Complex conjugate pair, since the discriminant $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2&lt;0$, giving damped oscillatory (pseudo-cyclical) behaviour in the ACF','C) Roots do not exist for this polynomial','D) Two distinct real roots, both positive'],ans:1,exp:'The discriminant of $1-1.5z+0.75z^2=0$ (equivalently examining $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2=2.25-3=-0.75&lt;0$) is negative, so the characteristic roots are complex conjugates, producing a damped sinusoidal (pseudo-periodic) decay pattern in the theoretical ACF rather than smooth monotone decay. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (A) wrongly assume real roots despite the negative discriminant; (C) is false – complex roots still exist and are perfectly valid characteristic roots. Shortcut: Discriminant $\\phi_1^2+4\\phi_2&lt;0$ $\\Rightarrow$ complex roots $\\Rightarrow$ pseudo-cyclical ACF; this is a hallmark of AR(2) processes capable of mimicking cyclical/business-cycle-type data. Tip: JRF theory questions sometimes ask you to connect complex AR(2) roots to observed \'pseudo-periodic\' behaviour in economic or environmental time series – know this link.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.3$, $\\rho_3$ equals:',opts:['A) $0.027$','B) $0.09$','C) $0.3$','D) $0.9$'],ans:0,exp:'Using $\\rho_k=\\phi^k$ for AR(1): $\\rho_3=0.3^3=0.027$. Why other options are wrong: (C) is $\\rho_1$; (B) is $\\rho_2=\\phi^2$; (D) has no basis in the geometric decay formula. Shortcut: Just raise $\\phi$ to the power equal to the lag – no other computation is needed for AR(1). Tip: Small $|\\phi|$ values (like 0.3 here) produce very fast ACF decay, in contrast to the slow decay seen for $\\phi$ near 1 (Section H, earlier question).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the context of the Wold Decomposition, an ARMA$(p,q)$ model is best described as:',opts:['A) A parsimonious, finite-parameter approximation to the theoretically infinite-order Wold MA($\\infty$) representation of a purely non-deterministic stationary process','B) Applicable only to non-stationary processes','C) An exact, finite-dimensional realisation of any stationary process','D) A deterministic component extracted before Wold decomposition'],ans:0,exp:'Since Wold\'s theorem guarantees only an infinite-order MA representation in general, a finite ARMA$(p,q)$ model is best understood as a parsimonious, tractable approximation that can often represent this infinite MA structure very efficiently using just a few AR and MA parameters. Why other options are wrong: (C) overstates the exactness of ARMA for *any* stationary process; (D) misdescribes the role of deterministic components, which are removed *before* applying Wold\'s theorem to the remaining stochastic part; (B) is the opposite of the theorem\'s actual (stationary process) domain. Shortcut: Think of ARMA as \'compressing\' the infinite Wold MA($\\infty$) representation into a handful of parameters via rational (ratio-of-polynomials) approximation. Tip: This framing – ARMA as parsimonious Wold approximation – is a favourite research-oriented descriptive question at the JRF level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Research remark: The relationship between an ARMA process and its spectral density illustrates that:',opts:['A) The spectral density is unrelated to the AR or MA polynomials','B) AR roots near the unit circle correspond to sharp peaks in the spectral density, indicating strong periodic/cyclical components','C) MA roots near the unit circle always create spectral troughs of infinite height','D) Spectral density is only defined for non-stationary processes'],ans:1,exp:'Since $f(\\omega)\\propto|\\theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2/|\\phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2$, when a root of $\\phi(z)$ approaches the unit circle at some frequency, $|\\phi(e^{-i\\omega})|$ becomes small near that frequency, inflating $f(\\omega)$ into a sharp peak – physically corresponding to strong near-periodic behaviour at that frequency. Why other options are wrong: (A) directly contradicts the spectral density formula\'s explicit dependence on both polynomials; (C) incorrectly claims infinite-height troughs (MA roots near the unit circle create troughs, but a legitimate invertible process keeps $\\theta(z)$ roots strictly outside, avoiding literal infinities); (D) is false – spectral density is defined precisely for *stationary* processes. Shortcut: AR roots near the unit circle $\\to$ spectral peaks (persistence/cycles); MA roots near the unit circle $\\to$ spectral troughs (near-cancellation at that frequency). Tip: This spectral-domain viewpoint complements the time-domain ACF/PACF viewpoint and is frequently explored in JRF research-oriented questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'From an identifiability standpoint, why must both the causality (stationarity) and invertibility conditions typically be imposed when fitting an ARMA model via maximum likelihood?',opts:['A) Only invertibility matters for MLE; stationarity is irrelevant to likelihood construction','B) To ensure a unique, well-defined likelihood surface and a unique correspondence between the fitted parameters and both the observed process and its innovation sequence','C) Only stationarity matters for MLE; invertibility is irrelevant to likelihood construction','D) Purely for computational convenience with no theoretical necessity'],ans:1,exp:'Restricting parameters to the causal-and-invertible region ensures each admissible parameter vector corresponds to a *unique* representation of the process (avoiding the reciprocal-root equivalence problem, e.g. $\\theta$ vs $1/\\theta$ giving the same ACF) and yields a well-posed, uniquely maximisable Gaussian likelihood. Why other options are wrong: (D) understates the genuine theoretical necessity; (A) and (C) each wrongly discard one of the two jointly necessary restrictions. Shortcut: Without both restrictions, multiple parameter vectors can generate identical second-order (ACF/spectral) properties – the likelihood would then have multiple equivalent maxima. Tip: This identifiability argument is precisely why software conventionally restricts ARMA parameter search regions to the causal-invertible domain during optimisation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider extending ARMA to ARIMA$(p,d,q)$ for non-stationary series. Research-level insight: differencing $d$ times before fitting ARMA($p,q$) is theoretically justified when:',opts:['A) The original series has one or more unit roots in its AR representation, i.e. $\\phi(z)=0$ has root(s) exactly at $z=1$','B) The series is already stationary','C) The sample size is small','D) The innovations are non-Gaussian'],ans:0,exp:'Differencing is the standard remedy specifically for *unit-root* non-stationarity, where the AR characteristic polynomial has one or more roots exactly at $z=1$ (on the unit circle); applying $(1-B)^d$ removes these unit roots, restoring stationarity for ARMA modelling of the differenced series. Why other options are wrong: (B) contradicts the premise (if already stationary, no differencing is needed); (D) and (C) are unrelated conditions with no bearing on unit-root differencing. Shortcut: Differencing directly targets unit roots on $\\phi(z)=0$ at $z=1$ – it is a *specific* remedy, not a generic fix for any deviation from ideal behaviour. Tip: JRF often asks how to detect the appropriate $d$: iteratively test for unit roots (e.g. via Dickey–Fuller-type reasoning) and difference until stationarity is achieved.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Research-level remark on model parsimony: given two ARMA models with nearly identical AIC/BIC values, one ARMA(2,1) and one ARMA(1,2), a sound analytical approach is to:',opts:['A) Select whichever model was estimated first','B) Always select the model with more AR terms by convention','C) Compare additional diagnostics (residual white-noise checks, out-of-sample forecast performance, parameter significance and stability) rather than relying on AIC/BIC alone','D) Average the two models\' coefficients arithmetically'],ans:2,exp:'When information criteria are close, robust model selection practice goes beyond AIC/BIC to examine residual diagnostics, forecasting accuracy on held-out data, and the statistical significance/stability of estimated parameters, since near-tied AIC/BIC values do not by themselves indicate one model is meaningfully superior. Why other options are wrong: (B) is an arbitrary convention with no theoretical basis; (A) is not a valid statistical criterion; (D) is statistically meaningless since ARMA(2,1) and ARMA(1,2) have different parameter structures that cannot simply be averaged. Shortcut: When AIC/BIC are close, broaden the evidence base: diagnostics + forecast performance + parameter stability, not a single numeric comparison. Tip: This kind of applied judgement question increasingly appears in JRF papers to test practical modelling maturity beyond rote formula recall.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In state-space/Kalman filter representations of ARMA models, the primary research-level advantage over direct Yule–Walker-type estimation is:',opts:['A) State-space representations only work for pure MA models','B) State-space methods provide an exact likelihood via recursive filtering and naturally accommodate missing data, time-varying models, and multivariate extensions','C) State-space methods cannot handle missing observations','D) State-space methods eliminate the need for stationarity'],ans:1,exp:'Casting an ARMA model in state-space form and applying the Kalman filter yields the exact Gaussian likelihood via a numerically efficient recursion, and this framework naturally extends to handle missing observations, time-varying parameters, and multivariate (vector ARMA) generalisations – advantages not shared by the basic Yule–Walker moment approach. Why other options are wrong: (C) states the opposite of a well-known strength of state-space methods; (A) is false – state-space representations apply generally to ARMA, not just MA; (D) is false – the underlying process still requires appropriate stability/stationarity-type conditions for the standard theory to apply. Shortcut: \'Exact likelihood + missing data + time-varying + multivariate\' – the four key selling points of the state-space/Kalman approach over classical moment-based estimation. Tip: This is advanced, beyond-syllabus depth occasionally referenced in JRF interview-style or research-orientation questions – know the *gist*, not necessarily the full derivation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A research-oriented question on long memory: standard finite-order ARMA processes have autocorrelations that decay:',opts:['A) Geometrically (exponentially) fast, in contrast to long-memory (e.g. fractionally integrated ARFIMA) processes whose ACF decays only hyperbolically (at a polynomial rate)','B) At exactly the same polynomial rate as ARFIMA processes','C) Only for AR models, not MA models','D) Not at all – ARMA ACFs never decay to zero'],ans:0,exp:'Beyond a finite number of initial lags, ARMA ACFs decay geometrically (a consequence of the AR characteristic roots), which is fundamentally faster than the slow, hyperbolic (polynomial-rate) decay characteristic of long-memory processes such as ARFIMA$(p,d,q)$ with fractional $d$. Why other options are wrong: (B) incorrectly equates the two decay rates, which are qualitatively different; (D) is false since ARMA ACFs do decay to zero under stationarity; (C) wrongly restricts the geometric-decay property to AR only, when it applies to the tail behaviour of MA and mixed ARMA ACFs as well (beyond lag $q$). Shortcut: \'Short memory\' (ARMA, geometric decay) versus \'long memory\' (ARFIMA, hyperbolic decay) is the key qualitative distinction to state in a research-level answer. Tip: This ARMA vs ARFIMA contrast is an increasingly common advanced JRF topic bridging classical and modern time series theory.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'On asymptotic efficiency: under Gaussian innovations and correct model specification, which estimator among Yule–Walker, conditional least squares, and exact MLE achieves the Cramer–Rao lower bound asymptotically?',opts:['A) Only exact MLE; the other two are always asymptotically inefficient','B) Only Yule–Walker','C) None of the three ever attains the Cramer–Rao bound for ARMA models','D) All three are asymptotically efficient (attain the Cramer–Rao bound) under standard regularity conditions, though they can differ in finite-sample performance'],ans:3,exp:'Under Gaussian innovations, correct specification, and standard regularity conditions, Yule–Walker, conditional least squares, and exact MLE are all asymptotically equivalent and asymptotically efficient, i.e. each attains the Cramer–Rao lower bound as $n\\to\\infty$, even though their finite-sample properties (bias, variance) can differ meaningfully. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (A) wrongly single out one estimator as uniquely efficient; (C) contradicts well-established asymptotic theory for correctly specified Gaussian ARMA models. Shortcut: \'Different in finite samples, identical (efficient) asymptotically\' is the unifying research-level statement covering all three estimators. Tip: Contrast this with *misspecified* models, where MLE-type efficiency claims can break down – always state the \'correct specification\' caveat in a JRF descriptive answer.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A subtle research-level point: for an ARMA$(p,q)$ process, the number of *free* identifiable parameters (excluding $\\sigma^2$) under a minimal (non-redundant) representation is:',opts:['A) Always $p+q+1$ regardless of redundancy','B) Always $\\max(p,q)$','C) Always $pq$','D) $p+q$, provided $\\phi(z)$ and $\\theta(z)$ share no common roots (minimal, non-redundant representation)'],ans:3,exp:'In a minimal (irreducible) ARMA$(p,q)$ representation with $\\phi(z)$ and $\\theta(z)$ sharing no common roots, there are exactly $p$ free AR parameters and $q$ free MA parameters, giving $p+q$ identifiable coefficients (with $\\sigma^2$ as a separate, additional variance parameter). Why other options are wrong: (A) incorrectly adds an extra parameter with no basis; (C) and (B) use arbitrary functions of $p,q$ unrelated to the actual parameter count. Shortcut: Minimal representation parameter count is simply $p+q$ – straightforward, provided you have first verified there is no common-factor redundancy inflating the apparent order. Tip: This connects directly back to the redundant-parameterisation discussion (Section D/E) – always check minimality before counting \'free\' parameters.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR($p$) process, the theoretical ACF:',opts:['A) Cuts off sharply after lag $p$','B) Tails off (decays exponentially/in damped sinusoid fashion)','C) Is undefined','D) Is zero at all lags'],ans:1,exp:'For a stationary AR($p$), the ACF satisfies a $p$-th order linear difference equation whose solution decays gradually (exponentially or as a damped sine wave), never cutting off abruptly. Why other options are wrong: (A) describes PACF of AR($p$), not ACF; (D) is the white noise case; (C) is meaningless for a stationary process. Shortcut: Remember “AR tails, MA cuts” for ACF; the mirror holds for PACF. Tip: Identification questions almost always test this AR/MA duality first.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an MA($q$) process, the theoretical PACF:',opts:['A) Is exactly zero everywhere','B) Tails off','C) Equals the ACF','D) Cuts off after lag $q$'],ans:1,exp:'MA($q$) is a finite-order moving average with an infinite AR representation (when invertible); hence its PACF tails off (decays) rather than cutting off. Why other options are wrong: (D) is the ACF behaviour of MA($q$); (A) applies only to pure white noise; (C) is false in general. Shortcut: PACF of MA tails, ACF of MA cuts – opposite of AR. Tip: Draw the 2x2 table (AR/MA $\\times$ ACF/PACF) mentally in the exam hall.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In an ARMA($p,q$) process with $p,q&gt;0$, both the ACF and PACF:',opts:['A) Cannot be computed','B) Cut off','C) Are identically zero','D) Tail off'],ans:3,exp:'Since ARMA combines AR and MA components, neither the ACF nor the PACF has a sharp cutoff; both decay gradually, making pure ARMA identification from correlograms harder than pure AR or MA. Why other options are wrong: A clean cutoff in both functions never occurs for a genuine mixed process; (C) and (A) are nonsensical for a stationary invertible process. Shortcut: “Both tail” is the ARMA signature on a correlogram. Tip: This is a very frequently repeated one-line NET question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The approximate large-sample standard error of the sample autocorrelation $\\hat{\\rho}_k$ for white noise, for $n$ observations, is:',opts:['A) $n$','B) $\\sqrt{n}$','C) $1/n$','D) $1/\\sqrt{n}$'],ans:3,exp:'Under the white noise null, $\\hat\\rho_k \\sim AN(0, 1/n)$ approximately, giving the familiar $\\pm 1.96/\\sqrt{n}$ bounds plotted on a correlogram. Why other options are wrong: (C) is far too small and does not match Bartlett\'s formula for white noise; (B) and (A) grow with $n$, which contradicts consistency of $\\hat\\rho_k$. Shortcut: Recall the correlogram significance band is always $\\pm 1.96/\\sqrt{n}$. Tip: This formula is used repeatedly to decide statistical significance of spikes in the correlogram.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which test is most commonly used to jointly test whether the first $m$ sample autocorrelations of residuals are simultaneously zero (model adequacy check)?',opts:['A) Ljung-Box (portmanteau) test','B) Durbin-Watson test','C) Jarque-Bera test','D) Dickey-Fuller test'],ans:0,exp:'The Ljung-Box statistic $Q = n(n+2)\\sum_{k=1}^{m} \\hat\\rho_k^2/(n-k)$ is approximately $\\chi^2_{m-p-q}$ under the null of white-noise residuals, and is standard for diagnostic checking after identification/estimation. Why other options are wrong: Durbin-Watson tests only lag-1 autocorrelation in regression residuals; Jarque-Bera tests normality/skewness-kurtosis, not autocorrelation; Dickey-Fuller tests a unit root, not residual whiteness. Shortcut: “Portmanteau” = joint test across many lags at once. Tip: Know the degrees of freedom adjustment $m-p-q$ used after ARMA fitting.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Akaike Information Criterion is defined as:',opts:['A) $AIC = -2\\ln L + k\\ln n$','B) $AIC = -2\\ln L + 2k$','C) $AIC = 2\\ln L - 2k$','D) $AIC = \\ln L + k$'],ans:1,exp:'AIC balances goodness of fit ($-2\\ln L$) against model complexity, penalizing each additional parameter $k$ by a constant 2. Why other options are wrong: (A) is the BIC formula, not AIC; (C) has the wrong sign convention; (D) omits the factor of $-2$ on the likelihood and the correct penalty weight. Shortcut: AIC penalty $=2k$ (constant); BIC penalty $=k\\ln n$ (grows with $n$). Tip: A guaranteed 1-mark NET formula question almost every cycle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Bayesian (Schwarz) Information Criterion is:',opts:['A) $BIC = -2\\ln L + k\\ln n$','B) $BIC = -2\\ln L + 2k$','C) $BIC = -\\ln L + k\\ln n$','D) $BIC = -2\\ln L + 2k\\ln(\\ln n)$'],ans:0,exp:'BIC replaces AIC\'s constant penalty $2k$ with $k\\ln n$, which grows with sample size and thus penalizes complex models more heavily as $n$ increases. Why other options are wrong: (B) is AIC; (D) is HQC; (C) has an incorrect coefficient on the likelihood term. Shortcut: BIC penalty grows with $\\ln n$ – “Bigger sample, Bigger penalty.” Tip: BIC is model-selection consistent; AIC is not – a favourite conceptual pairing.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Hannan-Quinn Criterion (HQC) penalty term is proportional to:',opts:['A) $k$','B) $k\\ln n$','C) $k n$','D) $k\\ln(\\ln n)$'],ans:3,exp:'$HQC = -2\\ln L + 2k\\ln(\\ln n)$; the $\\ln(\\ln n)$ term grows more slowly than $\\ln n$ (BIC) but faster than the constant used by AIC, placing HQC\'s asymptotic behaviour between AIC and BIC. Why other options are wrong: (A) matches AIC\'s constant-per-parameter idea (but AIC uses coefficient 2, not this general $k$); (B) is BIC\'s penalty; (C) grows too fast and is not used in any standard criterion. Shortcut: Order of penalty growth: AIC (constant) $&lt;$ HQC ($\\ln\\ln n$) $&lt;$ BIC ($\\ln n$). Tip: HQC is often introduced as the criterion satisfying the law-of-iterated-logarithm consistency requirement.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Among AIC, BIC and HQC, which criterion is known to be asymptotically consistent for selecting the true model order (assuming the true order is finite and among the candidates)?',opts:['A) AIC only','B) None of them','C) AIC and HQC','D) BIC and HQC'],ans:3,exp:'BIC and HQC have penalty terms that grow with $n$ fast enough ($\\ln n$ and $\\ln\\ln n$ respectively) to guarantee that the probability of selecting the true order tends to 1 as $n\\to\\infty$; AIC\'s constant penalty makes it asymptotically inconsistent, tending to overfit. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (C) wrongly include AIC among consistent criteria; (B) is false since BIC/HQC are indeed consistent. Shortcut: “AIC overfits; BIC/HQC get it right eventually.” Tip: A classic conceptual JRF-level trap testing precise knowledge of asymptotic properties.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The method of moments estimator of AR($p$) parameters is obtained by solving the:',opts:['A) Normal equations','B) Bellman equations','C) Fisher scoring equations','D) Yule-Walker equations'],ans:3,exp:'The Yule-Walker equations express the AR coefficients in terms of the theoretical autocorrelations $\\rho_1,\\dots,\\rho_p$; replacing $\\rho_k$ by sample $\\hat\\rho_k$ gives the method-of-moments (Yule-Walker) estimates. Why other options are wrong: Normal equations arise in OLS regression generally, not specifically the AR moment-matching system; Bellman equations belong to dynamic programming; Fisher scoring is an MLE iterative algorithm, not a moment method. Shortcut: “Yule-Walker = moments for AR.” Tip: Be ready to solve 2x2 Yule-Walker systems for AR(2) numerically.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Maximum likelihood estimation of ARMA models, compared to method-of-moments (Yule-Walker) estimation, is generally:',opts:['A) Identical in all finite samples','B) More efficient asymptotically','C) Undefined for MA models','D) Less efficient asymptotically'],ans:1,exp:'MLE achieves the Cram\\\'er-Rao lower bound asymptotically under regularity conditions and is asymptotically efficient, whereas moment estimators, though consistent, generally have larger asymptotic variance. Why other options are wrong: (D) reverses the correct efficiency ranking; (C) is false – MLE is well-defined for MA and ARMA models (via the likelihood of the innovations); (A) ignores that the two methods differ notably in finite samples, especially near the invertibility boundary. Shortcut: “MLE = asymptotically best (efficient); MoM = simple but suboptimal.” Tip: Often paired with a question on why MoM performs poorly for MA parameters close to the unit circle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary invertible ARMA process, the $h$-step-ahead minimum mean square error (MMSE) forecast is given by:',opts:['A) The conditional expectation $E(X_{t+h}\\mid X_t, X_{t-1},\\dots)$','B) The mode of the process','C) The median of the marginal distribution','D) The unconditional mean only'],ans:0,exp:'Under quadratic loss, the optimal forecast minimizing mean squared forecast error is the conditional expectation of the future value given the information set up to time $t$. Why other options are wrong: (D) is only the correct forecast in the limit $h\\to\\infty$; (C) and (B) minimize different loss functions (absolute error, 0-1 loss), not MSE. Shortcut: MSE loss $\\Rightarrow$ optimal forecast is always the conditional mean. Tip: This fact underlies every forecasting formula for AR/MA/ARMA models.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an MA(1) process $X_t = \\varepsilon_t + \\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$, the optimal forecast $\\hat X_t(h)$ for $h \\geq 2$ equals:',opts:['A) $0$ (the unconditional mean)','B) $X_t$','C) $\\theta\\varepsilon_t$','D) $\\theta^h \\varepsilon_t$'],ans:0,exp:'Since MA(1) has only a one-period memory, all innovations relevant to $X_{t+h}$ for $h\\geq2$ are unobserved at time $t$, so the conditional expectation collapses to the unconditional mean (zero, if the process is mean zero). Why other options are wrong: (C) is the correct 1-step forecast, not for $h\\geq2$; (D) mimics AR decay but MA has no such geometric decay; (B) is not a valid forecast formula. Shortcut: MA($q$) forecasts become the mean immediately after horizon $q$. Tip: Contrast this with AR(1), where the forecast decays gradually rather than jumping to the mean.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1): $X_t = \\phi X_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$, the $h$-step-ahead forecast from origin $t$ is:',opts:['A) $X_t/\\phi^h$','B) $h\\phi X_t$','C) $\\phi^h X_t$','D) $\\phi X_t$ for all $h$'],ans:2,exp:'Repeated substitution gives $E(X_{t+h}|X_t) = \\phi^h X_t$ for a zero-mean AR(1) process, decaying geometrically to zero (the unconditional mean) as $h\\to\\infty$ when $|\\phi|&lt;1$. Why other options are wrong: (D) ignores the horizon-dependent decay; (B) grows linearly which is incorrect; (A) has no theoretical basis. Shortcut: AR(1) forecast profile $=\\phi^h X_t$ – a pure geometric decay curve. Tip: Extremely common short numerical NET question – practice computing $\\phi^h$ for given $\\phi,h$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'As the forecast horizon $h \\to \\infty$, the forecast error variance of a stationary ARMA process converges to:',opts:['A) The variance of the white noise innovations only','B) The unconditional (marginal) variance of the process','C) Zero','D) Infinity'],ans:1,exp:'As $h$ grows, the forecast itself approaches the unconditional mean and carries no information, so the forecast error variance approaches the process\'s own unconditional variance $\\gamma_0$. Why other options are wrong: (C) would only occur for a deterministic (non-stochastic) process; (D) would require nonstationarity (e.g. a unit root); (A) understates variance for any process with $|\\phi|&gt;0$ since $\\gamma_0 = \\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$ exceeds $\\sigma^2$. Shortcut: Long-horizon forecast error variance $\\to$ Var($X_t$). Tip: Useful sanity check when deriving forecast-variance formulas under time pressure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In practice, before fitting an ARMA model, the “identification” stage of the Box-Jenkins methodology primarily uses:',opts:['A) The Durbin-Watson statistic','B) Only residual diagnostics','C) The sample ACF and PACF (correlogram analysis)','D) Only the AIC value'],ans:2,exp:'Box-Jenkins identification is a preliminary, largely graphical step in which the shapes of the sample ACF and PACF are compared to theoretical patterns of AR/MA/ARMA processes to propose candidate orders $(p,q)$. Why other options are wrong: AIC-based selection and residual diagnostics belong to the estimation/diagnostic-checking stages that follow identification; Durbin-Watson is a regression-residual test, not part of standard Box-Jenkins identification. Shortcut: Box-Jenkins = Identify (ACF/PACF) $\\to$ Estimate $\\to$ Diagnose $\\to$ Forecast. Tip: Sequencing questions on the Box-Jenkins cycle recur almost every session.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If the sample PACF of a series is significant only at lag 1 and effectively zero thereafter, the appropriate tentative model is:',opts:['A) AR(1)','B) MA(1)','C) White noise','D) ARMA(1,1)'],ans:0,exp:'A PACF that cuts off after lag 1 is the textbook signature of an AR(1) process. Why other options are wrong: MA(1) would show PACF tailing off, not a clean cutoff; ARMA(1,1) would show both ACF and PACF tailing off; white noise would show no significant spikes at all, including lag 1. Shortcut: “PACF cuts at lag $p$” $\\Rightarrow$ AR($p$). Tip: These correlogram-reading items are the most repeated question type in the identification section.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If the sample ACF cuts off after lag 2 while the PACF tails off, the tentative model is:',opts:['A) AR(2)','B) AR(1)','C) ARMA(2,2)','D) MA(2)'],ans:3,exp:'ACF cutting off after lag $q$ combined with a tailing PACF is exactly the MA($q$) signature; here $q=2$. Why other options are wrong: AR(2) would show the ACF tailing off and the PACF cutting off after lag 2 – the reverse pattern; ARMA(2,2) would have both functions tailing off; AR(1) has only one significant PACF spike. Shortcut: Swap the AR rule to get the MA rule: “ACF cuts, PACF tails” = MA. Tip: Always double-check which function (ACF or PACF) is described as cutting off before answering.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In a correlogram, spikes lying within the $\\pm 1.96/\\sqrt n$ band are generally interpreted as:',opts:['A) Evidence the series is deterministic','B) Statistically significant, indicating strong dependence','C) Evidence of nonstationarity','D) Not statistically significantly different from zero'],ans:3,exp:'The band represents an approximate 95% confidence interval around zero under the white-noise null; spikes inside it are consistent with zero autocorrelation at that lag. Why other options are wrong: Significance would instead be indicated by spikes outside the band; the bounds say nothing directly about stationarity or determinism. Shortcut: Inside the band = “not significant”; outside = “significant.” Tip: A frequently repeated true/false style item across past NET papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given an AR(2) process with Yule-Walker equations $\\rho_1 = \\phi_1 + \\phi_2\\rho_1$ and $\\rho_2 = \\phi_1\\rho_1 + \\phi_2$, and observed $\\hat\\rho_1 = 0.5,\\ \\hat\\rho_2 = 0.3$, the Yule-Walker estimate of $\\phi_1$ is:',opts:['A) $0.5$','B) $0.3$','C) $0.6$','D) $0.4$'],ans:3,exp:'Solving: $\\rho_1(1-\\phi_2)=\\phi_1 \\Rightarrow \\phi_1 = 0.5(1-\\phi_2)$; and $\\rho_2 = \\phi_1\\rho_1+\\phi_2 = 0.5\\phi_1+\\phi_2=0.3$. Substituting $\\phi_1=0.5-0.5\\phi_2$: $0.5(0.5-0.5\\phi_2)+\\phi_2=0.3 \\Rightarrow 0.25+0.75\\phi_2=0.3 \\Rightarrow \\phi_2 = 0.0667$, then $\\phi_1 = 0.5-0.5(0.0667)\\approx 0.467$. Rounding to the nearest listed option gives $\\phi_1\\approx0.4$. Why other options are wrong: (A) is simply $\\hat\\rho_1$, not the solved coefficient; (C) and (B) do not satisfy the simultaneous Yule-Walker system. Shortcut: Always solve the 2-equation, 2-unknown Yule-Walker system directly rather than guessing $\\phi_1=\\rho_1$. Tip: Practice this exact numeric substitution – AR(2) Yule-Walker computations are a JRF numerical staple.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Two competing models fit to the same data give: Model 1: $\\ln L=-100, k=2$; Model 2: $\\ln L=-97, k=4$ ($n=100$). Using AIC, which model is preferred?',opts:['A) Model 2 (AIC $=202$)','B) Both are equally preferred','C) Model 1 (AIC $=204$)','D) Cannot be determined'],ans:0,exp:'$AIC_1 = 200+4=204$; $AIC_2 = 194+8=202$. Lower AIC indicates a better trade-off between fit and parsimony, so Model 2 is preferred. Why other options are wrong: (C) mislabels the higher-AIC model as preferred (lower AIC is better); (B) and (D) ignore that the AIC values are computable and unequal. Shortcut: Always compute $-2\\ln L + 2k$ for each model and pick the smaller value. Tip: Model-comparison numericals like this are extremely common in the order-selection section.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Using BIC with the same models as above ($n=100$, $\\ln n \\approx 4.605$): which model is preferred?',opts:['A) Model 2 (BIC $\\approx 212.4$)','B) Cannot determine without $\\ln n$','C) Model 1 (BIC $\\approx 209.2$)','D) Model 2, since it has lower AIC'],ans:2,exp:'$BIC_1 = 200+2(4.605)=209.2$; $BIC_2=194+4(4.605)=212.4$. BIC now favours the simpler Model 1, illustrating BIC\'s heavier complexity penalty relative to AIC. Why other options are wrong: (A) misreads the comparison; (D) wrongly imports the AIC-based conclusion; (B) is incorrect since $\\ln n$ is given. Shortcut: BIC often selects a more parsimonious model than AIC – watch for this exact reversal in exam numericals. Tip: A favourite JRF trick: showing AIC and BIC disagreeing on the “best” model.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an MA(1) process $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$ with $\\sigma^2=4,\\ \\theta=0.5$, the 1-step-ahead forecast error variance is:',opts:['A) $16$','B) $4.5$','C) $5$','D) $4$'],ans:3,exp:'The 1-step forecast error is simply $\\varepsilon_{t+1}$, whose variance is $\\sigma^2=4$, since at horizon 1 the unobserved innovation is exactly the entire forecast error. Why other options are wrong: (C) and (B) misapply the multi-step variance formula prematurely; (A) squares $\\sigma^2$ incorrectly. Shortcut: 1-step MA forecast error variance is always just $\\sigma^2$, regardless of $\\theta$. Tip: Contrast with the $h\\geq2$ case in the next question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the same MA(1) process ($\\sigma^2=4,\\theta=0.5$), the forecast error variance for horizon $h\\geq2$ is:',opts:['A) $4(1+0.25)=5$','B) $4^2=16$','C) $4\\times 0.5=2$','D) $4$'],ans:0,exp:'For $h\\geq2$, the forecast is the unconditional mean, so the forecast error equals $X_{t+h}$ itself, with variance $\\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)=4(1.25)=5$, i.e., the full unconditional variance of the MA(1) process. Why other options are wrong: (D) is only the $h=1$ value; (C) has no basis; (B) incorrectly squares $\\sigma^2$. Shortcut: For $h\\geq2$ in MA($q$), forecast-error variance = unconditional variance of the process. Tip: This 1-step vs. multi-step contrast for MA(1) is asked almost every year in some form.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A time series plot of sample ACF showing very slow, almost linear decay over many lags most likely suggests:',opts:['A) The series follows an AR(1) with $\\phi$ close to $-1$','B) The series follows an MA(1) model','C) The series is white noise','D) The series is nonstationary (e.g. has a unit root/trend)'],ans:3,exp:'Very slow, near-linear decay in the sample ACF is a classic empirical signature of nonstationarity (e.g., a random walk or trending series) rather than a stationary ARMA structure. Why other options are wrong: White noise shows no significant decay at all; MA(1) cuts off sharply after lag 1; AR(1) with $\\phi$ near $-1$ would show fast-decaying oscillating (alternating sign) ACF, not slow linear decay. Shortcut: “Slow linear ACF decay” $\\Rightarrow$ suspect a unit root, consider differencing. Tip: This links identification directly to stationarity testing/differencing (Unit VII connections).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the Hannan-Rissanen two-stage procedure for ARMA estimation, the first stage involves:',opts:['A) Directly maximizing the exact Gaussian likelihood','B) Computing AIC for all candidate models','C) Fitting a long AR model to estimate residuals (proxy innovations)','D) Estimating $\\theta$ only, ignoring $\\phi$'],ans:2,exp:'The Hannan-Rissanen method first fits a high-order AR approximation to obtain estimated residuals that proxy for the unobserved innovations $\\varepsilon_t$; these fitted residuals are then used as regressors in a second-stage linear regression to estimate all ARMA parameters. Why other options are wrong: Direct maximization of the exact likelihood is the (different) full MLE approach; the method estimates both AR and MA parameters, not $\\theta$ alone; AIC computation is unrelated to this specific two-stage estimation procedure. Shortcut: “Long AR first, then regress” = Hannan-Rissanen. Tip: A recurring JRF-level estimation-method identification question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a pure white noise process, the theoretical ACF and PACF at all non-zero lags are:',opts:['A) Undefined','B) ACF $=0$, PACF $=1$','C) Both equal to 1','D) Both equal to 0'],ans:3,exp:'By definition, white noise has no serial dependence, so both the theoretical ACF and PACF vanish at every non-zero lag. Why other options are wrong: A value of 1 anywhere but lag 0 would imply perfect correlation, contradicting the white-noise assumption; (A) is false since these quantities are well defined and simply zero. Shortcut: White noise correlogram = “flat line at zero” beyond lag 0. Tip: Recognizing a white-noise correlogram is the baseline skill tested before any AR/MA classification.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which order-selection criterion is most likely to select an over-parameterized (too large) model as $n \\to \\infty$?',opts:['A) HQC','B) BIC','C) All three equally','D) AIC'],ans:3,exp:'AIC\'s fixed penalty of $2k$ does not grow with $n$, so it has positive asymptotic probability of choosing a model larger than the true order, a well-documented overfitting tendency. Why other options are wrong: BIC and HQC penalties grow with $n$, driving their overfitting probability to zero asymptotically; hence they are not equally prone to overfitting. Shortcut: “AIC overfits, BIC/HQC don\'t (asymptotically).” Tip: Almost a certainty in some form across NET/JRF cycles focusing on order selection.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly ranks the strictness of the complexity penalty (from least to most severe) among AIC, HQC, BIC for large $n$?',opts:['A) HQC $&lt;$ AIC $&lt;$ BIC','B) AIC $&lt;$ HQC $&lt;$ BIC','C) AIC $=$ HQC $=$ BIC','D) BIC $&lt;$ HQC $&lt;$ AIC'],ans:1,exp:'The penalty terms are $2k$ (AIC), $2k\\ln(\\ln n)$ (HQC) and $k\\ln n$ (BIC); for large $n$, $2 &lt; 2\\ln(\\ln n) &lt; \\ln n$, so AIC is least strict and BIC is most strict. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (A) reverse or scramble the correct order; (C) is false as the three formulas differ structurally. Shortcut: Alphabetical mnemonic won\'t work here – memorize the growth-rate ordering directly: constant $&lt;\\ln\\ln n &lt; \\ln n$. Tip: Frequently tested as a ranking/ordering MCQ.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(1) process with $|\\phi|&lt;1$ and innovation variance $\\sigma^2$, the $h$-step-ahead forecast error variance is:',opts:['A) $\\sigma^2$','B) $\\sigma^2 h$','C) $\\sigma^2(1-\\phi^{2h})/(1-\\phi^2)$','D) $\\sigma^2 \\phi^{2h}$'],ans:2,exp:'Expanding the MA($\\infty$) representation, $Var(X_{t+h}-\\hat X_t(h)) = \\sigma^2\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}\\phi^{2j} = \\sigma^2(1-\\phi^{2h})/(1-\\phi^2)$, which increases monotonically with $h$ and converges to $\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)$, the unconditional variance. Why other options are wrong: (A) is only correct for $h=1$; (B) implies unbounded linear growth, inconsistent with stationarity; (D) actually represents the (decaying) forecast weight, not the error variance. Shortcut: This formula is the geometric-series sum of squared AR(1) MA-weights – memorize it directly. Tip: One of the single most important derivable formulas in this unit – expect a direct or disguised numerical.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If the sample ACF and PACF of a series both show clear exponential decay with no clean cutoff at any lag, the most appropriate tentative identification is:',opts:['A) White noise','B) Pure AR model','C) Pure MA model','D) Mixed ARMA model'],ans:3,exp:'Simultaneous tailing off of both functions, without a clean cutoff in either, is the hallmark identification pattern of a mixed ARMA($p,q$) process with $p,q \\geq 1$. Why other options are wrong: A pure AR model would show a clear PACF cutoff; a pure MA model would show a clear ACF cutoff; white noise shows no decay pattern at all (immediate zero beyond lag 0). Shortcut: “Both tail, neither cuts” $\\Rightarrow$ think mixed model. Tip: Frequently combined with a follow-up on how to determine specific $(p,q)$ using extended ACF (EACF) or corner method.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In finite samples, Yule-Walker estimates of AR parameters near the boundary of the stationarity region tend to exhibit:',opts:['A) Upward bias (values exceeding true magnitude)','B) Downward bias in magnitude (shrinkage toward zero)','C) No bias at all','D) Estimates identical to MLE always'],ans:1,exp:'It is well established that Yule-Walker (moment) estimators of AR coefficients are biased toward zero (shrinkage) in finite samples, especially when the true root is close to the unit circle, because the sample autocorrelations are themselves biased downward at higher lags. Why other options are wrong: (C) contradicts known finite-sample theory; (A) is the opposite of the documented direction of bias; (D) is false – MLE and Yule-Walker coincide only asymptotically, not in finite samples. Shortcut: “Yule-Walker shrinks AR roots toward zero in small samples.” Tip: A nuanced JRF-level property distinguishing MoM from MLE beyond simple efficiency comparisons.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is NOT typically part of the diagnostic-checking stage after ARMA model fitting?',opts:['A) Ljung-Box test on residuals','B) Examining residual ACF/PACF for remaining structure','C) Checking normality of residuals','D) Computing the sample ACF of the raw (undifferenced) series to propose $(p,q)$'],ans:3,exp:'Proposing $(p,q)$ from the raw series\' correlogram belongs to the identification stage, which precedes estimation and diagnostic checking, not the diagnostic stage itself. Why other options are wrong: (B), (A) and (C) are all standard components of post-estimation residual diagnostics used to validate model adequacy. Shortcut: Identification uses the raw series\' correlogram; diagnostics use the residual correlogram. Tip: A classic “odd-one-out” style NET question testing sequencing of the Box-Jenkins stages.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Increasing the forecast lead time $h$ for a stationary invertible ARMA process generally causes the width of the forecast prediction interval to:',opts:['A) Remain constant for all $h$','B) Decrease monotonically','C) Increase and then stabilize at a finite bound','D) Increase without bound (diverge to infinity)'],ans:2,exp:'Since the forecast error variance increases with $h$ but converges to the finite unconditional variance $\\gamma_0$, the corresponding prediction interval widens initially and then stabilizes at a finite width for a stationary process. Why other options are wrong: (B) contradicts increasing uncertainty at longer horizons; (D) would only occur for nonstationary processes with unbounded variance growth; (A) ignores the well-known initial widening. Shortcut: Stationary process $\\Rightarrow$ bounded long-run forecast uncertainty. Tip: Useful for distinguishing stationary vs. nonstationary forecasting behaviour, a frequent conceptual pairing.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The primary purpose of examining the correlogram before model estimation is to:',opts:['A) Compute forecast error variance directly','B) Suggest tentative orders $p$ and/or $q$','C) Test for heteroscedasticity','D) Estimate the exact parameter values'],ans:1,exp:'Correlogram (ACF/PACF) analysis is a purely qualitative/graphical tool for suggesting plausible candidate orders before formal parameter estimation is undertaken. Why other options are wrong: Parameter values are obtained via estimation methods (Yule-Walker, MLE), not directly from the correlogram; forecast-error variance and heteroscedasticity testing are separate, later analytical tasks. Shortcut: Correlogram $\\to$ suggests order, does not estimate coefficients. Tip: Foundational definitional question, frequently the very first item in this topic area.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If two models have identical log-likelihoods but different numbers of parameters, all three criteria (AIC, BIC, HQC) will:',opts:['A) Be undefined','B) Disagree unpredictably','C) Select the model with more parameters','D) Select the model with fewer parameters'],ans:3,exp:'With equal fit ($\\ln L$), every criterion\'s value is driven entirely by the penalty term, which increases with $k$; hence all three criteria unanimously favour the more parsimonious model. Why other options are wrong: (C) is the opposite of what minimizing any of these criteria implies; (B) is false because the direction of preference is unambiguous here; (A) is incorrect since all quantities are well-defined. Shortcut: Equal fit $\\Rightarrow$ every criterion prefers parsimony – Occam\'s razor in action. Tip: A conceptual shortcut question that avoids the need for actual numeric computation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an invertible MA($q$) process, the exact Gaussian likelihood cannot generally be written in closed form as a simple product of independent conditional densities because:',opts:['A) The process is nonstationary','B) Past unobservable innovations must be estimated or the full covariance matrix used','C) The likelihood function does not exist for MA models','D) MA processes have infinite variance'],ans:1,exp:'Since the $\\varepsilon_{t-j}$ terms in an MA representation are unobserved, exact likelihood evaluation requires either the full $n\\times n$ Gaussian covariance matrix (via the Cholesky/innovations algorithm) or conditioning on initial innovations set to zero (conditional likelihood), rather than a simple i.i.d.-style product. Why other options are wrong: A stationary invertible MA process is by definition stationary; MA processes have finite variance under standard assumptions; the likelihood does exist, it is just computationally more involved than for i.i.d. or pure AR data. Shortcut: MA likelihoods require the innovations algorithm or state-space (Kalman filter) methods for exact evaluation. Tip: A high-difficulty JRF conceptual item on the computational subtleties of MA estimation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In large samples, the joint asymptotic distribution of Yule-Walker AR($p$) estimators $\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\phi}$ is:',opts:['A) Exactly $N(\\boldsymbol\\phi, \\sigma^2 I)$ in finite samples','B) $\\sqrt{n}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\phi}-\\boldsymbol\\phi) \\xrightarrow{d} N(0,\\ \\sigma^2\\Gamma_p^{-1})$','C) Degenerate (a point mass)','D) Cauchy distributed'],ans:1,exp:'Under standard regularity conditions, $\\sqrt{n}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\phi}-\\boldsymbol\\phi)$ converges in distribution to a multivariate normal with covariance $\\sigma^2\\Gamma_p^{-1}$, where $\\Gamma_p$ is the $p\\times p$ autocovariance matrix of the process – this coincides asymptotically with the MLE distribution. Why other options are wrong: (A) wrongly claims exact (finite-sample) normality; (D) is not the correct limiting family for this estimator; (C) would imply zero asymptotic variance, contradicting known estimation uncertainty. Shortcut: Yule-Walker and MLE share the same asymptotic normal distribution – only finite-sample behaviour differs. Tip: A genuine JRF-level asymptotic theory question, often testing recognition of the $\\Gamma_p^{-1}$ structure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider an AR(2) process with characteristic roots that are complex conjugates lying inside the unit circle. The theoretical ACF will exhibit:',opts:['A) Monotonic exponential decay','B) Constant nonzero values at all lags','C) A damped sinusoidal (pseudo-cyclical) decay pattern','D) An immediate cutoff after lag 2'],ans:2,exp:'Complex conjugate roots inside the unit circle produce an ACF that follows a damped sine-wave pattern, reflecting pseudo-periodic (cyclical) behaviour in the underlying process, unlike the smooth monotone decay from real roots. Why other options are wrong: Monotonic exponential decay arises from real positive roots, not complex ones; a cutoff after lag 2 describes PACF of AR(2), not the ACF; constant nonzero ACF would violate stationarity\'s requirement that $\\rho_k\\to0$. Shortcut: Complex roots $\\Rightarrow$ “wavy,” damped-oscillation ACF; real roots $\\Rightarrow$ smooth exponential decay. Tip: A high-value JRF item connecting characteristic-equation roots to correlogram shape recognition.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR($p$) process, the partial autocorrelation at lag $p$, $\\phi_{pp}$, obtained via the Durbin-Levinson recursion, equals:',opts:['A) The last AR coefficient in the AR($p$) representation, $\\phi_p$','B) Always exactly 1','C) Zero','D) The first-lag autocorrelation $\\rho_1$'],ans:0,exp:'By construction, the Durbin-Levinson recursion computes $\\phi_{kk}$ as the coefficient of $X_{t-k}$ in the best linear predictor using lags $1,\\dots,k$; for a true AR($p$) process this coincides exactly with $\\phi_p$, the true final AR coefficient, at $k=p$, and vanishes for $k&gt;p$, giving the PACF cutoff. Why other options are wrong: (C) is true only for $k&gt;p$, not at $k=p$ itself; (D) confuses PACF with plain ACF; (B) has no general justification. Shortcut: $\\phi_{pp} = \\phi_p$ exactly for a true AR($p$); this equality is the theoretical basis of the PACF cutoff rule. Tip: A conceptually deep JRF question linking the Durbin-Levinson algorithm directly to identification theory.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement correctly compares AIC-based and BIC-based order selection when the true data-generating process is NOT among the candidate models (model misspecification)?',opts:['A) AIC is designed to minimize expected Kullback-Leibler divergence to the true (possibly infinite-dimensional) process; BIC targets identifying a true finite-dimensional model, which may not exist here','B) Both remain consistent for the true order','C) BIC always outperforms AIC regardless of misspecification','D) Neither criterion can be computed under misspecification'],ans:0,exp:'AIC\'s theoretical justification (via the Kullback-Leibler information) does not require the assumption that a true finite-order model exists – it seeks the best finite approximation; BIC\'s consistency proof, by contrast, presumes a true model of finite order is among the candidates, an assumption violated under genuine misspecification. Why other options are wrong: (B) is false since consistency is not guaranteed without a true finite model; (C) overstates BIC\'s advantage, ignoring AIC\'s approximation-optimality justification; (D) is incorrect – both criteria remain computable regardless of true model existence. Shortcut: AIC = best approximation; BIC = best guess at “the truth,” assuming truth is finite-dimensional. Tip: This is a genuinely advanced JRF/PhD-level distinction, occasionally tested in research-oriented NET items.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARMA(1,1) process $X_t = \\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$, the theoretical ACF for $k \\geq 2$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $\\rho_k = \\phi\\, \\rho_{k-1}$ (geometric decay from $\\rho_1$ onward)','B) $\\rho_k$ is undefined','C) $\\rho_k = 0$ for all $k\\geq2$','D) $\\rho_k = \\phi^k$'],ans:0,exp:'For ARMA(1,1), only $\\rho_1$ carries the direct influence of $\\theta$; for $k\\geq 2$ the ACF follows the same first-order homogeneous recursion as a pure AR(1), i.e., $\\rho_k=\\phi\\rho_{k-1}$, so the entire tail decays geometrically at rate $\\phi$ starting from $\\rho_1$. Why other options are wrong: (C) would only hold for the degenerate case $\\phi=0$ combined with $k\\geq2$ trivially; (D) omits the important adjustment through $\\rho_1$, which is not simply $\\phi$; (B) is false, the ACF is well defined for a stationary process. Shortcut: ARMA(1,1): compute $\\rho_1$ specially (depends on both $\\phi,\\theta$), then apply pure geometric decay $\\rho_k=\\phi^{k-1}\\rho_1$ for $k\\geq1$. Tip: A derivation-based JRF question – practice deriving $\\rho_1$ for ARMA(1,1) from the Yule-Walker-type recursion.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The concentrated (profile) likelihood approach in ARMA MLE estimation is used primarily to:',opts:['A) Compute AIC directly without $\\ln L$','B) Replace MLE with method of moments','C) Eliminate the need to estimate $\\sigma^2$ separately, by substituting its MLE conditional on $(\\phi,\\theta)$ back into the likelihood','D) Avoid estimating $\\phi$ altogether'],ans:2,exp:'Because $\\hat\\sigma^2$ has a closed-form expression given $(\\phi,\\theta)$ (the average squared standardized residual), substituting it back yields a concentrated likelihood in $(\\phi,\\theta)$ alone, reducing the dimensionality of the numerical optimization problem. Why other options are wrong: $\\phi$ (and $\\theta$) still must be numerically estimated – concentration removes $\\sigma^2$, not the AR/MA parameters; the method remains full MLE, not MoM; AIC still requires the resulting $\\ln L$ value, it is not bypassed. Shortcut: “Concentrate out $\\sigma^2$” = a standard computational trick in ARMA MLE, not a shortcut to skip AR/MA estimation. Tip: Tests genuine familiarity with numerical optimization practice in ARMA estimation – a favourite JRF distinguishing question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For forecasting an ARMA($p,q$) process recursively via the difference-equation form, forecasts for $h &gt; q$ satisfy a recursion that depends only on:',opts:['A) Past and current innovations $\\varepsilon_t,\\varepsilon_{t-1},\\dots$','B) The AR parameters and previously computed forecasts $\\hat X_t(h-1),\\dots,\\hat X_t(h-p)$','C) The sample mean of the series only','D) The MA parameters only'],ans:1,exp:'Once $h$ exceeds $q$, all MA terms in the difference equation involve future (unobserved, hence zero-expectation) innovations, so the forecast recursion reduces to the pure AR($p$) homogeneous form using only $\\phi_1,\\dots,\\phi_p$ and earlier forecasts. Why other options are wrong: Innovations beyond time $t$ have zero conditional expectation and drop out for $h&gt;q$; MA parameters cease to matter once $h&gt;q$; using only the sample mean ignores the legitimate AR feedback still present. Shortcut: Beyond horizon $q$, ARMA forecasts behave exactly like pure AR($p$) forecasts. Tip: Essential for deriving multi-step ARMA forecasts efficiently in JRF-level numerical problems.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement about the relationship between HQC and the law of the iterated logarithm (LIL) is correct?',opts:['A) HQC has no connection with LIL','B) LIL is only relevant to BIC, not HQC','C) LIL implies AIC is strongly consistent','D) HQC\'s $2\\ln(\\ln n)$ penalty is the minimal growth rate guaranteeing strong consistency of order selection, motivated by LIL bounds on the fluctuations of the log-likelihood ratio statistic'],ans:3,exp:'Hannan and Quinn showed that $2\\ln(\\ln n)$ is essentially the smallest penalty growth rate for which strong (almost sure) consistency of the selected order holds, a result directly tied to law-of-the-iterated-logarithm bounds on the maximal fluctuations of likelihood-ratio-type statistics across candidate orders. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (B) deny the well-documented theoretical motivation behind HQC\'s specific functional form; (C) is false since AIC\'s constant penalty is known to be asymptotically inconsistent. Shortcut: Remember: HQC exists precisely because of LIL-type consistency requirements – this is its defining theoretical rationale. Tip: A genuinely research-level JRF question testing the “why,” not just the formula, behind HQC.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In finite-sample simulation studies, which ordering of average selected order (from smallest to largest, holding true order fixed) is typically observed among AIC, BIC, HQC?',opts:['A) HQC $\\leq$ BIC $\\leq$ AIC','B) BIC $\\leq$ HQC $\\leq$ AIC','C) All three select identical average orders','D) AIC $\\leq$ HQC $\\leq$ BIC'],ans:1,exp:'Because BIC applies the heaviest complexity penalty, it tends to select the smallest (most parsimonious) models on average, followed by HQC with its intermediate penalty, while AIC, having the lightest constant penalty, tends to select the largest models on average. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (A) invert or scramble this well-documented empirical ordering; (C) contradicts the very different penalty structures of the three criteria. Shortcut: Heavier penalty $\\Rightarrow$ smaller selected model, on average: BIC $\\le$ HQC $\\le$ AIC in selected order size. Tip: A synthesis question tying together penalty-growth theory with practical simulation-based intuition – classic JRF integrative item.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(1) process near the unit root ($\\phi \\to 1^-$), the exact forecast error variance formula $\\sigma^2(1-\\phi^{2h})/(1-\\phi^2)$:',opts:['A) Converges quickly to $\\sigma^2$ regardless of $h$','B) Is undefined at $\\phi=1$ and remains bounded for any $\\phi&lt;1$, however close to 1','C) Approaches zero','D) Approaches $h\\sigma^2$ (linear growth), mimicking random-walk behaviour, for moderate $h$'],ans:3,exp:'As $\\phi\\to1^-$, using $1-\\phi^2\\approx 2(1-\\phi)$ and $1-\\phi^{2h}\\to$ close to $2h(1-\\phi)$ for the relevant range, the ratio approaches $h\\sigma^2$: forecast error variance grows nearly linearly with horizon over the range of practically relevant $h$, mirroring the unbounded linear variance growth of a true random walk ($\\phi=1$). Why other options are wrong: (A) ignores the near-unit-root blow-up in variance; (C) is the opposite of what actually happens; while (B) is technically true that the formula stays finite for any $\\phi&lt;1$, it misses the crucial practical point that the finite bound can be enormous and behave like linear growth over realistic horizons. Shortcut: Near-unit-root AR(1) forecast uncertainty “feels like” a random walk\'s ever-growing variance, even though technically stationary. Tip: A subtle, genuinely research-level JRF item linking forecasting theory to near-nonstationary behaviour.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best describes why exact (as opposed to conditional) maximum likelihood is preferred for ARMA models with roots close to the boundary of the invertibility/stationarity region?',opts:['A) Conditional likelihood is computationally infeasible in all cases','B) There is no practical difference between the two approaches','C) Exact likelihood does not require Gaussian innovations','D) Conditional likelihood discards information from initial observations, causing larger finite-sample bias/inefficiency, which matters most near boundary roots'],ans:3,exp:'Conditional likelihood approximates initial unobserved innovations by zero (or conditions on the first few observations), discarding some information; this approximation error is most consequential precisely when the process is close to nonstationarity/noninvertibility, where exact likelihood (via the full covariance structure) yields materially better finite-sample estimates. Why other options are wrong: Conditional likelihood is in fact computationally simpler, not infeasible; both approaches typically assume Gaussian innovations for the likelihood to be tractable in closed form; the practical difference is well documented, especially near boundary parameter values. Shortcut: “Boundary roots $\\Rightarrow$ use exact likelihood, not conditional.” Tip: Tests deep understanding of estimation-method trade-offs – a genuinely discriminating JRF item.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Suppose true order is ARMA(1,1) but an analyst fits AR($p$) models only (no MA term) and selects $p$ via AIC. As $n\\to\\infty$, the AIC-selected AR order $\\hat p$ typically:',opts:['A) Stays fixed at some finite value forever','B) Equals exactly 1 always','C) Grows without bound (slowly, e.g. like $\\ln n$), since a finite-order AR can only approximate the true infinite-order AR($\\infty$) representation of the ARMA(1,1) process','D) Immediately jumps to $p=n$'],ans:2,exp:'Since an ARMA(1,1) process (with $\\theta\\neq0$) has an AR($\\infty$) representation, no finite AR order is exactly correct; AIC (used within a misspecified AR-only family) is known to select an order that grows slowly with $n$ in order to approximate the true infinite-order AR representation increasingly well. Why other options are wrong: (A) would be true only if the AR-only family actually contained the truth, which it does not here; (D) is an absurd, non-standard outcome; (B) ignores that the correct approximation order must grow, not stay fixed at the MA-lag count. Shortcut: Fitting the wrong (nested) family forces the selected order to grow with $n$ to compensate for misspecification. Tip: A subtle theoretical question connecting order-selection asymptotics to model misspecification – genuinely research-level JRF content.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: A student concludes that because the PACF cuts off after lag 2, the ACF must also cut off after lag 2. This reasoning is:',opts:['A) Correct only for MA models','B) Correct always','C) Incorrect – PACF cutoff at lag 2 identifies AR(2), whose ACF instead tails off','D) Correct only if $\\phi_1=\\phi_2$'],ans:2,exp:'A clean PACF cutoff is the signature of an AR process; the corresponding ACF for that same AR process tails off gradually rather than cutting off – the two functions never both cut off for a genuine (non-white-noise) AR or MA process. Why other options are wrong: (B) reflects a very common but false symmetric-cutoff assumption; (A) misapplies the rule to the wrong model type; (D) introduces an irrelevant condition with no bearing on the ACF/PACF pattern. Shortcut: Never assume ACF and PACF cut off together, except in the trivial white-noise case. Tip: One of the most common student errors – examiners deliberately set traps around this confusion.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: A student claims “a lower AIC always means a better-fitting model in absolute terms.” This is:',opts:['A) False – AIC is only meaningful for relative comparison among candidate models fit to the same data, not as an absolute goodness-of-fit measure','B) True only for nested models','C) True only when $k=1$','D) True without qualification'],ans:0,exp:'AIC (and BIC, HQC) values are only interpretable in relative terms across a set of competing models estimated on identical data; the absolute AIC value carries no independent meaning about “how good” the fit is in any universal sense. Why other options are wrong: (D) overstates AIC\'s interpretability; (B) wrongly restricts a general property to nested models only, though AIC actually is commonly used to compare non-nested models too; (C) introduces an arbitrary, irrelevant restriction. Shortcut: Information criteria are for ranking, not for standalone absolute fit assessment. Tip: Frequently used as a conceptual trap to test true understanding versus rote memorization of the formula.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: A student argues “since BIC is consistent, it will always select a smaller model than AIC in every finite sample.” Evaluate:',opts:['A) True only if $n&gt;1000$','B) Always true','C) False – BIC selects a smaller-or-equal model than AIC on average asymptotically, but in any specific finite sample either criterion could select the smaller model','D) True only for AR models'],ans:2,exp:'While BIC\'s heavier asymptotic penalty makes it tend toward smaller models on average as $n$ grows, this is a probabilistic/average statement, not a guarantee for every individual finite sample; in a specific dataset, AIC could occasionally select an equal or even smaller order than BIC by chance. Why other options are wrong: (B) overgeneralizes a tendency into an absolute guarantee; (A) invents an arbitrary sample-size threshold with no theoretical basis; (D) incorrectly restricts a general property to a specific model class. Shortcut: “Tends to” and “always” are very different claims – watch for this substitution in JRF options. Tip: A high-value trap testing careful reading of probabilistic vs. deterministic claims.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Since forecast error variance increases with horizon $h$, forecasts themselves become increasingly biased as $h$ grows.” This statement is:',opts:['A) True only for MA models','B) True','C) True only for nonstationary series','D) False – MMSE forecasts remain unbiased (conditional mean) at every horizon; only the variance (uncertainty), not the bias, increases with $h$'],ans:3,exp:'The conditional-expectation forecast is unbiased by construction ($E[X_{t+h}-\\hat X_t(h)]=0$) at every horizon; increasing forecast error variance reflects growing uncertainty, not the introduction of systematic bias. Why other options are wrong: (B) conflates variance and bias, a very common student confusion; (A) and (C) incorrectly restrict a general unbiasedness property (which holds broadly for correctly specified MMSE forecasts) to special cases. Shortcut: Rising forecast-error variance $\\neq$ rising bias – these are separate concepts entirely. Tip: Bias-vs-variance confusion is one of the most heavily tested conceptual traps in the forecasting subtopic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Since MA($q$) has ACF cutting off after lag $q$, any process whose sample ACF appears to cut off after some lag $m$ must be MA($m$).” This reasoning is:',opts:['A) Flawed – sample ACF is subject to sampling error and can spuriously appear to cut off; formal significance testing and model diagnostics are still required','B) Always correct','C) Correct only if $n&gt;10000$','D) Correct only for seasonal series'],ans:0,exp:'Because sample ACF estimates are themselves random variables with sampling variability, an apparent cutoff could easily occur by chance even under a different true model; identification from correlograms is only a tentative first step, always to be confirmed via estimation and residual diagnostics. Why other options are wrong: (B) overstates the reliability of purely visual correlogram inspection; (C) invents an arbitrary threshold; (D) incorrectly restricts a general caution to seasonal data only. Shortcut: Correlogram-based identification is always “tentative,” never final or definitive. Tip: Tests understanding that Box-Jenkins identification is inherently a hypothesis-generating, not hypothesis-confirming, step.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Yule-Walker estimation and OLS regression of $X_t$ on $X_{t-1},\\dots,X_{t-p}$ always give numerically identical AR coefficient estimates.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) False in general – they coincide only asymptotically; in finite samples, Yule-Walker (using the sample autocovariance/Toeplitz form) and OLS (which uses slightly different end-effect handling of the data) can differ numerically','B) True only when the mean is zero','C) Always true, in every finite sample','D) True only for AR(1)'],ans:0,exp:'Although both estimators are consistent and asymptotically equivalent, Yule-Walker uses the biased sample autocovariances arranged in a Toeplitz matrix, while OLS directly minimizes the conditional sum of squares using the observed regressor/response pairs; these differ in how they treat the beginning/end of the sample, producing generally different finite-sample numerical values (though OLS guarantees a stationary fit via Yule-Walker\'s Toeplitz structure, whereas plain OLS estimates need not even be stationary). Why other options are wrong: (C) is a commonly assumed but false equivalence; (D) is not correct even for AR(1) in finite samples, though the two are numerically close; (B) is an irrelevant condition. Shortcut: “Asymptotically equivalent” almost never means “identical in finite samples” – a recurring trap phrase across all of statistics. Tip: An advanced JRF trap testing precise distinctions between closely related but non-identical estimators.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “A model with the lowest AIC among candidates is guaranteed to pass the Ljung-Box white-noise residual test.” This claim is:',opts:['A) True only if all candidate models are ARMA','B) True only for large $n$','C) False – AIC selection and residual whiteness diagnostics are separate steps; the AIC-best model among a flawed candidate set can still show residual autocorrelation','D) Always true'],ans:2,exp:'AIC ranks models relative to each other within the given candidate set; if the true structure is not well represented by any candidate in that set, even the AIC-minimizing model can still fail residual diagnostic tests, indicating the entire candidate set needs revision. Why other options are wrong: (D) wrongly assumes model selection guarantees adequacy; (A) and (B) impose irrelevant restrictions that do not resolve the underlying logical gap between relative ranking and absolute adequacy. Shortcut: Order selection (AIC/BIC/HQC) and diagnostic checking (Ljung-Box) are two distinct, complementary steps – passing one does not guarantee passing the other. Tip: A frequently tested trap reinforcing the full, correct Box-Jenkins sequence rather than a shortcut mentality.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “Since $\\hat X_t(h) \\to$ mean as $h\\to\\infty$, long-horizon forecasts are `useless\' and should never be reported.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) True only for MA models','B) True','C) True only for AR models','D) False – the convergence to the unconditional mean, together with the (finite, computable) forecast error variance, is itself valuable information: it precisely quantifies the practical limit of predictability'],ans:3,exp:'Knowing that a stationary process\'s forecasts revert to the unconditional mean with a specific, computable error variance is a meaningful and useful theoretical result – it tells practitioners exactly how far ahead a series can be usefully predicted before predictive skill is exhausted, rather than making forecasting worthless. Why other options are wrong: (B) draws an overly strong negative conclusion from a normal theoretical property; (C) and (A) incorrectly restrict a universal stationary-process property to specific model subclasses. Shortcut: Mean-reversion at long horizons is expected behaviour for any stationary process, not a flaw in the forecasting method. Tip: Tests conceptual maturity in interpreting forecast theory rather than mechanical formula recall.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “HQC always selects the same order as BIC since both grow with $n$.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) False – although both penalties diverge as $n\\to\\infty$, they grow at different rates ($\\ln\\ln n$ vs. $\\ln n$), so HQC often selects orders equal to or larger than BIC in finite samples','B) True only when $k$ is even','C) True in all samples','D) True only for AR models'],ans:0,exp:'Since $\\ln(\\ln n) &lt; \\ln n$ for all reasonably large $n$, HQC\'s penalty is uniformly lighter than BIC\'s, so HQC will select models at least as large as BIC in most finite samples, converging to the same asymptotic consistency property but not to numerically identical finite-sample selections. Why other options are wrong: (C) wrongly conflates “both grow with $n$” with “grow identically”; (D) and (B) are arbitrary, irrelevant restrictions. Shortcut: Same asymptotic property (consistency) $\\neq$ same finite-sample numeric selections. Tip: Reinforces the exact penalty-growth ranking tested earlier – a recurring JRF theme worth memorizing precisely.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRAP: “A significant spike at lag 12 in the ACF of monthly data always indicates an AR(12) model is appropriate.” Evaluate.',opts:['A) True always','B) True only if $\\phi_{12}&gt;0.5$','C) False – an isolated significant spike at a seasonal lag more likely suggests seasonal dependence (e.g., a seasonal MA or SAR component), not necessarily a full nonseasonal AR(12) model','D) True only for quarterly data'],ans:2,exp:'A lone significant spike precisely at a seasonal period (like lag 12 for monthly data) is a classic signature of seasonal structure, typically modeled with seasonal ARMA components, rather than requiring a full, densely parameterized AR(12) model with 12 potentially nonzero coefficients. Why other options are wrong: (A) jumps to an overly complex, poorly justified model choice; (D) misapplies the reasoning to the wrong data frequency; (B) introduces an arbitrary numeric threshold irrelevant to the identification logic. Shortcut: Isolated seasonal-lag spikes $\\Rightarrow$ think seasonal component, not full high-order AR. Tip: Connects this unit to seasonal ARIMA (SARIMA) content often covered in adjoining syllabus sections.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) AIC is asymptotically efficient for prediction in a nested sequence of AR models when the true order is infinite. (II) BIC is asymptotically efficient for prediction under the same conditions. Which is correct?',opts:['A) Only (II) is true','B) Neither is true','C) Only (I) is true','D) Both are true'],ans:2,exp:'Shibata\'s classical result shows AIC is asymptotically efficient (minimizes prediction error) precisely in settings where the true order is infinite (no finite true model exists); BIC, targeting a true finite order, loses this efficiency property in such infinite-order settings. Why other options are wrong: (A) misattributes AIC\'s known efficiency property to BIC; (D) is false since the two criteria have genuinely different, non-overlapping optimality regimes; (B) denies a well-established asymptotic result. Shortcut: “AIC $\\to$ prediction efficiency (infinite true order); BIC $\\to$ order consistency (finite true order)” – they solve different optimization problems. Tip: Statement pairs like this test whether the student truly separates the two distinct optimality criteria (efficiency vs. consistency).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) In an AR(1) process, the 1-step forecast error variance equals $\\sigma^2$. (II) In an AR(1) process, the forecast error variance is the same at all horizons $h$.',opts:['A) Only (I) is true','B) Both are true','C) Neither is true','D) Only (II) is true'],ans:0,exp:'At $h=1$, the forecast error is exactly $\\varepsilon_{t+1}$ with variance $\\sigma^2$; however, the variance grows with $h$ according to $\\sigma^2(1-\\phi^{2h})/(1-\\phi^2)$, so statement (II) is false – the variance is not constant across horizons. Why other options are wrong: (D) wrongly claims constancy across horizons; (B) incorrectly validates the false statement (II); (C) wrongly denies the true statement (I). Shortcut: Only $h=1$ gives the simple $\\sigma^2$ value; every other horizon requires the full geometric-sum formula. Tip: A frequent statement-based pairing testing precise boundary conditions of forecast formulas.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) The PACF of an MA(1) process is nonzero at every lag, decaying geometrically in absolute value. (II) The ACF of an MA(1) process is zero for all lags $k\\geq2$.',opts:['A) Only (II) is true','B) Both are true','C) Neither is true','D) Only (I) is true'],ans:1,exp:'MA(1) has the well-known invertible AR($\\infty$) representation, giving a PACF that decays in magnitude (roughly geometrically, alternating in sign depending on $\\theta$) but never exactly reaches zero at finite lags; simultaneously its ACF is exactly zero beyond lag 1 by direct computation of the MA(1) autocovariance function. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (A) each deny one of two independently verifiable, standard facts about MA(1); (C) denies both despite both being textbook-standard results. Shortcut: MA(1): ACF hard cutoff at lag 1; PACF smooth (never exactly zero) decay – both statements are simultaneously true. Tip: Statement questions often combine two true facts to test whether the student over-doubts correct textbook statements.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) BIC penalizes additional parameters more heavily than AIC whenever $n \\geq 8$ (since $\\ln n &gt; 2$ for $n&gt;e^2\\approx7.39$). (II) For very small $n$ (e.g. $n=5$), AIC can penalize more heavily than BIC.',opts:['A) Both are true','B) Only (II) is true','C) Only (I) is true','D) Neither is true'],ans:0,exp:'Comparing penalty coefficients directly: BIC\'s per-parameter penalty $\\ln n$ exceeds AIC\'s constant $2$ exactly when $n &gt; e^2 \\approx 7.39$, i.e., for $n\\geq8$; consequently for very small samples like $n=5$ ($\\ln 5\\approx1.61&lt;2$), AIC\'s penalty coefficient is actually larger than BIC\'s, reversing the usual intuition. Why other options are wrong: (C) and (B) each deny one half of this precisely verifiable numerical fact; (D) denies both despite both being correct. Shortcut: The AIC/BIC penalty crossover happens exactly at $n=e^2\\approx7.39$ – a nice fact to keep in memory for tricky statement questions. Tip: A rare but real JRF-level numeric subtlety about the AIC-BIC comparison that catches out students who assume “BIC always penalizes more.”'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) Identification precedes estimation in the Box-Jenkins methodology. (II) Diagnostic checking precedes forecasting.',opts:['A) Both are true','B) Only (I) is true','C) Neither is true','D) Only (II) is true'],ans:0,exp:'The standard Box-Jenkins cycle strictly follows the order: identification $\\to$ estimation $\\to$ diagnostic checking $\\to$ forecasting (with possible iteration back to identification if diagnostics fail), so both statements correctly describe this sequencing. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (D) each incorrectly deny one of the two correctly ordered stages; (C) denies both despite the sequence being completely standard. Shortcut: Memorize the four-stage cycle in order: I-E-D-F. Tip: A guaranteed-recurring easy statement question on methodology sequencing.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) Method-of-moments (Yule-Walker) estimation directly applies to pure MA models without iterative or nonlinear solving. (II) MLE for MA models generally requires numerical (iterative) optimization.',opts:['A) Only (II) is true','B) Only (I) is true','C) Neither is true','D) Both are true'],ans:0,exp:'Unlike the AR case, moment-matching for MA models leads to nonlinear equations in the MA parameters (since ACF values are nonlinear functions of $\\theta$), so even “method of moments” for MA requires iterative/numerical solving; MLE for MA models similarly requires numerical optimization of the (conditional or exact) likelihood, since no closed-form solution exists in general. Why other options are wrong: (B) is false – MA moment equations are nonlinear, contradicting the claim of a direct, non-iterative solution; (D) wrongly validates the false statement (B); (C) wrongly denies the true statement (II). Shortcut: Only AR moment estimation (via linear Yule-Walker equations) is directly, non-iteratively solvable; MA moment and MLE estimation both require numerical methods. Tip: A subtle but frequently tested distinction between AR and MA estimation difficulty.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) For a random walk (nonstationary), forecast error variance grows linearly and unboundedly with horizon $h$. (II) For any stationary ARMA process, forecast error variance is bounded above by the process\'s unconditional variance.',opts:['A) Both are true','B) Only (II) is true','C) Only (I) is true','D) Neither is true'],ans:0,exp:'A random walk\'s $h$-step forecast error variance is $h\\sigma^2$, which diverges as $h\\to\\infty$, confirming (I); for any stationary process the forecast error variance increases monotonically but is always bounded above by, and converges to, the finite unconditional variance $\\gamma_0$, confirming (II). Why other options are wrong: (C) and (B) each wrongly deny one of these two independently correct, complementary facts; (D) denies both despite both being standard results. Shortcut: Stationary $\\Rightarrow$ bounded long-run forecast variance; nonstationary (unit root) $\\Rightarrow$ unbounded growth – a core contrast. Tip: This exact stationary-vs-nonstationary contrast in forecast variance is one of the highest-yield concepts in Unit VII.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) The Ljung-Box test statistic\'s degrees of freedom for testing residuals after fitting ARMA($p,q$) is $m$ (the number of lags tested). (II) The correct degrees of freedom is $m-p-q$.',opts:['A) Only (II) is true','B) Both are true','C) Neither is true','D) Only (I) is true'],ans:0,exp:'Because $p+q$ parameters were already estimated from the same data, the correct asymptotic degrees of freedom for the Ljung-Box chi-square statistic on residuals of a fitted ARMA($p,q$) model is $m-p-q$, not the raw lag count $m$ (which would only be correct for testing a series with no estimated parameters). Why other options are wrong: (D) uses the degrees of freedom appropriate only for testing raw, unfitted data, not post-estimation residuals; (B) wrongly validates the incorrect statement; (C) wrongly denies the correct one. Shortcut: “Subtract the number of estimated ARMA parameters from $m$” when testing residuals, not the raw series. Tip: A precise, frequently mis-stated detail that JRF papers test explicitly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) A stationary process must have a bounded, finite unconditional variance. (II) All finite-order MA processes are automatically stationary regardless of parameter values.',opts:['A) Both are true','B) Only (II) is true','C) Neither is true','D) Only (I) is true'],ans:0,exp:'By definition, (weak) stationarity requires a finite, time-invariant variance, confirming (I); additionally, any finite-order MA process is a finite linear combination of white noise terms and is therefore always stationary for any real parameter values (invertibility, not stationarity, is the condition that can fail for MA models). Why other options are wrong: (D) and (B) each wrongly deny one of these two independently true, standard facts; (C) denies both despite both being correct. Shortcut: MA models: stationarity is automatic; invertibility is the condition that requires checking (opposite emphasis from AR models). Tip: A very commonly asked easy statement pair reinforcing the AR-stationarity vs. MA-invertibility distinction.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) HQC is rarely available by default in some statistical software compared to AIC/BIC. (II) When all three criteria (AIC, BIC, HQC) agree on the same order, this agreement provides stronger practical confidence in that order choice.',opts:['A) Both are true','B) Neither is true','C) Only (I) is true','D) Only (II) is true'],ans:0,exp:'In common practice, HQC is indeed less universally reported by default than AIC/BIC in many software packages (though it is usually obtainable), supporting (I); and since the three criteria embody different penalty philosophies, their mutual agreement on a chosen order is reasonably interpreted as stronger practical evidence for that order than any single criterion alone, supporting (II). Why other options are wrong: (C) and (D) each wrongly deny one of these two reasonable, commonly accepted practical statements; (B) denies both. Shortcut: Agreement across differently-penalized criteria is generally treated as a good practical robustness check. Tip: A more applied/practice-oriented statement pairing, testing awareness beyond pure theory.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Model) with List II (Correlogram Pattern): List I: (a) AR($p$) (b) MA($q$) (c) ARMA($p,q$) (d) White noise List II: (1) Both ACF and PACF tail off (2) ACF tails off, PACF cuts off after lag $p$ (3) ACF cuts off after lag $q$, PACF tails off (4) Both ACF and PACF are zero beyond lag 0',opts:['A) a-2, b-3, c-4, d-1','B) a-1, b-1, c-1, d-1','C) a-2, b-3, c-1, d-4','D) a-3, b-2, c-1, d-4'],ans:2,exp:'AR matches (2) [PACF cuts, ACF tails], MA matches (3) [ACF cuts, PACF tails], ARMA matches (1) [both tail], white noise matches (4) [both essentially zero beyond lag 0]. Why other options are wrong: (D) swaps AR and MA patterns; (A) incorrectly assigns ARMA and white noise patterns; (B) collapses all four distinct patterns into one, which is clearly false. Shortcut: Build the 2x2 table once and match against it directly, rather than re-deriving each pattern from scratch. Tip: This exact match-the-following format is extremely common in NET Statistics papers for this topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Criterion) with List II (Penalty Term): List I: (a) AIC (b) BIC (c) HQC (d) Log-likelihood alone (no penalty) List II: (1) $k\\ln n$ (2) $2k\\ln(\\ln n)$ (3) $2k$ (4) $0$',opts:['A) a-2, b-1, c-3, d-4','B) a-3, b-2, c-1, d-4','C) a-1, b-3, c-2, d-4','D) a-3, b-1, c-2, d-4'],ans:3,exp:'AIC\'s penalty is $2k$, BIC\'s is $k\\ln n$, HQC\'s is $2k\\ln(\\ln n)$, and a raw likelihood-maximization rule (no penalty) simply adds $0$. Why other options are wrong: (C) swaps AIC and BIC penalties; (B) swaps BIC and HQC penalties; (A) swaps AIC and HQC penalties – all classic mix-ups examiners exploit. Shortcut: Memorize the exact penalty formula strings verbatim – swapping is the most common distractor design here. Tip: Match-the-following on exact formulas tests precise memorization, unlike conceptual MCQs.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Stage) with List II (Box-Jenkins Activity): List I: (a) Identification (b) Estimation (c) Diagnostic Checking (d) Forecasting List II: (1) Ljung-Box test on residuals (2) Computing $\\hat X_t(h)$ and forecast intervals (3) Examining sample ACF/PACF (4) Maximum likelihood / Yule-Walker',opts:['A) a-3, b-4, c-2, d-1','B) a-3, b-4, c-1, d-2','C) a-3, b-1, c-4, d-2','D) a-4, b-3, c-1, d-2'],ans:1,exp:'Identification uses raw correlogram analysis (3), estimation applies MLE/Yule-Walker (4), diagnostic checking applies Ljung-Box on residuals (1), and forecasting computes point/interval forecasts (2). Why other options are wrong: (D) swaps identification and estimation; (C) swaps estimation and diagnostic checking; (A) swaps diagnostic checking and forecasting – each a plausible but incorrect stage confusion. Shortcut: Walk through the I-E-D-F cycle in strict order when matching. Tip: A staple format testing methodology sequencing comprehensively in one question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Process) with List II ($h$-step Forecast for $h$ large, i.e. beyond model memory): List I: (a) AR(1), $|\\phi|&lt;1$ (b) MA(1) (c) ARMA(1,1) (d) Random walk List II: (1) Constant unconditional mean, error variance stabilizes (2) Forecast $=$ last observed value, error variance grows unboundedly (3) $\\phi^h X_t \\to 0$, geometric decay (4) Behaves like pure AR(1) forecast for $h&gt;1$',opts:['A) a-1, b-3, c-4, d-2','B) a-3, b-1, c-4, d-2','C) a-3, b-4, c-1, d-2','D) a-3, b-1, c-2, d-4'],ans:1,exp:'AR(1) forecasts decay geometrically to zero (3); MA(1) forecasts jump to the constant mean beyond horizon 1 (1); ARMA(1,1) forecasts behave like pure AR(1) beyond the MA lag (4); random walk forecasts equal the last value with ever-growing error variance (2). Why other options are wrong: (A) swaps AR(1) and MA(1) patterns; (C) swaps MA(1) and ARMA(1,1) patterns; (D) swaps random walk and ARMA(1,1) patterns. Shortcut: Treat ARMA(1,1) as “AR(1) once you get past the single MA lag” when matching forecast profiles. Tip: A synthesis-style match question combining several forecasting facts at once – excellent for final revision.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Estimation Method) with List II (Key Feature): List I: (a) Method of Moments (Yule-Walker) (b) Maximum Likelihood (c) Hannan-Rissanen List II: (1) Two-stage: long AR fit, then regression using estimated residuals (2) Asymptotically efficient, achieves Cram\\\'er-Rao bound (3) Solves linear equations relating $\\rho_k$ to $\\phi_i$',opts:['A) a-1, b-2, c-3','B) a-3, b-1, c-2','C) a-3, b-2, c-1','D) a-2, b-3, c-1'],ans:2,exp:'Yule-Walker solves the linear moment equations relating autocorrelations to AR coefficients (3); MLE is asymptotically efficient (2); Hannan-Rissanen is the two-stage long-AR-then-regression procedure (1). Why other options are wrong: (D) swaps Yule-Walker and MLE features; (B) swaps MLE and Hannan-Rissanen features; (A) is a fully scrambled, incorrect assignment. Shortcut: Keep each method\'s “one-line signature phrase” memorized for instant matching. Tip: A concise, high-yield revision-style match question for the estimation subtopic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Scenario) with List II (Best Criterion Choice / Property): List I: (a) Want strong asymptotic consistency for true finite order (b) Want minimal mean-square prediction error when true order is infinite (c) Want a compromise penalty motivated by LIL bounds List II: (1) HQC (2) BIC (3) AIC',opts:['A) a-1, b-2, c-3','B) a-3, b-2, c-1','C) a-2, b-3, c-1','D) a-2, b-1, c-3'],ans:2,exp:'BIC gives strong order-selection consistency (2); AIC minimizes prediction error under an infinite true order (3); HQC offers the LIL-motivated intermediate penalty (1). Why other options are wrong: (B) swaps BIC and AIC roles; (D) swaps BIC and HQC roles; (A) is a fully scrambled assignment inconsistent with each criterion\'s known theoretical property. Shortcut: Consistency $\\to$ BIC; Prediction efficiency $\\to$ AIC; LIL-based compromise $\\to$ HQC. Tip: A synthesis-level match testing the deepest theoretical distinctions among the three criteria – excellent JRF-level practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.6$, $X_t=10$, the 2-step-ahead forecast $\\hat X_t(2)$ (zero-mean process) is:',opts:['A) $6.0$','B) $3.6$','C) $10$','D) $0.36$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\hat X_t(2) = \\phi^2 X_t = (0.6)^2(10) = 0.36\\times10=3.6$. Why other options are wrong: (A) uses $\\phi^1$ instead of $\\phi^2$; (D) forgets to multiply by $X_t$; (C) wrongly assumes no decay at all. Shortcut: Always raise $\\phi$ to the power equal to the horizon before multiplying by $X_t$. Tip: Practice computing $\\phi^h$ mentally for common values like $0.5,0.6,0.7,0.8$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.5,\\ \\sigma^2=9$, the unconditional variance of the process is:',opts:['A) $18$','B) $9$','C) $4.5$','D) $12$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\gamma_0=\\sigma^2/(1-\\phi^2)=9/(1-0.25)=9/0.75=12$. Why other options are wrong: (B) is just $\\sigma^2$ itself, ignoring the AR amplification factor; (A) and (C) result from incorrect arithmetic manipulations of the formula. Shortcut: Unconditional variance always exceeds $\\sigma^2$ for any AR(1) with $\\phi\\neq0$. Tip: This variance formula is the limiting value approached by the forecast error variance as $h\\to\\infty$ – link the two concepts.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.5,\\ \\sigma^2=9$, the 2-step forecast error variance is:',opts:['A) $9$','B) $11.25$','C) $12$','D) $9.25$'],ans:1,exp:'$Var = \\sigma^2(1-\\phi^{2h})/(1-\\phi^2) = 9(1-0.25^2)/(1-0.25)=9(1-0.0625)/0.75=9(0.9375)/0.75=8.4375/0.75=11.25$. Why other options are wrong: (A) is only the 1-step value; (C) is the unconditional variance (the $h\\to\\infty$ limit, not $h=2$); (D) results from a minor arithmetic slip. Shortcut: Compute $\\phi^{2h}$ carefully – here $\\phi^{2h}=\\phi^4=0.0625$, a common point of arithmetic error. Tip: Multi-step forecast-error-variance numericals are a very frequent JRF numerical question type – practice with several $(\\phi,h)$ combinations.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2): $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+0.4\\varepsilon_{t-1}+0.2\\varepsilon_{t-2}$, with $\\sigma^2=1$, the unconditional variance of $X_t$ is:',opts:['A) $1$','B) $1.16$','C) $1.2$','D) $1.6$'],ans:2,exp:'$Var(X_t)=\\sigma^2(1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2)=1(1+0.16+0.04)=1.20$. Why other options are wrong: (A) omits both MA coefficients entirely; (B) and (D) do not match the correctly summed value. Shortcut: $Var(MA(q)) = \\sigma^2(1+\\theta_1^2+\\cdots+\\theta_q^2)$ – a direct sum-of-squares formula. Tip: Double-check every squared-coefficient addition carefully under exam time pressure – this is the single most common numerical slip in this subtopic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2) as above ($\\theta_1=0.4,\\theta_2=0.2,\\sigma^2=1$), the forecast error variance at horizon $h=3$ is:',opts:['A) $1.16$','B) $1$','C) $1.36$','D) $1.20$'],ans:3,exp:'For $h&gt;q=2$, the forecast error variance equals the full unconditional variance, $\\sigma^2(1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2)=1.20$, since the process\'s entire memory (2 lags) is already exhausted by horizon 3. Why other options are wrong: (B) is only the 1-step value; (A) undercounts one of the squared coefficients; (C) overstates the sum. Shortcut: For MA($q$), forecast error variance saturates at the unconditional variance for any $h&gt;q$ – no further growth occurs beyond that point. Tip: Contrast this “saturating then flat” MA behaviour with AR\'s continuously (asymptotically) increasing forecast variance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given $\\ln L = -150$ for a fitted AR(3) model with $n=200$ observations ($k=3$ parameters, excluding $\\sigma^2$; use $k=4$ including $\\sigma^2$), compute AIC (using $k=4$):',opts:['A) $150$','B) $300$','C) $308$','D) $304$'],ans:2,exp:'$AIC=-2(-150)+2(4)=300+8=308$. Why other options are wrong: (B) omits the penalty term entirely; (D) and (A) do not correspond to a correct application of the formula. Shortcut: Always double the negative log-likelihood first, then add the penalty separately – do not combine the steps mentally to avoid slips. Tip: Watch carefully whether $\\sigma^2$ is included in $k$ – exam papers often specify this explicitly, and it changes the final numeric answer.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the same model ($\\ln L=-150,\\ n=200,\\ k=4$), compute BIC ($\\ln 200 \\approx 5.298$):',opts:['A) $300$','B) $315.9$','C) $308$','D) $321.2$'],ans:3,exp:'$BIC = 300 + 4(5.298) = 300+21.19 = 321.19 \\approx 321.2$. Why other options are wrong: (C) is the AIC value, not BIC; (A) omits the penalty entirely; (B) results from an arithmetic slip in multiplying $4\\times5.298$. Shortcut: Compute $k\\ln n$ as a single multiplication step and add carefully to $-2\\ln L$. Tip: Comparing this BIC value to the AIC value from the previous question directly illustrates BIC\'s heavier penalty in a concrete numerical setting.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the same model ($\\ln L=-150,\\ n=200,\\ k=4$), compute HQC ($\\ln(\\ln 200) = \\ln(5.298)\\approx1.6673$):',opts:['A) $308$','B) $300$','C) $321.2$','D) $313.3$'],ans:3,exp:'$HQC = 300+2(4)(1.6673)=300+13.34=313.34\\approx313.3$. Why other options are wrong: (A) is the AIC value; (C) is the BIC value; (B) omits the penalty entirely. Shortcut: Confirm ordering: $AIC(308) &lt; HQC(313.3) &lt; BIC(321.2)$ – consistent with the known penalty-severity ranking. Tip: This three-part numeric sequence (AIC, BIC, HQC on the same model) is an excellent self-check: if your computed ordering violates $AIC&lt;HQC&lt;BIC$, recheck your arithmetic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Sample size $n=64$. The approximate 95% significance bound for sample ACF under the white-noise null is:',opts:['A) $\\pm 0.125$','B) $\\pm 1.96$','C) $\\pm 0.245$','D) $\\pm 0.5$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{n} = \\pm1.96/8 = \\pm0.245$. Why other options are wrong: (A) uses an incorrect denominator; (B) forgets to divide by $\\sqrt n$ at all; (D) has no basis in the formula. Shortcut: For $n=64$, $\\sqrt n=8$ exactly, making this an easy mental-math check. Tip: Choose sample sizes that are perfect squares (like 64, 100, 400) to practice quick mental computation of these bounds.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Ljung-Box statistic computed as $Q=18.5$ testing $m=10$ lags on residuals of a fitted ARMA(1,1) model ($n$ large). Degrees of freedom used should be:',opts:['A) $10$','B) $12$','C) $8$','D) $9$'],ans:2,exp:'$df = m-p-q = 10-1-1=8$, since one AR and one MA parameter were estimated from the same data used to compute the residual autocorrelations. Why other options are wrong: (A) wrongly uses the raw lag count without adjustment; (B) and (D) do not correspond to any correct subtraction of estimated parameters. Shortcut: Always subtract $p+q$ (total estimated ARMA parameters) from $m$ for residual-based Ljung-Box tests. Tip: Compare $Q=18.5$ against $\\chi^2_{8,0.05}\\approx15.51$ to conclude residual autocorrelation is significant (reject white-noise null) – a natural follow-up numerical step.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=-0.5$, the sign pattern of $\\rho_k = \\phi^k$ as $k=1,2,3,4,\\dots$ is:',opts:['A) All negative','B) All positive','C) Always zero','D) Alternating in sign: $-,+,-,+,\\dots$'],ans:3,exp:'With $\\phi=-0.5$: $\\rho_1=-0.5,\\rho_2=0.25,\\rho_3=-0.125,\\rho_4=0.0625,\\dots$, alternating in sign while decaying in magnitude. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (A) each ignore the alternating sign pattern produced by a negative $\\phi$; (C) is only true in the limit $k\\to\\infty$, not for finite $k$. Shortcut: Negative $\\phi$ in AR(1) $\\Rightarrow$ oscillating (alternating-sign) ACF decay; positive $\\phi \\Rightarrow$ smooth same-sign decay. Tip: A quick, high-yield identification cue: an alternating-sign correlogram immediately suggests a negative AR coefficient.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(2): $X_t = 0.5X_{t-1}+0.2X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$. Using $\\rho_1=\\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2)$, compute $\\rho_1$:',opts:['A) $0.2$','B) $0.625$','C) $0.5$','D) $0.7$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\rho_1 = \\phi_1/(1-\\phi_2) = 0.5/(1-0.2)=0.5/0.8=0.625$. Why other options are wrong: (C) is simply $\\phi_1$ itself, ignoring the denominator adjustment; (D) and (A) do not follow from the correct formula substitution. Shortcut: For AR(2), always divide $\\phi_1$ by $(1-\\phi_2)$, never by $(1-\\phi_1)$ or similar incorrect substitutions. Tip: This specific AR(2) $\\rho_1$ formula (derivable from the Yule-Walker system) is worth memorizing directly for speed.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) of the previous question ($\\phi_1=0.5,\\phi_2=0.2,\\rho_1=0.625$), using the second Yule-Walker equation $\\rho_2=\\phi_1\\rho_1+\\phi_2$, compute $\\rho_2$:',opts:['A) $0.2$','B) $0.3125$','C) $0.5125$','D) $0.625$'],ans:2,exp:'$\\rho_2 = (0.5)(0.625)+0.2 = 0.3125+0.2=0.5125$. Why other options are wrong: (D) merely repeats $\\rho_1$; (A) is just $\\phi_2$ alone; (B) omits adding $\\phi_2$ after computing the product term. Shortcut: Substitute the already-computed $\\rho_1$ carefully into the second Yule-Walker equation – a very common two-step numerical chain in JRF papers. Tip: Practice deriving $\\rho_1,\\rho_2,\\rho_3,\\dots$ sequentially for a given AR(2) using the recursive Yule-Walker relation $\\rho_k=\\phi_1\\rho_{k-1}+\\phi_2\\rho_{k-2}$ for $k\\geq2$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Two models: Model A ($p=2,q=0$), Model B ($p=1,q=1$), both with $k=3$ (including $\\sigma^2$) fitted to the same $n=150$ data with identical $\\ln L=-200$. Which has the lower AIC?',opts:['A) Both are exactly equal','B) Model A','C) Cannot be determined without more information','D) Model B'],ans:0,exp:'Since both models have identical $\\ln L$ and identical $k$, their AIC values are numerically identical: $AIC=400+6=406$ for both, regardless of the specific $(p,q)$ split. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (D) wrongly assume one model is favoured despite identical inputs to the AIC formula; (C) is incorrect since the AIC formula depends only on $\\ln L$ and $k$, both of which are given. Shortcut: AIC depends only on $\\ln L$ and total parameter count $k$ – not on how those parameters are distributed between $p$ and $q$. Tip: A useful reminder that information criteria are blind to the AR/MA split, only counting total parameters.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a random walk $X_t=X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ with $\\sigma^2=4$, the 5-step-ahead forecast error variance is:',opts:['A) $20$','B) $4$','C) $\\infty$','D) $16$'],ans:0,exp:'For a random walk, $Var(X_{t+h}-\\hat X_t(h)) = h\\sigma^2 = 5(4)=20$, growing linearly and without bound as $h$ increases, reflecting the process\'s nonstationarity. Why other options are wrong: (B) is only the 1-step value; (D) results from squaring $\\sigma^2$ instead of multiplying by $h$; (C) is only the limiting behaviour as $h\\to\\infty$, not the exact finite value at $h=5$. Shortcut: Random walk forecast error variance formula: simply $h\\sigma^2$ – one of the simplest yet most important nonstationary contrasts to memorize. Tip: Directly contrast this unbounded linear growth with the bounded AR(1) formula covered earlier – a favourite paired-question theme.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For $n=400$, the 95% white-noise ACF significance bound is:',opts:['A) $\\pm 1.96$','B) $\\pm 0.098$','C) $\\pm 0.049$','D) $\\pm 0.0245$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\pm1.96/\\sqrt{400}=\\pm1.96/20=\\pm0.098$. Why other options are wrong: (C) results from an incorrect denominator; (A) omits division by $\\sqrt n$; (D) uses an incorrect denominator. Shortcut: For $n=400$, $\\sqrt n=20$ – another convenient perfect-square sample size for quick mental verification. Tip: Always sanity check: larger $n$ must give a smaller significance band, consistent with increasing precision.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARMA(1,1): $\\phi=0.6,\\ \\theta=0.3,\\ \\sigma^2=1$. The variance of the process is given by $\\gamma_0 = \\sigma^2\\dfrac{1+2\\phi\\theta+\\theta^2}{1-\\phi^2}$. Compute $\\gamma_0$ (approx.):',opts:['A) $1.45$','B) $1.983$','C) $1.09$','D) $2.20$'],ans:3,exp:'Numerator: $1+2(0.6)(0.3)+0.09 = 1+0.36+0.09=1.45$. Denominator: $1-0.36=0.64$. $\\gamma_0=1.45/0.64\\approx2.27$, closest to option (D). Why other options are wrong: (A) is only the numerator value, not the full ratio; (B) and (C) result from incorrect denominator substitutions. Shortcut: Always compute numerator and denominator fully separately for ARMA(1,1) variance before dividing – a two-step numerical chain prone to partial-computation errors. Tip: This exact ARMA(1,1) variance formula is a favourite JRF derivation-and-compute combination question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A fitted AR(1) has $\\hat\\phi=0.7$ with estimated standard error $0.1$ ($n$ large, using asymptotic normality). An approximate 95% confidence interval for $\\phi$ is:',opts:['A) $(0.5, 0.9)$','B) $(0.3, 1.1)$','C) $(0.504, 0.896)$','D) $(0.6, 0.8)$'],ans:2,exp:'$0.7 \\pm 1.96(0.1) = 0.7\\pm0.196$, giving approximately $(0.504, 0.896)$. Why other options are wrong: (A) uses a rounded multiplier of roughly 2 instead of the precise 1.96, giving an approximately correct but less precise interval; (D) uses too small a multiplier; (B) uses too large a multiplier. Shortcut: Standard asymptotic CI construction: $\\hat\\theta \\pm 1.96\\times SE(\\hat\\theta)$ applies directly to ARMA parameter estimates under large-sample normality. Tip: Confirms that $\\phi=1$ (nonstationarity) lies just outside this particular confidence interval – a useful applied interpretation point.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(1) with $\\theta=0.5$, the theoretical $\\rho_1$ is given by $\\rho_1=\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$. Compute $\\rho_1$:',opts:['A) $0.4$','B) $0.44$','C) $0.25$','D) $0.5$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\rho_1 = 0.5/(1+0.25)=0.5/1.25=0.4$. Why other options are wrong: (D) is simply $\\theta$ itself, without the denominator adjustment; (B) and (C) result from incorrect arithmetic in the denominator or numerator. Shortcut: MA(1) $\\rho_1$ is always strictly less than $|\\theta|$ (and bounded within $[-0.5,0.5]$) due to the denominator adjustment – useful for quick sanity checks. Tip: The bound $|\\rho_1|\\leq0.5$ for any MA(1) is itself sometimes tested as a standalone conceptual fact.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the state-space/Kalman filter approach to exact ARMA likelihood evaluation, the likelihood is computed via:',opts:['A) Bootstrapping residuals repeatedly','B) The prediction error decomposition, using one-step-ahead prediction errors and their variances from the Kalman recursions','C) Direct inversion of the full $n\\times n$ covariance matrix every iteration','D) Ignoring the covariance structure entirely'],ans:1,exp:'The Kalman filter recursively produces one-step-ahead prediction errors (innovations) and their variances, which combine via the prediction error decomposition to give the exact Gaussian log-likelihood efficiently, avoiding costly direct large-matrix inversion. Why other options are wrong: Direct full-matrix inversion is computationally far more expensive and is exactly what the Kalman/innovations approach is designed to avoid; ignoring covariance structure would give an invalid likelihood; bootstrapping is a resampling technique unrelated to exact likelihood computation. Shortcut: “Prediction error decomposition” is the key phrase linking state-space methods to efficient exact ARMA likelihood computation. Tip: A genuinely research/software-implementation-level question, useful for those pursuing further econometrics/time-series research.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Ding, Granger and Engle\'s long-memory literature critiques short-memory ARMA order-selection criteria (AIC/BIC/HQC) primarily because:',opts:['A) Long-memory processes are always nonstationary, making all three criteria invalid','B) AIC and BIC give identical results for long-memory data','C) Applying finite-order ARMA order selection to genuinely long-memory (fractionally integrated) processes can lead to selection of spuriously high finite orders, since no finite ARMA order can capture hyperbolic autocorrelation decay','D) These criteria cannot be computed for long-memory data'],ans:2,exp:'Long-memory (e.g., ARFIMA) processes exhibit hyperbolically (not exponentially) decaying autocorrelations that no finite-order ARMA model can replicate exactly; applying standard order-selection criteria to such data tends to select increasingly large finite orders as $n$ grows, since the criteria are attempting to approximate a fundamentally infinite-memory structure. Why other options are wrong: The criteria remain computationally well-defined even for long-memory data (they simply select an inappropriate finite approximation); AIC and BIC generally disagree substantially in this setting, not coincide; many long-memory processes are in fact stationary (for the fractional differencing parameter $d\\in(0,0.5)$), contradicting the blanket nonstationarity claim. Shortcut: Long memory $\\Rightarrow$ hyperbolic ACF decay $\\Rightarrow$ no finite ARMA order is ever exactly correct. Tip: A genuinely advanced research-literature question connecting this unit to fractional integration/long-memory topics often found elsewhere in the syllabus.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best explains why cross-validation-based order selection (e.g., minimizing out-of-sample one-step forecast MSE) is asymptotically related to AIC rather than BIC?',opts:['A) Cross-validation always selects the true finite model, like BIC','B) Leave-one-out cross-validation has been shown to be asymptotically equivalent to AIC-based model selection under general regularity conditions','C) Cross-validation cannot be applied to time series data at all','D) They are entirely unrelated theoretically'],ans:1,exp:'A well-known theoretical result (Stone, and related work) establishes that leave-one-out cross-validation is asymptotically equivalent to AIC-based selection, both targeting predictive accuracy rather than true-model identification, explaining their shared tendency toward the same, sometimes larger, selected models. Why other options are wrong: The two methods are demonstrably related, not unrelated; cross-validation shares AIC\'s prediction-oriented (not consistency-oriented) asymptotic behaviour, so it does not behave like BIC; time-series-adapted cross-validation (e.g., rolling-origin evaluation) is a well-established and widely used practical technique. Shortcut: “Cross-validation $\\approx$ AIC (asymptotically), not BIC” – an important, often-overlooked equivalence result. Tip: A genuinely research-oriented item linking classical order-selection theory to modern predictive-validation practice.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In a Bayesian framework, BIC can be interpreted as a large-sample (Laplace) approximation to which quantity?',opts:['A) Twice the log marginal likelihood (Bayes factor numerator) under specific “unit information” prior assumptions','B) The posterior mean of the parameters','C) The maximum likelihood estimate itself','D) The prior predictive density directly, with no approximation'],ans:0,exp:'Schwarz\'s original derivation shows that $-2\\ln L + k\\ln n$ approximates $-2$ times the log marginal likelihood (up to an $O(1)$ term) under a Laplace approximation with priors carrying roughly “unit information,” directly linking BIC to Bayesian model comparison via Bayes factors. Why other options are wrong: BIC is not itself a posterior mean of parameters; it is an approximation, not an exact expression, of the marginal likelihood – so “no approximation” is false; it is unrelated to the MLE value directly, beyond using $\\ln L$ evaluated at the MLE as an input. Shortcut: “BIC $\\approx$ $-2\\times$ log Bayes factor numerator” is the essential Bayesian justification behind an otherwise frequentist-looking formula. Tip: A genuinely deep research-level connection between frequentist order-selection criteria and Bayesian model-comparison theory – occasionally tested in advanced JRF cycles.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement about penalized-likelihood order selection consistency under a diverging number of candidate models (as $n\\to\\infty$, e.g., high-dimensional ARMA/AR search) is most accurate?',opts:['A) Standard AIC/BIC consistency results always continue to hold unchanged','B) BIC becomes inconsistent while AIC becomes consistent in this setting','C) As the candidate model space grows with $n$, additional regularity/penalty adjustments (beyond the classical fixed-dimension AIC/BIC forms) are generally needed to retain selection consistency','D) Order selection becomes trivially easy as the candidate space grows'],ans:2,exp:'Classical AIC/BIC consistency proofs generally assume a fixed, finite candidate model space; when the number of candidate orders is allowed to diverge with $n$ (e.g., very high-dimensional model search), additional theoretical adjustments (larger penalties, modified criteria) are typically required in the literature to preserve consistency guarantees. Why other options are wrong: Classical fixed-dimension results do not automatically extend without modification; growing candidate spaces make selection harder, not trivially easy; the direction of the consistency reversal in (B) is not the standard finding in this literature. Shortcut: “More candidate models than fixed classical theory assumes” $\\Rightarrow$ need modified/adjusted penalty theory. Tip: A frontier research-level question, useful primarily for JRF candidates preparing for a research career in high-dimensional time-series model selection.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best describes the relationship between the Yule-Walker estimator and the innovations algorithm-based estimator for AR($p$) coefficients, in terms of the resulting fitted model\'s stationarity?',opts:['A) Both methods can produce nonstationary fitted models with equal probability','B) The innovations algorithm never converges for AR data','C) The Yule-Walker method, because it uses the sample autocovariance (positive semi-definite) sequence directly, always yields a fitted AR polynomial with roots outside the unit circle (a stationary fit), unlike unconstrained OLS','D) Neither method guarantees a stationary fitted model'],ans:2,exp:'Because the sample autocovariance sequence used in Yule-Walker estimation is guaranteed to be positive semi-definite, the resulting fitted AR coefficients are mathematically guaranteed to correspond to a stationary process (roots outside the unit circle), a property that plain unconstrained least-squares (OLS) estimation of the same AR equation does not share. Why other options are wrong: This stationarity guarantee is a specific, well-known advantage of the Yule-Walker approach, so “neither method” is incorrect; the two methods do not share equal nonstationarity risk, since Yule-Walker\'s guarantee is structural, not probabilistic; the innovations algorithm is a well-established, convergent recursive procedure for computing best linear predictors, not a non-convergent method. Shortcut: “Yule-Walker always gives a stationary fit; OLS on the same lags does not guarantee this.” Tip: A precise, often-overlooked structural advantage of Yule-Walker estimation, valuable at the JRF/research level for justifying its continued use despite generally lower efficiency than MLE.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding pivotal (approximately distribution-free) inference for ARMA order selection using information criteria?',opts:['A) The distribution is always Cauchy regardless of sample size','B) AIC/BIC/HQC differences between nested models have an exactly known finite-sample distribution for all $n$','C) No distributional theory exists connecting these criteria to likelihood-ratio tests','D) Under the null that the smaller model is correct, twice the log-likelihood-ratio (closely related to the AIC/BIC penalty comparison) is only asymptotically $\\chi^2$-distributed, not exactly so in finite samples'],ans:3,exp:'Comparing nested ARMA models via information criteria is closely connected to the classical likelihood-ratio test, whose statistic is only asymptotically chi-square distributed under standard regularity conditions; exact finite-sample distributional results are generally unavailable for ARMA models, so all such inference (including implicit information-criterion comparisons) relies on large-sample approximations. Why other options are wrong: Exact finite-sample distributions are not generally available for ARMA likelihood-ratio-type statistics, contradicting (B); a well-developed theoretical connection to likelihood-ratio testing does exist, contradicting (C); the Cauchy distribution has no role here, contradicting (A). Shortcut: “Information-criterion-based model comparisons inherit the asymptotic (not exact) nature of likelihood-ratio theory.” Tip: A genuinely subtle theoretical point, useful for JRF candidates who wish to understand precisely why order-selection inference is inherently a large-sample approximation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best characterizes the modern (post-2000) research consensus on combining multiple order-selection criteria (AIC, BIC, HQC) in applied ARMA modelling, rather than relying on a single criterion?',opts:['A) A common recommended practice is to report all three, use their agreement as a robustness signal, and fall back on residual diagnostics (e.g. Ljung-Box) when they disagree, rather than treating any single criterion as definitive','B) Only BIC should ever be used in applied work','C) Combining criteria is discouraged and considered statistically invalid','D) Only AIC should ever be used in applied work'],ans:0,exp:'Given that AIC, BIC and HQC embody genuinely different (and individually defensible) optimality goals, contemporary applied time-series practice generally recommends reporting multiple criteria together with formal residual diagnostics, using agreement across criteria as supporting evidence and resolving disagreements via out-of-sample or diagnostic evidence rather than dogmatically favouring one criterion. Why other options are wrong: Combining criteria is a standard, well-accepted applied practice, not discouraged; exclusively favouring either AIC or BIC ignores the genuinely different and complementary theoretical justifications each provides. Shortcut: “Use multiple criteria together, plus diagnostics, rather than any single criterion in isolation.” Tip: A practical, applied-research synthesis question – a good note to end deep revision of the order-selection subtopic on.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In multi-step forecasting of a nonlinear or regime-switching extension of ARMA (briefly, at a research-awareness level), the simple “iterate the conditional mean forward” approach used for linear ARMA models:',opts:['A) Is generally no longer exactly optimal, because $E[g(X_{t+1})] \\neq g(E[X_{t+1}])$ for nonlinear $g$, so naive iteration does not equal the true conditional expectation at longer horizons','B) Is unaffected by Jensen\'s inequality considerations','C) Becomes trivially easier to compute exactly in nonlinear models','D) Remains exactly optimal (MMSE) in general nonlinear settings'],ans:0,exp:'For genuinely nonlinear time-series models, naively iterating a one-step-ahead conditional-mean forecasting rule forward does not generally yield the true multi-step conditional expectation, precisely because of Jensen\'s-inequality-type effects (the expectation of a nonlinear function is not the function of the expectation); exact multi-step forecasts typically require simulation or numerical integration methods instead. Why other options are wrong: Exact optimality of naive iteration is a special property of linear Gaussian ARMA models, not nonlinear ones in general; computing the true nonlinear multi-step forecast is generally harder, not easier, than the linear case; Jensen\'s inequality is precisely the relevant consideration explaining the discrepancy, not something to be dismissed. Shortcut: “Naive iteration is exactly optimal only in the linear/Gaussian ARMA world – nonlinear extensions need simulation-based forecasting.” Tip: A forward-looking, research-orientation question connecting this classical linear-forecasting unit to more advanced nonlinear time-series topics that may appear in JRF-level or PhD-entrance style papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which best summarizes why, despite its known asymptotic inefficiency for pure order selection, AIC remains extremely widely used in applied ARMA/time-series practice?',opts:['A) AIC\'s focus on minimizing prediction error (rather than identifying a “true” finite model, which may not exist in reality) aligns well with the practical goal of most applied forecasting exercises','B) There is no good reason; it is used purely out of tradition','C) AIC is actually the most theoretically justified criterion in every possible sense','D) AIC is simply easier to compute than BIC or HQC'],ans:0,exp:'In most real applications, no finite-order ARMA model is literally “true”; the practical goal is usually good out-of-sample predictive performance, which is precisely the criterion AIC was designed to optimize (via its Kullback-Leibler/prediction-error justification), explaining its continued widespread applied popularity despite its known lack of order-selection consistency. Why other options are wrong: AIC is not universally “most justified” – its justification is specifically prediction-oriented, not all-encompassing; all three criteria have essentially identical computational cost once $\\ln L$ is obtained, so ease of computation is not a genuine distinguishing factor; dismissing its use as “pure tradition” ignores its well-founded theoretical prediction-error justification. Shortcut: “AIC targets prediction; BIC targets truth-finding – most applied practitioners actually care more about the former.” Tip: An excellent concluding synthesis question tying together the entire order-selection subtopic\'s theoretical and applied dimensions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is a necessary condition for weak stationarity?',opts:['A) Mean is a linear function of $t$','B) Covariance depends on both $t$ and $k$','C) Variance is constant and finite','D) Mean depends on time $t$'],ans:2,exp:'Weak stationarity requires constant mean, constant finite variance, and covariance depending only on lag $k$, not on $t$. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (A) describe time-varying mean; (B) contradicts the lag-only covariance requirement. Shortcut: Recall the three-part definition: constant mean, constant variance, lag-only covariance. Tip: This exact definitional question appears almost every NET cycle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The process $X_t = X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ with $\\varepsilon_t\\sim\\iid(0,\\sigma^2)$ is called:',opts:['A) Moving average process','B) Random walk','C) White noise','D) Trend-stationary process'],ans:1,exp:'This is the defining equation of a random walk. Why other options are wrong: White noise has no dependence on the past; MA process is a function of current/past errors only; trend-stationary has a deterministic trend term. Shortcut: Spot the recursive form $X_t=X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ – always random walk. Tip: Guaranteed one-mark definitional question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a random walk with $X_0=0$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$ equals:',opts:['A) $t^2\\sigma^2$','B) $t\\sigma^2$','C) $\\sigma^2/t$','D) $\\sigma^2$'],ans:1,exp:'$X_t=\\sum_{i=1}^t \\varepsilon_i$, so $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=\\sum \\operatorname{Var}(\\varepsilon_i)=t\\sigma^2$. Why other options are wrong: (D) is the variance of a single error, not the cumulative sum; (C) and (A) do not follow from summing independent variances. Shortcut: Sum of $t$ independent equal-variance terms $\\Rightarrow$ variance multiplies by $t$, not divides or squares. Tip: Frequently used as the basis of numerical NET questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'$X_t\\sim I(1)$ means:',opts:['A) $X_t$ is already stationary','B) $X_t$ is invertible','C) $X_t$ needs to be differenced twice to become stationary','D) $X_t$ needs to be differenced once to become stationary'],ans:3,exp:'$I(d)$ notation means the series must be differenced $d$ times to reach stationarity; $I(1)$ means once. Why other options are wrong: (A) describes $I(0)$; (C) describes $I(2)$; (B) is an unrelated MA property. Shortcut: Match the subscript in $I(d)$ directly to the number of differences needed. Tip: Basic notation question, appears very frequently.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The backshift operator $B$ is defined by:',opts:['A) $B X_t = -X_t$','B) $B X_t = X_{t+1}$','C) $B X_t = X_t - X_{t-1}$','D) $B X_t = X_{t-1}$'],ans:3,exp:'By definition $B X_t = X_{t-1}$, shifting the series back one period. Why other options are wrong: (B) would be a forward-shift; (C) is the differencing operator $\\nabla$, not $B$; (A) is unrelated. Shortcut: $B$ "goes back" one step – the name itself is the mnemonic. Tip: Do not confuse $B$ with $\\nabla=1-B$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An ARIMA(0,1,0) model is equivalent to:',opts:['A) AR(1) process','B) White noise','C) MA(1) process','D) Random walk'],ans:3,exp:'With $p=0,q=0$, only $d=1$ differencing remains: $\\nabla X_t=\\varepsilon_t$, the random walk. Why other options are wrong: White noise itself is stationary ($d=0$); (A) and (C) require nonzero $p$ or $q$. Shortcut: $(0,1,0)$: no AR, no MA, just one difference $\\Rightarrow$ random walk. Tip: Common "identify the special case" NET question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which test has the null hypothesis "the series has a unit root"?',opts:['A) Augmented Dickey-Fuller test','B) Shapiro-Wilk test','C) KPSS test','D) Ljung-Box test'],ans:0,exp:'ADF\'s null hypothesis is $H_0:\\rho=0$ in the test regression, equivalent to a unit root being present. Why other options are wrong: KPSS reverses this null (tests for stationarity); Ljung-Box tests for autocorrelation; Shapiro-Wilk tests normality. Shortcut: Remember: "A"DF = "A"ssumes non-stationarity under $H_0$. Tip: One of the most repeated NET/JRF questions in time series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The first-difference operator $\\nabla$ satisfies:',opts:['A) $\\nabla = 1-B$','B) $\\nabla = B-1$','C) $\\nabla = 1+B$','D) $\\nabla = B^{-1}$'],ans:0,exp:'By definition, $\\nabla X_t = X_t - X_{t-1} = (1-B)X_t$. Why other options are wrong: (C) would give $X_t+X_{t-1}$; (B) is the negative; (D) is the inverse backshift (forward shift). Shortcut: Differencing subtracts the lagged value – hence $1-B$. Tip: Basic operator algebra, tested very frequently.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A trend-stationary series becomes stationary after:',opts:['A) Differencing once','B) Applying a seasonal filter','C) Removing the deterministic trend by regression','D) Taking logarithms'],ans:2,exp:'Trend-stationary series have a deterministic trend; regressing it out (detrending) yields a stationary residual series. Why other options are wrong: Differencing (A) is the correct approach for difference-stationary (unit root) series, not trend-stationary; (D) and (B) address different issues (variance stabilization, seasonality). Shortcut: "Trend-stationary" $\\to$ "detrend"; "difference-stationary" $\\to$ "difference." Tip: A classic conceptual distinction tested almost every cycle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is an example of a random walk with drift?',opts:['A) $X_t = \\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$','B) $X_t = \\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t,\\ |\\phi|&lt;1$','C) $X_t = X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$','D) $X_t = \\delta + X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t,\\ \\delta\\neq 0$'],ans:3,exp:'The drift term $\\delta\\neq 0$ added to the random walk equation defines random walk with drift. Why other options are wrong: (C) is the plain random walk (no drift); (B) is stationary AR(1); (A) is MA(1). Shortcut: Look for the additive constant $\\delta$ alongside the $X_{t-1}$ term. Tip: Basic identification question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the ARIMA$(p,d,q)$ notation, $q$ represents:',opts:['A) Number of seasonal lags','B) Order of differencing','C) Order of the MA polynomial','D) Order of the AR polynomial'],ans:2,exp:'In ARIMA$(p,d,q)$, $p$ = AR order, $d$ = differencing order, $q$ = MA order. Why other options are wrong: (D) describes $p$; (B) describes $d$; (A) is unrelated to standard ARIMA notation (relevant to SARIMA). Shortcut: Remember the mnemonic "AR-I-MA" mapped directly to $(p,d,q)$. Tip: Order-swap distractors are the most common trap for this notation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Overdifferencing a series that is already stationary typically results in:',opts:['A) No change to the model','B) Improved forecast accuracy','C) Reduced variance','D) An artificial unit root in the MA polynomial'],ans:3,exp:'Differencing an already-stationary series introduces a spurious MA(1) component with a root near $+1$ (i.e., $\\theta\\approx -1$), and inflates variance. Why other options are wrong: (C) is opposite of what happens; (A) is false since a real structural change occurs; (B) is false as forecast accuracy typically worsens. Shortcut: "Overdifferencing $\\to$ MA root near unity" – a standard signature to memorize. Tip: Frequently tested as a diagnostic-interpretation question in JRF papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If a series requires differencing twice to become stationary, it is said to be:',opts:['A) $I(1)$','B) $I(0)$','C) $I(2)$','D) Non-integrated'],ans:2,exp:'By definition, $I(d)$ requires $d$ differences; here $d=2$. Why other options are wrong: (B) needs no differencing; (A) needs one difference; (D) is not standard terminology. Shortcut: Count the differences stated in the question and match directly to $d$. Tip: Direct definitional recall, frequently asked.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The characteristic equation of an AR($p$) process has a unit root when:',opts:['A) $\\phi(\\infty)=0$','B) $\\phi(0)=0$','C) $\\phi(1)=0$','D) $\\phi(-1)=0$'],ans:2,exp:'A unit root exists at $z=1$ when the characteristic polynomial $\\phi(z)$ evaluated at $z=1$ equals zero, i.e. $\\phi(1)=0$, equivalent to $\\sum\\phi_i=1$. Why other options are wrong: (B) evaluates at the origin, unrelated to unit roots; (D) would relate to a root at $z=-1$ (alternating/seasonal root); (A) is not meaningful for a polynomial. Shortcut: "Unit" root means root equals 1 – substitute $z=1$ into $\\phi(z)$. Tip: Tests precise algebraic understanding of the unit-root definition.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of these series is most likely to exhibit random-walk behaviour in practice?',opts:['A) Stock price levels under the efficient market hypothesis','B) IQ scores in a fixed population','C) Daily temperature deviations from seasonal average','D) Coin toss outcomes (0/1 sequence)'],ans:0,exp:'Under the efficient market hypothesis, stock prices should follow a random walk since all available information is already priced in. Why other options are wrong: (C) is typically mean-reverting/stationary around seasonal cycle; (B) is stationary (bounded, fixed distribution); (D) is i.i.d., not cumulative. Shortcut: Random walk = "cumulative sum of shocks" – match this pattern to cumulative price levels. Tip: A conceptual application question linking theory to real-world series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Spurious regression is a risk primarily when:',opts:['A) The model has no intercept','B) One or both regressors are non-stationary and unrelated','C) Both regressors are stationary','D) Sample size is very small'],ans:1,exp:'Granger and Newbold (1974) showed that regressing unrelated non-stationary ($I(1)$) series on each other yields significant but meaningless results. Why other options are wrong: (C) is the safe case where classical regression theory applies normally; (D) and (A) are not the primary cause identified in the literature. Shortcut: "Spurious" links directly to non-stationary + unrelated regressors. Tip: A conceptually rich, frequently repeated JRF-adjacent question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The KPSS test differs from the ADF test primarily in that:',opts:['A) KPSS requires larger samples','B) KPSS reverses the null and alternative hypotheses relative to ADF','C) KPSS estimates an ARIMA model directly','D) KPSS cannot be used for seasonal data'],ans:1,exp:'KPSS sets $H_0$: stationary vs. $H_1$: unit root, exactly reversed relative to ADF\'s $H_0$: unit root vs. $H_1$: stationary. Why other options are wrong: (A), (D), (C) are not defining distinctions between the two tests. Shortcut: Remember KPSS = "Konfirms Stationarity" as null (mnemonic). Tip: A very frequently tested contrast question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which model corresponds to Simple Exponential Smoothing in the ARIMA framework?',opts:['A) ARIMA(1,1,1)','B) ARIMA(1,0,0)','C) ARIMA(0,1,0)','D) ARIMA(0,1,1)'],ans:3,exp:'ARIMA(0,1,1), $\\nabla X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}$, is the ARIMA-theoretic equivalent of Simple Exponential Smoothing. Why other options are wrong: (B) is stationary AR(1); (C) is the plain random walk; (A) is a more general model, not the exact SES equivalence. Shortcut: Memorize the special-case correspondence table from the revision notes. Tip: Frequently tested equivalence fact linking forecasting methods to ARIMA.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the ADF test regression $\\nabla X_t=\\alpha+\\beta t+\\rho X_{t-1}+\\sum\\gamma_i\\nabla X_{t-i}+\\varepsilon_t$, rejecting $H_0$ implies:',opts:['A) The series has a unit root','B) The model is misspecified','C) The series is stationary','D) The series has seasonal structure'],ans:2,exp:'Rejecting $H_0:\\rho=0$ implies $\\rho&lt;0$, meaning the series reverts towards its mean, i.e. it is stationary. Why other options are wrong: (A) is what failing to reject implies; (D) and (B) are unrelated to the hypothesis test\'s outcome. Shortcut: Reject unit-root null $\\Rightarrow$ stationary; standard hypothesis-test logic. Tip: Frequently paired with KPSS contrast questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly orders the Box–Jenkins methodology steps?',opts:['A) Diagnostic Checking, Identification, Estimation','B) Estimation, Identification, Diagnostic Checking','C) Identification, Diagnostic Checking, Estimation','D) Identification, Estimation, Diagnostic Checking'],ans:3,exp:'The Box–Jenkins cycle proceeds: identify tentative model order, estimate parameters, then check diagnostics (and iterate if needed). Why other options are wrong: All other orderings misplace at least one stage relative to the standard sequence. Shortcut: "IED" – Identify, Estimate, Diagnose, in that order. Tip: A guaranteed process-sequence question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A time series plot showing a slowly, near-linearly decaying ACF most likely indicates:',opts:['A) A unit root / non-stationarity','B) A pure MA(1) process','C) White noise','D) Seasonality only'],ans:0,exp:'Slow, near-linear ACF decay is the classic visual/statistical signature of a non-stationary (unit-root) series. Why other options are wrong: White noise shows near-zero ACF at all lags $&gt;0$; MA(1) ACF cuts off after lag 1; seasonality shows periodic spikes, not slow linear decay. Shortcut: "Slow decay $=$ unit root" is a standard pattern-recognition shortcut. Tip: Frequently tested via ACF plot interpretation questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The order of integration $d$ in ARIMA$(p,d,q)$ equals:',opts:['A) The number of unit roots in the AR polynomial requiring removal','B) The number of MA terms','C) The number of AR terms','D) The forecast horizon'],ans:0,exp:'$d$ equals the minimal number of unit roots present (and hence differences needed) to render the series stationary. Why other options are wrong: (C) and (B) describe $p$ and $q$ respectively; (D) is unrelated to model order. Shortcut: Directly links $d$ to the count of unit roots in the characteristic equation. Tip: Conceptual link frequently tested alongside unit-root theorems.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement about ARIMA(1,1,0) is correct?',opts:['A) It requires two differences','B) It has no AR component after differencing','C) It is stationary without any differencing','D) $\\nabla X_t = \\phi_1 \\nabla X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$'],ans:3,exp:'ARIMA(1,1,0) has $p=1,d=1,q=0$, giving the AR(1) model on the once-differenced series: $\\nabla X_t=\\phi_1\\nabla X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$. Why other options are wrong: (B) is false since $p=1$ retains an AR term; (C) is false since $d=1$ requires differencing; (A) is false since $d=1$, not 2. Shortcut: Substitute $(p,d,q)=(1,1,0)$ directly into the general ARIMA equation. Tip: A standard "write the equation from the notation" question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of these is NOT a standard assumption for the ARMA part of an ARIMA model?',opts:['A) Errors $\\varepsilon_t$ must be normally distributed','B) Roots of $\\phi(z)=0$ lie outside the unit circle','C) $\\phi(z)$ and $\\theta(z)$ share no common factors','D) Roots of $\\theta(z)=0$ lie outside the unit circle'],ans:0,exp:'Basic ARIMA theory requires errors to be white noise (uncorrelated, mean zero, constant variance); normality is often assumed for exact inference but is not a defining structural assumption of the ARIMA specification itself. Why other options are wrong: (B), (D), (C) are genuine standard assumptions (stationarity, invertibility, identifiability). Shortcut: Distinguish "structural" assumptions from "distributional" assumptions used for inference. Tip: A subtle distinction frequently used as a trap in JRF-level papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For $X_t=\\delta+X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, $\\operatorname{E}(X_t)$ (with $X_0=0$) equals:',opts:['A) $\\delta$','B) $0$','C) $t\\sigma^2$','D) $\\delta t$'],ans:3,exp:'Recursively substituting gives $X_t=\\delta t+\\sum_{i=1}^t\\varepsilon_i$, so $\\operatorname{E}(X_t)=\\delta t$. Why other options are wrong: (B) ignores drift entirely; (A) omits the time multiplication; (C) is the variance expression, not the mean. Shortcut: Drift accumulates linearly with $t$: mean grows as $\\delta t$. Tip: A very common numerical/derivation-based question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is true about the variance of a random walk with drift?',opts:['A) It equals $\\delta^2 t$','B) It remains constant over time','C) It decreases over time','D) It equals $t\\sigma^2$, same as without drift'],ans:3,exp:'Adding a deterministic drift $\\delta t$ changes the mean but not the variance contribution, which still comes from $\\sum\\varepsilon_i$, giving $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=t\\sigma^2$. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (C) are false since variance still grows linearly; (A) confuses the drift\'s effect on mean with variance. Shortcut: Drift affects mean only; variance formula is unchanged from the plain random walk. Tip: Tests careful separation of mean vs. variance effects of drift.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A researcher observes that after one differencing, the ACF of a series cuts off sharply after lag 1. This suggests fitting:',opts:['A) ARIMA(1,1,0)','B) ARIMA(0,1,1)','C) ARIMA(1,1,1)','D) ARIMA(0,0,0)'],ans:1,exp:'A sharp cutoff in ACF after lag 1 (on the differenced series) is the classic signature of an MA(1) process, giving ARIMA(0,1,1). Why other options are wrong: (A) would be suggested by a PACF cutoff (AR signature), not ACF; (C) suggests mixed structure with both cutoffs; (D) implies no structure at all. Shortcut: ACF cutoff $\\to$ MA order; PACF cutoff $\\to$ AR order (standard Box-Jenkins identification rule). Tip: Central identification-stage skill, tested very frequently.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The seasonal differencing operator for period $s$ is defined as:',opts:['A) $1+B^s$','B) $B^s-B$','C) $1-B^s$','D) $1-B$'],ans:2,exp:'Seasonal differencing removes seasonal non-stationarity: $\\nabla_s X_t = X_t - X_{t-s} = (1-B^s)X_t$. Why other options are wrong: (D) is the ordinary (non-seasonal) differencing operator; (A) and (B) do not correspond to any standard seasonal differencing definition. Shortcut: Seasonal version simply replaces $B$ with $B^s$ in the ordinary difference operator. Tip: Key building block for SARIMA questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which combination correctly represents a SARIMA model notation?',opts:['A) ARIMA$(p,q,d)_s$','B) ARIMA$(p,d,q)\\times(P,D,Q)_s$','C) ARIMA$(P,D,Q)$ only','D) ARIMA$(p,d,q)+s$'],ans:1,exp:'SARIMA combines non-seasonal $(p,d,q)$ and seasonal $(P,D,Q)_s$ orders multiplicatively in its polynomial structure. Why other options are wrong: (C) omits the non-seasonal part; (D) is not standard notation; (A) has the order of $d$ and $q$ swapped. Shortcut: Look for both a plain triplet and a subscripted triplet with period $s$. Tip: Common notation-recognition question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following would most likely indicate that a fitted ARIMA model was overdifferenced?',opts:['A) Residuals appear as pure white noise','B) PACF of residuals shows no significant spikes','C) Ljung-Box test shows no significant autocorrelation','D) Estimated MA coefficient close to $-1$'],ans:3,exp:'An MA coefficient near $-1$ (root of $\\theta(z)$ near $+1$) is the classic sign of overdifferencing. Why other options are wrong: (B), (C), (A) all describe a well-specified, correctly-fitted model rather than a diagnostic problem. Shortcut: "MA coefficient $\\approx -1$" is the specific numerical fingerprint to watch for. Tip: A precise diagnostic-recognition question frequently repeated in JRF papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Fractionally integrated models (ARFIMA) generalize $d$ to:',opts:['A) Positive even integers only','B) Negative integers only','C) Complex numbers','D) Any real number, including non-integers'],ans:3,exp:'ARFIMA allows $d$ to be any real number, capturing long-memory processes typically for $0&lt;d&lt;0.5$. Why other options are wrong: (B), (C), (A) do not correspond to the actual generalization used in ARFIMA theory. Shortcut: "Fractional" in the name directly signals non-integer real-valued $d$. Tip: A research-level fact frequently tested at JRF level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which value range of $d$ in ARFIMA characterizes long-memory (persistent) behaviour?',opts:['A) $0&lt;d&lt;0.5$','B) $d&lt;0$','C) $d&gt;1$','D) $d=1$'],ans:0,exp:'Long memory (hyperbolically decaying autocorrelations) is associated with $0&lt;d&lt;0.5$ in the ARFIMA framework. Why other options are wrong: (B) corresponds to anti-persistence (intermediate memory in the other direction); (D) is standard $I(1)$ non-stationarity; (C) is generally outside the stationary, invertible range typically considered. Shortcut: Remember the specific bound: strictly between 0 and 0.5 for long memory. Tip: A precise numerical-range fact, often tested as a direct recall question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $\\phi(1)=0$ for an AR($p$) process?',opts:['A) The process has zero mean','B) The process is invertible','C) The process is seasonal','D) The process has at least one unit root'],ans:3,exp:'$\\phi(1)=0$ means $z=1$ solves the characteristic equation, i.e., a unit root is present, implying non-stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (A), (B), (C) are unrelated consequences of this specific algebraic condition. Shortcut: Direct application of the unit-root definition given in the theory notes. Tip: Repeatedly tested definitional-application question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In practice, if both ADF and KPSS tests suggest the opposite conclusion for the same series, the most likely explanation is:',opts:['A) Seasonal effects are absent','B) The series may be a fractionally integrated (long-memory) process','C) One of the tests is always wrong','D) The sample size is too large'],ans:1,exp:'Conflicting ADF/KPSS conclusions are a well-known empirical signature often associated with fractional integration (long memory) rather than a pure $I(0)$/$I(1)$ dichotomy. Why other options are wrong: (C) is an oversimplification; large sample size (D) generally improves rather than worsens test reliability; (A) is unrelated to the conflict described. Shortcut: Conflicting stationarity test results $\\to$ consider fractional integration as a research-level explanation. Tip: A genuinely research-oriented JRF question testing deeper conceptual integration.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following pairs is correctly matched?',opts:['A) Random walk – ARIMA(1,0,0)','B) White noise – ARIMA(0,1,0)','C) Random walk with drift – ARIMA(0,1,0) with nonzero constant','D) Simple Exponential Smoothing – ARIMA(1,1,0)'],ans:2,exp:'Random walk with drift is ARIMA(0,1,0) plus a nonzero constant term (drift) in the differenced equation. Why other options are wrong: (A) mismatches random walk with a stationary AR(1); (B) mismatches white noise, which is already $I(0)$, not $I(1)$; (D) mismatches SES, whose correct equivalence is ARIMA(0,1,1). Shortcut: Cross-check each pairing against the special-cases table from Stage 2 revision notes. Tip: A frequently used "match the correct pair" style NET question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best explains why standard $t$-distribution critical values cannot be used for the Dickey-Fuller test statistic?',opts:['A) The errors are assumed non-normal','B) Under $H_0$, the limiting distribution is a non-standard functional of Brownian motion, not Student\'s $t$','C) The sample size is always too small','D) The test uses a two-sided alternative'],ans:1,exp:'Under the unit-root null, the OLS estimator\'s scaled and centered distribution converges to a functional of Brownian motion rather than a standard $t$ or normal distribution, necessitating specially tabulated DF critical values. Why other options are wrong: (C), (D), (A) do not correctly explain the theoretical reason; the issue is asymptotic distribution theory under non-stationarity, not sample size, test-sidedness, or error normality per se. Shortcut: Recall: unit-root asymptotics differ fundamentally from stationary-regression asymptotics. Tip: One of the most conceptually demanding and frequently tested JRF-level theoretical points.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the AR(2) process $X_t=1.5X_{t-1}-0.5X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$, the characteristic equation is $1-1.5z+0.5z^2=0$. This process:',opts:['A) Is invertible but not stationary','B) Has complex roots only','C) Is stationary since both roots exceed 1 in modulus','D) Has a unit root since $z=1$ is a root'],ans:3,exp:'Substituting $z=1$: $1-1.5+0.5=0$, confirming $z=1$ is a root, so the process has a unit root and is non-stationary; factoring gives roots $z=1$ and $z=2$. Why other options are wrong: (C) is false precisely because one root equals 1 (not exceeding 1); (A) confuses AR stationarity with MA invertibility, which is not applicable here; (B) is false since roots are real (1 and 2). Shortcut: Always test $z=1$ directly in $\\phi(z)$ before assuming stationarity. Tip: A calculation-based unit-root identification question, high JRF value.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $W_t = \\nabla X_t$ follows ARMA(1,1) with $\\phi_1=0.6,\\theta_1=0.3$, and roots of both polynomials lie outside the unit circle, then $X_t$ is:',opts:['A) Non-invertible','B) ARIMA(1,0,1)','C) ARIMA(1,1,1), non-stationary in levels but with stationary, invertible differenced series','D) Stationary in levels'],ans:2,exp:'Since $W_t=\\nabla X_t$ is a stationary, invertible ARMA(1,1), $X_t$ itself is ARIMA(1,1,1): non-stationary at the level but reducible to a well-behaved stationary process after one differencing. Why other options are wrong: (D) contradicts the premise that differencing was needed; (B) omits $d=1$; (A) is false since the roots condition given confirms invertibility. Shortcut: "Differenced series is stationary ARMA" directly defines the ARIMA structure with $d=1$. Tip: Tests precise application of the ARIMA definition to a concrete parameter scenario.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is TRUE regarding forecast intervals for an ARIMA(0,1,0) model as the forecast horizon $\\ell\\to\\infty$?',opts:['A) They shrink to zero','B) They converge to a fixed width','C) They remain constant from $\\ell=1$','D) They widen without bound'],ans:3,exp:'Since the random walk\'s forecast error variance grows as $\\ell\\sigma^2$, the forecast interval widens without bound as $\\ell$ increases. Why other options are wrong: (B) applies to stationary ARMA models, not $I(1)$ processes; (A) and (C) contradict the linearly growing variance property. Shortcut: $I(1)$ processes: forecast uncertainty is unbounded in the long run, unlike stationary ARMA. Tip: A conceptually important contrast frequently tested at JRF level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement about the roots of $\\theta(z)=0$ in an ARIMA model is correct?',opts:['A) They must lie outside the unit circle for invertibility','B) They have no bearing on model validity','C) They must lie inside the unit circle for stationarity','D) They determine the order of integration $d$'],ans:0,exp:'Invertibility of the MA component requires all roots of $\\theta(z)=0$ to lie strictly outside the unit circle. Why other options are wrong: (C) confuses invertibility with stationarity (MA roots relate to invertibility, not stationarity, which concerns AR roots); (D) is false since $d$ is determined separately by differencing needed for the AR part / raw series; (B) is false as invertibility is essential for a valid, unique representation. Shortcut: "MA roots outside unit circle" = invertibility; "AR roots outside unit circle" = stationarity – do not conflate the two. Tip: A commonly confused pair of conditions, heavily tested at JRF level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Suppose a series requires $d=2$ to achieve stationarity. Which of the following real-world series is this MOST consistent with?',opts:['A) Daily returns on a stock','B) A stationary AR(1) process','C) Price index levels (e.g., CPI) which themselves show accelerating/decelerating growth','D) White noise'],ans:2,exp:'Price index levels can exhibit non-constant growth rates (a "trend in the trend"), often requiring two differences ($I(2)$) to reach stationarity, unlike typical $I(1)$ macro series. Why other options are wrong: (A) stock returns are usually already $I(0)$ (stationary); (B) and (D) are stationary by definition, requiring $d=0$. Shortcut: $I(2)$ series usually involve a changing rate of change, not just a changing level. Tip: An applied, higher-order-integration question typical of advanced JRF papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Wold decomposition theorem is relevant to ARIMA modelling because:',opts:['A) It defines the unit-root test','B) It is unrelated to time series','C) It proves all series are stationary','D) It justifies representing any purely non-deterministic stationary process as an infinite MA process, underlying ARMA theory applied after differencing'],ans:3,exp:'Wold\'s theorem underlies the MA($\\infty$) representation of stationary processes, which is the theoretical foundation for ARMA modelling applied to the differenced (stationary) series in ARIMA. Why other options are wrong: (C) is false, as Wold\'s theorem concerns representation of already-stationary processes, not a universal stationarity claim; (A) misattributes an unrelated result; (B) is factually incorrect. Shortcut: Wold\'s theorem $\\to$ MA($\\infty$) representation $\\to$ theoretical basis for ARMA/ARIMA. Tip: A theory-linking question frequently used to test depth of understanding.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A model is estimated as ARIMA(2,1,1) but diagnostic checking reveals residual autocorrelation at lag 3. The most appropriate next Box-Jenkins step is:',opts:['A) Immediately discard ARIMA modelling altogether','B) Ignore the residual correlation as it is negligible','C) Accept the model as final','D) Re-identify a revised model (e.g., increase $p$ or $q$) and repeat estimation and diagnostics'],ans:3,exp:'The Box-Jenkins methodology is iterative: significant residual autocorrelation signals model inadequacy, requiring re-identification (revising $p$, $d$, or $q$) followed by re-estimation and re-diagnosis. Why other options are wrong: (C) and (B) ignore evidence of misspecification; (A) is an overreaction when the ARIMA framework itself remains appropriate, just needs refinement. Shortcut: Diagnostic failure $\\to$ loop back to identification stage, not abandon the framework. Tip: Tests understanding of the iterative nature of Box-Jenkins methodology.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best distinguishes a "stochastic trend" from a "deterministic trend"?',opts:['A) Both terms are synonymous','B) A stochastic trend has constant slope over time; a deterministic trend does not','C) A deterministic trend can never be removed','D) A stochastic trend accumulates random shocks permanently; a deterministic trend follows a fixed, predictable function of time'],ans:3,exp:'A stochastic trend (unit root) means shocks have permanent effects that accumulate over time; a deterministic trend is a fixed function of $t$ (e.g., $\\alpha+\\beta t$) unaffected by random shocks\' persistence. Why other options are wrong: (B) reverses key features incorrectly; (A) is false, as they require fundamentally different treatments; (C) is false since deterministic trends are easily removed via regression. Shortcut: "Stochastic" = random and permanent; "deterministic" = fixed and predictable. Tip: A precise conceptual distinction central to JRF-level understanding.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is a valid reason for preferring differencing over detrending when a series has a genuine unit root?',opts:['A) Detrending removes stochastic shocks\' permanent effect incorrectly, leaving remaining non-stationarity','B) Detrending is computationally impossible','C) Differencing removes seasonality automatically','D) Differencing always increases sample size'],ans:0,exp:'If the true trend is stochastic, detrending (regression on $t$) fails to remove the unit root\'s permanent shock effects, so the "detrended" residual remains non-stationary; only differencing correctly addresses a true unit root. Why other options are wrong: (D) is false, as differencing reduces the effective sample size by $d$ observations; (B) is factually wrong; (C) conflates ordinary and seasonal differencing incorrectly. Shortcut: Match the type of non-stationarity to the correct remedy: unit root $\\to$ differencing. Tip: Tests deep understanding of why the trend-stationary/difference-stationary distinction matters practically.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An analyst notices that $\\nabla X_t$ still shows a slowly-decaying ACF. This most likely suggests:',opts:['A) No further action is needed','B) $d=1$ was insufficient; consider $d=2$','C) The series was already stationary before differencing','D) The MA order should be reduced to zero'],ans:1,exp:'Persistent slow ACF decay after one differencing suggests the true order of integration may be higher, warranting a second difference ($d=2$). Why other options are wrong: (C) contradicts the observed slow decay, which is a non-stationarity signature; (D) and (A) ignore the diagnostic evidence presented. Shortcut: Slow ACF decay is diagnostic regardless of how many times you\'ve already differenced – keep checking. Tip: A layered diagnostic-reasoning question typical of advanced JRF item banks.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Cointegration extends ARIMA-type analysis by:',opts:['A) Requiring all series to be stationary before analysis','B) Eliminating the need for differencing entirely','C) Allowing two or more $I(1)$ series to have a stationary linear combination despite individual non-stationarity','D) Applying only to seasonal series'],ans:2,exp:'Cointegration (Engle-Granger) identifies cases where individually non-stationary $I(1)$ series move together such that a specific linear combination is stationary, enabling valid long-run regression relationships. Why other options are wrong: (A) contradicts the very premise of cointegration (series remain individually non-stationary); (B) and (D) misstate the scope of the concept. Shortcut: Cointegration = "non-stationary individually, but stationary together" – the defining paradox. Tip: A natural extension question frequently used to test broader conceptual linkage.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following would most strongly suggest a series should be modelled as SARIMA rather than plain ARIMA?',opts:['A) Constant variance throughout','B) Regular, periodic spikes in the ACF at seasonal lags (e.g., every 12th lag for monthly data)','C) Presence of a linear deterministic trend only','D) A single outlier in the data'],ans:1,exp:'Periodic ACF spikes at seasonal lags (e.g., 12, 24 for monthly data) directly indicate seasonal dependence requiring seasonal AR/MA/differencing terms, i.e., a SARIMA specification. Why other options are wrong: (C) suggests a deterministic trend adjustment, not necessarily seasonality; (D) and (A) are unrelated to seasonal structure. Shortcut: Seasonal ACF spikes at multiples of the seasonal period $s$ are the key visual cue for SARIMA. Tip: A practical model-selection question frequently tested in applied JRF items.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRUE or FALSE: "Every ARIMA model is non-stationary."',opts:['A) False, but only for seasonal models','B) True, since ARIMA always involves differencing','C) True, because $d$ is always greater than zero','D) False; ARIMA(p,0,q) is stationary since $d=0$ implies no differencing is applied'],ans:3,exp:'When $d=0$, ARIMA$(p,0,q)$ reduces exactly to a stationary, invertible ARMA$(p,q)$ model; ARIMA does not always imply non-stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (C) incorrectly assume $d&gt;0$ always; (A) incorrectly restricts the exception to seasonal cases only. Shortcut: Always check the value of $d$ before assuming non-stationarity from the "ARIMA" label alone. Tip: A classic examiner trap exploiting the common misconception that ARIMA $\\Rightarrow$ non-stationary by definition.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRUE or FALSE: "The KPSS test\'s null hypothesis is that a unit root is present."',opts:['A) False; KPSS has no defined null hypothesis','B) True, but only for seasonal series','C) True','D) False; KPSS\'s null is that the series is stationary'],ans:3,exp:'KPSS reverses the ADF convention: its null hypothesis is stationarity, with the unit root as the alternative. Why other options are wrong: (C) and (B) state the opposite of the correct null; (A) is false, as KPSS does have a well-defined null. Shortcut: Do not default to assuming all stationarity-related tests share ADF\'s null structure. Tip: A very frequently used trap question reversing test conventions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRUE or FALSE: "Differencing a series always removes any need for further checking."',opts:['A) False, only for seasonal data','B) False; the differenced series must still be checked for remaining unit roots or overdifferencing','C) True, but only for random walks','D) True, differencing guarantees stationarity'],ans:1,exp:'Differencing does not automatically guarantee correct order was chosen; the differenced series should still be examined (via ACF/PACF, unit-root tests) for adequacy. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (C) wrongly assume automatic correctness; (A) incorrectly restricts the caveat to seasonal data only. Shortcut: Always verify post-differencing diagnostics rather than assuming success. Tip: Tests whether students blindly apply differencing without verification – a common trap.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRUE or FALSE: "A random walk has a constant variance over time."',opts:['A) True','B) False, variance decreases over time','C) False; variance grows linearly as $t\\sigma^2$','D) True, if the drift is zero'],ans:2,exp:'The random walk\'s variance is $t\\sigma^2$, which grows without bound as $t$ increases, regardless of drift. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (D) incorrectly claim constancy; (B) incorrectly claims a decreasing trend. Shortcut: Drift affects the mean, not whether variance is constant – do not conflate the two. Tip: A frequently repeated trap testing careful distinction between mean and variance behaviour.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRUE or FALSE: "Trend-stationary and difference-stationary series require the same corrective procedure."',opts:['A) True, both require detrending','B) True, both require differencing','C) False; trend-stationary requires detrending, difference-stationary requires differencing','D) False, neither requires any correction'],ans:2,exp:'These are fundamentally different types of non-stationarity requiring different remedies: detrending for deterministic trends, differencing for stochastic (unit-root) trends. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (A) incorrectly assume a single universal remedy; (D) incorrectly claims no correction is needed at all. Shortcut: Always match the diagnosis (deterministic vs. stochastic trend) to the correct remedy. Tip: One of the most heavily tested conceptual traps in this topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRUE or FALSE: "The order $d$ in ARIMA can be estimated by simply counting how many times the series \'looks like\' it needs differencing from a plot alone, with no formal test required."',opts:['A) True, visual inspection alone is always sufficient and preferred over formal testing','B) True, but only for JRF-level analysis','C) False, formal tests are only needed for seasonal data','D) False; while plots provide preliminary guidance, formal unit-root tests (ADF, KPSS) should confirm the appropriate order of differencing'],ans:3,exp:'Visual inspection (ACF, time plot) is useful for a preliminary assessment, but rigorous practice requires confirmation via formal unit-root tests before finalizing $d$. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (B) overstate the sufficiency of visual methods alone; (C) incorrectly limits the need for formal testing to seasonal cases only. Shortcut: Use plots for intuition, but formal tests for confirmation – exam answers should reflect both. Tip: Tests whether students appreciate the complementary role of graphical and formal statistical methods.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRUE or FALSE: "An ARIMA(0,0,0) model is simply white noise."',opts:['A) True','B) True, but only if $\\sigma^2=1$','C) False; it always includes a trend term','D) False; it does not exist as a valid model'],ans:0,exp:'With $p=q=d=0$, the model reduces to $X_t=\\varepsilon_t$, which is exactly white noise. Why other options are wrong: (D) is false since this is indeed a valid (if trivial) ARIMA special case; (B) incorrectly imposes an unnecessary variance restriction; (C) incorrectly attributes a trend that is not implied by this specification. Shortcut: Set all three orders to zero and simplify the general ARIMA equation directly. Tip: Basic but occasionally confusing edge-case question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRUE or FALSE: "A unit root always implies the series is a pure random walk."',opts:['A) False; a unit root only implies $d\\geq 1$ is needed, but the differenced series can follow any stationary ARMA$(p,q)$ structure, not necessarily white noise','B) True, if there is no drift','C) True','D) False, because unit roots do not exist in AR processes'],ans:0,exp:'A unit root establishes non-stationarity requiring differencing, but after differencing the resulting stationary process can have any valid ARMA$(p,q)$ structure – the pure random walk is just the simplest special case (ARIMA(0,1,0)). Why other options are wrong: (C) and (B) incorrectly generalize the simplest case to all unit-root series; (D) is factually false since unit roots are a standard feature examined in AR processes. Shortcut: "Unit root" only tells you about $d$; it says nothing about $p$ or $q$. Tip: A subtle but high-value distinction frequently exploited as a trap.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRUE or FALSE: "Seasonal differencing $(1-B^s)$ and ordinary differencing $(1-B)$ always give identical results."',opts:['A) False, seasonal differencing is never useful','B) True, only when $s=1$','C) False; they remove different types of non-stationarity (seasonal periodicity vs. general trend) and are generally not interchangeable','D) True'],ans:2,exp:'Ordinary differencing removes trend-type non-stationarity, while seasonal differencing specifically removes periodic (seasonal) non-stationarity; they address different features and are often both needed together. Why other options are wrong: (D) is false in general; (B) is a trivial degenerate case ($s=1$ makes them coincide, but this is not the general claim); (A) is false, as seasonal differencing is essential for many real series (e.g., monthly sales data). Shortcut: Match each differencing type to the specific non-stationarity feature it targets. Tip: Tests precise understanding of the distinct roles of the two differencing operators.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'TRUE or FALSE: "In practice, both $p$ and $q$ must always be nonzero for a valid ARIMA model."',opts:['A) True, unless $d=0$','B) True','C) False, only $q$ can be zero, never $p$','D) False; either or both of $p,q$ may be zero, giving pure AR, pure MA, or pure differenced (I only) special cases'],ans:3,exp:'Special cases like ARIMA(1,1,0) ($q=0$) or ARIMA(0,1,1) ($p=0$) are both valid and commonly used; there is no requirement that both be nonzero. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (A) incorrectly impose an unnecessary joint nonzero requirement; (C) arbitrarily restricts which parameter can be zero, which is incorrect. Shortcut: Review the special-cases table – many valid models have $p=0$ or $q=0$. Tip: A frequently used trap targeting incomplete understanding of ARIMA\'s flexibility.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) All stationary processes are $I(0)$. (II) All $I(0)$ processes are stationary. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Neither I nor II','B) Only II','C) Only I','D) Both I and II'],ans:3,exp:'By definition, $I(0)$ precisely means "already stationary, requiring zero differences," making both statements true as they describe the same equivalence in both directions. Why other options are wrong: Options (C), (B), (A) each incorrectly deny at least one direction of this definitional equivalence. Shortcut: $I(0)$ and "stationary" are essentially synonymous in this context – the equivalence holds both ways. Tip: A standard definitional statement-based question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) The ADF test\'s null hypothesis is that the series is stationary. (II) The KPSS test\'s null hypothesis is that the series is stationary. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Both I and II','B) Only II','C) Only I','D) Neither I nor II'],ans:1,exp:'Statement I is false since ADF\'s null is a unit root (non-stationarity); Statement II is true, correctly describing KPSS\'s null. Why other options are wrong: (C) incorrectly validates the false ADF statement; (A) incorrectly validates both; (D) incorrectly denies the true KPSS statement. Shortcut: Keep a clear mental table of which test has which null before answering such paired statements. Tip: A very common paired true/false format testing the ADF/KPSS contrast.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) Overdifferencing can introduce a spurious MA unit root. (II) Underdifferencing leaves residual non-stationarity in the fitted model. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only II','B) Only I','C) Neither I nor II','D) Both I and II'],ans:3,exp:'Both statements correctly describe well-known consequences: overdifferencing introduces artificial MA structure near a unit root, while underdifferencing fails to remove genuine non-stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (A) each deny one true statement; (C) denies both, which are in fact both true. Shortcut: Both directions of differencing error have well-documented, distinct consequences – know both. Tip: Tests comprehensive understanding of differencing pitfalls in both directions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) A deterministic trend can be removed by regressing the series on time. (II) A stochastic trend can also be fully removed by regressing on time. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Both I and II','B) Neither I nor II','C) Only I','D) Only II'],ans:2,exp:'Statement I is correct (standard detrending); Statement II is false, since regressing a stochastic-trend (unit-root) series on time does not remove the permanent stochastic component – only differencing does. Why other options are wrong: (D) incorrectly validates the false claim about stochastic trends; (A) incorrectly validates both; (B) incorrectly denies the true first statement. Shortcut: Deterministic trend $\\to$ regression works; stochastic trend $\\to$ regression does not fully work. Tip: A high-value conceptual statement pairing frequently tested at JRF level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) ARIMA(p,d,q) with $d&gt;0$ implies the raw series is non-stationary. (II) The differenced series $W_t=\\nabla^d X_t$ in a correctly specified ARIMA model is stationary and invertible. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Neither I nor II','B) Only I','C) Both I and II','D) Only II'],ans:2,exp:'Both statements are core parts of the ARIMA definition: $d&gt;0$ signals raw non-stationarity, and by construction the differenced series is stationary/invertible ARMA. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (D) each incorrectly deny one true statement; (A) incorrectly denies both. Shortcut: These two statements together essentially restate the formal ARIMA definition. Tip: A comprehensive definitional-check question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) Random walk with drift has a linear deterministic component in its mean. (II) Random walk with drift is stationary. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only II','B) Both I and II','C) Neither I nor II','D) Only I'],ans:3,exp:'Statement I is correct ($\\operatorname{E}(X_t)=\\delta t$ is linear in $t$); Statement II is false, since variance still grows as $t\\sigma^2$, so the process remains non-stationary. Why other options are wrong: (A) wrongly validates the false stationarity claim; (B) wrongly validates both; (C) wrongly denies the true first statement. Shortcut: Presence of drift changes the mean structure but does NOT confer stationarity. Tip: A frequently repeated trap-adjacent statement pairing.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) Wold\'s decomposition applies only to non-stationary processes. (II) Wold\'s decomposition underlies the MA($\\infty$) representation used in ARMA theory. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only I','B) Neither I nor II','C) Only II','D) Both I and II'],ans:2,exp:'Statement I is false since Wold\'s theorem applies to (purely non-deterministic) stationary processes, not non-stationary ones; Statement II is correctly describing its theoretical role. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (D) incorrectly validate the false first statement; (B) incorrectly denies the true second statement. Shortcut: Wold\'s theorem is fundamentally a stationary-process result – this is a common point of confusion. Tip: A conceptually demanding statement-based question testing precise theorem scope.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) SARIMA models can incorporate both seasonal and non-seasonal differencing simultaneously. (II) The general SARIMA equation involves four polynomial components: $\\phi(B),\\Phi(B^s),\\theta(B),\\Theta(B^s)$. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only II','B) Both I and II','C) Only I','D) Neither I nor II'],ans:1,exp:'Both statements accurately describe the SARIMA structure: simultaneous seasonal and ordinary differencing, and four separate AR/MA polynomial components (seasonal and non-seasonal). Why other options are wrong: (C) and (A) each incorrectly deny one true statement; (D) incorrectly denies both. Shortcut: Recall the full SARIMA equation structure from the theory notes to verify both claims directly. Tip: Tests complete recall of the SARIMA formulation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) Cointegrated series must each individually be $I(1)$ (or higher) for cointegration to be meaningful. (II) A cointegrating relationship, when it exists, is itself typically $I(0)$ (stationary). Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Neither I nor II','B) Only II','C) Both I and II','D) Only I'],ans:2,exp:'Both statements are foundational to cointegration theory: the individual series are non-stationary, but a specific linear combination among them is stationary. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (B) each incorrectly deny one true statement; (A) incorrectly denies both true statements. Shortcut: Cointegration = individually non-stationary + jointly stationary combination – both parts are needed for the definition. Tip: A well-integrated conceptual question linking ARIMA extensions to cointegration.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider: (I) ARFIMA models are used to capture long-memory (persistent) dependence not well captured by standard ARIMA. (II) In ARFIMA, $d$ must always be a positive integer. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only I','B) Only II','C) Both I and II','D) Neither I nor II'],ans:0,exp:'Statement I correctly describes ARFIMA\'s purpose; Statement II is false, since ARFIMA specifically allows non-integer (fractional) $d$, which is its defining feature. Why other options are wrong: (B) wrongly validates the false integer-only claim; (C) wrongly validates both; (D) wrongly denies the true first statement. Shortcut: The entire point of ARFIMA is to allow fractional, not integer-only, values of $d$. Tip: Tests precise recall of the ARFIMA definition versus standard ARIMA.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Model) with List II (Equation/Property), then select the correct code. List I | List II; (a) ARIMA(0,1,0) | (i) $\\nabla X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}$; (b) ARIMA(0,1,1) | (ii) $\\nabla X_t=\\phi_1\\nabla X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$; (c) ARIMA(1,1,0) | (iii) $\\nabla X_t=\\varepsilon_t$; (d) ARIMA(0,2,2) | (iv) $\\nabla^2 X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1}+\\theta_2\\varepsilon_{t-2}$ Codes:',opts:['A) (a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)','B) (a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)','C) (a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)','D) (a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)'],ans:2,exp:'Substituting each $(p,d,q)$ into the general ARIMA equation confirms: (a)$\\to$(iii), (b)$\\to$(i), (c)$\\to$(ii), (d)$\\to$(iv). Why other options are wrong: All other codes contain at least one mismatched pairing when checked against the general ARIMA formula substitution. Shortcut: Substitute each triplet directly into $\\phi(B)\\nabla^dX_t=\\theta(B)\\varepsilon_t$ and compare. Tip: A classic match-the-following format on ARIMA special cases.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Test/Concept) with List II (Key Feature), then select the correct code. List I | List II; (a) ADF test | (i) Null: stationarity; (b) KPSS test | (ii) Null: unit root; (c) Wold\'s theorem | (iii) MA($\\infty$) representation; (d) Overdifferencing | (iv) MA root near $+1$ Codes:',opts:['A) (a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)','B) (a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)','C) (a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)','D) (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)'],ans:2,exp:'Correct matching: ADF\'s null is unit root (ii), KPSS\'s null is stationarity (i), Wold\'s theorem gives MA($\\infty$) (iii), overdifferencing gives MA root near 1 (iv). Why other options are wrong: Other codes swap the ADF/KPSS null assignments or misassign the Wold/overdifferencing pairs. Shortcut: Fix ADF=unit-root-null and KPSS=stationarity-null firmly in memory before attempting such matches. Tip: Tests integrated recall across several key concepts simultaneously.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Researcher/Year) with List II (Contribution), then select the correct code. List I | List II; (a) Box | Jenkins (1970) | (i) Unit root test; (b) Dickey | Fuller (1979) | (ii) Spurious regression; (c) Granger | Newbold (1974) | (iii) Wold decomposition; (d) Wold (1938) | (iv) Identification-estimation-diagnostics cycle Codes:',opts:['A) (a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)','B) (a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)','C) (a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)','D) (a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)'],ans:3,exp:'Correct historical attributions: Box &amp; Jenkins formalized the identification-estimation-diagnostics cycle; Dickey &amp; Fuller developed the unit-root test; Granger &amp; Newbold identified spurious regression; Wold proved the decomposition theorem. Why other options are wrong: All other codes misattribute at least one contribution to the wrong researcher(s). Shortcut: Cross-reference against the Historical Background section from Stage 1 theory notes. Tip: A historically-oriented matching question occasionally tested at JRF level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Series Type) with List II (Correct Remedy), then select the correct code. List I | List II; (a) Trend-stationary | (i) Seasonal differencing; (b) Difference-stationary | (ii) Detrending (regression on $t$); (c) Seasonal non-stationarity | (iii) Ordinary differencing Codes:',opts:['A) (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i)','B) (a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i)','C) (a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii)','D) (a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii)'],ans:0,exp:'Trend-stationary is corrected by detrending (ii), difference-stationary by ordinary differencing (iii), and seasonal non-stationarity by seasonal differencing (i). Why other options are wrong: All other codes swap at least one series-remedy pairing incorrectly. Shortcut: Match each type of non-stationarity to its uniquely appropriate corrective technique. Tip: Reinforces the central trend-stationary vs. difference-stationary distinction via matching format.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A random walk has $\\sigma^2=4$. What is $\\operatorname{Var}(X_{10})$ given $X_0=0$?',opts:['A) 100','B) 40','C) 10','D) 4'],ans:1,exp:'$\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=t\\sigma^2=10\\times 4=40$. Why other options are wrong: (D) is just $\\sigma^2$; (C) is just $t$; (A) incorrectly computes $t^2$ instead of $t$. Shortcut: Direct substitution into $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=t\\sigma^2$. Tip: A simple, high-frequency numerical application.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a random walk with drift $\\delta=2$ and $X_0=0$, find $\\operatorname{E}(X_5)$.',opts:['A) 2','B) 10','C) 5','D) 25'],ans:1,exp:'$\\operatorname{E}(X_t)=\\delta t = 2\\times 5=10$. Why other options are wrong: (A) is just $\\delta$; (C) is just $t$; (D) incorrectly computes $t^2$. Shortcut: Direct substitution into $\\operatorname{E}(X_t)=\\delta t$. Tip: Straightforward numerical question, frequently repeated with different numbers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given a random walk with $\\sigma^2=9$, find $\\operatorname{Cov}(X_8,X_{12})$.',opts:['A) 8','B) 9','C) 108','D) 72'],ans:3,exp:'$\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t,X_{t+k})=\\min(t,t+k)\\sigma^2=8\\times 9=72$ (using the smaller time index, here $t=8$). Why other options are wrong: (A) is just the time index; (B) is just $\\sigma^2$; (C) incorrectly uses $12\\times 9$ instead of the smaller index $8\\times 9$. Shortcut: Always use the SMALLER of the two time indices when computing random walk covariance. Tip: A frequently mis-answered numerical question due to the min-index rule.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An AR(1) process has characteristic equation $1-0.8z=0$. Is this process stationary?',opts:['A) No','B) Yes, since the root $z=1/0.8=1.25$ has modulus $&gt;1$, satisfying the stationarity condition','C) Cannot be determined','D) No, since $z=1$ is a root'],ans:1,exp:'Solving $1-0.8z=0$ gives $z=1.25$, with $|z|=1.25&gt;1$, lying outside the unit circle, so the AR(1) process is stationary ($|\\phi_1|=0.8&lt;1$ is the equivalent standard condition). Why other options are wrong: (A) incorrectly concludes non-stationarity; (D) is false since $z=1$ is not a root here ($1-0.8(1)=0.2\\neq 0$); (C) is incorrect since the root is directly computable. Shortcut: For AR(1), root outside unit circle $\\Leftrightarrow$ $|\\phi_1|&lt;1$ – a quick equivalent check. Tip: Tests the direct algebraic verification of the stationarity condition via root computation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'An MA(1) model has estimated $\\hat\\theta_1=-0.95$. What does this suggest?',opts:['A) Possible overdifferencing, since the root of $\\theta(z)=0$ is close to $+1$','B) The series needs further differencing','C) The model is non-invertible','D) The model is definitely correctly specified'],ans:0,exp:'$\\hat\\theta_1$ close to $-1$ implies the MA root of $1+\\theta_1 z=0 \\Rightarrow z=-1/\\theta_1\\approx 1.05$, close to 1 – a classic overdifferencing symptom. Why other options are wrong: (D) overstates certainty; (B) is the opposite of the correct interpretation (this suggests too much, not too little, differencing); (C) is not directly implied since the root magnitude ($\\approx 1.05$) still technically exceeds 1, though it is borderline. Shortcut: Coefficient near $-1$ (root near $+1$) is the numerical fingerprint of overdifferencing. Tip: A calculation-supported diagnostic question frequently tested at JRF level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Compute $\\nabla^2 X_t$ in terms of $X_t, X_{t-1}, X_{t-2}$.',opts:['A) $2X_t - X_{t-1} - X_{t-2}$','B) $X_t - X_{t-2}$','C) $X_t + 2X_{t-1} - X_{t-2}$','D) $X_t - 2X_{t-1} + X_{t-2}$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\nabla^2 X_t = (1-B)^2 X_t = X_t - 2X_{t-1}+X_{t-2}$ by binomial expansion of $(1-B)^2=1-2B+B^2$. Why other options are wrong: (B) omits the middle term entirely; (C) and (A) have incorrect signs/coefficients not matching the binomial expansion. Shortcut: Expand $(1-B)^2$ using the binomial theorem before substituting into $X_t$. Tip: A standard operator-algebra numerical question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A monthly series requires both first-order ordinary differencing and first-order seasonal differencing ($s=12$) to become stationary. What is the correct combined differencing expression?',opts:['A) $(1-B)(1-B^{12})X_t$','B) $(1-B)X_t$ alone suffices','C) $(1-B^{12})X_t$ alone suffices','D) $(1-B)+(1-B^{12})X_t$'],ans:0,exp:'Applying both differences requires their product: $(1-B)(1-B^{12})X_t$, representing $d=1$ ordinary and $D=1$ seasonal differencing combined. Why other options are wrong: (D) incorrectly adds rather than multiplies the operators; (C) and (B) each omit one required differencing operation. Shortcut: Multiple types of differencing needed are combined multiplicatively, not additively. Tip: Frequently tested SARIMA-related numerical/notational question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $X_t \\sim I(2)$, how many times must the series be differenced to reach an $I(0)$ (stationary) representation?',opts:['A) 0','B) 1','C) 2','D) 3'],ans:2,exp:'By definition of $I(2)$, exactly two differences are required to achieve stationarity. Why other options are wrong: (A) would apply to already-stationary $I(0)$ series; (B) applies to $I(1)$; (D) overstates the requirement. Shortcut: Directly read off the subscript in $I(d)$ notation. Tip: A very basic but frequently repeated numerical/definitional question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given ARIMA(2,1,1) with $\\phi_1=0.4,\\phi_2=0.2,\\theta_1=0.3$, write the equation for the differenced series $W_t=\\nabla X_t$.',opts:['A) $X_t - 0.4X_{t-1}-0.2X_{t-2}=\\varepsilon_t+0.3\\varepsilon_{t-1}$','B) $W_t - 0.4W_{t-1}-0.2W_{t-2}=\\varepsilon_t+0.3\\varepsilon_{t-1}$','C) $W_t + 0.4W_{t-1}+0.2W_{t-2}=\\varepsilon_t-0.3\\varepsilon_{t-1}$','D) $W_t = 0.4W_{t-1}+0.2W_{t-2}+0.3\\varepsilon_{t-1}$'],ans:1,exp:'Substituting into $\\phi(B)W_t=\\theta(B)\\varepsilon_t$ with the given coefficients gives $W_t-0.4W_{t-1}-0.2W_{t-2}=\\varepsilon_t+0.3\\varepsilon_{t-1}$, correctly applied to the once-differenced series $W_t$, not the raw levels. Why other options are wrong: (D) omits the current error term $\\varepsilon_t$ entirely; (A) incorrectly applies the equation to levels $X_t$ rather than the required differenced series $W_t$; (C) has incorrect signs on both AR and MA coefficients. Shortcut: Always difference first ($W_t=\\nabla X_t$), then apply the stationary ARMA($p,q$) equation to $W_t$, not $X_t$. Tip: Tests careful, error-free equation construction from given parameters – a common JRF numerical task.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A series $X_t$ has sample variance of first differences equal to 25, while the sample variance of second differences is 55. What does this most likely suggest?',opts:['A) The series is best modelled with $d=2$','B) The series is best modelled with $d=1$; second-differencing has inflated variance, suggesting overdifferencing','C) No conclusion can be drawn','D) The series requires $d=0$'],ans:1,exp:'An increase in variance upon taking a further (second) difference is the classic sign of overdifferencing, indicating that $d=1$ was already sufficient. Why other options are wrong: (A) incorrectly favors the higher-variance, overdifferenced option; (D) ignores evidence that at least one difference was needed; (C) is an overly cautious non-answer when clear diagnostic evidence is present. Shortcut: Variance increasing upon further differencing signals you have gone one difference too far. Tip: Tests numerical diagnostic reasoning for choosing $d$ correctly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARIMA(1,1,0) model with $\\phi_1=0.5$, if $X_{t-1}=100$ and $X_{t-2}=95$, and $\\varepsilon_t=2$, find the one-step-ahead forecast component contribution from the AR term (i.e., $\\phi_1 \\nabla X_{t-1}$).',opts:['A) 100','B) 2.5','C) 5','D) 50'],ans:1,exp:'$\\nabla X_{t-1}=X_{t-1}-X_{t-2}=100-95=5$; the AR contribution is $\\phi_1\\nabla X_{t-1}=0.5\\times 5=2.5$. Why other options are wrong: (C) omits multiplying by $\\phi_1$; (D) and (A) do not correctly follow the required differencing and AR-coefficient multiplication steps. Shortcut: First compute the difference, then multiply by the AR coefficient – do not skip either step. Tip: A multi-step numerical question combining differencing and AR-term computation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Two series $Y_t$ and $Z_t$ are each $I(1)$, but $Y_t - 1.5Z_t$ is found to be $I(0)$. What is the implication?',opts:['A) The relationship is necessarily spurious','B) $Y_t$ and $Z_t$ are unrelated','C) $Y_t$ and $Z_t$ are cointegrated with cointegrating vector $(1,-1.5)$','D) Both series must actually be $I(0)$'],ans:2,exp:'A stationary linear combination of two individually $I(1)$ series is the defining feature of cointegration, here with cointegrating vector $(1,-1.5)$. Why other options are wrong: (B) contradicts the very existence of a stationary combination; (D) contradicts the stated premise that each series is individually $I(1)$; (A) is the opposite conclusion – cointegration specifically rules out spuriousness for this relationship. Shortcut: Stationary linear combination of $I(1)$ series $\\Rightarrow$ cointegration, not spuriousness. Tip: Tests numerical/conceptual integration of cointegration theory with a concrete example.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In modern time series research, which of the following best describes the relationship between ARIMA and structural break models?',opts:['A) ARIMA assumes constant parameters over time, while structural break models allow for regime shifts, which can be mistaken for unit roots if ignored','B) ARIMA always outperforms structural break models','C) Structural break models never require differencing','D) They are identical in all respects'],ans:0,exp:'A well-known research finding (Perron, 1989) is that ignoring structural breaks can lead to spurious findings of unit roots when the true process is actually stationary around a shifting mean/trend. Why other options are wrong: (D) and (B) overstate equivalence or universal superiority incorrectly; (C) is not a defining feature distinguishing the two frameworks. Shortcut: Structural breaks can masquerade as unit roots – a key research-level caveat to unit-root testing. Tip: A genuinely research-oriented question reflecting advanced literature (Perron\'s critique).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is a known limitation of the standard ADF test in the presence of structural breaks?',opts:['A) ADF test may have low power to reject the unit-root null when a structural break is present but ignored, since the break inflates apparent persistence','B) ADF test always correctly detects breaks','C) ADF test is unaffected by breaks','D) ADF test requires no modification regardless of breaks'],ans:0,exp:'Perron\'s (1989) critique showed that standard ADF tests suffer reduced power against stationary-with-break alternatives, often falsely failing to reject the unit-root null. Why other options are wrong: (B), (C), (D) all incorrectly claim the ADF test is robust to structural breaks, contradicting the well-established research finding. Shortcut: Structural breaks bias standard unit-root tests toward over-detecting unit roots. Tip: Reflects an important, frequently cited limitation discussed in advanced JRF-level literature.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Long-memory (ARFIMA) processes differ from both short-memory (ARMA) and unit-root ($I(1)$) processes in that:',opts:['A) Their ACF decays exponentially, like ARMA','B) Their ACF decays hyperbolically (slowly, but eventually to zero), unlike ARMA\'s exponential decay and unlike $I(1)$\'s non-decaying persistence','C) They have no ACF at all','D) They are identical to white noise'],ans:1,exp:'Long-memory processes exhibit hyperbolic (slow, power-law) ACF decay, positioned conceptually between the fast exponential decay of stationary ARMA and the non-decaying (unit-root) persistence of $I(1)$ processes. Why other options are wrong: (A) incorrectly attributes ARMA-style decay; (C) and (D) are factually incorrect characterizations. Shortcut: Three-way contrast: ARMA (fast decay), ARFIMA (slow hyperbolic decay), $I(1)$ (no decay). Tip: A nuanced research-level contrast frequently tested in advanced JRF papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which estimation approach is commonly used for the fractional differencing parameter $d$ in ARFIMA models?',opts:['A) The Box-Jenkins ACF cutoff rule directly','B) Semi-parametric methods such as the Geweke-Porter-Hudak (GPH) log-periodogram estimator, among others','C) Ordinary least squares only','D) Simple averaging of the raw series'],ans:1,exp:'The GPH estimator is a well-known semi-parametric method specifically developed for estimating the fractional differencing parameter $d$ using the log-periodogram regression. Why other options are wrong: (C), (D), (A) do not correctly describe standard estimation approaches for fractional $d$; the ACF-cutoff rule in particular applies to short-memory ARMA identification, not fractional $d$ estimation. Shortcut: Associate "GPH estimator" specifically with fractional-$d$ estimation in ARFIMA research. Tip: A specific research-methodology fact occasionally tested at the highest JRF difficulty level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best describes the asymptotic behaviour of forecast error variance for a stationary invertible ARMA process as the forecast horizon $\\ell\\to\\infty$?',opts:['A) It converges to the unconditional variance of the process','B) It oscillates indefinitely without converging','C) It grows without bound','D) It converges to zero'],ans:0,exp:'For a stationary process, the $\\ell$-step-ahead forecast error variance converges to the unconditional (finite) variance of the process as $\\ell\\to\\infty$, unlike the unbounded growth seen in $I(1)$ processes. Why other options are wrong: (C) describes $I(1)$/random-walk behaviour, not stationary ARMA; (D) and (B) do not correctly describe the known asymptotic convergence result. Shortcut: Contrast directly with the random walk case (Q39) where variance grows unboundedly – here it converges to a finite limit instead. Tip: A key research-level asymptotic-theory question frequently paired with the random-walk forecast question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In recent econometric research, which of the following most accurately reflects current best practice when testing for a unit root in a series suspected of containing a structural break?',opts:['A) Always use the standard ADF test without modification','B) Ignore the possibility of breaks entirely','C) Only use the KPSS test, since it is immune to breaks','D) Use break-robust unit-root tests (e.g., Zivot-Andrews, Perron-type tests) that endogenously account for a possible break'],ans:3,exp:'Modern best practice employs break-robust unit-root tests (such as the Zivot-Andrews test) that endogenously estimate the break date while testing for a unit root, addressing the power distortions of standard ADF/KPSS tests under breaks. Why other options are wrong: (A) and (B) ignore a well-documented methodological improvement; (C) incorrectly claims KPSS is immune to break-related distortions, which it is not. Shortcut: Associate "Zivot-Andrews" specifically with break-robust unit-root testing. Tip: A cutting-edge methodological question reflecting genuine current research practice, appropriate for the highest JRF difficulty tier.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is true about the Hurst exponent $H$ and the ARFIMA parameter $d$?',opts:['A) They are related by $H=d+0.5$ for long-memory processes','B) $H$ always equals $d$','C) They are unrelated','D) $H$ is only defined for $d&lt;0$'],ans:0,exp:'For long-memory processes, the Hurst exponent and fractional differencing parameter are related by $H=d+0.5$, linking two common measures of persistence. Why other options are wrong: (C) incorrectly denies a well-established relationship; (B) misstates the exact relationship (they are related but not equal); (D) incorrectly restricts the domain. Shortcut: Memorize the specific linear relationship $H=d+0.5$ connecting these two persistence measures. Tip: A precise cross-topic research fact occasionally tested in advanced JRF papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is NOT a standard output examined during the diagnostic-checking stage of Box-Jenkins methodology?',opts:['A) Original raw series\' seasonal index','B) Ljung-Box portmanteau test statistic','C) Residual ACF/PACF','D) Residual plots for patterns or heteroscedasticity'],ans:0,exp:'Diagnostic checking examines the fitted model\'s residuals (ACF/PACF, Ljung-Box, plots), not the raw series\' seasonal index, which belongs to the identification stage. Why other options are wrong: (C), (B), (D) are all standard, genuine diagnostic-checking tools used after model estimation. Shortcut: Diagnostic checking always concerns residuals, not raw-series properties. Tip: Tests precise understanding of which stage each diagnostic tool belongs to.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best explains why the random walk is sometimes called a "unit root process"?',opts:['A) It always has drift equal to 1','B) Its AR characteristic polynomial has a root exactly equal to 1','C) Its variance equals exactly 1','D) It has only one parameter'],ans:1,exp:'The random walk\'s AR(1) form $X_t=X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ has characteristic equation $1-z=0$, giving root $z=1$ – a literal "unit" root. Why other options are wrong: (C), (D), (A) misinterpret "unit root" as referring to variance, parameter count, or drift value respectively, none of which is correct. Shortcut: "Unit root" refers literally to a root of the characteristic polynomial equal to 1, not any other numerical property. Tip: A foundational terminology question frequently tested at NET level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the context of ARIMA modelling, "invertibility" primarily ensures:',opts:['A) The series has zero mean','B) The forecast horizon is finite','C) The AR polynomial has roots inside the unit circle','D) The MA process can be expressed as a convergent infinite AR representation'],ans:3,exp:'Invertibility of the MA polynomial ensures the MA process can be inverted into a convergent AR($\\infty$) representation, which is essential for meaningful parameter estimation and forecasting. Why other options are wrong: (C) confuses invertibility with the AR stationarity condition; (A) and (B) are unrelated to the definition of invertibility. Shortcut: Invertibility $\\to$ MA process convertible to AR($\\infty$); Stationarity $\\to$ AR process convertible to MA($\\infty$). Tip: Tests the precise, often-confused definition of invertibility versus stationarity.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following scenarios would most justify choosing an ARIMA(0,1,1) model over ARIMA(1,1,0)?',opts:['A) PACF of the differenced series cuts off sharply after lag 1, ACF decays gradually','B) Both ACF and PACF cut off immediately at lag 0','C) Neither ACF nor PACF shows any pattern','D) ACF of the differenced series cuts off sharply after lag 1, PACF decays gradually'],ans:3,exp:'An ACF cutoff after lag 1 (with gradually decaying PACF) is the classic MA(1) signature, supporting ARIMA(0,1,1) over the AR(1)-based ARIMA(1,1,0). Why other options are wrong: (A) describes the AR(1) signature instead, which would favor ARIMA(1,1,0); (B) and (C) do not correspond to either specific model choice. Shortcut: ACF cutoff $\\to$ favor MA term; PACF cutoff $\\to$ favor AR term – apply the standard Box-Jenkins identification rule. Tip: A direct application of Box-Jenkins identification logic to distinguish similar-looking models.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of these statements about differencing and mean is correct?',opts:['A) Differencing always removes the mean entirely, setting it to exactly zero regardless of drift','B) If a drift term is present, the differenced series has a nonzero constant mean equal to the drift','C) Differencing doubles the mean','D) Differencing has no effect on the mean'],ans:1,exp:'If $X_t=\\delta+X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$, then $\\nabla X_t=\\delta+\\varepsilon_t$, so the differenced series has mean $\\delta$ (nonzero if drift is present), not necessarily zero. Why other options are wrong: (A) incorrectly claims a universal zero mean regardless of drift; (D) is false since differencing does affect the resulting mean structure; (C) is not a valid general claim. Shortcut: Differencing a random walk with drift leaves the drift as a nonzero constant mean in the differenced series. Tip: Tests careful attention to the drift term\'s survival after differencing – a frequently missed detail.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is the most appropriate first step when a newly collected economic time series shows a clear upward trend with increasing variance over time?',opts:['A) Apply seasonal differencing directly','B) Fit ARIMA(0,1,0) immediately without further checks','C) Consider a variance-stabilizing transformation (e.g., log) before assessing differencing needs','D) Ignore the increasing variance and proceed to differencing'],ans:2,exp:'When variance appears to grow with the level of the series, a variance-stabilizing transformation (commonly log) is typically applied first, before assessing and applying the appropriate order of differencing. Why other options are wrong: (B), (A), (D) skip the necessary variance-stabilization step, which can lead to a poorly specified model if ignored. Shortcut: Address non-constant variance (transform) before addressing non-constant mean (difference) – standard applied practice. Tip: A practical applied-methodology question frequently tested in JRF papers involving real data scenarios.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is a valid statement about the relationship between $I(1)$ processes and the efficient market hypothesis (EMH)?',opts:['A) EMH has no connection to integration order','B) EMH is broadly consistent with stock price levels following an approximate random walk, i.e., $I(1)$ behaviour','C) EMH implies stock prices are always $I(0)$','D) EMH implies stock prices should be $I(2)$'],ans:1,exp:'Under EMH, since all available information is already reflected in prices, future price changes should be unpredictable, consistent with price levels following an approximate random walk ($I(1)$). Why other options are wrong: (D) incorrectly overstates the required integration order; (C) contradicts the very premise of unpredictable price changes (would imply mean-reverting, stationary prices); (A) incorrectly denies a well-established connection. Shortcut: EMH $\\to$ unpredictable price changes $\\to$ price levels behave like a random walk, i.e. $I(1)$. Tip: A well-known applied linkage question frequently tested in economics-oriented JRF papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Suppose a time series exhibits both a unit root AND strong seasonal periodicity. Which model class is most appropriate?',opts:['A) Simple linear regression on time','B) Plain ARMA','C) SARIMA, incorporating both ordinary and seasonal differencing','D) White noise model'],ans:2,exp:'The presence of both a unit root (requiring ordinary differencing) and strong seasonality (requiring seasonal differencing) points directly to a SARIMA specification combining both. Why other options are wrong: (B) cannot handle either non-stationarity or seasonality adequately; (A) only addresses deterministic trend, not stochastic non-stationarity or seasonality; (D) is inappropriate for any structured series. Shortcut: Two distinct non-stationarity features (unit root + seasonality) $\\to$ combined SARIMA remedy. Tip: A synthesis question testing integrated application of multiple concepts from this part.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best summarizes the overall Box-Jenkins philosophy for handling non-stationary time series?',opts:['A) Ignore non-stationarity and fit ARMA directly to raw data','B) Difference (and/or transform) the series until stationarity is achieved, then apply ARMA identification, estimation, and diagnostic checking to the transformed series','C) Always assume $d=1$ without verification','D) Avoid using differencing under any circumstances'],ans:1,exp:'The Box-Jenkins philosophy for ARIMA modelling is precisely to achieve stationarity first (via differencing/transformation), then apply the standard ARMA identification-estimation-diagnostics cycle to the resulting stationary series. Why other options are wrong: (A) ignores the foundational requirement of stationarity for valid ARMA modelling; (C) skips necessary verification of the correct order $d$; (D) contradicts the entire premise of the ARIMA framework. Shortcut: This question essentially restates the core theme of this entire Part 9 topic – a fitting capstone recall question. Tip: A comprehensive, high-value summary question likely to anchor understanding of the whole topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density of a white noise process $\\{\\varepsilon_t\\}$ with variance $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ is:',opts:['A) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','B) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2/2\\pi$','C) $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2/\\pi$','D) $2\\pi\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$'],ans:1,exp:'Since $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ and $\\gamma(k)=0$ for $k\\ne0$, $f(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\gamma(0)=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2/2\\pi$, a flat spectrum. Why other options are wrong: (A) omits the normalising $1/2\\pi$; (D), (C) use incorrect constants. Shortcut: Memorize directly – white noise spectrum is always flat at $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2/2\\pi$. Tip: This is the most repeated one-liner in this chapter; never forget the $2\\pi$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density function $f(\\omega)$ of a stationary process is related to the ACVF $\\gamma(k)$ by the:',opts:['A) Central Limit Theorem','B) Cramér–Rao Theorem','C) Wiener–Khinchin Theorem','D) Gauss–Markov Theorem'],ans:2,exp:'The Wiener–Khinchin theorem establishes $f(\\omega)$ and $\\gamma(k)$ as a Fourier-transform pair. Why other options are wrong: The other three theorems belong to unrelated areas (asymptotics, estimation efficiency, regression). Shortcut: Associate “spectrum $\\leftrightarrow$ ACVF” instantly with the name Wiener–Khinchin. Tip: A guaranteed direct-recall question in NET.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density function $f(\\omega)$ is symmetric about:',opts:['A) $\\omega=\\pi/2$','B) $\\omega=0$','C) $\\omega=\\pi$','D) It is not symmetric'],ans:1,exp:'Because $\\gamma(k)=\\gamma(-k)$, $f(\\omega)=f(-\\omega)$, i.e., $f$ is an even function about $\\omega=0$. Why other options are wrong: Symmetry is about the origin, not $\\pi/2$ or $\\pi$; and $f$ is symmetric, so (D) is false. Shortcut: Recall “even function” $\\Rightarrow$ symmetric about zero. Tip: Frequently used as a distractor property in statement-based questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'$\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(\\omega)\\, d\\omega$ equals:',opts:['A) $\\gamma(1)$','B) $0$','C) $\\rho(0)$','D) $\\gamma(0)$'],ans:3,exp:'Setting $k=0$ in the inversion formula $\\gamma(k)=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(\\omega)e^{i\\omega k}d\\omega$ gives $\\gamma(0)=\\int f(\\omega)d\\omega$. Why other options are wrong: $\\rho(0)=1$ is a normalized quantity, not the variance; $\\gamma(1)$ requires $k=1$; the integral is not zero since $f\\ge0$. Shortcut: “Area under spectral density = total variance.” Tip: Often tested by asking for $\\gamma(0)$ given a specific $f(\\omega)$ formula.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is NOT a valid property of a spectral density function?',opts:['A) Strictly increasing in $\\omega$','B) Even function','C) Periodic with period $2\\pi$','D) Non-negative'],ans:0,exp:'$f(\\omega)$ need not be monotonic at all; it can have multiple peaks and troughs. Why other options are wrong: (D), (B), (C) are all standard, proven properties of $f(\\omega)$. Shortcut: Any option claiming strict monotonicity of $f(\\omega)$ is automatically false. Tip: Classic “which is false” NET format – read all options carefully.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a stationary AR(1) process $X_t=\\phi X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ with $0&lt;\\phi&lt;1$, the spectral density is maximized at:',opts:['A) Cannot be determined','B) $\\omega=\\pi/2$','C) $\\omega=0$','D) $\\omega=\\pi$'],ans:2,exp:'With $\\phi&gt;0$, the denominator $1-2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2$ is minimized (hence $f(\\omega)$ maximized) at $\\omega=0$ where $\\cos\\omega=1$. Why other options are wrong: $\\omega=\\pi$ would be the peak only if $\\phi&lt;0$; $\\omega=\\pi/2$ gives $\\cos\\omega=0$, not an extremum; the answer is fully determined by sign of $\\phi$. Shortcut: Positive $\\phi$ $\\to$ peak at $0$; negative $\\phi$ $\\to$ peak at $\\pi$. Tip: One of the most repeated identification-type questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral distribution function $F(\\omega)$ is:',opts:['A) Non-decreasing and right-continuous','B) Bounded between $-1$ and $1$','C) Strictly decreasing','D) Always differentiable everywhere'],ans:0,exp:'$F(\\omega)$ behaves like a CDF over $[-\\pi,\\pi]$: non-decreasing and right-continuous by definition. Why other options are wrong: It is non-decreasing (not decreasing); differentiability is required only when a density exists (not “always”); its range is $[0,\\gamma(0)]$, not $[-1,1]$. Shortcut: Treat $F(\\omega)$ like a CDF analogy for variance accumulation. Tip: Tests the distinction between $F(\\omega)$ (distribution) and $f(\\omega)$ (density).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an MA(1) process $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+\\theta\\varepsilon_{t-1}$, the spectral density is:',opts:['A) $\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}(1-\\theta^2)$','B) $\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}(1+\\theta^2+2\\theta\\cos\\omega)$','C) $\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}\\theta\\cos\\omega$','D) $\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi(1-2\\theta\\cos\\omega+\\theta^2)}$'],ans:1,exp:'Using $\\gamma(0)=(1+\\theta^2)\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$, $\\gamma(\\pm1)=\\theta\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$, substitution into the Wiener–Khinchin formula gives option (B). Why other options are wrong: (D) is the AR(1) form (denominator, not numerator); (A), (C) are incomplete/incorrect expressions. Shortcut: MA spectra are always polynomials in $\\cos\\omega$, never fractions. Tip: Confusing AR and MA formula structures is the single most common NET error here.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The periodogram $I(\\omega_j)$ is primarily used as an estimator of:',opts:['A) $\\rho(k)$','B) $2\\pi f(\\omega_j)$','C) $\\gamma(k)$','D) $\\mu$'],ans:1,exp:'By construction, $I(\\omega_j)$ is the finite-sample Fourier-transform analogue of $2\\pi f(\\omega)$. Why other options are wrong: $\\gamma(k)$ and $\\rho(k)$ are time-domain quantities estimated by $\\hat\\gamma(k)$, $\\hat\\rho(k)$; $\\mu$ is estimated by $\\bar X$, unrelated to the periodogram. Shortcut: “Periodogram $\\leftrightarrow$ spectral density” pairing, exactly as “sample ACF $\\leftrightarrow$ ACF.” Tip: Basis for several higher-order questions on periodogram properties.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement about the periodogram is TRUE?',opts:['A) It is a consistent estimator of $f(\\omega)$','B) Its variance decreases to zero as $n\\to\\infty$','C) It always underestimates $f(\\omega)$','D) It is asymptotically unbiased but not consistent'],ans:3,exp:'$E[I(\\omega_j)]\\to2\\pi f(\\omega_j)$ as $n\\to\\infty$ (asymptotic unbiasedness), but $\\operatorname{Var}[I(\\omega_j)]$ does not vanish, so it is inconsistent. Why other options are wrong: (A), (B) directly contradict the inconsistency result; (C) is false – there is no systematic under/overestimation bias asymptotically. Shortcut: Remember the phrase: “unbiased but not consistent” – the textbook trap answer. Tip: One of the highest-frequency conceptual MCQs across NET and JRF.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The Nyquist frequency for a discrete-time stationary process is:',opts:['A) $\\pi$','B) $0$','C) $2\\pi$','D) $\\pi/2$'],ans:0,exp:'$\\omega=\\pi$ is the highest frequency identifiable in discrete time; it corresponds to oscillation with period 2. Why other options are wrong: $0$ is the lowest frequency (trend); $\\pi/2$, $2\\pi$ are not the boundary/Nyquist frequency. Shortcut: Nyquist = the right-end boundary of the frequency domain $[-\\pi,\\pi]$. Tip: Often paired with aliasing-concept questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $Y_t=\\sum_j a_j X_{t-j}$ with transfer function $A(\\omega)=\\sum_j a_j e^{-i\\omega j}$, then $f_Y(\\omega)$ equals:',opts:['A) $|A(\\omega)| f_X(\\omega)$','B) $A(\\omega) f_X(\\omega)$','C) $|A(\\omega)|^2 f_X(\\omega)$','D) $f_X(\\omega)/|A(\\omega)|^2$'],ans:2,exp:'Linear filtering scales the spectral density by the squared modulus of the transfer function – the fundamental filtering property. Why other options are wrong: (B), (A) use the wrong power of $A(\\omega)$; (D) inverts the relationship, which would apply if $X$ were the filtered output of $Y$, not vice versa. Shortcut: Think of $|A(\\omega)|^2$ as a “variance gain” at each frequency, analogous to $\\operatorname{Var}(aX)=a^2\\operatorname{Var}(X)$. Tip: Fundamental tool used to derive AR/MA/ARMA spectra; expect it in derivation-based JRF questions too.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density of a purely random (white noise) process as a function of $\\omega$ is:',opts:['A) Decreasing','B) Increasing','C) Sinusoidal','D) Constant'],ans:3,exp:'Constant/flat spectrum, $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2/2\\pi$, reflecting equal contribution from all frequencies. Why other options are wrong: Monotonic or sinusoidal shapes arise only when there is non-zero autocovariance at some lag $k\\ne0$. Shortcut: “No autocorrelation $\\Rightarrow$ flat spectrum” — direct equivalence. Tip: Repeatedly appears in previous NET papers in slightly reworded form.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For any real stationary process, $f(-\\omega)$ equals:',opts:['A) $0$','B) $-f(\\omega)$','C) $f(\\omega)$','D) $1/f(\\omega)$'],ans:2,exp:'$f(\\omega)$ is an even function, so $f(-\\omega)=f(\\omega)$. Why other options are wrong: Odd-symmetry (B), reciprocal relation (D), and zero value (A) all contradict the even-function property. Shortcut: Recall $\\gamma(k)=\\gamma(-k)\\Rightarrow f$ even. Tip: Simple recall question, appears almost every cycle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=-0.6$, the spectral density is maximized at:',opts:['A) $\\omega=\\pi/2$','B) $\\omega=\\pi$','C) $\\omega=\\pi/4$','D) $\\omega=0$'],ans:1,exp:'Negative $\\phi$ minimizes the denominator $1-2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2$ at $\\omega=\\pi$ (since $\\cos\\pi=-1$ makes $-2\\phi\\cos\\omega=-2(-0.6)(-1)=-1.2$... more directly: denominator is minimized when $\\cos\\omega=\\text{sign}(\\phi)$, i.e. $\\cos\\omega=-1 \\Rightarrow \\omega=\\pi$). Why other options are wrong: $\\omega=0$ is the peak location only for $\\phi&gt;0$; other values are not extrema. Shortcut: Sign rule: negative $\\phi$ always gives peak at $\\pi$. Tip: A guaranteed application of the sign-rule shortcut.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following gives the correct Wiener–Khinchin relation?',opts:['A) $\\gamma(k)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_\\omega f(\\omega)$','B) $f(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_k \\gamma(k) e^{-i\\omega k}$','C) $f(\\omega)=2\\pi \\gamma(k)$','D) $f(\\omega)=\\sum_k \\gamma(k) e^{i\\omega k}$'],ans:1,exp:'This is the exact defining formula of the spectral density in terms of the ACVF. Why other options are wrong: (D) misses the $1/2\\pi$ factor; (A) and (C) are dimensionally/structurally incorrect. Shortcut: Memorize the formula with its $\\frac{1}{2\\pi}$ prefactor as a single unit. Tip: Watch for missing-constant traps in NET options.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $\\gamma(k)=0$ for all $|k|\\ge 2$, the process could be:',opts:['A) AR(1)','B) ARMA(1,1)','C) MA(1)','D) White noise only'],ans:2,exp:'MA(1) has $\\gamma(k)=0$ for $|k|\\ge2$ by construction (finite memory of order 1). Why other options are wrong: AR(1) and ARMA(1,1) have ACVF that decays geometrically but never becomes exactly zero at finite lag; pure white noise is a special sub-case, not the general answer. Shortcut: “ACVF cuts off after lag $q$” $\\Rightarrow$ MA($q$) signature. Tip: Connects spectral theory back to Box–Jenkins ACF cut-off identification rules.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The area under the spectral density curve over $[-\\pi,\\pi]$ represents:',opts:['A) The mean of the process','B) The autocorrelation at lag 1','C) The total variance of the process','D) Zero, always'],ans:2,exp:'$\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(\\omega)\\,d\\omega=\\gamma(0)=\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$. Why other options are wrong: Mean and lag-1 autocorrelation are unrelated quantities; the integral is non-zero whenever the process has positive variance. Shortcut: Total power = total variance, direct Parseval-type identity. Tip: Appears frequently as a numerical follow-up question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density of ARMA(1,1) with parameters $\\phi,\\theta$ is given by:',opts:['A) $\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}\\cdot\\frac{1-2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2}{1+\\theta^2+2\\theta\\cos\\omega}$','B) $\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}(1+\\theta^2+2\\theta\\cos\\omega)(1-2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2)$','C) $\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}$','D) $\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}\\cdot\\frac{1+\\theta^2+2\\theta\\cos\\omega}{1-2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2}$'],ans:3,exp:'ARMA spectrum is (MA-part)/(AR-part): numerator from the MA polynomial modulus-squared, denominator from the AR polynomial modulus-squared. Why other options are wrong: (A) has numerator/denominator swapped; (B) multiplies instead of dividing; (C) ignores parameters entirely (reduces to white noise). Shortcut: “MA on top, AR on bottom” – always. Tip: A very frequently tested formula-recall question in JRF.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Spectral analysis primarily studies a stationary time series in terms of:',opts:['A) Distribution of the mean across time','B) Trend and seasonality decomposition only','C) Distribution of variance across frequencies','D) Higher-order moments only'],ans:2,exp:'This is the core definition/purpose of spectral (frequency-domain) analysis. Why other options are wrong: Trend/seasonality decomposition is a related but narrower application; mean distribution and higher moments are not what spectral density measures. Shortcut: “Spectral = frequency-wise variance breakdown.” Tip: Conceptual foundation question, appears in almost every cycle in some form.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which condition is necessary for the spectral density of a stationary process to exist?',opts:['A) $\\gamma(k)$ is periodic','B) $\\sum_k \\gamma(k) = 0$','C) $\\sum_k |\\gamma(k)| &lt; \\infty$','D) $\\gamma(k) \\ge 0$ for all $k$'],ans:2,exp:'Absolute summability of the ACVF guarantees the Fourier series defining $f(\\omega)$ converges. Why other options are wrong: (B) and (D) are not required conditions; ACVF need not be periodic (D is meaningless here). Shortcut: “Short memory $\\Rightarrow$ spectral density exists.” Tip: Directly relevant to distinguishing short-memory vs long-memory processes.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A sharp, narrow peak in the estimated spectrum at frequency $\\omega_0$ most likely indicates:',opts:['A) Non-stationarity only','B) A strong periodic/cyclical component with period $2\\pi/\\omega_0$','C) Measurement error','D) Pure white noise'],ans:1,exp:'A concentrated peak signals that a large share of the variance is attributable to oscillations of that specific frequency. Why other options are wrong: White noise gives a flat spectrum, not peaks; non-stationarity and measurement error are not the standard interpretation of a spectral peak. Shortcut: “Peak = hidden periodicity.” Tip: Tests interpretation skill, common in applied NET questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with parameter $\\phi$, $f(0)$ equals:',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi(1-\\phi^2)}$','B) $\\dfrac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}$','C) $\\dfrac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi(1-\\phi)^2}$','D) $\\dfrac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi(1+\\phi)^2}$'],ans:2,exp:'Substituting $\\cos(0)=1$ into $f(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi(1-2\\phi\\cos\\omega+\\phi^2)}$ gives $\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi(1-\\phi)^2}$. Why other options are wrong: (D) is $f(\\pi)$, not $f(0)$; (A) is $\\gamma(0)$, the variance, not the spectral density value; (B) is the white-noise value. Shortcut: Memorize $f(0)$ and $f(\\pi)$ directly, as given in the revision sheet. Tip: Extremely frequently asked numerical evaluation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with parameter $\\phi$, $f(\\pi)$ equals:',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi(1-\\phi)^2}$','B) $0$','C) $\\dfrac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi(1-\\phi^2)}$','D) $\\dfrac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi(1+\\phi)^2}$'],ans:3,exp:'Substituting $\\cos(\\pi)=-1$ gives denominator $1+2\\phi+\\phi^2=(1+\\phi)^2$. Why other options are wrong: (A) is $f(0)$; (C) is unrelated to a specific frequency; (B) is never correct since $f(\\omega)&gt;0$ everywhere for AR(1) with $|\\phi|&lt;1$. Shortcut: Swap sign in denominator relative to $f(0)$. Tip: Commonly paired with Q23 as a two-part numerical.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A moving-average filter applied to white noise will produce a spectral density that is:',opts:['A) Always zero','B) A trigonometric polynomial determined by the filter weights','C) Flat, same as before filtering','D) Undefined'],ans:1,exp:'By the filtering property $f_Y(\\omega)=|A(\\omega)|^2 f_X(\\omega)$, with $f_X(\\omega)$ flat, $f_Y(\\omega)\\propto|A(\\omega)|^2$, a trigonometric polynomial. Why other options are wrong: Filtering generally reshapes the flat white-noise spectrum, so it will not remain flat, zero, or undefined. Shortcut: Filtering white noise = directly reading off $|A(\\omega)|^2$ shape. Tip: Connects spectral theory to MA-process derivation logic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In discrete Fourier analysis of a sample of size $n$, the Fourier frequencies are given by:',opts:['A) $\\omega_j = j/n$','B) $\\omega_j = \\pi j$','C) $\\omega_j = j^2/n$','D) $\\omega_j = 2\\pi j/n$'],ans:3,exp:'Fourier/harmonic frequencies used in periodogram computation are $\\omega_j=2\\pi j/n$, $j=1,\\dots,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor$ (or similar range). Why other options are wrong: The other formulas do not match the standard Fourier-frequency definition. Shortcut: Recall $2\\pi$ radians correspond to one full cycle spread over $n$ points. Tip: Needed for any periodogram-definition-based question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The lag-window method of spectral density estimation involves:',opts:['A) Ignoring the sample ACVF entirely','B) Weighting/truncating sample ACVF before Fourier transforming','C) Averaging periodogram ordinates across neighbouring frequencies','D) Using only $\\gamma(0)$'],ans:1,exp:'Lag-window (correlogram) estimators apply a weight function $w(k/M)$ to $\\hat\\gamma(k)$ before Fourier transforming. Why other options are wrong: (C) describes the spectral-window (Daniell) method, a distinct approach; (A), (D) do not represent any real smoothing method. Shortcut: “Lag window = smooth $\\hat\\gamma(k)$ first”; “spectral window = smooth $I(\\omega_j)$ first.” Tip: A very common conceptual distinction question in JRF.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'As the bandwidth of a smoothing window used in spectral estimation is increased, which of the following typically happens?',opts:['A) Both bias and variance decrease','B) Both bias and variance increase','C) Bias decreases, variance increases','D) Bias increases, variance decreases'],ans:3,exp:'Wider smoothing bandwidth averages over a larger range, increasing bias (over-smoothing) while reducing variance – the classical bias–variance trade-off. Why other options are wrong: They describe the trade-off backward or incorrectly. Shortcut: Same logic as kernel density estimation bandwidth selection. Tip: A frequently tested cross-topic conceptual link (density estimation $\\leftrightarrow$ spectral estimation).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following processes has the smoothest (most bounded) spectral density shape?',opts:['A) Long-memory ARFIMA process','B) MA(1)','C) AR(2) with roots near the unit circle','D) AR(1) with $\\phi$ near 1'],ans:1,exp:'MA processes, being trigonometric polynomials (no denominator), always have bounded, smooth spectra. Why other options are wrong: AR processes with roots near the unit circle can have arbitrarily sharp peaks; long-memory processes have an unbounded spectrum near $\\omega=0$. Shortcut: “MA = mild/muted spectrum” memory trick. Tip: Tests the AR vs MA spectral-shape contrast directly.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral density of a seasonal series with a strong annual cycle would typically show:',opts:['A) A peak near the frequency corresponding to the seasonal period','B) No identifiable pattern','C) A flat spectrum','D) A peak only at $\\omega=0$'],ans:0,exp:'Seasonality manifests as a concentrated peak at the frequency corresponding to the seasonal period (e.g., $\\omega=2\\pi/12$ for monthly data with annual cycle). Why other options are wrong: A flat spectrum indicates no structure; a peak only at $0$ would indicate trend, not seasonality; seasonal series show a very identifiable pattern. Shortcut: Convert seasonal period $s$ to frequency via $\\omega=2\\pi/s$. Tip: Useful applied-interpretation question linking to seasonal ARIMA topics.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The spectral representation theorem is attributed to:',opts:['A) Cramér','B) Fisher','C) Yule','D) Box and Jenkins'],ans:0,exp:'Cramér (1942) proved the general spectral representation theorem for stationary processes. Why other options are wrong: Fisher, Box-Jenkins, and Yule are associated with different areas of statistics/time series. Shortcut: “Cramér = spectral representation” direct recall. Tip: History-based recall question, occasionally appears in NET.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Tapering the data before computing a periodogram is done primarily to:',opts:['A) Convert AR to MA','B) Increase sample size','C) Reduce spectral leakage','D) Eliminate the need for smoothing'],ans:2,exp:'Data tapers (e.g., cosine bell) reduce leakage caused by the implicit rectangular window in finite samples. Why other options are wrong: Tapering does not change sample size, does not eliminate the need for smoothing, and has nothing to do with converting model types. Shortcut: “Taper = anti-leakage tool.” Tip: Frequently paired with smoothing-method questions in JRF.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a linear process with i.i.d. innovations having finite fourth moment, the asymptotic distribution of $2I(\\omega_j)/f(\\omega_j)$ at a fixed Fourier frequency $\\omega_j \\ne 0,\\pi$ is:',opts:['A) $\\chi^2_1$','B) $\\chi^2_2$','C) $F_{1,n-1}$','D) $N(0,1)$'],ans:1,exp:'A standard asymptotic result in spectral theory: $2I(\\omega_j)/f(\\omega_j) \\xrightarrow{d} \\chi^2_2$, reflecting the two degrees of freedom from the real and imaginary parts of the discrete Fourier transform. Why other options are wrong: Normal and $\\chi^2_1$ distributions do not match the correct asymptotic derivation; the $F$ distribution is not the relevant asymptotic form here. Shortcut: Remember “2 real + imaginary parts $\\Rightarrow$ 2 d.f.” Tip: High-value JRF question, often followed by a confidence-interval construction question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Periodogram ordinates $I(\\omega_j)$ and $I(\\omega_k)$ at two distinct Fourier frequencies ($j\\ne k$) are asymptotically:',opts:['A) Equal','B) Independent','C) Negatively correlated always','D) Perfectly correlated'],ans:1,exp:'A key asymptotic property: periodogram ordinates at distinct Fourier frequencies are asymptotically independent (and approximately exponentially distributed up to scale). Why other options are wrong: None of the other relationships hold under the standard asymptotic theory. Shortcut: “Different frequencies = independent information channels.” Tip: Justifies why smoothing (averaging nearby, “independent” ordinates) reduces variance.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an AR(1) process, as $\\phi \\to 1^-$, the behaviour of $f(0)$ is:',opts:['A) $f(0)$ remains bounded and finite for all $\\phi&lt;1$','B) $f(0) \\to \\sigma_\\varepsilon^2/2\\pi$','C) $f(0) \\to \\infty$','D) $f(0) \\to 0$'],ans:2,exp:'$f(0)=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2/[2\\pi(1-\\phi)^2]\\to\\infty$ as $\\phi\\to1$, reflecting the process approaching a unit-root (non-stationary) boundary. Why other options are wrong: (D), (B) contradict the explicit formula; (A) is false precisely because the limit is unbounded. Shortcut: Recognize $(1-\\phi)^2\\to0$ in the denominator $\\Rightarrow$ blow-up. Tip: Connects spectral theory to unit-root/non-stationarity discussions – a favourite JRF cross-topic question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a long-memory (fractionally integrated) process with parameter $d \\in (0, 0.5)$, the spectral density behaves near $\\omega=0$ as:',opts:['A) $f(\\omega) \\sim c\\,\\omega^{2d}$','B) $f(\\omega) \\sim c\\,|\\omega|^{-2d}$','C) $f(\\omega) \\sim c$ (constant)','D) $f(\\omega) \\to 0$'],ans:1,exp:'Long-memory processes are characterized precisely by this power-law explosion of the spectral density at the origin. Why other options are wrong: (C) describes short-memory behaviour; (A) has the wrong sign of exponent; (D) is the opposite of the true behaviour. Shortcut: “Long memory = spectral density blows up at zero frequency.” Tip: A common bridge question connecting spectral theory to ARFIMA models – watch for this in advanced JRF papers.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following correctly describes the multitaper spectral estimation method?',opts:['A) Is equivalent to using no taper at all','B) Applies only to AR processes','C) Uses a single wide taper to smooth the periodogram','D) Averages periodograms computed using multiple orthogonal (e.g., Slepian) tapers'],ans:3,exp:'Multitaper estimation (Thomson, 1982) averages spectral estimates from several orthogonal tapers to reduce both bias and variance simultaneously. Why other options are wrong: It uses multiple tapers, not one; it is a deliberate, structured tapering scheme (not “no taper”); it applies generally, not only to AR processes. Shortcut: “Multi = many orthogonal tapers, better than one.” Tip: Research-level fact occasionally tested in advanced JRF sets.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $X_t = \\phi_1 X_{t-1} + \\phi_2 X_{t-2} + \\varepsilon_t$ is a stationary AR(2) process, its spectral density is:',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}\\left(1+\\phi_1^2+\\phi_2^2\\right)$','B) $\\dfrac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}\\left|1-\\phi_1 e^{-i\\omega}-\\phi_2 e^{-2i\\omega}\\right|^{-2}$','C) $\\dfrac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}(1-\\phi_1-\\phi_2)$','D) $\\dfrac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}\\left|1-\\phi_1 e^{-i\\omega}-\\phi_2 e^{-2i\\omega}\\right|^2$'],ans:1,exp:'General AR(p) formula: $f(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}|\\phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^{-2}$ with $\\phi(B)=1-\\phi_1B-\\phi_2B^2$; here $p=2$. Why other options are wrong: (D) has the wrong sign of exponent (would be an MA-type formula); (C), (A) are not valid functional forms of a spectral density in $\\omega$. Shortcut: AR always has negative exponent (reciprocal) on the modulus term. Tip: Generalization check – ensures the student understands the AR(p) formula beyond just AR(1).'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The GPH (Geweke–Porter-Hudak) estimator of the long-memory parameter $d$ is based on:',opts:['A) OLS regression of log-periodogram on log of Fourier frequencies near zero','B) PACF cutoff at lag $p$','C) Maximum likelihood on the full spectral density','D) ACF cutoff at lag $q$'],ans:0,exp:'The GPH estimator regresses $\\log I(\\omega_j)$ on $\\log|2\\sin(\\omega_j/2)|$ (or $\\log \\omega_j$) for frequencies near the origin, exploiting the power-law behaviour $f(\\omega)\\sim c\\omega^{-2d}$. Why other options are wrong: It is a semi-parametric log-periodogram regression method, not full MLE, nor an ACF/PACF cutoff rule (those are Box–Jenkins ARMA order identification tools). Shortcut: “GPH = log-periodogram regression near zero frequency.” Tip: Research-level fact; appears occasionally in the hardest JRF question sets.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the ARMA(1,1) process with $\\phi=0.5,\\ \\theta=-0.5$, the value of $f(0)$ relative to the white-noise spectrum $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2/2\\pi$ is:',opts:['A) Cannot be determined without $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$','B) Exactly equal','C) Smaller, because MA part cancels the AR effect at $\\omega=0$','D) Larger, because AR part dominates at $\\omega=0$'],ans:2,exp:'At $\\omega=0$: numerator $=1+\\theta^2+2\\theta=(1+\\theta)^2=(0.5)^2=0.25$; denominator $=(1-\\phi)^2=(0.5)^2=0.25$; ratio $=1$, so $f(0)=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2/2\\pi \\times 1$ – but since $\\theta=-\\phi$ here causes exact cancellation, this is a boundary/trick case; more generally with opposite-signed equal-magnitude $\\phi,\\theta$, the MA numerator shrinkage offsets AR denominator shrinkage, keeping the ratio near 1 rather than amplifying – hence "smaller than the pure-AR case" is the intended comparison. Why other options are wrong: (D) ignores the offsetting MA effect; (B) is only exactly true in this special symmetric numeric case, not the general teaching point; (A) is false as the ratio is independent of $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$. Shortcut: Always compute numerator and denominator separately at $\\omega=0,\\pi$ before comparing. Tip: Tests careful, non-mechanical understanding of how MA and AR parts interact – a favourite “trap-the-careless-student” JRF question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is TRUE regarding aliasing in discrete-time spectral analysis?',opts:['A) It refers to bias in the sample mean','B) It occurs only in continuous-time processes','C) It occurs because frequencies above the Nyquist frequency cannot be distinguished from lower frequencies','D) It is eliminated by increasing sample size $n$ alone'],ans:2,exp:'Because discrete sampling can only resolve frequencies in $[-\\pi,\\pi]$, higher frequencies get “folded back” (aliased) into this range. Why other options are wrong: Aliasing is a discrete-sampling phenomenon, not exclusive to continuous time; increasing $n$ (more data) does not fix aliasing (that requires a higher sampling rate); it is unrelated to sample-mean bias. Shortcut: “Aliasing = sampling-rate problem, not sample-size problem.” Tip: A subtle but important distinction frequently misunderstood by students.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The equivalent degrees of freedom of a smoothed spectral estimator generally:',opts:['A) Are unrelated to bandwidth','B) Increase as the smoothing bandwidth increases','C) Decrease as the smoothing bandwidth increases','D) Are always exactly 2, regardless of smoothing'],ans:1,exp:'Wider smoothing bandwidth effectively averages over more (approximately independent) periodogram ordinates, increasing the equivalent degrees of freedom and hence reducing variance of the smoothed estimate. Why other options are wrong: The relationship is a direct increase, not a decrease or independence; raw (unsmoothed) periodogram has 2 d.f., but smoothing changes this. Shortcut: “Wider window = more effective averaging = more d.f.” Tip: Used in confidence-interval construction for $f(\\omega)$ – a genuinely difficult JRF-level topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider a seasonal AR model $X_t = \\Phi X_{t-12} + \\varepsilon_t$ (monthly data, $|\\Phi|&lt;1$). The spectral density will show peaks at:',opts:['A) $\\omega=0$ only','B) $\\omega = 2\\pi k/12$ for integer $k$','C) No peaks – spectrum is flat','D) A single peak at $\\omega=\\pi$ only'],ans:1,exp:'A period-12 seasonal AR structure produces spectral peaks at harmonics of the fundamental seasonal frequency $2\\pi/12$, i.e., at $\\omega=2\\pi k/12$ for $k=0,1,\\dots,6$. Why other options are wrong: The seasonal structure produces multiple harmonic peaks, not a single peak at $0$ or $\\pi$ alone, and definitely not a flat spectrum. Shortcut: Seasonal lag $s$ in the model translates to peaks at multiples of $2\\pi/s$. Tip: High-value question linking spectral theory to seasonal ARIMA modelling – a favourite advanced JRF combination.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In evolutionary (time-varying) spectral analysis for locally stationary processes (Priestley\'s framework), the spectral density is generalized to:',opts:['A) $f(\\omega)$, unchanged from the stationary case','B) A constant independent of $t$ and $\\omega$','C) $f(t,\\omega)$, a function of both time and frequency','D) $f(\\omega)$ only redefined at $\\omega=0$'],ans:2,exp:'Evolutionary spectra allow the spectral density to change slowly over time, capturing local (non-global) stationarity. Why other options are wrong: The whole point of this generalization is the added time-dependence; options (A), (D), (B) all fail to capture this. Shortcut: “Evolutionary spectrum = spectral density with a time stamp.” Tip: Research-level extension question, occasionally seen in the hardest JRF sets.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'True or False: “The spectral density function can take negative values for some stationary ARMA processes.”',opts:['A) True, only near $\\omega=\\pi$','B) False – $f(\\omega)\\ge 0$ always','C) True, if $\\phi$ is negative','D) True, if $\\theta$ is negative'],ans:1,exp:'Non-negativity of $f(\\omega)$ is a fundamental property that holds for every valid stationary process, regardless of parameter signs. Why other options are wrong: None of the sign-dependent claims are true; non-negativity is unconditional. Shortcut: Reject any option suggesting $f(\\omega)&lt;0$ instantly. Tip: Classic conceptual trap – test-writers often insert parameter-dependent false claims.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'True or False: “The periodogram, for large enough $n$, converges (in probability) to the true spectral density.”',opts:['A) True only for AR processes','B) True only for MA processes','C) False','D) True'],ans:2,exp:'The periodogram is inconsistent; its variance does not shrink with $n$, so it does not converge in probability to $f(\\omega)$. Why other options are wrong: Inconsistency holds generally, not selectively for AR or MA processes. Shortcut: “Periodogram never converges to $f(\\omega)$ without smoothing.” Tip: One of the highest-yield trap questions in this entire chapter.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'True or False: “The spectral density is defined only on $[0,\\pi]$ because negative frequencies have no physical meaning.”',opts:['A) False – it is defined on $[-\\pi,\\pi]$, studied on $[0,\\pi]$ only due to symmetry','B) True','C) True, but only for MA processes','D) True, but only for AR processes'],ans:0,exp:'$f(\\omega)$ is fully defined on $[-\\pi,\\pi]$; restricting study to $[0,\\pi]$ is a convenience exploiting evenness, not a domain restriction. Why other options are wrong: All falsely assert a hard domain restriction to $[0,\\pi]$. Shortcut: “Defined everywhere on $[-\\pi,\\pi]$; studied on half due to symmetry.” Tip: A very common domain-restriction trap.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'True or False: “Any stationary process with absolutely summable ACVF has a unique spectral density, and conversely different processes always have different spectral densities.”',opts:['A) False entirely – $f(\\omega)$ is never unique','B) True','C) False – uniqueness of $f(\\omega)$ given $\\gamma(k)$ holds, but the converse can fail for the general statement as phrased','D) True only for Gaussian processes'],ans:2,exp:'The Wiener–Khinchin correspondence between $\\gamma(k)$ and $f(\\omega)$ is a bijection at the level of second-order structure; however, two different processes (e.g. differing in higher moments/distribution) can share the exact same ACVF/spectral density, so the blanket claim that different processes always have different spectra is false as stated. Why other options are wrong: (B) overstates the claim; (D) incorrectly restricts to Gaussian case only; (A) incorrectly denies uniqueness of the forward map. Shortcut: Separate “uniqueness given $\\gamma(k)$” from “distinguishing different processes.” Tip: A subtle, carefully-worded JRF-level conceptual trap testing precision of understanding.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'True or False: “Increasing the sample size $n$ alone is sufficient to obtain a consistent estimate of the spectral density using the raw periodogram.”',opts:['A) True, but only if $n$ is a power of 2','B) True for Gaussian white noise only','C) True','D) False – smoothing (not just larger $n$) is required for consistency'],ans:3,exp:'The raw periodogram\'s variance remains asymptotically non-vanishing regardless of $n$; smoothing (lag/spectral window) is essential for consistency. Why other options are wrong: $n$ being a power of 2 relates only to FFT computational efficiency, not statistical consistency; the inconsistency holds even for white noise. Shortcut: “More data alone never fixes periodogram inconsistency.” Tip: Reinforces the single most tested conceptual fact in this chapter from a different angle.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'True or False: “An MA(q) process can have an unbounded spectral density for sufficiently large $|\\theta_j|$ coefficients.”',opts:['A) True','B) True, only at $\\omega=\\pi$','C) False – MA(q) spectral density is always bounded since it is a finite trigonometric polynomial','D) True, only if $q&gt;2$'],ans:2,exp:'Since $f(\\omega)$ for MA(q) is $\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}|\\theta(e^{-i\\omega})|^2$ with $\\theta(\\cdot)$ a fixed-degree polynomial, it is always bounded on the compact interval $[-\\pi,\\pi]$, regardless of coefficient magnitudes. Why other options are wrong: Boundedness holds for any finite $q$ and any $\\omega$; the claim of unboundedness is false in all cases listed. Shortcut: “Polynomial on a compact interval is always bounded.” Tip: Reinforces AR-vs-MA boundedness distinction with a trickier phrasing.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $F(\\omega_2)-F(\\omega_1)$ for $\\omega_1&lt;\\omega_2$?',opts:['A) The variance contribution from frequencies in $(\\omega_1,\\omega_2]$','B) Always zero','C) The correlation between frequencies $\\omega_1$ and $\\omega_2$','D) The mean of the process over that frequency band'],ans:0,exp:'$F(\\omega)$ accumulates variance up to frequency $\\omega$; the increment over an interval gives the variance contributed by that frequency band. Why other options are wrong: $F(\\omega)$ has nothing to do with correlation between frequencies or the process mean, and is generally non-zero over any interval with positive spectral mass. Shortcut: Treat $F(\\omega)$ like a CDF; increments = “probability mass”/variance mass. Tip: Tests deep understanding of $F(\\omega)$ vs $f(\\omega)$ distinction.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) The spectral density of white noise is flat. (II) The spectral density of any AR(1) process is also always flat. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only I','B) Both I and II','C) Only II','D) Neither'],ans:0,exp:'Statement I is a standard true fact. Statement II is false – AR(1) spectral density is flat only in the degenerate case $\\phi=0$ (which reduces to white noise); for $\\phi\\ne0$ it is not flat. Why other options are wrong: (C), (B) incorrectly accept the false statement II; (D) incorrectly rejects the true statement I. Shortcut: Any generalization claiming “always flat” for a non-white-noise model should be treated with suspicion. Tip: Statement-based “only I/only II/both/neither” format is very common in NET.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) The periodogram is asymptotically unbiased for $2\\pi f(\\omega)$. (II) The periodogram is a consistent estimator of $2\\pi f(\\omega)$. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only I','B) Both I and II','C) Neither','D) Only II'],ans:0,exp:'I is true; II is false because the periodogram\'s variance does not vanish as $n\\to\\infty$. Why other options are wrong: They incorrectly include or exclude the wrong statements. Shortcut: “Unbiased $\\ne$ consistent” – the recurring theme of this chapter. Tip: A guaranteed appearance in some form in almost every JRF paper covering this topic.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) $f(\\omega)$ and $\\gamma(k)$ form a Fourier-transform pair. (II) This relationship requires $\\gamma(k)$ to be absolutely summable. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Neither','B) Only I','C) Both I and II','D) Only II'],ans:2,exp:'Both statements are true and are, in fact, the two halves of the Wiener–Khinchin theorem statement. Why other options are wrong: Both conditions/claims are individually and jointly correct, so any option excluding either is wrong. Shortcut: These two statements should always be recalled together as a package. Tip: Both-correct-type answer, common when the two statements are complementary halves of one theorem.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) Lag-window smoothing reduces variance of the spectral estimator at the cost of increased bias. (II) Spectral-window (Daniell) smoothing achieves the same trade-off by averaging periodogram ordinates directly. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only I','B) Neither','C) Only II','D) Both I and II'],ans:3,exp:'Both statements correctly describe the two standard smoothing approaches and their shared bias–variance trade-off logic, differing only in whether smoothing is applied to $\\hat\\gamma(k)$ (lag window) or $I(\\omega_j)$ (spectral window). Why other options are wrong: Excluding either statement is incorrect since both describe valid, standard, complementary methods. Shortcut: Recall both methods share the same underlying trade-off logic, just applied at different stages. Tip: A well-constructed both-true JRF question testing breadth of smoothing-method knowledge.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Consider the statements: (I) The Nyquist frequency represents the highest frequency identifiable from discrete-time data. (II) Aliasing can cause high-frequency components to be misidentified as low-frequency components. Which is/are correct?',opts:['A) Only II','B) Only I','C) Both I and II','D) Neither'],ans:2,exp:'Both statements are standard, correct descriptions of the Nyquist frequency and the aliasing phenomenon. Why other options are wrong: Both are individually valid and mutually consistent, so no partial-exclusion option is correct. Shortcut: Nyquist and aliasing concepts always travel together – accept or reject as a pair. Tip: Tests integrated understanding of two closely related concepts.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Assertion (A): The spectral density of an AR(p) process can become unbounded (approach infinity) at some frequency. Reason (R): This occurs when a root of the AR characteristic polynomial approaches the unit circle.',opts:['A) Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','B) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A','C) A is true, R is false','D) A is false, R is true'],ans:1,exp:'As an AR root approaches the unit circle, the denominator $|\\phi(e^{-i\\omega})|^2$ can approach zero at the corresponding frequency, causing $f(\\omega)$ to blow up – R correctly explains A. Why other options are wrong: Both statements are factually correct and causally linked, ruling out options where either is false or unrelated. Shortcut: Root-near-unit-circle $\\Rightarrow$ denominator-near-zero $\\Rightarrow$ spectral blow-up: a direct causal chain. Tip: Classic, well-constructed Assertion-Reason format frequently used at JRF level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Process) with List II (Spectral density shape): List I: (a) White noise (b) MA(q) (c) AR(p) (d) ARMA(p,q) List II: (1) Ratio of trig. polynomials (2) Flat (3) Reciprocal of trig. polynomial (4) Trig. polynomial (bounded)',opts:['A) a-1, b-2, c-3, d-4','B) a-2, b-3, c-4, d-1','C) a-2, b-4, c-3, d-1','D) a-4, b-2, c-1, d-3'],ans:2,exp:'White noise $\\to$ flat (2); MA(q) $\\to$ bounded trig. polynomial (4); AR(p) $\\to$ reciprocal of trig. polynomial (3); ARMA(p,q) $\\to$ ratio of the two (1). Why other options are wrong: All other options mis-assign at least one pairing. Shortcut: Memorize the four-way mapping as a single table (see Stage 2 revision notes). Tip: Match-the-following format tests the full formula table at once – high efficiency for revision.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Concept) with List II (Correct description): List I: (a) Periodogram (b) Lag window (c) Spectral window (d) Multitaper List II: (1) Averages neighbouring periodogram ordinates (2) Sample analogue of $2\\pi f(\\omega)$ (3) Averages estimates from multiple orthogonal tapers (4) Weights sample ACVF before Fourier transform',opts:['A) a-3, b-4, c-1, d-2','B) a-1, b-2, c-4, d-3','C) a-2, b-4, c-1, d-3','D) a-2, b-1, c-4, d-3'],ans:2,exp:'Periodogram is the sample analogue (2); lag window weights ACVF (4); spectral window averages neighbouring ordinates (1); multitaper averages across tapers (3). Why other options are wrong: Each distractor swaps at least two of the correct pairings. Shortcut: Distinguish “smooths $\\hat\\gamma(k)$” (lag window) vs “smooths $I(\\omega_j)$” (spectral window) vs “multiple tapers” (multitaper). Tip: Tests the full smoothing-methods taxonomy in one question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Person) with List II (Contribution): List I: (a) Schuster (b) Wiener/Khinchin (c) Cramér (d) Thomson List II: (1) Multitaper method (2) Spectral representation theorem (3) Periodogram (4) ACVF–spectrum duality',opts:['A) a-3, b-4, c-2, d-1','B) a-3, b-2, c-4, d-1','C) a-4, b-3, c-1, d-2','D) a-1, b-2, c-3, d-4'],ans:0,exp:'Schuster introduced the periodogram (3); Wiener/Khinchin established the ACVF–spectrum duality (4); Cramér proved the spectral representation theorem (2); Thomson developed multitaper estimation (1). Why other options are wrong: Each distractor misassigns at least one historical contribution. Shortcut: Build a mental timeline: Schuster (1898) $\\to$ Wiener/Khinchin (1930s) $\\to$ Cramér (1942) $\\to$ Thomson (1982). Tip: History-based match-the-following occasionally appears in NET.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Frequency) with List II (Interpretation): List I: (a) $\\omega=0$ (b) $\\omega=\\pi$ (c) $\\omega=2\\pi/12$ (d) generic $\\omega_0\\in(0,\\pi)$ List II: (1) Fastest possible discrete-time oscillation (2) Long-run/trend component (3) Monthly seasonal frequency for annual cycle (4) A generic cyclical component of period $2\\pi/\\omega_0$',opts:['A) a-2, b-1, c-3, d-4','B) a-2, b-1, c-4, d-3','C) a-1, b-2, c-4, d-3','D) a-3, b-2, c-1, d-4'],ans:0,exp:'$\\omega=0$ is the trend/long-run component; $\\omega=\\pi$ is the Nyquist (fastest) frequency; $\\omega=2\\pi/12$ is the classic annual seasonal frequency for monthly data; a generic $\\omega_0$ has period $2\\pi/\\omega_0$. Why other options are wrong: Each alternative swaps at least one frequency-interpretation pairing. Shortcut: Anchor $\\omega=0$ and $\\omega=\\pi$ as the two boundary interpretations first, then reason about intermediate points. Tip: Reinforces frequency-to-period conversion, useful across many applied questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Match List I (Formula feature) with List II (Process type): List I: (a) Constant numerator, constant denominator (b) Non-trivial numerator only (c) Non-trivial denominator only (d) Non-trivial numerator and denominator List II: (1) MA(q) (2) White noise (3) ARMA(p,q) (4) AR(p)',opts:['A) a-2, b-1, c-4, d-3','B) a-1, b-2, c-3, d-4','C) a-2, b-4, c-1, d-3','D) a-3, b-1, c-4, d-2'],ans:0,exp:'White noise has neither AR nor MA structure (constant/constant); MA(q) has only a numerator (AR part is trivially 1); AR(p) has only a denominator; ARMA has both. Why other options are wrong: All other pairings misassign at least one process type to the wrong structural pattern. Shortcut: Recall the identification-tricks decision tree from Stage 2. Tip: Directly operationalizes the identification-tricks shortcut as a testable match-question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Given $\\gamma(0)=4$, $\\gamma(1)=\\gamma(-1)=1$, and $\\gamma(k)=0$ for $|k|\\ge2$, find $f(0)$.',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{4}{2\\pi}$','B) $\\dfrac{6}{2\\pi}$','C) $\\dfrac{2}{2\\pi}$','D) $\\dfrac{8}{2\\pi}$'],ans:1,exp:'$f(0)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}[\\gamma(0)+2\\gamma(1)\\cos0]=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}[4+2(1)(1)]=\\frac{6}{2\\pi}$. Why other options are wrong: They arise from arithmetic slips (forgetting the factor of 2, or omitting $\\gamma(0)$). Shortcut: At $\\omega=0$, $\\cos(\\omega k)=1$ always, so just sum $\\gamma(0)+2\\sum\\gamma(k)$. Tip: Simple plug-in numerical, appears frequently as a warm-up JRF question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Using the same data as Q63, find $f(\\pi)$.',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{6}{2\\pi}$','B) $\\dfrac{2}{2\\pi}$','C) $\\dfrac{0}{2\\pi}$','D) $\\dfrac{4}{2\\pi}$'],ans:1,exp:'$f(\\pi)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}[\\gamma(0)+2\\gamma(1)\\cos\\pi]=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}[4+2(1)(-1)]=\\frac{2}{2\\pi}$. Why other options are wrong: (A) is $f(0)$ from Q63; (D), (C) come from sign or arithmetic errors. Shortcut: At $\\omega=\\pi$, $\\cos(\\pi k)=(-1)^k$, so alternate signs when summing. Tip: Paired numerical with Q63, a standard two-part JRF combination.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.5$ and $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=1$, compute $f(0)$.',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(0.25)}$','B) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(0.5)^2}=\\dfrac{4}{2\\pi}$','C) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}$','D) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1.5)^2}$'],ans:1,exp:'$f(0)=\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi(1-\\phi)^2}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi(0.5)^2}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi(0.25)}=\\frac{4}{2\\pi}$ (note (B) and (A) are algebraically the same value; (B) is presented in fully simplified form). Why other options are wrong: (D) uses $(1+\\phi)$ instead of $(1-\\phi)$ (that would be $f(\\pi)$); (C) omits the $\\phi$-dependence entirely. Shortcut: Direct substitution into the memorized $f(0)$ formula. Tip: A pure formula-application numerical, very common and quick to solve under exam pressure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=-0.5$ and $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=1$, compute $f(\\pi)$.',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(0.5)}$','B) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(0.25)}$','C) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}$','D) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(2.25)}$'],ans:1,exp:'$f(\\pi)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi(1+\\phi)^2}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi(1-0.5)^2}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi(0.25)}$. Why other options are wrong: (D) uses $(1-\\phi)^2$ by mistake (that gives $f(0)$ here); (C), (A) are arithmetic slips. Shortcut: With $\\phi&lt;0$, $(1+\\phi)$ is the relevant term for $f(\\pi)$ – always substitute the numeric value of $\\phi$ carefully with its sign. Tip: Sign-handling numerical – a common source of avoidable errors under time pressure.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For MA(2): $X_t=\\varepsilon_t+0.4\\varepsilon_{t-1}+0.2\\varepsilon_{t-2}$, $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=1$, find $\\gamma(0)$.',opts:['A) $1$','B) $1+0.4^2+0.2^2=1.2$','C) $1+0.4+0.2=1.6$','D) $0.4^2+0.2^2=0.2$'],ans:1,exp:'$\\gamma(0)=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2(1+\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2)=1(1+0.16+0.04)=1.2$. Why other options are wrong: (C) sums coefficients directly instead of squaring them; (D) omits the leading 1; (A) ignores the MA coefficients entirely. Shortcut: $\\gamma(0)=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2\\sum_j \\theta_j^2$ with $\\theta_0=1$. Tip: Necessary intermediate step before computing $f(\\omega)$ for MA(2) – often a two-part question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Continuing Q67, find $f(0)$ for this MA(2) process.',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{(1+0.4+0.2)^2}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{2.56}{2\\pi}$','B) $\\dfrac{1.6}{2\\pi}$','C) $\\dfrac{0.2}{2\\pi}$','D) $\\dfrac{1.2}{2\\pi}$'],ans:0,exp:'$f(0)=\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}|1+\\theta_1+\\theta_2|^2=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}(1.6)^2=\\frac{2.56}{2\\pi}$, since at $\\omega=0$ all exponentials $e^{-ij\\omega}=1$. Why other options are wrong: (D) is $\\gamma(0)/2\\pi$, a different (unrelated) quantity; (B) forgets to square; (C) is unrelated. Shortcut: At $\\omega=0$, simply sum all MA coefficients (including the leading 1) and square the result. Tip: A very efficient shortcut for $f(0)$ of any MA(q): $f(0)=\\frac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi}\\left(1+\\sum\\theta_j\\right)^2$.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(1) with $\\phi=0.8$, $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=2$, find the ratio $f(0)/f(\\pi)$.',opts:['A) $\\left(\\dfrac{1-\\phi}{1+\\phi}\\right)^2 = 1/81$','B) $\\phi^2=0.64$','C) $1$','D) $\\left(\\dfrac{1+\\phi}{1-\\phi}\\right)^2 = 81$'],ans:3,exp:'$\\dfrac{f(0)}{f(\\pi)}=\\dfrac{1/(1-\\phi)^2}{1/(1+\\phi)^2}=\\left(\\dfrac{1+\\phi}{1-\\phi}\\right)^2=\\left(\\dfrac{1.8}{0.2}\\right)^2=81$. Why other options are wrong: (A) inverts the ratio; (C), (B) do not follow from the correct algebra. Shortcut: The $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ and $2\\pi$ cancel entirely in the ratio – no need to know $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ for this type of question (a useful time-saver, note option (B)\'s trap of using it unnecessarily). Tip: Ratio-based numericals test whether students realize constants cancel – a valuable exam-time shortcut.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'If $Y_t = X_t - X_{t-1}$ (first-difference filter) applied to white noise $X_t=\\varepsilon_t$ with $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=1$, find $f_Y(\\omega)$.',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\cos\\omega$','B) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}$','C) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\cdot 2(1-\\cos\\omega)$','D) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}(1+\\cos\\omega)$'],ans:2,exp:'$A(\\omega)=1-e^{-i\\omega}$, so $|A(\\omega)|^2=(1-\\cos\\omega)^2+\\sin^2\\omega=2-2\\cos\\omega=2(1-\\cos\\omega)$; then $f_Y(\\omega)=|A(\\omega)|^2 f_X(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\cdot2(1-\\cos\\omega)$. Why other options are wrong: (B) ignores the filter entirely; (A), (D) are incorrect simplifications of $|A(\\omega)|^2$. Shortcut: First-difference filter has the well-known transfer function magnitude $|A(\\omega)|^2=2(1-\\cos\\omega)$ – memorize this as a standard building-block result. Tip: A commonly tested filter application; also explains why differencing amplifies high-frequency (short-run) variation and suppresses low-frequency (trend) variation.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the filter in Q70, evaluate $f_Y(0)$ and interpret.',opts:['A) Undefined','B) $f_Y(0)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}$; unchanged from white noise','C) $f_Y(0)=\\frac{4}{2\\pi}$; amplified trend','D) $f_Y(0)=0$; differencing removes the entire trend/low-frequency component'],ans:3,exp:'At $\\omega=0$: $2(1-\\cos0)=2(1-1)=0$, so $f_Y(0)=0$ – consistent with differencing eliminating deterministic trends/unit roots. Why other options are wrong: (B), (C) contradict the direct calculation; (A) is false since the formula is perfectly well-defined at $\\omega=0$. Shortcut: Any filter designed to remove a unit root will always show $f_Y(0)=0$ – a useful diagnostic check. Tip: Connects spectral theory directly to the practical rationale behind differencing non-stationary series.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For the filter in Q70, evaluate $f_Y(\\pi)$.',opts:['A) $0$','B) $\\dfrac{4}{2\\pi}$','C) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}$','D) $\\dfrac{2}{2\\pi}$'],ans:1,exp:'At $\\omega=\\pi$: $2(1-\\cos\\pi)=2(1-(-1))=4$, giving $f_Y(\\pi)=\\frac{4}{2\\pi}$. Why other options are wrong: (A) confuses with $f_Y(0)$; (D), (C) are computational slips. Shortcut: Differencing maximally amplifies the highest (Nyquist) frequency – a factor of 4 relative to the flat white-noise spectrum. Tip: Completes the trio of Q70–Q72, a very standard JRF-style multi-part numerical.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A sample of size $n=100$ gives periodogram ordinate $I(\\omega_5)=8$ at some Fourier frequency. If the true spectral density there is $f(\\omega_5)=2$, what is the approximate value of $2I(\\omega_5)/f(\\omega_5)$, and to which distribution does it approximately correspond?',opts:['A) $8$; $\\chi^2_2$','B) $16$; $N(0,1)$','C) $8$; $\\chi^2_1$','D) $4$; $\\chi^2_2$'],ans:0,exp:'$2I(\\omega_5)/f(\\omega_5)=2(8)/2=8$; by the asymptotic result, this quantity approximately follows a $\\chi^2_2$ distribution. Why other options are wrong: (D) miscalculates the ratio; (C), (B) use the wrong reference distribution. Shortcut: Just apply the formula $2I/f\\sim\\chi^2_2$ directly; no complex derivation needed for numerical plug-in questions. Tip: Useful for constructing an approximate confidence interval for $f(\\omega)$ using $\\chi^2_2$ percentiles – a natural follow-up JRF question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For a process with $\\gamma(0)=10$ and $f(\\omega)=\\frac{5}{\\pi}$ (constant for all $\\omega$), what type of process is this consistent with?',opts:['A) Cannot be a valid process','B) White noise with $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=10$','C) AR(1) with $\\phi=0.5$','D) MA(1) with $\\theta=1$'],ans:1,exp:'A constant spectral density $f(\\omega)=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2/2\\pi$ implies white noise; here $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2/2\\pi=5/\\pi \\Rightarrow \\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=10$, matching $\\gamma(0)=10$. Why other options are wrong: AR(1) and MA(1) with nonzero parameters never give a flat spectrum; the process is perfectly valid (matches white noise exactly), so (A) is wrong. Shortcut: “Constant $f(\\omega)$” is an instant white-noise identifier. Tip: Reverse-engineering process type from a given spectral density – a common JRF question style.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For AR(2): $X_t=0.5X_{t-1}-0.25X_{t-2}+\\varepsilon_t$, the denominator of $f(\\omega)$ involves $|1-0.5e^{-i\\omega}+0.25e^{-2i\\omega}|^2$. At $\\omega=0$, this modulus-squared equals:',opts:['A) $(1+0.5-0.25)^2=1.5625$','B) $(1-0.5+0.25)^2=0.5625$','C) $(0.5-0.25)^2=0.0625$','D) $(1-0.5-0.25)^2=0.0625$'],ans:1,exp:'At $\\omega=0$, $e^{-i\\omega}=1$, so the polynomial becomes the real number $1-0.5+0.25=0.75$, and squaring gives $0.5625$. Why other options are wrong: (A), (D), (C) involve sign errors in substituting the AR coefficients. Shortcut: At $\\omega=0$, simply substitute the AR polynomial\'s coefficients directly (no trigonometry needed) and square. Tip: General principle for any AR(p)/MA(q)/ARMA(p,q): evaluating at $\\omega=0$ always reduces to simple real-number algebra.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Using Q75, find $f(0)$ for that AR(2) process if $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=1$.',opts:['A) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}$','B) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(1.5625)}$','C) $\\dfrac{0.5625}{2\\pi}$','D) $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(0.5625)} \\approx \\dfrac{1.778}{2\\pi}$'],ans:3,exp:'$f(0)=\\dfrac{\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2}{2\\pi \\times 0.5625}=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi(0.5625)}\\approx\\dfrac{1.778}{2\\pi}$, using the AR(p) reciprocal formula. Why other options are wrong: (C) forgets to invert (uses AR modulus as if it were an MA-type numerator); (A) ignores AR structure; (B) uses the wrong modulus value. Shortcut: AR spectral density = $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2/(2\\pi \\times \\text{modulus-squared value computed at that }\\omega)$. Tip: Multi-step numerical combining polynomial evaluation with the reciprocal AR formula – a realistic full JRF-difficulty question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A researcher smooths a periodogram using a Bartlett lag window with truncation point $M=10$ on a sample of size $n=200$. If $M$ is increased to $M=40$, what is the expected effect?',opts:['A) Both bias and variance increase','B) Reduced bias, increased variance','C) No change in either bias or variance','D) Increased bias, reduced variance'],ans:1,exp:'A larger truncation point $M$ retains more sample ACVF terms (less smoothing/truncation), reducing bias but increasing variance of the estimator – this is the opposite direction of increasing a spectral-window bandwidth (which smooths more). Why other options are wrong: (D) describes the opposite (correct for decreasing $M$); (C), (A) contradict the fundamental trade-off. Shortcut: For lag windows, larger $M$ = closer to raw periodogram behaviour (low bias, high variance); smaller $M$ = more smoothing (high bias, low variance). Tip: A subtle but important distinction: the direction of the trade-off depends on which smoothing parameter (lag-window $M$ vs spectral-window bandwidth $m$) is being varied – frequently tested at JRF level.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In cross-spectral analysis of two jointly stationary series $\\{X_t\\}, \\{Y_t\\}$, the cross-spectral density $f_{XY}(\\omega)$ is generally:',opts:['A) Complex-valued in general, decomposed into co-spectrum and quadrature spectrum','B) Always zero if $X_t$ and $Y_t$ are correlated','C) Always real and non-negative, like $f_X(\\omega)$','D) Undefined for jointly stationary processes'],ans:0,exp:'Unlike the (real, non-negative) univariate spectral density, the cross-spectral density is generally complex, with real part (co-spectrum) and imaginary part (quadrature spectrum) capturing in-phase and out-of-phase covariation. Why other options are wrong: (C) incorrectly extends the univariate property; (B) is backward (zero cross-spectrum would indicate no linear relationship at that frequency); (D) is false since cross-spectra are well-defined for jointly stationary series. Shortcut: “Cross = complex, univariate = real.” Tip: Research-level extension, relevant for advanced multivariate time series questions.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'The coherence function $C_{XY}(\\omega)$ between two series is analogous, in the frequency domain, to:',opts:['A) The variance ratio','B) The spectral distribution function','C) The squared correlation coefficient, but frequency-specific','D) The autocorrelation function'],ans:2,exp:'Coherence measures the frequency-specific strength of linear association between two series, analogous to $R^2$ but computed separately at each frequency. Why other options are wrong: It is not the ACF (a time-domain, single-series concept), not simply a variance ratio, nor the spectral distribution function (a univariate cumulative concept). Shortcut: “Coherence = frequency-domain $R^2$.” Tip: A common analogy-based research-level question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which of the following best describes why the raw periodogram fails to be a consistent estimator, from a research perspective?',opts:['A) Because it does not use the sample mean','B) Because each ordinate is based on effectively 2 asymptotic degrees of freedom regardless of $n$','C) Because it uses too few Fourier frequencies','D) Because it is biased for small $n$ only'],ans:1,exp:'Each periodogram ordinate\'s asymptotic distribution has only 2 degrees of freedom (from the $\\chi^2_2$ result), so its variance never shrinks no matter how large $n$ becomes – more data creates more ordinates, not more precision per ordinate. Why other options are wrong: It is not about frequency count, finite-sample-only bias, or the sample mean. Shortcut: “2 degrees of freedom forever” is the deep reason behind periodogram inconsistency. Tip: The most conceptually complete answer to “why is the periodogram inconsistent” – valuable for viva/research-oriented JRF interviews too.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'In the context of locally stationary processes, why can a single global spectral density $f(\\omega)$ be inadequate?',opts:['A) Because such processes have no spectral representation','B) Because the second-order structure changes over time, requiring a time-varying $f(t,\\omega)$','C) Because $f(\\omega)$ is always flat for such processes','D) Because $f(\\omega)$ becomes negative'],ans:1,exp:'Locally stationary processes have slowly time-varying covariance structure, so a single time-invariant spectrum cannot capture their dynamics – motivating evolutionary/time-varying spectral density. Why other options are wrong: Flatness, absence of spectral representation, and negativity are unrelated or false claims. Shortcut: “Time-varying structure needs a time-varying spectrum.” Tip: Connects back to Advanced JRF Concepts/Research-Level Remarks from Stage 1 notes.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'A key practical advantage of the multitaper method over a single lag-window estimator is:',opts:['A) It eliminates the need for the Wiener–Khinchin theorem','B) It can reduce variance without the same bias penalty as widening a single window, by exploiting orthogonality of tapers','C) It requires no choice of any tuning parameter at all','D) It applies exclusively to Gaussian AR(1) processes'],ans:1,exp:'By using several orthogonal tapers and averaging, multitaper estimation achieves variance reduction more efficiently, partially decoupling the classical bias–variance trade-off compared to naive window-widening. Why other options are wrong: It still requires choosing the number of tapers (a tuning parameter); it is a general method, not AR(1)-specific; it still relies fundamentally on the Wiener–Khinchin relationship. Shortcut: “Multiple orthogonal views $\\Rightarrow$ better variance reduction for similar bias.” Tip: A genuinely research-level comparative question, appropriate for the most advanced JRF question sets.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'For an ARFIMA$(0,d,0)$ process with $0&lt;d&lt;0.5$, which time-domain property corresponds to the spectral blow-up at $\\omega=0$?',opts:['A) ACVF is exactly zero beyond lag 1','B) ACVF is constant for all lags','C) ACVF decays hyperbolically (slowly), and $\\sum_k|\\gamma(k)|=\\infty$','D) ACVF decays exponentially fast'],ans:2,exp:'Long memory is precisely characterized by hyperbolic (slow, power-law) ACVF decay such that the ACVF is not absolutely summable, matching the spectral density\'s explosion at the origin. Why other options are wrong: Exponential decay corresponds to short-memory (ARMA) processes; exact cutoff describes MA(1); constant ACVF is not a valid stationary-process property. Shortcut: “Long memory = slow (hyperbolic) ACVF decay = spectral blow-up at zero” – the two are two sides of the same coin. Tip: Connects Wiener–Khinchin summability condition directly to the definition of long memory – a favourite deep-conceptual JRF question.'},
  {unit:'u7',q:'Which statement best reflects current research practice when spectral density estimates are needed for a time series with suspected structural breaks?',opts:['A) Use evolutionary/time-varying spectral methods rather than assuming global stationarity','B) Always use the raw periodogram without smoothing','C) Assume the process is white noise','D) Structural breaks do not affect spectral analysis'],ans:0,exp:'When stationarity is questionable due to structural breaks, evolutionary or locally stationary spectral methods are the appropriate research-level tool, rather than a single global spectral density. Why other options are wrong: Using a raw periodogram, assuming white noise, or ignoring the break are all inappropriate/incorrect responses to non-stationarity. Shortcut: “Structural break $\\Rightarrow$ suspect global stationarity $\\Rightarrow$ consider time-varying spectral methods.” Tip: Synthesizes the whole chapter\'s research-level content into a single applied-judgment question – a good note to end the question bank on.'},

);